// 六级 8000 词 · 每组配套短文（由 build/passages/*.txt 生成，字母排序 + 频率全部 + 单个级别时显示）
CET6.passages({"basic":{"1":{"w":["a","able","abortion","about","above","abroad","accountant","across","adaptation","addicted","adjustment","admirable","adolescence","afraid","after","afternoon","again","age","agenda","ago"],"title":"A Year Abroad","t":"Ten years **ago**, Lin, then a quiet **accountant** at a small firm, decided to spend **a** year working **abroad**. Her friends were **afraid** she would regret it. Having grown up in a tiny town, she had never travelled **across** a border before, and the **adjustment** proved far harder than she had expected. **After** a long week at the office, she often spent the **afternoon** of her day off walking along the river, thinking **about** home.\n\nYet the experience taught her a great deal about **adaptation**. She learned to cook with unfamiliar ingredients, to keep a careful **agenda** of meetings in a foreign language, and to ask for help **again** and again without feeling embarrassed. Her colleagues found her determination **admirable**. One of them, a former debate champion, once took her to a public forum where people argued calmly over difficult issues such as **abortion** and gun control, something she had rarely witnessed at home.\n\nLooking back, Lin believes that no **age** is too late for change. Her teenage son, now deep in **adolescence** and **addicted** to video games, sometimes asks her why she left. She tells him that living abroad made her **able** to see her own life from **above**, as if from a mountain top, and that this view was worth every lonely night."},"2":{"w":["agree","agreement","agricultural","ahead","air","airmail","airport","airspace","alcoholic","all","allergic","alley","almost","along","already","also","always","amazing","american","among"],"title":"Letters by Airmail","t":"My grandfather **always** said that the most precious news arrived by **airmail**. In the 1980s he worked for an **American** company that sold **agricultural** machinery to farmers in remote villages, and his letters described the fields, the weather and the people he met **along** the way. My grandmother kept **all** of them in a box under her bed.\n\nBefore his first trip, the two of them had reached an **agreement**: he would come home every spring, no matter how busy he was. She did not **agree** easily, because she knew how long the journey could be. There was no **airport** within three hundred kilometres, and in those years even the **airspace** over the border region was closely controlled.\n\nHis letters were full of small, **amazing** details: a lantern festival in a narrow **alley**, a farmer who was **allergic** to the very wheat he grew, a village where **alcoholic** drinks had been banned because of a quarrel decades earlier. **Among** the stories, my favourite is about a storm that forced him to sleep in a barn for three nights.\n\nWhen I read them now, I can **almost** smell the fresh mountain **air**. He has **already** passed away, but his words are **also** a reminder that the future is never simply waiting **ahead** of us; it grows out of the promises we keep."},"3":{"w":["and","anecdote","angry","animal","announcement","annoy","another","answer","any","anybody","anything","apple","april","apron","arm","armchair","around","arrive","as","asian"],"title":"The Shelter on the Edge of Town","t":"Every Saturday in **April**, my aunt volunteers at an **animal** shelter on the edge of town. She ties on a faded **apron**, rolls up her sleeves **and** spends the morning feeding dogs that seem to have been forgotten by **anybody** who once loved them.\n\nLast spring the shelter made an **announcement** that worried everyone: it would close unless it found **another** source of funding. My aunt was **angry** at first. \"If people can spend money on **anything** they like,\" she said, \"why can't they spare a little for creatures that depend on us?\" But anger, she soon realised, was not an **answer**.\n\nSo she started collecting stories. Her favourite **anecdote** concerns an elderly **Asian** gentleman who would **arrive** every afternoon with a bag of sliced **apple** for a shy brown dog. He would sit in a worn **armchair** in the corner, the dog resting its head on his **arm**, **as** if the two of them had known each other for years. Visitors who heard this story rarely left without making a donation.\n\nSome neighbours complained that the barking began to **annoy** them, yet even they came **around** in the end. Today the shelter is still open, and my aunt insists that **any** problem becomes smaller once people are willing to listen to each other."},"4":{"w":["ask","assistance","astronomer","at","athletics","attentively","august","aunt","autonomous","autumn","away","awesome","baby","back","backache","bad","bag","bakery","ball","ballpoint"],"title":"Watching the Sky","t":"Last **August**, my **aunt**, an amateur **astronomer**, invited me to spend a week with her in a small village far **away** from the city lights. Each night we climbed the hill behind her house **at** around ten o'clock, carrying a telescope, a flask of tea and a **bag** of bread from the local **bakery**.\n\nI had always thought of stargazing as a lazy hobby, but it turned out to be surprisingly demanding. After an hour of bending over the telescope I had a terrible **backache**, and I was too tired to **ask** questions. My aunt, on the other hand, watched the sky **attentively**, recording each observation with an old **ballpoint** pen. \"Patience matters more than equipment,\" she told me. \"Even **bad** weather can teach you something.\"\n\nShe explained that she belonged to an **autonomous** research network in which amateurs share their data with universities. Professional scientists often rely on their **assistance** to track comets and variable stars. Before retiring, she had coached **athletics** at a secondary school, and she still approached every task with the energy of a coach on a training field.\n\nOn the last night, a meteor shot across the sky like a burning **ball**. \"That was **awesome**!\" I shouted, as excited as a **baby** seeing snow for the first time. When I went **back** to the city that **autumn**, I bought a telescope of my own."},"5":{"w":["banana","bank","barbecue","barbershop","basket","basketball","bathrobe","bathtub","battleground","be","beancurd","bear","beat","beautiful","because","become","bed","bedclothes","bedroom","beef"],"title":"House Rules","t":"When my cousin rented his first apartment after graduation, he quickly learned that a small home can easily **become** a **battleground**. His roommate wanted to hang a **basketball** hoop on the **bedroom** door; he wanted quiet. One insisted on cooking **beef** every night, while the other preferred **beancurd** and vegetables. Even the **bathtub** caused arguments, **because** neither of them liked cleaning it.\n\nThings changed one Sunday morning. My cousin came home from the **barbershop** with a fresh haircut and found his roommate standing in the kitchen in a **bathrobe**, holding a **basket** full of fruit. \"I think we should talk,\" the roommate said, peeling a **banana**. They sat on the floor beside a pile of freshly washed **bedclothes** and drew up a list of rules.\n\nThe list was simple: no music after eleven, separate shelves in the fridge, and a **barbecue** on the balcony every Friday to which both of them could invite friends. They also agreed to put a little money each month into a shared **bank** account for repairs.\n\nThey had expected their old habits to **beat** the new rules within a week, but the opposite happened. Within a month the apartment felt calm, even **beautiful**. My cousin admits he can still hardly **bear** the sound of a bouncing ball, but he now sleeps soundly in his own **bed**. Sharing a home, he says, is less about space than about learning to **be** patient."},"6":{"w":["beehive","beer","before","begin","behind","believe","bell","belly","below","bent","beside","better","between","big","bike","bingo","biochemistry","biography","bird","birdcage"],"title":"The Beekeeper's Notebook","t":"My neighbour, Mr. Zhou, taught **biochemistry** for thirty years **before** he retired to a cottage at the foot of a hill. Most mornings he rides an old **bike** down the lane, a basket of tools tied to the back and a straw hat pulled low over his eyes. **Behind** the cottage stands his pride and joy: a white wooden **beehive**, humming with life.\n\nHe has always had a **bent** for patient observation. \"People **believe** bees work at random,\" he told me, \"but every one of them knows its job.\" He showed me how the colony keeps its temperature **between** thirty-four and thirty-six degrees, and how a single careless move can turn a calm hive into a **big** problem. When the queen is about to leave with a swarm, he rings a small brass **bell**, an old custom his grandfather swore by.\n\nNot everything around the cottage is about bees. **Beside** the door hangs an empty **birdcage**; he set the **bird** free years ago, he says, because it sang **better** outside. On summer evenings he sits on the porch with a glass of cold **beer**, patting his round **belly** and laughing at his own jokes. On Fridays he walks to the village hall to play **bingo** with old friends.\n\nNow he has decided to **begin** a new project: a **biography** of his grandfather, the man who first taught him to listen to the hum **below** the surface of things."},"7":{"w":["birthday","birthplace","bit","black","blackboard","block","blow","blue","boat","boating","body","book","bookcase","bookmark","bookshelf","bookshop","bookstore","boom","bored","boring"],"title":"The Little Bookshop by the Lake","t":"For my twentieth **birthday**, I took a train to my grandmother's **birthplace**, a small town beside a lake in the south. I expected the trip to be **boring**; the guidebook described the place in a single dull paragraph. Instead, I found a town in the middle of an unexpected tourism **boom**.\n\nIts main street, barely one **block** long, was lined with cafés and shops painted **blue** and white. At the far end stood a tiny **bookshop** run by a retired teacher. An old **blackboard** outside listed the day's recommendations in neat chalk, and inside, every wall was covered by a **bookshelf** that reached the ceiling. \"A big chain has opened a **bookstore** in the next city,\" the owner said, \"but people still come here to talk.\"\n\nShe showed me a **book** of local history and a hand-painted **bookmark** her students had made decades ago. When I admitted that I had often been **bored** by history lessons at school, she laughed. \"Then you were reading the wrong books.\" From a glass **bookcase** she pulled out a photo album bound in **black** leather, and there was my grandmother at sixteen, **boating** on the lake.\n\nThat afternoon I rented a small **boat** myself. A cold wind began to **blow** across the water, and my whole **body** shook, but I stayed out a **bit** longer than I had planned, trying to see the town the way she once had."},"8":{"w":["borrow","botanical","botany","both","bottle","bowling","box","boxing","boy","bravery","bread","break","breakfast","breakthrough","breathless","brewery","bride","bridge","bright","bring"],"title":"A Wedding in the Old Brewery","t":"Last May my cousin, a **boxing** coach, married a **bride** who had spent six years studying **botany**. They had met, of all places, at a **bowling** alley, where she was trying in vain to **break** her own record and he offered, rather boldly, to give her a few tips. \"It took real **bravery**,\" she joked in her wedding speech, \"because he was terrible at bowling.\"\n\nThe ceremony was held in a former **brewery** that had been turned into a restaurant, right next to the city's **botanical** garden. Guests crossed a wooden **bridge** over a stream to reach the hall, and each of them received a small **box** of flower seeds and a glass **bottle** of homemade lemonade. The couple had asked everyone to **bring** a book instead of a gift, so that they could start a little library at the school where the bride now teaches.\n\nThe night before, nobody slept much. My sister and I **both** stayed up decorating the hall, and by morning the bride's younger brother, a lively **boy** of ten, was **breathless** from running errands. **Breakfast** was nothing more than coffee and **bread**, yet it felt like a feast.\n\nFor the bride, the day was a personal **breakthrough**: after years of shyness, she spoke in front of two hundred people without notes. Several guests had to **borrow** tissues from me. The future, she said, looked as **bright** as the spring sunshine pouring through the old brewery windows."},"9":{"w":["british","brochure","brother","brotherhood","brown","brunch","brush","buffet","build","building","bun","bungalow","bureaucratic","bus","businessman","businesswoman","busy","but","buy","by"],"title":"Two Brothers, One Business","t":"My uncle and his younger **brother** grew up in a **brown** brick **bungalow** on the edge of a fishing town. Their father sold steamed **buns|bun** from a cart near the **bus** station, and from an early age the two boys dreamed of owning a real shop. Today my uncle is a successful **businessman** who runs a chain of bakeries, **but** he still remembers the smell of that cart.\n\nWhen they decided to **build** their first café, everything seemed to go wrong. The licensing process was so **bureaucratic** that they had to submit the same documents five times, and a **British** investor who had promised to **buy** a share pulled out at the last minute. For months they were too **busy** to see their families. What kept them going, they say, was a sense of **brotherhood** that no setback could weaken.\n\nThe café finally opened in an old **building** near the harbour. They printed a simple **brochure** with photos of the sea and offered a weekend **brunch** **buffet** that soon drew queues on Saturday mornings. Customers loved the hand-painted walls; my uncle had picked up a **brush** himself to finish them **by** opening day.\n\nTheir success also inspired my aunt, who has since become a **businesswoman** in her own right, selling the café's recipes online. Running a family business, she likes to say, is like kneading dough: it takes strength, patience and a little warmth."},"10":{"w":["bye","cab","call","can","caption","car","card","care","careful","carry","case","cat","catch","celebration","certainly","chair","chairwoman","challenging","change","changeable"],"title":"Rescue in the Rain","t":"The weather in our city is famously **changeable**, and on the evening of my sister's graduation **celebration** it **certainly** lived up to its reputation. Bright sunshine turned into heavy rain within minutes, and our plans for a picnic had to **change** at once.\n\nWe decided to **call** a **cab** instead of driving the **car**, because the roads were already flooding. Just as we were leaving, my sister noticed that her **cat** was missing. In any other **case** we would have waited until morning, but she was far too worried. \"How **can** I celebrate while he is out in this storm?\" she said, and refused to say **bye** to anyone until we promised to help.\n\nSearching in the dark proved more **challenging** than we expected. We had to be **careful** on the slippery steps, and I tried to **carry** a torch and an umbrella at the same time. At last we found him hiding under a garden **chair** in the neighbour's yard, soaked and furious. It took three of us to **catch** him.\n\nBack home, the **chairwoman** of the neighbourhood committee, who had joined the search, laughed when she saw us. She wrote a short **caption** under the photo she had taken, \"Graduates Rescue Tiger\", and printed it on a greeting **card** for my sister. It reminded us that the people who truly **care** about you are the ones who show up in the rain."},"11":{"w":["chant","chat","cheap","check","chef","chick","child","chinese","choir","chopsticks","cinema","city","class","classroom","clean","cleaner","clear","click","clone","close"],"title":"The Dumpling Class","t":"Every Thursday evening, a **Chinese** **chef** named Wei turns his small restaurant in the old part of the **city** into a **classroom**. The tables are pushed together, aprons are handed out, and a dozen students, from a retired **choir** singer to a curious ten-year-old **child**, gather around him to learn how to make dumplings.\n\nThe **class** is surprisingly **cheap**, because Wei is less interested in money than in company. While the students fold dough, he likes to **chat** about the history of each dish. He insists that everyone use **chopsticks** rather than forks, and he makes the rules **clear** from the first lesson: wash your hands, keep your station **clean**, and **check** the pot before you walk away.\n\nSome evenings are chaotic. Once a new student tried to **clone** Wei's famous sauce by following a recipe on his phone, **click** after click, only to burn it completely. Another time a fluffy yellow **chick**, the pet of a neighbour's son, wandered in through the back door, and the whole room began to **chant** its new name, \"Little Dumpling,\" until it fell asleep in a basket.\n\nAfter class, the students sometimes walk together to the **cinema** across the street, leaving the restaurant to the **cleaner**, an old friend of Wei's who refuses to be paid. \"Food brings strangers **close**,\" Wei says. \"That is the real recipe.\""},"12":{"w":["clothes","cloud","cloudy","cocoa","coffee","coke","college","come","comma","company","computer","conversation","cook","cooker","cookie","cool","copy","coral","cordless","corner"],"title":"A Café Called The Comma","t":"Near my **college** there is a tiny café called The **Comma**. Its owner, a former journalist, chose the name because she believes everyone needs a short pause in the middle of a busy sentence. The place sits on a quiet **corner**, and on **cloudy** afternoons it is full of students hiding from their deadlines.\n\nThe menu is short but generous. You can order strong **coffee**, hot **cocoa** with cinnamon or an ice-cold **coke**, and every drink **comes|come** with a homemade **cookie**. The owner likes to **cook** everything herself on an old electric **cooker** in the back, and she keeps a **copy** of every recipe in a notebook she refuses to put on a **computer**.\n\nWhat I love most is the **conversation**. Strangers share tables, and you never know what you will learn. Last week a marine biologist explained to me why **coral** reefs lose their colour when the sea grows too warm; the week before, an elderly tailor told me how fashions in **clothes** have changed since the 1970s. Even the owner joins in, carrying a **cordless** phone in case her suppliers call.\n\nWhen I leave, the evening air is usually **cool**, and a thin **cloud** often hangs over the hills. I walk home slowly, feeling that the best **company** is sometimes found by accident."},"13":{"w":["cost","could","country","countryside","courtyard","cover","cowboy","crayon","credit","crossing","crossroads","crowded","cry","cuisine","cup","cut","cyclist","dad","dance","dark"],"title":"A Long Ride Home","t":"When my **dad** turned fifty, he surprised everyone by deciding to ride a bicycle across the **country**. He had never been a serious **cyclist**, and the plan seemed likely to **cost** him far more than money. Yet he trained for a year, and one spring morning he set off from our **crowded** apartment block with a small tent and a battered **cup** tied to his backpack.\n\nHis messages from the road were short but vivid. He described a **crossing** of a wide river on an old ferry, a village **dance** that went on until midnight, and a farmer in a wide hat who looked like a **cowboy** from an old film. He sampled the local **cuisine** wherever he stopped, and more than once he slept in the **courtyard** of a friendly family who refused his money.\n\nHalfway through the journey he reached a **crossroads**, both literally and in his mind. Heavy rain had **cut** off the road ahead, and he wondered whether he **could** go on. He called my mother and admitted that he almost wanted to **cry**. She reminded him that he did not need to **cover** every kilometre; the point was to see the **countryside** he had always admired from train windows.\n\nHe came home three months later, thin and sunburnt. On our kitchen wall now hangs a map that my little niece decorated in **crayon**, tracing his route. Whenever my dad tells the story, he gives the **credit** to the strangers who helped him, especially on the **dark** nights when he felt most alone."},"14":{"w":["darkness","database","date","daughter","day","dead","deadline","dear","december","decide","decoration","deep","deeply","delighted","desk","develop","development","dialogue","dictionary","die"],"title":"The Night Before the Deadline","t":"It was a cold night in **December**, and the office was almost empty. Mrs. Liang sat at her **desk**, staring at a screen full of numbers. The **deadline** for her team's report was nine o'clock the next morning, and the **database** that held three years of customer records had suddenly stopped responding.\n\nShe thought of her **daughter**, who had a school concert that evening, and felt a **deep** pang of guilt. She had promised to be there. Now the **date** was circled in red on her calendar for a very different reason.\n\nAt eleven the power failed, and the room fell into total **darkness**. For a moment she wondered whether the whole project was **dead**. Then she found a torch in a drawer, beside an old **dictionary** and a box of Christmas **decoration** lights left over from last year. She strung the lights across the window, laughed at herself and kept working by their soft glow.\n\nBy three in the morning the system was back. She **decided|decide** not to chase every number; instead she would describe the **development** of the main trends in plain language and **develop** a short list of recommendations. When her manager read the report the next **day**, he was **delighted**. \"**Dear** me,\" he said, \"this is the clearest thing you have ever written.\"\n\nThat evening she sat down with her daughter for a long **dialogue** about the concert she had missed. She was **deeply** moved when the girl said, \"Mum, you always told me never to let a good idea **die**. I'm glad you didn't, either.\""},"15":{"w":["different","difficult","dig","dinner","dinosaur","dirty","disability","disabled","disappointment","discuss","disgusting","dish","disobey","disturbing","do","doctor","dog","doll","dollar","door"],"title":"Digging for a Dinosaur","t":"Last summer my nephew Leo, who was eight, decided to **dig** for a **dinosaur** in his grandparents' back garden. He had seen a documentary about fossils and was certain that a giant skeleton lay hidden under the vegetable beds. His grandmother, a retired **doctor**, did not have the heart to tell him otherwise.\n\nFor three days he worked with a small spade, his clothes growing more **dirty** by the hour. The family **dog** followed him everywhere, barking at every worm. His little sister offered to help, but only if her favourite **doll** could supervise from a chair by the kitchen **door**.\n\nOn the fourth day he found something: a long, curved bone. The whole family gathered round to **discuss** it over **dinner**. His grandfather examined it and announced, with great seriousness, that it was **different** from any dinosaur bone he had ever seen. In fact it was the bone of a cow, probably buried decades ago, and it smelled **disgusting**.\n\nLeo's **disappointment** was obvious, and he refused to touch a single **dish** that evening. His grandmother told him that being wrong is not **disturbing** at all; it is simply part of how science works. She also told him about a famous fossil hunter with a physical **disability** who made major discoveries despite being told that fieldwork was too **difficult** for **disabled** people.\n\nThe next morning Leo was back in the garden. He had been told not to dig near the roses, and for once he did not **disobey**. He even sold a few \"fossils\" to his relatives for a **dollar** each, promising to **do** better next time."},"16":{"w":["double","down","download","downtown","draw","dress","drier","drink","drive","driver","dry","dumpling","during","dustbin","dusty","dvd","dynasty","each","ear","early"],"title":"The Day Before Spring Festival","t":"**Early** on the morning before Spring Festival, my uncle, a taxi **driver**, picked me up at the train station. \"We have a lot to do,\" he said, and pulled into the traffic heading **downtown**. He loves to **drive**, but even he admitted that the holiday rush was enough to make anyone feel **down**.\n\nOur first stop was his mother's flat, where the whole family was preparing for the New Year's Eve dinner. **Each** of us had a job. My cousins folded **dumpling** after dumpling at the kitchen table, while my aunt tried to **dry** her freshly washed **dress** with a hair **drier**, because the rain had made it impossible to hang anything outside. Grandpa sat in the corner with a hot **drink**, telling anyone who would listen about the Tang **Dynasty**, his favourite period of history.\n\nI was sent to tidy the storeroom, a **dusty** space full of old furniture. I filled the **dustbin** twice with broken toys and discovered a box of my grandmother's **DVD** collection, mostly costume dramas from the 1990s. My little nephew asked me to **draw** a dragon on a red paper lantern, then begged me to **download** a cartoon for him on my phone.\n\n**During** dinner the noise was so loud that I could barely hear the person speaking right into my **ear**. Grandpa raised his glass and wished everyone **double** happiness in the coming year. Outside, fireworks began to light up the sky."},"17":{"w":["earth","east","eastwards","easy","eat","educator","egg","eggplant","eight","eighth","either","eleven","else","email","empty","encouragement","end","english","enjoy","enjoyable"],"title":"A Teacher Who Moved East","t":"Ms. Grant is a retired **educator** from Canada who has taught **English** in **eleven** countries. For most of her career she moved steadily **eastwards**, from Europe to the Middle East and finally to a small mountain town in western China. \"I always felt pulled toward the **east**,\" she says, \"as if the **earth** itself were turning me in that direction.\"\n\nHer life there was simple but **enjoyable**. She lived in a small house with an **empty** courtyard, rose at **eight** every morning and walked to school past fields of **eggplant** and chilli. Teaching was not always **easy**: some of her students had never met a foreigner, and at first they were too shy to speak at all. What changed everything, she believes, was **encouragement**. She praised every attempt, however small, and soon even the quietest pupils raised their hands.\n\nShe also learned a great deal from them. They taught her to **eat** with chopsticks, to fry an **egg** with tomato the local way, and to sing folk songs she still remembers. When she could not find something at the market, a student always knew someone **else** who sold it.\n\nIt is now the **eighth** year since she retired, but she keeps in touch with former students by **email**, and many of them have become teachers themselves. Nothing, she says, makes her **enjoy** life more than hearing about their classes. Asked whether she misses the travelling or the teaching more, she laughs and says she cannot choose **either**. \"The **end** of teaching,\" she adds, \"is not knowledge but courage.\""},"18":{"w":["enough","eraser","european","even","evening","ever","every","everybody","everyone","everything","everywhere","exam","example","except","excuse","exercise","expect","expensive","explorer","eye"],"title":"Exam Week","t":"In the week before the final **exam**, our dormitory turned into a strange little world. **Everybody** studied **everywhere**: on the stairs, in the laundry room, even in the corridor at midnight. My roommate kept an **eraser** behind her ear like a carpenter's pencil and filled notebook after notebook with formulas.\n\nI tried to follow her **example**, but by Wednesday **evening** I felt that I had learned nothing. **Everything** I read seemed to slip away the moment I closed the book. I began to invent an **excuse** for **every** break: a walk, a snack, a phone call home. My mother listened patiently and then asked, \"Have you **ever** tried taking a real rest?\"\n\nSo I did. I went for a run along the river, my first proper **exercise** in days. On the way I passed a bookshop window displaying the diary of a famous **European** **explorer** who had crossed Antarctica. The book was far too **expensive** for a student, but I stood there reading the first page through the glass. He wrote that on the hardest days he did not **expect** to reach the end; he only tried to walk far **enough** to see the next hill.\n\nThat line had caught my **eye**, and it stayed with me. **Except** for one difficult chemistry question, the exam went better than I had feared. When **everyone** gathered afterwards to compare answers, I realised that **even** the strongest students had been just as nervous as I was."},"19":{"w":["eyewitness","face","facial","factory","fail","fairness","fall","family","famous","far","farm","fast","fat","father","february","feel","few","field","fifteen","fifth"],"title":"The Night the Roof Fell","t":"One cold night in **February**, heavy snow caused part of the roof of an old **factory** on the edge of our town to **fall** in. Luckily, the night shift had ended **fifteen** minutes earlier, and no one was hurt. The only **eyewitness** was a **farm** worker driving home along the road beside the **field**.\n\nThe next morning a reporter interviewed him. He was a large man with a **fat** woollen scarf around his neck and a **face** red from the wind, and his **facial** expression changed several times as he spoke, from shock to relief. \"I heard a sound like thunder,\" he said. \"At first I thought I was imagining things, but I could **feel** the ground shake even from **far** away.\"\n\nThe factory had been the town's most **famous** employer for decades. My **father** worked there for twenty years, and so did half the families on our street. For many people, the accident showed how badly the place had aged. A **few** workers complained that the managers had wanted to cut costs **fast** and had ignored warnings about the old building.\n\nIn the weeks that followed, the town held meetings about **fairness** in compensating the workers who had lost shifts. The company promised not to **fail** them, and the mayor visited the site for the **fifth** time that winter. For my **family**, it was the first time we talked seriously about what the town might become without the factory."},"20":{"w":["fifty","fill","film","find","fine","fingernail","finish","fire","firefighter","firewood","firmly","first","fish","fit","five","flaming","flashlight","flight","floor","flower"],"title":"Twenty-Five Years in Uniform","t":"My neighbour Chen has been a **firefighter** for more than half of his **fifty** years, and he is still as **fit** as a man of thirty. When I asked him to describe his **first** real emergency, he smiled and told me about a winter night in a mountain village.\n\nA family had been drying **firewood** beside an old stove, and a spark had set the pile alight. By the time his team arrived, **flaming** pieces of wood were dropping from the roof onto the **floor** below. The power was out, so they worked by **flashlight**, moving slowly from room to room. It took them **five** hours to **finish** the job.\n\n\"People think the hardest part is the **fire**,\" he said, \"but it's actually the waiting afterwards.\" He described standing in the snow while the family tried to **find** their belongings. An old woman held **firmly** onto a pot of winter **flowers|flower**, the only thing she had carried out. A boy kept asking whether his pet **fish** had survived; luckily, the tank was untouched.\n\nChen now trains young recruits and shows them a short safety **film** before every exercise. He also visits schools, where children **fill** the hall with questions. One asked why he keeps every **fingernail** cut so short; another wanted to know whether he had ever taken a **flight** in a rescue helicopter. He answers them all patiently. The best reward, he says, is hearing a family tell him that they are **fine**."},"21":{"w":["fluency","fly","foggy","food","foot","football","for","foreign","forget","forgetful","form","format","forty","forward","founding","four","fourteen","fourth","franc","free"],"title":"French at Forty","t":"At **forty**, my aunt decided to learn French. She had always loved **foreign** films, and after the **founding** of a small language school in our neighbourhood, she finally had no excuse. \"If I don't start now,\" she said, \"I'll **forget** the idea for another ten years.\"\n\nClasses were held **four** evenings a week. There were **fourteen** students, ranging from a teenage **football** player to a retired pilot who used to **fly** to Paris every month. The teacher used an unusual **format**: no textbooks for the first month, only songs, games and **food**. Each student had to bring a dish and describe it in French.\n\nMy aunt struggled at first. She is a little **forgetful**, and she often walked to class on **foot** through the **foggy** streets, repeating new words under her breath, only to lose them the moment she arrived. She even filled in the enrolment **form** for the next level twice by mistake.\n\nBy the **fourth** month, however, she noticed real progress. Her **fluency** was still limited, but she could hold a conversation **for** several minutes without panicking. One evening the teacher showed the class an old **franc** coin and explained how French money had changed before the euro; my aunt kept it as a lucky charm.\n\nNow she is looking **forward** to a trip to Lyon next spring. Learning a language in middle age, she says, has made her feel **free** in a way she had not felt since university."},"22":{"w":["freeway","freezing","friday","fried","friend","friendly","friendship","from","front","fruit","full","fun","furnished","future","gale","game","garden","gardening","gate","get"],"title":"Weekends at the Cabin","t":"Every **Friday** in winter, my oldest **friend** and I drive out of the city on the **freeway** to her family's cabin in the hills. The cabin is small but fully **furnished**, with a wood stove, two narrow beds and a shelf **full** of board games.\n\nLast weekend the weather was **freezing**. A **gale** blew all night, rattling the windows and bending the trees in the **garden**. We stayed inside, playing a card **game** we had invented as children and eating **fried** rice with whatever vegetables and **fruit** we could find in the fridge. It was simple, but it was great **fun**.\n\nThe cabin belongs to her grandfather, who still drives up every spring to do the **gardening**. He is a **friendly** man who greets every visitor at the **front** door as if they were family. He once told us that a lasting **friendship** is like a garden: it needs time, patience and a little attention every season.\n\nOn Sunday morning the wind finally dropped. We walked down to the old wooden **gate** at the end of the lane and watched the sun rise over the fields. My friend asked me what I expected **from** the **future**. I said I did not know, but I hoped we would still **get** to spend weekends like this when we were old."},"23":{"w":["gift","gifted","giraffe","girl","give","glad","glass","glasshouse","go","goldfish","good","goodbye","grade","graduation","gram","grandchild","grandma","grandpa","grandparent","granny"],"title":"Grandpa's Glasshouse","t":"When I was a little **girl**, my **grandpa** kept a **glasshouse** at the bottom of his garden. Inside it grew tomatoes, orchids and a lemon tree that produced exactly one lemon a year. On sunny afternoons the **glass** walls turned the place into a warm, green world, and I would sit there for hours watching the **goldfish** in a small stone pond.\n\nMy **grandma**, whom everyone in the village called **Granny** Li, was the practical one. She weighed every **gram** of flour when she baked and kept careful notes on which seeds did well. Grandpa, by contrast, was a **gifted** storyteller. He told me that the tallest sunflower in the glasshouse was really a **giraffe** in disguise, and for years I half believed him.\n\nEvery **grandparent**, I think, wants to **give** something that lasts. For my **graduation** from primary school, he gave me a **gift** I still treasure: a notebook in which he had drawn every plant he had ever grown. \"You were always **good** at noticing things,\" he wrote on the first page. \"Keep your eyes open, whatever **grade** you get.\"\n\nYears later, when I had a child of my own, I took her to see the old glasshouse before it was sold. She was the youngest **grandchild** in the family, and she ran from plant to plant shouting with delight. As we said **goodbye** to the place, I felt sad, but I was also **glad**. Some things have to **go** so that their stories can travel further."},"24":{"w":["grass","great","green","greengrocer","grill","ground","group","grow","gruel","guess","guest","gym","gymnastics","ha","hair","half","hand","handbag","handtruck","happily"],"title":"The Greengrocer on Our Street","t":"Mr. Ho has been the **greengrocer** on our street for almost thirty years. Every morning at six he wheels crates of vegetables out of his van on a battered **handtruck**, stacking cabbages, carrots and **green** beans in neat rows on the pavement. He knows almost everyone by name and remembers what each **guest** in his little shop likes to buy.\n\nHe grew up in the countryside, where his parents taught him how to **grow** vegetables on a small plot of land. \"In those days we often had nothing but rice **gruel** for breakfast,\" he told me, \"but the food tasted **great** because we had grown it with our own **hands|hand**.\"\n\nOn summer evenings he fires up a small **grill** outside the shop and invites neighbours to taste roasted corn. A **group** of children from the local **gym** often stops by after **gymnastics** practice, their **hair** still wet, and sits on the **grass** of the little park across the road. One evening a girl asked him to **guess** how old she was. He studied her seriously and said \"twenty-five\", and she burst out laughing: \"**Ha**! I'm nine!\"\n\nLast month a woman accidentally left her **handbag** on the counter. Mr. Ho closed the shop for **half** an hour and walked around the neighbourhood until he found her. She thanked him **happily**, and he simply shrugged. For him, the shop is not just a business; it is the common **ground** on which the whole street meets."},"25":{"w":["happy","hard","hardworking","harmless","hat","have","he","head","headmistress","headteacher","health","healthy","hear","hearing","heart","heavy","hello","help","her","here"],"title":"The New Headmistress","t":"When Mrs. Wu became **headmistress** of our village school, many parents were doubtful. The previous **headteacher** had been a strict man who rarely smiled, and **he** had run the school the same way for twenty years. Mrs. Wu was different. On her first morning she stood at the gate in a bright red **hat**, saying **hello** to every child by name.\n\nShe believed that a **healthy** body and a **happy** mind go together. She replaced sugary snacks in the canteen with fruit, started a morning running club and invited a nurse to talk to the pupils about **health** and sleep. She also arranged free **hearing** tests, after which three children who had been labelled lazy turned out simply to have trouble following the teacher.\n\nNot everything went smoothly. Some teachers complained that her ideas meant more **heavy** work for them, and one or two parents thought her plans were a waste of money. Mrs. Wu listened to every complaint. \"I **hear** you,\" she would say, \"and I want to **help**.\" She was **hardworking** herself, often staying until late in the evening, and slowly people began to trust **her**.\n\nThe changes were mostly small and **harmless**, yet together they transformed the school. Five years later, when she announced her retirement, the hall was full. A former pupil, now a doctor, stood at the **head** of the stage and said that Mrs. Wu had taught them that learning does not **have** to be **hard** to matter. \"She led with her **heart**,\" he said, \"and that is why we are all **here** today.\""},"26":{"w":["hers","herself","hey","hi","hibernate","hibernation","high","hill","hilly","him","himself","his","hive","hobby","hold","holiday","home","homeland","hometown","homework"],"title":"Winter in the Hills","t":"My cousin Mei grew up in a **hilly** region in the north, and every winter **holiday** she goes back to her **hometown** to help her parents on their small farm. Last year she invited my brother and me to come with her.\n\nOn the first evening, her father took us up the **hill** behind the house to show us the bee **hive** he had wrapped in straw for the cold months. Bees do not really **hibernate**, he explained, but they cluster together to stay warm, which looks a little like **hibernation** from the outside. My brother, who has made insects his **hobby**, was delighted, and he asked so many questions that Mei's father finally laughed and told **him** to come back in spring.\n\nMei was busier than any of us. She cooked, chopped wood and still found time for her university **homework** in the evenings. \"This place is my **homeland**,\" she said, \"even though my life is in the city now.\" Her old bedroom was full of school prizes; one shelf was **hers**, the other her sister's.\n\nOn our last day we climbed to the **highest|high** point of the valley. \"**Hey**!\" my brother shouted to a shepherd far below, and the man waved back, calling \"**Hi**!\" in return. Mei stood quietly, trying to **hold** the view in her memory. My brother, true to **himself**, was already planning **his** next trip. I realised that for Mei, going **home** was not about the past at all; it was a way of reminding **herself** who she was."},"27":{"w":["hooray","hope","horse","hospital","hot","hotdog","hotel","hour","house","housework","how","hundred","hungry","hunter","hurry","husband","ice","idea","if","ill"],"title":"A Week on the Grassland","t":"Last summer my sister and her **husband** booked a week at a small **hotel** on a **horse** farm in Inner Mongolia. It was their first real holiday in years; until then their lives had been an endless cycle of office work and **housework**.\n\nThe owner, a former **hunter** who now protects the wildlife he once chased, met them at the gate. \"Here you never need to **hurry**,\" he said. Breakfast was served at whatever **hour** guests got up, and the old wooden **house** behind the stables had no television, only shelves of books.\n\nOn the third day the weather turned very **hot**. My sister felt **ill** after a long ride and spent the afternoon resting, and the owner's wife brought her a bowl of **ice** and some cold tea. **If** she had not felt better by evening, they would have driven to the nearest **hospital**, a **hundred** kilometres away. Luckily, by sunset she was **hungry** again and happily ate a **hotdog** grilled over the campfire, the owner's one concession to \"foreign food\".\n\nThat night, sitting under a sky full of stars, her husband had an **idea**: why not come back every year? \"**Hooray**!\" my sister cried, laughing at her own excitement. When she told me about the trip, she said she still did not understand **how** such a simple place could feel so restful. \"I **hope** we never lose that feeling,\" she added."},"28":{"w":["important","in","information","instead","interesting","into","irrigate","it","its","itself","january","jeep","jewelry","job","join","juice","juicy","july","jump","june"],"title":"Water at the Edge of the Desert","t":"**In** **January**, an engineer named Hassan took a **job** with a project that helps farmers **irrigate** land at the edge of a desert. On his first morning he climbed into an old **jeep** and drove three hours across rough tracks to reach the village. It was a long way from the city, but he found the work more **interesting** than anything he had done before.\n\nThe village had a well, but **its** water was too salty for most crops. **Instead** of drilling deeper, Hassan's team installed a simple drip system that delivered water straight **into** the roots of each plant. Farmers were invited to **join** training sessions, where they shared **information** about soil and weather. The most **important** lesson, Hassan says, was that the system had to be easy to repair; a machine that only an expert can fix is soon abandoned.\n\nBy **June** the first melons were ripening. In **July** the village held a small festival, and children ran from stall to stall drinking fresh **juice** and eating **juicy** slices of watermelon. One woman proudly showed him a piece of silver **jewelry** she had bought with the money from her first harvest.\n\nPrices for fruit at the regional market began to **jump**, and the village suddenly had a future. Hassan still visits whenever he can. \"The desert does not change **itself**,\" he likes to say, \"but **it** can be persuaded to cooperate under the right conditions.\""},"29":{"w":["just","kangaroo","keep","key","kilo","kind","kindergarten","kitchen","knife","knock","know","labourer","lake","land","language","large","last","late","latest","laugh"],"title":"A Kangaroo in the Playground","t":"My friend Sophie moved to a small town in Australia **last** year to teach at a **kindergarten** beside a **lake**. Before she left, she did not **know** a single word of the local slang, and she worried that **language** would be her biggest problem.\n\nOn her first day, a boy came running into the classroom shouting that a **kangaroo** was standing in the playground. Sophie thought he was joking, but when she looked out of the window, there it was, chewing grass as calmly as a cow. The children were not surprised at all; for them it was **just** another morning. The head teacher simply asked everyone to **keep** quiet and let the animal hop away.\n\nThe school runs on a tight budget. The cook, a former farm **labourer**, prepares lunch for forty children in a tiny **kitchen** with one sharp **knife** and an enormous pot. He buys rice by the **kilo** and vegetables from farmers who own **land** around the lake, and parents often **knock** on the kitchen door to drop off fresh eggs or bread.\n\nSophie soon found that the **key** to her new life was patience and a sense of humour. When she arrived **late** for a staff meeting after getting lost, everyone burst out **laughing|laugh**, and she laughed with them. In her **latest** email, she wrote that she has never met such **kind** people, and that a **large** part of her heart now belongs to that little town."},"30":{"w":["league","learn","leave","left","leftover","leg","lemonade","lend","lesson","let","letter","library","lie","life","light","like","listen","little","live","long"],"title":"The Library Volunteer","t":"Every Saturday my grandfather volunteers at the public **library** in our town. He is seventy-six and walks with a slight limp in his **left** **leg**, an old injury from his years in a local football **league**, but he refuses to **let** it slow him down.\n\nHis job is to help older people **learn** to use computers. Many of them have spent their whole **life** without a keyboard, and some are afraid to touch one at all. My grandfather is patient. He tells them to **listen** carefully, take notes and never be ashamed to ask the same question twice. \"Every mistake is a **lesson**,\" he says.\n\nHe also runs a reading corner for children. He likes to **lend** them books from his own collection and asks each child to write him a short **letter** about the story. His favourite reply came from a girl of seven, who wrote: \"I **like** this book because it has a **little** dog in it and the dog never tells a **lie**.\"\n\nWhen the library closes, he does not **leave** straight away. He switches off every **light** himself, collects the **leftover** biscuits from the staff room and carries them home in a paper bag. Sometimes he stops at a café on the way for a glass of **lemonade**. He says he wants to **live** a **long** time, if only to find out what the children will become."},"31":{"w":["look","lot","loudly","love","lovely","low","lunch","machine","madame","mail","mailbox","make","man","manager","many","map","march","market","match","math"],"title":"Madame Rose's Café","t":"In a quiet street near the old **market** there is a tiny café run by an elderly Frenchwoman whom everyone calls **Madame** Rose. She came to our city forty years ago, fell in **love** with a local **man** and never left. Her café serves only one dish at **lunch**, chosen each morning according to whatever **looks|look** freshest at the market.\n\nBefore she opened the café, Madame Rose worked as a **manager** in a textile factory, where she taught herself **math** by doing the accounts at night. She still keeps her books by hand and refuses to use a computer or even a coffee **machine**. \"A **lot** of people think old methods are slow,\" she says, \"but they help me **make** fewer mistakes.\"\n\nThe walls are covered with a large **map** of France and postcards sent by former customers from **many** countries. Every few days she walks to the **mailbox** at the corner to collect her **mail**, then reads each card **loudly** to whoever happens to be sitting nearby. In **March**, when her birthday comes around, the postcards arrive by the dozen.\n\nSome days business is **low**, and she admits that she sometimes feels tired. But then a regular customer arrives with flowers, or two strangers at the same table strike up a conversation and discover that they are a perfect **match** for a game of chess. On such days, she says, life is simply **lovely**."},"32":{"w":["matter","may","maybe","me","meal","mean","meaning","meat","medicine","meet","meeting","member","memorize","mend","mentally","mention","menu","messy","middle","milk"],"title":"Grandmother Stays Home","t":"When my grandmother turned eighty last **May**, the family held a **meeting** to decide whether she should move into a care home. I was the youngest **member** of the family at the table, and I remember feeling that nobody had asked her what she wanted.\n\nSo the next weekend I visited her alone. Her kitchen was as **messy** as ever, with jars of herbs on every shelf and a basket of socks waiting to be **mended|mend**. She put a glass of warm **milk** in front of **me** and began to cook, as she always did, without looking at a **menu** or a recipe book. \"People think old age is about forgetting,\" she said, chopping **meat** for dumplings. \"It isn't. I can still **memorize** a phone number faster than you.\"\n\nShe was right, of course. Physically she was slowing down, but **mentally** she was as sharp as ever. She told me that the doctors wanted her to take more **medicine**, and that she did not object, but that she would not give up the simple pleasure of cooking a **meal** for her family. \"If you **mean** to help me,\" she said, \"help me stay here.\"\n\nI asked her what home really meant to her. Its **meaning**, she explained, lay not in the house itself but in the daily routine: going to the market, chatting with neighbours, sitting in the **middle** of the courtyard on summer evenings. \"**Maybe** that sounds foolish,\" she added, \"but it is what keeps me going.\"\n\nWhen the family met again, I made sure to **mention** everything she had told me. It did not **matter** that I was the youngest; they listened. In the end we arranged for a nurse to visit twice a week, and my grandmother still lives in her own home. Whenever I **meet** her now, she reminds me that I am always welcome for dinner."},"33":{"w":["million","mind","mine","mineral","minibus","miniskirt","minute","miss","mistake","mistaken","mm","modem","modern","mom","moment","monday","money","month","moon","more"],"title":"The Mining Town","t":"When I was ten, my **mom** took a job as an engineer with a **mineral** company, and we moved to a small town built around an old copper **mine**. On our first **Monday** there, she drove me to school in the company **minibus**, pointing out the huge machines and the hills of grey rock along the way.\n\nAt first I hated it. The town felt a **million** miles from the city, and the internet depended on an ancient **modem** that made strange noises every time it connected. I often thought I had made a terrible **mistake** in agreeing to come. My mom told me I was **mistaken**. \"Give it a **month**,\" she said. \"You might change your **mind**.\"\n\nShe was right. Within weeks I had friends, and I discovered that the town was more **modern** than I had imagined. There was a new library, a swimming pool and a music teacher in a bright **miniskirt** who taught us to play the guitar. My mother, meanwhile, worked long hours, measuring rock samples to the nearest **mm** and saving every spare piece of **money** for my education.\n\nI still remember the **moment** I knew I belonged there. One clear night, a friend and I climbed onto the roof of her house to watch the full **moon** rise over the mine. \"I'd **miss** this if I ever left,\" she said. I did not reply, but I felt exactly the same. A single **minute** like that, I learned, can be worth **more** than any city."},"34":{"w":["morning","most","mother","motherland","motivation","motorbike","motorcycle","motto","mountain","moustache","mouth","move","much","music","must","my","myself","name","nationwide","near"],"title":"My Father's Songs","t":"Every **morning** for thirty years, my father rode an old **motorbike** to the factory where he worked. He was a quiet man with a thick **moustache**, and he rarely said **much** at breakfast. His **motto**, written on a card above his desk, was simple: \"Do what you **must**, then do what you love.\"\n\nWhat he loved **most** was **music**. When he retired, he surprised us all by announcing that he wanted to travel across the **motherland** on a **motorcycle** and record folk songs in every province. My **mother** thought he was joking. \"At sixty-five?\" she said, her **mouth** falling open. But his **motivation** was stronger than her doubts.\n\nHe spent two years on the road. He crossed **mountain** passes in the west, slept in village guesthouses and recorded hundreds of songs, many of which had never been written down. When local radio stations heard about his project, they launched a **nationwide** campaign asking listeners to send him the **name** of any traditional singer living **near** them.\n\nToday his recordings are kept in a university archive, and students study them every year. I sometimes listen to them **myself** late at night. Each song reminds me that it is never too late to **move** in a new direction, and that **my** father's quiet life was richer than any of us knew."},"35":{"w":["nearly","necessary","necktie","need","neither","never","new","news","newspaper","next","nice","night","nine","ninety","ninth","no","nobody","noise","noisily","none"],"title":"Tomorrow's Paper","t":"Mr. Lu is **ninety** years old, and for **nearly** seventy of those years he has read a **newspaper** every morning. He still wears a **necktie** to breakfast, even when **nobody** else is at home, because he believes that reading the **news** is a serious business.\n\nHis granddaughter once tried to convince him that a printed paper was no longer **necessary**. \"You can read everything on your phone,\" she said. \"It's **new** every minute.\" Mr. Lu smiled politely and said **no**. He does not dislike technology, he explained; he simply prefers the **noise** of turning pages to the silence of a screen.\n\nThese days his eyes are weaker, so he reads with a magnifying glass. He **never** skips the local section, and he cuts out articles he finds interesting and pastes them into thick notebooks. He has filled **nine** of them in the past year alone. **Neither** his children nor his grandchildren have the patience to read them all, but **none** of them would dream of throwing one away.\n\nLast week, on the **night** before his birthday, the family threw a surprise party. The children ran **noisily** around the living room while the adults sang. It was the **ninth** surprise party they had held for him, and he still pretended to be astonished. When his granddaughter asked what he **needed|need**, he thought for a long moment. \"A **nice** cup of tea,\" he said, \"and tomorrow's paper. Whatever happens **next**, I want to read about it.\""},"36":{"w":["noodle","noon","nor","north","nose","not","note","nothing","novelist","november","now","numb","number","nurse","nursing","october","of","off","office","offshore"],"title":"The Night Nurse","t":"Every year, from **October** to **November**, cold winds blow down from the **north** and the small hospital in our coastal town gets busier. My aunt has worked there as a **nurse** for twenty years. She starts her shift at **noon** and often does **not** get home until long after midnight.\n\nMany of her patients are fishermen who work on **offshore** boats. They arrive with hands so cold that they are completely **numb**, or with injuries they have ignored for days. My aunt treats them with patience, but she is also strict. \"**Nothing** is more dangerous than pretending you are fine,\" she tells them, tapping a finger on the end of their **nose**.\n\nIn the **office** behind the ward, she keeps a notebook in which she writes a short **note** about every patient she has cared for. The **number** of entries is now well over ten thousand. A local **novelist** once asked to read it, hoping to find material for a book about **nursing**, but my aunt refused; the stories, she said, belong to the patients.\n\nWhen she finally takes her coat **off** at the end of a long night, she usually eats a bowl of hot **noodle** soup at the stall across the road. She has neither the time **nor** the energy to cook. Yet when I ask whether she would like to retire **now**, she laughs. \"Not yet,\" she says. \"So many **of** them still need someone to look after them.\""},"37":{"w":["often","oh","oilfield","old","omelette","on","once","one","only","open","opener","or","orange","order","other","ouch","our","ours","ourselves","out"],"title":"Omelettes in the Desert","t":"My uncle **once** spent ten years as a cook on an **oilfield** in the desert. When I was a child, he **often** told me stories about that time, and the one I remember best is about an **omelette**.\n\nThe camp was a long way from anywhere, and supplies arrived **only** every two weeks, so he had to be creative. **One** winter the delivery truck broke down, and the workers had eaten everything except eggs, a sack of onions and a crate of **orange** juice. My uncle decided to cook the best omelettes they had ever tasted.\n\nHe stood beside the **old** stove for hours, keeping it alight with whatever scraps of wood he could find. At one point he burned his hand and cried \"**Ouch**!\" so loudly that half the camp came running. The workers laughed, then rolled up their sleeves and helped. Someone found a rusty tin **opener**, someone else fetched water, and slowly the kitchen filled with the smell of frying onions.\n\n\"**Oh**, those omelettes were nothing special,\" my uncle says now, \"but they put everyone in a good mood.\" That night, he told me, the men kept the canteen **open** late, played cards **or** sang songs, and for a few hours the hardship seemed to disappear. Nobody gave anyone an **order**; everyone simply looked after each **other**.\n\nWhen I asked him what he learned **out** there, he thought for a moment. \"That **our** problems feel smaller when we share them,\" he said. \"The food was **ours**, but the laughter belonged to everyone. We kept **ourselves** going **on** very little.\""},"38":{"w":["outgoing","outing","outside","outspoken","over","overweight","own","paddle","page","pair","palace","pancake","paper","paperwork","pardon","parent","park","parking","parrot","party"],"title":"A Day at the Summer Palace","t":"Last spring my parents organised a family **outing** to an old summer **palace** outside the city. My father is an **outgoing** man who loves crowds, while my mother is more **outspoken** and has strong opinions about everything, so the planning took longer than the trip itself.\n\nWe set off early in my father's **own** car and spent twenty minutes looking for a **parking** space near the gate. Inside, the **park** was beautiful: lakes, willow trees and long covered walkways painted with scenes from history. My younger brother, who is a little **overweight** and hates walking, complained until we rented a small boat and he was allowed to **paddle** it **over** the lake himself.\n\nAt lunchtime we sat **outside** a café and ate **pancake** rolls filled with roast duck. A **parrot** in a cage by the door kept repeating \"Hello! Hello!\" until my mother finally said, \"**Pardon**?\" and everyone burst out laughing. My brother bought a **pair** of cheap sunglasses and refused to take them off for the rest of the day.\n\nEvery **parent**, I think, wants their children to remember days like this. My mother, who spends most of the week buried in **paperwork** at the tax office, carried a small notebook and wrote a few lines on a new **page** whenever we stopped. Later she printed some photos on glossy **paper** and glued them inside. She calls it the best **party** she never had to plan."},"39":{"w":["pass","passage","passport","past","pay","pen","pence","pencil","people","per","perfect","performer","person","personally","photo","photographer","pianist","piano","pick","picture"],"title":"The Lost Passport","t":"The young **pianist** stood at the airport counter, searching every pocket of her coat. Her **passport** had vanished. In three hours she was supposed to **pass** through security and board a flight to Vienna, where she would play the **piano** in her first international competition.\n\nA **photographer** travelling with her team tried to help. He went through his own bags, then checked every **photo** he had taken that morning, hoping to spot where she might have left it. In one **picture**, taken at a café, the passport was clearly visible on the table beside a **pen** and a **pencil**.\n\nThey rushed back. The café owner had kept it safely behind the counter and refused to accept any money. \"You don't need to **pay** me,\" he said. \"Just play a **perfect** concert.\" He even gave her a handful of British coins, a few **pence** left behind by a tourist, \"for luck\".\n\nAfter the competition, in which she came second, she **personally** thanked him in a long letter. She wrote that she would never forget the kindness of a stranger who expected nothing in return. As a **performer**, she said, she had always thought of the audience as a crowd; now she saw each **person** in it as someone with a story of their own.\n\nIn the **past** few years she has played in many countries, and she often tells this story to young musicians. **People** remember kindness, she says, far longer than any performance. Before each concert she reads a short **passage** from her diary about that day. Once a year she also gives free concerts for travellers at the airport, two hours **per** day for a week, and lets the audience **pick** the programme."},"40":{"w":["piece","pioneer","pity","place","plan","plane","plant","plate","play","playmate","playroom","please","pleased","pleasure","policeman","polite","poor","popcorn","pork","possible"],"title":"The Playroom","t":"When my nephew was six, he had a **playmate** named Tom who lived across the street. Tom's family was **poor**, and his parents worked long hours at a factory that processed **pork**. Tom often came over after school, and my sister turned the spare bedroom into a **playroom** where both boys could build forts out of cardboard and blankets.\n\nMy sister made one rule: everyone had to be **polite**. You said \"**please**\" when you asked for a toy and \"thank you\" when you got it. The boys took the rule seriously. Once, when Tom accidentally broke a **plate** while carrying **popcorn** to the playroom, he apologised so earnestly that she could not help laughing. \"What a **pity**,\" she said, \"but it's only a **piece** of china.\"\n\nThe boys loved to **play** at being explorers. One week they **planned|plan** an expedition to the North Pole; the next, they built a cardboard **plane** and flew it to the moon. Tom said he wanted to be a **pioneer** like the astronauts he had read about. My nephew wanted to be a **policeman**, mostly because of the uniform.\n\nYears later, Tom became an engineer at a solar power **plant**. When he visited us last summer, he told my sister that the playroom had been the happiest **place** of his childhood. She was deeply **pleased**. \"Having you here was a real **pleasure**,\" she said, \"and I always knew you would do everything **possible** and a little more.\""},"41":{"w":["post","postbox","postcard","postcode","potato","practice","present","preview","price","printing","problem","pub","publicly","pull","punctuate","punctuation","pupil","push","put","puzzled"],"title":"Sixty Years of Postcards","t":"My grandfather has collected postcards for more than sixty years. His first **postcard**, sent by a cousin from Shanghai in 1962, still sits in a wooden frame above his desk, and his collection now fills twelve albums. Each time a new one arrives, he reads the message twice, checks the **postcode** and the stamp, and slips it carefully into a plastic sleeve.\n\nWhen I was a **pupil** at primary school, he gave me an unusual **present** for my birthday: a stack of blank cards and a promise. \"If you send me one every month,\" he said, \"I will answer every single one.\" I was **puzzled** at first, but it soon became a game. Every few weeks I walked to the red **postbox** at the corner and dropped in a card about school, my friends or a **problem** I could not solve.\n\nHis replies were always worth waiting for. He wrote in neat handwriting and liked to **punctuate** his sentences with tiny drawings. He taught me that **punctuation** matters as much as spelling, and he would gently **push** me to correct my mistakes. Once he even **put** a little **potato** stamp, carved by hand, on a card just to make me laugh.\n\nToday he rarely goes to the **post** office himself, so I go for him. Sometimes we meet at the little **pub** near his flat, where he reads my cards aloud, almost **publicly**, to the regulars. Stamps, he complains, now cost twice the **price** they used to, and **printing** has become so cheap that people forget the value of a handwritten line. But **practice**, he insists, keeps the habit alive. Last week he showed me a **preview** of his newest album and asked me to **pull** out my favourite card. I chose the very first one I ever sent him."},"42":{"w":["pyramid","quake","quarter","question","quick","quiet","quite","radio","railway","rain","raincoat","rainfall","raise","read","ready","really","reason","rebuild","receipt","receive"],"title":"After the Quake","t":"When a strong **quake** shook the mountain town where my cousin teaches, the **railway** line was cut and the only news came from an old **radio** in the school office. My cousin, who had been preparing a lesson about the builders of the Egyptian **pyramid**, suddenly found herself running a shelter for two hundred people.\n\nThe first night was the hardest. Heavy **rain** fell for hours, and the weather service reported the highest **rainfall** in a decade. Families huddled under plastic sheets, and my cousin gave her own **raincoat** to an elderly man who was shivering. Nobody slept; the only sounds were the rain on the roof and the **quiet** voices of parents comforting their children.\n\nHelp arrived **quickly|quick** once the roads reopened. Within a **quarter** of an hour the next afternoon, trucks of food and water appeared, and the volunteers were **ready** to work around the clock. Each family signed a **receipt** when they **received|receive** supplies, so that nothing went missing.\n\nThe children kept asking the same **question**: would the school be able to **rebuild** in time for the new term? My cousin did not know, but she found a **reason** to stay hopeful. Every evening she gathered the pupils in a circle to **read** stories aloud, and slowly their fear began to fade. \"We cannot stop earthquakes,\" she told them, \"but we can **raise** our voices together and help each other.\"\n\nMonths later, the school did reopen. My cousin admits that she was **quite** frightened during those first days, but she says that was when she **really** learned what a community means."},"43":{"w":["receptionist","red","redirect","regards","remember","report","rest","restaurant","return","reuse","reviewer","rewind","rewrite","rhyme","rice","rich","ride","right","ring","river"],"title":"The Restaurant by the River","t":"Last autumn a food **reviewer** from a national magazine visited a small **restaurant** by the **river** in our town. The owner, Mrs. Fang, had no idea who he was. The young **receptionist** greeted him with a smile, showed him to a table by the window and brought him a cup of roasted **rice** tea.\n\nThe menu was short and written by hand on a board painted **red**. Mrs. Fang believes in simple cooking: fresh fish from the river, vegetables from local farms and sauces that are **rich** but never heavy. She also tries to **reuse** everything she can, from glass jars to leftover bread, and she proudly told her guest that her kitchen produces almost no waste.\n\nThe reviewer later wrote a glowing **report**. He described the meal as \"a poem with every **rhyme** in the **right** place\" and promised to **return** as soon as he could. When the article appeared, the phone began to **ring** all day. So many people called that Mrs. Fang had to **redirect** calls to her daughter's mobile phone.\n\nShe told me that she had to **rewrite** her whole business plan to cope with the crowds, and some evenings she wishes she could **rewind** time to the quiet days before the review. But she will always **remember** the reviewer's kindness. Every year he sends a card with warm **regards**, and every summer he takes a long bicycle **ride** along the river and stops at her restaurant to **rest**."},"44":{"w":["road","room","round","rugby","run","running","sad","sailing","salesgirl","saleswoman","salty","same","saturday","say","saying","school","schoolbag","schoolmate","science","sea"],"title":"Saturdays by the Sea","t":"Every **Saturday** morning, my old **schoolmate** Jake and I take the coastal **road** to a small fishing village by the **sea**. We have done the **same** thing for nearly ten years, ever since we left **school**, and the trip has become a kind of ritual.\n\nJake teaches **science** at a secondary school, but at weekends he forgets about exams and spends hours **sailing** a little boat he built himself. I prefer **running** along the beach while he is out on the water. When we were younger, we both played **rugby** for the school team; now our knees remind us that those days are over.\n\nOur first stop is always the same shop. The **salesgirl**, a cheerful teenager, sells us coffee and **salty** fish cakes, while her mother, an experienced **saleswoman**, tries to persuade us to buy souvenirs we do not need. \"As the **saying** goes,\" she tells us every week, \"a day by the sea is never wasted.\"\n\nLast month, after Jake's father passed away, I worried that the trip would feel **sad** without its usual laughter. We drove in silence, and Jake did not **say** much. But at the harbour he pulled an old **schoolbag** out of the car. Inside were photos of his father teaching him to sail. We sat in the **room** above the café, looked at every picture and passed them **round** the table. Then Jake stood up and said, \"Come on, let's **run** to the lighthouse, just like he used to.\""},"45":{"w":["seagull","seashell","season","seat","seaweed","second","secondhand","secretary","see","seem","sell","semicircle","send","september","serve","service","set","settler","seven","seventh"],"title":"The Bookshop by the Harbour","t":"In **September**, when the tourist **season** ends, the seaside town where my aunt lives becomes quiet again. She runs a small **secondhand** bookshop near the harbour, and autumn is her favourite time of year. \"This is when the real readers come,\" she says.\n\nThe building is more than a century old; it was put up by a **settler** from the south who arrived with nothing but a boat and a box of books. Its front window is shaped like a **semicircle**, and on windy days you can **see** waves breaking against the rocks below. A **seagull** often lands on the windowsill, as if it too were curious about the books.\n\nMy aunt does not just **sell** books. She offers a free reading **service** to old people who can no longer see well, and twice a week she will **serve** them tea while she reads aloud. Her best friend, a retired **secretary** from the town hall, helps her keep the accounts and **send** books by post to customers in other cities.\n\nThe shop is full of small treasures: a basket of **seashell** necklaces made by local children, a jar of dried **seaweed** snacks and a comfortable **seat** in the corner where you can read for hours. Every **seventh** customer receives a free bookmark, a tradition she **set** up in her very first week.\n\nBusiness does not always **seem** good; some months she sells only a few dozen books. But on the **second** floor, where she lives, hangs a photo of the day she reopened the shop, **seven** years ago. Looking at it, she says, reminds her why she started."},"46":{"w":["several","shall","sharpener","shaver","she","sheep","shine","ship","shirt","shoe","shooting","shop","short","shorts","should","shout","show","sick","side","sideway"],"title":"The Film Crew in the Village","t":"Last summer a film crew arrived in my grandparents' village for the **shooting** of a historical drama. For **several** weeks the quiet streets were full of cameras, lights and actors in costume. The director, a tall woman with a loud voice, would **shout** \"Action!\" so often that even the village children began to copy her.\n\nMy grandmother, who has kept **sheep** for forty years, was asked to appear in one scene. She had to lead her flock along a narrow **sideway** behind the temple while the leading actor rode past on a horse. She was so nervous that she felt **sick** the night before. \"What **shall** I wear?\" she kept asking. In the end the crew gave her an old cotton **shirt** and a pair of straw sandals instead of her usual rubber **shoes|shoe**.\n\nThe village **shop** did more business that month than in the whole previous year. The owner even started selling pencil **sharpener** sets and electric **shaver** kits to crew members who had forgotten their own. My cousin worked as a runner, carrying lunch boxes from one **side** of the set to the other in **shorts** and a cap.\n\nWhen the film was finally released, the whole village gathered to watch it. My grandmother's scene was very **short**, barely ten seconds, but **she** was delighted. \"You **should** have seen her face,\" my grandfather told me later. The sun seemed to **shine** a little brighter that day. She says that next time a film crew comes, she wants a bigger part, perhaps on a **ship**, and that the film will **show** the world how beautiful our village is."},"47":{"w":["silk","similar","simple","simply","since","sincerely","sing","single","sir","sister","sit","six","sixteen","sixteenth","sixth","size","skateboard","skillfully","skirt","sky"],"title":"The Silk Skirt","t":"My **sister** turned **sixteen** last spring, and for her **sixteenth** birthday our grandmother made her a **skirt** of blue **silk**. Grandmother has been sewing **since** she was a girl, and she works so **skillfully** that her stitches are almost invisible.\n\nThe design was **simple**: no lace, no buttons, just one piece of fabric that moved like water. Grandmother spent **six** evenings on it, measuring my sister's **size** again and again, because she believes a skirt that does not fit is worse than no skirt at all. When my sister tried it on, she said it was the colour of the summer **sky**.\n\nMy sister is not usually interested in clothes. She would rather spend her weekends on a **skateboard** in the park with her friends. But she wore the skirt to her birthday dinner and did not take it off all evening. At one point she stood up and began to **sing** an old folk song that Grandmother had taught her, while the rest of us tried to **sit** still and not cry.\n\nAt the end of the night, my sister thanked our grandmother **sincerely**. \"I don't need anything else,\" she said. A neighbour bowed to her like a knight in an old film and said, \"At your service, **sir**,\" then realised his mistake, and everyone laughed.\n\nIt was the **sixth** skirt Grandmother had made for a grandchild, and each one has been **similar** in shape but different in colour. She says she will make one for every **single** granddaughter, because it is **simply** the best way she knows to say \"I love you\"."},"48":{"w":["sleep","slow","small","smell","smelly","smile","smoke","smoker","smoking","sneaker","snow","snowball","snowman","so","socket","softball","soldier","some","somebody","someone"],"title":"Grandfather Quits","t":"For thirty years my grandfather was a heavy **smoker**. He started **smoking** as a young **soldier**, when cigarettes were handed out to keep the men awake during long night watches, and he never stopped. The **smell** of tobacco filled every corner of his **small** flat, and his old jackets were so **smelly** that my grandmother refused to wash them with anything else.\n\nThen one winter he caught a bad cough that would not go away. The doctor told him plainly that if he did not stop, **someone** else would have to look after him within a few years. He went home in silence, put his last packet in a drawer and never opened it again.\n\nThe first weeks were hard. He could not **sleep**, and he became irritable over the smallest things. **Somebody** suggested he take up a hobby to keep his hands busy, so he joined a **softball** team for retired people. He was **slow** at first, but he enjoyed it **so** much that he soon forgot about cigarettes for hours at a time. He even bought a pair of bright white **sneakers|sneaker**.\n\nThat winter, when the first heavy **snow** fell, he went outside with my little cousins and built a **snowman** taller than any of them. They threw a **snowball** at him, and he laughed so loudly that the neighbours looked out of their windows. Watching from the doorway, my grandmother could not help **smiling|smile**. \"The only **smoke** in this house now,\" she said, \"comes from the kitchen.\"\n\nThese days he plugs his radio into the old **socket** in the hallway and listens to it on his morning walks. He tells **some** of his old friends that quitting was the hardest battle he ever fought, and the best."},"49":{"w":["something","sometimes","somewhere","son","song","soon","sorry","sound","south","spare","speak","spend","spoken","spoonful","sport","spring","squid","stairs","stamp","stand"],"title":"Letters from the South","t":"My neighbour Mrs. Tan has a **son** who moved to the **south** of the country for work several years ago. He rarely has time to visit, but every **spring** he sends her a letter by post, with a colourful **stamp** in the corner and a small gift inside.\n\nMrs. Tan keeps the letters in a box at the top of the **stairs**. **Sometimes**, when I help her carry her shopping, she invites me in and reads one aloud. Her son writes about his job at a seafood company, about the **squid** and fish that arrive at the harbour every morning, and about the **sport** he plays with colleagues after work. He always ends with the same line: \"I'm **sorry** I can't be there.\"\n\nHis **spoken** Chinese, she says with a laugh, now carries a strong southern accent, but his written words have never changed. When he calls, they rarely **speak** for more than a few minutes; letters, she believes, let people say **something** they would never say aloud.\n\nOn evenings when she feels lonely, she makes a cup of tea, stirs in a **spoonful** of honey and listens to an old **song** on the radio. She does not like to **spend** money on herself, but she always keeps a **spare** room ready, just in case her son decides to come home **soon**. \"He is **somewhere** out there working hard,\" she said. \"That's enough for me.\" She cannot **stand** the idea of being a burden to him.\n\nLast month the letter contained a surprise: a train ticket and a note that read, \"Come and see the sea.\" When she showed it to me, the **sound** of her laughter filled the whole flat."},"50":{"w":["star","start","station","stay","still","stomachache","stop","stopwatch","store","story","straight","strange","street","strong","student","study","subject","subtraction","such","suffering"],"title":"A Conversation at the Station","t":"Late one winter evening, a university **student** named Kai missed the last bus and had to walk to the railway **station** along a dark **street**. He was carrying a heavy bag of books, and he had been **suffering** from a terrible **stomachache** all afternoon. All he wanted was to get home.\n\nAt the station, a **strange** thing happened. An old man sitting on a bench asked him the time, then looked at Kai's books and asked what **subject** he was taking. \"Maths,\" Kai said. The old man smiled and pulled a battered **stopwatch** from his pocket. \"I taught maths for forty years,\" he said. \"Do you know what my pupils found hardest? Not calculus, but **subtraction**. People hate to take things away.\"\n\nThey talked for an hour while they waited for the delayed train. The old man told Kai the **story** of his own student days, when he had to **study** by candlelight and walk ten kilometres to school. He spoke in a calm, **strong** voice, looking **straight** at Kai as if every word mattered, and Kai forgot all about his stomach.\n\nWhen the train finally arrived, the old man did not get on. He said he would **stay** a little longer to watch the **stars|star**. Kai asked whether he was **still** teaching. \"In a way,\" the old man laughed. \"Every conversation like this is a lesson.\"\n\nKai never saw him again, but he often thinks of that night. He has since decided to **start** a free tutoring club at his university, and he keeps a small **store** of chalk and notebooks for pupils who cannot afford them. Whenever he feels like giving up, he remembers that **such** kindness can **stop** a bad day from turning into a bad life."},"51":{"w":["sugar","suggest","suggestion","suite","summer","sun","sunburnt","sunday","sunglasses","sunny","superman","supermarket","supper","sure","surface","surprise","surrounding","swap","sweet","swim"],"title":"The Supermarket Swap","t":"Last **summer** my family rented a holiday **suite** in a small coastal town. The weather was **sunny** every day, and by the end of the first week my little brother was so **sunburnt** that he had to wear a T-shirt even when he went to **swim**.\n\nOn **Sunday** morning we walked to the local **supermarket** to buy food for a barbecue. At the checkout we met an elderly couple who were staying in the room next to ours. They had bought far too much **sugar** and not nearly enough bread, while we had the opposite problem. My mother laughed and made a **suggestion**: why not **swap**? The couple agreed at once, and by the time we left the shop we had become friends.\n\nThat evening they joined us for **supper** on the balcony. The old man told us that he used to work as a diver and had spent half his life beneath the **surface** of the sea. He described the **surrounding** reefs in such detail that my brother, who believes he is a kind of **superman**, wanted to start diving lessons the next day. \"Are you **sure**?\" my father asked. \"You can hardly stand the **sun**.\"\n\nOn our last day the couple gave us a **surprise**: a jar of **sweet** homemade jam and a pair of old **sunglasses** for my brother, \"for when you become a diver\". \"I **suggest** you keep them,\" the old man told him with a wink, \"because the brightest light is sometimes found underwater.\""},"52":{"w":["swimming","table","take","talk","tall","tasteless","tasty","taxi","tea","teach","teacher","teamwork","teapot","telephone","television","tell","ten","tennis","tenth","text"],"title":"The Tennis Teacher","t":"My aunt has been a **teacher** at a primary school for twenty years, but her real passion is **tennis**. Every weekend she coaches a group of children at the community courts, and she says she learns as much from them as they learn from her.\n\nShe believes that sport can **teach** lessons no textbook can. The children practise **teamwork** in doubles matches, and they learn to **take** defeat calmly. After each session they sit around a folding **table** in the clubhouse, drinking **tea** from a big old **teapot** and **talking|talk** about what went well.\n\nOne of her pupils, a **tall** girl of **ten**, was too shy to speak during the first few weeks. My aunt never pushed her. Instead she would **tell** short stories about her own mistakes as a young player, and slowly the girl began to open up. By the **tenth** lesson she was laughing with the others and asking for extra practice.\n\nMy aunt is old-fashioned in some ways. She refuses to **text** parents and prefers to **telephone** them instead, because she thinks a voice carries more warmth than a message. She rarely watches **television**, saying she would rather be outside. On rainy days, when the courts are closed, she takes the children **swimming** at the indoor pool, and afterwards they share snacks that range from **tasty** to completely **tasteless**, since she lets the children choose.\n\nLast spring the club won a regional junior trophy. When a reporter asked my aunt for her secret, she simply called a **taxi** to take the whole team out for noodles. \"That,\" she said, \"is the secret.\""},"53":{"w":["than","thank","thankful","that","the","their","theirs","them","themselves","then","there","thermos","these","they","thin","thing","think","thinking","third","thirsty"],"title":"Lost in the Mountains","t":"On the **third** day of their hiking trip, my parents and **their** friends found **themselves** lost in the mountains. **They** had left the main trail to look for a waterfall, and by the afternoon **the** path had disappeared completely. Everyone was tired and **thirsty**, and the only drink left was a **thermos** of lukewarm tea.\n\nMy father, who is usually calm, admitted later that he was frightened. \"I kept **thinking** about the children at home,\" he told me. But my mother insisted that panic would not help. \"Let's **think** clearly,\" she said. \"We came up this valley, so the river must be down **there**.\"\n\nThey followed the stream for two hours. The ground was steep, the air was **thin**, and **these** were hardly ideal conditions for people in their fifties. Yet nobody complained. When they finally reached a small farmhouse, an old farmer welcomed **them** inside. He gave them water and bread and refused to take any money. The **thing** my mother remembers most is that he said **that** helping strangers was simply what people did in the mountains. **Then** he drove them back to their car on his tractor.\n\nMy parents still talk about that day. They say the tea from the thermos tasted better **than** any drink they have ever had, and they will always be **thankful** to the farmer. Every New Year they send him a gift and **thank** him again, and he replies with a bag of walnuts. Whenever my mother cracks one open, she says the walnuts are a reminder that the best part of the journey was **theirs** to share."},"54":{"w":["thirteen","thirty","this","those","though","thousand","three","thriller","through","throw","thunderstorm","thursday","ticket","till","time","tired","to","today","together","tomorrow"],"title":"The Thursday Storm","t":"Last **Thursday** began like any other day. I had bought a cinema **ticket** for a new **thriller** that evening and planned to go straight from work. But at about **three** o'clock the sky turned black, and **those** of us near the office windows saw lightning flash across the city.\n\nThe **thunderstorm** lasted for nearly **thirty** minutes. Rain poured **through** the gaps in the old roof, and someone had to **throw** towels on the floor to stop people slipping. The building has **thirteen** floors, and the power went off on every one of them. Hundreds of computers shut down at the same **time**, and more than a **thousand** unsaved documents were lost.\n\n**Though** everyone was **tired** and frustrated, the mood soon changed. Without screens to stare at, people began talking to colleagues they had sat beside for years without really knowing them. Someone found candles; someone else made tea on a camping stove. We sat **together** in the dark and told stories **till** the lights came back.\n\nI never made it **to** the cinema, but I don't regret it. **Today** my colleagues and I still laugh about that afternoon, and we have agreed to switch off our computers for one hour **this** Friday, just to talk. Who knows? **Tomorrow** might bring another storm."},"55":{"w":["tonight","too","tooth","toothache","toothbrush","toothpaste","totally","tourism","tourist","tournament","town","train","trainer","translator","travel","traveler","tree","trip","trouble","true"],"title":"The Translator's Travels","t":"**Tonight** my cousin Ling is flying home after a three-month **trip** abroad. She works as a **translator** for a **tourism** company, and her job is to **travel** with groups of Chinese tourists, explaining menus, museum labels and local customs.\n\nIt sounds like a dream job, but she says it is not **totally** glamorous. Every **tourist** has a different problem. One man lost his **toothbrush** and **toothpaste** on the first night and refused to buy new ones, insisting that the hotel should provide them. Another woman broke a **tooth** on a hard bread roll and suffered from a terrible **toothache** for days before Ling found a dentist willing to see her.\n\nStill, she loves the work. She has seen famous cities and tiny villages, and she once watched a tennis **tournament** from the front row because a client had a spare ticket. In one small **town** in the mountains, she met an elderly **traveler** who had walked across three countries with nothing but a backpack. He told her that the best way to see a place is to take the slowest **train** you can find.\n\nHer biggest **trouble**, she admits, is homesickness. She keeps a photo of the old **tree** outside our grandmother's house in her wallet. \"It's **true**,\" she wrote in her last message. \"You can go anywhere in the world, but home is still the place you dream about.\" She joked that her fitness **trainer** will be shocked by how much she has eaten. When I said I envied her, she replied, \"Come with me next time. You'll be **too** busy to complain.\""},"56":{"w":["try","tuesday","turn","tv","twelfth","twelve","twentieth","twenty","twice","two","umbrella","unbelievable","uncle","unconditional","under","underground","understand","undivided","unhealthy","unimportant"],"title":"Smartphones on Tuesday Nights","t":"Every **Tuesday** evening, my **uncle** goes to a small community centre to **try** to teach older people how to use smartphones. He started the class **twelve** years ago, after his own father found modern technology completely **unbelievable**. \"He could **understand** a **TV**,\" my uncle says, \"but a phone with no buttons was a mystery.\"\n\nThe class meets in a room **under** the library, which can only be reached by an old staircase from the **underground** car park. On rainy nights my uncle stands at the entrance with a big black **umbrella**, waiting to walk each student down. His patience is **unconditional**; he will explain the same thing **twice**, three times, or as often as needed.\n\nSome of his students have **unhealthy** habits that he gently tries to change, such as staying up too late reading news on their phones. Others worry that their questions are **unimportant**. He tells them that no question is foolish, and he gives each person his **undivided** attention.\n\nLast month the centre held a party to celebrate the **twentieth** anniversary of the library. My uncle's class was the **twelfth** group to perform, and they surprised everyone with a video they had filmed and edited themselves. It lasted **twenty** minutes and showed **two** of the oldest students, both over eighty, video-calling their grandchildren abroad.\n\nWhen the video ended, the audience applauded for a long time. My uncle simply smiled. Then he **turned|turn** to his students and said, \"Same time next Tuesday?\""},"57":{"w":["unmarried","unrest","unsafe","unsuccessful","until","untrue","up","us","use","usually","vanilla","veal","vegetable","very","videophone","viewer","village","villager","violinist","visit"],"title":"The Violinist Comes Home","t":"When the young **violinist** Lin Xia decided to **visit** her home **village** after ten years abroad, few people recognised her. She had left as a shy, **unmarried** girl of eighteen, determined to study music in Vienna, and many neighbours had assumed she would never come back.\n\nHer first concert in Europe was **unsuccessful**, and for a while she wondered whether the gossip at home was **untrue**: perhaps she really did not have enough talent. But she kept practising **until** she was invited to join a respected orchestra. Her family **usually** watched her performances on a **videophone** her brother had set up, and every time she bowed, they cheered in front of the screen.\n\nWhen she arrived, the **villagers|villager** had prepared a feast: roasted **veal**, fresh **vegetable** dishes and a cake flavoured with **vanilla**, her favourite since childhood. In the evening she played in the courtyard of the old school, which had been declared **unsafe** years earlier and had just been repaired. More than two hundred people sat on benches and on the ground.\n\nThe region had been through some **unrest** in recent years, as young people left for the cities and old businesses closed. That night, however, the mood was **very** different. One elderly **viewer**, watching from a wheelchair, said the music reminded him of what the village could still be. \"You came back for **us**,\" he told her. She smiled and promised to **use** every chance to return. Then she picked **up** her violin and played one more song."},"58":{"w":["wait","wake","walk","walkman","wall","want","warm","wash","washroom","watch","water","watermelon","way","wayside","we","wear","weather","weatherman","website","wednesday"],"title":"A Wednesday with the Weatherman","t":"Mr. Zhao has been the **weatherman** on our local radio station for twenty-five years, and every **Wednesday** he records a special programme about the **weather** in the countryside. Last month he invited me to come along.\n\nWe had to **wake** up at five. He insisted that we **walk** the first part of the **way**, along a muddy **wayside** path between rice fields, because \"you cannot describe a place from a car window.\" He **wears|wear** the same faded jacket on every trip and carries an old **walkman**, which he uses to record the sounds of birds and running **water**.\n\nAt a small farmhouse, an old farmer invited us in. He showed us the **wall** where he marks the height of every flood, and his wife insisted that we **wash** our hands in the tiny **washroom** before eating slices of cold **watermelon**. \"**We** don't need a **website** to know when the rain is coming,\" the farmer laughed. \"We **watch** the sky.\"\n\nMr. Zhao listened carefully, taking notes. He told me later that this is why he loves his job: people **want** to be heard, not just informed. On the drive home he was quiet for a long time. Then he said that every forecast is really a promise to **wait** with people through the storm, and to tell them when the **warm** days will return."},"59":{"w":["week","weekend","weight","welcome","well","west","western","westerner","westwards","wet","what","when","where","whether","which","while","white","who","whom","whose"],"title":"Heading West","t":"Last **week** my friend Tom, a **westerner** who has lived in China for ten years, asked me a question I could not answer: \"**Which** part of the country do you know least?\" I realised that I had never travelled to the **west**. So that **weekend** we packed our bags and drove **westwards** for two days.\n\nThe landscape changed slowly. Green fields gave way to dry hills, and the air became thinner and cooler. **While** Tom drove, I read about the history of the **western** provinces, **where** the ancient Silk Road once carried merchants and monks across the deserts.\n\nOn the third day heavy rain fell, and we arrived at a small guesthouse completely **wet**. The owner, an old woman in a **white** headscarf, gave us towels and hot soup. She asked **who** we were and **whom** we had come to see. When Tom explained that we had come simply to look around, she laughed and said we were **welcome** to stay as long as we liked.\n\nThat evening she told us about her family. Her son works in a factory in the east, and she does not know **whether** he will ever come back. \"Young people go where the jobs are,\" she said. \"**What** can we do?\" She showed us a photo of a girl **whose** smile looked exactly like hers: her granddaughter, now studying medicine.\n\n**When** we left, she insisted on giving us a bag of dried fruit, even though it added extra **weight** to our already full car. Tom said it was the best trip he had made in China, and I am now **well** aware how little I knew about my own country."},"60":{"w":["why","wide","wife","wildlife","will","willingly","willingness","wind","windbreaker","window","winter","wish","with","without","woman","woo","word","work","workday","worker"],"title":"Waiting for Snow Leopards","t":"My neighbour's **wife**, Anna, is a **wildlife** photographer. She spends most of every **winter** in a hut on the edge of a nature reserve, waiting for snow leopards. When I asked her **why** she chose such a difficult job, she said simply, \"Because I **wish** to show people what we are about to lose.\"\n\nHer husband, a quiet engineer, supports her **willingly**, although he admits that he misses her. While she is away he sends her a short **word** of encouragement every evening, and on each **workday** he checks the mountain forecast before he leaves home.\n\nAnna told me that the hardest part is not the cold **wind** but the waiting. Sometimes she sits for days by a tiny **window** in her hide, **with** a camera on her knees and **without** seeing a single animal. \"You need endless patience and a real **willingness** to fail,\" she said. Local **workers|worker** at the reserve help her carry equipment up the narrow mountain paths, and she pays them fairly for their **work**.\n\nHer photographs have now been shown around the world, and conservation groups use them to **woo** donors and governments. A **wide** range of people, from schoolchildren to scientists, write to her after seeing them. The most touching letter came from an elderly **woman** who said she had never seen a leopard but now felt she knew one.\n\nAnna will return to the mountains next month in her old red **windbreaker**. \"I **will** keep going,\" she said, \"as long as there is something left to photograph.\""},"61":{"w":["workforce","workmate","workplace","works","world","worn","worried","worry","worth","would","wounded","write","wrong","year","yellow","yes","yesterday","yet","you","young"],"title":"The Old Works","t":"In the town where I grew up, everyone called the old steel factory \"the **works**\". For three generations it employed almost the entire local **workforce**. My grandfather started there at sixteen, and my father joined him when he was still **young**.\n\n**Yesterday** I visited the site for the first time in many **years|year**. The factory closed long ago, and the buildings are now an industrial museum. Walking through the empty halls, I saw my grandfather's **worn** gloves displayed in a glass case, next to a **yellow** safety helmet with his name on it.\n\nA guide explained that the **workplace** had been dangerous. Accidents were common, and in one terrible fire in 1978, eleven men were **wounded**. My father had told me about it only once. He said his **workmate** pulled him out of the smoke, and that he would never have survived otherwise.\n\nI asked the guide if the workers had ever complained. \"Of course,\" she said. \"They **worried** about their health and their pay. But they were also proud. Many said it was the hardest job in the **world**, and the best.\"\n\nBefore I left, I signed the visitors' book. I wanted to **write** something meaningful, but I could only manage a few lines. I wrote that the factory had shaped my family, and that I hoped we had not been **wrong** to let it go. Then I added: \"**Yes**, it was **worth** it.\" My father **would** have agreed. As I drove away, the guide waved. \"Don't **worry**,\" she called out. \"**You** can come back any time. There is **yet** more to discover.\""},"62":{"w":["your","yours","yourself","yourselves","yummy","zero","zip","zipper","zoo","zoom"],"title":"A Letter to the Zoo Volunteers","t":"Dear volunteers,\n\nThank you for **your** hard work at the city **zoo** this summer. When we started the programme in May, we had **zero** experience with children's tours. Now families **zoom** across the city every weekend to join them, and many ask for you by name.\n\nSeveral of you have told me that you surprised **yourselves**: shy students became confident guides, and one of you even learned to calm a nervous penguin. I hope each of you is proud of **yourself** as well as of the team.\n\nA few practical notes before the autumn season. Please check that the **zipper** on every first-aid bag works properly; two of them broke last month. When the snack stall gets busy, help the staff **zip** the cool boxes closed so the ice lasts longer. The new honey cakes, by the way, are **yummy**, but they are for visitors, not for us!\n\nFinally, the photo album from this summer is ready, and one copy is **yours** to keep.\n\nWith warm thanks,\nMs. Chen"}},"freq.basic":{"1":{"w":["the","be","to","of","a","and","in","it","that","they","have","for","do","their","on","we","with","as","you","what"],"title":"The Words We Use Most","t":"Linguists who count words in newspapers and exams always reach **the** same conclusion: a few short words do most of **the** work in English. They may **be** small, but without them no sentence could stand.\n\nConsider a simple sentence: \"People who move **to** a new city often feel lonely at first.\" Most **of** the meaning comes from the longer words, yet it is **a** handful of tiny ones that hold the structure together, like nails **and** screws in a wooden chair. Put them **in** the wrong place, and **it** falls apart.\n\nTeachers know **that** learners often ignore these words. **They** focus on difficult vocabulary and **have** little patience **for** grammar. But what learners **do** with small words often decides **their** scores. A student who writes \"depend **on**\" correctly, or knows when to use \"a\" instead of \"the\", sounds natural; one who doesn't sounds foreign.\n\nSo what should **we** do? Read widely, and pay attention to the words that appear **with** every noun and verb. Treat them **as** old friends rather than background noise. If **you** notice how native writers use them, you will soon find that **what** once seemed difficult becomes easy."},"2":{"w":["can","more","people","from","but","by","this","about","or","not","at","one","make","say","will","when","than","other","your","our"],"title":"Why Small Choices Matter","t":"Psychologists say that **people** often underestimate the power of small daily choices. We believe that our lives are shaped **by** big decisions — where to study, whom to marry, which job to accept. **But** research suggests that habits matter just as much.\n\nThink **about** **this** example. A student who reads for twenty minutes every evening will read **more** than thirty books in a year. Another student, who waits for a free weekend, may read only two **or** three. The difference does **not** come **from** intelligence; it comes from routine.\n\nThe same is true **at** work. Employees who **make** a short plan each morning finish tasks faster **than** those who simply react to emails. \"Most people **say** they lack time,\" one researcher explains, \"**when** they really lack a system.\"\n\nOf course, no **one** can change every habit at once. Experts suggest choosing a single habit and keeping it for a month. Some prefer to start with sleep; **other** people begin with exercise or study. Whatever **your** choice, the key is consistency. As **our** grandparents used to say, a long journey begins with one step, and you **can** take that step today. Small changes **will** add up."},"3":{"w":["how","he","there","who","all","work","time","if","so","many","new","use","most","some","study","year","find","child","change","which"],"title":"Learning at Any Age","t":"**How** old is too old to learn something **new**? Many adults believe that learning belongs to childhood, but a recent **study** suggests otherwise. Researchers followed **some** two thousand adults for ten **years|year** and found that those who kept learning stayed healthier and happier.\n\nOne participant, a retired engineer named Mr. Wang, began piano lessons at sixty-eight. \"**He** practises every day,\" his wife says, \"and **there** is music in the house again.\" Another, a woman **who** had never used a computer, learned to **use** online banking and video calls to stay in touch with her grandchildren.\n\n**All** the participants agreed that learning takes effort. \"At first, I made **so** **many** mistakes,\" one woman admits. \"**If** I had worried about looking foolish, I would have given up.\" But **most** of them said that the effort was worth it. Learning gave them confidence, friends and a sense of purpose.\n\nThe researchers believe the findings could **change** how society treats older people. Instead of seeing retirement as the end of **work**, we might see it as a **time** for growth. \"Every **child** is curious,\" the lead scientist says. \"Our task is to **find** ways to keep that curiosity alive — **which** is easier than we think.\""},"4":{"w":["get","life","also","take","up","world","its","like","way","those","these","would","should","may","need","think","go","good","his","help"],"title":"A Good Life","t":"What makes a **good** **life**? Philosophers have debated this question for thousands of years, and people around the **world** still **think** about it today. Some believe happiness comes from wealth; others say it comes from love, health or meaningful work.\n\nMy grandfather had his own answer. **His** advice was simple: \"**Help** others, and you will **get** more than you give.\" He grew **up** in a poor village, and he spent **his** life working as a doctor in the countryside. He never became rich, but he was **also** never lonely. **Those** who knew him remember his kindness more than anything else.\n\nI sometimes **take** his advice when I feel lost. Modern life, with **its** constant pressure and endless choices, can make it hard to know which **way** to **go**. We **may** feel that we **need** more money, a better job or a bigger house. But **these** goals rarely bring lasting peace.\n\nPerhaps we **should** measure success differently. Instead of asking how much we own, we could ask how many people we have helped. My grandfather **would** have **liked|like** that question. \"A life is like a candle,\" he used to say. \"Its value is the light it gives.\""},"5":{"w":["out","come","see","even","food","only","into","because","no","woman","such","thing","know","give","over","her","she","show","student","my"],"title":"The Student Who Cooked","t":"When I moved **into** my first university dormitory, I did not **know** how to cook. At home, my mother had always prepared every meal, and I had **only** ever boiled an egg. **Even** making tea felt like an adventure.\n\nThings changed **because** of a **woman** named Mrs. Liu, who ran a small restaurant near the campus. One evening, she noticed me eating instant noodles for the third time that week. \"**No** **student** should live like this,\" **she** said firmly. She invited me to **come** to her kitchen on Saturday mornings and promised to **show** me a few simple dishes.\n\nI was nervous at first, but **her** patience made learning easy. She taught me that cooking is not about **such** complicated skills as chefs **see** on television. It is about care. \"The most important **thing**,\" she told me, \"is to taste your food and pay attention.\"\n\nOver the next year, I learned to make soup, fried rice and dumplings. **My** friends began to visit my room for dinner, and **food** became a way of making friends. When I graduated, I went back to thank Mrs. Liu. I wanted to **give** her a gift, but she just laughed. \"Cook for someone else,\" she said. \"That is the best way to pay me back.\" My student days are long **over**, but whenever I run **out** of ideas in the kitchen, I still hear her voice."},"6":{"w":["long","become","why","first","well","different","just","could","high","much","learn","often","between","great","better","problem","try","now","feel","job"],"title":"My First Real Job","t":"**Why** do young people find their **first** **job** so stressful? When I started work at a small design company, I thought I was well prepared. I had studied hard at university and done **well** in my exams. But I soon discovered that working life was **different** from anything I had imagined.\n\nOn my first day, my manager gave me a **problem** to solve: a client wanted a new logo by Friday. I **could** not sleep for two nights. I was afraid that my work would not be good enough and that everyone would **feel** disappointed in me. The pressure was **much** **higher|high** than at school.\n\nA senior colleague noticed how nervous I was. \"Don't **try** to be perfect,\" she told me. \"**Just** do your best and ask questions.\" It was **great** advice. She explained that mistakes are a normal part of how we **learn**, and that the gap **between** students and professionals is mostly experience.\n\nOver the next few months, I **became|become** more confident. I learned to plan my time **better**, and I **often** asked for feedback. **Now**, three years later, I lead my own small team. It has been a **long** journey, and I still remember that first frightening week. Whenever a new colleague joins us, I pass on the same advice I was given."},"7":{"w":["day","country","company","look","own","while","two","want","suggest","place","any","important","idea","after","health","through","city","man","without","american"],"title":"Moving to the City","t":"**Two** years ago, my cousin left her small village and moved to a big **city** to work for a technology **company**. Like many young people, she **wanted|want** a better life. \"In my village, there are few jobs,\" she explained. \"The city seemed like the right **place** to build my **own** future.\"\n\nThe first months were hard. Every **day** she spent hours on crowded buses, and she missed the quiet of the **country**. She had no friends and often ate dinner alone. Her **health** suffered, and she began to doubt her decision. She would **look** at photos of home and wonder whether she should go back.\n\nThen a friendly older **man** in her office gave her some advice. He **suggested|suggest** that she join a weekend hiking club. \"You can't get **through** hard times **without** friends,\" he said. It was an **important** lesson. **After** a few weekends in the mountains, she had made several friends, including an **American** engineer who was also new to the city.\n\nToday, my cousin is much happier. She has learned that **any** big change takes time, and **while** city life can be lonely, it also offers many opportunities. Her best **idea**, she says, was to stop waiting for happiness and start looking for it."},"8":{"w":["grow","where","parent","music","then","same","book","too","whether","very","something","school","both","person","around","believe","right","number","young","start"],"title":"The Music Teacher","t":"Every town has **something** special, and in our small town it was Mr. Gao, the music teacher at the primary **school**. He was a **young** man when he arrived, full of energy and ideas. He **believed|believe** that **music** could help children **grow** in confidence, **whether** or not they had any talent.\n\nAt first, some **parents|parent** were doubtful. They thought music was less important than maths or science, and they did not want their children to waste time. Mr. Gao listened politely, **then** invited them to a concert. When they heard their children sing together, **both** mothers and fathers were moved to tears. **Very** few of them complained after that.\n\nMr. Gao treated every child as a unique **person**. He gave each pupil a **book** of songs and encouraged them to write their own. Children who were shy in other classes would often **start** singing loudly in his room. A large **number** of his former students still play instruments today.\n\nHe taught at the school for thirty years. When he retired, students from **around** the country came back to thank him. Many of them said the **same** thing: he made them feel that they had a **right** to dream. \"Music doesn't care where you come from,\" he used to say. \"It only cares **where** you are going.\" And so we sang, **too**."},"9":{"w":["develop","keep","science","always","example","today","every","large","money","eat","each","hard","language","before","few","old","really","another","end","worker"],"title":"Learning a Language Through Food","t":"**Before** I moved to Italy, I studied Italian for a year but **really** understood very little. My teacher gave me **another** piece of advice: \"Learn the **language** through food.\" It sounded strange, but it turned out to be the best tip I received.\n\n**Each** morning in Florence, I went to a **large** market near my flat. The **old** woman at the vegetable stall was patient with me **every** time I visited. She would hold up a tomato or an onion and say its name slowly, and I would repeat it. Within a **few** weeks, I could buy everything I needed to cook and **eat** at home.\n\nLanguage and food are connected in many ways. Researchers in cognitive **science** say that we remember words better when they are linked to strong experiences, such as taste and smell. Cooking also helps learners **develop** practical vocabulary for **today**, not just for exams. And it saves **money**: eating at home is much cheaper than eating out!\n\nOf course, learning was **hard** at times. One **example** I'll never forget: I asked a **worker** at the bakery for \"a kiss\" instead of \"a roll.\" Everyone laughed, including me. But I **always** **kept|keep** trying. By the **end** of the year, I could talk about almost anything — as long as it was about food."},"10":{"w":["age","information","still","college","set","small","lot","themselves","understand","seem","last","pay","reason","back","development","animal","home","tell","family","put"],"title":"The Family Farm","t":"My grandparents have run the same small farm in the hills for fifty years. They grow vegetables, keep chickens and raise a few pigs. At their **age**, most people have retired, but they **still** work in the fields every morning. \"Work keeps us young,\" my grandmother likes to **tell** me.\n\nWhen I was a child, I spent every summer at their **home**. My grandfather taught me to care for each **animal** and to understand the seasons. I remember how he would **put** seeds into the soil with great care, as if each one were a treasure. \"Every plant has a **reason** to grow,\" he would say.\n\nOur **family** has changed a lot since then. Most of my cousins went to **college** in the city and now work in offices. They **seem** to have little interest in farming. My grandparents **understand** this, but they hope that someone will come **back** to take over the farm.\n\nRecent **development** in the region has brought new roads and more **information** about modern farming. Young farmers can now **set** up online shops and sell directly to customers, and they often earn more than office workers who **pay** high rents in the city. Last summer, two of my cousins spent a **lot** of time on the farm. They taught **themselves** how to sell vegetables online, and their first **small** business is already making money. My grandmother says it was the best summer since the **last** time the whole family lived on the farm."},"11":{"w":["talk","face","manager","spend","question","able","big","live","meet","future","down","never","instead","since","week","group","off","past","someone","far"],"title":"A Lesson from My Manager","t":"Last year, our team got a new **manager**, a quiet woman in her fifties. In her first **week**, she called a **group** meeting and asked each of us one simple **question**: \"What do you want your **future** to look like?\" Nobody had ever asked us that before.\n\nSome of us didn't know how to answer. I said I wanted to **live** in a **big** city and travel **far** from home. **Someone** else said he just wanted to stop working late. Our manager listened carefully and wrote **down** every answer.\n\n**Instead** of giving orders, she spent the next month talking with each of us. She would **talk** to us about our strengths and the problems we **faced|face**. \"You will **never** grow if you only do what you did in the **past**,\" she told me. She encouraged me to **spend** an hour a day learning new skills.\n\n**Since** then, our team has changed. We **meet** every Monday to share ideas, and people are more **able** to say what they think. Last month, the manager took a week **off** for the first time, and the team ran smoothly without her. \"That,\" she said when she returned, \"is the best sign of a good team.\""},"12":{"w":["tree","among","mind","call","during","begin","passage","service","ask","write","kind","early","move","must","poor","report","possible","bring","land","little"],"title":"The Old Tree","t":"In the **early** days of our village, people say, a traveller planted a **tree** beside the river. Two hundred years later, it still stands **among** the houses, and the villagers **call** it \"Grandfather Tree.\" Its branches are wide enough to shade the whole square.\n\nThe tree has seen many changes. **During** hard times, **poor** families met under it to share food. Children played there, and old men sat in its shade to talk. When a family had to **move** away, they would **bring** a **little** soil from under the tree to their new home.\n\nLast year, a company wanted to buy the **land** and build a shopping centre. The news spread quickly, and a local reporter wrote a **report** about the tree's history. People from nearby towns began to **write** letters and **ask** the government to protect it. \"It's **possible** to have development,\" one letter said, \"without losing our past.\"\n\nIn the end, the company agreed to change its plans. The tree now has a small fence, and the town offers a guided tour as a public **service**. Visitors often stop to read a **passage** from an old poem carved on a stone nearby. One elderly woman told me, \"You **must** understand: this tree is in the **mind** of everyone here. It is a **kind** of family member.\" When the tour **began|begin** this spring, the whole village came to celebrate."},"13":{"w":["stop","turn","across","moon","once","yet","simply","water","ever","under","cost","practice","chinese","friend","house","month","play","clear","care","hold"],"title":"Under the Moon","t":"Every autumn, my family celebrates the Mid-Autumn Festival at my grandparents' **house** in the countryside. It is one of the most important **Chinese** holidays, a time when families gather to admire the full **moon** and share mooncakes.\n\nThis year, I invited a **friend** from Spain to join us. She had never seen the festival before — this was her first **month** in China — and she was not sure what to expect. I explained that the holiday is **simply** about being together. \"It doesn't **cost** much,\" I said. \"It's about **care**, not money.\"\n\nWe arrived just before sunset. My grandmother made us **stop** at the door to wash our hands with cold **water** from the well, an old **practice** she has kept all her life. Then she **held|hold** my friend's hand and led her **across** the courtyard to a table covered with fruit and cakes.\n\nAs the moon rose, the children began to **play** games, and the adults sat **under** the old pear tree, talking quietly. When the sky became **clear**, my grandfather told the story of Chang'e, the goddess who flew to the moon. My friend listened closely. \"Have you **ever** heard it before?\" I asked. \"No,\" she said, \"but I feel as if I've known it **once**, long ago.\" As we **turned|turn** to go inside, the moon was still shining, though the night was not **yet** over."},"14":{"w":["though","leave","air","drive","next","side","three","dog","already","expect","love","strong","away","case","hour","run","teacher","close","let","here"],"title":"The Dog Who Waited","t":"My **teacher** in primary school had a small brown **dog** named Lucky. Every morning, Lucky walked with her to school and waited by the gate until classes finished. The children **loved|love** him, and he seemed to **love** them too.\n\nOne winter, our teacher fell ill and had to spend **three** weeks in hospital. We **expected|expect** Lucky to stay at home, but he came to the school gate every morning anyway. He sat by the **side** of the road for hours, waiting. Even on the coldest days, when the **air** was freezing, he did not **leave**.\n\nThe headmaster finally decided to **let** him wait inside the classroom. \"In this **case**,\" he said, \"we'll make an exception.\" Lucky lay **close** to the heater, and the children shared their lunch with him. **Though** he was small, he was **strong** and patient.\n\nWhen our teacher came back, Lucky heard her car from far **away**. He jumped up and **ran|run** to the gate, barking with joy. She had **already** heard about his loyalty from the other teachers, and she cried as she hugged him. \"I'm **here**,\" she whispered. **Next** spring, she started to **drive** a shorter route so that Lucky could walk with her for an **hour** every morning."},"15":{"w":["light","enough","healthy","plan","travel","almost","easy","computer","itself","full","build","read","key","everyone","story","yourself","watch","foreign","library","low"],"title":"The New Library","t":"Last year, our town opened a new public **library** in an old factory building. The architects wanted to **build** something bright and welcoming, so they added large windows to let in natural **light**. On sunny days, the reading room is **full** of sunshine.\n\nThe library has become a meeting place for **everyone**. Children come to **read** picture books and listen to a **story** in the afternoon. Students use the **computer** room to study, and older people **watch** films on Saturday evenings. There is even a corner with books in **foreign** languages for people who love to **travel**.\n\nThe best thing about the library is that it is **easy** to use. Membership is free, and the opening hours are long. \"We **plan** to stay open **almost** every day of the year,\" the director told me. She believes that reading is as important as eating **healthy** food: it keeps the mind strong.\n\nOf course, the library is not perfect. It does not have **enough** seats at weekends, and the coffee shop is too small. But the building **itself** is beautiful, and the town's **low** budget did not stop the team from doing a great job. The **key**, the director says, is simple: \"Come in, find a quiet corner and just be **yourself**.\""},"16":{"w":["word","thinking","order","doctor","buy","fail","sound","break","present","together","mean","enjoy","car","tourist","clean","building","field","form","matter","free"],"title":"The Doctor's Advice","t":"My uncle is a **doctor** in a busy city hospital. Last summer, he told me something I have never forgotten: \"Health is not only a **matter** of medicine. It is a **form** of daily care.\" He believes that many people **fail** to look after themselves until it is too late.\n\nHis advice sounds simple. Walk instead of driving the **car**. **Enjoy** meals **together** with family rather than eating alone. Keep your home **clean** and open the windows. Take a short **break** every hour when you work at a computer. \"None of these things costs money,\" he says. \"They are **free**.\"\n\nHe also talks about the importance of **thinking** positively. \"When patients are worried, every small **sound** in their body seems serious,\" he explains. \"I try to calm them with a few kind **words|word**.\" He says that a good doctor must be **present** for patients, not just for their illnesses.\n\nMy uncle works in a modern **building**, but he spends his weekends in the countryside, walking through rice **fields|field** with his camera. He is not a typical **tourist**; he talks to farmers and learns about their lives. \"Our health depends on **order** and balance,\" he says. \"That doesn't **mean** we must be perfect. It means we must pay attention.\" He never tries to **buy** happiness; he finds it in simple things."},"17":{"w":["meeting","bad","plant","market","remember","class","fast","similar","sometimes","raise","short","teach","rich","until","conversation","exercise","half","quite","true","usually"],"title":"The Conversation Class","t":"Last term, I joined an English **conversation** **class** at a community centre. I had studied grammar for years, but I could not hold a **short** conversation without feeling nervous. The teacher, a retired journalist, promised that our speaking would improve **quite** quickly if we practised every week.\n\nEach **meeting** began with a simple **exercise**. We worked in pairs and talked about our daily lives: what we ate, where we shopped at the **market**, how we spent our weekends. **Sometimes** the topics were serious, such as whether **rich** countries should do more to help poor ones. The teacher would **raise** a question and let us argue.\n\nShe had a gift for making people relax. \"Don't worry about **bad** grammar,\" she said. \"Speak first, correct later.\" She liked to **teach** through stories, and she would **usually** tell us one at the start of class. I still **remember** her story about a **plant** that grew through a crack in the pavement. \"Learning is **similar**,\" she said. \"It finds a way.\"\n\nBy the end of the term, I could speak **fast** enough to keep up with a real conversation. It was not easy; for the first **half** of the course I almost gave up. But I stayed **until** the last lesson, and I'm glad I did. It is **true** what she told us: confidence comes from practice."},"18":{"w":["agree","four","open","stay","workplace","news","sell","serve","second","fall","wrong","meaning","modern","farm","million","difficult","note","ago","ourselves","paper"],"title":"From Farm to Office","t":"**Four** years **ago**, my friend Lei left his family's **farm** to work in the city. Like **millions|million** of young people, he hoped to find a **modern** job with a good salary. He found work in an office, but he soon discovered that his new **workplace** was **difficult** in ways he had not expected.\n\nAt first, he felt like a stranger. Colleagues used words whose **meaning** he did not understand, and he made many **wrong** decisions. He took **notes|note** on everything, writing in a small **paper** notebook he kept in his pocket. Every evening, he read the **news** to learn about the business world.\n\nIn his **second** year, things began to change. Lei realised that his farming background was useful. He knew how to **sell** vegetables at the market, and he understood how to **serve** customers patiently. His manager began to **agree** with his ideas, and he was promoted.\n\nStill, Lei misses the countryside, especially in autumn when the leaves **fall**. He goes home every holiday and **stays|stay** for a week. \"We must not forget where we come from,\" he says. \"We should remind **ourselves** that every job has value. An office is not better than a farm. It is just **open** at different hours.\""},"19":{"w":["sure","road","name","simple","thank","body","hope","cut","several","yes","decide","price","subject","works","behind","hear","five","left","nothing","slow"],"title":"The Slow Road","t":"Last spring, I **decided|decide** to walk from my hometown to the next city, a journey of about **five** days. Friends thought I was crazy. \"Why not take the train?\" they asked. I wasn't **sure** myself, but I **hoped|hope** that a **slow** journey would help me think.\n\nThe **road** was quiet and **simple**. At one crossroads, I took a **left** turn just to follow a river. I passed villages whose **names|name** I had never heard, and I stopped to talk with farmers working in the fields. **Several** times, strangers invited me to eat with them. One old man refused to accept money. \"**Nothing** is more valuable than company,\" he said. I could only **thank** him again and again.\n\nThe walk was hard on my **body**. My feet hurt, and I **cut** my hand while climbing over a fence. On the third day, I almost gave up. But I could **hear** my grandfather's voice: \"When you have walked half the way, the other half is **behind** you.\"\n\nBy the end, I had learned that the **price** of speed is attention. In a car, we miss the small things: the smell of rain, the sound of birds, the art in a farmer's garden. I now visit museums with new eyes, studying the **works** of rural painters. Walking has become my favourite **subject** to write about. When people ask whether I'd do it again, I say **yes** without hesitation."},"20":{"w":["machine","visit","answer","bit","bridge","meat","discuss","middle","deep","friendship","receive","along","lie","shoe","hand","soon","carry","per","wide","worth"],"title":"The Old Bridge","t":"In the **middle** of my hometown, a stone **bridge** crosses a **wide** river. It is more than three hundred years old, and people say it was built by **hand** without any **machine**. For centuries, farmers have used it to **carry** vegetables and **meat** to the market on the other side.\n\nWhen I was a boy, my grandfather often took me there. We would walk **along** the river and talk about everything. He told me that the bridge was a symbol of **friendship** between two villages that once fought over water. \"A bridge is **worth** more than a wall,\" he said.\n\nRecently, the town decided to **discuss** whether to replace the old bridge with a modern one. Engineers said that the water was too **deep** and the stone too old. Many residents disagreed. Within a week, the council **received|receive** hundreds of letters asking to keep it.\n\nI went back to **visit** last month. The answer had been decided: the bridge would be repaired, not replaced. Workers will **soon** begin, and they hope to finish in a year. I stood on the bridge for a **bit**, watching children run across in their school **shoes|shoe**. \"The **answer** to the past,\" my grandfather once said, \"should not **lie** in forgetting it.\" The repairs will cost about two hundred yuan **per** person in the town, and almost everyone has paid."},"21":{"w":["push","tv","park","cook","driver","speak","member","sleep","fish","draw","minute","weight","green","check","listen","earth","mother","single","night","happy"],"title":"My Mother's Garden","t":"Every evening after work, my **mother** goes into the small garden behind our house. She says it is the only place where she can **listen** to her own thoughts. While the rest of the family watches **TV**, she waters her vegetables and checks every plant for insects.\n\nThe garden is not large, but it is full of life. There are **green** beans, tomatoes and a tiny pond where a **single** **fish** lives. My mother likes to **check** the soil with her fingers. \"The **earth** tells you what it needs,\" she says. \"You just have to pay attention.\"\n\nShe did not always love gardening. When she was younger, she worked as a taxi **driver** and rarely had time to rest. She would come home late at **night**, too tired to **cook** or even **speak**. Then a doctor warned her that she needed to lose **weight** and **sleep** more. A friend suggested gardening, and she decided to try it.\n\nNow she spends at least thirty **minutes|minute** in the garden every day. She has become a **member** of a local gardening club that meets in the **park** on weekends. Sometimes she brings her sketchbook to **draw** the flowers. \"I never imagined I could be so **happy** with dirt on my hands,\" she laughs. Her advice to anyone who feels stressed is simple: don't **push** yourself too hard; grow something instead."},"22":{"w":["fire","tourism","everything","eye","outside","room","wish","office","again","anything","seven","cover","heart","stand","else","pleasure","western","spring","forget","television"],"title":"A Room with a View","t":"When my grandparents retired, they moved to a small flat in a quiet mountain town. Its best feature is a sunny **room** with a large window. From there, they can see green hills, a river and, in **spring**, fields covered with flowers. \"It is a **pleasure** to wake up here,\" my grandmother says.\n\nThe town has changed in recent years. **Tourism** has brought new hotels and cafés, and visitors from Beijing and Shanghai arrive every weekend. Some local people **wish** the town could stay as it was, but others welcome the money. My grandfather, who once worked in a government **office**, has opinions about **everything** and is happy to share them with anyone.\n\nMy grandparents do not own a **television**. Instead, they spend their evenings **outside**, walking along the river or sitting on a bench. My grandmother says she has a weak **heart**, so she walks slowly. \"The doctor told me I could **stand** for an hour but not run,\" she jokes. \"I have no wish to run anywhere **else**.\"\n\nWhen I visit, we sit by the **fire** in winter or by the window in summer and drink tea. Clouds **cover** the hills, and my grandfather points out birds with a sharp **eye**. He likes to read **Western** novels translated into Chinese, though he admits he **forgets|forget** the characters' names. I always promise to come back **again** soon. I visited **seven** times last year, and I would not change **anything** about those visits."},"23":{"w":["restaurant","late","surprise","drink","fit","necessary","worry","game","perfect","smell","forward","post","third","morning","rest","coffee","copy","sit","bag","sport"],"title":"The Morning Café","t":"On the corner of my street, there is a small **restaurant** that serves the best breakfast in town. Every **morning**, office workers stop there to **drink** a cup of **coffee** and eat fresh bread before work. The owner, Mrs. Fang, knows almost every customer by name.\n\nI discovered the café by **surprise**. One rainy day, I was running **late** for an important meeting and needed somewhere to **sit** down. Mrs. Fang brought me tea and told me not to **worry**. \"Nothing is more **necessary** than breakfast,\" she said. That was the start of a long friendship.\n\nThe café is not **perfect**. The tables are small, and the kitchen is so close that the **smell** of frying onions gets into your clothes. But people love it. On weekends, families come to **rest**, read the newspaper or play a board **game**. Mrs. Fang even keeps a **copy** of every local newspaper for customers.\n\nShe is also a keen fan of **sport**. On the wall, there is a **post** card from a famous football player who once ate there. \"He came on the **third** day after I opened,\" she remembers proudly. She keeps fit by walking to work, carrying a heavy **bag** of fresh vegetables. She looks **forward** to retiring one day, but not yet. \"As long as I'm **fit**, I'll keep cooking,\" she says."},"24":{"w":["size","walk","bear","girl","sugar","red","clothes","homework","famous","moment","north","six","store","beautiful","return","lesson","bottle","piece","dollar","either"],"title":"The Shop on North Street","t":"My aunt runs a small clothing **store** on **North** Street, one of the oldest streets in our town. It is not **famous**, but it is **beautiful**, with wooden floors and large windows. Customers come not only for the **clothes** but also for her friendly conversation.\n\nShe opened the shop **six** years ago, after spending a **moment** of crisis deciding whether to leave her office job. \"I couldn't **bear** another year at a desk,\" she says. She used her savings to buy fabric and sewing machines, and she taught herself how to design dresses for women of every **size**.\n\nRunning a business taught her many **lessons|lesson**. In the first year, she lost money and often wanted to give up. But she learned to listen to customers and **return** their trust with good service. She also learned that she could sell **either** expensive dresses or cheap ones, but not both.\n\nHer shop is also a meeting place for local girls. After school, a **girl** from the next street often comes to do her **homework** at the counter, and my aunt gives her a **piece** of cake and a **bottle** of water. On the wall hangs her first **dollar**, sent by an American customer, framed in **red** paper. My aunt is careful with money; she puts very little **sugar** in her tea and rarely buys anything for herself. \"A good **walk** and a good chat,\" she says, \"are better than any shopping trip.\""},"25":{"w":["expensive","catch","latest","street","bird","hospital","maybe","ship","countryside","british","pass","table","meal","certainly","kitchen","send","agricultural","motivation","yellow","baby"],"title":"Life in the Countryside","t":"After living in the city for ten years, my friend Anna, a **British** teacher, decided to move to the **countryside** in southern China. She rented a small house with a **kitchen** garden and a view of rice fields. \"City life was **expensive** and tiring,\" she explains. \"I wanted something simpler.\"\n\nHer new life is very different. In the morning, she wakes to the sound of a **bird** singing outside her window. She walks down the only **street** in the village to buy vegetables and eggs, and farmers often **pass** her a few extra tomatoes for free. Neighbours invite her to share a **meal** at their **table** at least twice a week.\n\nOf course, there are challenges. The nearest **hospital** is an hour away, and she once had to travel by boat when the road flooded; a small **ship** carried patients across the river. When she **caught|catch** a fever last winter, a neighbour cared for her like a family member. She also struggled to understand the local **agricultural** methods at first.\n\nAnna now teaches English online and **sends|send** short videos to friends back home. Her **motivation**, she says, comes from the people around her. A neighbour recently had a **baby**, and Anna was invited to the celebration, where everyone wore **yellow** for luck. \"I'm **certainly** not rich,\" she says, \"but I'm happy.\" She follows the **latest** news from the city, and she says she doesn't miss it. **Maybe**, she thinks, she will stay forever."},"26":{"w":["shop","double","interesting","blue","holiday","warm","window","agreement","welcome","box","head","nearly","picture","cheap","fruit","heavy","weather","south","text","email"],"title":"A Holiday by the Sea","t":"Last summer, my family spent our **holiday** in a small fishing town in the **south**. The **weather** was **warm**, the sky was **blue**, and the sea was so clear that we could see fish swimming near the shore. It was the most relaxing week I'd had in years.\n\nWe stayed in a **cheap** guesthouse with a **window** facing the harbour. The owner, an old fisherman, gave us a warm **welcome** and a **box** of fresh **fruit** on the first day. He said tourists were becoming more common, but the town still felt like a place where everyone knew each other.\n\nEvery morning, I walked to the market to **shop** for breakfast. The stalls were full of **interesting** things: dried fish, sea shells and **heavy** baskets of crabs. I took a **picture** of almost everything and sent them to friends by **email**. My sister preferred to **text** her friends, sending them **double** the number of photos I did.\n\nOne evening, the owner invited us to join a local festival. Fishermen carried a wooden boat on their **heads|head** through the streets, and children threw flowers into the sea. \"We have an **agreement** with the sea,\" the owner explained. \"We respect it, and it feeds us.\" By the end of the week, we had **nearly** forgotten our busy city lives."},"27":{"w":["river","chef","ready","mistake","star","train","foot","party","bank","running","noise","orange","wear","trouble","date","myself","floor","throw","dish","ahead"],"title":"The Chef's First Party","t":"My cousin is a young **chef** who works in a busy restaurant beside the **river** in our city. Last month, she was asked to cook for a large **party**: a **bank** was celebrating its fiftieth anniversary, and two hundred guests were expected. She had never cooked for so many people before.\n\nThe preparation took days. She planned every **dish** carefully, choosing a menu of local fish, vegetables and a dessert made with **orange** and honey. She checked the **date** three times to make sure everything would be **ready**. \"I didn't want to make a single **mistake**,\" she said.\n\nOn the day of the party, things went wrong. The kitchen was full of **noise**, a pot of soup fell on the **floor**, and an assistant arrived late because his **train** was delayed. My cousin had to keep **running** between the kitchen and the hall. She was in **trouble**, but she did not panic.\n\nLater she told me that she had one rule: stay calm and think **ahead**. \"I kept telling **myself** not to panic,\" she said. She asked a colleague to **throw** away the spilled soup and make a new one, and she put her best **foot** forward. When the meal was finally served, the guests applauded. That night, she felt like a **star**. She still likes to **wear** the apron from that day as a lucky charm."},"28":{"w":["friendly","medicine","miss","autonomous","sun","whom","film","mine","disability","fill","busy","suggestion","wait","west","bed","village","traveler","please","above","die"],"title":"The Village Doctor","t":"In a small mountain **village** in the **west** of China, a young doctor named Dr. Lin runs the only clinic. She is **friendly** and patient, and the villagers trust her completely. She treats everyone, including farmers, children and a few elderly people with a **disability**.\n\nHer days are **busy**. She starts early in the morning and often works until late at night. Patients sometimes **wait** for hours outside the clinic, sitting in the **sun**. She has only one assistant and limited **medicine**, so she must use her resources carefully. In winter, she sometimes sleeps on a small **bed** in the back room.\n\nDr. Lin came from the city. Her parents wanted her to work in a large hospital, but she made her own choice. \"The city has many doctors,\" she says. \"Here, people really need me.\" The village has been an **autonomous** community for centuries, and its people are proud and independent.\n\nA **traveler** who visited last year wrote a report about her work and made a short **film**. After it was shown online, people sent donations to **fill** the clinic with new equipment. One viewer's **suggestion** was to build a small library, and Dr. Lin loved the idea. She says she **misses|miss** her family, but she has found a home **above** the clouds. \"This life is **mine**,\" she says. \"**Please** don't feel sorry for me.\" The old village chief, for **whom** she cared until he **died|die** last spring, called her \"our mountain daughter.\""},"29":{"w":["worried","cool","himself","math","mountain","bright","door","everywhere","fine","finish","photo","grade","lunch","join","below","ground","jump","near","whose","deeply"],"title":"A Day in the Mountains","t":"Last Saturday, my friend Hao and I climbed a **mountain** near our city. Hao was **worried** about the weather, but the morning was **bright** and **cool**, perfect for hiking. We set off early and planned to **finish** the climb before **lunch**.\n\nThe path was steep, and in some places we had to **jump** over rocks. Hao, who teaches **math** at a high school, kept counting our steps. \"We've done two thousand already,\" he said. \"**Fine**,\" I replied, \"but how many more?\" Wild flowers grew **everywhere**, and birds sang in the trees **below** us.\n\nNear the top, we met an old man sitting on the **ground**, eating noodles. He told us he climbed the mountain every week, even at seventy-five. He had once been a soldier, and he said he still pushed **himself** to stay strong. \"**Whose** idea was it to climb today?\" he asked. Hao laughed and pointed at me.\n\nAt the summit, we took a **photo** together and looked down at the city. It seemed so small from **near** the clouds. Hao said he wanted to **join** a hiking club, and I agreed to come with him. The old man walked us to the **door** of a small temple on the way down. I felt **deeply** grateful for such a peaceful day. Our legs ached, but our **grade** for the day, Hao joked, was an A."},"30":{"w":["english","father","hi","ill","station","workforce","arrive","credit","east","guest","page","summer","rain","season","wildlife","guess","mention","hotel","march","herself"],"title":"A Summer in the Hills","t":"Last **summer**, my **father** and I spent two weeks at a small **hotel** in the hills of Yunnan. It was the rainy **season**, and it seemed to **rain** almost every afternoon. But the countryside was green and beautiful, and we didn't mind.\n\nWe **arrived|arrive** by bus at a tiny **station** at the bottom of the hill. The owner of the hotel, a cheerful woman, met us with a big smile. \"**Hi**! Welcome!\" she said in **English**, the only English word she seemed to know besides \"OK.\" She treated every **guest** like family.\n\nThe area is famous for its **wildlife**. Every morning, we walked into the forest to watch birds and monkeys. My father, who had been **ill** the year before, grew stronger every day. He kept a notebook and filled a **page** with notes after each walk. \"**Guess** how many birds I saw today,\" he would ask me at dinner.\n\nThe owner ran the hotel by **herself**. She told us that young people were leaving the village, and the local **workforce** was getting older. Tourism was helping, she said, but she wished the government would give small businesses more **credit**. She **mentioned|mention** that she planned to open a small café next **March**, facing the **east**, where the sunrise is beautiful."},"31":{"w":["round","somewhere","ten","wife","excuse","hill","educator","beef","town","eight","front","letter","seat","boom","classroom","map","oh","pair","unhealthy","boy"],"title":"The Teacher in the Hills","t":"Mr. Zhou has been an **educator** for thirty years, teaching in a small school on a **hill** above his hometown. When he started, the **town** was poor, and the school had only one **classroom**. Today, thanks to an economic **boom** in the region, there are **ten** classrooms and more than three hundred students.\n\nHe still remembers his first day. **Eight** children sat in the **front** row, staring at him nervously. One **boy** had walked two hours to get there, carrying a **pair** of shoes in his hand so they would not get dirty. \"**Oh**, how small they were,\" Mr. Zhou says with a smile.\n\nOver the years, he has seen many changes. Children used to eat simple meals of rice and vegetables; now some eat too much **unhealthy** food, and he teaches them about nutrition. Instead of a **letter** from relatives in the city, students now receive video calls. He hangs a large **map** of the world on the wall so that they can see how far their families have travelled.\n\nHis **wife**, also a teacher, works at the same school. On weekends, they cook **beef** noodles for students who cannot go home. Mr. Zhou never makes an **excuse** to leave early, and every term he moves the students **round** the room so that each one gets a **seat** near the window. He hopes that, **somewhere** in the future, one of them will return to teach in the hills."},"32":{"w":["cleaner","european","glass","newspaper","sky","trip","menu","juice","match","afraid","chat","crowded","quarter","sea","tomorrow","totally","challenging","monday","none","january"],"title":"A Trip to the Coast","t":"Last **Monday**, my friend and I took a short **trip** to a fishing town by the **sea**. It was **January**, and the beach was empty. The **sky** was grey, the wind was cold, and the water looked **totally** different from the bright summer pictures I had seen in a **newspaper**.\n\nWe had planned to swim, but neither of us dared. \"I'm **afraid** I'll freeze,\" my friend said. Instead, we walked along the shore and watched fishermen bring in their boats. The town was quiet — **none** of the shops were **crowded**, and the restaurant owners were happy to **chat** with us.\n\nAt lunch, we found a small restaurant with a **menu** written on a board. We ordered fried fish, rice and fresh orange **juice**. A **European** couple at the next table told us they had been travelling around China for a month. \"Your country is **challenging** for travellers,\" they said, \"but always interesting.\"\n\nThat afternoon, we watched a local football **match** on a field near the harbour. A **cleaner** from our hotel was playing, and he scored a goal in the last **quarter** of the game. We cheered so loudly that people turned to look at us. Before leaving, we each bought a small **glass** bottle filled with sand. \"Let's come back **tomorrow**,\" my friend joked. We both knew we would return in summer."},"33":{"w":["mom","suffering","bell","fat","surface","borrow","hundred","weekend","afternoon","annoy","fly","harmless","ice","pick","dress","gift","graduation","outgoing","dark","daughter"],"title":"My Sister's Graduation","t":"Last summer, my older sister celebrated her **graduation** from university. It was a hot **afternoon**, and the ceremony took place outside in a big park. My **mom** cried from the moment it started, and even my father, who rarely shows emotion, seemed close to tears.\n\nMy sister is the first **daughter** in our family to go to university. She has always been **outgoing** and energetic, but her four years of study were not easy. She worked at a café every **weekend** to pay her rent, and she sometimes had to **borrow** money from friends. Her **suffering** during the exam months was real; she barely slept.\n\nFor the ceremony, she wore a simple blue **dress** under her graduation gown. When her name was called, a **bell** rang and more than a **hundred** students threw their caps into the air. A small dog ran across the stage, chasing the caps — **harmless**, but very funny.\n\nAfterwards, we gave her a **gift**: a silver bracelet with her name on the **surface**. My mother had chosen it carefully. \"Now you can **fly**,\" she said. We had dinner at a restaurant that evening and ordered too much **fat** and fried food, but nobody cared. My little brother tried to **annoy** my sister by telling embarrassing stories from her childhood. When we walked home after **dark**, she asked us to stop and **pick** some flowers. My father bought ice cream with extra **ice** for everyone."},"34":{"w":["hunter","radio","sweet","basketball","dinner","everybody","nice","arm","black","factory","fourth","garden","quick","straight","vegetable","website","white","adolescence","husband","ring"],"title":"Grandfather's Garden","t":"My grandfather was a **hunter** in his youth, but in old age he became a gardener. Behind his house in the countryside, he grew every **vegetable** you can imagine: cabbages, beans, carrots and **sweet** potatoes. **Everybody** in the village came to ask for his advice.\n\nHe worked in a **factory** for most of his life, and when he retired, the garden became his joy. Every morning, he listened to the **radio** while he watered the plants. He wore an old **black** hat and always carried a basket over one **arm**. The rows in his garden were always **straight**, as if he had used a ruler.\n\nMy grandmother loved flowers more than vegetables, and she often joked that her **husband** cared more about cabbages than about her. She grew **white** roses along the fence, and my grandfather let her have the **fourth** part of the garden for them. They argued about it every spring, but it was always a **nice** kind of argument.\n\nWhen I was in my **adolescence**, I spent many summers there. My cousins and I played **basketball** in the yard and helped pick beans for **dinner**. My grandfather was **quick** to praise us when we worked hard. Last year, my cousin built a **website** to sell vegetables from the farm. My grandfather still wears the wedding **ring** my grandmother gave him fifty years ago, and he still goes to the **garden** every morning."},"35":{"w":["sick","tired","april","dialogue","exam","hearing","ticket","viewer","workday","flight","quiet","soldier","tuesday","wind","beat","pioneer","twice","amazing","freezing","hot"],"title":"The Freezing Flight","t":"Last **April**, I took a **flight** from Beijing to Harbin for a job interview. I was nervous and **tired**, because I had studied for an important **exam** the night before. I had bought the cheapest **ticket** I could find. The plane left on a **Tuesday** morning, and I hoped to get some sleep on board.\n\nBut the weather had other plans. A strong **wind** began to shake the plane, and the pilot announced that we would land in a different city. Passengers became **quiet**, and a baby began to cry. I felt almost **sick** with worry. My heart **beat** so fast that I could hear it.\n\nNext to me sat an elderly **soldier** in uniform. He noticed my fear and started a friendly **dialogue**. He told me about his life as a **pioneer** in the northern forests, building roads in **freezing** temperatures. \"This wind is nothing,\" he laughed. \"I've seen worse **twice** a year.\"\n\nWe landed safely in a small city. It was so cold that the **hot** tea at the airport cooled in minutes. I missed my interview, but the company offered me another chance. A television **viewer** later told me she had seen a news report about our flight. Thanks to the old man, the experience felt **amazing** rather than frightening. His **hearing** was poor, so I had to shout my thanks. I still think of him on every long **workday**, and I hope I'll meet him again."},"36":{"w":["mentally","nine","saying","surrounding","agenda","cry","february","laugh","october","pull","supermarket","bike","breakfast","plate","son","cocoa","mail","silk","tooth","firefighter"],"title":"The Firefighter's Family","t":"My neighbour Mr. Liu is a **firefighter** who has worked at the city fire station for twenty years. His job is dangerous and **mentally** exhausting, but he says he would never choose another career. \"When you save someone's life, you never forget it,\" he told me.\n\nHis day begins early. He eats a simple **breakfast** of rice porridge and an egg, then rides his **bike** to work. His wife packs his lunch on a small **plate** covered with cloth. Their **son**, who is **nine**, often runs after him to the corner, shouting goodbye.\n\nLast **February**, there was a fire in a **supermarket** near our street. Mr. Liu and his team arrived within minutes. Smoke filled the **surrounding** buildings, and people began to **cry** for help. He had to **pull** an elderly woman out of a burning room. She lost a **tooth** when she fell, but she survived. In **October**, she sent him a thank-you card by **mail**, along with a silk scarf.\n\nMr. Liu likes to **laugh**, and he is full of old **sayings|saying**. His favourite is: \"Fire is a good servant but a bad master.\" On his days off, he makes hot **cocoa** for the neighbourhood children and teaches them about fire safety. His wife keeps a family **agenda** on the fridge so that she knows his shifts. She once gave him a **silk** tie for his birthday, though he jokes that he has never worn it."},"37":{"w":["unsafe","abroad","egg","nor","adjustment","block","dig","dry","dynasty","fifth","hello","mineral","thirty","yours","airport","assistance","boring","cloud","gate","lake"],"title":"Waiting at the Airport","t":"Last summer, I spent **thirty** hours at an **airport** in Europe because my flight home was cancelled. It was my first time travelling **abroad** alone, and I was nervous. \"**Hello**,\" said the woman at the information desk, \"I'm afraid there's nothing we can do until tomorrow.\"\n\nAt first, I was angry. The airport was crowded and **boring**, and I couldn't find anywhere comfortable to sleep. My phone battery was dead, and I had neither a charger **nor** enough money for a hotel. I sat near **gate** number seven and watched planes take off into the grey **clouds|cloud**.\n\nThen an older couple sat down next to me. They offered me a bottle of **mineral** water and a boiled **egg**. \"Is this seat **yours**?\" the man asked politely. We started talking, and I learned that they were historians who studied the Tang **Dynasty**. They had visited China five times and were on their **fifth** trip to Europe.\n\nTheir **assistance** made a big difference. They lent me a charger and told me about a park with a **lake** near the airport where I could walk. They also showed me how to **dig** through the airline's website to find a better flight. The weather was **dry** and sunny, and the walk helped me relax. \"Travel is all about **adjustment**,\" the woman said. \"Things go wrong, and you adapt.\" Before we parted, we exchanged addresses. That evening we found a whole **block** of small restaurants near the park and had dinner together. The airport was not **unsafe**, just tiring, and I left with new friends."},"38":{"w":["ride","sorry","spare","tea","tonight","wall","enjoyable","overweight","willingness","coke","trainer","asian","bent","careful","crossing","flower","hungry","neither","personally","sad"],"title":"The Personal Trainer","t":"Last year, my doctor told me I was **overweight** and needed to exercise. I hated sports, but I agreed to see a **trainer** at the gym near my office. \"I'm **sorry**,\" I told him on the first day, \"but I have no **willingness** to run.\"\n\nHe laughed. \"Then we won't run. We'll **ride** bikes and walk.\" He was **careful** not to push me too hard. Every session began with a cup of green **tea** and a short conversation. He **personally** designed a plan for me, based on my **spare** time and my eating habits.\n\nThe first weeks were difficult. I was always **hungry**, and I missed my daily can of **Coke**. Sometimes I felt **sad** and wanted to give up. But my trainer encouraged me. He told me about his own childhood in an **Asian** village, where he walked ten kilometres to school every day. \"Your body is not **bent** on failing,\" he said. \"It just needs time.\"\n\nSlowly, exercise became **enjoyable**. I started walking to work, passing a busy pedestrian **crossing** and a park full of **flowers|flower**. I even painted a motivational quote on my bedroom **wall**. After six months, I had lost fifteen kilograms. **Neither** my doctor nor I could believe it. \"Let's celebrate **tonight**,\" my trainer said. \"But not with cake.\""},"39":{"w":["thousand","apple","disappointment","fun","glad","modem","nobody","polite","publicly","smile","strange","august","chair","facial","founding","kindergarten","nurse","rebuild","sailing","secretary"],"title":"The Kindergarten Nurse","t":"Ms. Bai has worked as a **nurse** at a **kindergarten** for twelve years. She looks after more than a **thousand** children each year, treating cuts, fevers and the occasional broken arm. The children love her **smile**, and parents trust her completely.\n\nShe chose the job by chance. After graduating, she worked as a **secretary** in an office, but she found the work **strange** and lonely. \"**Nobody** talked to me all day,\" she remembers. One **August**, she saw an advertisement for a kindergarten nurse and decided to apply. It was the best decision of her life.\n\nHer days are full of **fun**. Children bring her drawings and **apples|apple**, and she keeps a small **chair** in her office for anyone who needs a rest, next to an old **modem** that no longer works and that the children love to \"repair.\" She teaches them to be **polite** and to wash their hands before meals. She even paints **facial** designs on children's cheeks during festivals.\n\nThere have been moments of **disappointment** too. When the kindergarten's building was damaged in a storm, she worried it would close. But parents raised money to **rebuild** it, and the school's **founding** director **publicly** thanked her for her help. In summer, she enjoys **sailing** on the lake near her home. \"I'm **glad** I found this job,\" she says. \"These children teach me something new every day.\""},"40":{"w":["bride","piano","angry","corner","dad","firmly","gym","mistaken","mouth","ours","sister","smoke","song","spoken","wake","winter","astronomer","autumn","breakthrough","brown"],"title":"A Winter Wedding","t":"Last **winter**, my older **sister** got married in a small hotel in the mountains. The **bride** wore a long white dress and a **brown** coat, and snow fell softly outside. Our **dad**, who rarely shows emotion, cried when he walked her down the aisle.\n\nThe wedding was simple but beautiful. A friend played the **piano**, and the guests sang my sister's favourite **song**. Her husband, an **astronomer**, gave a short speech about how they met while watching a meteor shower in **autumn**. \"It was a **breakthrough** moment in my life,\" he said, and everyone laughed.\n\nNot everything went smoothly. The hotel's heating stopped working, and the room filled with **smoke** from the old fireplace. A guest became **angry** and complained loudly. My sister just smiled. \"We are not here for perfection,\" she said **firmly**. \"We're here for love.\"\n\nThe next morning, I had to **wake** early to help clean up. In the **corner** of the room, I found a note in my sister's handwriting. It said: \"This day is **ours**, and no small problem will ruin it.\" I was **mistaken** to think she was nervous; she was calm all along. Later, she joked that she needed a week at the **gym** to recover from all the cake. I still remember her **spoken** vows, short and honest, and the way she laughed afterwards with her **mouth** full of cake."},"41":{"w":["businessman","click","database","dead","december","decoration","desk","friday","gardening","hometown","leg","loudly","september","shout","tall","unsuccessful","wash","wet","boat","bread"],"title":"Back to My Hometown","t":"Every **December**, I return to my **hometown**, a fishing village on the coast. This year, I arrived on a **Friday** evening in the rain. The streets were **wet**, and the wind was cold, but my mother was waiting at the bus stop with an umbrella and a bag of warm **bread**.\n\nThe village has changed a lot. When I left in **September** ten years ago, it was quiet and poor. Now there are new houses, a small hotel and a shop that sells Christmas **decorations|decoration** to tourists. A **businessman** from the city has opened a restaurant by the harbour, and young people use their phones to **click** and order food online.\n\nBut some things have not changed. My father still goes out in his small **boat** every morning, and he still comes home to **wash** the fish in the yard. He is **tall** and strong, though his **legs|leg** are not as fast as they once were. My mother spends her afternoons **gardening**, growing onions and herbs beside the house.\n\nOne evening, my father told me about his early life. His first business, a small fish shop, was **unsuccessful**, and he nearly gave up. \"I was so tired that I felt **dead**,\" he said. \"But your mother would **shout** at me to keep going.\" He laughed **loudly** at the memory. I wrote down his stories at the **desk** in my old bedroom, hoping to keep them in a family **database** for my children to read one day."},"42":{"w":["cat","evening","football","format","milk","railway","settler","sixth","adaptation","belly","birthday","blow","card","celebration","chopsticks","crossroads","cup","dance","dear","fluency"],"title":"A Birthday at the Crossroads","t":"My grandmother turned eighty last spring, and the whole family gathered for a **celebration** at her house near a busy **crossroads** in the old town. She has lived there for sixty years, since the **railway** first came to the area. \"I've watched this street change like a movie,\" she says.\n\nThe party began in the **evening**. My uncles brought food, my cousins decorated the walls, and the children made a **birthday** **card** with drawings of her favourite **cat**. We ate noodles for long life, and everyone used **chopsticks** except my cousin's husband, a Canadian who was still learning. \"It's an **adaptation**,\" he joked, holding his chopsticks like a pencil.\n\nAfter dinner, we watched a **football** match on television, and my grandmother cheered louder than anyone. Then she asked my sister to **dance** with her. Although her **belly** was full of cake, she danced happily for twenty minutes. My little brother tried to **blow** out the candles before she could, and everyone laughed.\n\nAt the end of the night, she made a short speech. \"My **dear** family,\" she began, \"this is my **sixth** decade in this house.\" She spoke with surprising **fluency**, though she was tired. Her own grandfather had been a **settler** from the north, she reminded us. She raised her **cup** of warm **milk** and thanked us all. My cousin later posted a video of the party, though it was in the wrong **format** and nobody could open it."},"43":{"w":["forty","grandparent","grass","happily","hurry","july","lend","mm","nationwide","parking","plane","pub","pupil","puzzled","regards","shall","shine","sincerely","sir","sunday"],"title":"A Letter to My Old Teacher","t":"Dear **Sir**,\n\nI hope you remember me. I was a **pupil** in your class more than **forty** years ago, in the small school by the river. I am writing on a rainy **Sunday** in **July**, at the same desk where I once did my homework. Outside, it has rained thirty **mm** in an hour, and the **grass** in the garden is shining.\n\nYou once told us that the sun **shall** **shine** again after every storm. I didn't understand you then, but I do now. When my business failed, and I felt completely **puzzled** about what to do, I remembered your words.\n\nI now work for a **nationwide** company and travel often by **plane**. Last month, I visited our old town. The school is gone, and there is a **parking** lot where the classroom used to be. The old **pub** opposite is still there, though, and the owner remembered you. He said you used to sit in the corner reading every afternoon.\n\nMy **grandparents|grandparent**, who lived next to the school, are no longer alive, but I often think of them. My grandmother used to **lend** me books when I was in a **hurry** to finish my homework. I **happily** remember those days.\n\nI would love to visit you, if you are well. Please give my **regards** to your family.\n\nYours **sincerely**,\nLi Ming"},"44":{"w":["swimming","tasty","twentieth","umbrella","wednesday","westerner","zero","banana","biography","dictionary","dinosaur","disobey","empty","encouragement","fairness","hobby","horse","pity","somebody","stamp"],"title":"My Little Brother's Hobbies","t":"My little brother is nine years old and has more **hobbies|hobby** than anyone I know. On **Wednesday** afternoons, he goes **swimming** at the local pool. On weekends, he rides a **horse** at a farm outside the city. And every evening, he reads a **biography** of a famous scientist or explorer.\n\nHis favourite subject is the **dinosaur**. He has a huge **dictionary** of prehistoric animals and can name more than a hundred species. Last month, he took second place in a school quiz, losing to a girl who answered every question correctly. \"It's a **pity**,\" he said, \"but she deserved to win. That's **fairness**.\"\n\nMy brother is generally well behaved, but he sometimes likes to **disobey** our parents. Once, he went out in the rain without an **umbrella**, and he came home soaking wet. Another time, he ate an entire bunch of **bananas|banana** and then complained of stomach ache.\n\nHe also collects old **stamps|stamp**. **Somebody** gave him a stamp printed in the early **twentieth** century, and he treats it like treasure. He keeps his collection in a box that was once **empty** but is now almost full. My parents give him a lot of **encouragement**, and he dreams of becoming a museum curator. When a **Westerner** visited our school, my brother asked him about stamps from his country. The visitor laughed and admitted that his own knowledge of stamps was close to **zero**. My mother made him a **tasty** cake to celebrate."},"45":{"w":["telephone","tennis","accountant","addicted","admirable","alcoholic","allergic","anecdote","aunt","basket","beer","beside","birthplace","bookstore","botany","bravery","brewery","brotherhood","brush","buffet"],"title":"My Aunt's Bookstore","t":"My **aunt** used to work as an **accountant** at a large company, but ten years ago she left to open a small **bookstore** in her **birthplace**, a quiet town in the south. It was a brave decision, and many people thought it would fail. But today, the shop is one of the most popular places in town.\n\nThe bookstore is full of character. Books are stacked in every corner, and a **basket** of free bookmarks sits **beside** the door. She sells books on everything from **botany** to cooking, and she likes to share an **anecdote** about every author. Visitors say her stories are as interesting as the books.\n\nOn weekends, she organises events. Once a month, she holds a reading evening with a **buffet** of homemade snacks. She serves tea and juice but no **alcoholic** drinks, because she wants families to feel welcome. Next door is a small **brewery**, and its owner sometimes sends over a bottle of **beer** for adult guests.\n\nMy aunt also teaches children to paint. She gives each child a **brush** and lets them decorate the shop windows. One boy was **allergic** to the paint, so she found a safer kind. \"Children become **addicted** to screens,\" she says, \"but painting gives them something real.\"\n\nThe people in the town admire her **bravery**. They say her shop has created a kind of **brotherhood** among readers. Many call her work **admirable**. She doesn't have a website, and she still takes orders by **telephone**. On Sundays, she plays **tennis** with friends in the park."},"46":{"w":["bureaucratic","bus","chick","choir","cinema","cookie","cuisine","deadline","delighted","disabled","disturbing","download","drier","eleven","except","explorer","fifteen","fifty","firewood","forgetful"],"title":"The Forgetful Explorer","t":"My uncle calls himself an **explorer**, though his adventures usually take place within **fifty** kilometres of home. He is famously **forgetful**: he loses his keys, his glasses and once even his car. But he is also the most cheerful person I know.\n\nLast winter, he invited me on a camping trip to the mountains. We took a **bus** to the last village and then walked for **fifteen** kilometres. My uncle carried the tent, while I carried **firewood** and food. Of course, he had forgotten the matches, **except** for one small box that I had packed by chance.\n\nThat night, we sat by the fire and talked. He told me about his work at a city office, which he described as slow and **bureaucratic**. \"Every **deadline** is a small nightmare,\" he said. He also told me some **disturbing** stories about a factory that once polluted a river near his childhood home.\n\nIn the morning, we visited a small farm where a **disabled** farmer raised **chicks|chick** and grew vegetables. He was **delighted** to have visitors and invited us to stay for lunch. His wife served local **cuisine** and homemade **cookies|cookie**. Their son sings in a church **choir** and dreams of studying music.\n\nWe returned home at **eleven** at night, tired but happy. My uncle immediately began to **download** his photos, while I used a hair **drier** to dry my wet socks. He says that next year we will go to the **cinema** instead. \"Less walking,\" he laughed."},"47":{"w":["furnished","gifted","grandma","ha","hair","handbag","hat","hey","hilly","homeland","housework","juicy","knife","lovely","memorize","messy","motorbike","motto","nose","novelist"],"title":"Grandma's Kitchen","t":"My **grandma** lives in a small, **hilly** village in the south, in a simple house **furnished** with old wooden chairs and a large table. Whenever I visit, she is in the kitchen, wearing a straw **hat** and an apron, chopping vegetables with an old **knife**.\n\nShe has a **gifted** hand for cooking. Her dumplings are famous in the village, and her fruit is always **juicy** and sweet. She never uses a recipe; she simply knows. \"Cooking is like singing,\" she says. \"You don't **memorize** it; you feel it.\"\n\nGrandma also has a **motto**: \"Work first, then rest.\" She does all the **housework** herself, though she is eighty years old. Her kitchen is never **messy**, and her grey **hair** is always neatly tied back. When I offer to help, she just says, \"**Ha**! You'll only get in the way.\"\n\nMy cousin, a **novelist** who lives in the city, often visits her for inspiration. He rides his **motorbike** from the city, carrying gifts in a small **handbag** for her. \"**Hey**, Grandma!\" he shouts as he arrives, and she always pretends to be surprised.\n\nShe loves her **homeland** deeply and says she will never move. \"The air here is clean,\" she says, touching her **nose**. \"I can smell the rain before it comes.\" When I leave, she gives me bags of vegetables. \"Come back soon,\" she says. \"It's **lovely** to have you here.\""},"48":{"w":["november","nursing","outing","passport","pen","pencil","performer","photographer","potato","receptionist","rice","shooting","sing","sixteen","smoking","snow","swap","taxi","teamwork","tenth"],"title":"The Photographer's Trip","t":"Last **November**, my friend Wen, a professional **photographer**, invited me on a work trip to a mountain village. She had been hired to take pictures for a travel magazine, and I agreed to help carry her equipment. We each packed a **passport** in case we crossed the border into Laos.\n\nOn the first day, we took a **taxi** from the airport to a small hotel. The **receptionist**, a cheerful young woman who was studying **nursing**, gave us a map and recommended a village **outing** the next morning. It was the **tenth** time Wen had visited the region, but she said she always found something new.\n\nThe village was beautiful. It began to **snow** lightly as we arrived, covering the rice fields in white. Families were cooking **rice** and **potato** soup over open fires. A local **performer** began to **sing** a traditional song, and children danced around him. Wen spent hours **shooting** photos of the scenes.\n\nLater, we sat with a group of villagers in a small house. A **sixteen**-year-old girl showed us her drawings, made with a single **pencil** and a cheap **pen**. Wen offered to **swap** one of her prints for a drawing, and the girl happily agreed. A sign on the wall said \"No **Smoking**,\" though the old men ignored it.\n\nWen says that good photography depends on **teamwork**. \"You can't do it alone,\" she told me. \"People must trust you.\""},"49":{"w":["theirs","thin","thriller","thursday","twenty","unconditional","underground","unimportant","vanilla","villager","workmate","yesterday","zoo","zoom","abortion","airmail","airspace","alley","announcement","anybody"],"title":"The Village Cinema","t":"Every **Thursday** evening, the **villagers|villager** of a small town in the north gather in an old warehouse for \"film night.\" A retired teacher started the event **twenty** years ago, using a projector he bought with his own money. It has become the most popular weekly event in town.\n\nThe films vary widely. Sometimes they watch an old comedy; at other times, a **thriller** or a documentary. Last month, they watched a foreign film about a family debate on **abortion**, which led to a serious but respectful discussion. \"Nothing is **unimportant** if it makes us think,\" the teacher says.\n\nThe warehouse is at the end of a narrow **alley**, beside an old **underground** car park. Inside, the seats are simple wooden benches, and a **thin** curtain hides the projector. Before each film, the teacher makes a short **announcement** about upcoming events. **Anybody** can come; entry is free.\n\nChildren love the evening. They eat **vanilla** ice cream and laugh loudly at cartoons about animals at the **zoo**. One boy likes to **zoom** in on pictures with his father's phone during breaks. His mother, a former **workmate** of the teacher, says the event gives the village a sense of community.\n\nThe teacher's love for film is **unconditional**. He still receives letters by **airmail** from a film club in Europe that sends him old movies. **Yesterday**, a plane flew low over the village, entering restricted **airspace**, and the noise interrupted the film. Everyone laughed. \"The seats are ours,\" the teacher said, \"but the sky is **theirs**.\""},"50":{"w":["apron","armchair","athletics","attentively","awesome","backache","bakery","ball","ballpoint","barbecue","barbershop","bathrobe","bathtub","battleground","beancurd","bedclothes","bedroom","beehive","bingo","biochemistry"],"title":"Saturday at Uncle Ma's House","t":"My uncle Ma is the most relaxed man I know. Every Saturday, he wakes up late in his **bedroom**, changes the **bedclothes**, puts on his **bathrobe** and sits in his old **armchair** with a cup of tea. \"Weekends are for rest,\" he says.\n\nAfter breakfast, he walks to the **bakery** on the corner to buy bread, then visits the **barbershop** next door for a haircut and a chat. The barber, an old friend, used to study **biochemistry** before he took over his father's shop. They argue about football and politics, and the shop becomes a friendly **battleground** of opinions.\n\nIn the afternoon, my uncle cooks. He wears a red **apron** and makes his famous fried **beancurd** with garlic. His garden has a small **beehive**, and he uses the honey in his sauces. In summer, he holds a **barbecue** for the neighbours, and the children play **ball** in the yard. \"This is **awesome**!\" my cousin always shouts.\n\nMy uncle once loved **athletics** and ran marathons, but now he suffers from **backache**. His doctor told him to relax in a warm **bathtub** every evening. He listens **attentively** to the doctor's advice, though he rarely follows it.\n\nOn Saturday evenings, he plays **bingo** with friends at the community centre. He keeps score with an old **ballpoint** pen that he has used for twenty years. \"Life is simple,\" he says. \"Enjoy the small things.\""},"51":{"w":["birdcage","blackboard","boating","bookcase","bookmark","bookshelf","bookshop","bored","botanical","bowling","boxing","breathless","brochure","brother","brunch","bun","bungalow","businesswoman","bye","cab"],"title":"A Weekend at the Lake","t":"Last month, my **brother** and I spent a weekend at a lake outside the city. We stayed in a small wooden **bungalow** owned by a friendly **businesswoman** who also ran a **bookshop** in town. Inside, every wall had a **bookshelf**, and a tall **bookcase** stood beside the fireplace.\n\nOn Saturday morning, we had **brunch** at a café by the water: coffee, eggs and a sweet **bun**. Then we went **boating** on the lake. My brother rowed so fast that he was soon **breathless**, and I laughed at him. In the afternoon, we visited a **botanical** garden, where we picked up a **brochure** about rare plants.\n\nMy brother is easily **bored**, so in the evening we went **bowling** in the nearby town. The bowling alley also had a small **boxing** club, and we watched a young boxer train. On the way back, we passed an old shop selling antiques, including an empty **birdcage** and an old school **blackboard**.\n\nBefore bed, I read a novel I had bought at the bookshop. I used a postcard as a **bookmark**. On Sunday, we took a **cab** to the train station. The owner of the bungalow waved and said, \"**Bye**! Come back soon!\" We promised we would."},"52":{"w":["caption","chairwoman","changeable","chant","clone","cloudy","comma","cooker","coral","cordless","courtyard","cowboy","crayon","cyclist","darkness","dirty","disgusting","doll","downtown","dumpling"],"title":"Growing Up Downtown","t":"I grew up in an old house with a **courtyard** in the **downtown** area of a busy city. My grandmother lived with us, and she made the best **dumplings|dumpling** in the neighbourhood. She cooked them on an old gas **cooker** that was older than my father.\n\nAs a child, I spent hours in the courtyard. I drew pictures with a **crayon** on the stone floor and played with my **doll**. On **cloudy** days, my grandmother told me stories about a **cowboy** who travelled across America, and I imagined galloping through the prairie. The weather was **changeable**, and we often ran inside when the rain began.\n\nOur street was always busy. Every morning, a **cyclist** delivered newspapers, and children would **chant** rhymes as they walked to school. Next door lived a woman who later became **chairwoman** of a local business association. She wrote **captions|caption** for photographs in the newspaper and taught me how to use a **comma** correctly.\n\nNot everything was pleasant. The alley behind our house was **dirty**, and in summer the smell from the rubbish bins was **disgusting**. At night, the **darkness** frightened me, and I kept a light on. My father bought us a **cordless** phone, the first in our street, which seemed like magic.\n\nToday, the old house is gone, replaced by a modern shopping centre. In its lobby there is an aquarium with **coral** and tropical fish. Sometimes I joke that the city has made a **clone** of every other city in the world, but my memories of that courtyard remain unique."},"53":{"w":["dustbin","dusty","dvd","ear","eastwards","eggplant","eighth","eraser","eyewitness","fingernail","flaming","flashlight","foggy","fourteen","franc","freeway","fried","gale","giraffe","glasshouse"],"title":"The Foggy Night","t":"It was the **eighth** of November, a cold and **foggy** night, when my car broke down on the **freeway** about **fourteen** kilometres from home. A strong **gale** was blowing, and I could barely see the road in front of me. I took out a **flashlight** and stepped outside.\n\nThe road was quiet. I walked **eastwards** along the shoulder, hoping to find help. My **ear** hurt from the cold wind, and my **fingernail** broke as I tried to open the bonnet. Finally, I saw lights from a small farm and knocked on the door.\n\nAn old farmer opened it. He invited me into his **dusty** kitchen, where a fire was **flaming** in the stove. His wife served me **fried** **eggplant** and rice. On the shelf were a **giraffe** statue, an old **DVD** player and a jar of foreign coins, including a French **franc** from his time as a sailor.\n\nThe farmer told me he had once been an **eyewitness** to a terrible accident on the same road. Since then, he always kept his lights on at night to help lost drivers. He also showed me his **glasshouse**, where he grew tomatoes all winter.\n\nIn the morning, his son towed my car to a garage. Before I left, I helped them carry rubbish to the **dustbin** outside and found a child's **eraser** on the ground. \"Our grandson's,\" the farmer laughed. \"He loses everything.\""},"54":{"w":["goldfish","goodbye","gram","grandchild","grandpa","granny","greengrocer","grill","gruel","gymnastics","handtruck","hardworking","headmistress","headteacher","hers","hibernate","hibernation","him","hive","hooray"],"title":"Visiting Grandpa and Granny","t":"Every winter holiday, I visit my **grandpa** and **granny** in their small village in the north. They have been married for sixty years, and I am their only **grandchild**. When I arrive, Granny shouts \"**Hooray**!\" and hugs me so tightly that I can hardly breathe.\n\nMy grandpa is a **hardworking** man who has been a farmer all his life. He used to be the village **headteacher** before he retired, and he still loves to teach. My granny was once **headmistress** of the girls' school in the next town. \"The books are **hers**,\" Grandpa says, pointing to a shelf. \"The tools are mine.\"\n\nIn winter, the village is quiet. Grandpa says that people **hibernate** like bears, staying indoors to keep warm. He explains how insects in their **hive** survive **hibernation** by staying close together. He keeps a bowl of **goldfish** by the window and feeds them every morning.\n\nGranny makes breakfast every day: a bowl of rice **gruel** and a few vegetables bought from the local **greengrocer**. She weighs each ingredient to the **gram**. On Sundays, Grandpa uses an old **grill** to cook meat, and I help him carry firewood with a **handtruck**. When I was young, I took **gymnastics** classes, and Granny still asks me to show her a cartwheel.\n\nWhen it is time to leave, Grandpa always walks me to the bus stop. \"Say **goodbye** to your parents for us,\" he says. I wave to **him** until the bus turns the corner."},"55":{"w":["hotdog","irrigate","jeep","jewelry","june","kangaroo","kilo","knock","labourer","league","leftover","lemonade","madame","mailbox","me","mend","minibus","miniskirt","motherland","motorcycle"],"title":"The Summer Market","t":"Every **June**, a small summer market opens in the square near my flat. It is one of my favourite events of the year. Farmers, craftsmen and food sellers set up stalls, and families come from all over the city. The **league** of local shopkeepers organises it, and the profits go to charity.\n\nThe market sells everything. One stall offers handmade **jewelry**, and another sells colourful **miniskirts|miniskirt** and scarves. A **labourer** who works in a nearby factory sells cakes made by his wife, and a farmer explains how he uses rainwater to **irrigate** his fields. A man in a **jeep** brings fresh fruit from the countryside, selling it by the **kilo**.\n\nChildren love the food. There are **hotdog** stands, ice cream and cold **lemonade**. My niece always asks **me** to buy her a toy **kangaroo** from a stall run by an Australian family. At the end of the day, sellers give their **leftover** food to the homeless shelter.\n\nThe organiser, an elegant French woman known as **Madame** Durand, has lived in China for twenty years. She calls China her second **motherland**. She drives a red **motorcycle** to the market every morning, and she helps sellers **mend** their tents when the wind damages them.\n\nOn the last evening, there is a concert. People arrive by **minibus** and sit on the grass. Before the music starts, my neighbour always comes to **knock** on my door to make sure I'm coming. A few days later, there is usually a thank-you card from Madame Durand in my **mailbox**."},"56":{"w":["moustache","necktie","ninety","ninth","noisily","noodle","noon","numb","offshore","oilfield","omelette","opener","ouch","outspoken","paddle","palace","pancake","paperwork","pardon","parrot"],"title":"The Oil Engineer","t":"My uncle worked for thirty years as an engineer on an **offshore** **oilfield** in the South China Sea. He spent weeks at a time on a platform far from land, surrounded only by water. \"At **noon**, the sun was so hot that my hands went **numb** from holding the hot rails,\" he remembers. \"At night, the wind was so strong that the platform shook.\"\n\nLife on the platform was simple. The workers ate **noodles|noodle**, eggs and the occasional **omelette**. My uncle kept a small **parrot** in his room, which learned to say \"**Pardon**?\" whenever someone spoke. The other workers loved it, and it became the team's lucky charm.\n\nMy uncle is **outspoken** and never afraid to say what he thinks. He once complained to the company about the endless **paperwork**, which he said took more time than real work. His manager, a strict man with a thick **moustache** and a bright **necktie**, laughed and agreed.\n\nNow he is retired and lives near the sea. He goes out in a small boat and likes to **paddle** along the coast. On Sundays, he makes **pancakes|pancake** for his grandchildren and tells them stories about the platform. \"**Ouch**!\" he shouted last week, when he burned his finger on the pan. The children laughed **noisily**.\n\nLast spring, he visited the old **palace** in Beijing for the **ninth** time. He plans to live to **ninety**, and he jokes that the secret is a cold drink every evening, which is why his bottle **opener** is the most important tool in his kitchen."},"57":{"w":["pence","pianist","playmate","playroom","pleased","policeman","popcorn","pork","postbox","postcard","postcode","preview","printing","punctuate","punctuation","pyramid","quake","raincoat","rainfall","receipt"],"title":"The Old Post Office","t":"In the centre of my hometown stands an old post office with a red **postbox** outside. When I was a child, I went there every week with my grandmother to buy stamps and send a **postcard** to my aunt in England. The clerk, a kind woman, always gave me a sweet and said she was **pleased** to see me.\n\nThe post office was also the town's meeting place. A **policeman** often stopped there for tea, and an old **pianist** played in the café next door. My **playmate** and I sat in a small **playroom** at the back while the adults talked. We ate **popcorn** and read comics.\n\nToday, the post office has changed. People now type a **postcode** into their phones instead of writing letters, and the old **printing** machine has been replaced by computers. But the building has been kept as a museum. Visitors can **preview** an exhibition of old letters online before they visit.\n\nOne room shows letters written during the great **quake** of 1976, when thousands of people wrote to their families. A schoolteacher explains how the letters were written in a hurry, with little **punctuation**. \"People didn't **punctuate** their sentences,\" she says, \"because every second mattered.\"\n\nThe museum café sells **pork** buns and tea. For fun, the menu also shows the prices in British **pence**, just as my aunt's old letters from England did. Outside, I watched a man in a yellow **raincoat** checking the **rainfall** gauge. When I paid for my tea, the waiter handed me a **receipt** shaped like a stamp, and a small paper **pyramid** for my table number."},"58":{"w":["redirect","reuse","reviewer","rewind","rewrite","rhyme","rugby","salesgirl","saleswoman","salty","saturday","schoolbag","schoolmate","seagull","seashell","seaweed","secondhand","semicircle","seventh","sharpener"],"title":"By the Sea","t":"On the **seventh** day of the summer holiday, my **schoolmate** Lina and I took a bus to the sea. It was a sunny **Saturday**, and the beach was full of families. We carried our snacks in a **schoolbag** and found a quiet place near the rocks.\n\nWe spent the morning collecting **seashells|seashell** and watching **seagulls|seagull** fly over the water. The air was **salty**, and the rocks were covered in green **seaweed**. Lina drew a **semicircle** in the sand and asked me to make a sandcastle inside it.\n\nIn the afternoon, we watched a game of **rugby** on the beach. A **saleswoman** walked along the sand selling ice cream, and a young **salesgirl** sold cheap **secondhand** sunglasses. I bought a pair for my little brother.\n\nLina wants to be a writer. She brought a notebook and a pencil **sharpener**, and she wrote a short poem about the sea. \"Does it **rhyme**?\" I asked. \"Not yet,\" she said. \"I'll **rewrite** it tonight.\" She likes to **rewind** her favourite films to study how characters talk. Her teacher, a strict **reviewer**, always tells her to **redirect** her energy into reading.\n\nBefore we left, we collected our rubbish so that we could **reuse** the bags. The sun was setting, and the sea looked like gold."},"59":{"w":["shaver","sheep","shirt","shorts","sideway","sixteenth","skateboard","skillfully","skirt","smelly","smoker","sneaker","snowball","snowman","socket","softball","spoonful","squid","stairs","stomachache"],"title":"Winter Fun","t":"When I was a child, winter was my favourite season. As soon as the first snow fell, my brother and I would rush outside in our boots and coats to build a **snowman**. We gave him a carrot nose and a red scarf, and we always started a **snowball** fight afterwards.\n\nMy mother insisted we dress warmly. She made me wear a thick **shirt** under my coat and long trousers instead of **shorts**. My sister, who loved fashion, wanted to wear a **skirt** in the snow, but my mother refused. \"You'll get a **stomachache** from the cold,\" she warned.\n\nOur neighbour, an old farmer, kept **sheep** in a field behind our house. In winter, he carried hay to them every morning. He was a heavy **smoker**, and his clothes were always **smelly**, but he was kind. He taught my brother to ride a **skateboard** along the frozen path, moving **skillfully** around the ice.\n\nAt lunch, my mother made **squid** soup and gave each of us a **spoonful** of honey. In the evening, my father plugged the heater into the old **socket** in the living room. We climbed the **stairs** to bed, tired and happy.\n\nOn the **sixteenth** of January, my birthday, my parents gave me a pair of new **sneakers|sneaker** and a **softball**. My father, who had just received a new electric **shaver**, looked unusually smart that day. We walked to the park by a narrow **sideway** behind the houses, throwing snow at each other all the way."},"60":{"w":["stopwatch","subtraction","suite","sunburnt","sunglasses","sunny","superman","supper","swim","tasteless","teapot","thankful","them","thermos","thirsty","thirteen","thunderstorm","till","toothache","toothbrush"],"title":"A Summer Picnic","t":"Last summer, my family went on a picnic by a lake. It was a **sunny** day, and we were all **thirsty** after the long walk from the car park. My mother had packed a **thermos** of iced tea, a **teapot** for later and enough sandwiches for an army.\n\nMy little brother, who is **thirteen**, wanted to **swim** in the lake immediately. He wore his **Superman** T-shirt and a pair of **sunglasses**. My father brought a **stopwatch** to time his races. \"Ten minutes!\" my brother shouted proudly. My father pretended to do a quick **subtraction**: \"Then you've got five minutes left before lunch.\"\n\nAfter lunch, the sky grew dark, and a **thunderstorm** began. We ran to a small café and waited **till** the rain stopped. The café served a **tasteless** cake, but we were **thankful** for the shelter. My mother complained that her shoulders were **sunburnt**.\n\nThat evening, we stayed in a hotel **suite** near the lake. We ate a simple **supper** of noodles and vegetables. My brother had a **toothache**, so my mother gave him a new **toothbrush** and told him to brush gently.\n\nBefore bed, my father told us a story. \"Was it a good day?\" he asked. \"Yes,\" we told **them**. \"The best.\""},"61":{"w":["toothpaste","tournament","translator","twelfth","twelve","unbelievable","uncle","undivided","unmarried","unrest","untrue","us","veal","videophone","violinist","walkman","washroom","watermelon","wayside","weatherman"],"title":"My Uncle the Translator","t":"My **uncle** is a **translator** who works for an international company. He speaks five languages and travels around the world. Last year, he invited my brother and me to visit him in Spain, where he was working at a tennis **tournament**.\n\nThe trip was **unbelievable**. He showed **us** the city, took us to restaurants and introduced us to his friends. One of them was a **violinist** who played in the city orchestra; another was a **weatherman** on local television. My uncle is **unmarried**, and he says his work is his family.\n\nHe told us stories about his career. Once, during a period of political **unrest** in a foreign country, he had to translate for journalists. Some newspapers published **untrue** reports, and he worked hard to correct them. \"A translator must give **undivided** attention to every word,\" he said.\n\nIn Spain, we ate grilled **veal** and cold **watermelon**. My uncle laughed at my old **walkman**, which I had brought to listen to music. \"That's a museum piece,\" he said. He still remembers using an early **videophone** in the 1990s.\n\nOn the **twelfth** day, we drove into the countryside and stopped at a **wayside** café. The **washroom** was tiny, and I forgot my **toothpaste** at the hotel. We stayed for **twelve** days in total, and it was the best holiday of my life."},"62":{"w":["westwards","willingly","windbreaker","woo","worn","wounded","yourselves","yummy","zip","zipper"],"title":"Heading West","t":"Last autumn, my friends and I drove **westwards** across the desert. We packed warm jackets, and I wore my old **windbreaker**, **worn** at the elbows but still warm. My friend's bag broke on the first day because the **zipper** stuck, and we had to **zip** it closed with tape.\n\nOn the way, we helped a **wounded** dog by the roadside and took it to a vet. My friends **willingly** shared their food with it. At night, we cooked noodles over a fire. \"**Yummy**!\" said my friend, though the noodles were half-cooked. The trip helped me **woo** back my love of travel. \"Enjoy **yourselves**,\" the vet had told us, and we did."}},"cet4":{"1":{"w":["abandon","ability","abnormal","aboard","absence","absent","absolute","absolutely","absorb","abstract","abundant","abuse","academic","academy","accelerate","acceleration","accent","accept","acceptable","acceptance"],"title":"A Mathematician Learns Chinese","t":"When Maria arrived in Shanghai to study at a language **academy**, she nearly decided to **abandon** the whole plan within a month. Her **academic** background was in mathematics, a world of **abstract** symbols and **absolute** rules, and the chaos of learning Chinese felt deeply **abnormal** to her. Every time she opened her mouth, her **accent** gave her away.\n\nHer teachers, however, refused to **accept** her pessimism. They explained that language learning rarely follows a straight line; progress tends to **accelerate** after a long, frustrating plateau. To prove the point, one teacher showed her a graph of a former student's test scores, which rose slowly for months and then climbed sharply, like a car gaining **acceleration** on an open road.\n\nMaria began to change her habits. She stopped treating mistakes as signs of a lack of **ability** and started to **absorb** the language from everything around her: street signs, conversations overheard **aboard** the metro, menus and songs. Opportunities to practise were **abundant**, she realised; she had simply been too afraid to use them. She also learned that an occasional **absence** from class was **acceptable**, but that being mentally **absent** while sitting in the room was far worse.\n\nNot everything was pleasant. Online, she once faced a wave of **abuse** from strangers who mocked her pronunciation. But her classmates rallied around her, and the experience taught her that **acceptance** in a new culture is earned slowly, through patience rather than perfection. A year later, she was **absolutely** certain she had made the right choice."},"2":{"w":["access","accessory","accident","accidental","accommodate","accommodation","accompany","accomplish","accord","accordance","accordingly","account","accumulate","accuracy","accurate","accuse","accustom","accustomed","ache","achieve"],"title":"An Accidental Career","t":"Few people plan to become hotel managers. For Chen Hao, it was entirely **accidental**. After a minor car **accident** forced him to give up his job as a delivery driver, he took a temporary position at the front desk of a small guesthouse, simply because it was the only work available. He did not expect to stay more than a month.\n\nYet he quickly became **accustomed** to the rhythm of the place. He learned how to **accommodate** guests with very different needs, from backpackers looking for cheap **accommodation** to elderly couples who wanted quiet rooms with easy **access** to the lift. He would **accompany** nervous first-time travellers to the bus station and help them **accomplish** small tasks, such as buying tickets or finding a pharmacy for a stubborn **ache**. Staff and guests were in complete **accord** that he was the friendliest person in the building.\n\nHis employer noticed his talent. In **accordance** with the guesthouse's policy of promoting from within, Chen was given more responsibility, and his salary rose **accordingly**. He began to **accumulate** knowledge about everything from booking systems to local history. Guests praised the **accuracy** of his advice; his directions were always **accurate**, and his restaurant suggestions rarely disappointed.\n\nNot every day was easy. Once a guest tried to **accuse** him of stealing a gold **accessory** that later turned up in her own handbag. Chen remained calm and polite, and the woman apologised. Over time he learned to **accustom** himself to such pressures. Ten years later he runs his own hotel. His greatest success, he says, was not something he planned to **achieve**; it was learning to say yes when life offered him an unexpected path. \"In my **account** of my life,\" he jokes, \"the accident is the hero.\""},"3":{"w":["achievement","acid","acknowledge","acquaintance","acquire","acquisition","acre","act","action","active","activity","actor","actress","actual","actually","acute","ad","adapt","add","addition"],"title":"A Second Life on Stage","t":"At forty-two, after twenty years in accounting, Wang Lin decided to **act** on a dream she had kept secret since childhood: she wanted to become an **actress**. Her friends thought it was a joke. An old **acquaintance** even asked whether she was having a midlife crisis.\n\nShe began by taking evening classes at a community theatre. The teacher, a retired **actor**, told the students that acting is not about pretending but about understanding. \"Every **action** on stage must have a reason,\" he said. Wang Lin found this surprisingly familiar; in accounting, too, every number had to be explained.\n\nThe work was demanding. She had to **acquire** new skills, from controlling her voice to moving naturally in front of an audience, and to **adapt** to a world in which emotion mattered more than precision. Her **acquisition** of these skills was slow at first, but her teacher **acknowledged|acknowledge** that she was one of the most **active** students in the group, always volunteering for extra rehearsals and every **activity** the theatre organised.\n\nHer first role was small: a farmer's wife in a play about a family who loses an **acre** of land to a factory. The play relied on a single sharp image to make its point, a jar of **acid** rainwater placed in the centre of the stage. When the curtain fell, the applause was **actually** louder than she had expected, and a local newspaper ran a small **ad** praising the production. For her, that applause was an **achievement** worth more than any promotion.\n\nHer **actual** income from acting is still tiny, so she keeps a part-time job. But she says the experience has given her something money cannot **add**: an **acute** awareness of how other people feel. In **addition**, she has discovered that it is never too late to begin a second life."},"4":{"w":["additional","address","adequate","adhere","adjective","adjust","administration","admire","admission","admit","adopt","adult","advance","advanced","advantage","adventure","adverb","advertise","advertisement","advice"],"title":"Learning to Write at University","t":"When I received my letter of **admission** to university, I was thrilled, but I soon had to **admit** that my English writing was far from **adequate**. My grammar was weak, and I still could not tell an **adjective** from an **adverb** without thinking hard.\n\nThe university **administration** offered **additional** classes for first-year students, and I signed up immediately. Our teacher, Professor Liu, was an energetic woman whom everyone came to **admire**. On the first day she gave us one piece of **advice**: \"**Adopt** the habits of good writers, not just their words.\" She asked us to read widely and to **adhere** to a simple rule: write something every day, even if it was only a paragraph.\n\nShe also encouraged us to treat writing as an **adventure**. One week she asked us to **advertise** an imaginary product; another week she made us write a formal **address** welcoming foreign visitors to the campus. I still remember the **advertisement** I wrote for a pair of shoes that could walk by themselves. It was ridiculous, but it taught me how much a single well-chosen word can change.\n\nSlowly, I began to **adjust** to university life. I discovered that being an **adult** learner had one great **advantage**: I could choose to learn for myself rather than for exams. By the end of the year my writing had made real **advances|advance**, and Professor Liu invited me to join her **advanced** seminar. It was the first time I had felt truly proud of my English."},"5":{"w":["advisable","advise","advocate","affair","affect","affection","affluent","afford","african","against","agency","agent","aggressive","agony","agriculture","aid","aim","aircraft","airline","airplane"],"title":"Medicine by Air","t":"Every year, a small charity **agency** based in Beijing sends medical **aid** to remote villages in East Africa. Its founder, Dr. Sun, started the project after a trip that changed his life. He had grown up in an **affluent** family and had never seen real poverty until he visited an **African** village where children were dying of treatable diseases.\n\nHe describes the scene he witnessed there as one of quiet **agony**: mothers walking for hours to reach a clinic that had no medicine. \"I could not **afford** to look away,\" he says. Today the charity works with a local **agent** who organises transport, and with an **airline** that donates spare cargo space on its regular flights. When roads are impassable, a small **aircraft** carries supplies directly to the villages; some of the older villagers had never seen an **airplane** land before.\n\nThe charity's **aim** is not only to treat illness but also to strengthen local **agriculture**, since poor nutrition **affects|affect** health more than any other single factor. Volunteers teach farmers how to protect crops **against** drought and pests, and they **advise** families on how to store food safely.\n\nDr. Sun is careful not to be too **aggressive** in promoting his own ideas. \"It is **advisable** to listen first,\" he says, \"and to **advocate** solutions that local people already believe in.\" Many villagers now regard him with genuine **affection**. When asked about his work, he insists that it is not his personal **affair** but a shared responsibility. \"Everyone who can help,\" he says, \"should.\""},"6":{"w":["alarm","alcohol","alert","algebra","alike","alive","allow","alloy","alone","aloud","alphabet","alter","alternative","although","altitude","altogether","aluminum","amaze","ambition","ambulance"],"title":"The Best Failure of My Life","t":"Last summer my cousin Jun, a maths teacher who usually spends his holidays marking **algebra** homework, set himself an unusual goal: to climb a mountain more than five thousand metres high. It was a long-held **ambition**, but his family reacted with **alarm**. His mother asked whether he would really climb **alone**; his wife quietly checked how far the nearest hospital was.\n\nJun prepared carefully. He gave up **alcohol**, trained every weekend and bought lightweight equipment, including walking poles made of an **aluminum** **alloy**. He also learned that at high **altitude** even fit climbers can fall ill, so he joined a small team led by an experienced guide who would **allow** no shortcuts.\n\n**Although** the first days went well, the weather changed suddenly on the fourth morning. A strong wind made it impossible to continue, and the guide had to **alter** the plan. Instead of pushing for the summit, the team took an **alternative** route along a lower ridge. Jun was disappointed, but the guide reminded them that the mountain would still be there next year; the important thing was to come home **alive**.\n\nThat evening, a climber from another group collapsed with altitude sickness, and an **ambulance** had to be called from the valley. The incident kept everyone **alert** for the rest of the trip. Jun later said that experienced and inexperienced climbers were **alike** in one way: nobody could predict how their body would react.\n\nHe never reached the top, but the journey still managed to **amaze** him. On the final night he read **aloud** from a small notebook in which he had written every Tibetan word he had learned, in order, like a child learning the **alphabet**. \"**Altogether**,\" he said, \"this was the best failure of my life.\""},"7":{"w":["amount","ampere","amplify","amuse","analysis","analyze","ancestor","anchor","ancient","angel","anger","angle","ankle","announce","announcer","annual","ant","anticipate","anxiety","anxious"],"title":"The Voice on the Radio","t":"For more than thirty years, Mr. Kang was the most familiar voice in his province. As a radio **announcer**, he would **announce** the morning news, introduce music programmes and, once a year, host the **annual** Spring Festival concert. Listeners trusted him like an old friend; some even called him \"the **angel** of the airwaves\".\n\nHis secret, he once explained, was preparation. Before every broadcast he would **analyze** each story carefully, asking himself what listeners would **anticipate** and what might surprise them. He believed that careful **analysis** could reveal an **angle** that others had missed. \"A good news programme does not just report facts,\" he said. \"It helps people understand them.\"\n\nHe was also known for his calm voice. Even when reporting on floods or accidents, he never let **anger** or **anxiety** creep into his tone. \"If listeners hear that the **anchor** is **anxious**, they will panic,\" he said. \"My job is to **amplify** the facts, not the fear.\"\n\nOutside work, he was a modest man with simple pleasures. He liked to **amuse** his grandchildren by reading stories in funny voices, and he spent weekends studying the history of his **ancestors|ancestor**, who had lived in the region since **ancient** times. He once spent a whole afternoon watching a line of **ants|ant** carry food across his garden path, and he later described the scene on air as a lesson in teamwork.\n\nHe retired after a fall in which he broke his **ankle**, but he still receives letters from listeners, and the **amount** of mail, he jokes, has hardly changed since his last broadcast. His old studio microphone ran on only a few **amperes|ampere** of current, he likes to point out, yet it reached millions of homes."},"8":{"w":["anyhow","anyone","anyway","anywhere","apart","apartment","apologize","apology","apparatus","apparent","appeal","appear","appearance","appetite","applause","appliance","applicable","applicant","application","apply"],"title":"The Job Interview","t":"Last spring my friend Lucy decided to **apply** for a job at a company that designs kitchen **appliances|appliance**. She had studied engineering but had little real experience, and she was worried that her **application** would be rejected **anyway**.\n\nThe company received more than five hundred **applicants|applicant**. Lucy was invited to an interview, and on the day she was so nervous that she had no **appetite** for breakfast. She arrived early and tried to look calm in the waiting room, though it was **apparent** to **anyone** watching that she was shaking.\n\nThe interview did not begin well. She accidentally knocked over a glass of water and had to **apologize** twice. But then something changed. The interviewers asked her to explain a strange piece of **apparatus** sitting on the table, a prototype toaster with sensors that could detect when bread was about to burn. Lucy forgot her nerves and began to describe how the design could be improved. Her ideas were simple but clearly **applicable** to real products.\n\nHer **appearance** might not have been perfect, but her enthusiasm made a strong **appeal** to the panel. One of them later told her that her honest **apology** had actually made a good impression; people who never make mistakes, he said, rarely learn much.\n\nA week later she got the job. She now lives in a small **apartment** near the office and says she could not imagine working **anywhere** else. Her colleagues **appear** to agree: at the end of her first project, the team gave her a round of **applause**. \"**Anyhow**,\" she says, laughing, \"I keep my drinks well **apart** from the prototypes these days.\""},"9":{"w":["appoint","appointment","appreciate","approach","appropriate","approval","approve","approximate","approximately","arbitrary","architect","architecture","area","argue","argument","arise","arithmetic","army","arouse","arrange"],"title":"Designing the New Library","t":"When the city council decided to build a new public library, it held a competition and invited architects from around the country to submit designs. The council would **appoint** a panel of judges, and the winning **architect** would be announced within six months.\n\nThe competition soon began to **arouse** strong feelings. Some residents wanted a modern glass building; others **argued|argue** that the city's traditional **architecture** should be respected. Public meetings were held in every **area** of the city, and each **argument** was carefully recorded. One elderly man complained that the choice of judges was **arbitrary**, but the council explained that each judge had been selected for a specific skill.\n\nThe winning design came from a young woman named Gao Yan. Her **approach** was to combine old and new: a building of local stone with large windows that let in natural light. Before construction could begin, she needed **approval** from the planning department, which had to **approve** every detail, from fire exits to the **approximate** number of books each floor could hold. The answer, she worked out with simple **arithmetic**, was **approximately** two hundred thousand.\n\nProblems continued to **arise** during construction. At one point heavy rain flooded the site, and soldiers from a nearby **army** base were sent to help pump out the water. Gao Yan had to **arrange** extra meetings almost every day.\n\nWhen the library finally opened, she said she was proud that the building felt **appropriate** to the city's history. She told reporters that she would always **appreciate** the residents who had shared their ideas, and she still keeps a notebook listing every **appointment** she had during those two busy years."},"10":{"w":["arrangement","arrest","arrival","arrow","art","article","artificial","artist","artistic","ascribe","ash","ashamed","aside","asleep","aspect","assemble","assembly","assess","assign","assignment"],"title":"Art from the Streets","t":"In our final year at secondary school, our **art** teacher gave the class an unusual **assignment**: each of us had to create a work of art from objects found on the street. She said she would **assess** our projects not on beauty but on imagination, and she would **assign** each student a different part of the city.\n\nMy **arrival** in my area, an old industrial district, was not encouraging. Most of the factories had closed, and the ground was covered with **ash** from a recent fire. At first I felt almost **ashamed** of how little I could find. But I decided to put my disappointment **aside** and look more carefully. Soon I had collected rusty nails, broken glass, a bicycle chain and a wooden **arrow** that had once been part of a sign.\n\nBack at school I tried to **assemble** these objects into a sculpture. I worked late into the night, and more than once my mother found me **asleep** on the floor of the garage. The **arrangement** I finally chose was simple: the arrow pointed upward from a nest of nails, as if a small factory were trying to rise again.\n\nWhen the projects were displayed at a school **assembly**, a local journalist wrote an **article** about them. One sculpture, made from **artificial** flowers and plastic bottles, caught the eye of a professional **artist**, who praised its **artistic** courage. My own piece did not win, but the journalist described it in her report, and the image of the rising arrow seemed to **arrest** the attention of readers.\n\nMany years later, I still think about that assignment, and I **ascribe** much of my interest in design to that teacher. She taught us that every **aspect** of a city, even its ruins, can tell a story."},"11":{"w":["assist","assistant","associate","association","assume","assumption","assure","astonish","astronaut","athlete","atmosphere","atmospheric","atom","atomic","attach","attack","attain","attempt","attend","attention"],"title":"A Lecture from Space","t":"When the space agency announced that a retired **astronaut** would give a public lecture at our university, the hall filled up within minutes. I was lucky: as a student **assistant** in the physics department, I had been asked to **assist** with the event, which meant I could **attend** for free.\n\nThe astronaut, a calm woman in her sixties, began by describing her training. Most people, she said, **assume** that astronauts are simply exceptional **athletes|athlete**, but that **assumption** is only half true. Physical fitness matters, but so does the ability to stay calm under pressure. She described an **attempt** to dock with a space station that nearly failed, and how her team managed to **attain** their goal by working slowly and checking every step.\n\nShe also talked about the Earth's **atmosphere**. Seen from orbit, she said, it looks astonishingly thin, like the skin of an apple. Many of the **atmospheric** changes that scientists worry about, from pollution to rising temperatures, are visible from space. She explained how the movement of every **atom** of oxygen and carbon is linked to life on the planet, and how **atomic** clocks on satellites help us measure these processes.\n\nSome students were surprised to learn that she had once been the target of an online **attack** after speaking out about climate change. She said she had learned not to **attach** too much importance to such comments. \"I can **assure** you,\" she told us, \"that the view from space makes small quarrels look very small indeed.\"\n\nAt the end, the president of the student science **association** thanked her and asked how ordinary people could help. She smiled and said that we should pay **attention** to the world around us and **associate** our daily choices with their long-term effects. Her honesty seemed to **astonish** the hall, and everyone rose to applaud."},"12":{"w":["attentive","attitude","attract","attraction","attractive","attribute","audience","audio","aural","author","authority","auto","autobiography","automatic","automatically","automation","automobile","auxiliary","available","avenue"],"title":"An Autobiography at Ninety","t":"Last year an elderly **author** in our city published her **autobiography** at the age of ninety. The book describes her childhood on a farm, her career as an engineer in the **automobile** industry and her late discovery of writing. To everyone's surprise, it became the most **attractive** book of the season for young readers.\n\nPart of its **attraction** lies in her **attitude**. She writes about failures and losses without bitterness, and she does not **attribute** her success to talent but to stubborn hard work. She also describes the arrival of **automation** in the factories where she worked, when machines began to perform **automatically** tasks that had once required dozens of workers. She admits that the change was painful for many families, but she argues that people adapted more quickly than anyone expected.\n\nWhen the publisher released an **audio** version of the book, read by the author herself, it reached an even wider **audience**. Many listeners said that her voice made the story more vivid; one reviewer described the recording as \"a rare **aural** pleasure\". Copies are now **available** in libraries across the country.\n\nHer success has made her a minor local **authority** on the history of the region. Schools invite her to speak, and she always asks for an **attentive** audience; if students look at their phones, she stops and waits. She refuses to use an **automatic** translation app when answering letters from abroad, preferring to look up each word in a dictionary.\n\nShe still drives an old **auto** she has owned for forty years, and she keeps an **auxiliary** battery in the boot in case it fails. Last month the city renamed a small street after her; the sign now reads \"Author's **Avenue**\". Her book continues to **attract** new readers every week."},"13":{"w":["average","aviation","avoid","await","awake","award","aware","awful","awfully","awkward","ax","axis","bachelor","background","backward","bacteria","badly","badminton","baggage","bake"],"title":"The Awkward Engineer","t":"My uncle is a forty-year-old **bachelor** who works as an engineer in the **aviation** industry. He is brilliant at mathematics; he can explain why a plane rotates around its vertical **axis** or how wind affects its flight. But in everyday life he is famously **awkward**.\n\nLast summer he decided to take up **badminton**, partly to lose weight and partly, I suspect, to meet new people. His first lesson went **badly**. He kept stepping **backward** at the wrong moment, missed almost every shot and finally fell over his own racket. \"That was **awful**,\" he admitted afterwards, laughing. \"I'm **awfully** glad nobody filmed it.\"\n\nBut he did not give up. He was **aware** that most beginners struggle, and he refused to **avoid** the courts just because he was embarrassed. He studied videos, practised every evening and gradually improved. His coach, who had a **background** in professional sport, said that his progress was well above **average** for someone his age.\n\nHe also changed other habits. He began to **bake** his own bread instead of buying cakes, and he learned that the **bacteria** in fermented foods could be good for his health. After a minor accident, he even replaced the old **ax** he used to chop firewood at his country cottage with a safer saw.\n\nSix months later, he entered a local amateur tournament. He did not win, but he received a small **award** for \"most improved player\". When he collected his **baggage** at the airport after a business trip the next week, he was still smiling about it. He told me that he used to lie **awake** at night worrying about failure; now he lies awake planning his next match. \"I'll **await** next year's tournament,\" he said, \"with my racket ready.\""},"14":{"w":["balance","balcony","balloon","ban","band","bang","banner","bar","barber","bare","barely","bargain","bark","barn","barrel","barrier","base","baseball","basic","basically"],"title":"Music in the Barn","t":"Every summer our village holds a music festival in an old wooden **barn** at the edge of the fields. It began twenty years ago, when a local **band** needed somewhere to rehearse and a farmer offered them his empty building. Today it attracts hundreds of visitors.\n\nThis year the organisers faced a problem. The regional government announced a **ban** on large outdoor events because of the risk of fire, and the festival committee had to **balance** safety against tradition. In the end they moved everything indoors and built a wooden **barrier** around the stage to keep the crowd at a safe distance.\n\nOn the opening night, the barn was decorated with a red **banner** and dozens of coloured **balloons|balloon**. The village **barber**, who plays the drums, opened the concert with a loud **bang** that made a dog outside begin to **bark**. A small **bar** in the corner sold lemonade and local beer from a wooden **barrel**, and children ran around in **bare** feet on the straw-covered floor.\n\nAt a **basic** level, the festival is simply a party, but for many villagers it means much more. It is the **base** of the community's identity, the one night of the year when everyone gathers in one place. \"**Basically**,\" said the farmer's grandson, \"it is the village's way of saying we are still here.\"\n\nTickets are cheap, a real **bargain** compared with city concerts, and the money is used to repair the barn. This year the committee **barely** covered its costs, but nobody complained. After the last song, a group of teenagers organised a game of **baseball** in the field under the stars, and the older guests watched from the **balcony** of the farmhouse until midnight."},"15":{"w":["basin","basis","bat","bath","bathe","bathroom","battery","battle","bay","beach","beam","bean","beard","beast","beauty","bee","beforehand","beg","beggar","beginner"],"title":"Summers by the Bay","t":"Every summer, my grandfather rents a small house by a quiet **bay** in the south. He has been going there for fifty years, and he still remembers the first time he saw the **beach**, a long curve of white sand framed by green hills.\n\nHe likes to prepare everything **beforehand**. Weeks before the trip he checks the **battery** in his old radio, buys a sack of dried **beans|bean** and packs a metal **basin** for washing fish. My grandmother laughs at him, but she admits that his planning has saved them more than once.\n\nLife at the bay is simple. In the morning he takes a cold **bath** in the sea; in the evening he likes to **bathe** his tired feet in warm water. The little cottage has no modern **bathroom**, only a shower behind the kitchen. At night a **bat** sometimes flies through the open window, and on calm evenings the **beam** of the lighthouse sweeps slowly across the water.\n\nHe is a gentle man with a long white **beard**, and children on the beach often follow him, asking for stories. His favourite is about a **beast** that supposedly lives in the deep water, a monster that, he admits, he invented to stop his own children from swimming too far out. Another tells of the **battle** fought between local fishermen and a great storm in 1962.\n\nHe also loves to teach. Last year he showed a **beginner** how to catch fish with a simple line, and he patiently explained how a **bee** finds its way back to the hive. Once, when a **beggar** came to the door, he invited the man in for dinner. \"Nobody should have to **beg** for food in a place of such **beauty**,\" he said. That evening, he insists, was the **basis** of a lasting friendship."},"16":{"w":["beginning","behalf","behave","behavior","being","belief","belong","beloved","belt","bench","bend","beneath","beneficial","benefit","berry","besides","bet","betray","beyond","bible"],"title":"The Bench Under the Cherry Tree","t":"In the park near my grandmother's home there is an old wooden **bench** **beneath** a cherry tree. Since the **beginning** of her retirement, she has sat there every morning, feeding the birds and watching the world go by. She says it is the most **beneficial** habit she has ever formed.\n\nThe bench has a history. A small brass plate on its back reads \"In memory of my **beloved** wife\", followed by a line from the **Bible**. My grandmother never knew the man who placed it there, but she feels that the bench now **belongs|belong** to her as much as to anyone.\n\nShe is a keen observer of human **behavior**. She notices how children **behave** when they think nobody is watching, how teenagers **bend** over their phones and how old men argue about football. She once made a **bet** with a neighbour about whether a shy young couple would ever speak to each other; she won, and the neighbour paid her with a jar of wild **berry** jam.\n\nHer **belief** is simple: every human **being** deserves a little kindness. She has helped lost tourists, comforted a crying child and once fixed a stranger's broken **belt** with a piece of string. She never expects thanks, and she says the **benefit** to herself is greater than any reward.\n\n**Besides** her park friends, she has very few visitors, but she does not feel lonely. \"Some people will **betray** your trust,\" she told me once, \"but most people are better than you expect.\" Last year, on **behalf** of the neighbourhood, the local council presented her with a small certificate. She keeps it in a drawer, but she says the real reward lies far **beyond** any piece of paper."},"17":{"w":["bicycle","bill","billion","bind","biology","birth","biscuit","bite","bitter","bitterly","blade","blame","blank","blanket","blast","blaze","bleed","blend","bless","blind"],"title":"Mr. Han's Lessons","t":"Mr. Han, my old **biology** teacher, rode the same **bicycle** to school for thirty years. He was a quiet man with a sharp sense of humour, and he liked to begin each lesson with a strange fact. \"The human body contains about thirty-seven trillion cells,\" he once announced. \"Not a million, not a **billion**, but trillions. Remember that the next time you feel small.\"\n\nHe believed that science should be felt, not just learned. When we studied plants, he showed us how to **blend** soil, sand and water to give seeds the best start, and he made each of us slice a leaf with a razor **blade** and examine it under a microscope. When we studied the senses, he asked us to taste a **bitter** herb and compare it with a sweet **biscuit**. On one winter morning, when the wind blew **bitterly**, he took us outside to show us how small animals survive under a **blanket** of snow.\n\nHe never liked to **blame** students for their mistakes. If someone handed in a **blank** sheet of paper, he would simply sit down with them and ask what they had not understood. Once, during a lesson on the circulation of blood, a boy cut his finger and began to **bleed**; Mr. Han calmly bandaged it and turned the accident into a lesson.\n\nHis own life was not easy. A terrible **blaze** caused by an electrical fault destroyed his house and his books, and the **blast** of an exploding gas bottle damaged his hearing. Later his eyesight weakened, and he feared he would go **blind**. Yet he never seemed resentful. He would often say that we should **bless** every day we are given.\n\nWhen he retired, our class paid the **bill** for a small party. He told us that teaching was the thread that would always **bind** us together, and that a teacher's words, like a mosquito's **bite**, can leave a mark long after the moment has passed. When my daughter's **birth** was announced last year, one of the first messages I received came from him."},"18":{"w":["blood","bloom","blossom","board","boast","boil","bold","bolt","bomb","bond","bone","boost","boot","booth","border","bore","born","bosom","boss","bottom"],"title":"The Border Market","t":"Every spring, when the apple trees **bloom** and the hills are covered in pink **blossom**, a small market opens near the **border** between two mountain provinces. Farmers from both sides set up a **booth** or two and sell everything from honey to leather **boots|boot**.\n\nThe market's **boss** is a cheerful woman named Mrs. Lei, who was **born** in the village and has organised the event for twenty years. She likes to **boast** that it is the oldest market in the region, and she may well be right. Its history is written on an old wooden **board** at the entrance, which lists every year the market has been held since 1950.\n\nMrs. Lei is **bold** in her ideas. She persuaded the local government to **boost** the market's budget so that farmers could build new stalls, and she started a programme to strengthen the **bond** between families on both sides of the border, who share a common history and, in many cases, the same **blood**. \"Politics may divide maps,\" she says, \"but not people.\"\n\nThe food is the main attraction. Visitors queue for soup made by slowly cooking a pig's **bone** for hours, letting the pot **boil** gently until the broth turns white. At the **bottom** of the hill, an old man sells hand-forged locks and an iron **bolt** that he swears will last a hundred years.\n\nThe market has had its dark moments. During a war in the last century, a **bomb** destroyed part of the village, and many families left. Some young people today find the market old-fashioned and say it can **bore** them within an hour. But for the older villagers, it is something they hold close to their **bosom**, a place full of shared memories."},"19":{"w":["bough","bounce","bound","boundary","bow","bowl","brain","brake","branch","brand","brandy","brass","brave","breadth","breast","breath","breathe","breed","breeze","brick"],"title":"The Band in the Square","t":"Every Sunday afternoon, a small **brass** band plays under the old oak tree in our town square. The musicians sit on folding chairs, and a gentle **breeze** carries the music across the fountain to the cafés on the other side. Children dance, dogs **bounce** around the square, and people of every age stop to listen.\n\nThe band was founded fifty years ago by a retired engineer who wanted to **breed** a love of music in the town. Its first rehearsals took place in a **brick** warehouse near the river. Today it has thirty members, from teenagers to a ninety-year-old trumpeter who still has the **breath** to play for two hours. \"When you play the trumpet, you learn to **breathe** properly,\" he says. \"It's better than any medicine.\"\n\nThe conductor, a **brave** young woman who took over three years ago, has widened the **breadth** of the band's music. Besides traditional marches, they now play film music, jazz and even pop songs. She says that music has no **boundary** and that every **branch** of it deserves respect. At the end of each concert, she turns to the audience and takes a deep **bow**.\n\nNot everyone welcomed the changes at first. Some older members felt **bound** by tradition and complained that the new pieces were too hard on the **brain**. But the crowds grew larger every month. A local coffee **brand** now sponsors the concerts, and a restaurant offers a free **bowl** of soup to every musician after the show.\n\nOn cold days, the old trumpeter keeps a small flask of **brandy** in his coat pocket, \"for the lungs,\" he insists. Once, a heavy **bough** from the oak tree fell during a storm and narrowly missed the drummer. Nobody was hurt. The drummer, who arrives every week on an old bicycle with a squeaking **brake**, joked that he had never moved so fast in his life. Then he placed a hand on his **breast** and laughed."},"20":{"w":["brief","brighten","brilliant","brim","brisk","bristle","brittle","broad","broadcast","broken","bronze","brood","brook","broom","brow","bruise","brute","bubble","bucket","bud"],"title":"Mornings by the Brook","t":"Every morning, my grandfather takes a **brisk** walk to the **brook** at the bottom of our garden. He carries a wooden **bucket** to collect water for his vegetables and a small **broom** to sweep the path. The walk is **brief**, no more than ten minutes, but he says it is the best part of his day.\n\nIn spring he watches for the first **bud** on the willow trees. He likes to point out the tiny **bubbles|bubble** rising from the stones on the bed of the stream, and the dragonflies whose wings shine like thin **bronze** plates in the sunlight. On a **brilliant** sunny day, the light seems to **brighten** everything, even his mood.\n\nHe is not a sentimental man. He spent forty years as an engineer, and he is quick to explain why old ice becomes **brittle** or why a **broad** river moves more slowly than a narrow stream. But he also has a gentle side. When a neighbour's cat came home with a deep **bruise** on its leg, he cleaned the wound and kept the animal warm for a week.\n\nSometimes he seems to **brood** over things. My grandmother says she can tell from his **brow**: when he is worried, it wrinkles like an old map. He worries about the way some people treat animals, and he once called a man who kicked a stray dog \"a **brute**\". The dog's fur, he remembers, would **bristle** whenever that man came near.\n\nEvery evening he listens to the news **broadcast** on his old radio, which has a **broken** dial but still works. \"Life is like water in a glass,\" he likes to say, filling his own to the **brim** at dinner. \"Pour slowly, and nothing spills.\""},"21":{"w":["budget","bulb","bulk","bull","bullet","bunch","bundle","burden","bureau","burn","burst","bury","bush","business","butcher","butter","butterfly","button","cabbage","cabin"],"title":"The Butcher on Our Street","t":"Mr. Wang has run the **butcher** shop on our street for thirty years. It is a small **business**, but it is the heart of the neighbourhood. Every morning he hangs a **bunch** of fresh herbs by the door and arranges cuts of meat, blocks of **butter** and heads of **cabbage** in neat rows on the counter.\n\nLast year the shop almost closed. A large supermarket opened nearby and began selling meat in **bulk** at very low prices. Mr. Wang's monthly **budget** no longer balanced, and the rent became a heavy **burden**. When the shop's lighting failed, he could not even afford to replace a single **bulb**. He applied for help from the city's small business **bureau**, but the paperwork took months.\n\nThen something unexpected happened. A local journalist wrote an article about him, describing him as \"as strong as a **bull** and as gentle as a **butterfly**\". The story spread online, and customers began to arrive from all over the city. Some brought him a **bundle** of old newspapers to wrap the meat in; others simply came to talk.\n\nMr. Wang admits that he was close to giving up. \"I felt my dreams were about to **burst** like a balloon,\" he said, \"and that I would have to **bury** thirty years of work.\" Instead, sales doubled. He hired an assistant, painted the shop and planted a small rose **bush** by the door.\n\nHe still works long hours, and last winter he **burned|burn** his hand badly on the grill. But he says he would not change his life. On the wall behind the counter hangs a photo of his father outside a wooden **cabin** in the mountains, next to a single brass **button** from his father's old coat. \"He used to say that hard times come at you as fast as a **bullet**,\" Mr. Wang says, \"and that the only answer is to stand your ground.\""},"22":{"w":["cabinet","cable","cafe","cafeteria","cage","cake","calculate","calculation","calculator","calendar","calm","camel","camera","camp","campaign","campus","canal","cancel","cancer","candidate"],"title":"The Campus Campaign","t":"When Lily decided to run for president of the student union, she knew it would be a difficult **campaign**. She was a second-year engineering student with no political experience, and the other **candidate** was a popular senior who had already served on the committee.\n\nShe began with a careful plan. She hung a large **calendar** on the wall of her dorm room, marked every event on **campus**, and used a **calculator** to work out how many students she could meet each day. Her **calculation** suggested that if she spoke to fifty people a day, she could reach almost everyone before the vote. Her friends teased her for trying to **calculate** everything, but she stayed **calm** and stuck to the plan.\n\nHer main promise was simple: to improve the student **cafeteria**, which served cold food and closed too early. She also wanted to open a small **cafe** in the library where students could study late. She used her **camera** to photograph empty shelves and broken chairs and shared the pictures online.\n\nHalfway through, she almost had to **cancel** everything. Her mother called to say that her grandfather had been diagnosed with **cancer**. Lily rushed home, but her grandfather insisted that she go back. \"A **camel** does not stop walking because the desert is long,\" he told her.\n\nWhen she returned, her supporters had set up a small **camp** outside the main building, with posters, a **cage** of paper birds symbolising freedom of choice and a homemade **cake** for everyone who voted. A **cable** television channel even interviewed her, and she promised to keep a filing **cabinet** of student complaints open to all.\n\nShe won by a narrow margin. Her first act as president was to organise a cleaning day along the old **canal** behind the university, a place everyone had forgotten."},"23":{"w":["candle","candy","cannon","canoe","canteen","canvas","cap","capable","capacity","capital","captain","captive","capture","carbon","career","careless","cargo","carpenter","carpet","carriage"],"title":"The Carpenter's Canoe","t":"My grandfather was a **carpenter** for fifty years, and the most remarkable thing he ever made was a wooden **canoe**. He built it slowly over one winter in a shed behind the house, working by the light of a **candle** whenever the power failed. My grandmother called it his foolish project, but she brought him tea and **candy** every evening anyway.\n\nHe had learned his trade as a young man, when he worked on a ship that carried **cargo** between ports along the coast. The **captain**, an old sailor with a loud voice, saw that the young man was **capable** of far more than cleaning decks and taught him to repair the wooden **carriage** of the ship's ancient **cannon**, a relic kept for decoration. From then on, my grandfather knew what he wanted for his **career**.\n\nHe was never **careless**. He measured each board twice, protected the floor of the shed with an old **carpet** and covered his work with a sheet of **canvas** at night. He even read about how different trees store **carbon**, because he wanted to use wood that had been grown sustainably. The finished canoe had the **capacity** to carry two adults and a child.\n\nThe next summer he took it to a lake near the **capital**, where he and my grandmother paddled across the water while a small crowd watched from the shore. A photographer managed to **capture** the moment, and the picture was later printed in a local newspaper. In it, my grandfather is wearing his favourite **cap** and laughing like a child.\n\nFor years that photo hung on the wall of the village **canteen**, where he ate lunch every day, and visitors still stop to look at it. Even in old age, he used to say, a man should never be a **captive** of routine."},"24":{"w":["carrier","carrot","cart","carve","cash","cassette","cast","castle","casual","catalog","cathedral","cattle","cause","cautious","cave","cease","ceiling","celebrate","cell","cellar"],"title":"A Week in a Cathedral Town","t":"Last autumn I spent a week in a small European town famous for its medieval **cathedral** and the ruins of a **castle** on the hill above it. I had chosen it almost at random from a travel **catalog** in a bookshop, and I expected a quiet, slightly boring trip.\n\nIt turned out to be one of the most memorable weeks of my life. On the first morning I joined a guided tour that began in the cathedral's **cellar**, a cold stone room where wine had once been stored. The guide explained that the town's wealth had come from **cattle** farming, and that the great painted **ceiling** of the cathedral had been paid for by farmers' donations. Craftsmen had spent decades learning to **carve** the wooden figures above the altar, and the bells had been **cast** in a local workshop.\n\nThe guide was a **casual**, friendly man who wore jeans and an old jumper. He told us that during a war in the seventeenth century, villagers had hidden in a **cave** outside town, waiting for the fighting to **cease**. When the danger passed, they returned and continued to **celebrate** their harvest festival, which is still held every October.\n\nI also discovered the town's market, where farmers sold everything from **carrot** cake to homemade cheese from a wooden **cart**. Most stalls accepted only **cash**, so I had to be **cautious** with my spending. One old man was even selling music on **cassette** tapes, which made me laugh; I had not seen one since childhood.\n\nOn my last evening I tried to call home, but my **cell** phone had no signal in the narrow old streets. Later I learned the **cause**: the thick stone walls. Instead, I handed a postcard to a postal **carrier** who promised it would reach China within two weeks. It arrived in three."},"25":{"w":["cement","cent","centigrade","centimetre","central","century","ceremony","certain","certainty","certificate","chain","chairman","chalk","challenge","chamber","champion","chance","channel","chaos","chapter"],"title":"The Youngest Champion","t":"At the age of eleven, Ming became the youngest **champion** in the history of our city's chess club. The club meets in a small **chamber** on the second floor of the town hall, a room that has hardly changed in a **century**; its **cement** floor has been worn smooth by generations of footsteps. On the wall hangs a framed **certificate** from 1925, the year the club was founded.\n\nMing did not seem to be a natural genius. When he first came to the club, the **chairman** told his mother that it was almost **certain** he would lose most of his games. But Ming had one quality that set him apart: he loved a **challenge**. Every time he lost, he went home, set up the board and replayed the game, move by move.\n\nHis coach, a retired maths teacher, wrote patterns on a blackboard with a piece of **chalk** and explained how each move was linked to the next like the links of a **chain**. \"Chess is not about luck or **chance**,\" he said, \"but about bringing order to **chaos**.\" He showed Ming how to control the **central** squares of the board and how to **channel** his energy into planning several moves ahead.\n\nThe final match of the championship ended with a small **ceremony** in the town hall. Ming played against a man in his fifties who had held the title for ten years. The temperature that afternoon was nearly thirty-five degrees **centigrade**, and the old fan in the corner barely moved the air. After four hours, Ming won by the narrowest of margins. The prize was a small trophy, barely ten **centimetres|centimetre** tall, and a coin worth one **cent**, painted gold, according to the club's oldest tradition.\n\nMing's mother says that this victory opened a new **chapter** in her son's life. He now studies with a coach online and hopes to compete nationally. One thing is a **certainty**: he will keep replaying every game he loses."},"26":{"w":["character","characteristic","characterize","charge","charity","charming","chart","chase","cheat","cheek","cheer","cheerful","cheese","chemical","chemist","chemistry","cheque","cherry","chess","chest"],"title":"The Charity Fair","t":"Every December our school holds a **charity** fair, and the star of the event is always Mr. Ross, the **chemistry** teacher. He is a **charming**, slightly eccentric man whose **character** seems to belong in an old novel. His most famous **characteristic** is a bright red bow tie, which he wears every day, even in summer.\n\nMr. Ross is in **charge** of the science stall. He trained as a **chemist** before becoming a teacher, and he uses simple **chemical** reactions to amaze visitors: he turns water purple, makes foam rise from a bottle and lets children dissolve sugar to see how crystals form. Parents **cheer** and children scream with delight.\n\nHis stall also sells food. He makes small sandwiches with **cheese** and **cherry** jam, a strange combination that has become a school tradition. Students **chase** each other around the hall, trying to be the first to buy one. Last year a boy tried to **cheat** by cutting in line, and Mr. Ross made him wait until the very end, then gave him an extra sandwich \"for patience\".\n\nHe keeps a **chart** on the wall showing how much money the fair has raised each year, from a few hundred yuan in the first year to more than twenty thousand last year. The money goes to a children's hospital, and every year the hospital director comes to collect a **cheque** and thank the students in person.\n\nOutside the stall, Mr. Ross sets up a giant **chess** board where parents and children play against each other, and visitors describe the atmosphere as warm and **cheerful**. If you had to **characterize** the fair in one image, it would be Mr. Ross at the end of the day, with flour on his **cheek** and a hand pressed to his **chest**, laughing at the final total."},"27":{"w":["chew","chicken","chief","childhood","childish","chill","chimney","chin","chocolate","choice","choke","choose","chop","church","cigarette","circle","circuit","circular","circulate","circumference"],"title":"Grandmother's House","t":"When I think of my **childhood**, I think of my grandmother's old house in the countryside. It had a crooked **chimney**, a yard full of **chickens|chicken** and a kitchen that always smelled of wood smoke. On winter mornings a **chill** would creep through the windows, and my grandmother would wrap me in a blanket and give me a cup of hot **chocolate**.\n\nShe was the **chief** cook of the family and took the job seriously. She could **chop** vegetables with astonishing speed, and she insisted that I **chew** every mouthful slowly. \"Your stomach has no teeth,\" she would say, tapping my **chin** with her finger. Once I tried to swallow a large piece of meat too quickly and began to **choke**; she calmly patted my back until it came out, then laughed at my **childish** pride.\n\nOn Sundays she took me to the small **church** at the edge of the village, not because she was very religious but because she loved the music. Afterwards we walked home in a wide **circle** through the fields, and she taught me the name of every plant. She also explained how electricity travels around a **circuit**, using a battery and a bulb she kept in a drawer, and how blood **circulates|circulate** through the body.\n\nMy grandfather, meanwhile, smoked a single **cigarette** every evening on the porch, a habit she disliked but tolerated. He liked to measure things: the **circumference** of the old oak tree, the height of the wheat. He once drew a **circular** map of the village on the back of an envelope.\n\nWhen I was ten, my parents gave me a **choice**: to stay in the city for the summer or to spend it in the countryside. I did not hesitate to **choose** the old house. It was the best decision of my young life."},"28":{"w":["circumstance","citizen","civil","civilian","civilization","civilize","claim","clap","clarify","clasp","classic","classical","classification","classify","classmate","claw","clay","clearly","clerk","clever"],"title":"The Museum Clerk","t":"In a small museum in the centre of our city, a **clerk** named Mrs. Zhou has spent twenty years trying to **classify** objects from the region's past. Her work is quiet and detailed: each pot, coin and letter must be described, photographed and placed in the right category. The **classification** system she designed is now used by museums across the province.\n\nMrs. Zhou is a **clever** woman with a dry sense of humour. She likes to say that every object tells a story about the **circumstance** in which it was made. A **clay** bowl, for example, can reveal what people ate, how they cooked and even what kind of **civilization** they belonged to. Some of the pots still carry the marks of an animal's **claw**, left when a cat walked across the wet clay thousands of years ago.\n\nHer favourite collection consists of letters written by ordinary **citizens|citizen** during a **civil** war more than a century ago. Most of the writers were **civilians|civilian**, not soldiers: farmers, teachers and shopkeepers who described their fears and hopes with surprising honesty. \"We often **claim** that history is about kings and battles,\" she says, \"but it is really about people like these.\"\n\nShe has also helped schools use the museum to teach history. She invites students to read **classic** texts alongside the objects and to listen to **classical** music from the same period. One former student, now a teacher herself, says that Mrs. Zhou helped her see the past **clearly** for the first time. \"She never tried to **civilize** us,\" the student laughed. \"She just made us curious.\"\n\nWhen Mrs. Zhou retired last year, the director gave a speech to **clarify** how much she had done for the museum, and the audience began to **clap** for several minutes. My mother, her old **classmate** from school, stood beside her and held her hand in a tight **clasp**."},"29":{"w":["cliff","climate","climb","cloak","clock","closely","cloth","clothe","clothing","club","clue","clumsy","coach","coal","coarse","coast","coat","cock","code","coil"],"title":"The Climbing Club","t":"Every Saturday morning, members of our town's climbing **club** meet at the bus station and travel together to the **coast**. Their goal is a tall white **cliff** above the sea, popular with climbers because of its firm rock and beautiful views. The **climate** there is mild, but the wind can be fierce, so everyone brings a warm **coat**.\n\nThe club's **coach**, a former mountain guide, insists that safety comes first. Before each **climb** he checks every rope and every **coil** of wire, and he watches each beginner **closely**. He has developed a simple **code** of hand signals so that climbers can communicate even when the wind makes it impossible to hear. \"If you're **clumsy** on the ground,\" he jokes, \"you'll be clumsy on the rock.\"\n\nThe coach is also a keen historian. He tells new members that two hundred years ago the cliffs were used by smugglers who wore a dark **cloak** to hide in the shadows. Ships carried **coal** along the coast, and the old harbour wall still has rusty iron rings where boats were tied. On one climb, a member found an old silver button wedged in a crack: a **clue**, perhaps, to a smuggler's escape.\n\nMembers bring their own **clothing**, but the club provides gloves made of a **coarse** **cloth** that grips the rock well. The club also raises money to feed and **clothe** homeless people in town during the winter.\n\nAt the end of each trip, the group returns to a seaside café, where an old **clock** above the counter stopped long ago at exactly half past four. The owner keeps a noisy **cock** in the back garden, which crows whenever a bus arrives, whatever the time of day."},"30":{"w":["coin","cold","collaborate","collapse","collar","colleague","collect","collection","collective","collision","colonel","colony","color","column","comb","combination","combine","comedy","comfort","comfortable"],"title":"A Colleague's Coins","t":"My **colleague** Mr. Tian has an unusual hobby: he collects old coins. His **collection** includes more than three thousand pieces from fifty countries, and he keeps them in neat rows in a glass cabinet in his office. Each **coin** has a small label describing its origin, its date and the **color** of its metal.\n\nHe began to **collect** coins as a child, after his grandfather, a retired army **colonel**, gave him a silver dollar from the 1930s. The colonel had served in several countries, including a former British **colony** in Southeast Asia, and he had brought home coins from every place he visited. \"Every coin is a tiny history book,\" he told his grandson.\n\nMr. Tian believes that collecting is a **collective** activity. He belongs to a club of collectors who meet every month to swap pieces and share knowledge. They often **collaborate** with local museums, and last year they helped to **combine** two private collections into a public exhibition. The **combination** of rare coins and old photographs attracted thousands of visitors.\n\nNot everything has gone smoothly. Once, a shelf holding part of his collection began to **collapse** after a minor **collision** with a cleaning trolley, and dozens of coins rolled across the floor. Mr. Tian spent the whole afternoon on his knees, trying to **comb** the carpet for every missing piece. The rest of us laughed, and the scene became a kind of office **comedy**.\n\nHe also writes a weekly **column** about coins for a local newspaper. On **cold** winter evenings he sits in his **comfortable** armchair, polishing coins and listening to the radio; he says the hobby brings him **comfort** after a stressful day. Even his dog, whose **collar** is decorated with tiny coins, seems to enjoy it."},"31":{"w":["command","commander","comment","commerce","commercial","commission","commit","commitment","committee","common","commonly","communicate","communication","communism","communist","community","companion","comparable","comparative","comparatively"],"title":"Saving the Park","t":"When the city government announced a plan to build a shopping centre on the site of our neighbourhood park, residents were quick to **comment**. Within a week, a **committee** of volunteers had formed to **communicate** their concerns to the authorities. Its leader was Mr. Fang, a retired army **commander** who still spoke with the quiet authority of someone used to giving a **command**.\n\nMr. Fang believed that **communication** was the key. He organised public meetings, wrote letters to the newspapers and invited the city planning **commission** to visit the park. He argued that **commerce** was important, but that a **community** also needed green spaces where people could meet. \"A **commercial** centre can be built anywhere,\" he said. \"A hundred-year-old tree cannot.\"\n\nThe members had grown up in very different times. Some older members remembered the early years of **communism**, when every factory had its own park and sports ground; one former **communist** official recalled that public spaces had been considered a basic right. Younger members, by contrast, were **commonly** more interested in cafés and bike lanes. Yet they all shared a **common** goal.\n\nMr. Fang asked each member to **commit** at least two hours a week to the campaign, and their **commitment** impressed even the planners. After months of negotiation, the city agreed to use a **comparable** site for the shopping centre, a former car park only a kilometre away, at a **comparatively** small extra cost.\n\nToday the park remains, and Mr. Fang still walks there every morning with his faithful **companion**, a white-haired dog named General. A university later used the case in a **comparative** study of urban planning in Chinese cities."},"32":{"w":["compare","comparison","compass","compel","compete","competent","competition","competitive","compile","complain","complaint","complete","completely","complex","complicate","complicated","component","compose","composition","compound"],"title":"The Composition Competition","t":"Every year our university holds a music **competition** for student composers. The rules are simple: each entrant must **compose** an original piece of no more than ten minutes for a small orchestra. Last year my roommate Jia decided to **compete** for the first time.\n\nShe was a **competent** pianist but had never written a full **composition**, and at first she found the task **complicated**. A piece of music, she explained, is like a chemical **compound**: each **component**, from melody to rhythm, affects all the others. Change one element and you may **complicate** the whole structure.\n\nShe worked late every night, and our room was soon full of scribbled notes. She tried to **compare** her work with famous pieces, but the **comparison** only made her feel worse. \"I can't **complete** anything,\" she would **complain**, throwing pages on the floor. Her main **complaint** was that she had no **compass** to guide her through such a **complex** process.\n\nOur music teacher suggested that she stop listening to other composers for a while. \"Nobody can **compel** inspiration,\" he said. \"**Compile** a notebook of small ideas instead, and see which ones grow.\" Jia followed his advice, and slowly the piece began to take shape.\n\nThe atmosphere on the day of the final was intensely **competitive**, with twenty entrants from across the country. Jia did not win first prize, but her piece came third, and the judges praised it for being **completely** original. Afterwards she told me that the real victory had been finishing at all."},"33":{"w":["comprehension","comprehensive","compress","comprise","compromise","compute","comrade","conceal","concentrate","concentration","concept","concern","concerning","concert","conclude","conclusion","concrete","condemn","condense","condition"],"title":"The Art of Concentration","t":"Many students believe that the secret of success is simply hard work. Professor Ding, who teaches reading **comprehension** at our university, disagrees. In her view, the key **concept** is **concentration**: the ability to **concentrate** on one task for a long time without distraction.\n\nHer course is **comprehensive**. It **comprises|comprise** lectures, discussions and weekly reading tests, and students are often asked to **condense** a long article into a single paragraph. \"If you can **compress** an idea without losing its meaning,\" she says, \"you truly understand it.\"\n\nShe is open about her **concern** that smartphones have damaged young people's attention. She does not **condemn** technology; she uses it herself to **compute** reading speeds and test results. But she asks students not to **conceal** their phones under their desks. Instead, everyone places their phone in a box at the front of the room. Some students found this rule unfair at first, but after a few weeks most accepted it as a reasonable **compromise**.\n\nHer **conclusion**, based on years of teaching, is clear: students who read in silence for thirty minutes a day improve faster than those who study for hours with constant interruptions. She **concludes|conclude** each semester with a reading **concert**, in which students read favourite passages aloud while the others listen.\n\nWhen I asked for her advice **concerning** exam preparation, she gave a very **concrete** answer: sleep well, read every day and treat each classmate as a **comrade** rather than a rival. Under the right **conditions|condition**, she said, anyone can learn to read deeply."},"34":{"w":["conduct","conductor","conference","confess","confidence","confident","confine","confirm","conflict","confuse","confusion","congratulate","congratulation","congress","conjunction","connect","connection","conquer","conquest","conscience"],"title":"The Conductor's Confession","t":"At an international music **conference** in Shanghai last year, the famous **conductor** Li Wen surprised his audience by deciding to **confess** a secret: for the first ten years of his career, he had almost no **confidence** in himself. \"Every time I stood in front of an orchestra,\" he said, \"I was afraid they would discover I didn't know what I was doing.\"\n\nHis early years were full of **conflict**. Critics described his style as strange, and some musicians openly questioned his decisions. The **confusion** was partly his own fault; he tried to please everyone and ended up satisfying no one. He kept changing his mind during rehearsals, which would **confuse** the players.\n\nThings changed when an old teacher told him to stop trying to **conquer** the music and simply listen to it. \"Your job is to **conduct**, not to control,\" the teacher said. \"Your only judge should be your **conscience**.\" Li Wen began to treat each concert as a conversation rather than a **conquest**. He learned to **connect** with each musician individually and to see the **connection** between his own feelings and the sound of the orchestra.\n\nSlowly he became more **confident**. A decade later he was invited to perform at a gathering of the national **congress**, and the event helped to **confirm** his reputation. Today he refuses to **confine** himself to one style, performing everything from Bach to film music.\n\nAfter his speech, dozens of young musicians lined up to **congratulate** him. One student handed him a card that read simply, \"**Congratulations|congratulation** on your honesty.\" He laughed and said that, like a **conjunction** in a sentence, a conductor's role is to join things together, not to stand out."},"35":{"w":["conscious","consciousness","consent","consequence","consequently","conservation","conserve","consider","considerable","considerate","consideration","consist","consistent","constant","constitution","construct","construction","consult","consume","consumer"],"title":"Small Habits, Big Consequences","t":"Every **consumer** makes choices that affect the planet, often without being fully **conscious** of them. This was the main message of a public lecture on environmental **conservation** that I attended last month. The speaker, a biologist named Dr. Hu, argued that the problem is not a lack of information but a lack of **consciousness**.\n\nShe began with a simple example. A single cup of coffee, she explained, may **consume** dozens of litres of water before it reaches your hands, if you **consider** the whole process of growing, roasting and transporting the beans. The **consequence** is that small daily habits add up to a **considerable** impact. **Consequently**, she said, people who want to **conserve** resources should start with ordinary routines rather than grand gestures.\n\nDr. Hu was **considerate** towards her audience. She did not blame anyone; she simply asked us to take her points into **consideration**. Real change, she said, must **consist** of many small steps, and those steps must be **consistent**: **constant** small efforts matter more than occasional heroic ones.\n\nShe also discussed the role of law. In some countries, she noted, the **constitution** now includes the right to a healthy environment, and governments must **consult** citizens before approving major projects. She showed photos of a new **construction** project near a wetland, where engineers had to **construct** special bridges so that animals could cross safely.\n\nAt the end, a student asked whether governments should ever act without public **consent**. Dr. Hu smiled and said that real progress happens only when people agree to change. \"Laws can guide us,\" she said, \"but habits change the world.\""},"36":{"w":["consumption","contact","contain","container","contemporary","contempt","content","contest","continent","continual","continue","continuous","contract","contradiction","contrary","contrast","contribute","contribution","control","convenience"],"title":"The Container Library","t":"In a small coastal town, an old shipping **container** has been turned into a public library. The idea came from a local teacher, Ms. Bai, who noticed that the town's children had little **contact** with books outside school. Instead of complaining, she decided to **contribute** something herself.\n\nShe signed a **contract** with the port authority to rent an unused container for a symbolic fee. Volunteers painted it bright blue, built shelves and installed solar panels to **control** the temperature inside. The finished library can **contain** about two thousand books, from **contemporary** novels to atlases of every **continent**.\n\nAt first, some residents treated the project with **contempt**, calling it a waste of time. In **contrast**, the children loved it from the first day. Ms. Bai organised a reading **contest** every month, and the **content** of the shelves changed **continually|continual** as people donated books. The library's popularity seemed to be in direct **contradiction** with the common belief that young people no longer read.\n\nThe project has changed adults' habits too. Parents who once spent hours scrolling on their phones now borrow books for themselves. Ms. Bai keeps a record of book **consumption** in the town, and the numbers **continue** to rise. \"Reading is like learning to swim,\" she says. \"Once you start, the movement becomes **continuous**.\"\n\n**Contrary** to her fears, the library has never been damaged by vandals. Its greatest **contribution**, she believes, is not the books themselves but the simple **convenience** of having them close to home."},"37":{"w":["convenient","convention","conventional","conversely","conversion","convert","convey","convince","cooperate","coordinate","cope","copper","cord","cordial","core","corn","corporation","correct","correction","correspond"],"title":"New Life for the Copper Works","t":"For decades the **copper** factory on the edge of our city was the town's economic **core**. When it closed, many people feared the area would decline. But five years later, a local **corporation** decided to **convert** the old buildings into a creative centre for artists, designers and small businesses.\n\nThe **conversion** was not easy. The project manager had to **coordinate** the work of architects, engineers and hundreds of builders, and **convince** sceptical residents that the plan would benefit them. He held public meetings every month to **convey** the latest progress and answer questions. \"We cannot **cope** with every problem alone,\" he admitted. \"We need people to **cooperate**.\"\n\nThe final design mixed the old and the new. The original brick walls were kept, but each workshop was fitted with modern lighting, and every old power **cord** was replaced. A large hall now hosts an annual design **convention**, attracting visitors from across the country. **Conversely**, the small studios upstairs remain quiet and private.\n\nResidents found the centre surprisingly **convenient**. Families come at weekends to buy handmade goods, and farmers sell fresh **corn** and vegetables at a weekly market in the courtyard. The atmosphere is relaxed and **cordial**, very different from the **conventional** shopping malls downtown.\n\nSome critics say the project has made the area too expensive. The manager accepts that mistakes were made and says the corporation will **correct** them; rents for local artists have already been reduced as a **correction**. Whether the centre will truly **correspond** to the community's needs, he says, will become clear only with time."},"38":{"w":["correspondent","corresponding","corridor","costly","cottage","cotton","cough","council","count","counter","county","couple","courage","course","court","cousin","cow","coward","crack","craft"],"title":"The Correspondent Retires","t":"After thirty years as a foreign **correspondent**, Mr. Gao retired to a small **cottage** in the countryside of a southern **county**. He had reported from war zones and summit meetings, but he says the bravest decision of his life was to leave the city and start again.\n\nThe cottage was old and **costly** to repair. Its narrow **corridor** leaked when it rained, and one wall showed a long **crack** that ran from floor to ceiling. Mr. Gao learned the **craft** of plastering from a local builder, and slowly the house became comfortable. He planted **cotton** in the garden, partly as an experiment, and bought a **cow** from a neighbour, though he admits he has no idea how to milk it.\n\nHis **cousin**, a retired judge from the city **court**, visits him every summer. The two men sit by the kitchen **counter** in the evenings, arguing about politics and laughing at old stories. Whenever they disagree, Mr. Gao's wife reminds them that they are a **couple** of stubborn old men.\n\nThe local **council** asked him to write a column for the county newspaper. He agreed on one condition: he would write about ordinary people. His articles describe farmers, teachers and shopkeepers, and every story comes with a **corresponding** photograph. Readers say they **count** among the most honest pieces in the paper.\n\nMr. Gao believes that real **courage** is not the absence of fear. He has met soldiers who felt no fear at all and **cowards|coward** who were brave when it mattered. \"Every life is a long **course**,\" he says, \"and nobody gets through it without a few bruises.\" He still has a dry **cough** from years on dusty roads, but he says he has never felt healthier."},"39":{"w":["crane","crash","crawl","crazy","cream","create","creative","creature","creep","crew","cricket","crime","criminal","cripple","crisis","critic","critical","criticism","criticize","crop"],"title":"The Night the Crane Fell","t":"At three o'clock one morning, a construction **crane** on the edge of our city collapsed with a terrible **crash**. Nobody was hurt, but the accident caused a **crisis** for the company that owned it, and the local media began to **criticize** its safety record.\n\nRumours spread quickly. Some people claimed that a **criminal** gang had damaged the crane on purpose; others insisted that it had simply been poorly maintained. The police investigated for a week but found no evidence of a **crime**. Instead, engineers discovered that a steel bolt had been cracked for months, and that workers had reported it without anyone taking action.\n\nThe company's director at first rejected the **criticism**, calling the reports **crazy**. But one newspaper **critic** wrote a sharp article describing how cost-cutting had begun to **cripple** the firm's maintenance department. The article proved **critical**: within days the director resigned, and a new team was appointed.\n\nThe new manager, a former engineer, took a more **creative** approach. She invited workers to suggest improvements and set up a system under which any **crew** member could stop work if they saw danger. \"We need to **create** a culture of honesty,\" she said. \"Problems should never be allowed to **creep** in quietly.\"\n\nLife on the site slowly returned to normal. The workers even started a weekly game of **cricket** at lunchtime, a sport the new manager had learned while studying abroad. Nearby farmers, whose **crop** of vegetables had been flattened by the fallen crane, received compensation. One farmer joked that the only **creature** truly frightened by the crash was his cat, which refused to **crawl** out from under the bed for two days. To celebrate the reopening, the manager brought cakes topped with fresh **cream** for every worker."},"40":{"w":["cross","crow","crowd","crown","crucial","crude","cruel","crush","crust","crystal","cube","cubic","cucumber","cue","cultivate","culture","cunning","cupboard","cure","curiosity"],"title":"The Crystal Shop","t":"In the old part of the city there is a tiny shop that sells **crystals|crystal**, minerals and fossils. Its owner, Mrs. Ning, has run it for forty years, and her **curiosity** about the natural world seems endless. She can explain how a perfect **cube** of salt forms, or how to calculate the **cubic** volume of a rock sample, as easily as other people discuss the weather.\n\nThe shop attracts a strange **crowd**: geologists, children, artists and the occasional tourist who wanders in by accident. Mrs. Ning treats them all with the same warm patience. She keeps her rarest pieces in an old wooden **cupboard** behind the counter and brings them out only when she senses genuine interest. \"People need a **cue**,\" she says. \"When their eyes light up, I know they are ready.\"\n\nHer most famous object is a **crude** lump of grey rock that hides a glittering **crust** of tiny crystals inside; she calls it the **crown** of her collection. She found it as a girl on a mountain path, while a **crow** watched from a tree, and she says it taught her that beauty is often hidden beneath rough surfaces.\n\nMrs. Ning believes that schools should **cultivate** children's interest in science from an early age. In her view, a society's **culture** depends on its willingness to ask questions. She has little patience for **cunning** dealers who pass off fake stones as real, and she considers it **cruel** to sell children worthless objects at high prices.\n\nHer advice for anyone who wants to **cross** from curiosity to real knowledge is **crucial**: never **crush** a question, however strange it seems. She admits that collecting rocks will not **cure** loneliness, but she says it helps. And at lunchtime she always eats the same thing: a simple sandwich of bread and **cucumber**."},"41":{"w":["curious","curl","current","curse","curtain","curve","cushion","custom","customer","cycle","daily","dairy","dam","damage","damp","danger","dangerous","dare","daring","darling"],"title":"The Dairy by the Dam","t":"Near the old **dam** outside our town there is a small **dairy** farm run by a family that has kept cows for four generations. Its **customer** base is small but loyal: local cafés, a few schools and families who drive out every weekend to buy fresh milk, cheese and yoghurt.\n\nThe farm's owner, a **daring** young woman named Su Yan, took over from her father five years ago. She was **curious** about modern methods and wanted to break with **custom**. Instead of selling to a big company at low prices, she opened a farm shop with soft **cushion** seats and checked **curtains|curtain**, where visitors can taste the products. \"If you **dare** to try something new,\" she says, \"people notice.\"\n\nRunning the farm is not easy. Milk production follows a natural **cycle**, and her **daily** routine begins at four in the morning. The fields near the river are often **damp**, which can be **dangerous** for the animals' health. Last spring a flood caused serious **damage** to the barn, and the **current** was so strong that it swept away part of the fence.\n\nSome neighbours warned her of the **danger** of taking on too much risk, and one old farmer said she must be under a **curse** when the bank refused her a loan. \"There is no such thing as bad luck,\" she replied. \"There's only bad planning.\"\n\nToday the farm is doing well. Visitors love watching the calves **curl** up in the straw, and the road to the farm, with its long **curve** around the hill, has become a popular cycling route. Her father, now retired, still calls her \"my **darling**\" in front of customers, which embarrasses her terribly."},"42":{"w":["dash","data","dawn","daylight","deadly","deaf","deal","death","debate","debt","decade","decay","deceit","deceive","decent","decision","deck","declare","decline","decorate"],"title":"Ten Years at Sea","t":"When my grandfather was young, he worked for a **decade** on a cargo ship that sailed between Shanghai and the Pacific islands. He still remembers standing on the **deck** at **dawn**, watching the first **daylight** spread across the sea. \"Those were the best years of my life,\" he says, \"and the most dangerous.\"\n\nThe work was hard and sometimes **deadly**. During one storm, a sailor was swept overboard, and my grandfather witnessed his first **death**. He says the experience changed him; from that moment he made a **decision** never to take life for granted.\n\nHe also learned about honesty. One day the captain discovered that a crew member had tried to **deceive** him by hiding stolen goods in the cargo hold. The captain was furious, not only about the theft but about the **deceit**. \"A ship survives on trust,\" he told the crew. He made the man **declare** everything he had taken and pay back the **debt** from his wages.\n\nMy grandfather left the sea after ten years to start a family. He found a **decent** job in a shipyard, where he helped **decorate** the interiors of passenger ships. The shipbuilding industry began to **decline** in the 1990s, and many yards fell into **decay**, but he kept his job until retirement.\n\nNow in his eighties, he is slightly **deaf**, but he loves to **debate** history with his grandchildren. He still keeps the **data** from his voyages, notebooks full of weather readings and distances, and every evening he makes a **dash** to the window to watch the sunset. \"Life has given me a good **deal**,\" he says."},"43":{"w":["decrease","deduce","deed","deepen","deer","defeat","defect","defence","defend","define","definite","definitely","definition","degree","delay","delegate","delegation","delete","deliberate","deliberately"],"title":"The Delegation to Geneva","t":"Last spring our university sent a **delegation** of ten students to an international conference on environmental policy in Geneva. Each **delegate** had to prepare a speech that would **define** a clear position on climate change and **defend** it against criticism.\n\nOur team leader, Professor Wu, insisted that every argument be supported by evidence. \"Do not **deduce** conclusions from feelings,\" she told us. \"Begin with a precise **definition** of the problem.\" She made us **delete** every sentence that was vague or emotional, and she was **definite** about one thing: we would not exaggerate.\n\nBad weather held up our flight for six hours, and the **delay** left us only one evening to rehearse. We worked late into the night. One teammate, a quiet engineering student, realised that our data contained a serious **defect**: the figures for carbon emissions had been entered in the wrong units. If he had not noticed, our whole presentation would have been ruined.\n\nAt the conference our speeches were well received. One rival team **deliberately** tried to confuse us with difficult questions, but we answered calmly. Since it was not a competition, we did not **defeat** anyone, but we earned the respect of many participants. One judge described our **defence** of reducing plastic use as \"a careful and **deliberate** argument\".\n\nThe experience helped to **deepen** my interest in environmental law, and I am now working towards a **degree** in the field. I also learned that the world will not **decrease** its emissions without countless small good **deeds|deed**. On our last day we walked in a forest outside the city and saw a **deer** standing silently among the trees. I will **definitely** go back."},"44":{"w":["delicate","delicious","delight","deliver","delivery","demand","democracy","democratic","demonstrate","dense","density","deny","depart","department","departure","depend","dependent","deposit","depress","depression"],"title":"Behind Every Parcel","t":"When my cousin Yue graduated, she took a job in the **delivery** **department** of a large online shop. She expected a simple office job, but she soon discovered that her work was at the centre of the company's success.\n\nEvery day millions of customers **demand** fast service, and her department must **deliver** parcels to cities across the country within forty-eight hours. Yue's task is to plan routes in areas with a high population **density**, where traffic is **dense** and drivers struggle to find parking. She uses data to **demonstrate** which routes save the most time.\n\nThe job can be stressful. In the busiest season the pressure can **depress** even the most cheerful staff, and one of her colleagues took sick leave for **depression**. Yue believes the company should **depend** less on overtime and more on better planning. She does not **deny** that the work is hard, but she says the managers have started to listen.\n\nHer department also handles fragile goods. Yue once supervised the **departure** of a truck carrying **delicate** glass vases to an exhibition. Every box had to be packed with care, and the customer paid a **deposit** for special insurance. When the vases arrived unbroken, the customer sent the team a box of **delicious** cakes, to everyone's **delight**.\n\nYue says her job has shown her how **dependent** modern society is on invisible workers. She also believes that workplaces should be more **democratic**, giving ordinary staff a voice in decisions. \"**Democracy** starts with small things,\" she says, \"like asking the drivers before you change their routes.\" Before the trucks **depart** each morning, she walks around the yard to say hello to every driver."},"45":{"w":["depth","deputy","derive","descend","describe","description","desert","deserve","design","desirable","desire","despair","desperate","despise","despite","destination","destroy","destruction","destructive","detail"],"title":"Into the Canyon","t":"Last summer a team of geologists set out to explore a remote **desert** in western China. Their **destination** was a deep canyon that had never been fully mapped. The team leader, Dr. Ma, and his **deputy**, a young scientist named Lin, spent months planning every **detail** of the journey.\n\nThe desert is famous for both its beauty and its dangers. Temperatures rise above fifty degrees by day and fall below zero at night, and strong winds can be extremely **destructive**, causing sandstorms that bury roads and vehicles. Old maps **describe** villages that have since disappeared completely, and local people **derive** many legends from their ruins.\n\nOn the fifth day the team began to **descend** into the canyon. Its **depth** surprised them: more than three hundred metres from the rim to the bottom. Lin wrote a careful **description** of the rock layers, which showed traces of ancient rivers and the **destruction** caused by earthquakes thousands of years ago.\n\n**Despite** their careful planning, things went wrong. A sandstorm damaged their radio, and for two days they could not contact the outside world. Some team members fell into **despair**, and one became so **desperate** that he wanted to turn back immediately. Dr. Ma remained calm. He reminded them that the canyon would not **destroy** them if they stayed together.\n\nWhen they finally returned, the team received praise from scientists around the world. Dr. Ma insists that Lin **deserves|deserve** most of the credit. A good explorer, he says, should never **despise** fear but should respect it. \"It is **desirable** to feel a **desire** for adventure,\" he says, \"but the **design** of every expedition must put safety first.\""},"46":{"w":["detect","detection","determination","determine","device","devil","devise","devote","dew","diagram","dial","dialect","diameter","diamond","diary","dictate","dictation","diet","differ","difference"],"title":"The Engineer's Diary","t":"At the age of seventy, my great-uncle still keeps a **diary** in which he records everything he observes in his garden: the amount of **dew** on the grass each morning, the **diameter** of the sunflowers, the first frost. He spent his career as an engineer, and he cannot stop measuring things.\n\nHis most famous invention was a small **device** that could **detect** tiny flaws in the industrial **diamonds|diamond** used in cutting tools. Before his work, the **detection** of such flaws required expensive machines; he managed to **devise** a cheaper method using a simple light and a lens. He drew the first **diagram** on the back of an envelope during a train journey.\n\nHis **determination** was legendary. When managers doubted his idea, he spent weekends in the workshop testing it, and he would **devote** every spare hour to improving the design. \"The **devil** is in the details,\" he likes to say, a phrase he picked up from a German colleague.\n\nHe grew up in a mountain village and still speaks a local **dialect** that even his children struggle to understand. As a boy, he practised writing through **dictation**: his teacher would **dictate** passages from old books, and the pupils copied them word for word. He believes this taught him patience.\n\nToday he keeps to a strict **diet** of mostly vegetables and fish, and he still uses an old telephone with a rotating **dial**. When I asked him what had made the biggest **difference** in his life, he said it was learning that people **differ** in how they think, and that no single method can **determine** success."},"47":{"w":["difficulty","digest","digital","diligent","dim","dimension","dip","direct","direction","directly","director","dirt","disable","disadvantage","disagree","disappear","disappoint","disapprove","disaster","discard"],"title":"The Director's First Film","t":"When the young film **director** Chen Lu began shooting her first feature film, everything seemed to go wrong. On the first day, heavy rain turned the location into a field of mud and **dirt**, and the crew could not film in the **dim** evening light. One actor began to **disagree** with every decision she made, and another seemed to **disappear** whenever he was needed.\n\nBy the end of the first week, the project looked like a **disaster**. The producers were worried; one of them said openly that he would **disapprove** any further spending, and Chen Lu feared she would **disappoint** everyone who had trusted her.\n\nBut she was **diligent**. She spent every night studying the footage on her **digital** camera, trying to **digest** what had gone wrong. She realised that her biggest **difficulty** was not the weather but her own lack of clear **direction**. She had been trying to please everyone, and the actors were confused about what she wanted.\n\nShe decided to **discard** half of her original script and focus on the central story. She spoke **directly** to each actor about their role and gave them more freedom. She also learned to turn the **disadvantage** of the rain into an advantage: several of the most powerful scenes in the finished film take place in storms, and the reflections on wet streets add a new **dimension** to the images.\n\nWhen the film was released, critics praised its honesty. Chen Lu admits that the early problems almost managed to **disable** the whole project. But whenever her confidence began to **dip**, she remembered her teacher's advice: \"A director's job is to **direct**, not to be loved.\""},"48":{"w":["discharge","discipline","disclose","discount","discourage","discover","discovery","discrimination","discussion","disease","disguise","disgust","dishonour","disk","dislike","dismiss","disorder","display","displease","disposal"],"title":"The Doctor's Discovery","t":"Dr. Lin had worked at the city hospital for fifteen years when she made an important **discovery**. While reviewing old patient records stored on a computer **disk**, she noticed that people from one particular neighbourhood were far more likely to suffer from a rare lung **disease**. Many had been treated and allowed to **discharge** themselves after a few days, only to return months later.\n\nAt first her colleagues tried to **discourage** her from investigating. Some feared it would **displease** the factory owners in the area; others simply did not want to **disclose** information that might cause panic. But Dr. Lin believed that the **discipline** of medicine demands honesty. She organised a **discussion** with local residents and showed them a **display** of maps and statistics.\n\nWhat she went on to **discover** was disturbing. A nearby chemical plant had been dumping waste near the river, and its owners had tried to **disguise** the problem by planting trees along the fence. When the evidence became public, residents reacted with **disgust**, and the scandal brought **dishonour** on the company.\n\nThe factory's managers first tried to **dismiss** the findings, blaming \"a minor seasonal **disorder**\". But the government ordered an investigation, and the company was forced to pay for the safe **disposal** of its waste.\n\nDr. Lin also spoke out against **discrimination**: some residents had been refused jobs elsewhere simply because they came from the area. Today the hospital offers a **discount** on health checks for local families. Dr. Lin admits that she has always had a strong **dislike** of conflict, but she says she could not stay silent."},"49":{"w":["dispose","dispute","dissatisfy","dissolve","distance","distant","distinct","distinction","distinguish","distress","distribute","distribution","district","disturb","ditch","dive","diverse","divide","division","divorce"],"title":"Two Brothers and a Field","t":"In a small farming **district** in the south, two brothers had been locked in a bitter **dispute** for years. Their father had left them a large piece of land but had never said how to **divide** it, and the **division** of the property had become a source of **distress** for the whole family.\n\nThe older brother wanted to keep the fields near the river; the younger argued that the short **distance** between the house and the river made those fields far more valuable. Neither was willing to compromise, and their relationship grew cold and **distant**. Their wives stopped speaking to each other, and one couple even talked of **divorce** because of the strain.\n\nFinally, an elderly village leader offered to help. She asked both brothers to explain what they wanted and listened patiently. She helped them **distinguish** between what they truly needed and what they simply wanted to win. \"There is a **distinct** difference,\" she said, \"between protecting your family and defeating your brother.\"\n\nShe proposed a creative solution: instead of splitting the land, they would farm it together and **distribute** the profits equally. She suggested they plant **diverse** crops, from rice to vegetables, to reduce the risk of a bad harvest, and that the **distribution** of work be agreed every spring. Any brother who wanted to **dispose** of his share would have to offer it to the other first.\n\nAt first the old quarrel continued to **disturb** their peace. The brothers argued over a drainage **ditch** and over whether to let their children **dive** into the river from the old bridge. But slowly the anger began to **dissolve**. Today their farm is one of the most successful in the area, the village leader has received a **distinction** from the county for her work, and neither brother, they say, has felt **dissatisfied|dissatisfy** since."},"50":{"w":["dock","document","domestic","dominate","donkey","dorm","dormitory","dose","dot","doubt","doubtful","doubtless","downstairs","downward","dozen","draft","drag","dragon","drain","drama"],"title":"Dormitory Drama","t":"Every university **dormitory** has its own stories, but ours seemed to produce more **drama** than most. In my first year, I shared a small room on the third floor with three other students, and our **dorm** quickly became famous throughout the building.\n\nThe trouble began in September, when the girls in the room below complained that we were too noisy. Every time we dropped a book or moved a chair, they heard it. One evening a girl from **downstairs** knocked on our door holding a written **document** that listed every noise we had made that week.\n\nMy roommate Jiang, a drama student with a talent for performance, decided to apologise in style. She bought a **dozen** cakes, painted a red **dragon** on a card and wrote an elaborate **draft** of an apology letter. **Doubtless**, she thought, this would solve the problem. But when she presented it, the neighbours were **doubtful**; they suspected it was a joke.\n\nThings got worse when the pipes in our bathroom began to leak. Water would **drain** away only slowly, and a small **dot** of damp on the floor grew larger each day. Eventually the water seeped **downward** into the room below, and our neighbours were understandably furious.\n\nIn the end the university sent a plumber, who cleared the pipes with a single **dose** of chemical cleaner. Jiang and I helped **drag** the neighbours' wet carpet outside to dry, and working together, we got to know them. One came from a farming family and kept a photograph of a **donkey** on her desk; another was studying **domestic** policy and dreamed of working near the **dock** of a big port city. By the end of the year we were all close friends, and nobody tried to **dominate** the others. If you had told me in September that this would happen, I would have had no **doubt** you were joking."},"51":{"w":["dramatic","drawer","drawing","dread","dream","drift","drill","drip","drop","drought","drown","drug","drum","drunk","duck","due","dull","dumb","dump","durable"],"title":"The Summer of the Drought","t":"The summer I turned twelve was the driest anyone in our village could remember. The **drought** lasted four months, and the river that usually ran beside our house shrank to a thin, **dull** line of mud. My grandfather, who had farmed the land for fifty years, said he had never seen such a **dramatic** change.\n\nEvery morning I listened for the **drip** of water from the old tap in the yard, but it was almost always silent. My father decided to **drill** a new well, and the whole village came to watch. When water finally gushed out, people cheered and someone began to beat a **drum** in celebration. Even the **ducks|duck** in the dry pond seemed to understand.\n\nMy grandfather kept a box of old maps in a **drawer** of his desk. One evening he showed me a **drawing** he had made as a young man of the same river in flood, so wide and fast that a child could **drown** in it. \"Nature has a long memory,\" he said. \"We have a short one.\"\n\nThat summer taught me to **dread** waste. We learned to save every **drop** of water, to reuse washing water on the garden and to **dump** nothing into the riverbed. A doctor visited the village with medicine, and he warned that dirty water could be as dangerous as any **drug**.\n\nNot everyone coped well. One neighbour came home **drunk** every night, and another simply stopped talking, as if the heat had struck him **dumb**. But most people helped each other. When the rains finally came, clouds would **drift** over the hills for days before the first heavy storm, which everyone agreed was long **due**. I still keep a **durable** old bucket from that year in my garage, and I still **dream** about the sound of that first rain."},"52":{"w":["duration","dusk","dust","duty","dwelling","dye","dying","dynamic","eager","eagle","earn","earnest","earthquake","ease","easily","eastern","echo","economic","economical","economics"],"title":"After the Earthquake","t":"When a powerful **earthquake** struck a mountain region in the **eastern** part of the province, thousands of people lost their homes. Many families had lived in the same **dwelling** for generations, and in a few terrible seconds those houses were reduced to **dust**.\n\nAmong the first volunteers to arrive was a young economist named Lu Fang. She had studied **economics** at university and had always been interested in how communities recover after disasters. But she soon realised that **economic** theories meant little when people needed food and shelter. \"My **duty**,\" she said, \"was simply to help.\"\n\nShe worked from dawn until **dusk**, handing out blankets and organising temporary schools. She was **eager** to listen to people's stories and **earnest** in her efforts to understand their needs, and many villagers said that her calm presence helped to **ease** their fears. She also noticed an old man nursing a wounded **eagle** he had found on the mountainside; he said that caring for it gave him something to live for.\n\nThe **duration** of the crisis was longer than anyone had expected. Aftershocks continued for weeks, and at night the **echo** of falling rocks could be heard across the valley. Some injured people lay **dying** in remote villages that rescue teams could not **easily** reach.\n\nWhen the emergency phase ended, Lu Fang helped residents plan a more **economical** way to rebuild, using local materials and simple designs. She encouraged women to set up small businesses selling cloth coloured with natural **dye**. \"Recovery must be **dynamic**,\" she said. \"People need to **earn** a living, not just receive help.\""},"53":{"w":["economy","edge","edition","editor","editorial","educate","education","effect","effective","efficiency","efficient","effort","eighteen","eighty","elaborate","elastic","elbow","elder","elderly","elect"],"title":"The Editor at Eighty","t":"At **eighty**, Mrs. Qian is still the chief **editor** of a small regional newspaper. She began working there at **eighteen** as a typist, and over six decades she has seen the paper survive every kind of crisis, from political change to the collapse of the local **economy**.\n\nShe is known for her **elaborate** attention to detail. Every evening she reads each page of the next day's **edition** with a red pen, correcting grammar and checking facts. Her **editorial** column, published every Friday, discusses issues such as **education**, transport and the needs of **elderly** residents, and readers say her writing is clear, fair and surprisingly modern.\n\nYounger journalists admire her **efficiency**. She can write a complete article in twenty minutes and runs the newsroom in a remarkably **efficient** way. But she insists that speed matters less than **effort**. \"Good journalism must **educate** people,\" she says, \"not just entertain them.\"\n\nMrs. Qian believes the paper has had a real **effect** on the town. When the city council planned to close a primary school, her articles helped residents organise an **effective** campaign to keep it open. Two years later she supported a young candidate in the local council race; when he was **elected|elect**, he thanked her publicly.\n\nShe is small and thin, with sharp eyes and a habit of resting her **elbow** on the desk while she reads, and her colleagues treat her as the respected **elder** of the newsroom. Asked why she has not retired, she smiles and says that a newspaper needs an **elastic** mind more than young legs. \"Journalism keeps me on the **edge**,\" she says. \"That's why I am still here.\""},"54":{"w":["election","electric","electrical","electricity","electron","electronic","electronics","element","elementary","elephant","elevator","eliminate","elimination","elsewhere","embarrass","embrace","emerge","emergency","emit","emotion"],"title":"A Day at the Electronics Fair","t":"Every autumn our city hosts a large **electronics** fair, and this year I went with my younger brother, who is in his last year of **elementary** school. He is obsessed with anything **electronic**, from robots to game consoles, and he could not wait to see the latest inventions.\n\nThe fair took place in a huge hall that seemed to hum with **electricity**. We took a glass **elevator** to the top floor, where companies displayed **electric** cars, **electrical** tools and solar panels. One engineer explained that every device depends on the movement of the **electron**, and that understanding this basic **element** of physics is the key to all modern technology.\n\nMy brother's favourite stand belonged to a start-up that builds robots for zoos. Its latest model, a giant mechanical **elephant**, could walk, raise its trunk and **emit** a loud trumpeting sound. Visitors gathered around it with obvious **emotion**, and my brother laughed so loudly that I felt a little **embarrassed|embarrass**.\n\nIn another area, a company demonstrated a system designed to **eliminate** waste in factories. The **elimination** of unnecessary energy use, the speaker said, could cut costs by half. She also showed how her system responds to an **emergency**, such as a power cut, by switching automatically to batteries.\n\nAs we were leaving, a small drone seemed to **emerge** from behind a curtain and flew over our heads, to the delight of the crowd. My brother wanted to stay, but the hall was closing. \"We can find more robots **elsewhere**,\" I promised. He has already decided on his promise for the next student council **election**: a robotics club for every class. He wants every school to **embrace** technology the way he does."},"55":{"w":["emotional","emperor","emphasis","emphasize","empire","employ","employee","employer","employment","enable","enclose","encounter","encourage","ending","endless","endure","enemy","energy","enforce","engage"],"title":"Restoring the Past","t":"When Zhou Jie applied for a job at the Palace Museum, he did not expect to be hired. Thousands of people wanted to work there, and the museum could **employ** only a few each year. But his passion for the history of the Qing **empire** impressed the interview panel, and he became the youngest **employee** in the restoration department.\n\nHis **employer** placed great **emphasis** on patience. On his first day his supervisor told him that every object in the museum had belonged to someone, perhaps even an **emperor**, and that restoring it was a form of respect. She liked to **emphasize** that mistakes could not be undone.\n\nThe work was slow and sometimes frustrating. Restoring a single painted screen could take two years, and Zhou often felt that the task was **endless**. He had to **endure** long hours in silent rooms, bent over tiny cracks in the wood. Yet he found it deeply **emotional** to watch faded colours return to life under his brush.\n\nHe also learned that the past has a powerful **enemy**: time, dust and human carelessness. The museum must **enforce** strict rules to protect its collection, and visitors who **encounter** a guard may be reminded not to touch anything. Zhou believes that these rules **enable** future generations to enjoy the treasures.\n\nHis job has also given him new **energy** for teaching. He now runs workshops that **engage** children in simple restoration projects, and he likes to **encourage** them to ask questions. At the end of each session he gives every child a small card **enclosed|enclose** in a paper envelope, with a picture of the object they helped repair. Every story, he tells them, deserves a good **ending**, and he hopes some of them will one day find **employment** in heritage work themselves."},"56":{"w":["engine","engineer","engineering","enhance","enlarge","enormous","enroll","ensure","enter","entertain","entertainment","enthusiasm","enthusiastic","entire","entitle","entrance","entry","envelope","environment","envy"],"title":"The Engineering Camp","t":"When my niece decided to **enroll** in a summer **engineering** camp, the whole family was surprised. She had always been more interested in music and **entertainment** than in machines. But after watching a documentary about a young **engineer** who designed solar-powered cars, she filled in the application form, sealed it in an **envelope** and posted it the same day.\n\nThe camp was held in an **enormous** university workshop. On the first morning the students walked through the main **entrance** and found rows of tools, computers and an old car **engine** waiting to be taken apart. Their task for the week was to build a small vehicle that would **ensure** the safe delivery of an egg across the room.\n\nMy niece's team showed remarkable **enthusiasm**. They worked through lunch, argued about designs and tested every idea. One teammate suggested they **enlarge** the wheels to make the car more stable; another wanted to **enhance** its speed with a stronger motor. My niece, unexpectedly **enthusiastic**, kept notes in a notebook she later called \"my **entire** summer in one book\".\n\nOn the final day, every team had to **enter** its vehicle in a competition. Families came to watch, and the teachers tried to **entertain** them with jokes while the students made last-minute repairs. My niece's car carried its egg safely across the finish line, and her team's **entry** won second prize, which **entitled|entitle** them to a visit to a car factory.\n\nThe teachers also talked about the **environment**, explaining how engineers can design greener technology. My niece says she now feels a little **envy** whenever she sees engineers on television. \"Next year,\" she says, \"I want first prize.\""},"57":{"w":["episode","equal","equality","equation","equip","equipment","equivalent","era","erect","error","escape","especially","essay","essential","establish","establishment","estate","estimate","evacuate","evaluate"],"title":"The Night of the Typhoon","t":"When a powerful typhoon approached the coast last autumn, the local government decided to **evacuate** a small fishing village on an island. The order was not popular. Many residents were elderly, and some had lived through every storm of the past **era** without ever leaving home. \"We have survived worse,\" one old fisherman said.\n\nThe rescue team had to **estimate** how many people remained and **evaluate** the condition of the roads. Their leader, a calm woman named Captain Hou, explained that the most **essential** task was to make sure no one was left behind. Her team was well **equipped|equip**, with boats, radios and medical **equipment**, but she knew that technology could not replace trust.\n\nShe went from house to house, listening to each family. She understood that for many villagers their small **estate** of land and boats was their whole life, and that leaving felt like a betrayal. She promised that their property would be protected and that every person would be treated as an **equal**, with complete **equality** in the shelters.\n\nOne **episode** from that night became famous. An elderly woman refused to leave until she had finished writing a short **essay** for her grandson about the history of the village, and a man tried to **escape** back to his house to rescue his dog. Captain Hou did not lose patience. \"Every **error** in a crisis can cost a life,\" she said, \"so we take our time with each person, **especially** the old.\"\n\nThe typhoon destroyed many houses, but nobody died. Afterwards, the government helped the villagers **establish** a new shelter on higher ground and **erect** stronger sea walls, and the **establishment** of an early-warning system has since become a model for other islands. When reporters asked Captain Hou how she had done it, she smiled. \"There is no simple **equation**,\" she said. \"But trust is worth the **equivalent** of a hundred boats.\""},"58":{"w":["evaporate","eve","event","eventually","everyday","evidence","evident","evil","evolution","evolve","exact","exactly","exaggerate","examination","examine","exceed","exceedingly","excellent","exception","excess"],"title":"The Evolution Exam","t":"Every student in our biology class remembers the final **examination** on **evolution**. Professor Li, an **excellent** teacher with a sharp sense of humour, had warned us that he would **examine** not only our knowledge but also our ability to reason from **evidence**.\n\nOn the **eve** of the exam, our study group met in the library. We tried to memorise **exactly** how species **evolve** over millions of years, but it soon became **evident** that memorising was not enough. One friend kept trying to **exaggerate** the difficulty of the topic, calling it \"**exceedingly** impossible\", which made everyone laugh.\n\nThe exam itself was an unusual **event**. Instead of asking us to repeat definitions, Professor Li gave us a series of real cases: a lake whose water was beginning to **evaporate** faster each year, birds whose beaks were changing shape, insects that had become resistant to chemicals. We had to explain the changes and predict what might **eventually** happen.\n\nThe questions demanded **exact** reasoning. Answers that piled up **excess** detail without logic received low marks; the professor did not want us to **exceed** the word limit just to show off. He also included one trick question about whether evolution is \"good\" or \"**evil**\". The correct answer, of course, was that evolution has no moral direction.\n\nAlmost everyone passed, with only one **exception**: a student who had skipped classes all term. Years later, I still think about that exam whenever I notice small changes in **everyday** life, such as the sparrows in my garden. Professor Li taught us that science is not a list of facts but a way of looking at the world."},"59":{"w":["excessive","exchange","excite","excitement","exciting","exclaim","exclude","exclusive","exclusively","excursion","execute","executive","exert","exhaust","exhaustion","exhibit","exhibition","exist","existence","exit"],"title":"A Company Excursion","t":"Last month our company organised an **excursion** to a new science museum for all its staff. The idea came from our chief **executive**, who believes that employees work better when they **exchange** ideas outside the office. Not everyone was convinced; some colleagues complained that a museum trip was hardly **exciting**.\n\nThe museum's main **exhibition** was about energy. One room displayed a giant model of a power station; another showed how cars **exhaust** fumes into the air. A guide explained that **excessive** use of fossil fuels is one of the main causes of climate change, and that the **existence** of many species is now at risk.\n\nThe most popular **exhibit** was an interactive area where visitors could **exert** force on a bicycle to generate electricity. My colleagues competed to see who could light the most bulbs. Within ten minutes several of them were **exhausted|exhaust**, and one fell off the bike in **exhaustion**, to great **excitement** from the crowd. \"I didn't know we could still **excite** each other like this,\" our manager **exclaimed|exclaim**, laughing.\n\nThe museum also offered an **exclusive** tour of its research laboratories, open **exclusively** to groups who had booked in advance. Scientists showed us how they **execute** complex experiments and invited questions, taking care not to **exclude** those of us who knew nothing about physics.\n\nAt the **exit**, each visitor was given a small card with a question: \"What will you change tomorrow?\" Many of us still keep it on our desks. The trip reminded me that new ideas can **exist** anywhere, if we are willing to step outside our usual routine."},"60":{"w":["expand","expansion","expectation","expel","expend","expenditure","expense","experience","experiment","experimental","expert","explain","explanation","explode","exploit","explore","explosion","explosive","export","expose"],"title":"The Arctic Expedition","t":"For many years Dr. Zhou has been an **expert** in polar science. Last summer she led a team to **explore** a remote part of the Arctic, where melting ice has begun to **expose** rocks that have not seen sunlight for thousands of years.\n\nThe trip was expensive. The total **expenditure** came to more than two million yuan, and Dr. Zhou had to **explain** every **expense** to the university's finance committee. Some members questioned whether it was wise to **expend** so much money on a single trip. Her **explanation** was simple: the Arctic is changing faster than any other region, and understanding it is essential.\n\nHer team carried out an **experimental** study of how the ice reacts to rising temperatures. In one **experiment**, they drilled deep into a glacier to measure air bubbles trapped inside. The work was dangerous. Once, a gas pocket beneath the ice caused a small **explosion**, sending fragments of ice into the air. Nobody was hurt, but the team learned to treat the glacier as almost **explosive**.\n\nDr. Zhou also warned against efforts to **exploit** the region. As the ice melts, companies hope to **expand** oil drilling and **export** minerals, and the rapid **expansion** of shipping routes could cause serious pollution. \"We should not allow the Arctic to **explode** into a new industrial zone,\" she said.\n\nThe team returned with more data than anyone had expected, far beyond their own **expectations|expectation**, and Dr. Zhou describes the trip as the most intense **experience** of her life. It was not without difficulties: early in the journey, she admits, the team was forced to **expel** a member who repeatedly ignored safety rules. \"Science needs passion,\" she said, \"but it also needs discipline.\""},"61":{"w":["exposure","express","expression","extend","extension","extensive","extent","exterior","external","extinct","extra","extraordinary","extreme","extremely","eyesight","fabric","facility","fact","factor","faculty"],"title":"The New Science Building","t":"When the university decided to **extend** its science **faculty**, it planned a new building with **extensive** laboratories and a large greenhouse. The project attracted **extraordinary** attention, partly because of its unusual design: its **exterior** was covered in a light **fabric** made from recycled plastic that changes colour in the sunlight.\n\nThe architect explained that the shape of the building was not just decoration. One key **factor** in the design was protection from **extreme** weather. The fabric reduces **exposure** to strong sunlight in summer and keeps heat inside in winter. \"In **fact**,\" she said, \"the building uses forty per cent less energy than an ordinary one.\"\n\nThe new **facility** also includes an **extension** for the biology department, where researchers study species that are close to becoming **extinct**. They use **external** cameras to observe birds nesting on the roof, and they have discovered that some rare species are **extremely** sensitive to noise.\n\nNot everyone was pleased. Some students complained that the project had taken **extra** money from other departments. To what **extent**, they asked, should a university spend money on buildings rather than people? The president responded in a public letter, trying to **express** the university's long-term vision.\n\nAt the opening ceremony, a retired professor who had taught there for forty years gave a moving speech. His **eyesight** had weakened and he could barely read his notes, but his **expression** of joy was clear to everyone. \"Science,\" he said, \"is the art of looking carefully, with or without good eyes.\""},"62":{"w":["fade","fahrenheit","failure","faint","fair","fairly","faith","faithful","false","fame","familiar","famine","fan","fancy","fantasy","fare","farewell","farmer","farther","fashion"],"title":"The Singing Farmer","t":"Old Mr. Tang was an ordinary **farmer** until a short video of him singing in his fields went viral. Overnight he gained national **fame**. Millions of people watched him plough the land while singing folk songs in a strong, slightly rough voice, and his **fan** club soon grew to more than a hundred thousand members.\n\nThe story sounded like a modern **fantasy**, but Mr. Tang's life had been far from easy. As a child he had survived a terrible **famine**, when his family had little to eat for months. He remembered his mother's **faint** voice singing to him at night, and he said that her songs gave him **faith** that things would get better.\n\nFame did not change him much. He refused **fancy** clothes and expensive dinners and continued to work the land every day. When a television company invited him to perform in the capital, he insisted on paying his own train **fare**. It was only **fair**, he said, to treat everyone the same, rich or poor.\n\nHe had little patience for **false** rumours, either. When a newspaper claimed that his videos were staged, he replied calmly, \"I'm **fairly** sure I know how to sing in my own field.\" The story soon began to **fade**, and his **faithful** audience kept growing.\n\nMr. Tang said his biggest **failure** was never learning to read music; singing simply felt more **familiar** than reading notes. He disliked travelling any **farther** than the nearest town and once complained that the capital was \"a hundred degrees **Fahrenheit** and full of noise\". Yet his songs have since appeared in films and even **fashion** shows. At his last public concert, he waved **farewell** to the crowd and said simply, \"Go home and sing.\""},"63":{"w":["fashionable","fasten","fatal","fate","father-in-law","fatigue","fault","faulty","favor","favorable","favorite","fear","fearful","feasible","feast","feather","feature","federal","fee","feeble"],"title":"The Spring Festival Feast","t":"Every Spring Festival, my **father-in-law** prepares an enormous **feast** for the whole family. It is his **favorite** day of the year, and he spends a week planning the menu in a notebook marked with a single peacock **feather**.\n\nThis year, however, things went wrong. He was suffering from **fatigue** after a long illness and looked unusually **feeble**. My wife suggested ordering food from a restaurant, but he refused. \"It is my **fate** to cook for this family,\" he joked, \"and a small cold will not change it.\"\n\nThe first problem was the oven, which turned out to be **faulty**; the bread burned within minutes. My father-in-law insisted that it was not his **fault**. Then the electricity went off, and we had to **fasten** a torch to the kitchen wall so that he could see. My wife looked **fearful**, but he laughed and said he had cooked in far worse conditions during his years in the army.\n\nWe asked him to do us a **favor** and rest, but he would not. Instead, he gave each of us a job. The children washed vegetables, I chopped meat, and my wife set the table in a **fashionable** style she had seen in a magazine. By evening the meal was ready, and the mood could not have been more **favorable** for a celebration.\n\nThe main **feature** of the dinner was a whole fish cooked in the style of his hometown. During the meal, a cousin who works in a **federal** office in Canada called by video and joked about the **fee** he had paid to change his flight home. My father-in-law raised his glass and said that the only **fear** worth having is the fear of not trying. We agreed that his plan to cook alone had been barely **feasible**, but none of the evening's mistakes had proved **fatal**."},"64":{"w":["feed","feedback","feeling","fellow","female","fence","fertile","fertilizer","festival","fetch","fever","fiction","fierce","fight","figure","file","filter","final","finally","finance"],"title":"The Fertilizer Experiment","t":"Last spring, a group of agricultural students at our university decided to test whether natural **fertilizer** could replace chemicals on small farms. Their project was supported by a local **finance** company and a professor who had spent his career studying **fertile** soils in the south.\n\nThe students worked with a **female** farmer named Mrs. Huang, who had been growing vegetables on the same land for thirty years. They divided her field into two parts with a wooden **fence**. On one side they used chemical fertilizer; on the other, a mixture of compost and animal manure. Each week they would **fetch** soil samples and **file** reports in the university database.\n\nThe work was hard. One student fell ill with a high **fever** after a week of rain, and others struggled with the smell. They had to **filter** water samples to test for pollution, and **fierce** storms damaged several rows of plants. But they refused to give up. \"This is a **fight** for the future of farming,\" one student said.\n\nMrs. Huang gave them honest **feedback** throughout the season. She showed them how to **feed** the soil with crop waste and how to judge the health of plants from the colour of their leaves, and she told them about **fellow** farmers who had lost money after years of using too many chemicals.\n\nThe **final** results were clear. The natural side produced slightly fewer vegetables, but their quality was higher and the soil was healthier. When the students presented their **figures|figure** at an agricultural **festival**, the audience was impressed. **Finally**, Mrs. Huang said, someone had proved with data what farmers had always sensed. \"It's not **fiction**,\" she said. \"It's my **feeling** after thirty years.\""},"65":{"w":["financial","finding","finger","fireman","firm","fisherman","fist","fix","flag","flame","flare","flash","flat","flee","fleet","flesh","flexible","float","flock","flood"],"title":"The Night of the Flood","t":"Last July, heavy rain caused a sudden **flood** in a fishing village on the southern coast. The water rose so quickly that many families had to **flee** their homes in the middle of the night, carrying only what they could hold in their hands.\n\nAmong the first to help was a **fisherman** named Chen, who used his boat to rescue neighbours from rooftops. He was soon joined by a **fleet** of small boats from nearby villages and a team of firefighters. One **fireman** later described how Chen worked for twelve hours without stopping, one **fist** tight on the wheel and his **fingers|finger** bleeding from the ropes.\n\nAt one point a gas pipe broke, and a **flame** shot out of the water near a flooded shop. The firefighters fired a **flare** to warn other boats to keep away, and a **flash** of red light lit up the dark sky. Chen steered his boat around the danger and continued his rescue work.\n\nBy morning, the land around the village lay **flat** and covered in mud. Broken furniture would **float** past the remaining houses, and a **flock** of birds circled overhead, searching for food. Rescuers raised a red **flag** on the school roof to show people where they could find shelter.\n\nThe government later published a report on the **financial** cost of the disaster. Its main **finding** was that the village needed better drainage and a more **flexible** emergency plan, and a local engineering **firm** offered to **fix** the sea wall for free. Chen received a medal for bravery, but he refused to talk about it. \"We are all **flesh** and blood,\" he said. \"We help each other.\""},"66":{"w":["flour","flourish","flow","flu","fluent","fluid","flush","focus","fog","fold","folk","follow","following","fond","fool","foolish","footstep","forbid","force","forecast"],"title":"The Baker in the Fog","t":"Every morning before dawn, Mr. Zhao walks to his bakery through the **fog** that rolls in from the river, his **footsteps|footstep** echoing along the empty street. By five o'clock he is already covered in **flour**, kneading dough that will **flourish** into dozens of golden loaves by sunrise.\n\nThe bakery started as a small family business, but it now attracts customers from all over the city. Mr. Zhao says the secret is simple: **follow** old recipes, use fresh ingredients and never rush. He is especially **fond** of a traditional bread made with honey and nuts, and he likes to **fold** the dough by hand exactly as his grandmother did.\n\nLast winter, when **flu** spread through the neighbourhood, Mr. Zhao delivered bread for free to elderly customers who could not leave their homes. \"It would be **foolish** to worry about profit at a time like that,\" he said. \"Only a **fool** forgets his neighbours.\"\n\nHe has also learned some English, mainly because so many tourists now visit. He is not yet **fluent**, but he can explain each type of bread and tell **folk** tales about the old city. When a group of foreign visitors once praised his work, his face began to **flush** with pride.\n\nHe has a few strict rules. He will **forbid** anyone to use a phone in the kitchen, because he wants his staff to **focus** completely on their work. He believes that baking depends on the calm **flow** of a quiet mind, and that you cannot **force** good bread. He checks the weather **forecast** every evening, because humidity changes how the dough behaves, and he knows exactly how much **fluid** each recipe needs. On the **following** morning, he starts all over again."},"67":{"w":["forehead","foreigner","foremost","forest","forever","forgive","fork","formal","formation","former","formula","forth","fortnight","fortunate","fortunately","fortune","found","foundation","fountain","fox"],"title":"The Mountain Schools Foundation","t":"Ten years ago, a **former** teacher named Mrs. Ruan decided to **found** a small charity to help children in mountain villages. She began with almost nothing: not a **fortune**, but a few thousand yuan saved from her salary, and a clear idea. Today her **foundation** supports more than forty schools.\n\nMrs. Ruan likes to say that she has been **fortunate**. **Fortunately**, many people were willing to help from the start. A **foreigner** who had worked in the area as a volunteer donated money for books, and villagers built a small **fountain** in one schoolyard, which became a symbol of the project.\n\nHer approach is simple but effective. She avoids **formal** speeches and prefers to talk with families in their homes. She believes that the **foremost** goal of education is to give children confidence, and she once said that there is no **formula** for success, only patience and honesty.\n\nEvery **fortnight** she visits one of the schools in person. The journey often takes her through a thick **forest**, along narrow roads that **fork** in different directions, and she has learned to recognise every turn. Once, on a dark evening, a **fox** ran across the road in front of her car, and she laughed and said it was a good sign.\n\nNot everything has gone well. Some officials were suspicious of her work at first, and she had to **forgive** a few people who spread rumours about her. But she believes the **formation** of trust simply takes time. On the wall of her office hangs a photo of a boy gently touching the **forehead** of a newborn calf, a moment she says she will remember **forever**. \"We keep going back and **forth**,\" she says, \"but every trip brings us a little closer.\""},"68":{"w":["fraction","fragment","frame","framework","frank","freedom","freely","freeze","freight","frequency","frequent","frequently","fresh","friction","fridge","frighten","frog","frontier","frost","frown"],"title":"Three Winters on the Frontier","t":"For three years, Corporal Ma served at a small post on the northern **frontier**, where winter temperatures can fall to forty degrees below zero. The **frost** was so heavy that the windows stayed covered in ice for months, and the water in the pipes would **freeze** every night.\n\nLife at the post was simple. Supplies arrived by **freight** truck once a week, and the soldiers had a small **fridge** that was almost unnecessary, since everything stayed frozen outdoors anyway. Vegetables were rare, and a single **fresh** tomato was considered a feast.\n\nThe soldiers were **frank** with each other about their feelings. Long nights and isolation could create **friction** in a small group, so the commander encouraged them to talk **freely** about their problems. \"We are here to protect the **freedom** of others,\" he would say, \"but we must also protect each other.\"\n\nCorporal Ma kept a diary in which he recorded every unusual event: a wolf that seemed to **frighten** the dogs by howling outside the gate, a **frog** that somehow survived in a puddle of melting snow in May, the strange **frequency** of storms in early spring. Snowstorms were **frequent**, and he **frequently** described them in vivid detail.\n\nHe also kept a small **fragment** of rock from the mountain, polished and placed in a wooden **frame** on his desk; it reminded him that even the largest mountain is made of small pieces. When he finally left the post, his commander shook his hand with a slight **frown**, unwilling to show his emotion. Corporal Ma now works in a city office, but he says only a **fraction** of his heart has come back with him. The **framework** of his adult life, he says, was built at the frontier."},"69":{"w":["fruitful","frustrate","fry","fuel","function","fund","fundamental","funeral","funny","fur","furious","furnace","furnish","furniture","further","furthermore","gain","gallery","gallon","gang"],"title":"My Uncle's Workshop","t":"My uncle runs a small **furniture** workshop on the edge of the city. He learned the trade from his father, and he still uses some of the old equipment, including a **furnace** in the yard where he heats metal for handles and hinges. The workshop has a **funny** smell, a mixture of wood, oil and smoke, that I have loved since childhood.\n\nHis business has had difficult years. When cheap factory-made furniture became popular, he lost many customers, and the competition began to **frustrate** all his plans. He even considered closing down. But he applied for a small grant from a government **fund** that supports traditional crafts, and the money helped him **gain** new skills in design.\n\nNow he makes furniture that combines old techniques with modern **function**. His chairs are light and strong, and his tables fold away neatly in small flats. He can **furnish** whole apartments for young couples, and some of his pieces are displayed in an art **gallery** downtown. **Furthermore**, he teaches free classes for teenagers, some of whom were once members of a street **gang**.\n\nHe believes that hard work is the most **fundamental** part of any craft. \"You cannot **fry** an egg without heat,\" he likes to say, \"and you cannot make a good chair without sweat.\" He once became **furious** when a customer suggested he use cheap glue to save time.\n\nHis father's **funeral** last year was a quiet event attended by dozens of former apprentices. They brought simple gifts: a jar of homemade honey, a **gallon** of varnish, a coat lined with **fur** for the winter. My uncle says the most **fruitful** lesson his father taught him was to keep learning. He plans to expand the workshop **further** next year, and he says his father's memory will **fuel** his work for the rest of his life."},"70":{"w":["gap","garage","garbage","gardener","gas","gaseous","gasoline","gasp","gather","gauge","gay","gaze","gear","gene","general","generally","generate","generation","generator","generous"],"title":"The Street Gardener","t":"In our neighbourhood everyone knows Mr. Bai, the retired engineer who has become the street's unofficial **gardener**. Every morning he walks along the road with a bucket and a small trowel, planting flowers in every **gap** between the paving stones and collecting the **garbage** that others have left behind.\n\nBefore he retired, Mr. Bai spent forty years at a power station, maintaining the huge **generators|generator** that **generate** electricity for the city. He can explain how a **gas** turns into a liquid, why **gaseous** fuels burn cleanly and how much **gasoline** an average car uses. But he says nothing in his career gave him as much joy as watching a seed grow.\n\nHis **garage** is full of tools, seed packets and an old bicycle with rusty **gears|gear**. He uses a simple **gauge** to measure rainfall and keeps careful notes on which plants do best. Neighbours often **gather** in front of his house in the evening to chat, and children **gaze** in wonder at his sunflowers, which grow taller than any adult.\n\nMr. Bai is famous for being **generous**. He gives seedlings to anyone who asks and has taught a whole **generation** of children how to grow vegetables. \"Gardening is not about **genes|gene** or luck,\" he says. \"It's about patience.\" In **general** he prefers to work quietly, but he becomes cheerful and talkative whenever someone asks for advice.\n\nLast spring, during the Lantern Festival, his garden was hung with **gay** paper lanterns, and visitors would **gasp** at the colours. Most people **generally** agree that ours is now the most beautiful street in the district, thanks to one man and his bucket."},"71":{"w":["genetic","genius","gentle","gentleman","gently","genuine","geography","geology","geometry","germ","gesture","ghost","giant","glance","glare","glide","glimpse","glitter","global","globe"],"title":"Into the Mountains with Professor Wei","t":"Every spring Professor Wei takes her first-year **geology** students on a week-long field trip to the mountains. She is a **gentle** woman with a sharp mind, and many students consider her a **genius**. Her lectures combine **geography**, physics and even **geometry**, as she shows how the angles of rock layers reveal the history of the earth.\n\nOn the first morning, the group climbed a steep path to a viewpoint above the valley. Professor Wei pointed to an enormous rock shaped like a sleeping **giant** and explained that it had been shaped by ice millions of years ago. The students were allowed only a brief **glance** before she led them to a cave, where they caught a **glimpse** of crystals that seemed to **glitter** in the light of their torches.\n\nShe encourages students to touch the rocks **gently** and to observe carefully, and she uses a simple **gesture**, raising one hand, whenever she wants them to stop and listen to the wind or the water. One student joked that the cave was haunted by a **ghost**; she laughed and said that the only ghosts in geology are the fossils of creatures that died long ago.\n\nProfessor Wei also teaches about **global** issues. She explained how rising temperatures are affecting glaciers around the **globe**, and how scientists use **genetic** studies of ancient plants to understand past climates. She also reminded students to wash their hands carefully after handling soil, since it can contain harmful **germs|germ**.\n\nAt the end of the trip, as the students watched eagles **glide** over the valley, an old **gentleman** from a nearby village came to thank the group for clearing litter from the path. Professor Wei said that his **genuine** kindness was the best lesson of the week. The setting sun began to **glare** off the rocks as they walked back to the bus."},"72":{"w":["gloomy","glorious","glory","glove","glow","glue","goal","goat","gold","golden","golf","goodness","goods","goose","govern","government","governor","gown","grace","graceful"],"title":"The Governor and the Golden Goose","t":"Long ago, in a small town in the north, there lived a **governor** famous for his **goodness**. Unlike many officials, he did not **govern** by fear but by fairness; he believed that a **government** should serve the people, not the other way round.\n\nThe town was poor, and its winters were long and **gloomy**. Most families kept a **goat** or a **goose** for milk and eggs, and the only **goods** sold at the market were vegetables and wool. One winter a farmer brought the governor a strange gift: a goose whose feathers seemed to **glow** in the sunlight like **gold**. People began to call it \"the **golden** goose\", and crowds came from far away to see it.\n\nThe governor could have sold the bird for a fortune, but he refused. Instead, he used the visitors' curiosity to help the town. He organised a festival with games, music and even a simple form of **golf** played with sticks and wooden balls, and local women sewed colourful **gowns|gown** and woollen **gloves|glove** to sell to the visitors.\n\nThe festival brought **glory** to the town, and within a few years its fortunes had become **glorious**. The governor's **goal** had never been personal wealth but the dignity of his people. When he grew old, he handed over power with **grace**, and the townspeople remembered him for his **graceful** manners and simple life.\n\nAccording to the story, the golden goose lived for twenty years. When it died, the villagers made a small wooden statue of it, using a special **glue** to fix real feathers to the body. The statue, they say, still stands in the town square today."},"73":{"w":["gracious","gradual","gradually","graduate","grain","grammar","grammatical","grand","granddaughter","grandfather","grandmother","grandson","grant","grape","graph","grasp","grateful","gratitude","grave","gravity"],"title":"A Graduate's Gratitude","t":"When my cousin Lin received her degree last summer, she became the first person in our family to **graduate** from university. At the ceremony our **grandfather** sat in the front row in his best suit, holding a bunch of flowers. Our **grandmother** cried quietly, and Lin's younger brother, their only **grandson**, took photos of everything.\n\nLin's journey had not been easy. Our grandparents were farmers who grew **grain** and **grapes|grape** on a small plot of land, and they had never had the chance to study. Lin was the eldest **granddaughter**, and she spent much of her childhood helping in the fields. Her progress at school was **gradual**: she struggled with English **grammar** at first, and her essays were often full of **grammatical** mistakes.\n\nBut she was determined. She practised every evening, drawing a **graph** of her test scores on her bedroom wall and watching the line rise slowly. **Gradually** she began to **grasp** the logic of the language, and by her final year she was the top student in her class. A local company then offered her a **grant** to study economics in the city.\n\nIn her speech at the ceremony, Lin spoke about the **gravity** of the choices her family had made. Her grandparents had sold part of their land to pay for her education, a **grave** decision for people who depended on farming. She told the audience that she was deeply **grateful** and that no words could express her **gratitude**.\n\nAfter the speech, the president of the university, a **gracious** elderly man, came to shake our grandfather's hand. It was a **grand** moment for our family. Grandfather said little, but that evening he told me it was the happiest day of his life."},"74":{"w":["gray","greatly","greedy","greenhouse","greet","greeting","grieve","grind","grip","groan","grocer","grocery","gross","growth","guarantee","guard","guidance","guide","guilty","gulf"],"title":"The Grocer on the Corner","t":"Mr. Ou has run a small **grocery** on our street for twenty-five years. As a **grocer**, he sells everything from rice and oil to fresh vegetables, many of which he grows himself in a **greenhouse** behind the shop. He likes to **greet** every customer by name, and his warm **greeting** is the first thing many of us hear each morning.\n\nHis hair has turned **gray**, but he still works long hours. Every morning he uses an old machine to **grind** coffee beans for his regular customers, and he will happily **guide** newcomers around the shelves, offering cooking tips. His **guidance** is especially valued by students living away from home for the first time.\n\nA few years ago the business went through a difficult period. A large supermarket chain opened nearby, and its **gross** sales were many times higher than Mr. Ou's. Some people said he would not survive. He admits that he would sometimes **groan** at the thought of competing with such a giant, but he refused to become **greedy** or cut corners. Instead, he offered a simple **guarantee**: if any product was not fresh, customers could return it for free.\n\nHis honesty helped the shop survive and even enjoy steady **growth**, and customers say they trust him **greatly**. Once, when a security **guard** at the supermarket caught a boy stealing sweets, it was Mr. Ou who offered to pay and to talk with the boy. The child felt so **guilty** that he now helps in the shop every weekend.\n\nLast year Mr. Ou's wife died, and he closed the shop for two weeks to **grieve**. When he returned, the whole street was waiting; neighbours had filled the shop with flowers, and one child had written a card that read, \"Thank you for keeping your **grip** on all of us.\" Mr. Ou says the **gulf** between big business and small shops is enormous, but he believes kindness can cross it."},"75":{"w":["gum","gun","gunpowder","guy","gymnasium","habit","habitual","haircut","hall","halt","hamburger","hammer","handful","handkerchief","handle","handsome","handwriting","handy","hang","happen"],"title":"The Old Gymnasium","t":"Our school's old **gymnasium** was built in 1960, and it has seen generations of students come and go. Its wooden floor creaks under every step, and the long **hall** outside is lined with photos of past teams. On one wall hangs a faded poster about the history of **gunpowder**, left over from a science fair decades ago.\n\nThe man who looks after the building is Mr. Song, a cheerful **guy** in his sixties who has worked there for thirty years. He has a **habit** of whistling while he works, and his **habitual** greeting, \"Morning, champions!\", makes even tired students smile. He keeps a **hammer** and a box of nails in his pocket and can **handle** almost any repair, from broken chairs to loose basketball hoops.\n\nMr. Song is proud of his building. Before every sports day he cleans the floor by hand and scrapes off every piece of chewing **gum** stuck under the seats. He keeps a first-aid kit **handy** and always carries a clean **handkerchief**, which he once used to stop a student's nosebleed.\n\nHe also has a strong sense of humour. Every year, when the athletics coach fires the starting **gun** at the school race, Mr. Song pretends to jump in fright. He likes to **hang** small flags from the ceiling, and for one school food festival he even suspended a giant cardboard **hamburger** above the door.\n\nLast month the school announced that the gymnasium would soon be replaced, and that construction might **halt** indoor sports for a year. A **handful** of former students, now adults, came back to say goodbye. One of them, a **handsome** man in his forties with a fresh **haircut**, brought a card written in his best **handwriting**. \"Whatever may **happen**,\" he wrote, \"this place will always be home.\""},"76":{"w":["happiness","harden","hardly","hardship","hardware","hare","harm","harmful","harmony","harness","harsh","harvest","haste","hasten","hasty","hatch","hate","hateful","hatred","hawk"],"title":"The Harvest Festival","t":"Every autumn my grandparents' village celebrates the rice **harvest** with a festival that lasts three days. For the farmers it is a time of **happiness** after months of **hardship**. This year the summer had been **harsh**, with weeks of drought followed by sudden storms, and many families had feared they would **hardly** have enough to eat.\n\nMy grandfather has farmed the same fields for sixty years, and he believes that farming teaches **harmony** between people and nature. \"If you work against the land, you only **harm** yourself,\" he says. He refuses to use **harmful** chemicals and instead keeps ducks to eat the insects in his rice fields. Every spring he watches the eggs **hatch** and the ducklings grow.\n\nHe is a patient man with no time for **haste**. \"A **hasty** decision in farming can cost you a whole year,\" he tells me. When the rice is ready, he does not **hasten** the work but waits for the right day. He still remembers the time when farmers would **harness** buffalo to pull their ploughs; now the village shares a small tractor bought from the **hardware** store in town.\n\nVillage life is not always peaceful. My grandfather once had a bitter quarrel with a neighbour over water rights, and for years the two men seemed to **hate** each other. But he says that **hatred** only makes the heart **harden**. When the neighbour fell ill, my grandfather helped bring in his crops, and they became friends again. \"Being **hateful** wastes energy you need for work,\" he said.\n\nOn the last evening of the festival, the children ran into the fields to chase a **hare**, while a **hawk** circled slowly in the sky above. My grandfather watched them and smiled."},"77":{"w":["hay","hazard","headache","heading","headline","headmaster","headquarters","heal","heap","heat","heating","heaven","heavily","hedge","heel","height","heir","helicopter","hell","helmet"],"title":"Rescue in the Snow","t":"Last winter, a group of hikers became trapped on a mountain after a sudden snowstorm. Snow fell **heavily** for two days, and the temperature dropped far below zero. When the news reached the rescue team's **headquarters**, the commander immediately sent a **helicopter**.\n\nThe pilot, a calm woman named Captain Zhao, had flown dozens of rescue missions, but she said this one was among the most difficult. The wind was strong, and the **height** of the mountain made flying dangerous. Every **hazard** had to be considered, from falling ice to poor visibility, and before take-off each member of the crew put on a **helmet** and checked the radio.\n\nThe hikers had taken shelter beside a **heap** of rocks behind a low **hedge** of mountain shrubs. One of them, a **headmaster** from a nearby town, was suffering from a terrible **headache** caused by the altitude; another had injured his **heel** on the ice. They had used a bundle of dry **hay** from an abandoned shepherd's hut to keep a small fire going, and its **heat** was all that kept them alive.\n\nCaptain Zhao managed to land on a narrow ridge. The crew wrapped the hikers in blankets and turned up the **heating** inside the helicopter. One hiker said later that the warm cabin felt like **heaven**. \"Those two days were **hell**,\" he admitted.\n\nThe next morning, the rescue appeared under a large **headline** in the local newspaper, and the first **heading** of the article praised the pilot's courage. Doctors said the injured hiker's foot would **heal** within weeks. Another hiker, the **heir** to a family tea business, later donated money to buy new equipment for the rescue team."},"78":{"w":["helpful","helpless","hen","hence","herd","hero","heroic","heroine","hesitate","hide","highlight","highly","highway","hillside","hint","hire","historical","history","hit","hole"],"title":"The Heroine of the Highway","t":"One rainy evening last spring, a truck overturned on a mountain **highway** and blocked both lanes. Dozens of cars stopped, and the drivers stood **helpless** in the rain, unsure what to do. The driver of the truck was trapped inside, and smoke was rising from the engine.\n\nA young nurse named Li Wen did not **hesitate**. She climbed down the wet **hillside**, broke the window with a stone and pulled the driver out moments before the engine caught fire. Other drivers rushed to help, and someone called an ambulance. Later the newspapers called her \"the **heroine** of the highway\", and a video of the rescue became a **hit** online.\n\nLi Wen refused to see herself as a **hero**. She said she had simply done what any nurse would do, and that everyone who stopped had been **helpful**. \"There was no **heroic** decision,\" she said. \"I just saw a man who needed help.\" She gave only a **hint** of the danger she had faced: her hands had been cut by broken glass, and one shoe had been lost in a **hole** full of mud.\n\nReporters tried to **highlight** her bravery, but she preferred to talk about road safety. She pointed out that this stretch of road has a long **history** of accidents, and that **historical** records show dozens of crashes in the past decade. **Hence**, she argued, the government should improve the lighting and barriers.\n\nLi Wen grew up on a farm, where her mother kept a **hen** house and her father looked after a **herd** of goats, and she says she learned courage from them. She does not like to **hide** from difficult situations. Her hospital now plans to **hire** more emergency nurses, and Li Wen is **highly** respected by her colleagues."},"79":{"w":["hollow","holy","honest","honesty","honey","honeymoon","hook","hopeful","hopeless","horizon","horizontal","horn","horrible","horror","horsepower","host","hostess","hostile","household","housewife"],"title":"The Mountain Guesthouse","t":"When my cousin and her husband went on their **honeymoon**, they chose a small guesthouse in the mountains rather than an expensive hotel by the sea. Their **host**, an elderly farmer, and his wife, the **hostess**, welcomed them with tea and fresh **honey** from their own bees.\n\nThe house was simple but full of character. It had been built a century ago, and each room had a **horizontal** wooden beam across the ceiling, from which hung an old iron **hook** for drying herbs. A small **holy** shrine stood in a corner of the courtyard, decorated with flowers. My cousin said the atmosphere was peaceful and **hopeful**, nothing like the busy city.\n\nThe farmer's wife was a proud **housewife** who managed the **household** with great energy, and she was refreshingly **honest**. When my cousin asked whether the nearby lake was good for swimming, she said, \"Not unless you like ice.\" Her **honesty** made them laugh, and they trusted her advice for the rest of the week.\n\nOne evening, however, something **horrible** happened. A car drove into the village with its **horn** sounding non-stop, and two men began shouting at the farmer. They were **hostile** and claimed that he owed them money. My cousin watched in **horror** as the argument grew louder, but the farmer remained calm and eventually explained that the men had come to the wrong house.\n\nThe next morning, the farmer drove them to a viewpoint in his old tractor, which had only twenty **horsepower** but climbed the steep road without trouble. They watched the sun rise over the **horizon**, and my cousin said it was the most beautiful sight of her life. \"Nothing is **hopeless**,\" the farmer said, pointing to a green shoot growing out of the **hollow** trunk of an old tree."},"80":{"w":["however","huge","human","humble","humid","humor","humorous","hunger","hunt","hurt","hut","hydrogen","ice-cream","ideal","identical","identification","identify","identity","idiom","idle"],"title":"An Idle Summer","t":"Last summer, after my final exams, I spent two months doing almost nothing. At first, being **idle** felt like the **ideal** reward for a year of hard work. I slept late, ate **ice-cream** for breakfast and watched films until midnight. **However**, after a few weeks I began to feel strangely empty.\n\nMy grandmother noticed. She invited me to stay with her in a **humble** wooden **hut** at the edge of a forest, where she spends every summer. The air was **humid** and the nights were full of insects, but inside there was a **huge** collection of old books. \"A good book cures the **hunger** of the mind,\" she said.\n\nShe has a gentle sense of **humor** and loves to tell stories. One evening she told me about a **humorous** misunderstanding from her youth: she had once tried to explain a Chinese **idiom** to a foreign visitor and ended up confusing everyone. She taught me that the key to learning is not memorising facts but trying to **identify** what truly interests you.\n\nDuring the day we went walking in the forest. She showed me where villagers used to **hunt** for deer and how to recognise **identical** twin trees growing from the same root. She also explained a little chemistry, telling me how **hydrogen** combines with oxygen to form water, and said that science is a deeply **human** activity, born of curiosity.\n\nOne day I slipped on a rock and **hurt** my ankle. While I rested, she helped me with the **identification** of birds from an old guidebook. By the end of the summer I felt I had found a new sense of **identity**: not just a student who passes exams, but someone who wants to understand the world."},"81":{"w":["ignorant","ignore","illegal","illness","illusion","illustrate","illustration","image","imaginary","imagination","imagine","imitate","immediate","immediately","immense","immigrant","immigrate","impact","impatient","implement"],"title":"Drawing a New Home","t":"When Ana's family decided to **immigrate** to China twenty years ago, she was only seven. As a young **immigrant**, she felt completely **ignorant** of the language and culture, and she often felt that the other children would **ignore** her in the playground. To cope, she began to draw. Her pictures were full of **imaginary** animals and cities that existed only in her mind.\n\nHer teachers noticed her **imagination** and encouraged her. One teacher asked her to **illustrate** a school storybook, and Ana spent weeks on the work. Each **illustration** was detailed and colourful, and the book was printed for the whole school. \"I could not speak well,\" Ana says, \"but I could make people **imagine** what I felt.\"\n\nAs a teenager she suffered a long **illness** and spent months at home. She used the time to **imitate** the styles of famous artists, from Chinese ink painters to European masters, and she says she learned that originality is partly an **illusion**: every artist borrows from others before finding a personal voice.\n\nToday Ana is a successful illustrator, and her work has had an **immense** **impact** on children's publishing. She often visits schools and is **impatient** with people who say that art is a waste of time. She has also helped to **implement** a programme that teaches art to children from immigrant families.\n\nShe warns young artists about one danger: copying other people's work and selling it is **illegal**, and it can destroy an artist's reputation **immediately**. \"Your **image** is everything,\" she says. When I asked about her most **immediate** goal, she laughed. \"To finish my next book before my editor loses patience.\""},"82":{"w":["implication","imply","import","importance","impose","impossible","impress","impression","impressive","imprison","improve","improvement","inch","incident","incline","include","income","incorrect","increase","increasingly"],"title":"The Coffee Importer","t":"Ten years ago, Mr. Kong started a small business to **import** coffee beans from Africa. At first most people thought the idea was **impossible**. Coffee was still a minor drink in his hometown, and his **income** in the first year was barely enough to pay the rent.\n\nBut Mr. Kong understood the **importance** of quality. He travelled to farms in Ethiopia and Kenya and spent weeks learning how coffee was grown, because he wanted to **impress** his customers with beans that were fresh and fairly traded. His first visit left a deep **impression** on him: the farmers worked extremely hard, yet earned very little.\n\nHe decided to **improve** the system. Instead of buying through middlemen, he paid farmers directly and helped them invest in better equipment. The **improvement** in quality was **impressive**. Over the years demand began to **increase**, and coffee became **increasingly** popular among young people in his city.\n\nNot everything went smoothly. In one **incident**, a shipment was held at the port for months because customs officials claimed the paperwork was **incorrect**. Mr. Kong said the delay seemed to **imply** that someone wanted a bribe, but he refused to pay. The **implication** frightened his partners, who feared he might even be **imprisoned|imprison** on some false charge. He was not, and the shipment was eventually released.\n\nToday his company has thirty employees. Mr. Kong says he would never **impose** his own taste on customers, but he likes to **incline** them gently towards better coffee. His shops **include** a small room where people can watch the beans being roasted. \"Every **inch** of this business,\" he says, \"was built on patience.\""},"83":{"w":["indeed","indefinite","independence","independent","index","indicate","indication","indifferent","indignant","indirect","indispensable","individual","indoor","indoors","industrial","industrialize","industry","inefficient","inevitable","inexpensive"],"title":"Life After Steel","t":"For more than a century, our town depended on a single **industry**: steel. It was one of the first places in the province to **industrialize**, and its **industrial** history can be seen everywhere, from old chimneys to rows of workers' houses. But in the 1990s the steel mills became **inefficient**, and many closed. Their decline seemed **inevitable**.\n\nSome residents became **indifferent** to the town's future and moved away. Others were **indignant** that the government had done so little to help. Yet a group of young people refused to give up. They believed that every **individual** could play a part in rebuilding the community, and that **independence** from a single employer was the key.\n\nThey started small. One opened an **indoor** climbing centre in an old factory hall; another turned a warehouse into an **inexpensive** café where students could study **indoors** on cold days. A local teacher created an online **index** of the town's history, collecting photos and memories from older residents.\n\nRecent figures **indicate** that the town is slowly recovering. The number of new businesses has doubled in five years, a clear **indication** that the strategy is working. **Indeed**, many people who once left have returned.\n\nOf course, problems remain. The future is still **indefinite**, and the economy depends on **indirect** support from tourism and services. But the young founders believe that community spirit is **indispensable**. \"We are **independent** now,\" one of them said. \"We don't wait for a factory to save us.\""},"84":{"w":["infamous","infant","infect","infer","inferior","infinite","influence","influential","inform","ingredient","inhabit","inhabitant","inherit","initial","initiative","injection","injure","injury","ink","inn"],"title":"The Three-Hundred-Year-Old Inn","t":"On the edge of an ancient village in the south stands an old **inn** that has welcomed travellers for three hundred years. Its current owner, Mrs. He, **inherited|inherit** it from her grandmother. The building is famous for its wooden carvings, and some say it was once used by an **infamous** gang of smugglers who hid stolen goods in the cellar.\n\nMrs. He's **initial** plan was to sell the inn, but she changed her mind after reading her grandmother's diaries. Written in faded **ink**, they described every guest who had stayed there, from merchants to poets. Mrs. He felt she had a duty to the village and its **inhabitants|inhabitant**, many of whose families continue to **inhabit** the same houses after generations.\n\nShe took the **initiative** to restore the inn using traditional methods, hiring local craftsmen and insisting on natural materials. When one of the workers fell from a ladder and suffered a serious **injury**, she paid all his medical costs and visited him in hospital every day. He needed an **injection** of painkillers twice a day, and she worried that the accident would **injure** the project's reputation.\n\nToday the inn is popular with travellers who want an authentic experience. Its kitchen uses only local **ingredients|ingredient**, and guests are invited to cook with Mrs. He's family. Visitors often **inform** her that they feel as if they have travelled back in time. During flu season she cleans every room thoroughly so that guests do not **infect** one another.\n\nThe inn has also become **influential** in the region, inspiring other villages to preserve their heritage, and Mrs. He says her grandmother's **influence** is everywhere. \"From her diaries I could **infer** that she loved this place more than anything,\" she says. A guest once told her that modern hotels are **inferior** to her inn in every way except internet speed. Mrs. He laughed: \"The patience you learn here is **infinite**.\" Above the reception desk hangs a photo of her as an **infant** in her grandmother's arms."},"85":{"w":["inner","innocent","input","inquire","inquiry","insect","insert","inside","insight","insignificant","insist","inspect","inspection","inspire","install","installation","instance","instant","instantly","instinct"],"title":"Checking the Dam","t":"Every year, before the summer holidays, a team of engineers comes to **inspect** the old dam above our town. The **inspection** lasts three days, and its results are published in the local newspaper. This year I was invited to join the team as a student volunteer.\n\nThe team leader, Ms. Hu, has an **instinct** for detecting problems. She walks along the dam slowly, touching the concrete and listening to the water. \"Numbers are important,\" she said, \"but you also need **insight**.\" She showed me how a tiny crack that looks **insignificant** from the outside can reveal serious damage to the **inner** structure.\n\nMy job was to **insert** small sensors into holes along the wall and record the **input** from each device on a laptop. The monitoring system was **installed|install** five years ago, and the **installation** has already prevented several dangerous situations. In one **instance**, it detected water pressure rising **instantly** after a heavy storm, giving engineers time to open the gates.\n\nAt lunchtime we sat **inside** a small control room, where a strange long-legged **insect** kept landing on my sandwich. Ms. Hu laughed and said that even insects **insist** on joining the inspection. She told me she had once been accused of exaggerating a problem, but an official **inquiry** later proved her right. \"Always **inquire** further when something feels wrong,\" she said. \"People's lives depend on it.\"\n\nThe experience helped to **inspire** my interest in engineering. When the report was published, it stated that the dam was safe, an **instant** relief for the whole town. As we left, a little girl asked Ms. Hu, with an **innocent** smile, whether the dam would ever break. \"Not while we keep checking,\" Ms. Hu replied."},"86":{"w":["institute","institution","instruct","instruction","instrument","insufficient","insult","insurance","insure","integrate","intellectual","intelligence","intelligent","intend","intense","intensity","intensive","intention","intentional","interaction"],"title":"The Music Institute","t":"Mr. Pan is the director of a small music **institute** in the old part of our city. The **institution** was founded fifty years ago to teach traditional Chinese music, and it still offers classes in more than twenty **instruments|instrument**, from the erhu to the bamboo flute.\n\nMr. Pan believes that learning music develops both emotional and **intellectual** skills. \"Music is a form of **intelligence**,\" he says. \"An **intelligent** musician does not just play notes but understands their meaning.\" He likes to **instruct** students in small groups, giving clear **instructions|instruction** and plenty of time to practise.\n\nThe institute faces many challenges. Its funding is **insufficient**, and the building needs repairs. Last year Mr. Pan had to pay for **insurance** out of his own pocket to **insure** the valuable old instruments. When some officials suggested that the institute be closed, he considered it an **insult** to its history.\n\nInstead of giving up, he decided to **integrate** modern teaching methods into the programme. He started **intensive** summer courses for children and online lessons for adults, and he encouraged more **interaction** between students and local musicians by inviting performers to give workshops. The **intensity** of the summer course surprised many parents: children practised for six hours a day and loved it.\n\nMr. Pan's **intention** is not to turn every student into a professional. \"I **intend** to give them a lifelong love of music,\" he says. He admits that his decision to keep fees low is **intentional**, even though it makes life difficult. When asked about the **intense** pressure he faces, he simply smiles and plays a short tune on the flute instead of answering."},"87":{"w":["interest","interfere","interference","interior","intermediate","intern","internal","international","internet","interpret","interpretation","interpreter","interrupt","interruption","interval","interview","intimate","intimidate","introduce","introduction"],"title":"The First Interpretation","t":"When Zhang Rui was an **intern** at an **international** trade company, she was asked to act as **interpreter** at a meeting between Chinese and Spanish business partners. She had studied Spanish for five years and had reached an **intermediate** level, but she had never interpreted at a real meeting before.\n\nThe meeting began badly. One of the Spanish managers spoke very fast and kept making jokes that were difficult to **interpret**. When Zhang Rui asked him to repeat a sentence, he seemed annoyed by the **interruption**. Later a Chinese manager began to **interrupt** her translation to add his own comments, causing further **interference** in the conversation.\n\nDuring a short **interval**, Zhang Rui went outside to calm down. She searched the **internet** for some of the technical terms she had not understood and wrote them in her notebook. When she returned, she spoke more confidently. She admitted that her **interpretation** of a joke might not be perfect, and the Spanish manager laughed and said that her honesty was refreshing.\n\nThe meeting concerned the **interior** design of a new hotel, and the two companies had very different ideas. Zhang Rui noticed that the Chinese side was trying to **intimidate** the Spanish partners by quoting strict **internal** company rules. She did not **interfere** in the negotiation, but she translated carefully and politely, avoiding anything that might sound aggressive.\n\nBy the end of the day, the two sides had reached an agreement, and the Spanish manager thanked her with a surprisingly **intimate** gesture, holding her hand in both of his. A month later Zhang Rui was offered a full-time job. She says the experience taught her more than any textbook **introduction** to business. Her advice to other students is simple: **introduce** yourself with confidence, show genuine **interest** in people, and prepare for every **interview** as if it were the most important day of your life."},"88":{"w":["invade","invasion","invent","invention","inventor","inverse","invest","investigate","investigation","investment","invisible","invitation","invite","involve","inward","iron","irregular","island","isolate","issue"],"title":"The Island Inventor","t":"On a small **island** off the southern coast lives an **inventor** named Mr. Chen. He has spent forty years working in a workshop made of corrugated **iron**, and his **inventions|invention** range from solar-powered fishing boats to water filters that cost less than a meal.\n\nMr. Chen grew up in poverty. The island was **isolated|isolate** from the mainland, with an **irregular** ferry service that sometimes did not run for weeks, and clean water was its biggest **issue**. As a teenager he decided to **invent** a simple device that could turn seawater into drinking water using sunlight.\n\nHis first attempts failed. But he refused to give up, and eventually a university professor heard about his work and offered to **investigate** it. The **investigation** showed that his design was surprisingly efficient. A large company then offered to **invest** in the project, and the **investment** allowed him to produce thousands of filters.\n\nSome of his ideas are unusual. One invention works on an **inverse** principle: instead of heating water to purify it, it cools the air until moisture forms. Another uses an almost **invisible** net to collect water from sea fog. He says every invention begins with an **inward** question: what do people really need?\n\nThe island has not always been peaceful. Centuries ago, pirates would **invade** the coastline, and old songs still recall the terror of each **invasion**. Today visitors come for very different reasons. Mr. Chen often receives an **invitation** to speak at universities, and he likes to **invite** students to his workshop. Real innovation, he tells them, must **involve** local communities, not just engineers."},"89":{"w":["item","jacket","jail","jam","jar","jaw","jazz","jealous","jeans","jet","jewel","joint","joke","jolly","journal","journalist","journey","joy","joyful","judge"],"title":"The Jazz Journalist","t":"My aunt has worked as a **journalist** for thirty years, but her real passion is **jazz**. She writes a monthly column about music for a cultural **journal**, and she keeps a **jar** of old concert tickets on her desk, each one the memory of a different night.\n\nHer love of jazz began in the 1980s, when she heard a recording on a friend's radio. \"It was pure **joy**,\" she says. \"I had never heard anything so free.\" She started travelling to concerts, and each **journey** became an adventure. Once she took a **jet** to New Orleans with nothing but a small bag and a denim **jacket**, determined to hear the best musicians in the world.\n\nShe is a **jolly**, warm woman who loves to tell a **joke**, but she can also be a strict **judge** of music, with no patience for performers who are lazy or arrogant. Some musicians are **jealous** of her influence, since a good review in her column can bring a new band great success.\n\nShe also has a soft spot for unknown musicians. Last year she organised a **joint** concert for young performers in a small bar. The room was so crowded that people were **jammed|jam** against the walls, and some sat on the floor in torn **jeans**. A young saxophonist played so beautifully that **jaws|jaw** dropped all around the room.\n\nMy aunt's most precious **item** is a small silver **jewel** given to her by a famous singer to thank her for an honest review; she says it reminds her that criticism and kindness can go together. Her strangest memory, she jokes, is a night spent in a foreign **jail** after she lost her passport, though the police treated her kindly. \"Even that was **joyful** in its own way,\" she laughs. \"I wrote a column about it.\""},"90":{"w":["jungle","junior","jury","justice","justify","keen","keeper","kettle","keyboard","kick","kid","kill","kilogram","kilometer","kindness","king","kingdom","kiss","kite","knee"],"title":"The Jungle Keeper","t":"For fifteen years, Mr. Liang has worked as a **keeper** at a wildlife park in the south, where animals live in a large protected area of **jungle**. He is responsible for the orangutans, and he has a **keen** understanding of their behaviour; a single glance tells him whether an animal is happy, bored or unwell.\n\nHe began as a **junior** assistant, carrying buckets of fruit that sometimes weighed more than twenty **kilograms|kilogram**. Every morning he walks several **kilometers|kilometer** through the park, checking fences and feeding stations. He brings a small **kettle** to make tea during breaks and writes his daily reports on an old laptop with a worn **keyboard**.\n\nHis favourite animal is an orangutan named King, the oldest male in the group. Visitors call him the **king** of the **kingdom**, and he behaves like one, sitting on the highest branch and watching everyone below. Mr. Liang says King once pulled a young orangutan out of a pond, an act of **kindness** that astonished the staff.\n\nSometimes Mr. Liang has to deal with difficult situations. A visitor once threw stones at the animals, and Mr. Liang reported him to the police. The case went to court, and a **jury** decided that the man had broken the law. For Mr. Liang it was a matter of **justice**: animals cannot defend themselves, so people must. Some said the punishment was too harsh, but he found it easy to **justify**.\n\nChildren who visit the park love him. He explains how orangutans **kiss** each other's faces to show affection, and how they never **kill** for fun. Once a small **kid** asked whether he could **kick** a ball to King; Mr. Liang laughed and said King preferred bananas. At the end of each school visit the children fly a **kite** above the trees, and Mr. Liang kneels down on one **knee** to say goodbye to each of them."},"91":{"w":["kneel","knit","knob","knot","knowledge","lab","label","laboratory","lace","lack","ladder","lady","lag","lamb","lame","lamp","landing","landlady","landlord","lane"],"title":"The Landlady's Laboratory","t":"When I moved to the city for graduate school, I rented a room from an elderly **landlady** named Mrs. Gu. Her house stood at the end of a narrow **lane**, and its front door had a brass **knob** shaped like a lion's head. She lived alone, and on my first evening she welcomed me with tea and a plate of roast **lamb**.\n\nMrs. Gu had worked for forty years in a chemistry **laboratory**, and she still kept a small **lab** in her basement, full of glass bottles, each with a neat handwritten **label**. Her **knowledge** of chemistry was astonishing, but she was also a gentle **lady** who loved crafts. In the evenings she would **knit** sweaters for her grandchildren or tie decorative **knots|knot** from silk cord.\n\nThe house was old and full of small problems. The light on the stair **landing** often flickered, and the old **lamp** in my room worked only if I tapped it. The wooden **ladder** to the attic had a broken step. Whenever I suggested calling the **landlord**, she laughed: she was the owner herself, and she preferred to fix things on her own.\n\nMrs. Gu had been **lame** in one leg since an accident years ago, and she had to **kneel** carefully to reach the lower shelves. Yet she never seemed to **lack** energy. When I began to **lag** behind in my studies, she offered to help with my experiments and even sewed **lace** curtains for my window to cheer me up.\n\nWhen I finished my degree, she gave me one of her sweaters as a farewell gift. I still wear it every winter."},"92":{"w":["lantern","lap","largely","laser","lately","later","latin","latter","laughter","launch","laundry","lavatory","law","lawn","lawyer","lay","layer","layout","lazy","lead"],"title":"The Lawyer's Laundry","t":"My neighbour Mr. Hu is a **lawyer** who has worked in the city's courts for thirty years. Recently, however, he surprised everyone by opening a small **laundry** on the ground floor of our building. \"I spent my whole life studying the **law**,\" he said. \"Now I want to do something with my hands.\"\n\nHis shop has a clever **layout**: washing machines line one wall, folding tables stand in the middle, and a small reading corner by the window invites customers to sit while they wait. He even uses a **laser** thermometer to check the water temperature, a sign of his love for precise details. At the back there is a tiny **lavatory** with a sign in **Latin** that reads \"Patience is a virtue.\"\n\nMr. Hu is anything but **lazy**. He starts work at six and spends his evenings reading. Running the shop, he says, is **largely** a matter of listening to people. Customers often tell him their problems while they wait, and he gives free legal advice, though he insists he is no longer anyone's lawyer. **Lately** he has also begun teaching neighbours how to **lay** out clothes so that they dry without creases.\n\nEvery evening he sits by the door with a cushion on his **lap**, watching children play on the **lawn** outside. During the Spring Festival he hung a red **lantern** above the door, and the whole street filled with **laughter** when his cat tried to catch its tassel.\n\nHe plans to **launch** a second shop **later** this year. Of his two careers, law and laundry, he says the **latter** has taught him more about human nature. \"In court, you **lead** an argument,\" he says. \"Here, you wash away people's worries, one **layer** at a time.\""},"93":{"w":["leader","leadership","leading","leaf","leak","lean","leap","learned","learning","least","leather","lecture","legal","legend","legislation","leisure","lemon","length","lens","less"],"title":"A Lecture on Leadership","t":"Last month I attended a **lecture** on **leadership** given by a retired army general. He had been one of the **leading** figures in disaster relief for decades, and the hall was packed with students and young managers.\n\nHe began with a **legend** from his hometown. Long ago, a village **leader** saved his people from a flood by persuading them to **lean** on one another rather than flee in panic. \"A good leader,\" the general said, \"does not make people follow; he makes them believe.\" He explained that real **learning** comes from failure, and that even the most **learned** experts make mistakes.\n\nHe then described a mission in which his team had to cross a river after a dam began to **leak**. There was no bridge, so the soldiers had to **leap** from rock to rock. The **length** of the crossing was only twenty metres, but it took them an hour. \"The **least** experienced soldier went first,\" he said, \"because I wanted the others to see that courage has nothing to do with rank.\"\n\nHe also talked about the **legal** side of emergency work. New **legislation** now requires local governments to prepare disaster plans, and he had helped to draft some of the rules. But he warned that laws mean **less** than training and trust.\n\nAt the end he held up an old **leather** notebook and a camera **lens** he had carried for thirty years. \"These are my memories,\" he said. Since retiring, he has spent his **leisure** time photographing nature, especially the changing colour of each **leaf** in autumn. When someone asked for the secret of his long career, he laughed: \"A glass of hot water with **lemon** every morning.\""},"94":{"w":["lessen","lest","level","lever","leverage","liable","liar","liberal","liberate","liberation","liberty","librarian","license","lick","lid","lieutenant","lifetime","lift","lighten","lightly"],"title":"The Librarian","t":"For forty years Mrs. Zhou worked as the **librarian** of a small town library. She believed that access to books was a basic form of **liberty**, and she spent her whole **lifetime** fighting to keep the library open.\n\nThe library occupied a narrow old building whose heavy front door could only be opened with an iron **lever**. Every morning she would **lift** the lever, switch on the lights and **lighten** the dark reading room by opening the shutters. She moved **lightly** between the shelves, almost silently, and children used to say she was part of the building.\n\nIn the 1990s the town council wanted to close the library to save money. Mrs. Zhou used every bit of **leverage** she had, writing to newspapers and organising readers' meetings. She argued that the council would be **liable** for damaging the town's education, and that closing the library would hardly **lessen** its debts. Some officials called her a **liar** for exaggerating, but she had the figures to prove her point.\n\nShe was a **liberal** thinker who believed that education could **liberate** people from poverty, and she often described the **liberation** of her own mind when she first read novels as a girl. Yet she kept a strict **level** of order in the library: no food, no noise and no folded pages, **lest** the books be damaged.\n\nShe had a few eccentric habits. She would **lick** her finger before turning a page, and she kept her reading glasses in a box with a broken **lid**. A photo of her late husband, an army **lieutenant**, stood on her desk. When she retired, the town presented her with an honorary **license** to borrow books for the rest of her life."},"95":{"w":["lightning","likelihood","likely","likewise","limb","lime","limit","limitation","limited","line","linen","liner","link","lion","lip","liquid","liquor","list","listener","liter"],"title":"A Storm at Sea","t":"Last summer my grandparents celebrated their fiftieth wedding anniversary with a trip on an ocean **liner** across the Mediterranean. They had always dreamed of sailing, and my grandfather had kept a **list** of places he wanted to see since he was a young man.\n\nOn the second night a storm broke over the sea. **Lightning** lit up the sky, and the ship rocked so much that glasses slid across the tables. The captain spoke calmly to every **listener** on board through the loudspeakers, explaining that the **likelihood** of real danger was very low. \"We are **likely** to reach port by morning,\" he said.\n\nMy grandmother was not convinced. She held my grandfather's hand tightly and could barely move a **limb**. He tried to comfort her by talking about the past, reminding her of their honeymoon by the sea fifty years earlier, and she slowly relaxed. **Likewise**, other passengers shared stories to distract one another.\n\nThe ship had strict rules. Passengers could buy only a **limited** amount of **liquor** at the bar, and during the storm each person was given one **liter** of drinking water. The crew explained that every rule had a reason, though my grandfather grumbled about the **limitation**. \"There is a **limit** to how much safety a man can bear,\" he joked.\n\nBy morning the storm had passed. The crew served breakfast on clean white **linen**, with fresh juice and slices of **lime**. A passenger at the next table showed my grandmother a small carved **lion** he had bought in Athens, and she laughed for the first time since the storm. My grandfather said the trip was the perfect **link** between their past and their future. Standing in **line** to leave the ship, my grandmother kissed him on the **lips|lip** and said she would gladly do it all again. Behind them, the sea lay as smooth as **liquid** glass."},"96":{"w":["literary","literature","lively","liver","livestock","living","living-room","load","loaf","loan","local","locate","location","lock","locomotive","lodge","log","logic","logical","lonely"],"title":"The Mountain Lodge","t":"Every winter my uncle runs a small wooden **lodge** in the mountains, where travellers stop to rest on their way to a famous ski resort. The **location** is beautiful but remote: the nearest **local** village is fifteen kilometres away. He bought the building with a bank **loan** ten years ago, and he has worked hard to pay it back.\n\nThe lodge is simple. The **living-room** has a stone fireplace, with a tall pile of **logs|log** beside it to burn through the night. Life there can be **lonely**, especially in the quiet weeks before Christmas. To keep his mind busy, my uncle reads **literature** of every kind, from Chinese poetry to Russian novels. He belongs to a **literary** club that meets online, and he loves to argue about the **logic** of plots. \"A good story must be **logical**,\" he says, \"even when it is magical.\"\n\nHe also keeps a little **livestock**: three goats and a few chickens. Every morning he carries a **load** of hay to the goats and collects the eggs, and he bakes a fresh **loaf** of bread each day. Guests often ask for his recipe.\n\nThe lodge even has its own tiny museum: a toy **locomotive** from the old mountain railway, a rusty **lock** from the original front door and old maps that help visitors **locate** forgotten hiking trails. My uncle earns only a modest **living**, but he is content, and he says the **lively** conversations around the fire are worth more than money. His only worry is his health; his doctor has warned him that too much rich food is bad for his **liver**."},"97":{"w":["loop","loose","loosen","lord","lorry","lose","loss","loud","loudspeaker","lover","lower","loyal","loyalty","lubricate","luck","lucky","luggage","lumber","lump","lunar"],"title":"The Lost Suitcase","t":"Last spring I flew to Hangzhou for a conference and arrived to find that my **luggage** had not. I stood at the empty baggage belt for half an hour, listening to the **loudspeaker** repeat the same message in three languages. I was tired and angry, and I felt the trip had started with bad **luck**.\n\nThe airline staff were polite but not very helpful. They said my suitcase had been sent to another city by mistake and would be delivered by **lorry** the next day. I asked them to **lower** my anxiety by giving me a clear time, but they could only promise \"as soon as possible\". I tried hard not to **lose** my temper.\n\nFortunately, a **loyal** friend who lived nearby came to pick me up. She lent me clothes, a toothbrush and a **loose** jacket that was far too big. That evening we went to a festival in the old town to celebrate the **lunar** new year. There were music performances and food stalls, and dancers moved in a huge **loop** around the square while **loud** drums made my chest shake.\n\nAt one stall, an old man was selling wooden toys made from **lumber** he had collected from fallen trees. He used a small brush to **lubricate** the moving parts with oil and showed me how each toy worked. He told me that his grandfather had been the **lord** of a large estate before the war, but that the family had lost everything. \"Every **loss** teaches you something,\" he said.\n\nMy suitcase arrived two days later with a large **lump** of dried mud on one side. I didn't care. I felt **lucky** to have such a friend and to have met a man whose **loyalty** to his craft was so touching, and I left Hangzhou a true **lover** of its old streets. On the plane home, I promised myself to **loosen** my grip on the things I cannot control."},"98":{"w":["lung","luxury","mad","madam","magazine","magic","magnet","magnetic","magnificent","maid","main","mainland","mainly","maintain","maintenance","major","majority","male","manage","management"],"title":"Old Hotels on the Mainland","t":"When my friend Mia, who grew up on a small island, visited the **mainland** for the first time, she said the landscape was **magnificent**. Mountains rose like walls, rivers ran for thousands of kilometres, and the cities glowed at night like **magic**.\n\nShe had come to China to study hotel **management**, and her **major** project was a report on the **maintenance** of historic buildings. She visited dozens of old hotels and guesthouses, interviewing managers about how they **maintain** wooden floors, antique furniture and decorative tiles. She learned that the **main** problem is not money but skill: the **majority** of young workers no longer know traditional methods.\n\nOne hotel she visited was a former **luxury** villa built in the 1920s. Its owner, an elderly man who used to work as a **magazine** editor, showed her around and introduced her to the hotel's oldest employee, a **maid** who had worked there for fifty years. \"Good afternoon, **madam**,\" the woman said to Mia with a smile. She was **mainly** responsible for the guest rooms and could still **manage** a whole floor alone.\n\nThe owner also had unusual hobbies. He collected antique compasses and showed Mia how a **magnet** could make a needle point north. He explained that the building's old lift used a **magnetic** brake that still worked perfectly. Many visitors thought he was **mad** to keep such old machines, he said, but he loved them.\n\nMia's report concluded that historic buildings are like the **lungs|lung** of a city: they let it breathe. She noticed that **male** and female staff alike took great pride in the history of the places where they worked, and her professor praised the report highly."},"99":{"w":["mankind","manly","manner","manual","manufacture","manufacturer","marble","margin","marine","mark","marriage","married","marry","marvelous","mask","mass","massive","master","masterpiece","mat"],"title":"The Marble Sculptor","t":"In a small workshop near the coast, an old sculptor named Mr. Bao has spent forty years working with **marble**. He is a **master** of his craft, and his most famous **masterpiece**, a statue of a fisherman's wife waiting by the sea, stands in the centre of the town square.\n\nMr. Bao learned the trade as a teenager, working for a **manufacturer** of gravestones. The work was **manual** and exhausting, but he loved the feel of stone under his hands. Later he decided to **manufacture** his own designs, and his workshop became known for its **marvelous** sculptures of **marine** life: dolphins, turtles and breaking waves.\n\nHis wife was the first person to believe in him. They were **married** at twenty, and he says their **marriage** has been the foundation of his career. \"Whenever I wanted to give up, she reminded me why I started,\" he says. \"I would **marry** her again tomorrow.\"\n\nHe has a gentle **manner** and teaches young artists patiently. He tells them that the **mass** of a stone hides the shape inside it, and that the sculptor's job is to remove what is not needed. He insists that they wear a **mask** to protect their lungs from stone dust and leave a wide **margin** of safety when cutting, and he likes to **mark** each block with chalk before starting.\n\nSome critics call his style old-fashioned, but his work has a **massive** following. He believes that art should speak to all **mankind**, not just experts. Visitors often find him resting on a straw **mat** outside the workshop, sipping tea. A young apprentice once asked him what makes a **manly** man. Mr. Bao laughed: \"Patience, kindness and strong hands.\""},"100":{"w":["mate","material","materialism","mathematical","mathematics","maths","mature","maximum","mayor","meadow","means","meantime","meanwhile","measurable","measure","measurement","mechanic","mechanical","mechanically","medal"],"title":"Saving the Meadow","t":"When the **mayor** of our town announced a plan to build a car park on the old **meadow** behind the school, many residents were upset. The meadow had been a place for picnics and football for generations, and children went there to learn about wildflowers and insects. A group of parents decided to oppose the plan by every legal **means** they could find.\n\nTheir leader was a retired **mechanic** named Mr. Xu, a practical man who believed in facts. He began by taking careful **measurements|measurement** of the meadow and calculating the **maximum** number of parking spaces the town really needed. His **mathematical** analysis showed that the existing car park was only half full most days. \"The problem is **measurable**,\" he said. \"We don't need a new one.\"\n\n**Meanwhile**, a local teacher began holding lessons in the meadow. Her **maths** classes used the grass and flowers to teach geometry, and her students collected **material** for a science project on biodiversity. Even university students joined in, using advanced **mathematics** to model how the meadow absorbed rainwater.\n\nThe campaign was not about rejecting progress. Mr. Xu explained that he was not against cars, only against **materialism**, the idea that every space must make money. The mayor was not a bad man, he said, just one who thought too **mechanically**, seeing the town as a **mechanical** system rather than a community.\n\nIn the **meantime**, the mayor met the residents several times. In a **mature** and open discussion, he admitted that he had not considered all the facts. He cancelled the plan and took a formal **measure** to protect the meadow as a public park. The school later gave Mr. Xu a small **medal** for his work, which he keeps next to a photo of his old army **mate**."},"101":{"w":["media","medical","medium","melon","melt","memorial","memory","mental","merchant","mercury","mercy","mere","merely","merit","merry","mess","message","messenger","metal","method"],"title":"The Merchant's Memorial","t":"In the centre of our old market town stands a **memorial** to a **merchant** named Lu, who lived three hundred years ago. According to local history, he was no **mere** trader. He used his wealth to build schools and hospitals, and he was famous for showing **mercy** to farmers who could not repay their debts.\n\nToday the memorial is a favourite meeting place, and every year the town holds a **merry** festival in Lu's honour. Stalls sell sweet **melons|melon**, cakes and small **metal** souvenirs shaped like his old trading boat. Local **media** cover the event, and by the end of the day the square is a cheerful **mess** of coloured paper and music.\n\nLast year I interviewed the town historian, an elderly woman with an extraordinary **memory**. She explained that Lu's **method** of doing business was unusual for his time. He believed that a merchant's **merit** should be measured not **merely** by profit but by the good he did, and every month he sent a **messenger** to distant villages with a **message** offering fair prices for their crops.\n\nShe also told me a surprising detail. In old age Lu suffered from a **mental** illness, and he was treated by a doctor who used **mercury**, a common but dangerous **medical** practice at the time. \"His mind seemed to **melt** away slowly,\" she said sadly.\n\nDespite this, the town remembers him with affection. The historian says his story is a **medium** through which each generation learns about kindness and responsibility. \"He was not perfect,\" she said, \"but he chose generosity.\""},"102":{"w":["metric","microcomputer","microphone","microscope","midday","midnight","midst","might","mild","mile","military","mill","millimetre","miner","minimum","minister","ministry","minor","minority","minus"],"title":"The Miners' Museum","t":"In a small town in the north, an old coal **mill** and the buildings around it have been turned into a museum about mining. The project was supported by the **ministry** of culture, and the **minister** herself came to open it last year. In her speech she praised the courage of every **miner** who had worked there.\n\nThe museum is full of objects that tell the story of hard lives. One room displays tools, from heavy shovels to a delicate **microscope** once used to examine rock samples. Another shows how engineers used the **metric** system to measure tunnels to the nearest **millimetre**, and a large map marks every **mile** of tunnel beneath the town.\n\nA former miner, Mr. Yang, works as a guide. He describes how the men began work at **midnight** and finished at **midday**, rarely seeing sunlight in winter, and how in the **midst** of the darkness they relied completely on each other. Speaking into a **microphone** at the opening ceremony, he admitted that he had been afraid every single day. \"Even the strongest man has only so much **might**,\" he said.\n\nThe museum also explains the town's history during the war, when the mine was taken over by the **military**. A small **minority** of workers refused to cooperate and were punished. Mr. Yang says such stories should never be forgotten, even if they seem like **minor** details today.\n\nThe museum's budget is tight; it receives only the **minimum** funding needed to stay open. Visitors pay a small fee, and a **microcomputer** at the entrance counts each person. Last winter the temperature fell to **minus** twenty degrees, yet people still came, saying the weather was **mild** compared with what the miners had once endured."},"103":{"w":["miracle","mirror","miserable","mislead","missile","missing","mission","mist","mistress","misunderstand","mix","mixture","moan","mobile","mode","model","moderate","modest","modify","moist"],"title":"Stories by the Lake","t":"In a small town by a lake, the morning **mist** often hides the water completely, and local people say strange things happen on such days. One old story tells of a **missing** fisherman who reappeared after three days, claiming he had followed his own reflection across a **mirror** of still water. People called his return a **miracle**.\n\nMy grandmother, the **mistress** of a large old house near the lake, loved telling this story. She was a **modest** woman who never boasted, but she had a gift for storytelling. She could **mix** fact and legend so skilfully that we never knew where one ended and the other began, and her tales were a **mixture** of history, humour and gentle warnings.\n\nShe also taught us practical lessons. She warned us not to **misunderstand** people because of their appearance, and never to **mislead** others for our own benefit. \"A lie is like a **missile**,\" she said. \"Once you fire it, you cannot call it back.\" Whenever we began to **moan** about small problems, she reminded us how **miserable** life had been during the years of famine.\n\nHer house was cold and **moist** in winter, and she refused to **modify** it with modern heating. Her one concession to the modern world was a **mobile** phone that she kept in a drawer and rarely used; she preferred a slower **mode** of communication, the handwritten letter.\n\nShe lived a **moderate** life, eating simple food and walking every morning by the lake. She was a **model** for all of us, and her **mission** in life, she once said, was to make sure that no child in the family grew up without stories."},"104":{"w":["moisture","molecule","monitor","monkey","monthly","monument","mood","moral","moreover","mortal","mosquito","mostly","motel","motion","motivate","motive","motor","mould","mount","mourn"],"title":"The Desert Motel","t":"Last summer, during a long road trip, my family stopped for the night at a small **motel** beside a highway in the desert. The building was old, and the walls showed patches of **mould** caused by **moisture** from a leaking pipe. My mother was not impressed, but it was the only place to stay for fifty kilometres.\n\nThe owner, an old man with a kind face, welcomed us warmly. He said he had run the motel for forty years and that business was **mostly** quiet except during holidays. He kept a **monthly** record of every guest in a thick notebook and checked bookings on an ancient computer **monitor**. **Moreover**, he insisted on carrying our bags himself, even though he was nearly eighty.\n\nThat evening the **mood** was relaxed. We sat outside watching a neighbour's pet **monkey** play on the fence until the **mosquito** bites drove us indoors. There the owner told us about the area's history. Not far away stands a **monument** to the workers who built the highway; many died in accidents, and he still goes there every spring to **mourn** them. \"We are all **mortal**,\" he said quietly.\n\nHe also had a passion for science. He showed us a model of a water **molecule** he had made from clay and explained how the desert air pulls moisture from the ground. Every **motion** in nature has a reason, he said, just as every person has a **motive** for what they do. \"My motive is simple: I want travellers to feel at home.\"\n\nThe next morning he helped my father fix a problem with the car's **motor**. Before we left, he asked us to **mount** a small sign promoting the motel on our back window, and my father agreed, laughing. The old man had managed to **motivate** him to keep driving with a smile, he said, and the **moral** of the evening was worth more than any five-star hotel."},"105":{"w":["mouse","mouthful","movement","movie","mud","muddy","mug","multiple","multiply","murder","murderer","muscle","museum","mushroom","musical","musician","mute","mutter","mutton","mutual"],"title":"The Musical Mystery","t":"Last winter our university's drama club staged a **musical** based on a famous **murder** mystery. The story is set in a small **museum**, where a valuable painting disappears and the night guard is found dead. Every member of the cast is a suspect, and the audience must guess who the **murderer** is.\n\nThe production was ambitious. A young **musician** wrote the score, mixing jazz with traditional Chinese instruments. The songs were clever and full of humour, and one of them, about a detective searching for clues in the **mud** outside the museum, became an instant favourite. The stage was covered in fake **muddy** footprints, which the cleaners complained about for weeks.\n\nThe actors worked hard. The lead actor built up his **muscles|muscle** for a fight scene and practised his dance **movements|movement** every night. Another actor had to play a **mute** character who could only **mutter** strange sounds, which proved surprisingly difficult. During one rehearsal, an actress dropped her coffee **mug** in shock when the lights went out suddenly.\n\nThe director said the play had **multiple** endings, depending on the audience's vote. He believed that theatre should create **mutual** trust between actors and audience. \"We **multiply** the possibilities,\" he explained, \"and let the people decide.\"\n\nThe opening night was a huge success. Afterwards, the cast celebrated with a dinner of roast **mutton** and **mushroom** soup, and one student said it felt like being in a **movie**. The only surprise came when a real **mouse** ran across the stage during the final scene, and the audience burst into laughter. The director said it was the best twist of all, and I took a **mouthful** of soup and agreed."},"106":{"w":["mysterious","mystery","nail","naked","namely","nap","narrow","nasty","nation","national","nationality","native","natural","naturally","nature","naughty","naval","navigation","navy","nearby"],"title":"The Navigator","t":"My grandfather served in the **navy** for twenty years as an expert in **navigation**. He could guide a ship across the open sea using only the stars, and he loved to tell us that the night sky was the greatest **mystery** of all. Even with the **naked** eye, he said, a sailor could read the sky like a map.\n\nHe was a **native** of a small fishing village, where his father taught him to tie knots and to mend nets with a long iron **nail**. As a boy he was **naughty** and adventurous, always sneaking down to the harbour, so, **naturally**, he went on to join the **naval** academy.\n\nHis stories were full of **mysterious** events: a strange light seen over the water at midnight, a ship that appeared through a **narrow** channel in the fog and vanished again. He said there was always a **natural** explanation, but he enjoyed keeping us guessing. \"**Nature** has its own sense of humour,\" he liked to say.\n\nHe was proud of his **nation** but never boastful, believing that **national** pride should be quiet and respectful. On his ships he worked with sailors of every **nationality**, and he said that the sea treats everyone equally. Some captains were **nasty** and arrogant, he admitted, but most were simply tired men far from home.\n\nIn old age he lived in a small house **nearby**, and every afternoon he took a short **nap** in his armchair. On the wall he kept a list of three rules, **namely**: be honest, be patient and always know where you are going."},"107":{"w":["neat","necessarily","necessity","neck","necklace","needle","needless","negative","neglect","neighborhood","nephew","nerve","nervous","nest","net","network","neutral","nevertheless","newly","niece"],"title":"A Neighbourhood Network","t":"When my aunt moved into a **newly** built apartment block in the city, she felt lonely. She knew nobody in the **neighborhood**, and most residents seemed too busy to talk. Like many people, she had a rather **negative** view of modern city life.\n\nThen she had an idea. She started a small online **network** for residents to share information, from lost keys to recipes. At first only a few people joined, but soon it had hundreds of members, and people began offering to help each other, lend tools and look after pets. \"It wasn't a **necessity**,\" she said, \"but it turned out to be a gift.\"\n\nShe also organised a weekly craft club in the community room. Her **nephew** taught children to knit with a large **needle**, while her **niece** helped older women design a beaded **necklace** for a charity sale. Even shy residents, who were too **nervous** to speak at first, gradually relaxed, and an old man who had once been a tailor showed everyone how to make **neat** stitches.\n\nNot everything went smoothly. Some residents complained that the club was **needless** and noisy, and a few accused my aunt of trying to control the building. She stayed **neutral** in every argument and refused to take sides. \"You need a lot of **nerve** to start something like this,\" she admitted, \"but it's worse to **neglect** your neighbours.\"\n\n**Nevertheless**, the project succeeded. Residents built a bird **nest** box for the courtyard tree and put up a **net** to protect the garden from pigeons. One evening, after a long meeting, my aunt rubbed her stiff **neck** and smiled. A good community, she said, does not **necessarily** need money, only people willing to care."},"108":{"w":["nineteen","nitrogen","noble","nod","noisy","nonsense","normal","normally","northeast","northern","northwest","notebook","notice","noticeable","notify","notion","noun","novel","nowadays","nowhere"],"title":"A Novelist from the North","t":"At **nineteen**, Li Na left her small village in a **northern** province and moved to the capital to study literature. She carried a single bag and an old **notebook** in which she had written dozens of stories. Her dream was to publish a **novel**, though her parents considered the idea **nonsense**.\n\nShe soon discovered that city life was **noisy** and exhausting. Her rented room was in the **northwest** of the city, an hour from campus, and she had **nowhere** quiet to write. She worked part-time in a café and would often **nod** off on the bus. Yet she refused to give up. Every evening she filled her notebook with observations about the people she met.\n\nHer teachers began to **notice** her talent. One professor said her stories showed a **noticeable** improvement each term. He encouraged her to study grammar carefully, even simple things like the difference between a **noun** and a verb, and to read widely, and he introduced her to the **notion** that a writer must be both a dreamer and a careful observer.\n\nHer first novel, about a farming family in the **northeast**, was published when she was twenty-five. It told a quiet story about **noble** people living ordinary lives. Critics praised its honesty, and it soon became a bestseller. When the publisher called to **notify** her of its success, she cried.\n\n**Nowadays** Li Na lives a fairly **normal** life. She **normally** writes in the morning and teaches in the afternoon, and she still keeps her old notebook, stained with coffee and tea. In one lecture she explained that stories are like plants that need **nitrogen**: they grow from small, invisible nutrients found in daily life."},"109":{"w":["nuclear","nucleus","nuisance","numerous","nursery","nut","nylon","obey","object","objection","objective","obligation","oblige","obscure","observation","observe","observer","obstacle","obtain","obvious"],"title":"From Atoms to Seeds","t":"Dr. Wang spent thirty years as a **nuclear** physicist, studying the **nucleus** of the atom. When she retired, she surprised everyone by opening a plant **nursery** on the edge of the city. \"Atoms and seeds are not so different,\" she joked. \"Both hide enormous energy inside.\"\n\nThe move was not easy. Running a nursery involves **numerous** practical tasks she had never done before. She had to **obtain** permits, learn about soil and protect young trees from insects, which quickly became a **nuisance**. She also discovered that customers often **object** to high prices, and she had to explain why organically grown plants cost more.\n\nHer scientific training helped. She kept a careful record of every **observation**, measuring growth rates and soil moisture, and she believed that a good gardener must **observe** patiently, like a careful **observer** in a laboratory. She also insisted on an **objective** approach: if a method did not work, she changed it, regardless of her feelings.\n\nShe faced one major **obstacle**. A neighbour raised an **objection** to her greenhouse, claiming that it blocked his view. The local council ruled that she was under no legal **obligation** to remove it, but she decided to **oblige** him by moving it a few metres, and the two became friends.\n\nToday the nursery is popular with families, and children love the **nut** trees and tall sunflowers. Dr. Wang still wears her old **nylon** lab coat while she works. The most **obvious** lesson of her second career, she says, is that even the most **obscure** plants deserve attention. \"You must **obey** nature's rules,\" she says, \"or nothing grows.\""},"110":{"w":["obviously","occasion","occasional","occasionally","occupation","occupy","occur","occurrence","ocean","odd","offend","offer","officer","official","oil","okay","omit","oneself","onion","onto"],"title":"An Odd Occupation","t":"My uncle has an **odd** **occupation**: he is an onion farmer who became an ocean guide. For twenty years he grew **onions|onion** on a small farm by the coast, and only **occasionally** did he take tourists out on his boat to watch whales. Over time, the boat trips became more popular than the vegetables.\n\n**Obviously**, the change was not planned. It began on a special **occasion**, his fiftieth birthday, when a group of friends asked him to take them out to sea. During the trip a whale surfaced beside the boat, and one of the guests posted a video online. Soon strangers were calling to **offer** him money for similar trips.\n\nHe now runs **occasional** tours for families and students. He teaches them about the **ocean**, explains how whales communicate and warns them never to throw rubbish into the water. He takes care not to **offend** the local fishermen, who were at first suspicious of tourists **occupying|occupy** the harbour.\n\nNot every day goes smoothly. Once a fuel leak spread a film of **oil** across the water, and a coastguard **officer** came to investigate. My uncle cooperated fully and helped with the clean-up, and the **official** report praised his quick action. Such an **occurrence** is rare, he says, but accidents can **occur** to anyone.\n\nHe refuses to **omit** a single safety rule, even when customers complain. Before every trip he asks each passenger to put on a life jacket and climb **onto** the boat carefully. \"Is everyone **okay**?\" he shouts before leaving the harbour. The most important skill in his job, he says, is to forget **oneself** and focus on the safety of others."},"111":{"w":["opening","opera","operate","operation","operational","operator","opinion","opponent","opportunity","oppose","opposite","oppress","optical","optics","optimistic","option","optional","oral","orbit","orchestra"],"title":"Opening Night at the Opera","t":"When our city's new opera house held its **opening** night last spring, tickets sold out within minutes. The first performance was a classic Italian **opera**, accompanied by a full **orchestra** of eighty musicians, and for many people in the audience it was their first **opportunity** to see live opera.\n\nBehind the scenes, the evening was a complex **operation**. A team of technicians had to **operate** dozens of lights, speakers and cameras. The lighting **operator**, a young woman named Su Lan, sat in a small booth above the stage, controlling every effect. She had studied **optics** at university, and she used her knowledge of **optical** systems to design the lighting.\n\nNot everyone had supported the project. Some residents had tried to **oppose** it, arguing that the city should spend the money on schools instead, and one local politician, a long-time **opponent** of the mayor, called the opera house a luxury. Others felt that culture should not be **optional** and that everyone deserved access to music. People on **opposite** sides of the debate shared their **opinions|opinion** on television and online.\n\nThe building itself is remarkable. Its glass dome looks like a planet in **orbit**, and its main hall can be divided into smaller rooms. The managers say the hall became fully **operational** only a week before the opening, after months of testing.\n\nThe performance was a great success, and even critics who had been doubtful left feeling **optimistic**. In her **oral** introduction before the show, the director reminded the audience that throughout history art has sometimes been used to **oppress** ordinary people, and that this hall would belong to everyone. \"Tonight,\" she said, \"every **option** is open to you.\""},"112":{"w":["orderly","ordinary","ore","organ","organic","organism","organization","organize","orient","oriental","origin","original","originate","ornament","orphan","otherwise","ought","ounce","outcome","outdoor"],"title":"The Orphan's Ornaments","t":"As an **orphan**, Mei grew up in a children's home in the countryside. Her life was **ordinary** in many ways: school, chores and quiet evenings. But she had one **original** talent. She could make beautiful **ornaments|ornament** from almost anything: pieces of wood, old buttons, even lumps of iron **ore** she found near an abandoned mine.\n\nThe director of the home encouraged her and helped her **organize** a small stall at the local market, where she sold her decorations. The **outcome** was surprising. Tourists loved her work, especially pieces with **oriental** designs inspired by old Chinese paintings, and within a year a local craft **organization** invited her to teach workshops.\n\nMei believes that creativity must **originate** in observation. She spends many hours on **outdoor** walks, studying leaves, insects and every living **organism** she can find. She also grows **organic** vegetables in a small garden, which she says keeps her mind calm.\n\nHer workshop is small but **orderly**. Every tool has its place, and every bag of beads is weighed to the nearest **ounce**. An old wooden **organ** stands in the corner, and she plays it when she needs inspiration. Her students say the music helps them **orient** themselves when they feel lost.\n\nMei rarely talks about her **origin**. When asked, she says only that she **ought** to be grateful to the people who helped her. \"**Otherwise**,\" she says, \"I might never have discovered what my hands could do.\""},"113":{"w":["outdoors","outer","outlet","outline","outlook","output","outset","outskirt","outstanding","outward","oval","oven","overall","overcoat","overcome","overdue","overhead","overlook","overnight","overseas"],"title":"A Volunteer Overseas","t":"Two years ago my cousin left her job in Shanghai to work as an **overseas** volunteer at a school on the **outskirts|outskirt** of a city in Kenya. From the **outset** she knew it would be challenging. She arrived after an **overnight** flight, exhausted, wearing a heavy **overcoat** that was completely unsuitable for the African heat.\n\nHer job was to help the school improve its reading programme. She began by preparing an **outline** of the lessons and a plan to increase the school's **output** of trained teachers. The **overall** goal was to make the school self-sufficient within three years.\n\nShe faced many obstacles. The school's only **oven**, used to cook lunch for two hundred children, was broken, and repairs were long **overdue**. Electricity was unreliable, and the roof leaked whenever it rained. Yet she learned to **overcome** each problem with patience, and she tried never to **overlook** small details, because small problems quickly became big ones.\n\nHer **outlook** on life changed completely. She learned to enjoy simple things: lessons **outdoors** under a tree, the sound of children singing, the **oval** shape of the sun sinking over the hills. She discovered that **outward** appearances matter little; what counts is kindness.\n\nThe school now has its own small library, and its **outstanding** students have won national prizes. My cousin has returned home, but she keeps in touch. Before leaving, she helped the school open a small craft **outlet** in town to sell the students' artwork, which helps cover **overhead** costs such as electricity. \"The **outer** world thinks Africa is poor,\" she says. \"But I found a richness I had never seen before.\""},"114":{"w":["overtake","overtime","owe","owing","owl","owner","ownership","ox","oxygen","pace","pacific","pack","package","packet","pad","pail","pain","painful","paint","painter"],"title":"The Painter on the Cliff","t":"For thirty years Mr. Fu has lived in a small house on a cliff above the **Pacific** Ocean, where he works as a **painter**. Every morning he walks down to the beach at a slow **pace**, carrying a wooden **pail** of water, a sketch **pad** and a **packet** of brushes. He says the sea is his best teacher.\n\nMr. Fu did not always live such a quiet life. He once worked **overtime** every day at an advertising company in the city, painting posters and **package** designs for products he did not care about. \"I worked hard, but I felt as if I were running out of **oxygen**,\" he says. One day he decided to **pack** his things and leave.\n\nHe bought the house from its previous **owner**, an elderly farmer who kept an **ox** in the field behind it. The **ownership** papers were complicated, and Mr. Fu still **owes|owe** a small debt to the bank. But he has never regretted the move.\n\nHis life has not been without **pain**. A few years ago he broke his hand in a fall, and the recovery was long and **painful**. **Owing** to the injury, he could not **paint** for six months. During that time he sat on his porch at night, listening to an **owl** in the trees and planning new works in his head.\n\nHis paintings now hang in galleries around the world, and younger artists often ask him how to succeed. The secret, he says, is not talent but patience: \"Don't try to **overtake** anyone. Find your own rhythm, and let the work grow slowly.\""},"115":{"w":["painting","pale","palm","pan","panda","pane","panel","pant","pants","parade","paradise","paragraph","parallel","parcel","parliament","parlor","part","partial","partially","participate"],"title":"The Panda Parade","t":"Every year the city of Chengdu holds a festival in honour of the giant **panda**, and its highlight is a colourful **parade** through the streets. Hundreds of children dressed as pandas march alongside floats decorated with bamboo and flowers, and for visitors it feels like a **paradise** of colour and music.\n\nLast year my friend Ying was invited to **participate** as a volunteer. She had to help organise the children, check every **parcel** of costumes and make sure no one got lost. Her role was only **partial**; most of the planning had been done by a **panel** of artists, teachers and zoo staff.\n\nThe day began early. Ying had to **pant** her way up four flights of stairs to the costume room, where dozens of children were already waiting in their black-and-white **pants**. One small boy was so nervous that his face turned **pale**; she held his hand, and when the music started he forgot his fears.\n\nThe parade passed an old tea **parlor** that has served customers for a hundred years, and a street lined with shops selling **paintings|painting** of pandas. Ying noticed that the crowd moved in **parallel** with the parade, following it from street to street. Children waved, and adults clapped their **palms|palm** in time with the drums.\n\nAt the end of the day, a local official made a short speech praising the volunteers. He compared the event to a session of **parliament**, noisy, colourful and full of strong opinions, which drew polite laughter. Ying only **partially** heard it, because she was busy helping children find their parents. That night she wrote a **paragraph** about the day in her diary. The best **part**, she wrote, was seeing the shy boy smile through the **pane** of the bus window as he waved goodbye. Then she cooked a few dumplings in a small **pan** and fell asleep early."},"116":{"w":["particle","particular","particularly","partly","partner","passenger","passion","passive","paste","pastime","pat","patch","path","patience","patient","pattern","pause","pave","pavement","paw"],"title":"Grandmother's Garden","t":"My grandmother has a **particular** **passion** for gardening, and her favourite **pastime** is creating patterns with flowers. She plants red and white tulips in careful rows, and the **pattern** changes every year. The secret, she says, is **patience**: you cannot rush a seed.\n\nHer **partner** in this work is my grandfather, a retired doctor who once had thousands of patients. He likes to joke that the garden is his most demanding **patient**. Every morning he walks along the narrow stone **path** to check on the plants, stopping to **pat** the old cat that sleeps on the wall.\n\nThey are not **passive** gardeners. When heavy rain washed away part of the path, my grandfather decided to **pave** it himself with old bricks. He mixed a thick **paste** of cement and spent a week on the job. Now the path looks almost as smooth as a city **pavement**, though one corner still has a small **patch** of mud where the cat leaves a **paw** print every morning.\n\nMy grandmother is **particularly** proud of her roses. She knows the needs of every variety and can tell from the colour of a leaf whether a plant is sick. She once explained to me how tiny **particles|particle** of soil hold water and air, and how roots grow around them.\n\nSometimes she stops working and takes a long **pause**, looking at the garden in silence. She says the garden is **partly** a memory of her mother, who taught her to plant flowers when she was a child. Last spring a **passenger** on a passing tour bus asked to photograph the garden, and my grandmother proudly agreed."},"117":{"w":["payment","pea","peace","peaceful","peach","peak","pear","pearl","peasant","pebble","peculiar","peel","peep","penetrate","peninsula","penny","pension","pepper","perceive","percent"],"title":"Life on the Peninsula","t":"On a small **peninsula** in the south, there is a village where farmers have grown fruit for centuries. Each spring the hills turn white and pink with blossom, and by summer the trees are heavy with **peaches|peach** and **pears|pear**. Visitors describe the place as **peaceful**, a world away from the noise of the city.\n\nThe oldest **peasant** in the village is Mr. Huang, who is nearly ninety. He has spent his life in the fields and has never travelled more than fifty kilometres from home. He receives a small **pension** from the government, but he still works every day, picking fruit and planting **peas|pea** between the rows of trees. \"Work gives me **peace** of mind,\" he says.\n\nHe has a **peculiar** habit: every morning he climbs to the **peak** of the hill behind his house to watch the sunrise. He takes a **peep** at the sea through an old telescope and picks up a **pebble** from the path, which he adds to a jar in his kitchen. The jar is now full.\n\nMr. Huang is generous with visitors. He likes to **peel** an apple for each guest and sprinkle a little **pepper** on sliced cucumbers, a local custom. He refuses any **payment**, saying that hospitality should never cost a **penny**.\n\nHe once showed me a small **pearl** that his grandfather had found in an oyster on the beach, and he said that wisdom, like light, must **penetrate** the heart slowly. When I asked how he felt about modern life, he smiled. \"Young people **perceive** happiness differently,\" he said, \"but I am one hundred **percent** sure that a full stomach and a quiet mind are enough.\""},"118":{"w":["percentage","perfectly","perform","performance","perfume","perhaps","period","permanent","permanently","permission","permit","persist","personal","personality","personnel","perspective","persuade","pessimistic","pet","petrol"],"title":"The Philosopher's Job Interview","t":"Last year my cousin Hao applied for a job at a large company, but the **personnel** manager was not impressed at first. Hao had studied philosophy rather than business, and his **personal** statement was full of big ideas. \"**Perhaps** you would be happier elsewhere,\" the manager said.\n\nHao did not give up. He asked for **permission** to give a short presentation explaining why his background could be useful. He argued that philosophy teaches people to see problems from a different **perspective**, and he spoke calmly and **perfectly** clearly. Slowly the manager began to listen.\n\nHis strongest point concerned the company's recent **performance**. Hao had studied its reports and noticed that a large **percentage** of customers stopped using its products after a short **period**. He suggested a simple solution: improve customer service and focus on long-term relationships. \"Companies that **persist** with short-term thinking rarely succeed,\" he said.\n\nThe manager, who had been **pessimistic** about Hao's chances, was won over. \"You managed to **persuade** me,\" she said, smiling. \"That's not easy.\" She explained that she valued **personality** as much as qualifications, and Hao was offered a **permanent** position. He has since been promoted twice.\n\nHao now leads a small team and keeps the habits that got him the job. He likes to **perform** small experiments, testing new ideas with customers before making big decisions. His office smells faintly of **perfume** from the gift shop next door, and his **pet** cat sometimes appears in his video meetings. He cycles to work to save money on **petrol**, and the company's rules **permit** flexible hours, which suits him well. He says he hopes to stay there **permanently**."},"119":{"w":["petroleum","phase","phenomenon","philosopher","philosophy","phone","photograph","photographic","phrase","physical","physician","physicist","physics","picnic","pie","pierce","pig","pigeon","pile","pill"],"title":"The Physics Picnic","t":"Every summer the physics department at our university holds a **picnic** in the park beside the river. It is the one day of the year when professors and students forget about exams and simply enjoy themselves. Last year the event was organised by Professor Tao, a famous **physicist** who studies light.\n\nProfessor Tao is also something of a **philosopher**. Over lunch she likes to discuss big questions: what is time, why does the universe exist, and how should we live? She believes that **physics** and **philosophy** are closely connected. \"Every **phenomenon** we observe raises a question about meaning,\" she said as she handed out slices of apple **pie**.\n\nHer husband, a **physician** at the local hospital, came with their children. He reminded everyone to take a break from screens and enjoy some **physical** activity. A group of students played football, while others fed a **pigeon** that kept landing on the picnic blanket. One child chased a small **pig** that had escaped from a nearby farm, which caused great excitement.\n\nProfessor Tao brought an old camera and took a **photograph** of the whole group. She said she loved **photographic** film because it captures light in a way digital cameras cannot. She also explained that the world is entering a new **phase** of energy use as it moves away from **petroleum** towards solar power.\n\nAs the afternoon ended, someone used a stick to **pierce** a watermelon, and juice spilled everywhere. The students left a neat **pile** of rubbish bags by the gate. Professor Tao closed the day with her favourite **phrase**: \"Stay curious.\" Later she sent everyone a message on her **phone**, reminding them to drink plenty of water, and joking that sunshine is the only **pill** a scientist really needs."},"120":{"w":["pillar","pillow","pilot","pin","pinch","pine","pink","pint","pipe","pipeline","pistol","pit","pitch","plain","planet","plantation","plaster","plastic","platform","player"],"title":"The Pilot's Tea Plantation","t":"After thirty years as an airline **pilot**, Captain Ma retired to a tea **plantation** in the hills of Yunnan. He bought the land from an old farmer and built a simple wooden house on a **platform** of stone above the valley. From his window he can see rows of tea bushes stretching to the horizon and a forest of tall **pine** trees beyond.\n\nHis life is now **plain** and quiet. He wakes early, drinks a **pint** of water and walks among the tea bushes, using a small **pin** to attach labels to the plants he is testing.\n\nThe house is built around a central wooden **pillar** that holds up the roof. When Captain Ma first arrived, he had to repair cracks in the walls with **plaster**, and he replaced the old water **pipe** with a new **pipeline** connected to a mountain spring. He avoids **plastic** wherever possible, storing his tea in clay jars instead.\n\nHe misses flying sometimes. He keeps a small globe of the **planet** on his desk and points out all the cities he has visited. His grandson, a keen football **player**, visits every summer and practises on a flat **pitch** behind the house. Once the boy tumbled into an old **pit** near the field and came out muddy and laughing, and his grandfather gave his cheek an affectionate **pinch**.\n\nCaptain Ma says he sleeps better here than anywhere else, with his head on a **pillow** stuffed with dried tea leaves. Every year he holds a running race for local children and fires a toy starting **pistol** to begin it. His favourite colour, he says, is **pink**, the colour of the sky at dawn over the valley."},"121":{"w":["playground","pleasant","plentiful","plenty","plot","plough","pluck","plug","plunge","plural","plus","pocket","poem","poet","poetry","point","poison","poisonous","poke","pole"],"title":"The Playground Poet","t":"Every afternoon, an old man named Mr. Lu sits on a bench beside the school **playground**, writing in a small notebook that he keeps in his coat **pocket**. The children call him \"the **poet**\", and he is happy with the name. He has written thousands of short **poems|poem** about the things he sees: children laughing, leaves falling, a dog chasing a ball.\n\nMr. Lu was once a farmer. For forty years he used an old wooden **plough** to work a small **plot** of land in the countryside, and he says that farming and **poetry** have a lot in common: both require patience and an eye for small details. \"You **pluck** words from the air,\" he says, \"the way you pull weeds from the soil.\"\n\nHis poems are simple but full of meaning. One describes a child who tries to **poke** a hole in a cloud with a long bamboo **pole**. Another says that a teacher's kind words are like medicine, while cruel words are like **poison**. He warns children that gossip is **poisonous** and can **plunge** a friend into sadness.\n\nThe children love him. They bring him apples, ask him to explain difficult words and argue about grammar, such as whether \"children\" is a **plural**. One boy once tried to **plug** his ears when Mr. Lu read a sad poem, but later admitted it was his favourite.\n\nThere is **plenty** of joy in his life, he says. The neighbourhood is **pleasant**, the children are kind, and ideas are **plentiful**. His only complaint is his weakening eyesight. \"But that's a **plus**,\" he jokes. \"Now I imagine more than I see.\" The **point** of poetry, he says, is to notice what others miss."},"122":{"w":["police","policy","political","politician","politics","poll","pollute","pollution","pond","pool","pop","popular","population","porch","porridge","port","portable","porter","portion","portrait"],"title":"Cleaning the Pond","t":"For years, the **pond** in the middle of our neighbourhood park was a place where children fished and old men played chess. But rapid **population** growth and new factories nearby began to **pollute** the water, and by last summer it was covered in rubbish and green slime.\n\nThe **pollution** became a **political** issue. A local **politician** promised to clean the pond before the election, and a newspaper **poll** showed that most residents considered it a priority. Yet nothing happened, and many people said they were tired of **politics** and wanted action.\n\nSo a group of volunteers decided to act. Their leader, a retired harbour **porter** named Mr. Ding, organised weekend clean-ups. Volunteers used **portable** pumps to drain part of the water and removed dozens of bags of plastic waste, and Mr. Ding joked that his years at the **port** had prepared him well for heavy lifting.\n\nThe clean-up became surprisingly **popular**. Families brought snacks, children painted signs, and a local artist painted a **portrait** of the pond as it had looked fifty years earlier. Each volunteer took on a **portion** of the work, from collecting rubbish to planting reeds, and on cold mornings a nearby café served free bowls of rice **porridge**.\n\nEventually the city government changed its **policy** and agreed to install a filter system, and the **police** began fining people who dumped rubbish. Today the pond is once again a **pool** of clean water, and frogs **pop** up among the lily pads. Mr. Ding sits on the **porch** of the café every morning, watching children feed the ducks."},"123":{"w":["pose","position","positive","possess","possession","possibility","possibly","postage","postman","postpone","pot","potential","pound","pour","poverty","powder","power","powerful","practicable","practical"],"title":"From Poverty to Porcelain","t":"Mrs. Ren grew up in **poverty** in a mountain village where families often had no electricity. As a child she helped her mother make clay **pots|pot** to sell at the market; each pot weighed about a **pound** and sold for only a few coins. Yet she soon discovered that she **possessed|possess** a natural talent for shaping clay.\n\nWhen she was twenty, a teacher from the city visited the village and saw her work. He said she had great **potential** and encouraged her to study art. At first the idea seemed impossible, since her family's only valuable **possession** was an old bicycle. But the teacher helped her apply for a scholarship, and she won it.\n\nAt art school she learned to mix clay with a fine white **powder** to make porcelain, and to **pour** liquid glaze over each piece. Her teachers praised her **practical** skills and her ability to turn ideas into objects. Art, she once said, must also be **practicable**: \"A cup should be beautiful, but it must also hold tea.\"\n\nAfter graduating, she was offered a good **position** at a design company in Shanghai. Instead, she decided to **postpone** her city career and return home to open a pottery workshop. Many people thought she was making a mistake, but she saw the **possibility** of helping her village.\n\nToday her workshop employs thirty people, and her pottery is sold online across the country. The village **postman** jokes that her **postage** bills must be enormous. She describes her success as a **positive** example of the **power** of education. \"**Possibly** I was lucky,\" she says, \"but luck is more **powerful** when someone believes in you.\" When photographers ask her to **pose** for pictures, she laughs and insists on holding a lump of clay."},"124":{"w":["practically","praise","pray","prayer","precaution","preceding","precious","precise","precision","predict","preface","prefer","preferable","preference","prejudice","preliminary","premier","preparation","prepare","preposition"],"title":"Preparing Together","t":"Every student has a different **preference** when it comes to exam **preparation**. My roommate Lan likes to **prepare** months in advance, while I usually **prefer** to work intensely in the last few weeks. Our teacher, however, says that steady work is **preferable** to last-minute panic.\n\nThis year Lan decided to help me change my habits. She drew up a detailed plan with **precise** goals for each week. She studied the papers from the **preceding** years to **predict** which topics were likely to appear, and she marked every grammar point, from verb tenses to the correct **preposition** after each adjective. Her **precision** amazed me.\n\nWe began with a **preliminary** test to see where I stood, and the results were not good. I had a strong **prejudice** against grammar, thinking it boring and unnecessary, and Lan said this attitude was **practically** the main reason for my mistakes. \"Grammar is like the **preface** of a book,\" she said. \"Skip it, and you miss the meaning.\"\n\nShe also insisted on healthy habits as a **precaution** against stress. We slept eight hours a night, ate fruit instead of snacks and walked in the park every evening. Time is **precious**, she reminded me, and wasted hours can never be recovered.\n\nOn the night before the exam, Lan's grandmother called to say she would **pray** for us. I am not religious, but I felt grateful for her **prayer**. When the results came out, I had passed with a high score. Lan received warm **praise** from our teacher, who called her \"the **premier** study partner of the class\". She just laughed and said the hard work had been mine."},"125":{"w":["prescribe","presence","presently","preserve","president","press","pressure","presumably","pretend","pretty","prevail","prevent","previous","previously","pride","priest","primarily","primary","prime","primitive"],"title":"The Village School","t":"In our village there is a small **primary** school founded more than a century ago by a local **priest** who believed that every child deserved an education. Its current head teacher, Mr. Ge, is also **president** of the village education council. He is a quiet man, but his **presence** fills every room.\n\nThe school was once very **primitive**. Classes took place in a single room with a mud floor, and children shared books. **Previously**, many families kept their children at home to work in the fields, and Mr. Ge has spent thirty years trying to **prevent** this. He believes that education is **primarily** about giving children choices, not just passing exams.\n\nHe faces constant **pressure** to modernise. Officials **press** him to adopt new technology and standardised tests, while parents want the school to **preserve** traditional values. Mr. Ge tries to balance both. He has kept the old classroom as a museum but built a new library next to it. In his **previous** job as a city teacher, he learned that good ideas must be tested carefully.\n\nMr. Ge never tries to **pretend** that the school is perfect. When a student fell ill last year, the local doctor had to **prescribe** strong medicine, and Mr. Ge drove the child to the hospital himself. Kindness, he says, must always **prevail** over rules.\n\nThe school is now in its **prime**. Its students regularly win regional prizes, and many go on to university, and Mr. Ge takes great **pride** in their success. **Presently** he is planning a new science room. \"It will be **pretty** small,\" he admits, \"but **presumably** it will inspire someone to become a scientist.\""},"126":{"w":["prince","princess","principal","principle","print","prior","priority","prison","prisoner","private","privilege","prize","probability","probable","probably","procedure","proceed","process","procession","proclaim"],"title":"The Castle on the Hill","t":"On a hill above our town stands an old castle that once belonged to a **prince**. For centuries it was a **private** residence, closed to the public, but ten years ago it became a museum. Visitors can now walk through the rooms where the prince and **princess** lived and see the dark cell where a **prisoner** was once kept for twenty years.\n\nThe museum's **principal** guide is an elderly historian named Mr. Shen. He explains that the castle was later used as a **prison** during a period of political conflict, and that its walls still bear the names of people who were held there. \"It is a **privilege** to tell their stories,\" he says.\n\nMr. Shen follows one strict **principle**: he never exaggerates. If a story is only **probable**, he says so; if there is no evidence, he does not repeat it. He admits that the **probability** of some legends being true is very low, but he enjoys telling them anyway, **probably** because visitors love them.\n\nEvery spring the town holds a **procession** through the castle gates to remember its history. Children dress as knights and princesses, and a local official stands on the castle wall to **proclaim** the start of the festival. The best costume wins a small **prize**.\n\nTurning the castle into a museum was a long **process**. The town had to follow a complicated **procedure** to obtain permission, and protecting the old walls became its main **priority**. **Prior** to opening, experts spent three years repairing the roof, and the museum had to **print** thousands of new guidebooks. Now, Mr. Shen says, the project can **proceed** to its next stage: a library for children."},"127":{"w":["produce","product","production","productive","profession","professional","professor","profit","profitable","program","progress","progressive","prohibit","project","prominent","promise","promising","promote","prompt","pronoun"],"title":"The Professor's Farm","t":"**Professor** Ma teaches agriculture at a university in the north, but she spends most weekends on a small farm outside the city. Her **project** is simple but ambitious: to show that small farms can be both **productive** and **profitable** without harming the environment.\n\nShe began five years ago with a plot of poor land, and local farmers said it would never **produce** good crops. But Professor Ma used **progressive** methods: rotating crops, planting trees along the edges and using natural fertilisers. Each year the farm's **production** increased, and today its main **product** is organic vegetables, sold at a weekly market in the city.\n\nHer **profession** has given her a strong scientific background, but she insists that real farming requires practical experience. \"You can be a **professional** in the classroom and still know nothing about soil,\" she says. She believes the **progress** of agriculture depends on cooperation between scientists and farmers.\n\nHer work has made her a **prominent** voice in the region. Local officials invited her to help design a new **program** to **promote** sustainable farming. She supported a plan to **prohibit** certain harmful chemicals near rivers, and she showed farmers how to make a **profit** without using them.\n\nHer students describe her as **prompt**, energetic and full of humour. She once marked a student's essay by circling every incorrect **pronoun** in red and writing at the bottom: \"Good ideas, terrible grammar.\" She makes one **promise** to every new student: if they work hard, she will take them to the farm. Many of the most **promising** young farmers in the province began as her students."},"128":{"w":["pronounce","pronunciation","proof","proper","properly","property","proportion","proportional","proposal","propose","prospect","prosperity","prosperous","protect","protection","protective","protein","protest","proud","prove"],"title":"The Proposal","t":"When my cousin Wei decided to **propose** to his girlfriend, he spent months planning every detail. He wanted the **proposal** to be perfect, but he also wanted it to feel natural. \"It has to be **proper**,\" he told me, \"but not too formal.\"\n\nHis girlfriend, Lin, is a nutritionist who cares deeply about health. She is always reminding him to eat enough **protein** and vegetables, and she would **protest** loudly whenever he ate fast food. Wei decided to make this part of his plan: he would cook a healthy dinner and hide the ring inside a light dessert.\n\nHe practised cooking for weeks. He also practised speaking French, because Lin loves French films, but his **pronunciation** was terrible, and he could barely **pronounce** the word for \"marry\". Eventually he gave up and decided to speak from the heart.\n\nOn the day, everything went wrong. The dessert collapsed, a pan caught fire, and the smoke alarm went off. Lin rushed into the kitchen holding a **protective** towel over her face, and Wei, embarrassed, knelt down in the middle of the mess. She laughed so hard that she could barely answer. But she said yes.\n\nThey are now planning to buy a small **property** near a **prosperous** village known for its fruit, where they hope to enjoy a quiet life and future **prosperity** together. Wei wants to **protect** the natural landscape and plant trees around the house to provide **protection** from strong winds, and the **prospect** of country life excites them both.\n\nWei often jokes that his failed dinner was the best **proof** of love: Lin accepted him even when the kitchen was on fire. Lin says the evening helped to **prove** that his heart was in the right place. She is **proud** of him, and she hopes their life will always have the right **proportion** of laughter and calm, a **proportional** mix of work and play. Above all, she hopes he will learn to cook **properly**."},"129":{"w":["provide","provided","province","provision","psychological","public","publication","publish","pudding","puff","pulse","pump","punch","punctual","punish","punishment","puppet","purchase","pure","purely"],"title":"The Puppet Theatre","t":"In the capital of our **province** there is a small **puppet** theatre that has entertained families for more than a hundred years. Its shows are performed by a family of artists who carve wooden puppets by hand and dress them in silk costumes, and for many children it is their first experience of live theatre.\n\nThe current director, Mrs. Gong, is **punctual** to the minute. Every performance begins exactly on time, and latecomers must wait outside. She believes discipline is essential, but she never likes to **punish** her young performers harshly. When a puppeteer once dropped a puppet during a show, his only **punishment** was to sweep the stage.\n\nThe theatre receives some **public** funding, but it must also earn its own money. Mrs. Gong works hard to **provide** a programme that appeals to everyone, **provided** that it respects tradition. She recently decided to **publish** a book about the history of the theatre, and the **publication** became a local bestseller.\n\nShe is also interested in the **psychological** effects of puppetry. Children, she explains, often express their feelings more freely through puppets. In one popular show, a puppet boxer tries to **punch** a dragon but ends up making friends with it. Children laugh until they are out of breath, and parents can feel the **pulse** of excitement in the hall.\n\nBehind the stage, an old hand **pump** forces air into a machine that sends out a **puff** of smoke whenever the dragon appears. The effect is **purely** traditional, using no modern technology, and Mrs. Gong says the smoke is **pure** water vapour and completely safe.\n\nThe theatre's **provisions|provision** for visitors are simple: tea, sweets and a homemade **pudding** sold at the door. Last year a businessman offered to **purchase** the building and turn it into a hotel. Mrs. Gong politely refused."},"130":{"w":["purify","purity","purple","purpose","purse","pursue","pursuit","puzzle","qualify","quality","quantity","quarrel","quart","quarterly","queen","queer","queue","quicken","quilt","quit"],"title":"The Quilt Maker","t":"My grandmother has made **quilts|quilt** for sixty years, and each one is a small work of art. She chooses fabrics of the highest **quality**, sews every stitch by hand and decorates the edges with **purple** and gold thread. Her **purpose**, she says, is not to make money but to keep family stories alive.\n\nEvery quilt tells a different story. One shows the house where she grew up; another shows the river where she met my grandfather. She cares nothing for the **quantity** of quilts she makes, and some take a whole year. A few neighbours think she is a little **queer** for spending so long on one blanket, but she does not mind.\n\nShe learned the craft from her mother, who was famously strict. Once, as a girl, my grandmother wanted to **quit** after a long **quarrel** about a crooked seam. Her mother simply handed her the needle again and told her to try harder. \"Only patience can **qualify** you to call yourself a craftswoman,\" she said.\n\nMy grandmother's lifelong **pursuit** of perfection has made her well known. A cultural magazine wrote about her in its **quarterly** edition, calling her \"the **queen** of quilts\", and visitors sometimes **queue** outside her house to see her work. She keeps a small **purse** of coins by the door to pay the children who help her sort fabric.\n\nShe also has her own simple philosophy. She believes that hard work can **purify** the mind and that the **purity** of a design comes from simplicity. When I asked her how to **pursue** happiness, she gave me a **puzzle** instead of an answer: \"What is lighter than a feather but harder to hold?\" The answer, she said, is breath. She told me to slow down and not **quicken** my pace through life. Every evening she drinks a **quart** of warm milk before bed and sleeps under her oldest quilt."},"131":{"w":["quiz","quotation","quote","rabbit","race","racial","rack","racket","radar","radiate","radiation","radical","radioactive","radioactivity","radish","radium","radius","rag","rage","raid"],"title":"The Science Quiz","t":"Every year our school holds a science **quiz**, and this year's theme was the history of physics. Our team prepared for weeks, reading biographies and practising answers. Our favourite **quotation** came from Marie Curie: \"Nothing in life is to be feared, it is only to be understood.\" We decided to **quote** it at the start of our presentation.\n\nThe first round focused on Curie's discoveries. She and her husband discovered **radium** and studied **radioactivity**, the process by which certain elements **radiate** energy. Their work showed that **radioactive** materials could be used in medicine, but also that exposure to **radiation** was dangerous; Curie herself suffered serious health problems as a result.\n\nThe second round was about modern technology. Questions covered everything from the **radar** systems used at airports to the **radius** of the Earth, and one question, about how a tennis **racket** absorbs energy when it hits a ball, confused everyone.\n\nThere was also a round on social history. We were asked about scientists who had fought **racial** discrimination, and we discussed how some ideas once seen as **radical** have since become widely accepted. One student who usually stayed quiet gave a brilliant answer and received loud applause.\n\nThe final round was a **race** against the clock. Our captain was so nervous that she knocked over a **rack** of test tubes, and our team mascot, a small toy **rabbit**, fell to the floor. The teacher just laughed and handed her a **rag** to clean up. Then the other team launched what felt like a lightning **raid**, answering ten questions in a row, and our captain almost flew into a **rage**. In the end we came second. The prize was a basket of fresh vegetables, including a bunch of bright red **radishes|radish**."},"132":{"w":["rail","railroad","rainbow","rainy","rake","range","rank","rapid","rapidly","rare","rarely","rat","rate","rather","ratio","rational","raw","ray","razor","reach"],"title":"The Mountain Railway","t":"In the nineteenth century, building a **railroad** across the mountain **range** in the west was considered almost impossible. Engineers had to cut tunnels through solid rock and lay **rail** along narrow cliffs. Today the line is famous for its views, and tourists come from around the world to ride the old trains.\n\nMy grandfather worked on the railway for forty years, rising through the **ranks|rank** from track worker to station manager. He says the work was hard but **rarely** boring. On **rainy** days he and his team had to clear stones from the tracks with a **rake**, and in winter they battled snow and ice. Still, he says, a **rainbow** over the mountains made everything worth it.\n\nHe remembers how **rapid** the changes were. When he started, trains travelled slowly and the journey took two days; within a few decades, new engines had **rapidly** reduced it to six hours. The **rate** of progress, he says, was astonishing.\n\nLife at the stations had its own problems. A **rat** once stole his lunch, and he chased it around the platform with a broom. He also remembers a strange morning when a single **ray** of sunlight entered a tunnel for the first time after an earthquake opened a crack in the rock.\n\nGrandfather is a **rational** man who dislikes exaggeration; he would **rather** tell a simple true story than an exciting false one. He once explained to me the **ratio** of workers to engineers on the project: ten to one. He still keeps the old **razor** he used every morning at the station and a piece of **raw** iron from the first rail ever laid there. Some experiences are so **rare**, he says, that you must keep a reminder of them. Even now, in his eighties, he can **reach** the old station on foot in twenty minutes."},"133":{"w":["react","reaction","reader","readily","reading","real","reality","realize","realm","reap","rear","reasonable","rebel","rebellion","recall","receiver","recent","recently","reception","recite"],"title":"The Thursday Reading Club","t":"In our neighbourhood library, a **reading** club meets every Thursday evening. Its members range from teenagers to retired teachers, and each week they discuss a different book. The club was started **recently** by a librarian named Mrs. Zou, who believes that books help people understand **reality** more deeply.\n\nAt the first meeting, Mrs. Zou asked everyone to **recite** a favourite line of poetry. Some members were shy, but others **readily** agreed. A retired engineer recited a poem about his childhood, and several people were moved to tears. Their **reaction**, Mrs. Zou said, proved how powerful words can be.\n\nThe club has explored books from every **realm** of literature, from history to science fiction. One **recent** discussion focused on a novel about a **rebel** who led a **rebellion** against an unjust king, and members disagreed about whether the hero's actions were **reasonable**. \"How would you **react** in his position?\" Mrs. Zou asked.\n\nSome members joined to make friends. A young mother said she wanted to **rear** her children to love books, and that the club had helped her **realize** how much she missed reading. An old man said he could **recall** almost every book he had read as a child, and he enjoyed sharing these memories.\n\nAt the end of each season, the club holds a small **reception** with tea and cakes. Mrs. Zou says the greatest reward of her work is watching each **reader** grow more confident. \"We **reap** what we sow,\" she says. \"Plant a love of books, and it grows for life.\" She jokes that the **receiver** of the old telephone on her desk has hardly been touched since the club began, because everyone now comes in person. The club makes no money, but she says its **real** value is friendship."},"134":{"w":["recognition","recognize","recollect","recommend","recommendation","record","recorder","recover","recovery","recreation","recruit","reduce","reduction","reed","reel","refer","reference","refine","reflect","reflection"],"title":"My Uncle's Recovery","t":"Two years ago my uncle, a professional fisherman, was badly injured when a heavy fishing **reel** broke loose and struck his arm. Doctors warned that his **recovery** might take months, and he was not sure he would ever **recover** fully or return to the sea.\n\nIn hospital he kept a diary and used a small voice **recorder** to **record** his thoughts, which he says helped him **reflect** on his life. In one entry he described the **reflection** of the moon on the water and how much he missed the quiet nights on his boat.\n\nHis doctor would **recommend** simple exercises every day, and my uncle followed every **recommendation** carefully. Slowly the pain began to **reduce**, and the doctor noted a significant **reduction** in swelling. A physiotherapist helped him **refine** his movements, teaching him to use his arm without strain.\n\nWhen he finally left hospital, he could hardly **recognize** how much his life had changed. He could no longer lift heavy nets, but he discovered new forms of **recreation**: painting, reading and walking along the river among the **reeds|reed**. He also began to **recollect** stories from his childhood and wrote them down.\n\nHis paintings soon gained local **recognition**, and a small gallery offered to exhibit them. The local fishing association even asked him to **recruit** and train young fishermen in safety. In these lessons he often **refers|refer** to his own accident, using it as a **reference** to show why equipment must always be checked."},"135":{"w":["reform","refresh","refreshment","refrigerator","refuge","refusal","refuse","refute","regard","regarding","regardless","region","register","regret","regular","regularly","regulate","regulation","rehearsal","reign"],"title":"The Rehearsal","t":"Every spring, the city's youth orchestra performs a concert to mark the founding of the **region**. This year the conductor, Mr. Lei, decided to **reform** the programme completely: instead of playing the same classical pieces as always, the orchestra would perform music by young local composers.\n\nNot everyone agreed. Some older members of the board tried to **refute** his arguments, insisting that audiences preferred traditional music, and one violinist threatened to **refuse** to play. Mr. Lei listened patiently but would not change his mind, and his **refusal** to give up surprised many people.\n\nRehearsals were long and intense. The final **rehearsal** lasted seven hours, and the musicians took short breaks to **refresh** themselves with tea and fruit. Volunteers brought **refreshments|refreshment** from a nearby café and kept drinks cold in a small **refrigerator** backstage. Mr. Lei asked everyone to **register** their attendance each day and followed every **regulation** of the concert hall. He believed that **regular** practice was essential, and the orchestra met **regularly** three times a week.\n\nHe also tried to **regulate** the stress levels of his young players, treating each musician with kindness **regardless** of skill. Many students said they **regard** the orchestra as a **refuge** from the pressures of school.\n\nThe concert was a triumph. The audience applauded for ten minutes, and critics praised the new music. Mr. Lei said he did not **regret** his decision. When reporters asked him a question **regarding** the future, he smiled. \"In music,\" he said, \"no tradition can **reign** forever. Each generation must add something new.\""},"136":{"w":["rein","reinforce","reject","rejoice","relate","relation","relationship","relative","relatively","relativity","relax","release","relevant","reliability","reliable","reliance","relief","relieve","religion","religious"],"title":"The Horse Trainer","t":"My uncle trains horses on a ranch in Inner Mongolia, and he says the most important tool in his work is not the whip but the **rein**. \"You don't control a horse by force,\" he explains. \"You build a **relationship** with it.\" His method is simple: patience, kindness and clear signals.\n\nHe believes that horses, like people, need to **relax** before they can learn. When a new horse arrives, he spends days simply walking beside it, and only when the animal stops trying to **reject** his touch does he begin training. He uses gentle exercises to **reinforce** good behaviour and never punishes mistakes.\n\nHis work has made him **relatively** famous in the region. Riders from distant towns bring him their horses, and he is known for his **reliability**. Owners say he is the most **reliable** trainer they know, and they place great **reliance** on his advice. \"Every horse is different,\" he says. \"Speed is **relative**; what matters is trust.\"\n\nMy uncle is not **religious**, but he has deep respect for the traditional **religion** of the local herders, who regard horses as sacred. He likes to **relate** old legends about horses that carried heroes across the grasslands, and he says each story has some **relation** to real history, even if the details are exaggerated.\n\nOnce, a frightened horse broke free and ran towards a cliff. My uncle chased it on foot and managed to calm it just in time, and when he finally led it back, everyone felt enormous **relief**. \"That moment helped **relieve** years of worry,\" he joked. When the horse was ready to **release** to its owner, the whole family came to **rejoice** with him.\n\nAsked whether there was any science behind his methods, he laughed. \"I'm no Einstein,\" he said. \"I don't understand **relativity**. But I understand horses.\" Only knowledge that is **relevant** to daily life, he says, is worth keeping."},"137":{"w":["reluctant","rely","remain","remains","remark","remarkable","remedy","remind","remote","removal","remove","render","renew","rent","repair","repeat","repeatedly","repent","repetition","replace"],"title":"The Cabin in the Valley","t":"Last summer my friend Tao decided to **rent** an old cabin in a **remote** valley for three months so that he could write a novel. His family was **reluctant** to let him go: the nearest village was twenty kilometres away, and he would have to **rely** on a single bus that came once a week.\n\nThe cabin was in bad condition. The roof leaked, the door was broken, and the floor was covered with the **remains** of old furniture. Tao spent the first two weeks trying to **repair** everything. He had to **remove** layers of dust, **replace** the broken windows and **renew** the paint on the walls, and the **removal** of an old stove alone took three days.\n\nThe work was exhausting, but it had a **remarkable** effect on him: he found that physical labour helped him think. Each evening he would **repeat** the same routine, cooking a simple meal, reading for an hour and writing two pages. The **repetition**, he said, gave his life a calm rhythm.\n\nNot everything went well. He **repeatedly** forgot to fetch enough water from the stream, and once he had to walk for hours to find a doctor for a bad cough. The doctor's only **remedy** was rest and herbal tea. \"You city people never listen,\" the doctor said with a smile, a **remark** that Tao later used in his novel.\n\nBy the end of the summer, he had finished his first draft, and he says the experience helped to **render** his writing more honest. He does not **repent** his decision for a moment. A photo of the cabin hangs above his desk to **remind** him of those months, and he hopes to **remain** in touch with the people he met there."},"138":{"w":["reply","reporter","represent","representative","reproach","reproduce","republic","republican","reputation","request","require","requirement","rescue","research","researcher","resemble","reservation","reserve","residence","resident"],"title":"The Reporter at the Flood","t":"When a flood struck a mountain village last summer, a young **reporter** named Li Yun was sent to cover the **rescue** operation. She had joined the newspaper only six months earlier, and this was her first major story. Her editor made a single **request**: tell the truth, even when it is difficult.\n\nLi Yun spent three days with the rescue teams. She interviewed soldiers, volunteers and every **resident** she could find. Many had lost their homes; some had lost their entire **residence** and all their belongings. One elderly man refused to leave until he found his dog, and the soldiers helped him search. Li Yun described these moments with honesty and respect, and a **representative** of the provincial government praised the teams, saying they **represent** the best of the country.\n\nHer reporting also raised difficult questions. A **researcher** from a local university explained that the flood had been made worse by poor planning: his **research** showed that new buildings had been placed too close to the river, ignoring safety **requirements|requirement**. \"The law must **require** better planning in future,\" he said.\n\nSome officials were angry. One sent Li Yun a message full of **reproach**, accusing her of damaging the region's image. She did not **reply** immediately. Instead, she checked every fact again, and her editor supported her completely. \"A good **reputation** is built on honesty,\" he said.\n\nMonths later, the government decided to **reserve** land away from the river for new housing. A nature **reservation** was created along the banks, and scientists began a project to **reproduce** native plants that the water had destroyed. The new village, Li Yun said, would **resemble** the old one, only safer. Her article was later reprinted in a magazine on the history of the **republic**, and one historian praised her **republican** spirit, her belief that power must always answer to the people."},"139":{"w":["resign","resignation","resist","resistance","resistant","resolution","resolve","resort","resource","respect","respectful","respective","respectively","respond","response","responsibility","responsible","restless","restore","restrain"],"title":"The Temple Resort","t":"For years a small mountain village depended on farming, but its young people were leaving for the cities. Then the village committee made a bold **resolution**: they would turn an old temple complex into an eco-friendly **resort**. The plan aimed to **restore** the ancient buildings, protect natural **resources|resource** and create jobs for local families.\n\nThe project faced strong **resistance** at first. Some villagers feared that tourists would damage the temples, and they tried to **resist** every change. The village head, an energetic woman named Mrs. Lan, decided to **resign** from her government job in the city to lead the project full-time, and her **resignation** showed everyone how serious she was.\n\nMrs. Lan worked hard to **resolve** conflicts. She listened to each family's concerns and tried to **respond** to them fairly. She believed that **respect** was the key: \"Tourists must be **respectful** of our customs, and we must be **responsible** for protecting the mountain.\" Her **response** to every complaint was patient and practical.\n\nThe **responsibility** for different tasks was divided among families. Two groups managed the guesthouse and the vegetable gardens **respectively**, and each group kept its **respective** accounts. The buildings were repaired with **resistant** materials that could survive heavy snow.\n\nTourists now come from all over the country, and young people are returning. Mrs. Lan admits that she is sometimes **restless** at night, worrying about the future, but she tries to **restrain** her fears. \"We cannot control everything,\" she says. \"We can only do our best.\""},"140":{"w":["restraint","restrict","restriction","result","resume","retail","retain","retell","retire","retreat","retrieve","reveal","revenge","revenue","reverse","review","revise","revolt","revolution","revolutionary"],"title":"The Historian's Book","t":"When Mr. Qin decided to **retire** after forty years of teaching history, his students assumed he would spend his days fishing. Instead, he returned to an old project: a book about the Industrial **Revolution** and its effect on ordinary families. He had started it decades earlier but abandoned it because of his busy teaching schedule.\n\nHe began by trying to **retrieve** his old notes from a box in the attic. Some pages had faded, and he had to **revise** whole chapters. He decided to **review** every source again, from government reports on factory **revenue** to letters written by workers. Some letters described a **revolt** by mill workers who demanded better conditions.\n\nMr. Qin wanted his book to **reveal** the human side of history. The Industrial Revolution, he explains, was a **revolutionary** change, but its **result** was not always positive. Factories brought wealth, but they also brought new forms of suffering. Some workers felt anger and a desire for **revenge** against factory owners; others organised peacefully for reform.\n\nHe tried to **retain** a balanced view, showing both progress and pain, and he exercised great **restraint** in his conclusions. He also explained how governments began to **restrict** working hours, and how each new **restriction** improved workers' lives.\n\nWriting the book was not always easy. More than once he had to **retreat** to his study for days at a time. Whenever he felt stuck, he would take a break and **resume** writing the next morning, and sometimes he would **reverse** the order of chapters to make the story clearer. His grandson helped him **retell** some of the stories in simpler language for younger readers.\n\nThe finished book is now sold in **retail** bookshops across the city. Mr. Qin says his greatest reward is hearing from former students who have read it."},"141":{"w":["reward","rhythm","rib","ribbon","rid","riddle","rider","ridge","ridiculous","rifle","rigid","ripe","ripen","rise","risk","rival","roar","roast","rob","robber"],"title":"The Ridge Race","t":"Every autumn our village holds a horse race along the mountain **ridge**, and the competition between two **rival** families has become a local legend. For thirty years the Wangs and the Zhaos have competed for the prize: a red silk **ribbon** and a basket of **ripe** apples.\n\nThis year the favourite **rider** was a young woman named Zhao Li. She had trained for months, learning to follow the **rhythm** of her horse's steps. Her grandfather, a stubborn old man, insisted on a **rigid** training schedule: up at five, ride until noon, then study the weather. \"The real **risk** is not falling,\" he said. \"It's losing focus.\"\n\nThe race began with a **roar** from the crowd. The horses galloped up the steep path, and for a while it looked as if a Wang rider would win. But halfway up, his horse slipped on loose stones and he fell. Zhao Li stopped to help him, even though she knew it might cost her the race. Luckily, he had only bruised a **rib**.\n\nHer decision surprised everyone. Some called it **ridiculous** to give up the lead, but most people admired her. She still finished second, and the judges gave her a special **reward** for sportsmanship.\n\nThat evening both families sat together for a meal of **roast** lamb under the trees. The children asked a **riddle**: \"What grows stronger the more you share it?\" The answer was friendship. One grandfather joked that the families should finally get **rid** of their old quarrel, and another told the story of a **robber** who once tried to **rob** the village and was chased away by riders carrying an old **rifle**. As the moon began to **rise** over the hills, the apples on the trees continued to **ripen** in the cool night air."},"142":{"w":["robbery","robe","robot","rock","rocket","rod","role","roll","roller","roman","romantic","roof","root","rope","rose","rot","rotary","rotate","rotation","rotten"],"title":"The Rope-Climbing Robot","t":"Last spring our university held a **robot** competition, and my team decided to build a machine that could climb a **rope**. It sounded simple, but it turned out to be one of the hardest projects we had ever attempted.\n\nThe design was partly inspired by ancient **Roman** engineering. We used a **rotary** motor to drive a set of wheels, and a metal **rod** connected each wheel to the main body. The wheels had to **rotate** at exactly the right speed, and the **rotation** had to be smooth, or the robot would fall. A small rubber **roller** helped it grip the rope.\n\nEach member had a different **role**. I was responsible for programming, while my friend Lin designed the frame. We worked late into the night, and the lab smelled of **rotten** fruit because we kept forgetting to throw away our snacks. Lin joked that our project was as delicate as a **rose** and as stubborn as a **rock**.\n\nThe night before the contest, we found the lab in a mess. At first we feared a **robbery**, but nothing had been stolen; a cat had simply knocked over our tools. We laughed, though our hearts were racing like a **rocket**.\n\nOn the day of the contest, our robot began to climb, slowly at first and then faster. The crowd cheered as it reached the top and rang a bell hanging from the **roof** of the stage, then **rolled|roll** safely back down. We won second place. Lin collected the prize wearing a borrowed graduation **robe**, which made everyone laugh, and our professor said our success had its **root** in teamwork. Later Lin gave me a small rose plant and told me not to let it **rot** in the lab. She called it the most **romantic** day of her engineering life."},"143":{"w":["rough","roughly","rouse","route","routine","row","royal","rub","rubber","rubbish","rude","rug","ruin","rule","ruler","rumour","runner","rural","rush","rust"],"title":"The Mountain Bus","t":"Every morning at six, Mr. Liao drives his old bus along a **rural** **route** through the mountains, picking up children for school and farmers on their way to market. He has made the same journey for twenty years, and his **routine** never changes; passengers say they could set their watches by him.\n\nThe road is **rough** and narrow, and in winter it is often covered with snow. The bus is old, with patches of **rust** on its doors and a worn **rug** on the floor. Mr. Liao keeps a **rubber** mat by the door to stop passengers from slipping, and a bag for **rubbish** that he politely asks everyone to use.\n\nHe is known for his kindness. When a passenger is **rude**, he responds calmly; when children are sleepy, he plays music to **rouse** them. Once, when a farmer's cart broke down on the road, Mr. Liao stopped and helped push it to the side. His only **rule**, he says, is respect.\n\nThere have been difficult times. A **rumour** once spread that the service would be cancelled to save money, and villagers worried that their children would have no way to reach school. The children wrote letters to the county office, and the bus was saved. One of them, who uses a wooden **ruler** to measure the snow each morning, still keeps the reply.\n\nThe road passes the **ruin** of an old **royal** hunting lodge, where tourists sometimes stop for photos, and the bus covers **roughly** fifty kilometres a day. Every morning a young **runner** trains along the road, and Mr. Liao always slows down to wave. In the evening, when the last passenger has gone home, he likes to **rub** his tired eyes, drink tea and stay far from the city **rush**. A **row** of children waiting at a bus stop, he says, is the best sight in the world."},"144":{"w":["rusty","sack","sacred","sacrifice","saddle","sadly","sadness","safe","safely","safety","sail","sailor","sake","salad","salary","sale","salesman","salt","salute","sample"],"title":"The Old Sailor","t":"My grandfather spent forty years as a **sailor**, and his stories about the sea filled my childhood. He began working on fishing boats at sixteen, when his **salary** was so small that he could barely afford a bag of **salt**. \"We worked for the **sake** of our families,\" he said, \"not for money.\"\n\nLife at sea was hard. He carried heavy **sacks|sack** of fish, ate simple meals of rice and **salad**, and slept on a narrow bunk. Once a storm nearly sank his boat, and the crew had to **sacrifice** most of their catch to keep it afloat. Everyone survived, and he says that night taught him that **safety** must always come first.\n\nHe loved the moment when the ship would **sail** out of the harbour at dawn. To him the sea was almost **sacred**, and he would **salute** the lighthouse every time his boat passed it, a habit he had learned from an old captain. He liked to bring home a **sample** of seawater from every place he visited, keeping each one in a small bottle on a shelf.\n\nWhen he retired, he bought a small horse and an old leather **saddle** and spent his days riding along the coast. His English grew **rusty**, and he forgot many of the words he had learned from foreign sailors, but he never forgot the sea. For a while he even worked as a **salesman** for a boat company, though he admitted he was terrible at it; he could not bear to pressure customers during a **sale**.\n\n**Sadly**, he passed away last winter, though he died peacefully and **safely** at home in his sleep. His only **sadness**, he once told me, was not about death but about leaving the sea. On the shelf, his bottles of seawater remain **safe**, each one a memory of a different shore."},"145":{"w":["sand","sandwich","sandy","satellite","satisfaction","satisfactory","satisfy","sauce","saucer","sausage","save","saving","saw","scale","scan","scandal","scar","scarce","scarcely","scare"],"title":"The Disappearing Beach","t":"Last summer I joined a research trip to a **sandy** island off the southern coast. The island is famous for its beaches, but scientists have noticed that its **sand** is disappearing at an alarming rate, and our team used **satellite** images to measure the changes over twenty years.\n\nOur leader, Dr. Ou, showed us how to **scan** the images and compare them. The **scale** of the problem was shocking: in some places the coastline had moved back fifty metres. Fresh water was becoming **scarce**, and local farmers could **scarcely** grow vegetables.\n\nThe cause, it turned out, was partly a **scandal**. A construction company had been secretly removing sand to make concrete for buildings on the mainland. When local journalists exposed the story, the public was furious. The company tried to **save** its reputation by offering money to the villagers, but few were impressed.\n\nDr. Ou said our work had to **satisfy** both scientific standards and the villagers' need for justice. The government later described our report as **satisfactory**, and new laws were passed to protect the island. The villagers felt real **satisfaction** at seeing the company punished, though they knew the damage would take years to repair.\n\nLife on the island was simple. Each day we ate a **sandwich** with fish **sausage**, and in the evenings the villagers served us grilled fish with a sweet chilli **sauce** and tea in small cups on chipped **saucer** plates. One evening an old carpenter showed us the **saw** he had used for fifty years. He had a deep **scar** on his hand from a childhood accident, but he laughed about it. \"Life will always **scare** you a little,\" he said. \"That's no reason to stop working.\" He keeps his **savings|saving** in a box under his bed, hoping to help rebuild the village school."},"146":{"w":["scarf","scatter","scene","scenery","scent","schedule","scheme","scholar","scholarship","scientific","scientist","scissors","scold","scope","score","scorn","scout","scrape","scratch","scream"],"title":"The Scholar's Garden","t":"In the old part of the city there is a small garden that once belonged to a famous **scholar** of the Ming dynasty. Today it is a public park, and every spring it attracts thousands of visitors who come to enjoy the **scenery** and the **scent** of plum blossom.\n\nThe garden's caretaker, Mr. Fei, has looked after it for thirty years. He follows a strict **schedule**: every morning he sweeps the paths, trims the bushes with a large pair of **scissors** and **scatters|scatter** seeds for the birds. In winter he wears a thick woollen **scarf** and checks every tree for damage.\n\nMr. Fei is not a **scientist**, but he takes a **scientific** approach to gardening, recording the temperature, the timing of the blossoms and the condition of the soil. A local university once offered him a small **scholarship** to study botany, but he declined, saying the garden was his classroom.\n\nSome visitors treat the garden with **scorn**, dropping litter or climbing on the rocks. Mr. Fei will **scold** them gently and explain the history of each stone. When children **scream** with excitement or try to **scratch** their names on the bamboo, he sighs and teaches them to respect nature.\n\nThe city recently proposed a **scheme** to widen the garden's **scope** by adding a café and a gift shop. Mr. Fei opposed the plan, worried that it would spoil the **scene**. A group of local **scouts|scout** supported him; they volunteered to clean the pond and **scrape** moss from the stone steps, and their efforts won over many residents. In a public vote the plan received a low **score**, and the garden remained unchanged."},"147":{"w":["screen","screw","seal","seaman","seaport","search","secondary","secondly","secret","section","secure","security","seed","seek","segment","seize","seldom","select","selection","self"],"title":"The Secret of the Old Port","t":"For a hundred years, the small **seaport** of Hai'an was a busy centre of trade. Ships arrived from distant countries, and every **seaman** who stopped there told stories in the harbour taverns. Today the port is quiet, but it still holds a **secret** that local historians have tried to uncover for decades.\n\nAccording to legend, a merchant hid a box of gold coins somewhere in the old customs building before fleeing the city during a war. Many people have come to **seek** the treasure, but few have taken the search seriously, and **seldom** does anyone stay longer than a weekend.\n\nLast year, however, a local teacher named Mr. Tang decided to **search** properly. He studied old maps and documents in the city archives, especially a **section** describing the building's original design. He noticed that one **segment** of the basement wall had been rebuilt in the 1940s, and he wondered why.\n\nWith permission from the authorities, a team of engineers used a special scanning **screen** to examine the wall, and they discovered a hidden room behind it. Inside was a metal box with an official **seal**, held shut by a rusty **screw**. Police came to **secure** the site, and **security** guards stood outside while the box was opened.\n\nInside there was no gold, only a bag of rice **seeds|seed** and a letter. The merchant had written that in wartime, food mattered more than money. Mr. Tang called this a **secondary** discovery but a meaningful one: \"First, it shows the past is still here. **Secondly**, it reminds us what really matters.\"\n\nThe city plans to **select** objects from the room for a museum exhibition, and a **selection** of photographs is already on display. Mr. Tang says the search changed his sense of **self**. The authorities have since moved to **seize** the building from private developers and protect it permanently."},"148":{"w":["selfish","seller","semester","semiconductor","seminar","senate","senior","sense","sensible","sensitive","sentence","separate","separately","separation","sequence","series","serious","seriously","servant","session"],"title":"The Semiconductor Seminar","t":"In her final **semester** at university, my cousin Yan attended a **seminar** on **semiconductor** technology led by a **senior** engineer from a major company. Yan was studying physics, but she had never seriously considered a career in industry. The **session** changed her mind.\n\nThe engineer explained that semiconductors are at the heart of every phone, computer and car. He described the long **sequence** of steps needed to produce a single chip, from purifying materials to printing tiny circuits, and how one mistake can ruin a whole **series** of products.\n\nWhat impressed Yan most was his honesty. He admitted that the industry faces **serious** challenges, including shortages of materials and fierce international competition, and that some companies had been **selfish**, focusing on short-term profit rather than long-term research. \"We must take our responsibilities **seriously**,\" he said.\n\nHe also described the factories themselves. Chips are made in clean rooms where a single speck of dust can cause damage. Workers wear special clothes and must store personal items **separately** in lockers, and the most **sensitive** equipment is kept in a **separate** building.\n\nAfter the seminar, Yan asked him for advice. He told her that the most **sensible** choice was to gain practical experience before deciding, and that a good engineer needs a strong **sense** of curiosity. One **sentence** stayed with her: \"Never stop asking why.\"\n\nA year later Yan joined his company, where she works with engineers and **sellers|seller** to develop new products. The **separation** from her family in another city was hard at first, but she feels useful. She jokes that she is now a humble **servant** of the chip, and she was amazed when one of her team's products was mentioned in a debate in the American **Senate**."},"149":{"w":["setting","settle","settlement","seventeen","seventy","severe","severely","sew","sex","sexual","shade","shadow","shady","shake","shallow","shame","shampoo","shape","share","sharp"],"title":"The First House in the Valley","t":"At the edge of the forest stands an old wooden house built by the first family to arrive in our area **seventy** years ago, with nothing but a horse and a cart. The original **settlement** consisted of only three houses, but it gradually grew into a small village.\n\nThe house is now a museum run by the founders' granddaughter, Mrs. Ning, a **sharp**, humorous woman who loves telling stories. Her grandparents, she explains, had to **settle** many disputes with neighbours over land and water, and life was **severe** in winter. Snowstorms **severely** damaged crops, and families had to **share** food to survive.\n\nThe **setting** of the house is beautiful. It sits in the **shade** of tall trees beside a **shallow** stream, and in summer the **shady** garden is a favourite place for visitors to rest. In the evening the **shadow** of the trees stretches across the lawn.\n\nInside, Mrs. Ning shows visitors how her grandmother used to **sew** clothes by hand and make **shampoo** from boiled plant roots. She also explains the strict traditions of the time, when work was divided by **sex** and women had few rights. \"It's a **shame** that girls weren't allowed to go to school,\" she says. She believes society has made real progress against **sexual** discrimination, though there is still work to be done.\n\nMrs. Ning left the village at **seventeen** to study in the city, but she later returned to care for the house. Her life has taken a different **shape** from her grandmother's, she says, but the old values still guide her. When visitors leave, she likes to **shake** their hands and tell them to come back in spring."},"150":{"w":["sharpen","sharply","shave","shear","shed","sheet","shelf","shell","shelter","shepherd","shield","shift","shilling","shiver","shock","shoot","shopkeeper","shopping","shore","shortage"],"title":"The Island Shepherd","t":"On a windy island in the north, an old **shepherd** named Mr. Ma looks after a flock of two hundred sheep. Every spring he must **shear** their wool, a task that takes him three weeks, and he **sharpens|sharpen** his old shears carefully each morning.\n\nHis life follows the seasons. In winter he brings the sheep into a wooden **shelter** to protect them from storms, and he wraps himself in a woollen **sheet** when the wind makes him **shiver**. In summer the sheep graze near the **shore**, where the waves wash up colourful **shells|shell** that his grandchildren collect.\n\nMr. Ma is a practical man. He keeps his tools neatly on a wooden **shelf** in the barn and uses an old metal **shield** to protect the sheep's drinking water from seagulls. Once he had to **shoot** a fox that was attacking the lambs, something he says he did with great sadness.\n\nThe island has changed. Young people have moved away, and there is a **shortage** of workers. The only **shopkeeper** in the village complains that online **shopping** has taken away her customers, prices have risen **sharply**, and many families have had to **shift** from fishing to tourism.\n\nMr. Ma keeps a small box of old foreign coins, including a British **shilling** that a sailor gave his grandfather a century ago. When the news came that the island's last school would close, it was a **shock** to everyone. Still, he does not complain. Every morning he takes time to **shave** carefully, then walks to the cliffs to watch the sea. \"Every animal must **shed** its winter coat,\" he says. \"People, too, must let go of the past.\""},"151":{"w":["shortcoming","shortly","shot","shoulder","shower","shriek","shrink","shrug","shut","shuttle","shy","sickness","sideways","sigh","sight","sightseeing","sign","signal","signature","significance"],"title":"My Aunt Abroad","t":"Last spring my aunt, a famously **shy** woman, joined a **sightseeing** tour of Europe with a group of strangers. She had never travelled abroad before, and she admitted that her biggest **shortcoming** was a fear of new situations. \"I'll probably hide in the hotel,\" she joked.\n\nThe first days were difficult. She suffered from travel **sickness** on the long bus rides, and on the second night a cold **shower** in the hotel made her **shriek** in surprise. When the guide asked whether she was all right, she simply gave a small **shrug** and smiled. Yet she refused to give up.\n\n**Shortly** after arriving in Paris, everything changed. The group took a **shuttle** bus to the river, and she saw the great cathedral for the first time. The **sight** was so beautiful that she let out a long **sigh** of happiness. She asked a stranger to take a **shot** of her standing in front of it, and that photo has been framed in her living room ever since.\n\nShe began to grow in confidence. She learned to read street **signs|sign** in French, followed the guide's **signal** when it was time to move on, and even wrote her **signature** in a museum guestbook. Each small success, she said, had great **significance** for her.\n\nOn the last day she climbed the narrow staircase of an old tower. She had to turn **sideways** to squeeze past other tourists, carrying her backpack over one **shoulder**, and at the top she felt her old fears **shrink** away. When she came home, she told us that her world had opened up. \"I used to **shut** myself away,\" she said. \"Now I want to see everything.\""},"152":{"w":["significant","silence","silent","silly","silver","similarly","simplicity","simplify","sin","sincere","singer","singular","sink","site","situation","sixty","skate","sketch","ski","skill"],"title":"The Singer's Silence","t":"For twenty years, the folk **singer** Lan Xin performed in theatres across the country. Her voice was famous for its **simplicity** and warmth, and her songs were often about ordinary people. Then, at the age of **sixty**, she suddenly stopped singing and disappeared from public life.\n\nHer **silence** puzzled fans for years, and some of the rumours were **silly**, claiming she had moved abroad or joined a religious order. In reality, she had moved to a small village in the mountains, where she spent her days teaching children music and learning to **sketch** landscapes.\n\nI visited her last winter. Her house stood on a quiet **site** near a frozen lake, where children liked to **skate** and **ski**. She greeted me with a **sincere** smile and tea in a **silver** cup, and explained that she had needed time to rest. \"For years I was never **silent**,\" she said. \"I sang every night. I needed to hear the world again.\"\n\nShe talked about the **situation** of musicians today. She worries that the music industry has become too commercial, and that young singers feel pressure to **simplify** their art to please the market. **Similarly**, she believes people rely too much on technology. \"It is no **sin** to enjoy pop music,\" she said, laughing, \"but we must not forget our roots.\"\n\nShe is now teaching a new generation the **skill** of folk singing, and she says each student's voice is **singular**, like a fingerprint. Her decision to leave the stage was **significant**, but she does not regret it. As I left, the sun began to **sink** behind the mountains, and I heard her singing quietly to herself."},"153":{"w":["skilled","skillful","skim","skin","slam","slave","slavery","sleepy","sleeve","slender","slice","slide","slight","slightly","slip","slipper","slippery","slit","slogan","slope"],"title":"A Difficult Museum","t":"Last summer I visited a history museum in a coastal city that tells the story of **slavery** in the Atlantic world. The exhibition was not easy to see, but it was deeply moving. It showed how millions of people were taken from Africa and forced to work as **slave** labour on plantations across the ocean.\n\nThe guide, an elderly man with a **slender** frame and a quiet voice, led us through the rooms. He showed us an iron collar, a pair of rough shoes and a **slice** of bread that researchers had recreated from historical recipes. Each object revealed a **slight** but powerful detail of daily life; even a torn **sleeve** or a worn **slipper** told a story.\n\nOne room contained a large map showing ships crossing the ocean, and a screen on which visitors could **slide** through images of old documents. Many visitors only **skim** the texts, but the guide asked us to read slowly. He explained that people with **skilled** hands, such as carpenters and weavers, were often sold for higher prices, and that even the most **skillful** craftsmen were treated as property rather than people.\n\nHe also described the courage of those who resisted. Some managed to **slip** away at night, crossing **slippery** rocks and climbing a steep **slope** to escape; others hid messages in a narrow **slit** in the walls. Later, a famous **slogan** of the movement to end slavery declared that all people are born free.\n\nThe visit left me feeling **slightly** shaken. At the exit, a **sleepy** child was resting his head on his mother's shoulder, and the heavy museum door shut behind us with a **slam**. The guide had said that the purpose of the museum was not to make visitors feel guilty, but to make them remember. \"Injustice leaves marks on the **skin** of a society,\" he said, \"even when we cannot see them.\""},"154":{"w":["slowly","slum","sly","smart","smash","smog","smooth","smoothly","snake","snap","snowstorm","snowy","so-called","soak","soap","sob","sober","soccer","social","socialism"],"title":"Soccer in the Snow","t":"Every winter the students at our school hold a **soccer** match in the snow. The tradition began twenty years ago, when a **snowstorm** cancelled all lessons and a group of bored students decided to play outside. Today it is the most popular **social** event of the year.\n\nThe field is usually **snowy** and uneven, and players move **slowly** across it, laughing as they fall. Nobody plays **smoothly**; the ball sinks into the snow, and passes rarely reach their target. Even the **smart** players who take the game seriously soon give up and join the fun.\n\nThis year the match took place on a grey morning, when **smog** from the nearby city hung over the hills. Before the game, a teacher gave a short speech about teamwork. She mentioned that the school's founder had believed in a kind of **socialism** that valued community over competition, and that the match reflected that spirit.\n\nThe game was full of surprises. A **sly** midfielder hid behind the goalkeeper and scored an easy goal. Another player tried to **smash** the ball into the net but kicked a snowman instead. A long **snake** of small children ran across the field waving flags, and at one point the cord of the referee's whistle began to **snap**, so he had to shout his instructions instead.\n\nAfterwards everyone was cold and wet. Players had to **soak** their frozen feet in warm water and wash with hot water and **soap**. One younger student began to **sob** because he had lost his gloves, but his friends quickly found them, while the teachers stayed **sober** and organised, handing out hot drinks.\n\nThe local newspaper later described the match as \"the **so-called** world championship of snow soccer\", and many former students return every year to watch. One of them, who grew up in a **slum** on the edge of the city, said it was the happiest day of his school life. By evening the road had been cleared and was **smooth** again, and everyone went home smiling."},"155":{"w":["socialist","society","sock","soda","soft","softly","software","soil","solar","sole","solely","solemn","solid","soluble","solution","solve","somehow","sometime","somewhat","sophisticated"],"title":"Light for the Desert Village","t":"My cousin Mei is an engineer who designs **solar** energy systems for remote villages. Her work combines **sophisticated** technology with simple, practical ideas, and she believes that clean energy is the best **solution** to many of the problems faced by rural **society**.\n\nHer latest project took place in a village in the western desert, where the **soil** is dry and electricity is scarce. The villagers relied **solely** on diesel generators, which were expensive and polluting. Mei's team installed solar panels on every roof and wrote special **software** to monitor the system remotely.\n\nThe work was **somewhat** difficult. The panels had to be fixed to **solid** concrete bases to survive strong winds, and the team had to clean the panels using a **soluble** cleaning powder that would not harm the environment. **Somehow**, despite many setbacks, they finished the project in three months.\n\nThe villagers welcomed the team warmly. They held a **solemn** ceremony to celebrate the first night of electric light, and children cheered as the lamps came on. An old woman offered Mei a bottle of homemade **soda** and a pair of knitted woollen **socks|sock** as gifts. She spoke **softly**, thanking the team for their **soft** hearts as well as their hard work.\n\nMei's company is not a traditional **socialist** enterprise, but she believes that business should serve the community. The **sole** purpose of engineering, she says, is to improve people's lives. \"Technology does not **solve** problems by itself,\" she says. \"People do.\" She hopes to return **sometime** next year to see how the village has changed."},"156":{"w":["sore","sorrow","sort","soul","soup","sour","source","southeast","southern","southwest","sow","space","spacecraft","spaceship","spade","span","spark","sparkle","sparrow","speaker"],"title":"Grandfather's Southern Farm","t":"In the **southern** hills of the province, my grandfather runs a small farm where he grows vegetables and keeps bees. He says the soil there is the best **source** of happiness he knows. Every spring he uses an old **spade** to dig the fields and **sow** seeds by hand, just as his father did.\n\nThe farm lies in a valley that runs from **southwest** to **southeast**, so the fields get sunlight all day. In the morning a **sparrow** often sits on the fence, singing loudly, and my grandfather calls it his alarm clock. He says the bird has a free **soul**, unlike people who are tied to their phones.\n\nHis life has not been free of **sorrow**. My grandmother died ten years ago, and for a long time he could barely work. His back was always **sore**, and his mood turned **sour**. But slowly the farm brought him back to life. He began cooking again, making a simple vegetable **soup** that became famous among the neighbours.\n\nMy grandfather loves to watch the night sky. On clear evenings he points out satellites and tells me about the first **spacecraft** to reach the moon; as a boy, he dreamed of flying a **spaceship** into **space**. \"Every life is a short **span** of time,\" he says, \"but the stars make it feel bigger.\"\n\nHe has a special talent for storytelling. When neighbours gather in the evening, he is always the main **speaker**, and his eyes **sparkle** as he talks. He believes that a single small **spark** of kindness can change a person's life. \"There are all **sorts|sort** of people in the world,\" he says, \"and most of them are good.\""},"157":{"w":["spear","special","specialist","speciality","specialize","specially","species","specific","specify","specimen","spectacle","speech","speed","spell","spelling","sphere","spider","spill","spin","spirit"],"title":"The Spider Specialist","t":"Dr. Lu is a **specialist** in spiders, and her laboratory contains more than two thousand **specimens|specimen** from around the world. Some are tiny, no bigger than a grain of rice; others are larger than a child's hand. Visitors are often frightened, but Dr. Lu insists that most **species** are harmless and extremely useful.\n\nShe decided to **specialize** in spiders as a university student, after watching a **spider** build a perfect web in her garden. She was fascinated by the **speed** and precision of its work. \"It was a **spectacle** of engineering,\" she says. \"Every thread had a **specific** purpose.\"\n\nHer research focuses on spider silk, which is stronger than many metals. Some spiders **spin** silk so fine that it is almost invisible. Dr. Lu's team is trying to **specify** the exact chemical structure of the silk, hoping to use it in medicine and engineering, and she has a particular interest in the silk that forms a perfect **sphere** around a spider's eggs.\n\nDr. Lu often gives a **speech** at schools. She tells children that fear of spiders is natural but unnecessary, shows them how to identify the few dangerous species and explains that many cultures once believed spiders had a protective **spirit**. She also corrects their **spelling** of difficult scientific names, joking that even she has to **spell** each one twice.\n\nHer **speciality** is a small island species that hunts by throwing a sticky thread, like a hunter throwing a **spear**. She once travelled **specially** to the island to study it, and she accidentally let a whole jar of specimens **spill** across the floor of her tent. \"That was a **special** night,\" she laughs. \"Nobody slept.\""},"158":{"w":["spiritual","spit","spite","splash","splendid","split","spoil","sponge","sponsor","spontaneous","spoon","sportsman","spot","spray","spread","springtime","sprinkle","spur","spy","square"],"title":"Springtime in the Square","t":"Every year, in early **springtime**, the people of our town gather in the main **square** to celebrate the end of winter. The festival is **spontaneous** in spirit: there is no official schedule, and anyone can perform. Musicians play, children dance and old people sit on benches in the sun.\n\nThe festival began centuries ago as a **spiritual** ceremony to give thanks for a good harvest. Today it is mostly a cultural event, but some traditions remain. At noon an elder uses a willow branch to **sprinkle** water over the crowd, and children try to dodge the **spray**, which is said to bring good luck.\n\nThe local football club is the main **sponsor** of the festival, and its most famous **sportsman**, a young goalkeeper, always joins the celebrations. Last year he took part in a game in which children tried to throw a wet **sponge** at his face; he laughed and let them win.\n\nNot everything is perfect. In **spite** of the cheerful mood, there are always arguments. Some people complain that the festival has become too commercial; others worry that crowds of tourists **spoil** the peaceful atmosphere. Once, a dispute over stall prices threatened to **split** the organising committee in two.\n\nThe festival has many small rituals. Children are not allowed to **spit** or shout during the opening song. A bowl of sweet porridge is shared with a single wooden **spoon**, and everyone takes one sip, while couples hold hands and **splash** each other at the fountain.\n\nEveryone has a favourite **spot** in the square. Mine is beside the old clock, where you can see the whole scene, and a stranger once joked that I looked like a **spy** watching the crowd. The festival's fame continues to **spread**, and it now attracts visitors from all over the region. For many, it is a **spur** to celebrate life after a long winter, and the scene on that day is always **splendid**."},"159":{"w":["squeeze","squirrel","stab","stability","stable","stack","stadium","staff","stage","stain","stair","staircase","stake","stale","standard","standpoint","stare","startle","starvation","starve"],"title":"Behind the Scenes at the Stadium","t":"Every weekend, thousands of fans crowd into the city **stadium** to watch football. Behind the excitement is a team of hardworking **staff** who make sure everything runs smoothly. My uncle, Mr. Zhou, has worked there for twenty years as head of maintenance.\n\nHis job is demanding. Before every match he checks every **stair** on the main **staircase**, repairs broken seats and makes sure the **stage** used for concerts is safe. He is responsible for the **stability** of the old stands, which were built in the 1960s. \"A stadium must be **stable** like a mountain,\" he says. \"If anything moves, we fix it.\"\n\nHe has learned to deal with every kind of mess. Fans leave behind piles of rubbish, **stale** bread and coffee cups, and the cleaning team must remove every **stain** from the seats. He once had to **squeeze** through a narrow gap under the stands to retrieve a lost phone, and another time a family of **squirrels|squirrel** made a nest in the roof, and he spent a week trying to move them gently.\n\nMy uncle has high **standards|standard**. From his **standpoint**, every detail matters, because thousands of lives are at **stake**. When a manager once asked him to **stack** extra chairs in an emergency exit to save space, he refused. \"Safety first,\" he said.\n\nHe has had a few frightening moments. During one storm, part of the roof collapsed with a crash loud enough to **startle** the whole neighbourhood. Fans began to **stare** at the damage, but nobody was hurt. He also remembers a charity match he organised during a famine abroad, after reading that thousands would **starve** without help; the money raised was used to fight **starvation** in three countries. \"We can't solve every problem,\" he says, \"but we can always take a **stab** at it.\""},"160":{"w":["state","statement","statesman","static","statistical","statistics","statue","status","statute","steadily","steady","steal","steam","steamer","steel","steep","steer","stem","step","stern"],"title":"The Statesman's Statue","t":"In the centre of the old port city stands a bronze **statue** of a nineteenth-century **statesman** who helped to modernise the region. He is shown standing proudly beside the wheel of a **steamer**, the kind of ship that first connected the city to the outside world. His expression is **stern**, but local children love to climb on his shoulders.\n\nHistorians describe him as a practical reformer. Early in his career he made a famous **statement**: \"A **state** grows strong only when its people are educated.\" He introduced new laws, including a **statute** that created the region's first public schools, and he encouraged the building of a **steel** works and a railway that ran up a **steep** hill to the coal mines.\n\nHis reforms were based on careful research. He was one of the first officials in the country to use **statistics**, collecting **statistical** data on population, trade and health. He believed that society should never remain **static**; it must move forward **steadily**, one **step** at a time. Critics accused him of trying to **steer** the country too quickly, but he insisted that progress must be **steady**.\n\nHis personal life was simple. He walked to work every day and refused to accept gifts. When a servant was caught trying to **steal** money from his house, he forgave him and helped him find honest work, saying that most crime **stems|stem** from poverty rather than wickedness.\n\nToday his house is a museum. Visitors can see the small **steam** engine he used to demonstrate new technology, as well as his desk and letters. A guide explained that his **status** as a hero is still debated, but everyone agrees that he changed the city forever."},"161":{"w":["steward","stewardess","stick","sticky","stiff","stiffen","stimulate","sting","stir","stitch","stock","stocking","stomach","stone","stony","stool","stoop","storage","storey","storm"],"title":"A Night at the Mountain Airport","t":"Last winter my flight from Kunming was diverted by a violent **storm** and forced to land at a small mountain airport. The passengers were tired and nervous, but the cabin crew remained calm. The chief **steward**, a quiet man in his fifties, walked up and down the aisle, speaking softly to anyone who looked frightened, while a young **stewardess** handed out blankets and cups of hot tea.\n\nThe airport was little more than a two-**storey** building made of grey **stone**, surrounded by **stony** fields. Inside, the waiting room had a few benches and a single wooden **stool** behind the counter. The staff opened their **storage** room and brought out every blanket and pillow they had in **stock**.\n\nMany of us had not eaten for hours, and my **stomach** was making embarrassing noises. An old woman who ran the airport café began to **stir** a huge pot of noodle soup, and the smell seemed to **stimulate** everyone's appetite and lift their spirits. The floor soon became **sticky** with spilled tea, but nobody cared.\n\nAs the night went on, our legs grew **stiff** from sitting on the hard benches. The steward suggested that we **stick** together in small groups and take turns walking around the room. Whenever a gust of wind shook the windows, I felt my whole body **stiffen**. One elderly passenger had to **stoop** to pass under a low pipe and cut his hand on a nail; the stewardess cleaned the wound, warning him that the medicine might **sting** a little.\n\nMeanwhile, the café owner found a needle and thread and offered to repair torn clothes. With a few quick **stitches|stitch**, she mended a businessman's jacket and a hole in a young woman's **stocking**. By dawn the storm had passed, and we left as friends."},"162":{"w":["stormy","stove","strain","stranger","strap","strategic","strategy","straw","strawberry","stream","strength","strengthen","stress","stretch","strict","strictly","strike","string","strip","stripe"],"title":"The Strawberry Farm","t":"On a **stormy** evening last spring, a **stranger** knocked on the door of the Li family's farmhouse, soaked to the skin. His car had broken down on the mountain road. Mrs. Li invited him in, lit the **stove** and made him a bowl of hot soup. He turned out to be an agricultural expert, and that chance meeting changed the farm's future.\n\nThe Lis grow **strawberries|strawberry** on a hillside beside a clear mountain **stream**. For years they had struggled to compete with large farms in the valley, and the financial **strain** had caused a lot of **stress** in the family. Their old **strategy** was simply to grow more fruit and sell it cheaply, but prices kept falling.\n\nThe stranger suggested a different approach. He explained that the farm's greatest **strength** was its location: cool air, clean water and beautiful views. Instead of competing on price, the family should invite city visitors to pick their own fruit. It was a **strategic** decision that would **strengthen** their position in the market.\n\nThe changes were simple but effective. They covered the ground with **straw** to keep the fruit clean, divided the field into neat rows with **string** and painted the farmhouse with red and white **stripes|stripe** so that visitors could find it easily. Mr. Li removed a long **strip** of weeds along the path and built a small wooden bridge over the stream.\n\nThe family keeps **strict** rules for visitors. Children must wear a safety **strap** on the steep path, and picking is **strictly** limited to the marked area. On busy weekends, the line of cars can **stretch** for half a kilometre. \"What **strikes|strike** me most,\" Mrs. Li says, \"is how one kind act on a rainy night brought us such good luck.\""},"163":{"w":["stroke","strongly","structural","structure","struggle","stuff","stumble","stupid","style","submarine","submerge","submit","subsequent","subsidy","substance","substantial","substitute","subtract","suburb","subway"],"title":"My Father's Long Road Back","t":"Three years ago, my father suffered a **stroke** while waiting for the **subway** on his way to work. He was an architect who had spent thirty years designing the **structure** of bridges and stations, and he was famous among his colleagues for his elegant **style**. Suddenly, he could barely speak or move his right hand.\n\nThe **subsequent** months were the hardest of our lives. My father had to **struggle** to do the simplest things. He would **stumble** when he walked, and he could no longer **subtract** small numbers in his head. \"I feel so **stupid**,\" he once said, close to tears. I told him **strongly** that it was the illness, not him, and that he must never give up.\n\nHis recovery required a **substantial** amount of time and money. We moved to a quiet **suburb** with a park nearby, and I helped him **submit** an application for a government **subsidy** for patients in long-term care. The doctors prescribed a new medicine, a **substance** that helps damaged nerves to recover, but they reminded us that there is no **substitute** for daily practice.\n\nPart of his therapy took place in a warm pool. At first he was afraid to **submerge** his face, but slowly he gained confidence. At home, he spent hours building a model **submarine**, a hobby that trained his fingers and his patience. He kept all his tools and **stuff** in a box beside his chair.\n\nToday my father can walk to the park alone and sketch simple buildings again. He has even started checking the **structural** drawings for a friend's small project. \"The brain is like a building,\" he says. \"If one part fails, you can learn to support it in another way.\""},"164":{"w":["succeed","success","successful","successfully","succession","successive","suck","sudden","suddenly","suffer","sufficient","sufficiently","suit","suitable","sulphur","sum","summarize","summary","summit","sunlight"],"title":"To the Summit of the Volcano","t":"Last autumn, a team of five students set out to climb an active volcano in the north of the country. Their goal was to reach the **summit** and collect gas samples for a university research project. It was the third attempt in three **successive** years; the previous two had both failed because of bad weather.\n\nPreparing for the trip was not easy. The team needed a large **sum** of money for equipment, and it took months to find sponsors. Each member bought a warm **suit** suitable for freezing nights, and the leader checked that they had **sufficient** food and water for a week. \"We must be **sufficiently** prepared,\" she said. \"The mountain does not forgive mistakes.\"\n\nThe climb began well, with bright **sunlight** and clear skies. But on the second day, the weather changed **suddenly**. A **sudden** wind blew clouds of ash across the path, and the strong smell of **sulphur** made breathing difficult. Two students began to **suffer** from headaches, and the leader had to decide whether the ground was **suitable** for camping.\n\nThey waited for a day, and the wind dropped. On the final morning, they had to **suck** in the thin, cold air with every step. After a **succession** of steep climbs, they finally reached the top and **successfully** collected all the samples they needed.\n\nBack at the university, the leader wrote a short **summary** of the expedition for the sponsors. \"If I had to **summarize** the trip in one sentence,\" she wrote, \"I would say that patience is more important than speed.\" The project was a great **success**, and the team was praised for a **successful** and safe expedition. They proved that, with careful planning, ordinary students can **succeed** where others have turned back."},"165":{"w":["sunrise","sunset","sunshine","super","superficial","superior","supplement","supply","support","suppose","supreme","surely","surgeon","surgery","surname","surplus","surprising","surprisingly","surrender","surround"],"title":"The Surgeon of the Valley","t":"Dr. Wen is the only **surgeon** in a small county hospital, in a valley that high mountains **surround** on every side. Her **surname** is common, but everyone in the county knows who \"Doctor Wen\" is. She performs about three hundred operations a year, often working from **sunrise** to **sunset**.\n\nMany people find her decision **surprising**. She graduated from one of the best medical schools in the country, and her **superior** in a big city hospital offered her a well-paid position. **Surprisingly**, she chose to return to her home town instead. \"City hospitals have **super** equipment and plenty of doctors,\" she explains. \"Here, people really need me.\"\n\nLife in the valley is not easy. The hospital often runs short of medicine, and Dr. Wen depends on a regular **supply** of drugs from a charity in the provincial capital. When the charity has a **surplus**, it sends extra bandages and vitamins, which she gives to poor patients as a **supplement** to their diet. Local officials **support** her work, but money is always tight.\n\nSome of her cases are simple: a **superficial** cut, a broken arm or a child with a fever. Others require complex **surgery** in difficult conditions. Once, during a power failure, she completed an operation by the light of a torch. \"I **suppose** I was too busy to be afraid,\" she says.\n\nDr. Wen believes that every life is of **supreme** value, and she refuses to **surrender** even in hopeless cases. On her rare days off, she likes to sit in the **sunshine** outside the hospital, drinking tea with her patients. \"If you love your work,\" she says, \"you will **surely** find it worth the effort.\""},"166":{"w":["surroundings","survey","survival","survive","suspect","suspend","suspicion","suspicious","sustain","swallow","swamp","swan","swarm","sway","swear","sweat","sweater","sweep","swell","swift"],"title":"The Swans of the Swamp","t":"Every winter, hundreds of white swans fly south to a large **swamp** on the edge of our county. Last year I joined a team of volunteers to carry out a **survey** of the birds. We wanted to know how many had arrived and whether their numbers were rising or falling.\n\nThe work was harder than I expected. In the early mornings it was freezing, and I wore a thick **sweater** under my coat; by noon I was covered in **sweat** from walking through the mud. A **swarm** of insects followed us everywhere, and tall reeds would **sway** in the wind, hiding the birds from view. Above us, a **swift** little **swallow** sometimes darted past, catching insects in the air.\n\nWe soon noticed something worrying. Several birds were sick, and one dead **swan** was found near the river mouth. The team leader began to **suspect** that the water had been polluted. Her **suspicion** grew stronger when we saw a **suspicious** pipe running from a nearby factory into the swamp. A local fisherman told us, \"I **swear** the fish here have been dying for months.\"\n\nWe reported our findings to the environmental office. Officials tested the water and decided to **suspend** the factory's licence until it installed proper filters. The factory owner argued that the decision would **sweep** away hundreds of jobs, but the officials insisted that clean water was essential for the **survival** of the wetland.\n\nThe case taught me how fragile our natural **surroundings** are. A wetland can **sustain** thousands of birds and fish, but only if people protect it. After heavy rain, the river began to **swell** again and wash fresh water through the swamp. This winter, our survey showed that the swans had returned in larger numbers. Nature can **survive** a great deal, if we give it a chance."},"167":{"w":["swing","switch","sword","symbol","sympathetic","sympathize","sympathy","synthetic","system","systematic","systematical","tablet","tag","tail","tailor","tale","talent","tame","tan","tank"],"title":"The Tailor's Shop","t":"On a narrow street in the old town, there is a tiny shop run by Mr. Gu, a **tailor** who has been making suits for over forty years. His **talent** is famous across the city; politicians, actors and ordinary office workers all come to him for perfectly fitted clothes. A small wooden sign above the door, carved with a needle and thread, has become a **symbol** of the street.\n\nMr. Gu works in a **systematic** way. He takes more than twenty measurements of every customer and keeps detailed records. For years he wrote everything in notebooks, but recently his granddaughter persuaded him to **switch** to a **tablet**. She also designed a simple **system** for orders, so that each suit now has a paper **tag** with a number and a delivery date. \"It is a more **systematical** method,\" he admits, \"but I still trust my eyes more than the machine.\"\n\nHe dislikes **synthetic** fabrics and prefers natural wool and silk. His favourite material is a soft **tan** leather, which he uses for belts and the edges of pockets. Fashion may **swing** from tight to loose and back again, he says, but good cloth never goes out of style.\n\nThe shop is full of character. A **tame** grey cat sleeps on the counter, waving its **tail** at customers, and a fish **tank** sits in the window. On the wall hangs an old **sword** that belonged to Mr. Gu's grandfather, and he loves to tell the **tale** of how it survived a war.\n\nNot everyone can afford his prices, and Mr. Gu is **sympathetic** to students and young workers. \"I **sympathize** with them,\" he says. \"I was poor once too.\" Out of **sympathy**, he often repairs their clothes for free."},"168":{"w":["tap","tape","target","task","taste","tax","teaching","team","tear","technical","technician","technique","technology","tedious","teenager","telegram","telegraph","telescope","temper","temperature"],"title":"The Old Telescope","t":"When I was a **teenager**, I found an old brass **telescope** in my grandfather's attic. It was broken and dusty, but my grandfather's face lit up when he saw it. \"That was my most precious possession,\" he said, wiping a **tear** from his eye. We decided to repair it together, and it became our summer **task**.\n\nMy grandfather had worked for many years as a **technician** at a **telegraph** office. In those days, people sent a **telegram** for every important event, and he knew every **technical** detail of the machines. He had no formal **teaching** experience, but he was a patient teacher. He showed me how to **tap** the lenses gently to loosen the dirt and how to fix loose parts with **tape**.\n\nThe work was often **tedious**. Some days we spent hours cleaning a single screw, and it would **tax** my patience to the limit. When I lost my **temper**, my grandfather would laugh and say that astronomers must learn to wait. He taught me a simple **technique** for checking whether a lens was perfectly clean: hold it against a candle and look for shadows.\n\nBy August, the telescope was ready. We worked as a **team** on clear nights, choosing a **target** in the sky and adjusting the focus until it was sharp. The night **temperature** in the hills dropped quickly, so we brought blankets and hot drinks.\n\nModern **technology** has given us powerful digital telescopes, but I still prefer the old one. It has a special **taste**, like an old book. Every time I look through it, I remember my grandfather's voice explaining the names of the stars."},"169":{"w":["temple","temporary","tempt","temptation","tenant","tend","tendency","tender","tense","tension","tent","term","terminal","terrible","terrific","territory","terror","test","textbook","textile"],"title":"A Summer in the Mountains","t":"Last summer, my friend Lin spent her vacation **term** teaching in a remote mountain village. When she first arrived at the bus **terminal**, nobody was there to meet her, and she felt a moment of **terror**. Then a group of children came running down the road, shouting her name.\n\nAt first, she lived in a **temporary** **tent** beside the school. After a week of rain, she became the **tenant** of a small room above a **textile** shop, next to an ancient **temple**. Every morning she heard the temple bell, and she could see the forest that marked the edge of the wild **territory** where bears still lived.\n\nTeaching was harder than she had imagined. The school had only one **textbook** for every three students, and many children had never taken a written **test**. They had a **tendency** to stay silent in class, afraid of making mistakes. Lin felt **tense** during her first lessons, but she slowly learned to make the children laugh.\n\nThere were difficult moments. A **terrible** storm once destroyed part of the school roof, and **tension** grew between two families who argued about who should pay for repairs. Lin was often **tempted|tempt** to go home early. \"The **temptation** was strong,\" she admits, \"especially when I was tired and cold.\"\n\nBut there were also **terrific** days. The villagers invited her to dinner, serving **tender** mountain goat cooked with herbs. She learned to **tend** the school garden with the children and to grow beans and potatoes. When she finally left, the whole class walked her to the bus. \"I went there to teach,\" she says, \"but I learned more than they did.\""},"170":{"w":["theatre","theme","theoretical","theory","thereby","therefore","thermometer","thick","thickness","thief","thirdly","thirst","thorn","thorough","thought","thoughtful","thread","threat","threaten","thrill"],"title":"The Play That Almost Wasn't","t":"Every spring, the drama club at our school puts on a play in the town **theatre**. This year we chose a comedy about a clumsy **thief** who breaks into a rich man's garden. The **theme** of the play is simple: honesty brings happiness, while greed brings trouble.\n\nOur director, Mr. Han, is a **thoughtful** teacher who believes that every actor needs to understand the story. He began rehearsals with a short lesson on dramatic **theory**, but he soon realised that **theoretical** discussions bored us. \"Acting is learned on the stage, not in books,\" he said, and from then on we practised every afternoon.\n\nPreparation was **thorough**. Firstly, we designed the set; secondly, we made the costumes; **thirdly**, we practised the songs. The costume team sewed every button with strong **thread**, and the stage crew built a rose garden from paper, complete with sharp **thorns|thorn** made of wire. In one scene, the thief cries out with pain when he steps on a **thorn**, and the audience always laughs.\n\nTwo weeks before the show, there was a serious **threat** to the whole project. The theatre's heating system broke down, and the **thermometer** in the hall showed only five degrees. The owner said the cold might **threaten** the safety of the audience, and he planned to cancel the event. We collected money for repairs and found **thick** curtains to cover the windows, **thereby** keeping much of the heat inside. The **thickness** of the curtains made a real difference.\n\nOn the opening night, the hall was full. My mouth was dry with **thirst** and nerves, but as soon as I stepped onto the stage, I felt a great **thrill**. The **thought** of that evening still makes me smile. We had worked hard, and **therefore** we deserved our success."},"171":{"w":["thrive","throat","throne","throng","throughout","thrust","thumb","thunder","thus","tick","tide","tidy","tie","tiger","tight","timber","timetable","timid","tin","tiny"],"title":"The Island Ferry","t":"Twice a day, a small wooden ferry carries passengers from the fishing port to a quiet island two kilometres off the coast. Its captain, Mr. Ma, has made the trip for thirty years. He knows every rock in the bay and checks the **timetable** of the **tide** before each journey, since the water near the island is dangerously shallow at low tide.\n\nIn summer, a **throng** of tourists waits at the pier, and children push to the front to see the boat arrive. Mr. Ma greets them with a smile and a raised **thumb**. The ferry is built of local **timber**, and he keeps it clean and **tidy**; every rope is coiled neatly, and every knot is **tight**.\n\nThe island's economy has begun to **thrive** in recent years. Guesthouses have opened **throughout** the village, and fishermen now sell fresh fish and **tin** cans of seafood to visitors. A **tiny** museum displays an old wooden chair that islanders jokingly call the \"**throne**\", because a local hero once sat on it after defeating pirates.\n\nNot every crossing is peaceful. One evening last August, black clouds rolled in, and **thunder** shook the sky. A wave struck the ferry with a sudden **thrust**, and a **timid** little boy began to cry. Mr. Ma calmly asked him to watch the clock above the wheel and count each **tick** until they reached the shore. **Thus** the child forgot his fear, and the boat arrived safely.\n\nMr. Ma will retire next year, and his son will take over. \"The sea is like a **tiger**,\" the old captain warns him, with a rough voice from years of shouting into the wind; his **throat** is often sore by evening. \"You must respect it. Never **tie** the boat loosely, and never forget that the water is always in charge.\""},"172":{"w":["tip","tire","tissue","title","toast","tobacco","toe","toilet","tolerance","tolerate","tomato","tomb","ton","tone","tongue","tool","top","topic","torch","torrent"],"title":"A Night Hike on Mount Tai","t":"Last October, I joined a group of friends on a night hike to the **top** of Mount Tai to watch the sunrise. Our guide gave us a useful **tip** before we started: walk slowly, drink often and never rely on a phone for light. Each of us carried a **torch**, and the line of lights moving up the mountain looked like a river of stars.\n\nThe climb was long and tiring. After three hours, I began to **tire**, and the stone steps seemed endless. My **toe** hurt where my shoe rubbed against it, and my **tongue** felt dry. Along the way we passed a tea house, a public **toilet** and the ancient **tomb** of a local official. Every few hundred metres, small shops sold hot drinks, boiled eggs and **tomato** noodles.\n\nOur guide had a calm **tone** of voice and a great **tolerance** for complaints. He explained that porters still carry goods up the mountain by hand, sometimes more than a **ton** each week in total. He spoke with respect about their hard work, which few people could **tolerate** for long.\n\nAs we climbed, conversation turned to every **topic** imaginable. One friend told us about her new job, and another argued that people should stop selling **tobacco** at tourist sites. At one point we crossed a narrow bridge over a mountain stream that had become a **torrent** after the recent rains.\n\nWe reached the summit just before dawn. The wind was freezing, and I wiped my nose with a **tissue** while we waited. When the sun finally rose, the clouds turned gold. We had brought bread and fruit, and someone proposed a **toast** with paper cups of hot tea. My legs were sore, but I knew it was a **title** I had earned: I had climbed Mount Tai. Our guide's last advice was that a good walking stick is the most important **tool** of all."},"173":{"w":["tortoise","torture","toss","total","touch","tough","tour","towards","towel","tower","toy","trace","track","tractor","trade","tradition","traditional","traffic","tragedy","trail"],"title":"The Old Railway Trail","t":"On the edge of our town, there is a walking **trail** that follows an old railway **track**. Trains stopped running there forty years ago, when the line could no longer compete with road **traffic**. Today the path attracts walkers, cyclists and school groups who **tour** the area on weekends.\n\nThe railway was once the centre of local **trade**. Farmers used it to send grain to the city, and a stone **tower** beside the station still shows where water was stored for the steam engines. Old photographs show a **tractor** waiting beside the platform, ready to unload goods. You can still **trace** the outline of the old platform in the grass. The workers who laid the rails by hand in the 1920s described the summer heat as a kind of **torture**.\n\nThe line also has a darker history. In 1962 a **tragedy** occurred when a train hit a cart at a crossing, killing three people. A small stone memorial stands at the spot, and local people still **touch** it gently as they pass.\n\nMy grandfather loves the trail. He grew up beside the station and remembers how children would **toss** coins onto the rails and wait for trains to flatten them into thin metal discs. \"It was a **tough** life,\" he says, \"but people were close to one another.\" He says that **traditional** ways of life have disappeared, but the trail keeps an important **tradition** alive.\n\nEvery summer, the town holds a festival there. Children bring their **toy** trains, and old men tell stories of the railway's golden years. A group of students recently found a **tortoise** living in a pond near the old station; they named it \"Engine\" and built a little fence around its home. After a long walk, I always sit by the pond with a **towel** around my neck and walk slowly back **towards** the town. The **total** length of the trail is only eight kilometres, but walking it is like travelling through time."},"174":{"w":["training","traitor","tram","tramp","transfer","transform","transformation","transformer","transistor","translate","translation","transmission","transmit","transparent","transplant","transport","transportation","trap","tray","treason"],"title":"The Translator's Radio","t":"Ms. Fang is a professional translator who spent three years on a single project: the **translation** of a long historical novel into English. The book tells the story of a general who was accused of **treason** and called a **traitor** by the emperor, though he was in fact innocent. \"The hardest part was to **translate** the old official language,\" she says. \"I had to **transform** ancient phrases into natural modern English.\"\n\nShe works in a small flat in an old part of the city, where a **tram** still runs past her window. On her desk sits an old **transistor** radio that belonged to her father. It no longer works perfectly, and when it fails, she opens it up and checks every wire. She once had to replace a burnt-out **transformer** with one she bought at a flea market.\n\nHer father was a radio engineer, and he taught her how sound is turned into signals that **transmit** information across great distances. \"Radio **transmission** seemed like magic to me as a child,\" she remembers. Her professional **training** began much later, when she studied languages at university.\n\nTranslation has changed her life. Ten years ago, she agreed to **transfer** from a teaching job to a publishing house, and the move led to a complete **transformation** of her career. She felt as if she had been **transplanted|transplant** into a new world, full of books and ideas.\n\nShe warns young translators to avoid one common **trap**: translating word by word. A good translation should be **transparent**, she says, so that readers forget they are reading a translation. Every morning she drinks her tea from a wooden **tray** and watches the people outside — a businessman hurrying to work, a **tramp** sleeping on a bench, students waiting for public **transportation**. \"Every face is a story,\" she says, \"and translation is a way to **transport** stories from one culture to another.\""},"175":{"w":["treasure","treat","treatment","treaty","tremble","tremendous","trend","trial","triangle","tribe","trick","trifle","trim","triumph","troop","tropical","troublesome","trousers","truck","truly"],"title":"The Island Nobody Mapped","t":"For decades, local fishermen told stories about **treasure** hidden on a small **tropical** island off the southern coast. Last year, a team of young scientists finally travelled there — not to look for gold, but to study the island's rare plants and animals. They found something much more valuable than treasure: a living forest that had remained almost untouched for centuries.\n\nThe journey was **troublesome** from the start. Their **truck** broke down twice on the way to the port, and the boat trip took a whole day. When they arrived, they discovered that the only path into the forest had disappeared, so they had to **trim** branches with knives to make their way forward. Insects bit through their **trousers**, and one student became ill with a fever; the team doctor gave him immediate **treatment**, and he recovered after three days.\n\nThe island is home to a small **tribe** of fishing families who have lived there for generations. The scientists were careful to **treat** them with respect and to follow local rules. An old **treaty** signed with the government protects the island from logging, and the tribe guards it closely.\n\nThe forest was **truly** remarkable. In a **triangle** of land between two rivers, the team found a **tremendous** variety of plants, including several species unknown to science. A **troop** of monkeys followed them everywhere, playing every **trick** they could think of, such as stealing hats and snacks. Whenever a large bird flew overhead, the monkeys would **tremble** and hide.\n\nThe expedition was a scientific **triumph**. However, the team leader warns of a worrying **trend**: tourism companies want to build hotels on the island. \"To them, a forest may seem a **trifle**,\" she says, \"but losing it would be a disaster.\" The island now faces a **trial** of its own: whether people will choose to protect it."},"176":{"w":["trumpet","trunk","trust","truth","tub","tube","tuck","tuition","tumble","tune","tunnel","turbine","turbulent","turkey","turning","turnip","tutor","twin","twinkle","twist"],"title":"The Twins and the Trumpet","t":"My cousins Hao and Ming are **twin** brothers who grew up on a farm in the hills. Their family raised chickens and a few **turkeys|turkey**, and grew cabbages and **turnips|turnip** in the fields behind the house. Life was simple, but the boys had one dream: they wanted to play music.\n\nWhen they were ten, their father bought them a second-hand **trumpet** from a market in town. It was dented and its **tube** was slightly bent, but the boys took turns practising every evening beside the old tree, its huge **trunk** hiding them from the neighbours. At first they could barely play a single **tune**.\n\nTheir parents could not afford music **tuition**, so a retired teacher in the village offered to be their **tutor** for free. She taught them to breathe correctly and to **tuck** in their shirts before every performance. \"Music is about **truth**,\" she told them. \"If you don't feel it, the audience won't either.\" The boys learned to **trust** her completely.\n\nThe family went through **turbulent** times. A flood washed away part of the road, and a landslide closed the **tunnel** that connected the village to the town. For weeks, the boys had to walk to school along a muddy path, and Ming once took a bad **tumble** down a slope. A sharp **twist** of his ankle kept him in bed for a month, and his mother made him soak his foot in a wooden **tub** of warm water every night.\n\nBut the twins never gave up. At sixteen, they won a regional competition. They now study at a music academy in the city. Every time they return home, they stop at the **turning** in the road where a tall wind **turbine** stands on the hill. Its lights **twinkle** in the dark, and the boys play a few notes for the village before walking the last kilometre home."},"177":{"w":["type","typewriter","typhoon","typical","typist","ugly","ultimate","ultimately","unable","unbearable","uncertain","uncomfortable","unconscious","uncover","undergo","undergraduate","underline","underneath","understanding","undertake"],"title":"Grandmother's Typewriter","t":"Last autumn, as an **undergraduate** studying history, I decided to **undertake** a small research project about my grandmother's life. She had worked as a **typist** in a government office in the 1960s, and she still kept her old **typewriter** in a wooden box **underneath** her bed. I wanted to **uncover** the stories behind the letters she had typed.\n\nThe project began on an **ugly** night. A **typhoon** struck the city, and I was **unable** to return to my dormitory, so I stayed at her flat. The power failed, the heat was almost **unbearable**, and we sat in the dark listening to the wind. It was a **typical** summer storm in our region, but my grandmother said it reminded her of a much worse one in 1969.\n\nBy candlelight, she showed me piles of old documents. I asked her what **type** of work she had done. At first she was **uncertain** about how much to tell me, and she seemed **uncomfortable** discussing politics. But gradually she began to talk. I took notes and would **underline** the most important names and dates.\n\nOne story moved me deeply. During that 1969 typhoon, a colleague had been hit by a falling window and knocked **unconscious**. My grandmother carried him downstairs and ran through the storm to find a doctor. He had to **undergo** a long operation but **ultimately** recovered, and the two families remained friends for decades.\n\nMy report earned a high mark, but its **ultimate** value was personal. Through the project, I gained a deeper **understanding** of my grandmother's generation: quiet, hardworking and brave. When I finished, she smiled and let me try her typewriter. It was louder than I expected."},"178":{"w":["undertaking","undo","undoubtedly","uneasy","unexpected","unfair","unfortunate","unfortunately","unhappy","uniform","union","unique","unit","unite","unity","universal","universe","university","unjust","unkind"],"title":"Standing Together","t":"Last spring, the cleaning staff at our **university** received **unexpected** news: the management planned to cut their wages by twenty percent. Most of the cleaners were middle-aged women who had worked there for many years, and many felt **unhappy** and **uneasy** about the future. \"It is **unfair**,\" one of them said. \"We already work long hours for very little money.\"\n\nThe decision was **unfortunate** in many ways. **Unfortunately**, the cleaners had no **union** to represent them, and several were afraid of losing their jobs if they complained. Some managers were **unkind**, telling them that they could easily be replaced.\n\nStudents soon heard about the situation. A group from the law department argued that the cut was **unjust** and called on everyone on campus to **unite** in support of the workers. They organised a meeting in a lecture hall, where an astronomy professor gave a short speech. \"In a **universe** of billions of stars,\" he said, \"the only thing that makes us special is how we treat one another. Respect must be **universal**.\"\n\nThe campaign was a **unique** example of **unity** between students and staff. Students wore cleaners' **uniform** jackets for a day, and every teaching **unit** signed a letter of protest. It was a difficult **undertaking**, but it attracted attention from local newspapers.\n\nAfter two weeks, the management agreed to **undo** the decision and promised to discuss future changes with the workers. The cleaners were **undoubtedly** the real heroes of the story; they had found the courage to speak. Today, they have formed their own small association, and many students greet them by name in the corridors."},"179":{"w":["unknown","unless","unlike","unlikely","unload","unlucky","unnecessary","unpleasant","unsatisfactory","unstable","unsuitable","unusual","unusually","unwilling","up-to-date","upon","upper","upright","upset","upside-down"],"title":"The Upside-Down House","t":"In a small park on the edge of our city stands one of the most **unusual** buildings in the country: an **upside-down** house. From the outside, the roof sits on the ground and the front door hangs high in the air. Inside, the furniture is attached to the ceiling, so visitors feel as if they are walking **upon** the roof.\n\nThe designer, a young architect who was almost **unknown** before the project, says the idea came to him on an **unlucky** day. His car broke down, his phone died, and everything seemed to go wrong. \"I felt as if my whole world had turned over,\" he remembers. \"Then I thought: why not build a house that shows that feeling?\"\n\nThe project was not easy. Many engineers were **unwilling** to work on it, saying that the structure would be **unstable**. The first design was **unsatisfactory**, and the city council said the land was **unsuitable** for such a strange building. The architect refused to give up. He used **up-to-date** computer models to prove that the house was safe, and the council finally agreed.\n\n**Unlike** ordinary museums, the house encourages people to play. Children love to sit \"under\" the sofa on the ceiling, and adults take funny photos standing **upright** beside an upside-down bed. Some visitors find the experience **unpleasant**: the sense of balance becomes **upset**, and a few feel dizzy in the **upper** rooms.\n\nOn **unusually** busy days, workers must **unload** extra boxes of souvenirs from trucks every hour. The architect says it is **unlikely** that he will build another one. \"**Unless** you change the way you look at things,\" he says, \"you will never see anything new. Being serious all the time is **unnecessary**.\""},"180":{"w":["upstairs","upward","urban","urge","urgent","usage","used","useful","useless","user","usual","utility","utilize","utmost","utter","vacant","vacation","vacuum","vague","vain"],"title":"A Flat Above the Bakery","t":"When my friend Yang moved to the city for work, she found a **vacant** flat **upstairs** from a small bakery in an old **urban** neighbourhood. The rent was low, and the landlord seemed friendly, so she signed the contract on the same day. She hoped to make the most of the space before her summer **vacation**.\n\nThe flat was full of **used** furniture: an old sofa, a broken chair and a **vacuum** cleaner that made more noise than progress. \"It's completely **useless**,\" Yang laughed. She decided to **utilize** every corner, turning a small cupboard into a **useful** bookshelf and hanging plants by the window.\n\nProblems soon appeared. The landlord's instructions about paying the **utility** bills were **vague**, and she spent hours on the phone trying to understand them. One night water began pouring through the ceiling, and she sent an **urgent** message to the landlord, but her calls were in **vain**. She had to **utter** a few angry words before a neighbour finally came to help.\n\nYang began to study her energy **usage** carefully, using an app that showed how much electricity she consumed each day. As a regular **user**, she noticed that the numbers kept moving **upward** in hot weather, when the air conditioner was on. She felt a strong **urge** to complain, but her neighbours **urge** patience: rising prices are **usual** in summer.\n\nDespite the difficulties, Yang has grown to love the flat. Every morning the smell of fresh bread rises from the bakery below, and the baker waves to her as she leaves. \"I'm doing my **utmost** to turn this place into a real home,\" she says. \"It isn't perfect, but it's mine.\""},"181":{"w":["valid","valley","valuable","value","van","vanish","vanity","vapour","variable","variation","variety","various","vary","vase","vast","vehicle","veil","velocity","velvet","venture"],"title":"The Antique Dealer of the Valley","t":"Mr. Kong runs a small antique shop in a quiet mountain **valley**, three hours' drive from the nearest city. Every Saturday he loads his old **van** and drives to villages across the region, looking for **valuable** objects that people no longer want. \"Everything has **value** to someone,\" he says. \"My job is to find that person.\"\n\nHis shop is full of **various** treasures: an old clock, a set of silver spoons, a painted **vase** from the nineteenth century and a box of **velvet** dresses that once belonged to a wealthy family. The **variety** is remarkable, and prices **vary** greatly. Some items cost only a few yuan; others are worth more than a new **vehicle**.\n\nStarting the shop was a risky **venture**. Mr. Kong left a well-paid job in the city and spent most of his savings on the business. Income is a **variable** thing: in some months he sells almost nothing, while in others a single sale pays the rent for a year. He says that seasonal **variation** in tourism makes planning difficult.\n\nHe has learned to recognise fakes. Every valuable item must come with a **valid** certificate, and he examines each piece with great care. Customers with too much **vanity** often want to buy things just to impress their friends, and he gently advises them to choose objects they truly love.\n\nThe valley itself is part of the shop's charm. In the early morning, a thin **veil** of mist covers the fields, and **vapour** rises from the river as the sun warms the water. Then, as the wind picks up **velocity**, the mist begins to **vanish**, revealing a **vast** view of green hills. \"Antiques remind us that time passes,\" he says, \"but this view has stayed the same for centuries.\""},"182":{"w":["verb","verify","version","vertical","vessel","vest","veteran","vex","via","vibrate","vibration","vice","victim","victorious","victory","video","videotape","view","viewpoint","vigorous"],"title":"The Old Captain's Story","t":"My grandfather is a **veteran** sea captain who spent forty years commanding cargo ships. Last summer, I decided to record his life story on **video** for a school project. He was delighted, and he even found an old **videotape** of his retirement party to help me.\n\nWe filmed in his small flat, which has a wonderful **view** of the harbour. He sat in his favourite chair, wearing a woollen **vest** and holding a cup of tea. As a young man, he had served as **vice** captain on a fishing **vessel**, before taking command of his own ship at the age of thirty-two.\n\nHe told stories in a **vigorous** voice, waving his hands as he spoke. He described the **vibration** of the engines under his feet and how the whole ship would **vibrate** in a storm. He once rescued twenty-three people from a sinking ferry; the **victims|victim** were cold and frightened, but every one of them survived. \"That was my greatest **victory**,\" he said quietly.\n\nHe also loved sailing races. In 1985 his crew was **victorious** in an international competition, and the newspaper published a photo of him holding the cup. Before I included that story, I had to **verify** the date **via** an online newspaper archive, because his memory sometimes mixed up the years.\n\nEditing the film was harder than I expected. Some of his stories had more than one **version**, and he would become **vexed|vex** when I asked him to repeat them. He also corrected my English script, pointing out a wrong **verb** in my introduction. From his **viewpoint**, a sailor must be honest and exact. My final video ended with a shot of a ship's tall **vertical** mast against the sunset."},"183":{"w":["vine","vinegar","violence","violent","violet","violin","virtual","virtually","virtue","virus","visible","vision","visitor","visual","vital","vitally","vitamin","vivid","vocabulary","voice"],"title":"Life on a Hillside Vineyard","t":"On a sunny hillside in the north-west, the Zhang family has grown grapes for three generations. Rows of **vine** plants cover the slopes, and in spring small **violet** flowers bloom between them. Visitors say the place looks like a painting, with colours so **vivid** that photographs hardly do them justice.\n\nThe vineyard is famous not only for its grapes but also for its fruit **vinegar**, which is rich in **vitamin** C. Mrs. Zhang believes that hard work is a **virtue**, and she still picks grapes by hand every autumn. Her husband plays the **violin** in the evenings, and his music floats across the fields.\n\nLife on the farm is not always peaceful. Two years ago, a **violent** storm destroyed **virtually** half the harvest. The **violence** of the wind was terrible; it tore leaves from the vines and broke wooden posts. A year later, a plant **virus** spread through the region, and the family had to remove hundreds of infected plants.\n\nThe Zhangs' daughter, who studied marketing, had a **vision** for the farm's future. She created a **virtual** tour on the internet so that people could explore the vineyard from home. The tour is a **visual** delight, with videos of sunrise over the fields and close-ups of ripe fruit. The number of **visitors|visitor** has since doubled.\n\nShe says that good communication is **vital** for small farms. \"Many farmers have great products, but their work is not **visible** to customers,\" she explains. She has even built a simple **vocabulary** of wine terms in English for foreign guests. \"It is **vitally** important,\" she says, \"that farmers have a **voice** in the modern market.\""},"184":{"w":["volcano","volleyball","volt","voltage","volume","voluntary","volunteer","vote","voyage","wage","waggon","wagon","waist","waiter","waken","wallet","wander","war","warmth","warn"],"title":"Volunteers on the Volcano Island","t":"Last summer, I spent a month as a **volunteer** on a small island with an active **volcano**. The program was **voluntary**, and none of us received a **wage**, but we were given free food and a place to sleep. Our job was to help a local school repair its buildings and to teach the children English.\n\nThe **voyage** to the island took two days by boat. When we finally arrived, the captain stood beside us and pointed to the smoking peak. \"Don't **wander** too far up the mountain,\" he said. \"I must **warn** you that the ground can be very hot.\"\n\nThe island has a long and sad history. During the **war**, many families fled, and the population is still small. Electricity is limited: the school's generator produces only 220 **volts|volt**, and the **voltage** drops whenever too many machines are used at once. Each evening we had to reduce the **volume** of the radio to save power.\n\nDespite the hardships, the island was full of **warmth**. Every morning, the sound of roosters would **waken** us at dawn. In the afternoons we played **volleyball** on the beach with the children, and once a week a farmer arrived with a horse-drawn **wagon** full of vegetables; the children called it the \"**waggon**\", the old British spelling they had learned from a textbook.\n\nOne evening, the village held a meeting to **vote** on how to spend a small government grant. They chose to build a new library. To celebrate, the owner of the only restaurant invited us to dinner. The **waiter**, who was also his son, wore a red cloth around his **waist** and served us fresh fish. When I tried to pay, the owner refused to let me open my **wallet**. \"You came to help us,\" he said. \"Tonight, you are our guests.\""},"185":{"w":["waste","waterfall","waterproof","wave","wavelength","wax","weak","weaken","weakness","wealth","wealthy","weapon","weary","weave","wedding","weed","weekday","weekly","weep","weigh"],"title":"A Wedding by the Waterfall","t":"Last month, my cousin Lili held her **wedding** in a mountain village beside a famous **waterfall**. She chose a **weekday** instead of a weekend to avoid the crowds, and the weather forecast in the **weekly** newspaper promised sunshine. But on the morning of the wedding, dark clouds rolled in, and everyone hurried to find **waterproof** jackets.\n\nThe village is known for its handmade cloth, and the bride's dress was made by local women who **weave** silk by hand. Her grandmother, who once saved the family from poverty with her weaving business, sat in the front row. She is now **weak** with age, and illness has begun to **weaken** her body, but nothing could stop her from attending.\n\nLili and her husband come from very different backgrounds. His family is **wealthy**, while hers has always been poor, and some relatives worried that the difference in **wealth** would cause problems. But the couple share the same values and seem to be on the same **wavelength** in everything. \"His only **weakness**,\" Lili joked, \"is that he can't cook.\"\n\nBefore the ceremony, the guests helped to prepare the garden. Children pulled out every **weed**, and old men lit **wax** candles along the path. Nobody wanted to **waste** a moment of the beautiful day. When the rain stopped, the sun came out, and the guests gave a **wave** of joy.\n\nDuring the speeches, Lili's grandmother began to **weep**. \"Humour is the best **weapon** against hard times,\" she told the couple. \"Use it often.\" By evening the guests were **weary** but happy. As the party ended, Lili's father tried to **weigh** a huge bag of presents and laughed that it was heavier than the bride."},"186":{"w":["weld","welfare","well-known","westward","whale","whatever","whatsoever","wheat","wheel","whenever","whereas","wherever","whichever","whip","whirl","whisky","whisper","whistle","whitewash","whoever"],"title":"The Harbour Town","t":"On the east coast there is a small harbour town that was once **well-known** for whaling. Two hundred years ago, sailors left its port to hunt the **whale** in distant seas. Today the town has changed completely: hunting is banned, and tourists come to watch the giant animals swim safely past the coast.\n\nThe old harbour still has a few reminders of the past. A rusty iron **wheel** from a whaling ship stands in the square, and fishermen still **weld** metal parts in a workshop by the pier. Behind the town, fields of **wheat** stretch **westward** towards the hills, turning gold in late summer.\n\nThe town's history was not always pleasant. Old records show that some ship owners treated sailors cruelly, and captains sometimes used a **whip** to punish them. For years, local historians tried to **whitewash** these facts, but a young museum director decided to tell the full story. \"**Whatever** happened, people deserve the truth,\" she says. \"There is no reason **whatsoever** to hide it.\"\n\nToday, the town cares deeply about the **welfare** of its people and its environment. **Whenever** a whale is spotted, a **whistle** sounds from the lighthouse, and visitors rush to the shore. Children **whisper** excitedly as the animals rise from the water and the waves **whirl** around them.\n\nOpinions about tourism differ. Some residents welcome the visitors, **whereas** others complain about crowded streets. **Whoever** you ask, though, agrees that whale watching is better than whale hunting. In the evening, visitors gather in the old sailors' bar, where the owner serves local **whisky** and tells stories of the sea. **Wherever** you sit, you can hear the waves outside. Tourists can choose between two boat tours, and **whichever** they pick, they rarely leave disappointed."},"187":{"w":["whole","wholly","wicked","widely","widen","widespread","widow","width","wild","willing","win","wine","wing","winner","wipe","wire","wireless","wisdom","wise","wit"],"title":"The Wise Widow of the Hills","t":"In a village high in the hills lives an old **widow** named Mrs. Bai. She has lived alone since her husband died twenty years ago, but she is never lonely. Her small house sits among **wild** flowers, and birds with bright **wing** feathers visit her garden every morning. People from all over the region come to ask her for advice, because she is known for her **wisdom**.\n\nMrs. Bai has a sharp **wit** and a kind heart. When young people complain about their problems, she listens patiently, then offers a few **wise** words. \"Life is not about whether you **win** or lose,\" she often says. \"Even the **winner** of a race must eventually go home and cook dinner.\"\n\nHer reputation has spread **widely**. A few years ago, a journalist wrote about her, and the story became **widespread** on the internet. Now visitors arrive from distant cities. The road to her village has been **widened|widen**, though its **width** is still barely enough for two cars to pass.\n\nMrs. Bai makes her own fruit **wine** and shares it with guests. She is always **willing** to help others, whether by teaching children to read or by repairing a neighbour's broken radio. She learned to fix electrical **wire** connections from her husband, who was an engineer. She laughs at **wireless** technology. \"In my day,\" she says, \"we had to climb a hill to hear the radio.\"\n\nShe is not **wholly** serious, however. She enjoys telling **wicked** jokes, and the children love her stories about naughty foxes and clever rabbits. Before every story, she would **wipe** her glasses and smile. \"The **whole** world is in these stories,\" she says, \"if you know how to listen.\""},"188":{"w":["withdraw","within","withstand","witness","wolf","wonder","wonderful","wood","wooden","wool","woollen","workman","workshop","worldwide","worm","worse","worship","worst","worthless","worthwhile"],"title":"The Woodworking Workshop","t":"In an old factory building on the edge of town, a carpenter named Mr. Tian runs a small **workshop** where young people learn traditional skills. The building is old but strong; it has been able to **withstand** fifty years of wind and rain. Inside, the air smells of fresh **wood** and polish.\n\nMr. Tian was once a **workman** in a large furniture factory. When the factory closed, he decided to **withdraw** his savings and open the workshop. Many friends said it would be **worthless** to teach old crafts in the age of machines. \"They thought I was mad,\" he says. \"But I knew it would be **worthwhile**.\"\n\nHis students make **wooden** chairs, tables and toys. **Within** a few months, beginners can build a simple stool, and the best students produce furniture that sells **worldwide** through online shops. Mr. Tian also runs evening classes in which older women knit **woollen** scarves using **wool** from local sheep.\n\nThe workshop has faced difficult times. The **worst** moment came three years ago, when a fire destroyed part of the roof. Things got **worse** when a storm flooded the building a week later. Mr. Tian was a **witness** to the whole disaster, standing helplessly in the rain. \"I **wonder** how we survived,\" he says.\n\nBut the community rallied around him. Neighbours brought tools, and a local temple where people **worship** donated money for repairs. Today the workshop is busier than ever. Mr. Tian warns his students to check every piece of timber for a **worm** hole before using it. He also likes to joke that a hungry student can **wolf** down a whole lunch in five minutes. \"Making something with your hands is **wonderful**,\" he says. \"It feeds the soul.\""},"189":{"w":["worthy","wound","wrap","wreath","wreck","wreckage","wrist","writer","writing","x-ray","yard","yawn","yearly","yell","yield","youngster","youth","youthful","zeal","zealous"],"title":"Youth and Zeal","t":"Every summer, a group of **zealous** volunteers runs a free camp for village children in our county. The camp was started by a retired **writer**, Mr. Su, who believes that **youth** is the best time to discover a love of reading and **writing**. \"Young people have so much **zeal**,\" he says. \"We just need to give them the right direction.\"\n\nThe camp takes place in the **yard** of an old school, surrounded by trees. Every morning begins with games, and the noise is incredible; children **yell**, laugh and run in all directions. By afternoon, many of them **yawn** in the heat, so the teachers move the reading lessons into the shade.\n\nMr. Su treats every **youngster** with respect. He says that each child's story is **worthy** of being heard. One girl wrote about the **wreck** of her father's fishing boat during a storm. She described the **wreckage** floating on the water and the **wound** on her father's arm, which needed an **x-ray** at the county hospital. The story moved everyone to tears.\n\nAt the end of each camp, the children **wrap** small gifts for their parents and hang a **wreath** of flowers at the school gate. The camp has become a **yearly** tradition, and former campers often return as volunteers. \"Hard work will always **yield** good results,\" Mr. Su tells them.\n\nAt seventy, he is still full of **youthful** energy. He wears a simple watch on his **wrist** and checks it constantly, worried about wasting time. \"Life is short,\" he says, \"so read as much as you can.\""},"190":{"w":["zebra","zone"],"title":"The Zebra Crossing","t":"Every morning, an old man in a bright orange jacket stands at the **zebra** crossing outside our primary school. He has helped children cross the road safely for fifteen years. The area around the school is a special **zone** where cars must drive slowly, but some drivers still ignore the rules.\n\nHe knows every child by name. He holds up his sign, waits for the traffic to stop and then waves the children across the black-and-white stripes. Parents say he is the most important person in the neighbourhood. When asked why he still works at the age of seventy-five, he simply smiles. \"Seeing them arrive safely,\" he says, \"is the best way to start my day.\""}},"freq.cet4":{"1":{"w":["human","research","social","researcher","might","less","increase","experience","part","product","author","level","likely","goal","state","university","public","focus","benefit","brain"],"title":"Why Friendship Is Good for the Brain","t":"For decades, scientists have studied what keeps the **human** **brain** healthy. Much of this **research** has focused on diet and exercise, but a growing body of evidence suggests that **social** connections **might** be just as important.\n\nA team at a leading **university** recently followed two thousand adults over twenty years. The lead **researcher**, a psychologist and the **author** of several popular books, found that people with close friendships were **less** **likely** to suffer memory loss in old age. Those who felt lonely, by contrast, performed at a lower **level** on memory tests.\n\nWhy should friendship matter? One explanation is that conversation and shared **experience** challenge the brain, forcing it to stay active. Another is that friends reduce stress, which can damage brain cells. Whatever the reason, the **benefit** appears to be real.\n\nThe findings have implications for **public** policy. In many countries, the number of people living alone continues to **increase**. Some governments now **state** openly that loneliness is a health problem, and they have set a **goal** of reducing it. Community centres, volunteer programmes and even a new **product** — apps that connect older people with neighbours — are **part** of the response.\n\nThe researchers hope their work will shift the **focus** of health advice. \"We tell people to eat well and exercise,\" the author says. \"We should also tell them to call a friend.\""},"2":{"w":["self","create","employee","result","technology","behavior","government","fact","media","cause","tend","lead","ability","impact","include","provide","education","effect","sense","actually"],"title":"Technology at Work","t":"Modern **technology** has changed the way people work, but its **impact** on **employees|employee** is not always positive. A recent study by a **government** research centre found that office workers check their emails more than seventy times a day. As a **result**, many feel constantly distracted and tired.\n\nThe study's authors argue that technology **tends|tend** to **create** new demands. Smartphones and laptops **provide** flexibility, but they also blur the line between work and home. In **fact**, many employees reported answering messages late at night, which can **lead** to poor sleep and stress.\n\nSocial **media** is another **cause** of distraction. Workers who spend time online during the day often feel less satisfied with their jobs. The **effect** is especially strong among young people, whose **sense** of **self** is closely linked to their online image.\n\nYet technology is not the enemy. Used wisely, it can improve our **ability** to learn and communicate. The researchers recommend that companies **include** digital skills in workplace **education** and encourage healthy **behavior**, such as turning off notifications after work. \"People think they are in control of their devices,\" one expert said. \"But **actually**, the devices often control them.\""},"3":{"w":["area","economic","community","however","improve","value","process","recent","skill","memory","base","scientist","share","team","happen","rather","art","society","personal","learning"],"title":"Learning in the Community","t":"**However** advanced our schools become, much important **learning** still **happens|happen** outside the classroom. A **recent** study by social **scientists|scientist** found that young people who take part in **community** activities develop **skills|skill** that school alone cannot teach.\n\nThe study was **based|base** on interviews with three thousand teenagers across the country. Those who joined local sports **teams|team**, volunteered or took part in **art** projects reported greater confidence and better relationships. Many said they had learned to **share** responsibilities and solve problems together.\n\nResearchers believe that this **process** works because community activities connect learning with real life. When students help clean a river or organise a festival, they see the **value** of their work immediately. The experience also stays in their **memory** far longer than a textbook lesson.\n\nThere is an **economic** argument too. Employers increasingly look for teamwork and communication, not just exam results. Communities that invest in youth programmes may therefore **improve** local job prospects. Some **areas|area** have already begun to fund such programmes through local businesses.\n\nOf course, not all students have equal access to these opportunities. Children from poorer families often have **rather** fewer choices. Experts argue that **society** must make community learning available to everyone, because its **personal** and social benefits are too important to ignore."},"4":{"w":["culture","business","consider","decision","allow","attention","space","lose","resource","system","power","finding","avoid","local","relationship","means","individual","interest","belief","risk"],"title":"The Risks of Living Online","t":"In just two decades, the internet has transformed our **culture**, our **business** and our **relationships|relationship**. Most of us now spend several hours a day online, and many young people **consider** their phones essential. But researchers warn that this new way of living carries hidden **risks|risk**.\n\nOne recent **finding** is that constant online activity weakens our **attention**. When we switch quickly between apps, the brain has little time to think deeply. Another concern is privacy: every click provides data that companies use to influence our **decisions|decision**. Few people fully understand how much **power** this gives large technology firms.\n\nThe problem is not technology itself but the **system** that surrounds it. Many platforms are designed to keep users online for as long as possible. Their business model depends on our time and **interest**. In this way, our attention has become a valuable **resource**, much like oil or water.\n\nWhat can an **individual** do? Experts suggest simple steps: **avoid** using phones before bed, turn off notifications and create phone-free **space** at home. **Local** schools and libraries can also teach digital skills. These are not complicated **means**, but they help us keep control.\n\nMany people hold the **belief** that they can multitask without any cost. Research suggests otherwise. If we **allow** technology to shape every moment, we may **lose** something important: the ability to be fully present."},"5":{"w":["tax","global","test","factor","program","environment","control","growth","rise","view","data","effort","history","point","term","common","climate","consumer","speaker","positive"],"title":"Climate and the Consumer","t":"Climate change is one of the most serious problems of our time. Scientists warn that global temperatures continue to **rise**, and the **data** show clearly that human activity is a major **factor**. Yet many people still feel that the problem is too large for them to **control**.\n\nEconomists take a different **view**. They argue that **consumer** choices can drive real change. When enough people buy energy-efficient products, companies have an incentive to invest in cleaner technology. Some governments support this **effort** by introducing a carbon **tax**, which makes polluting products more expensive.\n\nHistory offers useful lessons. In the past, public pressure helped ban harmful chemicals and reduce air pollution in many cities. The **history** of these campaigns suggests that change is possible when citizens, businesses and governments work together.\n\nNot everyone agrees on the best approach. One speaker at a recent conference argued that **growth** and environmental protection are compatible. Another **speaker** disagreed, saying that rich countries must consume less. The key **point**, both agreed, is that the **global** **climate** affects everyone.\n\nMany schools now include environmental education in their **program**, and some **test** students on their knowledge of sustainable living. In the long **term**, experts believe, these lessons will help create a more **positive** relationship between people and the natural **environment**. It is now **common** to see children reminding their parents to recycle."},"6":{"w":["industry","rate","choice","screen","design","offer","opportunity","living","academic","natural","population","quality","claim","challenge","issue","require","project","expert","support","crop"],"title":"Feeding a Growing World","t":"By 2050, the world's **population** is expected to reach almost ten billion. Feeding so many people will be one of the greatest **challenges|challenge** of the century. Farmers must grow more food on less land, while protecting the **natural** environment.\n\nMany **experts|expert** **claim** that technology **offers|offer** the best **opportunity** to solve this **issue**. Scientists have developed new **crop** varieties that grow faster and resist disease. Some farms now use drones and computer **screens|screen** to monitor soil and water, and an **academic** study found that such methods can increase harvests at a remarkable **rate**.\n\nBut technology alone is not enough. Large-scale farming often **requires|require** heavy use of chemicals, which can damage soil **quality** and water supplies. The food **industry** also wastes huge amounts of food every year. Critics argue that we need to **design** smarter systems, not just bigger ones.\n\nConsumers have a role too. Every food **choice** we make sends a message to producers. Choosing local, seasonal produce can **support** small farmers and reduce transport emissions. In some cities, community **projects|project** teach residents to grow vegetables on rooftops.\n\nThe future of food is uncertain, but one thing is clear: making a **living** from farming must become fairer and more sustainable. As one expert put it, \"Feeding the world is not only a technical problem. It is a moral one.\""},"7":{"w":["real","promote","towards","achieve","average","difference","income","habit","energy","reach","understanding","condition","activity","affect","century","continue","negative","success","kid","advice"],"title":"The Power of Small Habits","t":"Why do some people **achieve** their goals while others give up? According to psychologists, the **difference** often lies in small daily **habits|habit**. A **real** change in life, they argue, rarely comes from one big decision; it comes from many small actions repeated over time.\n\nConsider exercise. Research shows that the **average** person who sets a vague goal, such as \"get fit,\" is unlikely to **reach** it. But people who commit to a specific **activity** — a twenty-minute walk after dinner, for example — are much more likely to **continue**. Over months, their **energy** levels rise and their health improves.\n\nHabits also **affect** our finances. People who save a small part of their **income** every month build wealth more steadily than those who wait for a large bonus. Financial advisers often give the same **advice**: start small and be consistent.\n\nOf course, bad habits can have a **negative** effect too. Checking social media late at night, for instance, reduces sleep and makes it harder to focus the next day. A better **understanding** of how habits form can help people **promote** healthy routines and break harmful ones.\n\nEven a **kid** can learn this lesson. Parents who encourage children to read for ten minutes each day help them develop a positive attitude **towards** learning. As scientists of the past **century** discovered, **success** depends less on talent than on the right **conditions|condition** for growth."},"8":{"w":["concern","course","later","appear","reduce","feeling","situation","career","role","follow","stress","force","task","prevent","seek","adult","emotion","measure","model","publish"],"title":"Stress at Work","t":"In many countries, workplace **stress** has become a major health **concern**. A recent survey found that more than half of **adult** workers feel anxious about their jobs at least once a week. The **situation** is especially serious among young employees at the start of their **careers|career**.\n\nResearchers have tried to **measure** the causes. Long hours, unclear **tasks|task** and pressure from managers are common factors. Many workers also say they feel **forced|force** to stay online after work, answering emails late at night. Over time, this can **reduce** both productivity and well-being.\n\nStress is not only a physical problem but also an emotional one. Workers who feel unappreciated often describe a **feeling** of helplessness, and some begin to avoid colleagues. Psychologists say that ignoring these signs can **prevent** people from seeking help until it is too late. Recognising each **emotion** is the first step towards managing it.\n\nSome companies have begun to take action. They offer training **courses|course** on stress management and encourage employees to **seek** support. One technology firm has developed a new **model** of flexible working that lets staff choose their own hours. Its managers **follow** a simple rule: no emails after seven o'clock.\n\nThe results **appear** promising. A study **published|publish** **later** that year found that employees at such companies took fewer sick days. Managers play a key **role** in this change, experts say, because workers often copy the behaviour of their leaders."},"9":{"w":["access","event","species","argue","decade","encourage","economy","communication","nature","conflict","traffic","alone","influence","performance","degree","digital","increasingly","produce","add","gain"],"title":"Life in the Digital Age","t":"Over the past **decade**, **digital** technology has transformed nearly every part of daily life. People are **increasingly** connected through phones and social networks, and **access** to information has never been easier. Yet experts **argue** that these changes bring both opportunities and risks.\n\nOn the positive side, digital tools have made **communication** faster and cheaper. Families separated by distance can talk face to face, and small businesses can reach customers around the world. Some economists believe that the digital **economy** will continue to **gain** importance and **produce** millions of new jobs.\n\nAt the same time, technology can have a strong **influence** on our behaviour. Many people feel anxious when they are **alone** without their phones. Studies show that constant online activity can harm sleep and reduce **performance** at work or school. In some cases, online disagreements even lead to real **conflict** between friends.\n\nTechnology also affects the natural world. Scientists use digital sensors to track endangered **species** and monitor air **traffic** over protected areas. Such data **encourage** better decisions about how to protect **nature**.\n\nThe key, experts say, is balance. Technology is a tool, and the **degree** to which it helps or harms depends on how we use it. A major technology **event** in Beijing last year focused on this question. One speaker **added|add** that the best innovations are those that make us more human, not less."},"10":{"w":["against","exist","material","percent","act","especially","within","compare","explain","due","effective","evidence","further","financial","professor","region","training","mental","physical","policy"],"title":"Mind and Body","t":"For centuries, doctors treated the mind and the body as separate. **Physical** health was the concern of medicine, while **mental** health was often ignored. Today, a growing body of **evidence** suggests that the two are closely linked.\n\nA **professor** of psychology at a leading university recently led a study comparing people who exercise regularly with those who rarely do. The results showed that regular exercisers were thirty **percent** less likely to suffer from depression. \"Exercise is one of the most **effective** treatments for mild anxiety,\" she explained.\n\nResearchers are now trying to **explain** why. One theory is that exercise reduces stress hormones and improves sleep. Another is that physical activity helps people feel more in control of their lives. Whatever the reason, the benefits seem to **exist** across different ages and cultures.\n\nThese findings have important implications for public **policy**. In some **regions|region**, doctors now prescribe exercise **training** programmes instead of medicine. Governments are also considering **financial** support for community sports centres, **especially** in poorer areas. Such programmes must be designed carefully, **due** to differences in people's health and fitness.\n\n**Further** research is needed, of course. Scientists want to **compare** different types of exercise and study the **material** conditions that make healthy living possible. But the message is already clear: we must **act** now, **within** our own daily routines. The fight **against** mental illness may begin with a simple walk."},"11":{"w":["scientific","journal","passion","patient","identify","certain","survey","gas","background","beyond","importance","organization","pattern","respect","artist","involve","account","image","topic","contribute"],"title":"The Science of Passion","t":"Why do some people pursue a hobby with lifelong **passion**, while others lose interest quickly? A recent **survey** published in a **scientific** **journal** tried to answer this question. Researchers interviewed thousands of people, from office workers to a famous **artist**, about the activities they love most.\n\nThe study **identified|identify** a clear **pattern**. People who stayed passionate about an activity usually felt that it **involved|involve** growth and challenge. They also believed that their efforts **contributed|contribute** to something larger than themselves. A painter, for example, might see her work as part of a cultural tradition, not just a personal **image** of beauty.\n\n**Background** also mattered. Many participants said that a parent or teacher had shown **respect** for their interests when they were young. This support gave them confidence to continue **beyond** childhood. The researchers stress the **importance** of encouragement, especially in the early stages of learning.\n\nInterestingly, passion is not always positive. In **certain** cases, people became so focused on one activity that they ignored health or relationships. One **patient** described working on his hobby until he was exhausted. Experts say a healthy balance is essential.\n\nThe findings have practical uses. An **organization** that supports young people now uses the research to design its programmes. When choosing a **topic** for a new class, it takes **account** of students' interests. As one researcher put it, \"Passion is like **gas** in a car: it keeps you moving, but you must still steer.\""},"12":{"w":["despite","achievement","choose","professional","tiny","tool","fountain","action","consequence","demand","fear","reflect","shift","top","anxiety","nation","position","engage","least","realize"],"title":"Living with Anxiety","t":"**Anxiety** has become one of the most common health problems among young people. Surveys across the **nation** suggest that students feel more pressure than ever before. They **fear** failure, worry about their future **position** in society and feel constant **demand** to succeed.\n\nPsychologists say this trend **reflects|reflect** a broader **shift** in modern life. Social media encourages people to compare themselves with others who seem to be at the **top** of their field. Even a **tiny** mistake can feel like a disaster. The **consequence** is a culture of stress, where many students rarely feel satisfied with their **achievement**.\n\n**Despite** these challenges, there are effective ways to cope. **Professional** counsellors recommend practical **tools|tool**, such as breathing exercises, regular sleep and time away from screens. Taking **action** early is important: those who seek help at the first signs of anxiety recover more quickly.\n\nSchools also have a role. Some universities now **engage** students in group discussions about mental health, helping them **realize** that they are not alone. Others have created quiet spaces on campus, such as gardens with a **fountain**, where students can rest.\n\nExperts advise that we should **choose** our goals carefully. Instead of trying to be perfect, we should focus on progress. \"At **least** once a day,\" one counsellor suggests, \"do something just because you enjoy it.\""},"13":{"w":["connect","various","color","creative","explore","variety","although","prove","reading","connection","deal","determine","knowledge","lack","manage","internet","wage","introduce","solution","shape"],"title":"The Creative Mind","t":"What makes a person **creative**? For a long time, people believed creativity was a gift that only artists possessed. Today, research suggests that it is a skill anyone can develop. **Although** some people are naturally more imaginative, most of us can learn to think in new ways.\n\nStudies **prove** that creativity depends on **knowledge** and experience. The more we **explore** **various** subjects, the more ideas we can **connect**. A designer who reads about biology, for example, might find a new **solution** to an engineering problem. Scientists call this the **connection** of distant ideas.\n\nDaily habits also **shape** creativity. **Reading** widely, travelling and meeting different people expose us to a **variety** of perspectives. Even the **color** of a room can affect our thinking: one study found that blue walls encourage imagination.\n\nHowever, many people **lack** the time or freedom to be creative. Workers who earn a low **wage** may need several jobs to survive, leaving little energy for new ideas. Companies that want creative employees must learn to **manage** workloads fairly.\n\nThe **internet** has made it easier to share ideas, and many schools now **introduce** creative projects into their lessons. Teachers say that such projects help students **deal** with complex problems. In the end, our curiosity, not our talent, may **determine** how creative we become."},"14":{"w":["strategy","whole","crisis","personality","collect","perform","potential","intelligence","maintain","progress","reality","daily","death","cell","happiness","medical","private","recently","resident","argument"],"title":"The Science of Happiness","t":"What is the secret of **happiness**? Scientists have studied this question for decades, and their findings often challenge common beliefs. Money, for example, matters less than most people imagine. Once basic needs are met, **daily** relationships and a sense of purpose have a far greater effect.\n\nOne long-term study followed hundreds of people from youth until **death**. Researchers found that those with strong friendships lived longer and healthier lives. Their **medical** records showed fewer heart problems, and even their brain **cells|cell** appeared to age more slowly.\n\n**Personality** plays a role too. People who are optimistic tend to recover faster from a **crisis**, such as losing a job. But happiness is not fixed. Psychologists have developed practical **strategies|strategy** to **maintain** a positive mood: writing a gratitude journal, helping others and spending time in nature.\n\nSome experts warn against confusing happiness with constant pleasure. \"Real happiness includes sadness and challenge,\" one researcher argues. Her **argument** is based on interviews with people who had faced difficult **reality** but still described their lives as meaningful.\n\nGovernments have **recently** begun to **collect** data on happiness as well as income. In some cities, every **resident** is asked to rate their well-being once a year. Officials hope to measure **progress** not only in economic terms. A **private** company in Denmark even uses artificial **intelligence** to analyse what makes workers **perform** best. The **whole** field, experts say, has great **potential** to improve lives."},"15":{"w":["decline","earn","successful","type","apply","enable","phone","beauty","island","rural","absence","robot","imagine","recognize","accept","audience","interaction","rely","current","responsibility"],"title":"Robots and Rural Life","t":"In many **rural** areas, young people are leaving for the cities, and farmers are growing older. The **absence** of workers has caused farm production to **decline** in some regions. Now, a surprising helper has appeared: the **robot**.\n\nOn a small **island** in southern Japan, farmers have begun to **rely** on machines to plant rice and pick fruit. These robots **enable** elderly farmers to continue working and to **earn** a living. One farmer said he could hardly **imagine** life without them.\n\nThe technology is not perfect. Some robots cannot **recognize** ripe fruit, and others break down in bad weather. The **current** models are also expensive. Yet engineers believe that costs will fall as technology improves. A **successful** project in one village has already attracted interest from farmers across the region.\n\nNot everyone is ready to **accept** this change. Some worry that robots will reduce human **interaction** and destroy traditional farming culture. Others argue that it is society's **responsibility** to support farmers in new ways, rather than leaving them alone.\n\nThe village has even become a tourist attraction. Visitors film the robots on their **phones|phone**, and a local television programme about them attracted a large **audience**. Many people say they want to **apply** similar ideas in their own communities. Whatever **type** of future we choose, the **beauty** of rural life is worth protecting."},"16":{"w":["amount","desire","disease","code","teaching","elderly","stranger","depend","source","major","colleague","gap","safe","step","supply","unite","writing","law","taste","attract"],"title":"Caring for an Ageing Society","t":"As populations grow older, caring for **elderly** people has become a **major** challenge in many countries. A large **amount** of public money is spent on hospitals and care homes, yet there is still a **gap** between what older people need and what is available.\n\nOne problem is the **supply** of carers. Nursing is hard work, and many young people choose other careers. In some places, families **depend** on paid helpers who are almost **strangers|stranger** to them. Experts warn that loneliness can be as dangerous to health as **disease**.\n\nSome communities have found creative solutions. In Japan, schools and care homes share buildings, so children and older people meet every day. In the Netherlands, university students live in care homes for free, in exchange for spending time with residents. Such projects **unite** different generations and **attract** wide public interest.\n\nTechnology is another **source** of help. Simple devices can remind people to take medicine, and a door **code** can keep residents **safe** without locking them in. But researchers stress that machines cannot replace human warmth.\n\nGovernments are taking **steps|step** too. A new **law** in several countries gives workers paid leave to care for parents. Universities have improved the **teaching** of care skills, and many nurses now study creative **writing** and music to make their work more personal. One **colleague** of mine, a nurse, says older people share the same **desire** as everyone else: to be respected. \"They don't want pity,\" she says. \"They want to **taste** life fully until the end.\""},"17":{"w":["associate","ensure","struggle","link","majority","national","popular","pursue","throughout","rule","thought","significant","consumption","probably","prepare","protect","traditional","management","forest","automation"],"title":"Protecting the Forest","t":"**Throughout** history, **forests|forest** have provided food, shelter and medicine. Yet in the past century, the **majority** of the world's ancient forests have been cut down. Scientists warn that this loss has **significant** effects on climate and wildlife.\n\nThe main cause is human **consumption**. Rising demand for wood, paper and farmland has pushed companies to clear huge areas. In many countries, **national** governments have struggled to enforce the **rule** of law in remote regions. Some forest communities **struggle** to protect their land against powerful businesses.\n\n**Traditional** knowledge can help. Indigenous people often **associate** forests with their history and identity, and they have managed them carefully for generations. Researchers have found a strong **link** between local ownership and healthy forests. When communities control the land, they usually **protect** it.\n\nTechnology is also changing forest **management**. **Automation** allows satellites and drones to watch forests around the clock, sending alerts when illegal logging begins. Such tools help to **ensure** that rules are followed.\n\nIndividuals can also make a difference. Choosing recycled paper is a **popular** way to reduce demand. Environmental groups encourage citizens to **pursue** greener lifestyles and **prepare** for a future with fewer resources. As one activist put it, \"We **probably** won't save every tree, but every **thought** about what we buy matters.\""},"18":{"w":["regard","win","approach","chance","general","stick","complex","conduct","damage","feature","mostly","emotional","purchase","relevant","graduate","leader","greenhouse","interview","trade","complete"],"title":"What Makes a Good Leader?","t":"What qualities make a successful **leader**? Business schools have studied this question for decades. Many researchers **conduct** interviews with company managers, while others **regard** history as the best teacher. The results suggest that leadership is more **complex** than people once believed.\n\nIn **general**, good leaders share several **features|feature**. They communicate clearly, listen carefully and stay calm under pressure. They also show **emotional** intelligence: the ability to understand their own feelings and those of others. Employees say that leaders who care about them are **mostly** more effective than those who focus only on profit.\n\nA modern **approach** to leadership emphasises teamwork. Instead of giving orders, leaders encourage staff to share ideas and **complete** projects together. When problems occur, they take responsibility rather than blaming others. Poor leadership can **damage** a company's reputation and drive talented workers away.\n\nYoung graduates often ask how they can become leaders. Experts advise them to **stick** to their values and look for every **chance** to learn. A recent **graduate** who now runs a small company told an **interview** panel that his first job, in a **greenhouse** growing vegetables, taught him patience. \"You can't **win** if you don't understand the people around you,\" he said.\n\nLeadership skills are **relevant** in every field, from global **trade** to small family businesses. Even the decision to **purchase** a new machine involves judgement, communication and trust."},"19":{"w":["international","pressure","specific","basic","serious","message","experiment","principle","temperature","brand","cathedral","boost","describe","generally","invest","particularly","perhaps","content","main","solve"],"title":"Testing a Theory","t":"Scientific progress depends on a simple **principle**: every theory must be tested. When scientists **describe** the natural world, they must be able to prove their ideas through careful **experiment**. This **basic** rule has guided research for centuries.\n\nConsider a recent study on climate. Researchers wanted to know how rising **temperature** affects old stone buildings. They studied a famous **cathedral** in northern Europe, measuring the **pressure** of heat and moisture on its walls. Their findings were **serious**: warmer weather was damaging the stone faster than expected.\n\nThe **main** lesson of the study was that ancient buildings need **specific** protection. Experts **generally** agree that monitoring and early repairs are the best way to **solve** the problem. Some governments now **invest** in new technology to protect historic sites.\n\nThe study also attracted **international** attention. Its **message** was shared widely on social media, and some companies used the topic to **boost** their **brand** image. Critics warned that such marketing can oversimplify scientific **content**.\n\n**Perhaps** the most important point is that science is a process, not a set of final answers. New evidence can change our understanding, **particularly** when technology allows more accurate measurement. The scientists who studied the cathedral plan to continue their work for another ten years."},"20":{"w":["easily","appeal","concept","identity","chemical","female","reward","institution","coin","address","central","available","discover","unique","exactly","response","harm","observe","overall","communicate"],"title":"The Meaning of Money","t":"Money is a **central** part of modern life, but it is also a powerful idea. Historians explain that the first **coins|coin** appeared around 2,600 years ago, and merchants tested their **chemical** purity by weight. Since then, the **concept** of money has changed **exactly** as quickly as society itself.\n\nToday, money shapes our sense of **identity**. Some people judge others by their income, and advertisements constantly **appeal** to our desire for wealth. Researchers **observe** that people who focus only on money often feel less satisfied with their lives.\n\nPsychologists have tried to **discover** why. One theory is that money works as a **reward**, giving short-term pleasure. But when people compare themselves with others, this pleasure quickly fades. The **overall** effect can even **harm** mental health.\n\nFinancial **institutions|institution** also influence how we think about money. Banks make credit **easily** **available**, encouraging people to spend more than they earn. In **response**, some schools now teach financial skills so that students can **communicate** about money and make wise choices.\n\nThere are also differences between groups. Studies show that **female** workers still earn less than men in many industries, and campaigns aim to **address** this unfairness. Each country has a **unique** history with money, but the lesson is universal: wealth is useful, but it cannot buy everything."},"21":{"w":["property","loss","trend","candidate","employer","aim","thus","essential","ignore","novel","particular","treat","balance","completely","function","limited","being","diet","special","standard"],"title":"Getting the First Job","t":"For many young people, finding a first job is one of the most stressful experiences of their lives. Every year, millions of **candidates|candidate** compete for a **limited** number of positions. **Employers|employer**, meanwhile, complain that graduates lack practical skills.\n\nRecruitment experts say that the **trend** is changing. Many companies now **aim** to hire people with strong communication and teamwork, not just high grades. A degree is still **essential**, but it is no longer enough. Candidates who **ignore** this shift may find themselves at a disadvantage.\n\nWhat do employers look for in **particular**? Surveys suggest that flexibility, honesty and the ability to learn are high on the list. Employers also **treat** volunteer work and internships as evidence of maturity. A student who has organised a charity event or written a **novel**, for example, shows creativity and determination.\n\nExperts also warn against overworking. Young employees often try to impress their managers by working long hours, but a healthy work-life **balance** is important. Poor **diet**, lack of sleep and stress can lead to a **loss** of energy and health. \"You are a human **being**, not a machine,\" one career adviser reminds students.\n\nFinally, preparation makes a **special** difference. Candidates should research the company, understand its **function** in the market and practise answering questions. Some even study the company's **property** and investments. Such efforts meet a high **standard** and may **completely** change an interview's result. **Thus**, the job search becomes not only a test but a learning experience."},"22":{"w":["production","farmer","spider","aware","occur","reveal","advantage","aspect","period","prefer","customer","investment","option","remove","scale","trust","dream","lower","style","user"],"title":"Farming in the Digital Age","t":"For thousands of years, **farmers|farmer** relied on experience and weather to decide when to plant and harvest. Today, digital technology is changing agricultural **production** in remarkable ways. Sensors, drones and apps allow farmers to monitor crops on a large **scale**.\n\nThe **advantage** of these tools is clear. Data can **reveal** problems before they **occur**, such as a lack of water or the presence of insects. Farmers can then **remove** harmful pests without spraying the whole field. This approach can **lower** costs and reduce pollution.\n\nNot every aspect of the change is positive, however. Some farmers are not **aware** of the risks of sharing data with large technology companies. Others find the new systems difficult to use. Experts say that companies must design products with the **user** in mind and earn farmers' **trust**.\n\nConsumers also play a role. Many **customers|customer** now **prefer** food produced in an environmentally friendly **style**. Supermarkets offer more organic **options|option**, and some farms invite visitors to see how food is grown. Every **aspect** of the supply chain is becoming more transparent.\n\nThe **period** ahead will require significant **investment** in training and equipment. Young farmers, in particular, **dream** of combining tradition with technology. One young woman in Yunnan uses a drone to protect her tea fields from a harmful **spider** mite. \"My grandparents taught me patience,\" she says. \"Technology gives me speed.\""},"23":{"w":["competition","destination","vacation","recommend","respond","save","track","worse","demonstrate","enhance","owner","senior","burn","motivate","staff","flood","anyone","debate","establish","generation"],"title":"Choosing a Holiday Destination","t":"Choosing a **vacation** **destination** has become more complicated than ever. Travellers can now **track** prices online, read thousands of reviews and compare options in seconds. Yet many people say that too much choice makes decisions **worse**, not better.\n\nTravel experts **recommend** a simple approach. First, decide what you want: relaxation, adventure or culture. Then set a budget. Planning ahead can **save** money, as prices tend to rise closer to the travel date. Many travel companies now **respond** to customer feedback quickly, adjusting offers to stay ahead of the **competition**.\n\nTourism also raises serious questions. In some popular places, the number of visitors has caused environmental damage. Forest fires **burn** more often in dry regions, and a **flood** of tourists can overwhelm small towns. There is an ongoing **debate** about how to **establish** sustainable tourism.\n\nSome local business **owners|owner** have found creative solutions. They limit visitor numbers and **demonstrate** traditional crafts to **enhance** the cultural experience. In one mountain village, **senior** residents teach visitors to cook local dishes, sharing knowledge with a new **generation**. Hotel **staff** are encouraged to recommend quieter places to **anyone** looking for peace.\n\nUltimately, travel can **motivate** us to understand the world better. The best holidays, many people say, are not the most expensive ones, but those that leave us with new perspectives."},"24":{"w":["powerful","record","repeat","wealth","purpose","truth","board","citizen","fashion","remote","electronic","figure","addition","cultivate","independent","root","safety","voice","comment","suffer"],"title":"Voices from Remote Villages","t":"In **remote** villages, many people still lack access to clean water, healthcare and the internet. For decades, their **voices|voice** were rarely heard in national debates. Today, **electronic** devices and social media are beginning to change this.\n\nA **powerful** example comes from a mountain village in Guizhou. A young teacher began to **record** short videos about daily life there and post them online. In **addition** to showing beautiful scenery, the videos revealed the **truth** about difficult conditions: children walking hours to school, elderly people who **suffer** without medical care.\n\nThe videos quickly attracted attention. Thousands of viewers left a **comment** asking how they could help. A charity **board** decided to fund a new school, and local officials promised to improve road **safety**. The teacher became a public **figure**, but she insisted that her **purpose** was simple: to give villagers a voice.\n\nExperts say such stories show the potential of digital media to **cultivate** active **citizens|citizen**. When people can share their experiences, they become more **independent** and confident. However, critics warn that online attention can also be shallow and short-lived; the same problems may **repeat** if real change does not take **root**.\n\nSome worry that rural life is becoming a **fashion** online, attracting viewers without creating real **wealth** for villagers. The teacher agrees that attention alone is not enough. \"Videos open the door,\" she says. \"But people must walk through it.\""},"25":{"w":["oil","profit","speed","theory","billion","monitor","debt","plastic","signal","classical","widely","characteristic","clearly","conclusion","operate","previous","sign","succeed","define","divide"],"title":"The Price of Plastic","t":"Since the 1950s, the world has produced more than eight **billion** tonnes of **plastic**. It is cheap, light and useful, which is why it is used so **widely**. But scientists have now **clearly** shown that plastic pollution is one of the greatest environmental threats of our time.\n\nThe problem has a long history. Plastic is made from **oil**, and its production has grown with the **speed** of economic development. Companies make huge **profits|profit** from plastic packaging, and consumers enjoy convenience. One **characteristic** of plastic, its durability, is also its biggest problem: it can last for hundreds of years.\n\nResearchers **monitor** plastic waste in rivers and oceans, and the data send a worrying **signal**. Tiny pieces of plastic have been found in fish, salt and even drinking water. A common **theory** suggests that these particles may harm human health, though more research is needed to reach a firm **conclusion**.\n\nSome countries have begun to act. **Previous** attempts to reduce plastic use often failed, but newer policies seem to **succeed**. Taxes on plastic bags, for example, have cut use dramatically. Factories that **operate** recycling systems receive government support, and some poorer countries receive help to reduce their **debt** in exchange for environmental action.\n\nExperts **define** the challenge as a shared responsibility. Governments, companies and individuals must **divide** the work fairly. Every **sign** of progress counts: one city orchestra now bans plastic bottles at its **classical** concerts, and many schools teach children to recycle from an early age."},"26":{"w":["urban","dangerous","handle","status","partner","satisfaction","instruction","label","gratitude","attempt","valuable","huge","method","total","advance","finance","oppose","structure","basis","express"],"title":"The Power of Gratitude","t":"In a busy **urban** life, it is easy to focus on what we lack rather than what we have. Yet psychologists say that **gratitude** — the habit of noticing and appreciating good things — can bring **huge** benefits. People who regularly **express** thanks report greater life **satisfaction** and better health.\n\nOne study asked participants to write down three things they were grateful for each evening. The **instruction** was simple, but the results were striking. After two months, the **total** level of stress among participants had fallen, while their sleep and mood had improved. Researchers concluded that gratitude provides a strong **basis** for well-being.\n\nWhy does this simple **method** work? One explanation is that gratitude changes the **structure** of our attention. Instead of noticing every problem, we begin to see **valuable** moments: a kind word from a **partner**, a beautiful sunset or a meal shared with friends. This shift helps people **handle** stress more calmly.\n\nNot everyone accepts these findings. Some critics **oppose** the idea that gratitude can solve serious problems. They warn that it is **dangerous** to tell people in poverty simply to \"be grateful,\" because it may ignore real injustice. Others say we should not **label** every positive emotion as a cure.\n\nStill, many experts believe gratitude is worth practising. Some companies now encourage employees to thank colleagues publicly, and **finance** departments even report fewer conflicts. In schools, teachers **attempt** to **advance** students' emotional skills through gratitude exercises. Regardless of social **status**, anyone can benefit from saying \"thank you\" a little more often."},"27":{"w":["adapt","line","original","score","spread","agriculture","budget","advertise","hire","object","rent","revolution","atmosphere","bacteria","drawing","livestock","direct","embrace","frequently","list"],"title":"A Farming Revolution","t":"Over the past century, **agriculture** has gone through a quiet **revolution**. New machines, chemicals and seeds have transformed the way farmers grow crops and raise **livestock**. In many countries, food production has increased dramatically, and fewer people go hungry.\n\nBut this progress has a cost. Large farms often depend on chemicals that damage soil and water. Some fertilisers release gases into the **atmosphere**, contributing to climate change. Overuse of medicine in animals has also helped dangerous **bacteria** **spread**, threatening human health.\n\nToday, many farmers are trying to **adapt**. They **embrace** new methods that protect the environment while maintaining production. Some **hire** experts to test soil quality; others **rent** small machines that use less fuel. Farmers **frequently** share ideas online, and some forums let users **score** each method out of ten.\n\nThe change also affects how food is sold. Supermarkets **advertise** \"organic\" and \"local\" products, and consumers are willing to pay more for them. Some farms sell **direct** to customers, who **line** up at weekend markets for fresh vegetables. Even so, many small farmers struggle to manage their **budget**.\n\nEducation is key. In one village, children made a **drawing** of their ideal farm, full of trees and animals. Their teacher displayed the pictures in a **list** of \"future farm ideas.\" The **original** goal was to teach environmental awareness, but the project became an **object** of national attention when a newspaper reported on it."},"28":{"w":["pretty","satisfy","vary","advocate","appreciate","article","assume","combine","detail","improvement","initiative","outcome","planet","remain","network","pollution","vision","waste","worldwide","firm"],"title":"Fighting Pollution Together","t":"**Pollution** is one of the greatest threats facing our **planet**. From plastic in the oceans to smoke in city air, its effects are felt **worldwide**. Scientists **assume** that the problem will get worse unless governments, companies and citizens **combine** their efforts.\n\nEncouragingly, there has been real **improvement** in some areas. Many cities have introduced cleaner buses and stricter rules on factories. In London, for example, a new **initiative** charges drivers of old cars a daily fee. The **outcome** has been a significant drop in air pollution levels.\n\nEnvironmental groups **advocate** even stronger action. They argue that we must reduce **waste**, especially single-use plastic, and invest in renewable energy. Some companies have responded positively. One technology **firm** has promised to run all its offices on solar power by 2030.\n\nIndividuals also play a part. Small changes, such as recycling or using public transport, may seem **pretty** minor, but together they make a difference. A recent **article** in a scientific journal showed that consumer choices **vary** widely between countries, and that education strongly influences behaviour.\n\nThe challenges **remain** enormous, but there is reason for hope. Global **networks|network** of scientists and activists now share data and ideas instantly. Their shared **vision** is of a cleaner, healthier world. As one activist put it, \"We must **appreciate** what nature gives us. It cannot **satisfy** our needs forever if we destroy it.\" Every **detail** of our daily lives, from what we eat to how we travel, affects the environment."},"29":{"w":["accident","luxury","pursuit","wedding","strengthen","encounter","generate","political","charge","expectation","highly","limit","adopt","recommendation","search","video","character","entire","extent","fight"],"title":"Weddings in Modern China","t":"In China, the **wedding** has long been one of the most important events in a family's life. In the past, ceremonies were often simple, but today many couples, **highly** influenced by social media, hold luxurious celebrations. Some families even borrow money to meet social **expectations|expectation**.\n\nSociologists say this trend reflects the **entire** country's rapid economic growth. As incomes rise, people **adopt** new lifestyles and **generate** new demands. **Luxury** weddings, complete with professional photographers and **video** crews, have become a symbol of success. But critics argue that the **pursuit** of status can put families under heavy pressure.\n\nThe government has tried to **limit** extravagant spending. **Political** campaigns encourage simpler ceremonies, and some local officials have published a **recommendation** for modest celebrations. To some **extent**, these efforts have worked. In some villages, couples now hold group weddings at low cost.\n\nYoung couples have their own views. Many say they want weddings that reflect their **character**, not just tradition. Some **search** online for creative ideas, while others choose to spend their money on travel instead. \"We don't want to **fight** with our parents,\" one bride explained, \"but we want our own style.\"\n\nThere are also practical concerns. Organisers must handle large crowds and prevent any **accident**. Many hotels **charge** high fees during popular months. Couples sometimes **encounter** difficulties in finding a free date. Experts suggest that families **strengthen** communication early to avoid conflict."},"30":{"w":["mainly","recognition","depression","practical","speech","confidence","editor","danger","extremely","virtue","consume","impossible","opinion","rapid","sort","ancient","attend","eliminate","initial","multiple"],"title":"The Ancient Art of Public Speaking","t":"Public speaking has been valued since **ancient** times. Greek philosophers taught students to persuade audiences through clear arguments and **virtue**. Today, the ability to give a good **speech** is still considered an essential skill.\n\nYet many people find it **extremely** difficult. Surveys show that fear of public speaking is one of the most common anxieties, and for some it can even lead to **depression**. \"When I stand in front of an audience, my mind goes blank,\" one student explains. \"It feels **impossible**.\"\n\nExperts offer **practical** advice. First, prepare carefully and **sort** your ideas into a clear structure. Second, practise in front of friends and ask for their **opinion**. Third, focus on the message rather than on yourself. **Initial** nervousness is normal, they say, and it usually disappears after the first minute.\n\nConfidence grows with experience. Many universities encourage students to **attend** debate clubs, where they can practise **multiple** times a week. Over time, students learn to speak at a natural pace rather than a **rapid**, nervous rush. Their **confidence** grows, and their fear is gradually **eliminated|eliminate**.\n\nSome speakers achieve great **recognition**. A former newspaper **editor** who now gives talks around the world says she was once terrified of speaking. \"The **danger** is not that people will judge you,\" she says. \"It's that you will never share your ideas.\" Her talks focus **mainly** on how we **consume** information in the digital age."},"31":{"w":["regardless","regularly","reliable","appropriate","extreme","normal","perceive","psychological","quit","rarely","universal","used","visitor","yield","examine","hide","ideal","illness","male","moral"],"title":"Why We Avoid the Doctor","t":"Many people **rarely** visit the doctor, even when they feel unwell. Some believe their symptoms are **normal**; others **perceive** medical visits as expensive or frightening. Doctors say that this habit can turn a small **illness** into a serious one.\n\nResearch suggests that the reasons are largely **psychological**. People fear bad news, so they **hide** their worries or wait for problems to disappear. **Male** patients, in particular, are less likely than women to seek help early. Many of them grew up believing that strength means never complaining.\n\nExperts argue that regular check-ups are the **ideal** way to prevent disease. People who see a doctor **regularly** are more likely to catch health problems early, when treatment is simpler and cheaper. A **reliable** doctor can also give **appropriate** advice on diet, exercise and stress.\n\nCultural attitudes play a role too. In some communities, people **used** to rely on traditional remedies rather than modern medicine. Doctors now work with community leaders to **examine** these beliefs respectfully. They stress that health care is a **universal** right, **regardless** of income or background.\n\nSimple changes can **yield** big results. Some hospitals now send text reminders to patients, and others offer evening appointments for busy workers. A **visitor** to one clinic in Shanghai noticed posters encouraging people to **quit** smoking and walk more. Doctors say there is also a **moral** dimension: caring for our health helps protect our families. In **extreme** cases, early action can save lives."},"32":{"w":["failure","greatly","obvious","count","implement","location","abandon","launch","path","refer","replace","wealthy","alternative","indeed","instance","range","regarding","resist","separate","statement"],"title":"Learning from Failure","t":"Most of us fear **failure**. We **abandon** projects at the first sign of trouble, worried that others will judge us. Yet history shows that failure is often a necessary step on the **path** to success.\n\nConsider the story of a young engineer who wanted to **launch** a small rocket company. His first three tests failed, and investors began to **resist** further funding. **Indeed**, many people told him to **replace** his dream with a safer career. But each failure taught him something new, and his fourth attempt succeeded.\n\nPsychologists **refer** to this ability as \"resilience.\" People who are resilient see failure as information rather than a judgement. For **instance**, a student who fails an exam might analyse which topics were weak and **implement** a better study plan. The **range** of benefits is wide: resilient people tend to be healthier and happier.\n\nIt is **obvious** that some failures have serious consequences. A medical mistake or a financial loss can affect many lives. But experts say that society **greatly** underestimates the value of small, everyday failures. Schools in some countries now **separate** grades from feedback, so that students can learn without fear.\n\nNot everyone has the same freedom to fail. **Wealthy** families can support children who try risky careers, while poorer students may have no **alternative**. A recent government **statement** **regarding** youth unemployment highlighted this problem. Experts argue that what really **counts|count** is creating safe spaces to try, fail and try again, wherever a person's **location** or background."},"33":{"w":["statistics","whatever","celebrate","critic","goods","witness","proportion","race","wild","stock","mirror","musician","rocket","represent","tie","complain","estimate","perspective","spot","beginning"],"title":"The Joy of Music","t":"From the **beginning** of human history, people have used music to **celebrate**, mourn and connect with one another. Archaeologists have found musical instruments more than 40,000 years old. Today, music remains a universal language that **represents|represent** our deepest emotions.\n\n**Statistics** show that most people listen to music every day. A recent survey found that the **proportion** of young people who play an instrument has risen in the past decade. A **musician** interviewed for the study said that music is like a **mirror**: it reflects who we are and how we feel.\n\nMusic also has a powerful effect on the brain. Scientists who **estimate** the benefits of musical training say it improves memory and concentration. **Whatever** our age, learning an instrument can keep our minds active. Some hospitals even use music to calm patients before surgery.\n\nNot everyone agrees on what counts as good music. A **critic** once **complained|complain** that pop songs all sound the same, while others defend them as fun and accessible. Such debates are a **race** without a finish line, since taste depends on personal **perspective**.\n\nMusic has an economic side too. Concert tickets, instruments and recordings are valuable **goods**, and the music industry's **stock** market value has grown rapidly. Some musicians **witness** **wild** success overnight, their careers rising like a **rocket**. Others work for years in a single **spot**, playing in small cafés. Yet many say the **tie** between musician and audience is the real reward."},"34":{"w":["couple","fan","final","fix","indicate","institute","nowadays","official","phenomenon","priority","proper","rapidly","threaten","ultimately","broad","conclude","delay","distance","distinguish","household"],"title":"The Changing Household","t":"**Nowadays**, the typical **household** looks very different from fifty years ago. Many young **couples|couple** choose to have fewer children, and more people live alone. Researchers at a national **institute** say that this **phenomenon** reflects **broad** changes in society.\n\nThe data **indicate** several causes. Housing has become expensive, especially in large cities, and many people **delay** marriage until their thirties. Work often requires long hours and travel over great **distances|distance**, leaving little time for family life. Some young people say that career development is their top **priority**.\n\nThese changes **rapidly** affect public policy. **Official** figures show that the population is ageing, and governments worry about future labour shortages. Some experts warn that falling birth rates could **threaten** economic growth. Others argue that society will **ultimately** adapt, as it has done before.\n\nIt is important to **distinguish** between choice and necessity. For some, living alone is a free and happy decision. For others, it results from financial pressure or a lack of **proper** support. Policy makers must understand these differences before they can **fix** the problem.\n\nResearchers **conclude** that there is no single \"right\" kind of family. A football **fan** who lives alone and a family of five may both lead fulfilling lives. The **final** goal, experts say, should be to give everyone the support they need."},"35":{"w":["judge","preference","rational","review","switch","talent","analyze","campaign","critical","extra","mark","sacrifice","smart","threat","urge","war","assess","evolve","item","discipline"],"title":"Talent and Discipline","t":"Is success the result of natural **talent** or hard work? This question has been debated for centuries, from the training of soldiers in times of **war** to the modern classroom. Recent research suggests that while talent matters, **discipline** often plays a more **critical** role.\n\nPsychologists who **analyze** the careers of top athletes, musicians and scientists have found a common pattern. Successful people practise deliberately, **assess** their weaknesses honestly and **review** their progress regularly. They are willing to **sacrifice** short-term pleasure for long-term goals. Many describe a strong **urge** to improve that drives them every day.\n\nOf course, being **smart** helps. But intelligence alone cannot guarantee success. Many gifted students fail because they never learn to work hard, while others with **extra** determination achieve remarkable results. Teachers say that a good **mark** in an exam is less important than a love of learning.\n\nOur **preference** for celebrating natural talent may even be harmful. When children are praised only for being clever, they may avoid challenges for fear of failure. A more **rational** approach is to praise effort and strategy.\n\nThe idea has spread beyond schools. Some companies **switch** their hiring focus from qualifications to attitude, and a national **campaign** encourages young people to develop practical skills. The greatest **threat** to success, experts say, is not a lack of ability but a lack of persistence. Skills **evolve** with practice, and every small **item** of progress counts. As one coach says, \"Don't **judge** yourself by your talent. Judge yourself by your effort.\""},"36":{"w":["genetic","possibility","bay","manufacturer","revolutionary","actor","gradually","hit","opposite","react","advise","analysis","application","closely","compete","differ","gather","helpful","reader","reform"],"title":"The Promise of Genetic Medicine","t":"In the past twenty years, **genetic** research has advanced at an astonishing speed. Scientists can now read a person's DNA in a few hours, opening the **possibility** of medicine designed for each individual. Many experts describe this as a **revolutionary** change in health care.\n\nThe **application** of this knowledge is already visible. Doctors can **advise** patients about their risk of certain diseases and recommend early tests. Drug **manufacturers|manufacturer** are developing treatments that target specific genes, and some patients **react** much better to these medicines than to traditional ones.\n\nYet the field also raises difficult questions. Some people fear that genetic information could be used against them, for example by insurance companies. Experts **differ** on how to protect privacy. Others argue that wealthy countries will benefit while poorer nations fall behind. A careful **analysis** of these risks is essential.\n\nResearchers are working **closely** with ethicists and governments to **reform** regulations. They **gather** opinions from patients, doctors and the public. Many believe that the best approach is to move **gradually**, testing new treatments carefully before using them widely.\n\nThe public's understanding is also changing. A popular film about a scientist, starring a famous **actor**, became a surprise **hit** last year, and many **readers|reader** have bought books explaining genetics in simple language. Universities **compete** to attract top researchers, and a new research centre has opened on the **bay** in San Francisco. Some critics hold the **opposite** view, warning that hopes are too high. Still, most agree that the research is **helpful** and worth pursuing."},"37":{"w":["survive","virtual","false","manner","responsible","upon","capacity","cold","commit","difficulty","excessive","freedom","kill","unnecessary","useful","employment","legal","mood","organize","transportation"],"title":"Freedom and Responsibility","t":"In modern societies, **freedom** is often seen as one of the highest values. People expect to choose their careers, express their opinions and live as they wish. But philosophers remind us that freedom comes with responsibility.\n\nConsider the internet. It gives us the freedom to share ideas instantly, yet it also spreads **false** information. Some users **commit** crimes online, such as stealing personal data, while many others **kill** hours scrolling through videos. Governments face great **difficulty** in deciding how much control is **unnecessary** and how much is essential. **Legal** experts argue that clear rules are **useful** for protecting both freedom and safety.\n\nThe same tension exists at work. Employees value the freedom to work from home, which became common in **virtual** offices during the pandemic. But some managers worry about **excessive** freedom reducing productivity. Others say that trust improves **mood** and motivation. Companies that **organize** flexible schedules carefully often see better results.\n\nFreedom also affects public life. Cities that improve public **transportation** give citizens more choices about where to live and work, and elderly people no longer have to wait in the **cold** for hours. Better **employment** opportunities can help young people become independent. In this **manner**, social policies expand personal freedom.\n\nUltimately, a free society depends **upon** citizens who are **responsible**. Each of us has the **capacity** to make choices that help or harm others. As a famous writer once said, freedom is not the right to do whatever we want, but the ability to do what we should. Without this balance, societies may not **survive** difficult times."},"38":{"w":["vital","fair","blood","destroy","gene","numerous","suppose","association","contribution","element","expand","immediate","relatively","survival","alter","enter","exert","mutual","persist","plenty"],"title":"The Secrets in Our Blood","t":"Every cell in the human body contains thousands of **genes|gene**, instructions that shape everything from eye colour to **blood** type. Scientists have identified **numerous** genes linked to disease, and their discoveries are changing medicine.\n\nOne **vital** area of research is the study of inherited illnesses. Some diseases **persist** in families for generations because of a single faulty gene. Researchers are now learning to **alter** these genes, offering hope to patients who once had little chance of **survival**. Such treatments are still **relatively** rare, but progress is rapid.\n\nInternational **association** has made this possible. Scientists in different countries share data and work together, and each **contribution** adds to the overall picture. A **mutual** commitment to open research has helped to **expand** knowledge quickly.\n\nHowever, genetic research also faces ethical challenges. Some people **suppose** that we could one day design \"perfect\" children, a prospect that worries many. Experts **exert** great effort to prevent the misuse of technology. They stress that genetic tools must be **fair** and available to everyone.\n\nThere is **plenty** of reason for hope. **Immediate** benefits include better diagnosis and more effective drugs. As new data **enter** the global research system, scientists hope to understand every **element** of the human genome. But they also warn that careless use could **destroy** public trust."},"39":{"w":["propose","remains","schedule","acquire","commercial","complicated","expense","predict","prior","requirement","sustain","continent","preserve","radical","sufficient","writer","adventure","collection","discovery","dispute"],"title":"Protecting Ancient Remains","t":"Across every **continent**, archaeologists are racing to **preserve** ancient sites before they disappear. Climate change, tourism and development threaten **remains** that have survived for thousands of years. Experts **predict** that many sites could be lost within decades.\n\nProtecting these places is **complicated**. Excavations require careful planning, special equipment and a fixed **schedule**. The **expense** is high, and governments often lack **sufficient** funds. A basic **requirement** for any project is the support of local communities, who may depend on the land for farming.\n\nSome researchers **propose** **radical** solutions. One idea is to create digital copies of sites, allowing visitors to explore them virtually without causing damage. Others suggest limiting tourist numbers and using the income to **sustain** conservation work. **Prior** experience shows that sites managed by local people are often better protected.\n\nOwnership can lead to **dispute**. Museums in wealthy countries hold many ancient objects, and some nations want their treasures returned. These debates are complex, involving history, law and **commercial** interests. Many museums now **acquire** new objects only with clear evidence of their origin.\n\nDespite the challenges, archaeology continues to inspire. Every new **discovery** adds to our understanding of human history. A famous **writer** once described archaeology as \"the greatest **adventure** of all.\" Some museums have even built a **collection** of children's books about ancient civilisations, encouraging young readers to care for the past."},"40":{"w":["super","particle","coal","feedback","otherwise","childhood","compel","consideration","regular","tendency","thrive","unlike","agency","annual","discussion","employ","inspire","player","recover","scholar"],"title":"Learning Through Play","t":"Many people believe that **childhood** should be a time of serious study. But educational **scholars|scholar** increasingly argue that play is just as important. Children who play freely, they say, develop creativity, social skills and a love of learning.\n\nResearch supports this view. An **annual** study by a national education **agency** found that children with regular free time performed better in reading and maths. Teachers who **employ** games in the classroom report that students are more engaged and **recover** faster from failure. Play, it seems, helps children **thrive**.\n\nUnfortunately, there is a growing **tendency** to replace play with homework and extra classes. Parents feel **compelled|compel** to give their children every advantage. **Otherwise**, they fear, their children will fall behind. But experts warn that too much pressure can harm mental health.\n\n**Unlike** traditional lessons, play allows children to learn from experience. A child building a tower of blocks learns about balance and gravity; a young **player** in a team game learns cooperation. Teachers can **inspire** curiosity by giving students time to explore. Regular **feedback** helps them reflect on what they have learned.\n\nThese ideas deserve serious **consideration** in education policy. In one school, teachers led a **discussion** with parents about the benefits of play, and many changed their views. Even simple activities, such as studying a **particle** of sand under a microscope or learning how **coal** forms, can feel **super** exciting when children discover them for themselves. A **regular** routine of play and study, experts say, offers the best of both worlds."},"41":{"w":["strongly","transport","truly","unless","carbon","comparison","devote","direction","diverse","feed","fellow","following","frustrate","historical","immediately","wisdom","alarm","ancestor","battle","born"],"title":"Lessons from Our Ancestors","t":"Every culture has stories about its **ancestors|ancestor**. These stories describe how people survived hard winters, fought in **battle** and learned to **feed** their families. Historians say such tales are more than entertainment; they contain practical **wisdom** that remains useful today.\n\nConsider traditional farming. In many regions, farmers once planted **diverse** crops in the same field. A **comparison** with modern methods shows that this approach protected soil and reduced pests. Scientists now **strongly** recommend similar techniques to cut **carbon** emissions and protect the environment.\n\n**Historical** records also reveal how communities organised **transport** and trade. Ancient merchants travelled great distances, sharing goods and ideas. Their journeys show that people were **truly** connected long before the internet. Each traveller depended on **fellow** traders for safety and information.\n\nOf course, not every old practice is wise. Some traditions caused harm, and modern knowledge can **frustrate** those who prefer the past. Experts warn that we should not **immediately** accept every ancient custom. The **following** principle is useful: learn from the past, but test its lessons carefully.\n\nMany people **born** in cities have little contact with these traditions. Schools and museums now **devote** time to teaching them. One museum uses an old village **alarm** bell to show how communities warned each other of danger. Its message is simple: **unless** we understand where we come from, we may lose our **direction**."},"42":{"w":["curiosity","extend","imitate","tradition","vehicle","artificial","bill","deed","dependent","detect","fiction","giant","regulation","african","court","muscle","platform","concert","rare","arrow"],"title":"The Age of Artificial Intelligence","t":"**Artificial** intelligence (AI) has moved from science **fiction** into everyday life. It recommends films, helps doctors **detect** diseases and even drives **vehicles|vehicle**. Some experts believe AI will be the most important technology of the century.\n\nAI systems learn by analysing huge amounts of data. They can **imitate** human language, recognise faces and predict behaviour. Technology **giants|giant** have invested billions in this field, and many companies now **extend** AI tools to customers through online **platforms|platform**.\n\nYet the rapid growth of AI raises serious concerns. Many workers fear that machines will replace them, and some people have become too **dependent** on technology. Governments are debating new **regulations|regulation** to protect privacy and prevent discrimination. In one well-known case, a **court** ruled that a company had used AI unfairly in hiring decisions.\n\nExperts stress that AI should support, not replace, human judgement. A doctor using AI to read X-rays, for example, still needs knowledge and experience. Technology cannot replace the human **curiosity** that drives discovery, nor the moral sense that guides good **deeds|deed**.\n\nAI is also changing culture. Some musicians use it to compose music for a **concert**, and a recent **African** film festival featured a story written with AI's help. Such projects are still **rare**, but they show new possibilities. Engineers compare AI to an **arrow**: it flies fast, but someone must aim it. Critics say that a careful **tradition** of public debate is needed, and a new **bill** in parliament may soon set the rules. Like a **muscle**, our ability to think independently must be exercised, or it will grow weak."},"43":{"w":["recruit","expose","highlight","overcome","stem","successfully","attitude","display","harmful","route","transform","aid","beneficial","efficient","emerge","emphasize","excite","fuel","host","prosperity"],"title":"Clean Energy Takes Off","t":"For more than a century, coal and oil have provided most of the world's energy. But burning these **fuels|fuel** releases gases that are **harmful** to the climate. Today, clean energy sources such as wind and solar power are beginning to **transform** the global economy.\n\nSeveral countries have **successfully** shifted away from fossil fuels. Denmark, for example, now produces about half of its electricity from wind. Experts **highlight** this as evidence that clean energy can support **prosperity**. New technologies have also made solar panels far more **efficient** and affordable.\n\nStill, there are challenges to **overcome**. Wind and sunlight are not always available, and storing energy is expensive. Some problems **stem** from old power networks that were designed for coal plants. Governments must invest in new **routes|route** for transmitting electricity across regions.\n\nPublic **attitude** is changing quickly. Many people are **excited|excite** by electric cars, and cities **display** charging stations in public squares. International organisations **aid** poorer nations in building solar farms, which are especially **beneficial** in remote areas.\n\nExperts **emphasize** that the transition must be fair. Workers in the coal industry need support and new skills, and companies may **recruit** them for clean energy projects. Last year, a major city agreed to **host** an international conference on renewable energy. New ideas continue to **emerge**, and many believe that clean energy will soon **expose** the true cost of fossil fuels."},"44":{"w":["recall","square","stage","strength","therefore","vast","wonder","appearance","convey","correct","electricity","era","explanation","finally","hardly","inform","internal","largely","meanwhile","ordinary"],"title":"Electricity and Everyday Life","t":"It is **hardly** possible to imagine modern life without **electricity**. It powers our homes, factories and phones, and its **appearance** in the late nineteenth century marked the start of a new **era**. **Ordinary** people suddenly had light at night and machines that saved hours of work.\n\nAt first, many people did not understand how electricity worked. Scientists struggled to **convey** their discoveries to the public, and some newspapers printed frightening stories. A clear **explanation** came **largely** from teachers and engineers who visited towns to demonstrate the new technology. Audiences watched in **wonder** as bulbs lit up on a **stage**.\n\nElectricity **finally** reached rural areas decades later. In China, a **vast** programme brought power to remote villages, and older villagers still **recall** the excitement. \"We gathered in the village **square** and cheered,\" one woman remembers. Meanwhile, industries grew rapidly, giving the country new economic **strength**.\n\nToday, electricity faces new challenges. Demand is rising, and much of it still comes from burning coal. Governments must **inform** citizens about energy saving and find **correct** solutions for the future. **Therefore**, many countries are investing in renewable energy and smarter grids.\n\nTechnology companies are also improving **internal** systems to reduce power use. **Meanwhile**, ordinary families can help by turning off lights and choosing efficient appliances. Small actions, repeated by millions, can make a real difference."},"45":{"w":["participate","president","visual","warn","crucial","dominate","inside","literature","native","poverty","privilege","series","suit","additional","advertisement","contract","fairly","filter","freeze","inevitable"],"title":"The Fight Against Poverty","t":"**Poverty** remains one of the most **crucial** challenges facing the world. Millions of people still lack clean water, education and health care. Yet experts say that real progress is possible when governments, businesses and communities **participate** together.\n\nOne successful approach is investing in education. Children who finish school earn more and are less likely to fall into poverty. Some countries provide **additional** support for girls, who often face greater barriers. A former **president** of a developing nation once said that education is \"the key that unlocks every door.\"\n\nCulture and identity matter too. **Native** communities often suffer more from poverty because they are excluded from economic opportunities. Programmes that respect local **literature**, language and traditions tend to be more effective. Experts **warn** that outside solutions rarely **suit** every community.\n\nBusiness also has a role. Large companies **dominate** many markets, and critics say they should share profits **fairly**. Some firms now sign a **contract** promising to pay suppliers a living wage. A recent **advertisement** campaign featured a **series** of short films showing farmers' lives from the **inside**.\n\nSome argue that inequality is **inevitable** in any economy. Others insist that being born rich should not be a **privilege** that decides a child's future. Many governments have chosen to **freeze** prices of basic food during crises. Clear **visual** data, such as maps of income levels, help citizens **filter** information and understand the problem."},"46":{"w":["kilometer","procedure","familiar","primitive","editorial","accelerate","necessarily","persuade","productive","properly","transformation","actual","behave","consult","definitely","everyday","intelligent","intend","massive","prospect"],"title":"Smart Machines in Everyday Life","t":"Many of the devices we use **everyday** are becoming more **intelligent**. Phones understand our voices, cars warn us of danger, and fridges remind us when we are running out of milk. Experts say this **transformation** will **accelerate** in the coming decade.\n\nThese changes bring **massive** benefits. Smart systems can make factories more **productive** and reduce energy waste. Doctors **consult** computer programmes to check diagnoses, and the **actual** time needed to analyse medical images has fallen dramatically. A city in Denmark even uses sensors to measure every **kilometer** of road for traffic and pollution.\n\nYet old worries remain. Not everyone is **familiar** with new technology, and older people may find it confusing. Critics warn that smart devices do not **necessarily** make life better. If machines **behave** in unexpected ways, the results can be dangerous. Clear **procedures|procedure** are needed to ensure that technology is used **properly**.\n\nSome writers compare our age to a **primitive** stage of a new civilisation. A recent newspaper **editorial** argued that we still do not fully understand the **prospect** of living with intelligent machines. Experts **definitely** agree on one point: people must remain in control.\n\nCompanies **intend** to introduce even more smart products, and they spend heavily to **persuade** consumers to buy them. Before following every new trend, however, we should ask a simple question: does this technology really improve our lives?"},"47":{"w":["remarkable","scene","abundant","afford","exchange","fee","honest","imagination","introduction","mix","okay","remind","salary","site","technique","turning","unable","active","advanced","arise"],"title":"A Remarkable Teacher","t":"Every school has one teacher whom students remember for a lifetime. At my old school, it was Mr. Wang, a physics teacher with a **remarkable** talent for making difficult ideas simple. His lessons were full of experiments, jokes and stories that sparked our **imagination**.\n\nMr. Wang was not interested in the most **advanced** technology. Instead, he used everyday objects to explain complex ideas. He would **mix** water and oil to show how liquids behave, or drop a ball down the stairs to explain gravity. His **technique** was simple: let students see, touch and wonder.\n\nHe was also honest about his own past. As a young man, he had been **unable** to **afford** university **fees|fee** and had worked in a factory for years. He earned a low **salary** but saved money to study at night. \"Hard times are not the end,\" he used to **remind** us. \"They are a **turning** point.\"\n\nHe encouraged us to be **active** learners. He organised an **exchange** programme with a school in another province and took us to visit a nuclear power **site**. Every class began with a short **introduction** to a famous scientist, and an **abundant** supply of questions followed.\n\nMr. Wang retired last year. At his farewell party, former students shared memories, and one **scene** made everyone laugh: a video of him dancing after his class won a science competition. Problems often **arise** in education, he once told us, but an **honest** teacher can solve most of them. \"**Okay**, now let's begin,\" he would say every morning, and we always did."},"48":{"w":["assumption","bond","confident","constant","convince","corporation","crack","device","directly","facility","infant","intellectual","loan","narrow","naturally","observation","punish","resolve","steady","tough"],"title":"The First Years of Life","t":"Scientists have long been fascinated by the minds of **infants|infant**. Through careful **observation**, researchers have discovered that babies learn far more in their first years than adults once believed. They recognise faces, respond to voices and form strong **bonds|bond** with their parents.\n\nEarly childhood is a period of **constant** change. A baby's brain makes millions of new connections every day, and **naturally**, the quality of care affects this process. Children who receive love and attention tend to grow up **confident** and curious. Those who face neglect may struggle with **intellectual** and emotional development.\n\nThese findings have influenced public policy. Some governments provide **loans|loan** and support to new parents, and large **corporations|corporation** offer longer parental leave. In some cities, every neighbourhood has a **facility** where parents can meet and learn about child care.\n\nExperts warn against one common **assumption**: that babies learn best from screens. In fact, research **directly** contradicts this. Babies learn language through real conversations, not through any electronic **device**. Parents are advised to talk, sing and read with their children every day.\n\nRaising children is **tough**, and many parents feel pressure to be perfect. Experts try to **convince** them that small, **steady** efforts matter more than expensive toys. They also advise parents not to **punish** children harshly, but to **resolve** problems patiently. The gap between rich and poor families can **narrow** when good support is available to everyone. As one researcher put it, \"Small **cracks|crack** in childhood can become large ones later. Early care is the best prevention.\""},"49":{"w":["unit","willing","assistant","deliver","federal","genius","keen","military","protection","sophisticated","stream","charity","drug","festival","hurt","insect","leadership","liberal","maintenance","phase"],"title":"Caring for Others","t":"Across the world, millions of people volunteer their time to help others. They work for a **charity**, organise community events or support people who are sick or **hurt**. Experts say that volunteering benefits both the helpers and those they help.\n\nConsider a volunteer **unit** in a small town in Yunnan. Its members **deliver** meals to elderly residents, plant trees by a mountain **stream** and protect a rare **insect** species from extinction. Their **leadership** comes from an energetic retired teacher with a **genius** for organising people.\n\nVolunteers must be **willing** to work hard. Some help in hospitals, where they assist doctors in giving out **drugs|drug** and comforting patients. Others work as an **assistant** in schools, helping children with reading. Many are **keen** to learn new skills, from first aid to the **maintenance** of community gardens.\n\nGovernments also play a role. In some countries, **federal** programmes fund volunteer work, and **military** personnel help during natural disasters. **Liberal** and conservative politicians alike praise volunteering, though they disagree on how much the state should support it.\n\nVolunteering has become more **sophisticated** in recent years. Online platforms match volunteers with organisations, and training programmes prepare them for each **phase** of their work. At the town's annual **festival**, volunteers are honoured for their **protection** of the environment and their care for others."},"50":{"w":["valley","regret","toy","treasure","horror","scent","wireless","contrast","competitive","globe","lifetime","neglect","notice","objective","occasionally","spirit","volume","widespread","anticipate","belong"],"title":"A Treasure in the Valley","t":"In a quiet **valley** in the south, archaeologists recently discovered a remarkable **treasure**: a collection of ancient pottery, coins and children's **toys|toy** buried more than two thousand years ago. The discovery has attracted **widespread** attention and drawn visitors from across the **globe**.\n\nLocal people had long told stories about the valley's **spirit**, but few had expected real treasure. \"We **occasionally** found old coins in the fields,\" one farmer said, \"but we never imagined this.\" Researchers now **anticipate** further discoveries in the area.\n\nThe find offers a striking **contrast** between ancient and modern life. Toys made of clay show that children played games similar to those of today. A small bottle that once held a **scent** made from flowers suggests that people valued beauty and pleasure. Historians say such objects **belong** to ordinary families, not just the rich.\n\nHowever, protecting the site has not been easy. Treasure hunters have damaged nearby areas, and the government has been accused of **neglect**. Some villagers **regret** that they did not report earlier finds. The local museum now uses **wireless** cameras to watch the site at night.\n\nThe discovery also sparked a **competitive** race between universities wanting to study it. An **objective** committee has been set up to decide who will lead the research. The huge **volume** of material will take years to analyse. Visitors who **notice** the care taken with every object often express wonder rather than **horror** at how much was once forgotten. For many, it is a once-in-a-**lifetime** experience."},"51":{"w":["domestic","favor","fund","journey","mass","obtain","substantial","symbol","treatment","attack","authority","blame","capable","champion","contain","core","deserve","estate","input","lift"],"title":"The Price of a Home","t":"In many large cities, buying a home has become a difficult **journey** for young people. Prices in the real **estate** market have risen sharply, and a **substantial** part of every salary goes to rent. For many families, a home is no longer just a place to live but a **symbol** of success.\n\nEconomists **blame** several factors. Low interest rates encouraged a **mass** of investors to buy property, and **domestic** demand grew faster than supply. Some investors **obtain** several flats and leave them empty, waiting for prices to rise. Critics **attack** this practice, saying that housing should not be treated like stocks.\n\nGovernments have responded in different ways. Local **authorities|authority** in some cities **fund** affordable housing projects, while others **lift** restrictions on building. A few cities offer special **treatment** to young professionals, such as lower deposits. Each policy **contains|contain** risks, and experts disagree on which works best.\n\nThe **core** problem, many argue, is inequality. People who already own property gain wealth, while those without property fall further behind. \"Everyone **deserves|deserve** a safe home,\" says one housing **champion**, who campaigns for tenants' rights. She believes the public should have more **input** into housing decisions.\n\nYoung people are adapting creatively. Some share flats with friends, and others move to smaller cities. Many are **capable** of building good lives without owning property. As one young engineer put it, \"I **favor** freedom over a mortgage. A home is where people care about you, not just where you sleep.\""},"52":{"w":["museum","perfectly","pleasant","proposal","rank","remedy","select","absolutely","accomplish","attractive","concerning","cycle","department","doubt","exposure","mile","prescribe","promise","repair","saving"],"title":"A Museum Comes Back to Life","t":"Ten years ago, the city **museum** was almost empty. Its rooms were dark, its displays old and its visitors few. Today, it **ranks|rank** among the most popular attractions in the region. How did this transformation happen?\n\nIt began with a **proposal** from a young curator. She suggested that the museum **select** a few powerful stories instead of showing thousands of objects. Her plan was **absolutely** clear: every room should help visitors feel something. The museum's **department** of education also created activities for children and families.\n\nChanges were made step by step. Old cases were **repaired|repair**, lights were improved and the café was made more **attractive**. The museum's leaders **promised|promise** to listen to visitors, and they introduced free entry on Sundays. The **savings|saving** from energy-efficient lighting helped pay for new exhibitions.\n\nOne exhibition, **concerning** the history of local medicine, proved especially popular. It showed how doctors once **prescribed|prescribe** strange **remedies|remedy**, from herbal soups to animal bones. Visitors also learned about the **cycle** of disease and recovery in ancient times. Some objects were displayed for the first time, giving them public **exposure** after centuries in storage.\n\nThere were doubters at first. Some critics expressed **doubt** that a small museum could **accomplish** such change. But visitors now travel many **miles|mile** to see it, and the atmosphere is **pleasant** and welcoming. \"The museum **perfectly** shows that history can be exciting,\" one visitor wrote in the guestbook."},"53":{"w":["stability","unexpected","campus","coast","division","guy","missing","passenger","version","apartment","button","flash","print","wolf","baggage","pigeon","convenient","attach","consist","exhaust"],"title":"Lost on the Coast","t":"Last summer, my friend Kai took a trip along the **coast** of southern China. He planned to stay in a small **apartment** near the sea, but the journey did not go as expected. Within hours of arriving, he discovered that his **baggage** was **missing**.\n\nThe airline staff were helpful but slow. They asked him to **print** a form, fill it in and **attach** a photo of his suitcase. The process seemed to **consist** of endless paperwork. Kai felt **exhausted|exhaust** after waiting for hours at the airport, surrounded by angry **passengers|passenger** with similar problems.\n\nThings soon improved. A friendly **guy** at the information desk lent him a phone charger and showed him a **convenient** bus route to his apartment. On the bus, Kai met a student from the local university who invited him to visit her **campus**. She showed him a hill where wild birds lived, including a rare **pigeon** with green feathers.\n\nKai later learned that the region has a rich wildlife. Researchers had even reported seeing a **wolf** in the hills, an **unexpected** discovery that attracted national attention. Scientists believe that the **stability** of the forest ecosystem has improved after years of protection.\n\nHis bag finally arrived three days later. Inside, everything was safe, though one **button** had come off his jacket. \"Next time, I'll keep a copy of everything,\" he said. He also learned that the airline's lost-baggage **division** had a newer **version** of its app that tracks bags in real time. \"In a **flash**,\" he joked, \"I became an expert in lost luggage.\""},"54":{"w":["frequent","heavily","ruin","adjust","alert","birth","capital","consistent","efficiency","endure","fresh","ingredient","insight","lecture","magazine","nevertheless","primarily","reasonable","reduction","shopping"],"title":"The Science of Cooking","t":"Cooking is not just an art; it is also a science. Every recipe depends on chemical reactions between **ingredients|ingredient**, and understanding these reactions can make us better cooks. A popular **lecture** series at a university in Beijing explores exactly this topic.\n\nThe lecturer, a chemistry professor, begins each class by cooking a simple dish. She explains why **fresh** vegetables taste sweeter and why bread rises. Her **insight** is that small changes in temperature or timing can **ruin** a dish or make it perfect. Students learn to **adjust** recipes based on scientific principles.\n\nThe course also addresses health. Many people rely **heavily** on processed food, which often contains too much salt and sugar. The professor argues that a **reduction** in such food would improve public health. \"Cooking at home is **primarily** about control,\" she says. \"You know what goes into your food.\"\n\n**Nevertheless**, she understands that modern life is busy. She suggests **reasonable** solutions, such as cooking larger meals and freezing portions. She also encourages students to be **alert** to marketing claims on food packaging. **Frequent** **shopping** at local markets, she says, is better than one big trip to a supermarket.\n\nThe course has become very popular. A national **magazine** described it as \"the most delicious class in the **capital**.\" Some students say it changed their lives, giving them **consistent** habits and greater **efficiency** in the kitchen. Many say they no longer **endure** stomach problems after every meal. One student even decided to open a restaurant after the **birth** of her first child, inspired by the course."},"55":{"w":["chain","contact","decrease","exciting","grant","implication","interpretation","isolate","minority","politician","possibly","previously","regulate","resistance","roughly","routine","stuff","substitute","trap","acceptance"],"title":"Working from Home","t":"Working from home has moved from a rare privilege to a common **routine** for millions of people. **Previously**, most companies expected employees to work in offices. Now, **roughly** a third of workers in some countries spend at least part of the week at home.\n\nThe **implications|implication** are significant. Workers save time on commuting and may enjoy greater flexibility. Many find the change **exciting** at first. However, some feel **isolated|isolate** without daily **contact** with colleagues. Researchers note that loneliness can **decrease** motivation and productivity over time.\n\nCompanies have responded in different ways. Some **grant** employees full freedom to choose where they work; others **regulate** home working strictly. A few large **chain** stores have even tried to **substitute** online meetings for most business trips. Not every employee shows **acceptance** of these changes, and some unions have expressed **resistance**.\n\nExperts warn of a common **trap**: working longer hours because the office is always at home. They recommend clear boundaries, such as a separate desk and fixed working times. \"Put work **stuff** away at the end of the day,\" one psychologist advises.\n\nThe debate has political dimensions too. A **politician** in one city proposed tax benefits for companies that allow home working. Critics argue that **minority** groups and low-income workers may not have suitable space at home. Each **interpretation** of the data seems to support different conclusions. It is **possibly** too early to judge the long-term effects."},"56":{"w":["bottom","confuse","conservation","expression","foundation","gold","grateful","hall","heat","investigate","mobile","motor","operation","orient","precious","presence","reflection","retrieve","shrink","ad"],"title":"Saving the Coral Reefs","t":"Coral reefs are among the most **precious** ecosystems on Earth. They cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they support a quarter of all marine species. But rising sea temperatures are causing many reefs to **shrink**, and scientists warn that some could disappear within decades.\n\nResearchers have begun to **investigate** the causes in detail. Ocean **heat** waves cause corals to lose their colour, a process known as \"bleaching.\" Pollution from farms and factories adds further damage. Divers who **retrieve** samples from the **bottom** of the sea report that some reefs look like underwater deserts.\n\n**Conservation** groups are working to protect them. One **foundation** in Australia uses **mobile** laboratories to grow young corals and replant them on damaged reefs. The **operation** is slow and expensive, but early results are encouraging. Scientists describe the healthy new corals as \"more valuable than **gold**.\"\n\nPublic **presence** matters too. Many tourists visit reefs, but **motor** boats and careless divers can harm them. Some resorts now help visitors **orient** themselves with clear rules about where to swim. A recent **ad** campaign encouraged tourists to choose eco-friendly tour companies.\n\nThe crisis has prompted deep **reflection** among scientists. \"We are **grateful** for every reef that survives,\" one researcher said at a conference **hall** in Sydney. Her **expression** was serious. \"But we must not **confuse** small successes with a solution. The real answer is to cut carbon emissions.\""},"57":{"w":["congress","construction","convenience","desert","gesture","guide","log","press","seal","section","temptation","tone","accommodation","architecture","audio","clothing","engineering","forecast","medium","nuclear"],"title":"Building for the Future","t":"Modern **architecture** faces a difficult challenge: how to create buildings that are beautiful, comfortable and good for the environment. **Construction** accounts for a large share of global carbon emissions, and many experts believe the industry must change rapidly.\n\nEngineers are developing new solutions. Some cities debate whether **nuclear** power or renewable energy should supply new districts, and many new buildings already have solar panels on the roof. Others are designed to stay cool in hot **desert** climates without air conditioning. One firm has developed a building that can **seal** itself automatically when a storm is **forecast**, saving energy and protecting residents.\n\n**Engineering** is not the only issue. Architects must also think about **convenience** and comfort. Good design can **guide** people naturally through a building, with each **section** connected by bright corridors and quiet spaces. Many cities now require new **accommodation** to include green areas and shared gardens.\n\nSome governments are taking action. A national **congress** in Europe recently passed laws to reduce emissions from construction. The **press** has praised these laws, though some builders resist the higher costs. The **tone** of the debate has sometimes become heated.\n\nNot all solutions are high-tech. Traditional builders in some regions use **log** walls and local stone, and some even recycle old wool **clothing** as insulation. A small **gesture**, such as planting trees around a building, can make a big difference. Some designers even include **audio** guides explaining the building's features. The **temptation** to build quickly and cheaply is strong, but a **medium**-sized eco-friendly building can save money over its lifetime."},"58":{"w":["province","rat","roof","venture","athlete","compound","leather","musical","imply","disappear","praise","render","strike","apart","arouse","contrary","cooperate","influential","readily","refuse"],"title":"The Rise of a Young Athlete","t":"At eighteen, Li Na became the youngest **athlete** from her **province** to win a national running championship. Her victory surprised many, since she grew up in a poor village with no proper sports facilities. She trained on dirt roads, wearing old **leather** shoes, and practised under a leaking **roof** when it rained.\n\nHer coach **readily** admits that he did not expect such success. \"She was talented but not obviously special,\" he says. \"What set her **apart** was her determination.\" Li Na would **refuse** to give up, even when injuries made training painful.\n\nHer story has **aroused|arouse** great public interest. Newspapers have **praised|praise** her as a role model, and an **influential** sports company has offered her a sponsorship deal. **Contrary** to expectations, however, she has decided to stay with her local team for another year. \"I want to **cooperate** with the people who helped me,\" she explains.\n\nLi Na's success also **implies|imply** wider changes in Chinese sport. Rural athletes now receive more support, and new training centres are being built. Some critics warn that commercial pressure could **render** young athletes vulnerable, and a business **venture** in sport is not always good for the athletes themselves.\n\nAway from the track, Li Na loves **musical** theatre and sings in a local choir. She jokes that the only thing she fears is the old **rat** that lives in her training hall. When asked about the future, she says that success can **disappear** quickly. \"Every race is a new start,\" she says. \"Lightning can **strike** anyone.\" Her training combines running with strength exercises and a special drink, a **compound** of herbs her grandmother prepares."},"59":{"w":["steal","accumulate","announce","award","calm","director","disagree","drop","external","flexible","injury","invite","justify","locate","loud","movement","occupation","onto","partly","relation"],"title":"Making a Film","t":"Last year, a young **director** named Zhou Yan released her first feature film. It tells the story of a family whose savings are **stolen|steal** by a cheat, and how they rebuild their lives. The film won a national **award**, and critics praised its **calm**, honest style.\n\nMaking the film was not easy. Zhou spent three years writing the script and trying to **locate** funding. Many investors **disagreed|disagree** with her vision, preferring a more commercial story. She had to **justify** every decision, from casting to music. \"At times I wanted to **drop** the whole project,\" she admits.\n\nThe shooting process brought new challenges. One actor suffered an **injury** during a difficult scene, and production stopped for two weeks. **External** factors, such as bad weather, also caused delays. Zhou learned to be **flexible**, adjusting her plans almost daily.\n\nHer **occupation** demands patience and teamwork. Zhou **invited|invite** her actors to share ideas, and she encouraged a close **relation** between cast and crew. Over time, the team began to **accumulate** a strong sense of trust. She was **partly** inspired by her grandmother, a quiet woman who survived great hardship.\n\nWhen the film was finished, the producers **announced|announce** a national release. At the premiere, the audience gave a **loud** round of applause. Zhou stepped **onto** the stage, holding back tears. Her film has since sparked a **movement** among young filmmakers who want to tell honest stories about ordinary lives."},"60":{"w":["reverse","seriously","title","trick","unfortunately","urgent","weak","boast","bound","combination","comfortable","commitment","creature","crowd","custom","description","destruction","found","impose","industrial"],"title":"Customs That Connect Us","t":"Every culture has its own **customs|custom**, from wedding ceremonies to ways of greeting friends. These traditions may seem strange to outsiders, but they help communities feel connected. Anthropologists say that customs are **bound** up with identity and history.\n\nSome customs date back thousands of years. In many villages, people still gather in large **crowds|crowd** for spring festivals, sharing food and music. A **description** of one such festival in an ancient text shows that the tradition was **founded|found** more than a thousand years ago. Villagers **boast** that their celebrations are the oldest in the region. On the last day, they **reverse** traditional roles: children give each adult a funny **title**, and visitors often become the target of a harmless **trick**.\n\nYet customs are not fixed. **Industrial** development and the movement of people to cities have changed many traditions. **Unfortunately**, some customs have disappeared completely, and their **destruction** represents a cultural loss. Experts say that preserving them is **urgent**.\n\nSome governments **impose** rules to protect traditional culture, but critics warn that forcing people to follow old customs can backfire. A more effective approach, they argue, is to encourage a **combination** of tradition and modern life. Many young people now take customs **seriously**, joining traditional music groups or learning old crafts.\n\nSome customs involve animals. In one southern village, every child receives a small wooden **creature** carved by elders, believed to protect them from evil. Visitors are sometimes surprised by such beliefs, but locals find them **comfortable** and meaningful. Preserving traditions requires real **commitment**. As one elder said, \"If we forget our customs, we become **weak**. If we remember them, we become strong.\""},"61":{"w":["joy","lay","obviously","overseas","package","philosopher","stretch","substance","target","ultimate","undergo","accurate","arrangement","collective","entertainment","equipment","excitement","marriage","pollute","portion"],"title":"The Philosophy of Joy","t":"What brings us real **joy**? An ancient Greek **philosopher** once argued that happiness comes from wisdom and friendship, not from wealth. Modern psychologists are now testing this idea, and their findings suggest that his view was largely **accurate**.\n\nIn one study, researchers followed people who had just **undergone|undergo** major life changes, such as **marriage**, moving **overseas** or losing a job. They found that people's happiness returned to roughly the same level within a year, whatever had happened. **Obviously**, money and success matter, but their effect is smaller than we expect.\n\nWhat lasts longer is a sense of meaning. People who **lay** strong foundations of friendship and purpose report greater well-being. **Collective** activities, such as singing in a choir or volunteering, provide deep satisfaction. In contrast, buying the latest **equipment** or **entertainment** **package** gives only short-term **excitement**.\n\nThe **ultimate** **target** of life, many philosophers argue, is not pleasure but understanding. Yet modern society often encourages the opposite. Advertising promises happiness through products, and we **stretch** our budgets to buy them. Meanwhile, we **pollute** the planet to produce goods we do not truly need.\n\nExperts suggest simple **arrangements|arrangement** to increase daily joy: spend a **portion** of each day outdoors, share meals with friends and focus on things of real **substance**. As the philosopher put it more than two thousand years ago, \"The secret of happiness is not in seeking more, but in enjoying less.\""},"62":{"w":["applicant","conserve","faith","indoor","neutral","officer","tower","canteen","orchestra","outdoor","punishment","shelf","sex","unlikely","educate","opening","plus","seldom","attribute","cancer"],"title":"A Day at the Community Centre","t":"Every Saturday, the community centre in our neighbourhood opens its doors to people of all ages. Regardless of age or **sex**, everyone is welcome. The **opening** event this year was a concert by a local youth **orchestra**, and hundreds of people filled the hall.\n\nThe centre offers a wide range of activities. Children play **outdoor** games in the courtyard, while older residents join **indoor** classes in painting and dancing. The **canteen** serves cheap, healthy meals, and a small library has a **shelf** of books in several languages. Volunteers work hard to **educate** visitors about healthy living, and doctors give free talks on **cancer** prevention.\n\nThe centre also helps people find jobs. Each month, a careers **officer** meets with every **applicant** to discuss their skills. It is **unlikely** that a job seeker will leave without useful advice. Some young people who once faced **punishment** for minor crimes have found new opportunities here.\n\nThe centre's director, a retired teacher, says she **seldom** takes a day off. She has great **faith** in the power of community. \"People need a **neutral** place to meet,\" she explains, \"where nobody judges them.\" She **attributes|attribute** the centre's success to its volunteers.\n\nThe building itself is an old **tower** that once belonged to a factory. Volunteers worked hard to **conserve** its original walls. \"It is a symbol of our history,\" the director says, \"**plus** a reminder that old things can have new life.\""},"63":{"w":["circle","cope","criticize","derive","desirable","discourage","equal","expansion","frame","luck","newly","origin","pride","primary","revenue","stimulate","suitable","superior","systematic","tempt"],"title":"The Origin of Good Luck","t":"Many cultures believe in **luck**. People carry special objects, avoid certain numbers or wear red to bring good fortune. But where do these beliefs come from? Historians say their **origin** often lies in ancient religion and daily life.\n\nTake the colour red in China. It has long been seen as **desirable**, a sign of joy and success. During festivals, families hang red decorations to **stimulate** good fortune and protect against evil. Some scholars **derive** this tradition from ancient stories about a monster that feared the colour red.\n\nPsychologists offer another explanation. Belief in luck helps people **cope** with uncertainty. When the future is unclear, rituals provide comfort and a sense of control. They also strengthen social bonds: families feel **pride** in sharing traditions. Critics **criticize** such beliefs as unscientific, but many people find them meaningful.\n\nSome businesses use luck to increase **revenue**. Shops sell lucky charms, and some companies choose \"lucky\" dates for the **expansion** of their business. Advertisers **tempt** consumers with products said to bring good fortune. **Newly** married couples often choose lucky wedding dates.\n\nExperts say there is no **systematic** evidence that lucky charms work. Yet believing in luck does not have to be harmful, as long as it does not **discourage** hard work. A **primary** school teacher I know keeps a small lucky **frame** on her desk. Inside is a drawing of a **circle** of children holding hands. \"It reminds me that every child is **equal**,\" she says. She believes that effort is always **superior** to luck, but the picture helps her find **suitable** words when students feel down."},"64":{"w":["upper","aside","attraction","capture","climb","coach","complaint","cross","definition","deny","engineer","excellent","exclusively","executive","grocery","impression","interpret","invent","lab","lag"],"title":"Inside a Science Lab","t":"For many students, a visit to a real science **lab** is an exciting experience. Last month, our class toured the research centre of a large technology company. The **executive** who welcomed us explained that the company works **exclusively** on new medical devices.\n\nWe first met an **engineer** who had helped **invent** a small sensor that measures blood sugar. She showed us how it could **capture** data every minute and send it to a phone. Her **definition** of a good invention was simple: \"It makes life better for ordinary people.\"\n\nThen we visited the **upper** floors, where scientists tested new materials. One researcher had to **climb** a ladder to reach a machine near the ceiling. Another showed us how to **interpret** data from an experiment. \"Science often **lags|lag** behind our hopes,\" he admitted. \"Progress is slow.\"\n\nThe visit left a strong **impression** on us. Our teacher, who also works as a football **coach**, said it was an **excellent** chance to see science in action. Some students had expected boring work, but the lab was full of life. Even the staff canteen, which sold fruit from a local **grocery**, seemed like an **attraction**.\n\nAt the end, a young scientist answered our questions. When asked whether her job was stressful, she did not **deny** it. \"There are days when I want to put everything **aside**,\" she said. \"But when an experiment works, the feeling is amazing.\" She enjoys the small daily victories, too: every solved problem is one more she can **cross** off her list. Patients rarely make a **complaint**, she added, because every device is tested so carefully."},"65":{"w":["length","master","mature","movie","nut","occasion","painting","police","reject","repeatedly","sand","sink","slip","soil","surprising","whenever","worthy","zone","abstract","admit"],"title":"Painting the Seaside","t":"Every summer, a group of amateur artists gathers on a quiet beach to practise **painting**. Their teacher, a **master** painter in his seventies, believes that nature is the best classroom. \"Look at the **sand**, the sea and the sky,\" he tells students. \"Everything you need is here.\"\n\nStudents of every age and **length** of experience join the class. Some are **mature** professionals looking for relaxation; others are teenagers who dream of becoming artists. The teacher **admits|admit** that he once **rejected|reject** abstract art, but he now encourages students to try **abstract** styles too.\n\nPainting by the sea has its challenges. Wind can **slip** paper from an easel, and the rising tide sometimes forces students to move. Once, a student's easel began to **sink** into wet sand. The teacher laughed and helped her move to a safer **zone** higher up the beach.\n\nThe class has become a local tradition. On special **occasions|occasion**, the artists display their work in the village hall, and local **police** officers help manage the crowds. **Whenever** a painting is sold, the money goes to a charity that protects the coastline and its fragile **soil**.\n\nThe results are often **surprising**. Students who **repeatedly** struggled with technique suddenly produce beautiful work. One woman painted a simple bowl of **nuts|nut** that won a regional prize. \"Every painting is **worthy** of attention,\" the teacher says, \"even if it isn't perfect.\" Some students later turned their paintings into a short **movie** about the class."},"66":{"w":["aggressive","automatic","basically","comprehension","consciousness","cue","dirt","fatigue","fertilizer","flat","headline","minimum","obstacle","ownership","port","reputation","security","slightly","teenager","weekly"],"title":"Why Teenagers Need More Sleep","t":"Every parent knows the scene: a **teenager** who cannot get out of bed in the morning. Many adults assume that young people are simply lazy, but scientists say the problem is **basically** biological. During adolescence, the body's internal clock shifts, making teenagers feel sleepy later at night.\n\nResearch shows that lack of sleep has serious consequences. Tired students show lower reading **comprehension**, and their **consciousness** of risks decreases. Chronic **fatigue** can also make teenagers more **aggressive** and anxious. Even **slightly** shorter sleep, repeated over weeks, affects school performance.\n\nSome schools have responded by starting classes later. The results have been encouraging: attendance has risen, and students say they feel more alert. A **headline** in a national newspaper called the change \"the simplest reform in education.\" However, schools face **obstacles|obstacle**, such as bus schedules and parents' working hours.\n\nExperts offer practical advice. Teenagers should keep phones out of the bedroom, since screen light gives the brain a false **cue** that it is still daytime. They should also follow a **weekly** routine, going to bed at roughly the same time. Doctors recommend a **minimum** of eight hours' sleep a night.\n\nParents can help, too. Some families create a \"sleep zone\" with no screens, and others establish rules about computer **ownership** in bedrooms. Sleep is not a luxury; it is a basic need, just like food or **security**. A good night's rest can protect a student's health and **reputation** at school. As one doctor put it, \"Sleep is not **automatic** for teenagers; it must be protected.\"\n\nSleep problems are not limited to teenagers, of course. Workers at a busy **port** who change shifts every week often feel exhausted, and families in a small **flat** beside a noisy road also sleep badly. In the countryside, farmers who rise before dawn to spread **fertilizer** face similar challenges. Doctors even advise cleaning bedrooms regularly, since **dirt** and dust can disturb breathing at night."},"67":{"w":["ban","essay","evolution","humor","lion","republican","roman","temporary","timber","tribe","tuition","wit","forgive","marine","output","pocket","possess","bat","carrot","exit"],"title":"Life in Ancient Rome","t":"The **Roman** Empire was one of the most powerful civilisations in history. At its height, it ruled territories from Britain to North Africa. Historians continue to study how Romans lived, worked and entertained themselves.\n\nRoman cities were remarkably advanced. They had public baths, roads and markets, and many buildings were made of stone and **timber**. Wealthy families **possessed|possess** large houses with gardens, while poorer citizens lived in crowded apartments. Some laws even placed a **ban** on building too high, for safety reasons. Archaeologists exploring tunnels beneath the city have found everything from coins to the bones of a **bat**.\n\nEntertainment played a central role in Roman life. Crowds gathered to watch chariot races and fights involving wild animals such as the **lion**, a cruelty that modern visitors find hard to **forgive**. Writers produced plays full of **humor** and **wit**, and poets wrote about love and politics. Students paid **tuition** to study with famous teachers, and many wrote an **essay** on philosophy or history.\n\nThe Romans traded across the Mediterranean, importing **marine** products such as fish sauce. The **output** of Roman farms was enormous, and simple vegetables like the **carrot** were common in daily meals. Traders carried coins in a leather **pocket** tied to their belts.\n\nRoman society was not a democracy in the modern sense, though early Rome was a **republican** state ruled by elected officials. Over time, it faced conflicts with every neighbouring **tribe** and many **temporary** alliances. Its **evolution** from republic to empire remains a major topic of study. Visitors to Rome today can still walk through ancient streets, **exit** through old city gates and imagine a world that, in many ways, shaped our own."},"68":{"w":["fridge","tag","van","concentration","hang","somewhat","breed","disadvantage","dislike","exhibit","folk","hardship","qualify","sake","sentence","shortage","software","utilize","altogether","approximately"],"title":"Running a Family Farm Today","t":"My cousin runs a small dairy farm in the north. He **breeds|breed** cows, grows vegetables and sells cheese at local markets. Life on the farm is **somewhat** romantic in films, but in reality it involves great **hardship**.\n\nThe biggest **disadvantage** of small farming is the unpredictable income. Prices change every month, and a **shortage** of rain can ruin a harvest. My cousin works **approximately** fourteen hours a day, **altogether** about a hundred hours a week. \"You do it for the **sake** of the land and your family,\" he says. \"Not for money.\"\n\nTechnology has helped. He uses **software** to track milk production and **utilize** resources more efficiently. Each cow wears an electronic **tag** that records its health. He keeps fresh cheese in a large **fridge** and delivers it in an old white **van**. These tools require **concentration** and training, and he had to **qualify** for a government programme to afford them.\n\nSome traditions remain. In the barn, he likes to **hang** old farming tools on the walls, and every autumn the village holds a **folk** festival to celebrate the harvest. Last year, his cheese was chosen to **exhibit** at a regional food fair.\n\nHe admits that he **dislikes|dislike** some parts of the job, especially paperwork. \"One **sentence** in a government form can take me an hour to understand,\" he jokes. But he would not choose any other life."},"69":{"w":["assure","chief","circumstance","conscious","contemporary","curious","decorate","delicate","distribution","ending","endless","escape","eventually","exclude","height","leap","lucky","mission","misunderstand","ought"],"title":"A Curious Mind","t":"Albert Einstein once said, \"I have no special talents. I am only passionately **curious**.\" Many scientists agree that curiosity is the **chief** source of discovery. Children are naturally curious, asking endless questions about the world. But as they grow older, many lose this habit. Studies of the **distribution** of curiosity across age groups suggest that it reaches its **height** in early childhood.\n\nResearchers have tried to understand why. One explanation is that schools often focus on correct answers rather than questions. Students learn to **exclude** wild ideas and follow rules. Under such **circumstances|circumstance**, curiosity may slowly disappear.\n\n**Contemporary** educators are trying to change this. Some teachers encourage students to ask **endless** questions, even if they have no answers. Others take students on field trips to observe nature. \"We **ought** to reward curiosity, not just correct answers,\" one teacher argues. She believes that a **conscious** effort can keep curiosity alive.\n\nCuriosity also matters beyond school. It helps adults **escape** boredom and adapt to change. Workers who are curious tend to learn new skills more quickly and **eventually** perform better. Some companies even **assure** employees that asking questions will not be punished.\n\nYet curiosity needs a **delicate** balance. Too much uncertainty can cause stress, and people may **misunderstand** each other's motives. Scientists say we should **decorate** our lives with small adventures: reading new books, visiting new places or learning a new language. As one researcher put it, \"A curious mind makes a **leap** of imagination that no computer can copy.\" Those who stay curious, she adds, are the **lucky** ones: they care less about a story's **ending** than about the questions it raises, and they see learning as a lifelong **mission**."},"70":{"w":["outlet","pose","promising","quantity","religion","religious","resolution","resort","rob","secure","setting","tap","touch","transfer","union","via","volunteer","weigh","accompany","acre"],"title":"A Weekend at the Mountain Resort","t":"Last month, my family spent a weekend at a mountain **resort** about three hours from the city. We travelled **via** a winding road through forests and farmland, passing small villages and fields of several **acres|acre**. The **setting** was beautiful, with lakes, waterfalls and old temples.\n\nThe resort is run by a local farmers' **union**, which uses the profits to support the village. Many staff are **volunteers|volunteer** from nearby towns. They **accompany** guests on walks and explain local customs. One guide told us about the area's **religious** history; several temples belong to a **religion** that has been practised there for centuries.\n\nThe resort is careful about safety. Guests' belongings are kept in **secure** lockers, and the staff warned us that thieves had once tried to **rob** a tourist on a mountain path. A small shop is an **outlet** for local crafts and honey, sold in a surprising **quantity** to visitors.\n\nOn the second day, we tried fishing. The guide showed us how to **tap** the water gently to attract fish, and how to **weigh** our catch on an old scale. My little brother was thrilled to catch a fish, though he refused to **touch** it. We also met a photographer who asked us to **pose** for pictures beside the lake.\n\nThe resort is a **promising** example of rural tourism. Its managers have made a **resolution** to protect the environment, limiting the number of guests and banning plastic bottles. Before we left, they helped us **transfer** our photos onto a USB stick as a souvenir. We plan to return next year."},"71":{"w":["anywhere","appliance","boss","branch","civil","density","disguise","exaggerate","fancy","grain","harvest","hunt","intense","limitation","lonely","merely","nearby","offend","outstanding","pain"],"title":"Harvest Time in the Village","t":"Every autumn, my grandparents' village celebrates the **harvest** with a week of festivals. Farmers bring in their **grain**, children help pick fruit and families gather to share food. For a few days, even the most **lonely** elderly villagers have company.\n\nMy grandfather loves this season. He tells stories about his youth, when villagers would **hunt** wild birds and work in the fields from dawn to dusk. Life was hard, and many people lived in constant **pain** from heavy labour. \"Young people today have no idea how difficult it was,\" he says, though my grandmother warns him not to **exaggerate**.\n\nThe village has changed a lot. Many families now own modern kitchen **appliances|appliance**, and some have opened guesthouses for tourists. A bank has opened a small **branch** in the main street, and a **nearby** town has built a new hospital. Still, life here has its **limitations|limitation**: young people must travel far to find good jobs.\n\nTourism has brought new challenges. Some visitors arrive in **fancy** cars and behave rudely, which can **offend** local people. Others try to **disguise** commercial activities as cultural events. The village committee works hard to keep tourism respectful and **civil**.\n\nDespite these problems, the harvest festival remains **outstanding**. The **intense** colours of autumn, the smell of fresh bread and the sound of folk music create an unforgettable atmosphere. It is not **merely** a celebration of food but of community. Even the village **boss**, a strict woman who manages the cooperative, dances with everyone. Experts note that the population **density** here is low, but the sense of belonging is strong. \"You won't find this **anywhere** else,\" my grandfather says proudly."},"72":{"w":["photograph","profitable","restore","restrict","skin","so-called","solid","specialize","strategic","thick","translate","wood","academy","actress","command","delivery","depress","district","draft","edge"],"title":"The Actress Who Became a Teacher","t":"Ten years ago, Lin Fang was a successful **actress**, appearing in films and on stage across the country. Then she surprised everyone by leaving her career to found a drama **academy** for children in a poor **district** of the city.\n\nThe decision was not **profitable**. The academy charges low fees, and Lin often uses her own savings to keep it running. Yet she believes her work has a deeper value. \"Drama helps children build **solid** confidence,\" she says. \"It teaches them to **command** attention and express themselves.\"\n\nHer teaching methods are unusual. Students begin each class by standing at the **edge** of the stage, describing what they see and feel. They **translate** their emotions into movement and voice. Lin often asks them to write a **draft** of their own short plays, which they later perform for parents. She keeps a **thick** notebook of their best ideas and reminds them that good acting is more than **skin** deep. Whenever a **delivery** of donated costumes arrives, the children rush to try them on.\n\nSome parents were doubtful at first. They worried that drama would **restrict** time for \"serious\" subjects. But many soon noticed improvements in their children's reading and speaking. One mother said that her **so-called** shy son had become talkative and happy.\n\nLin also wants to **restore** old theatres in the city. She has started a **strategic** partnership with local businesses, which help pay for repairs. In one theatre, workers replaced the rotten **wood** floor and repainted the walls. A local newspaper published a **photograph** of the reopening ceremony. \"Life on stage can **depress** you if you only chase fame,\" Lin says. \"Here, I see real joy.\" She now plans to **specialize** in teaching drama to children with disabilities."},"73":{"w":["export","king","molecule","plot","silence","spoil","technical","theatre","ton","welfare","council","jam","seed","variable","accent","runner","comprehensive","instantly","pace","pave"],"title":"A Night at the Theatre","t":"Last weekend, I went to the city **theatre** to see a new play about a **king** who loses his kingdom. The **plot** was simple, but the acting was powerful. In the final scene, the stage fell into complete **silence** as the king stood alone, holding a handful of **seeds|seed**.\n\nThe play's director explained afterwards that the seeds were a symbol of hope. \"Even after everything is lost, something can grow again,\" she said. Her **comprehensive** research into history and philosophy had shaped the story. She compared the kingdom to a **molecule**: break one bond, and the whole structure changes. Several actors spoke with a strong **accent** from the north, which made the play feel authentic.\n\nThe production was a **technical** achievement. Lights changed **instantly** to show the passage of time, and the stage could be raised or lowered by several **tons|ton** of machinery. The **pace** of the play was fast, and the lead actor, a former marathon **runner**, needed all his energy to keep the audience engaged for two hours.\n\nThe theatre has a long history. It was built by the city **council** in the 1950s to promote culture and public **welfare**. In recent years, it has struggled financially, and some feared it might close. But new funding has helped to **pave** the way for a revival.\n\nNot everyone enjoyed the play. One critic complained that too many special effects might **spoil** the story. Others felt that the ending was too **variable**, changing from night to night. Still, tickets sold quickly, and a traffic **jam** formed outside the theatre before every performance. The producers now hope to **export** the play to theatres abroad."},"74":{"w":["reaction","slowly","surely","wonderful","adequate","beach","comparable","confirm","construct","deliberate","distinct","exception","fade","fruitful","insufficient","integrate","investigation","joint","laboratory","latter"],"title":"A Day at the Beach Laboratory","t":"On a quiet **beach** in the south, scientists run a small marine **laboratory** that studies coastal pollution. Visitors are welcome, and last summer I spent a day there. It turned out to be a **wonderful** and surprising experience.\n\nThe lab's director explained that their **investigation** focuses on plastic waste. Every morning, researchers walk **slowly** along the shore, collecting samples. They test the water to **confirm** whether chemicals are present. The results are worrying: pollution levels are rising, and current protections are **insufficient**.\n\nThe team is working on solutions. Scientists have helped to **construct** floating barriers that collect rubbish at river mouths. They also **integrate** data from satellites and local fishermen to track pollution. This **joint** effort has been **fruitful**, reducing waste on some beaches by half.\n\nThe work requires **deliberate** planning. Each project must be **comparable** with international standards so that results can be shared. Researchers must also show that their methods are **adequate** for long-term monitoring. There is no **exception**: every sample is checked twice.\n\nThe director pointed out two **distinct** problems: plastic from cities and fertiliser from farms. The **latter** causes harmful algae to grow, which can kill fish. Public **reaction** has been strong, and many local residents now volunteer at beach clean-ups. \"Hope does not **fade** easily here,\" she said. \"We will **surely** win this fight if we work together.\""},"75":{"w":["load","lock","originate","preparation","prize","relative","relax","sensible","sight","southeast","span","stable","uncomfortable","absorb","accuracy","attain","broken","certainty","companion","component"],"title":"The Long Walk Home","t":"Last spring, my friend Chen walked from Beijing to his hometown in the **southeast**, a journey that would **span** two months. His **preparation** took half a year. He studied maps, trained his body and packed a light **load** of clothes and food. \"I wanted to **attain** something difficult,\" he said. Before leaving, he put a heavy **lock** on his flat and gave the key to a neighbour.\n\nThe journey was not always comfortable. On some days, his feet hurt so badly that every step felt **uncomfortable**. Once, a **broken** shoe forced him to stop in a small town. He learned to **relax** and accept setbacks rather than fight them. A **sensible** approach, he discovered, was to walk slowly and rest often.\n\nChen met many people along the way. A farmer invited him to stay for dinner, and an old woman told him stories about her youth. He found a loyal **companion** in a stray dog that followed him for a week. \"Travel teaches you that kindness is a key **component** of life,\" he says.\n\nWalking also changed how he saw the world. He began to **absorb** details he had never noticed: the **sight** of mountains at sunrise, the smell of rain, the sound of birds. He kept a diary, writing with great **accuracy** about each day. Some of his ideas **originate** from these quiet moments.\n\nWhen he finally arrived home, his family was overjoyed. His mother, a **relative** told me, cried for an hour. Chen's story even won a **prize** in a travel writing competition. He says the journey gave him **certainty** about what matters most: family, health and a **stable** sense of purpose."},"76":{"w":["costly","discount","discrimination","dismiss","distant","emphasis","enthusiasm","equip","extraordinary","forever","former","frequency","guard","guidance","halt","hate","illusion","incline","logic","magic"],"title":"The Magic of a Good Teacher","t":"Many people remember a teacher who changed their lives. For me, it was Ms. Zhou, my **former** maths teacher. She had an **extraordinary** ability to make **logic** feel like **magic**. Students who once **hated|hate** maths left her classes full of **enthusiasm**.\n\nHer secret was simple: she placed **emphasis** on understanding rather than memorising. When a student made a mistake, she never **dismissed|dismiss** it as foolish. Instead, she offered gentle **guidance** and asked questions until the student found the answer. \"Mistakes are not failures,\" she said. \"They are steps.\"\n\nMs. Zhou also fought against **discrimination** in the classroom. In our school, some teachers believed that girls were less **inclined|incline** to study science. She refused to accept this **illusion** and encouraged every student equally. Several of her female students later became engineers.\n\nShe worked hard to **equip** us with practical skills. Using a small telescope, she showed us how the **frequency** of light waves explains colour. She took us on trips to **distant** villages to measure rivers and hills. Such activities were **costly**, so she persuaded local shops to offer a **discount** on equipment.\n\nWhen Ms. Zhou retired, the whole school came to say goodbye. A former student, now a security **guard** at a museum, gave a short speech. \"She taught us that learning should never **halt**,\" he said. \"We will remember her **forever**.\""},"77":{"w":["mere","ocean","organism","politics","practically","release","secret","settle","shame","shoulder","signature","sphere","steep","unusual","usual","variation","virus","vote","wipe","wire"],"title":"Life in the Deep Ocean","t":"The deep **ocean** is one of the least explored places on Earth. It covers most of our planet, yet scientists know **practically** nothing about many of its creatures. Each new expedition reveals an **unusual** **organism**, from glowing fish to giant worms.\n\nLife in the deep sea is extreme. There is no sunlight, the water is freezing and the pressure is enormous. Yet animals have found ways to **settle** there. Some produce their own light; others survive on chemicals released from hot springs on the ocean floor. Scientists study the **variation** between species to understand how life adapts.\n\nExploring the deep ocean is far from **usual** scientific work. Researchers use robots connected to ships by a long **wire**, and each dive can take hours. On one trip, a robot descended a **steep** underwater cliff and filmed a creature no one had seen before. The scientists named it after the ship's captain, who added his **signature** to the official report.\n\nThe ocean also faces serious threats. Pollution, overfishing and climate change put many species at risk. A **mere** rise of two degrees in water temperature could **wipe** out entire ecosystems. Some scientists have even discovered a **virus** that spreads among deep-sea fish as waters warm.\n\nProtecting the ocean is a matter of **politics** as well as science. International organisations **vote** on fishing limits, and governments **release** reports on marine health. Experts say every country must **shoulder** responsibility. \"It would be a **shame** to destroy something we have barely begun to understand,\" one researcher says. Her **secret** hope is that the deep sea becomes a protected **sphere** of science, not exploitation."},"78":{"w":["acknowledge","ambition","approval","camp","cattle","chocolate","conference","conventional","deadly","disaster","disclose","economics","experimental","fate","formula","hole","import","leisure","navigation","outer"],"title":"The Economics of Chocolate","t":"Few foods are as popular as **chocolate**. People around the world enjoy it as a sweet treat or a gift. But behind every chocolate bar lies a complex story of **economics**, trade and the **fate** of millions of farmers.\n\nMost cocoa is grown in West Africa, where small farmers sell their beans at low prices. Large companies **import** the beans, process them and sell chocolate at high profit. Experts say this system is **conventional** but unfair. At a recent international **conference**, speakers called for higher payments to farmers.\n\nSome companies have begun to **acknowledge** the problem. They now **disclose** where their cocoa comes from and pay extra for fair-trade beans. Their **ambition** is to make chocolate both delicious and ethical. Consumers have shown **approval**, and fair-trade chocolate sales are rising.\n\nClimate change adds new risks. Droughts and floods can be **deadly** for cocoa trees, and a single bad season can be a **disaster** for farmers. Scientists are testing an **experimental** **formula** for fertiliser that helps trees resist drought. Others are using satellite **navigation** to track changes in rainfall.\n\nCocoa farming also competes with **cattle** ranching for land, and forests are often cleared, leaving a **hole** in the ecosystem. Some farmers have joined training **camps|camp** where they learn sustainable methods. For consumers, chocolate is a **leisure** product, but for farmers it is a matter of survival. Next time you break open the **outer** wrapper of a chocolate bar, it is worth thinking about its journey."},"79":{"w":["penny","pile","protein","publication","recreation","retire","shadow","similarly","strain","theoretical","upward","broadcast","fur","legislation","nationality","observer","poll","racial","recovery","telescope"],"title":"Looking at the Stars","t":"For thousands of years, people have looked at the night sky and wondered what lies beyond. Today, powerful telescopes allow us to see distant galaxies, but for many amateur **observers|observer**, a small **telescope** in the backyard is enough to bring great joy.\n\nMy neighbour, a **retired|retire** engineer, spends his evenings studying the stars. He began after a long illness, and he says that astronomy played a big part in his **recovery**. \"When you look **upward**, your problems seem small,\" he says. He keeps a **pile** of star maps beside his window.\n\nAstronomy is not just a **recreation**; it also contributes to science. Amateur astronomers have discovered comets and helped professionals confirm **theoretical** predictions. Their observations are often included in scientific **publications|publication**. **Similarly**, citizen science projects allow anyone to help classify galaxies online.\n\nSadly, light pollution makes stargazing harder in cities. Bright streetlights cast a **shadow** over the night sky, hiding all but the brightest stars. Some countries have passed **legislation** to reduce light pollution, and a recent opinion **poll** showed strong public support.\n\nAstronomy also brings people together, regardless of **nationality** or **racial** background. Many astronomy clubs welcome members from every culture. Last month, a local radio station began to **broadcast** a weekly programme about the stars. My neighbour says the hobby costs hardly a **penny** beyond the cost of equipment. On cold nights he wears a hat lined with **fur**, and he eats **protein** bars to stay warm. Despite the **strain** on his eyes, he never misses a clear night."},"80":{"w":["transparent","universe","vessel","wing","airline","drill","generator","hamburger","rag","stone","accustomed","anyway","architect","automatically","bureau","classify","clever","disappoint","duty","float"],"title":"Inside an Airport","t":"A modern airport is like a small city. Thousands of people pass through it every day, and hundreds of staff work behind the scenes. Last month, I visited a major airport to learn how it operates, guided by an **architect** who helped design it.\n\nThe terminal is built of glass and **stone**, with a **transparent** roof that lets in natural light. The architect explained that the design was meant to make passengers feel calm. \"Flying makes many people nervous,\" she said. \"We wanted the building to **float** like a cloud.\"\n\nWe visited areas that passengers rarely see. In one room, staff **classify** luggage using machines that work **automatically**, sorting bags by destination. In another, workers maintain a backup **generator** that supplies power in emergencies. Every week, staff take part in a fire **drill** to practise safety procedures.\n\nThe airport also houses offices for each **airline** and a customs **bureau**, where officers check imported goods and collect **duty**. Some passengers are **disappointed|disappoint** to discover how much tax they must pay. The officers are **accustomed** to complaints, though. \"It's our job,\" one said. \"**Anyway**, most people are polite.\"\n\nNear the end of the tour, we stopped at a café where travellers ate **hamburgers|hamburger** and coffee. A cleaner wiped tables with a **rag**, moving quickly between customers. Outside, a plane spread its **wings|wing** on the runway, ready for take-off. The architect smiled. \"Every airport is a **vessel** of dreams,\" she said. \"It connects people across the whole **universe** of human experience.\" It was a **clever** way to end the tour."},"81":{"w":["illustration","independence","infer","proof","proud","resemble","steadily","thoughtful","tremendous","typical","useless","victim","virtually","whereas","worst","accommodate","adhere","anger","arrange","assign"],"title":"Growing Up and Moving Out","t":"For many young people, leaving home is a symbol of **independence**. It is a **typical** step after university, though the timing varies greatly between cultures. In some countries, young adults move out at eighteen, **whereas** in others they live with their parents until marriage.\n\nResearchers have tried to understand the effects of this transition. One study found that young people who live alone improve their life skills **steadily**: they learn to cook, manage money and **arrange** their own schedules. Many say they feel **proud** of these achievements. But others feel lonely and anxious, and some describe their first year away from home as the **worst** of their lives.\n\nParents also experience **tremendous** change. Some feel sad or even experience **anger** when their children leave. Others welcome the new freedom. Psychologists say it is important for families to stay connected, perhaps through regular calls or **thoughtful** messages.\n\nHousing costs make independence difficult. In many cities, rents are so high that small flats can barely **accommodate** one person. Young workers become **victims|victim** of rising prices and may feel that their efforts are **useless**. Experts **infer** from the data that many young people now **virtually** depend on their parents for years after graduation.\n\nStill, independence is worth pursuing. It teaches young people to **adhere** to their own values and **assign** priorities wisely. As an **illustration**, one young woman described how living alone helped her discover a love of cooking. \"My kitchen **resembles|resemble** a science lab,\" she laughed, \"but every meal is **proof** that I can look after myself.\""},"82":{"w":["blind","burden","characterize","comfort","commonly","composition","compromise","confine","counter","declare","exploit","fabric","favorable","guarantee","ignorant","inexpensive","inquiry","instinct","latin","loyalty"],"title":"The Story of Fabric","t":"Clothing has been part of human life for thousands of years, and the history of **fabric** tells us much about culture and trade. The earliest fabrics were made from animal skins and plant fibres, but over time people learned to weave cotton, wool and silk.\n\nSilk is a famous example. For centuries, China guarded the secret of silk production, and merchants carried silk across the ancient trade routes. In Europe, wealthy families paid huge sums for it, and **Latin** texts describe it as a luxury of the gods. Its value was so high that some rulers **declared|declare** laws restricting who could wear it.\n\nThe modern textile industry is very different. Many clothes are now **inexpensive**, produced quickly in large factories. But critics argue that this system can **exploit** workers, who often face low pay and long hours. An **inquiry** by a human rights group found that some factories **confine** workers for twelve hours a day. Many consumers remain **ignorant** of these conditions.\n\nSome brands have begun to change. They **guarantee** fair wages and use sustainable materials. Others **characterize** themselves as \"slow fashion,\" producing fewer but better clothes. Finding a **compromise** between low prices and fair treatment is a heavy **burden** for the industry.\n\nConsumers also play a role. Many people buy clothes based on **instinct** or emotion rather than need. Experts suggest choosing quality over quantity and showing **loyalty** to ethical brands. **Commonly**, the most **favorable** choice is to repair old clothes rather than buy new ones. Some shops even offer a repair **counter**, where tailors fix clothes for free. As one designer says, \"Fashion should bring **comfort**, not guilt.\" The **composition** of our wardrobes reveals our values, and we should not be **blind** to its impact."},"83":{"w":["manual","miracle","mystery","noisy","obligation","odd","opponent","pack","permanent","poem","prompt","request","rid","rigid","rock","royal","sample","secondary","seize","selection"],"title":"The Mystery of the Royal Rock","t":"In the hills of northern Scotland stands a huge **rock** that local people call the \"**Royal** Stone.\" According to legend, ancient kings were crowned there. For centuries, its origin has remained a **mystery**, but recent scientific research has begun to reveal its secrets.\n\nScientists collected a **sample** of the stone and found that it came from a region hundreds of kilometres away. How did ancient people move such a heavy object? Some believe it was a **miracle**; others suggest that people used **manual** labour, wooden rollers and great patience. The research team proposed a third theory: the rock was carried by glaciers during the Ice Age.\n\nNot everyone accepted the findings. An **opponent** of the theory, a local historian, argued that the evidence was too **rigid** and ignored local traditions. He felt an **obligation** to defend the legend. The debate became so **noisy** that the local council received a **request** for a public meeting.\n\nThe rock attracts many visitors, especially in summer. Tourists **pack** the narrow roads, and local businesses are **prompt** to sell souvenirs. Some visitors leave **odd** objects at the site, such as coins and flowers. Authorities have tried to **rid** the area of litter, and some have threatened to **seize** items left illegally.\n\nThe council now plans to give the rock **permanent** protection. A poet who grew up nearby wrote a famous **poem** about it, describing it as \"the silent king of the hills.\" For locals, its history is of **secondary** importance; what matters is its place in their hearts. A **selection** of photos and stories is now displayed in the village museum."},"84":{"w":["singer","smooth","southern","specialist","stake","surgery","trace","uncover","unfair","unfortunate","unknown","utility","well-known","widen","altitude","artistic","battery","boundary","cancel","cast"],"title":"A Surgeon Who Sings","t":"Dr. Chen is a **well-known** heart **specialist** at a hospital in the **southern** city of Guangzhou. He has performed thousands of operations, but his other talent remained largely **unknown** until a video of him went viral: he is also a gifted **singer**.\n\nDr. Chen began singing as a child. His parents encouraged his **artistic** side, but they also wanted him to become a doctor. He chose medicine, but he never gave up music. Today, he sings in a local choir and performs at charity concerts. \"Music and **surgery** are similar,\" he says. \"Both need **smooth**, careful movements and deep concentration.\"\n\nHis work at the hospital is demanding. He often has to **cancel** plans because of emergencies. Once, during a long operation, the hospital's power failed, and doctors relied on a backup **battery**. Dr. Chen stayed calm, finishing the procedure safely. Such moments remind him that lives are at **stake** every day.\n\nHe also campaigns for fairer health care. He believes it is **unfair** that people in remote areas lack access to good hospitals. He has helped to **widen** a programme that sends doctors to mountain villages at high **altitude**. \"It is **unfortunate** that geography decides who gets treatment,\" he says. \"We must **uncover** the reasons and fix them.\"\n\nDr. Chen sees no **boundary** between his two passions. A local film director plans to **cast** him in a documentary about doctors who make music. Asked about the **utility** of singing for a surgeon, he laughs. \"It keeps my heart healthy,\" he says. \"And I can **trace** every happy memory to a song.\""},"85":{"w":["commission","committee","confusion","criticism","drain","ease","elsewhere","extensive","fame","favorite","formal","generous","genuine","glance","hostile","intensity","intention","kilogram","ladder","mathematical"],"title":"A Ladder to Fame","t":"Many young people dream of **fame**. Social media has made it seem easy: post the right video, and millions may watch it. But experts warn that the path to success is rarely smooth, and fame often brings **confusion** and stress.\n\nConsider the story of a young **mathematical** genius who became famous online for solving puzzles. At first, he enjoyed the attention. Fans praised his **genuine** passion and **generous** spirit, as he often taught others for free. But soon, **criticism** followed. Some users became **hostile**, posting unkind comments. \"I didn't expect such **intensity**,\" he said.\n\nHis experience reflects a wider problem. A government **commission** recently studied the effects of online fame on young people. Its report, presented to a parliamentary **committee**, found that many influencers feel pressure to perform constantly. The **extensive** study recommended better education about online safety.\n\nPsychologists say the key is to stay grounded. \"Fame can **drain** your energy,\" one expert warns. \"Remember why you started.\" She advises young people to build a career step by step, like climbing a **ladder**, rather than chasing sudden success. Many people are surprised to learn that a **formal** education still matters, even for online stars.\n\nThe young genius has learned to manage the attention with greater **ease**. He now spends less time online and more time studying and exercising; he has lost several **kilograms|kilogram** and sleeps much better. His **favorite** activity is teaching children in his hometown. His **intention** is to become a university professor. When asked about his future, he gives a quick **glance** at his notebook and smiles. \"There are more problems to solve **elsewhere**,\" he says."},"86":{"w":["measurement","mislead","moderate","modest","motive","neighborhood","notion","organic","paint","patience","payment","peninsula","pet","plain","plantation","prominent","reference","relate","reserve","romantic"],"title":"A Quiet Neighborhood","t":"Ten years ago, my parents moved to a small **neighborhood** on a **peninsula** in southern China. It is not **prominent** on any map, but for them it is the perfect place to retire. Life there moves at a **moderate** pace, and the people are friendly and **modest**.\n\nMy mother loves gardening. She grows **organic** vegetables in a small garden that was once part of an old tea **plantation**. She has endless **patience** with her plants, checking the soil every morning. Her **motive**, she says, is simple: \"Food tastes better when you grow it yourself.\"\n\nMy father spends his time differently. He likes to **paint** landscapes of the sea and sells his work at a local market. He accepts **payment** in cash or vegetables, depending on the buyer. His paintings are **plain** but beautiful, and neighbours often hang them in their homes.\n\nThe community has its own traditions. Every family keeps a **pet**, usually a cat or a dog, and children walk the animals along the coast. Local people **relate** stories about storms and fishermen, and an old library has a **reference** section full of local history books. The community centre **reserves|reserve** one evening a week for music and dance.\n\nSome outsiders hold the **notion** that such places are boring. My parents disagree. \"Don't let fast city life **mislead** you,\" my father says. \"Happiness can't be measured by any **measurement** of wealth.\" My mother finds their life deeply **romantic**: she and my father still walk on the beach every evening at sunset."},"87":{"w":["rough","salt","satellite","scatter","session","sharply","shy","spark","sunlight","tank","tip","viewpoint","voyage","wander","youth","blaze","cash","crystal","detection","inner"],"title":"Sailing Around the World","t":"Last year, my friend Lin completed a **voyage** around the world in a small sailing boat. The journey took eleven months and covered more than forty thousand kilometres. As a **shy** child, she had never imagined such an adventure. \"Travel changed my **viewpoint** on life,\" she says.\n\nThe trip was not easy. Seas were often **rough**, and storms sometimes forced her to change course. She relied on a **satellite** phone to receive weather reports and talk to her family. Her fresh water came from a **tank** that she refilled at each port. During long nights, she would watch the stars and let her thoughts **wander**.\n\nLin learned many lessons along the way. A fisherman in Indonesia gave her a useful **tip**: always carry extra **salt** and dried food in case of emergencies. In Africa, she watched local guides **scatter** seeds on farmland after the rainy season. She kept her **cash** in different places on the boat to reduce risk.\n\nThere were frightening moments too. Once, a fire broke out in the kitchen, and a small **blaze** spread **sharply** before she put it out. Another time, early **detection** of a leak saved her from disaster. \"The ocean doesn't forgive mistakes,\" she says.\n\nLin has now returned home and gives talks about her journey. In every **session**, she describes the **crystal** blue water of the Pacific and the warm **sunlight** of the Indian Ocean. Her stories **spark** curiosity in young listeners. \"My **youth** taught me fear,\" she tells them. \"The sea taught me courage, and my **inner** strength grew with every wave.\""},"88":{"w":["instrument","invention","lunar","mercury","minister","monument","scare","scratch","sew","solar","spin","toss","vitamin","wooden","beam","berry","collapse","competent","owl","refrigerator"],"title":"A Night at the Science Museum","t":"Last weekend, our city science museum held a special \"night of discovery\" for families. Visitors could explore the exhibits after dark, using torches to find hidden displays. It was one of the most exciting events of the year.\n\nThe museum began with a section on space. Children looked through an old **instrument** used by astronomers centuries ago and learned about the **lunar** cycle. A model of the **solar** system showed the planets **spinning|spin** slowly around the Sun. One display explained how scientists measured temperature with **mercury** before modern thermometers.\n\nAnother room focused on great **inventions|invention**. Visitors saw a **wooden** printing machine, an early **refrigerator** and the first light **beam** used in cinema. A guide explained that many inventions began with mistakes. \"Even a **competent** scientist sometimes fails,\" she said. \"But failure can **collapse** old ideas and open new ones.\"\n\nThe museum also explored nature. In a dark room, a stuffed **owl** seemed to watch visitors from a tree. Children learned which **berry** is safe to eat and why the body needs each **vitamin**. A sudden noise from a hidden speaker was designed to **scare** visitors, and many children screamed with delight.\n\nAt the end of the night, families gathered at the museum's **monument** to famous scientists. A government **minister** gave a short speech about the importance of science education. Children were invited to **toss** coins into a fountain and make a wish. Before leaving, many stopped at a craft table, where they could **sew** small badges onto their bags or **scratch** patterns into bookmarks as souvenirs."},"89":{"w":["tide","affair","angle","assist","bar","brave","calculate","chemistry","collaborate","concentrate","craft","cure","curse","desperate","devise","distribute","emit","enthusiastic","error","evil"],"title":"The Chemistry of Teamwork","t":"Why do some teams succeed while others fail? Researchers who study workplace **affairs|affair** say the answer often lies in \"team **chemistry**\": the way people **collaborate** and support each other. Even talented individuals can fail if they cannot work together.\n\nA recent study examined teams in hospitals, factories and technology companies. Researchers **calculated|calculate** how often team members communicated and **distributed|distribute** tasks. They found that teams who met regularly made fewer **errors|error**. Members were more **enthusiastic** and better able to **concentrate** on difficult problems.\n\nGood leaders **assist** this process. They encourage open discussion and help team members see problems from a different **angle**. They also know how to **cure** conflict before it spreads. A manager who is **brave** enough to admit mistakes often earns greater trust.\n\nSome teams face extreme pressure. In emergencies, workers may feel **desperate** and exhausted. A team of firefighters described how they **devise** plans in seconds, relying on years of shared experience. They **emit** calm even in dangerous situations, a skill that comes from practice.\n\nNot everyone believes in team chemistry. Some critics call it a **curse** of modern management, a vague idea that hides poor planning. Others argue that the real **evil** is a culture of blame. In one company, the managers set a high **bar** for teamwork by rewarding groups rather than individuals. Experts compare good teams to the **tide**: steady, powerful and impossible to stop. Teamwork, they say, is a **craft** that must be learned."},"90":{"w":["existence","fierce","fleet","forth","gaze","handful","illustrate","insist","invisible","kingdom","leverage","likelihood","literary","modify","northern","noticeable","obscure","oneself","outline","overlook"],"title":"The Power of Stories","t":"Stories have been part of human **existence** for as long as we can remember. In every **kingdom** and culture, people have told tales to entertain, teach and **illustrate** moral lessons. Even today, stories shape how we see the world and **oneself**.\n\n**Literary** scholars point out that stories share a common **outline**. A hero faces a challenge, struggles against **fierce** obstacles and finally changes. This structure appears in ancient myths and modern films alike. A **handful** of basic plots, some say, explain almost every story ever told.\n\nStories also have practical power. Business leaders **leverage** stories to inspire employees, and politicians use them to win votes. Research shows that people remember facts better when they are part of a story. The **likelihood** of recalling information increases **noticeably|noticeable** when it is linked to emotion.\n\nSome stories are **obscure**, passed down in small villages and almost forgotten. Researchers in **northern** Europe have travelled to remote areas to record them before they disappear. One storyteller described a **fleet** of ghost ships that sailed **forth** each winter. Another told of an **invisible** forest spirit who protects children.\n\nWriters often **modify** old stories for modern audiences. They **insist** that the heart of a story never changes, even when details do. As readers, we should not **overlook** the quiet power of stories. When we **gaze** at the stars or listen to a grandparent's tale, we join a tradition as old as humanity itself."}},"cet6":{"1":{"w":["abbreviation","abide","abolish","abrupt","absorption","absurd","abundance","accessible","accomplishment","acquaint","acquisitive","addict","adjacent","adjoin","adjustable","admiration","adolescent","adoption","adore","advantageous"],"title":"The Library Next Door","t":"When I was an **adolescent**, the most important place in my life was the small public library **adjacent** to my school. It occupied two rooms that **adjoin** an old post office, and it was open until nine every evening. For a shy teenager, it offered an **abundance** of books and a quiet place to escape.\n\nI was a reading **addict**. I read everything I could find, from adventure novels to science magazines, and my **absorption** in books was so complete that I often forgot to go home for dinner. The librarian, Mrs. Ren, was the first adult I truly regarded with **admiration**. She seemed to know every book in the building, and she would **acquaint** new readers with authors they had never heard of.\n\nThe library had strict rules, and everyone had to **abide** by them: no food, no phones and no talking above a whisper. Some rules seemed **absurd** to us. Library cards, for example, carried a strange **abbreviation** that nobody understood, and late fees were so high that poor students avoided borrowing books at all.\n\nMrs. Ren worked hard to change this. She persuaded the city council to **abolish** late fees and to make the library more **accessible** to children from poor families. She also pushed for the **adoption** of a new computer system and bought **adjustable** chairs and desks for readers of all ages. Each change was a small **accomplishment**, but together they transformed the place.\n\nHer retirement was **abrupt**; one spring she fell ill and left without a farewell party. Many of us were heartbroken, and we still **adore** her today. Looking back, I realise how **advantageous** those years were for me. In a world that often seems **acquisitive**, obsessed with owning more, the library taught me that the most valuable things can be borrowed for free."},"2":{"w":["adverse","aerial","aerospace","aesthetic","affective","affiliate","affirm","afflict","affluence","aggravate","aggression","agitation","agreeable","alas","album","alien","alignment","allegation","allege","allegiance"],"title":"Pages from an Engineer's Album","t":"My aunt is an engineer in the **aerospace** industry, and she keeps a thick photo **album** of her career. It contains **aerial** photographs of rocket launches, pictures of satellites under construction and a few photos of her younger self smiling beside giant machines. For her, the album has great **affective** value: every image brings back strong feelings.\n\nShe grew up in a poor village, and the **affluence** of her life today still surprises her. As a student, she felt like an **alien** in the big city, where everyone seemed richer and more confident. But she had a talent for mathematics, and she was soon recruited by a research institute **affiliated|affiliate** with a top university.\n\nHer work requires extreme precision. A tiny error in the **alignment** of a satellite's solar panels can cause serious problems in space. Engineers must also consider the **aesthetic** design of public displays, she jokes, because politicians like rockets that look impressive.\n\nHer career has not been free of difficulty. Ten years ago, a colleague made an **allegation** that her team had hidden test failures. Reporters began to **allege** that the whole project was unsafe, and some of them behaved with surprising **aggression**, waiting outside her home at night. The **agitation** lasted for months. She felt **afflicted|afflict** by constant stress, and the pressure began to **aggravate** an old stomach problem.\n\nThroughout the investigation, she continued to **affirm** that her team had followed every rule. Her **allegiance** was always to the truth, not to her employer. In the end, investigators found no evidence against her, and the **adverse** publicity disappeared. \"**Alas**, the newspapers never printed an apology,\" she says with a smile. Today she is a calm and **agreeable** woman who rarely mentions those months, though one page of her album is left blank in their memory."},"3":{"w":["alleviate","alliance","allied","allocate","allowance","ally","alongside","alteration","alternate","amateur","ambassador","ambient","ambiguity","ambiguous","ambitious","amends","amid","ample","amplitude","amusement"],"title":"The Amateur Orchestra","t":"Every Thursday evening, about forty people meet in a school hall to play music together. They are an **amateur** orchestra: their members include teachers, doctors, shop owners and retired engineers, and none of them is paid. For many, the weekly rehearsal is the best way to **alleviate** the stress of daily life.\n\nThe orchestra began as an **alliance** between two local schools that wanted to share instruments and teachers. Over time, other **allied** groups joined, including a community choir and a dance club. The city council was an important **ally**, agreeing to **allocate** a small budget for sheet music and a monthly **allowance** for the conductor.\n\nThe conductor, Mr. Qiu, is **ambitious**. He wants the orchestra to perform a full symphony next year, and he works hard **alongside** the players to improve their skills. His instructions, however, are sometimes **ambiguous**. When he says \"play it more softly but with more feeling,\" players are often confused. To avoid **ambiguity**, the first violinist now writes his comments directly on the music.\n\nRehearsals are not always smooth. **Amid** the noise of traffic outside, some players struggle to hear each other, and the **ambient** sound in the old hall is poor. A physics teacher in the orchestra once explained how the **amplitude** of sound waves affects volume, and the group made an **alteration** to its seating plan so that quieter instruments sit closer to the audience.\n\nThe orchestra performs on **alternate** Sundays in a local park. There is **ample** space for families, and children dance with obvious **amusement**. Last year a visiting **ambassador** from Austria attended a concert and praised the players warmly. When a trumpeter played a wrong note during the anthem, he made **amends** by buying everyone coffee afterwards."},"4":{"w":["analogous","analogue","analogy","analyst","analytic","analytical","anniversary","annually","anode","anonymous","antarctic","antenna","antique","antisocial","appealing","appendix","applaud","appraisal","appraise","appreciable"],"title":"A Winter in Antarctica","t":"Every year, a small team of scientists spends the long polar winter at a research station in the **Antarctic**. The station is resupplied **annually**, and for eight months the scientists have no contact with the outside world except through radio and satellite links. A tall **antenna** on the roof is their lifeline.\n\nLife there is **analogous** to life on a spaceship. Space agencies have even studied the station as an **analogue** of a mission to Mars. One psychologist used a simple **analogy**: \"Living in Antarctica is like living inside a small box that you cannot leave.\" People who are naturally **antisocial** rarely last long, because the team must cooperate closely.\n\nThe members have very different jobs. One is a weather **analyst** who sends daily reports to scientists around the world. Another uses **analytic** software to study ice samples, which record changes in the climate over thousands of years. Their work requires an **analytical** mind and great patience. A chemist tests new batteries in the extreme cold, carefully measuring how the **anode** reacts at minus sixty degrees.\n\nThe winter can bring unexpected dangers. Last year, a young engineer suffered a sudden pain in his side; his **appendix** had become inflamed. With no way to fly him out, the station doctor performed the operation herself. Everyone stood outside the room and began to **applaud** when she came out smiling.\n\nSmall comforts make life more **appealing**. The station has an **antique** piano, left by an expedition decades ago, and the team celebrates the station's **anniversary** with a special dinner. At the end of the season, each member writes an **appraisal** of the experience. An **anonymous** donor recently paid for a new greenhouse, and scientists will **appraise** its results next spring. Even a small supply of fresh vegetables makes an **appreciable** difference to morale."},"5":{"w":["appreciation","apt","arc","arch","arctic","array","arrogant","articulate","ascend","ascertain","ashore","aspiration","ass","assassinate","assault","assert","assertive","assessment","asset","assimilate"],"title":"Journey to the Arctic","t":"Last summer, a team of young researchers sailed north to the **Arctic** to study how melting ice affects local wildlife. Their ship carried an impressive **array** of instruments, from underwater cameras to drones. For the team leader, Dr. Sun, the trip was the realisation of a lifelong **aspiration**.\n\nWhen they finally went **ashore** on a small island, the landscape took their breath away. Ice cliffs rose above the sea, and the wind had carved one of them into a natural **arch**. The sun moved in a low **arc** across the sky and never set. Dr. Sun said that \"silent\" was the most **apt** word to describe the place.\n\nThe team's first task was to **ascertain** how much the ice had retreated since the previous survey. Two members had to **ascend** a steep glacier with ropes and ice axes. Their **assessment** was worrying: the ice had shrunk faster than expected. Local guides said polar bears were being forced to **assimilate** to new hunting grounds.\n\nThe expedition had difficult moments. One member, an **arrogant** young scientist, refused to follow the guide's advice and almost fell into a crevasse. Afterwards he admitted he had been a stubborn **ass**. Strong winds launched a fierce **assault** on their tents one night, and everyone stayed awake until dawn. To pass the time, someone read aloud from a historical novel about a plot to **assassinate** a king.\n\nDr. Sun is calm but **assertive**. She does not hesitate to **assert** her authority when safety is at stake. She is also **articulate**, explaining complex science in simple words. Back home, she gave talks about the trip, and audiences showed great **appreciation** for her photographs. \"Our planet's ice is a priceless **asset**,\" she told them. \"Once it is gone, we cannot buy it back.\""},"6":{"w":["assimilation","assurance","astonishment","astronomy","athletic","attachment","attendance","attendant","attorney","augment","authentic","automate","avail","availability","avert","axial","axle","backbone","bacon","bacterium"],"title":"Saving the Old Observatory","t":"On a hill above our town stands a small observatory built in the 1930s. For decades it was the home of the local **astronomy** club, but ten years ago the city announced plans to sell the land to a developer. Club members reacted with **astonishment**: few of them had imagined that the building could disappear.\n\nThe club's chairman, a retired engineer named Mr. Fu, became the **backbone** of the campaign to save it. He hired an **attorney** who agreed to work for free, and the two of them examined every document relating to the site. The attorney gave him **assurance** that the original owner's will had donated the land to the public, and the sale might therefore be illegal.\n\nMeanwhile, the club worked to **augment** its membership. It organised open nights, and **attendance** rose from a dozen people to over two hundred. The **assimilation** of so many newcomers was not always easy, but young, **athletic** members carried heavy equipment up the hill and brought fresh ideas. One student wrote software to **automate** the telescope's movements; another repaired the rusty **axle** of the rotating dome, so that it could once again turn smoothly around its **axial** support.\n\nVisitors were delighted to discover that the main telescope was **authentic**, made in Germany in 1931. A volunteer **attendant** guided guests up the narrow stairs and explained the history of each instrument. On cold winter mornings, the volunteers shared a simple breakfast of **bacon** and eggs before going home.\n\nThe campaign was not quick. Early appeals to the city council were to no **avail**, and the **availability** of public money remained uncertain. But in the end, the court ruled in the club's favour, and the sale was cancelled. \"We managed to **avert** a disaster,\" Mr. Fu says. Even a lab test that found a harmful **bacterium** in the building's old water tank did not reduce the members' **attachment** to the place; they simply replaced the pipes."},"7":{"w":["badge","baffle","bait","bald","ballet","ballot","bamboo","bandage","bandit","banker","bankrupt","bankruptcy","banquet","barge","barley","barometer","baron","barren","basement","batch"],"title":"The Baron's House","t":"On the edge of our city stands a grand house built in the 1890s by a wealthy **banker**. He was later given the title of **baron** for his services to the government, and he loved to hold an elaborate **banquet** in the great hall for hundreds of guests. Today the house is a museum, and visitors can explore every room, from the painted ballroom to the dark **basement** where wine was once stored.\n\nThe baron's fortune came from trade. Every week, a **barge** loaded with grain and **barley** travelled down the river to his warehouses. He kept a brass **barometer** in his study and checked it every morning, because storms could delay his boats. A display case shows a silver **badge** he wore at official ceremonies.\n\nHis success did not last. In 1921, after a series of bad investments, the bank went **bankrupt**. The **bankruptcy** shocked the city, and many ordinary families lost their savings. Historians remain **baffled|baffle** by his decisions; some believe he was tricked by dishonest partners, who used false promises as **bait**. Others note that **bandits|bandit** robbed several of his grain shipments in the countryside that year.\n\nAfter the collapse, the baron lived quietly in two rooms of the house. Photographs from that time show a thin, **bald** old man sitting in a garden that had become **barren**. When the city held a **ballot** on whether to buy the property, citizens voted overwhelmingly in favour.\n\nToday the museum is full of life. A local dance school performs **ballet** in the ballroom each summer, and craftsmen sell **bamboo** baskets in the courtyard. Each new **batch** of schoolchildren hears the guide tell the same story: on the day of the bankruptcy, the baron fell on the stairs, and a servant wrapped his injured hand in a simple **bandage**. \"Money can vanish in a day,\" the guide says. \"Kindness lasts longer.\""},"8":{"w":["bazaar","bead","bearing","beetle","beneficiary","benign","bestow","beware","bewilder","bias","biased","bid","biotech","bishop","bitterness","bizarre","blacksmith","bleach","bleak","blink"],"title":"The Charity Bazaar","t":"Every December, a church in the old part of our city holds a charity **bazaar** to raise money for the children's hospital. The **bishop** opens the event with a short speech, and then hundreds of visitors crowd between the stalls. The hospital is the sole **beneficiary**, and last year the bazaar raised more than two hundred thousand yuan.\n\nThe stalls offer an amazing variety of goods. Elderly women sell necklaces made of glass **beads|bead**, students sell homemade cakes, and a local **blacksmith** demonstrates his craft, hammering red-hot iron into hooks and candle holders. One stall sells jewellery made from the shells of a shiny green **beetle**, which many visitors find **bizarre**.\n\nThe highlight is an auction. Last year, a painting donated by a famous artist attracted a **bid** of fifty thousand yuan from a **biotech** company. The auctioneer, a calm man with a dignified **bearing**, did not even **blink** when the price kept rising.\n\nNot everything goes smoothly. One year, two stall owners argued over the best location, and the **bitterness** lasted for months. Another year, visitors complained that the judges of the cake competition were **biased** in favour of their friends. To avoid any **bias**, the organisers now use blind tasting.\n\nThe weather in December is often **bleak**, with grey skies and a cold wind, but nothing keeps people away. Volunteers clean the church floors with **bleach** before and after the event, and signs warn visitors to **beware** of pickpockets in the crowd. Newcomers are sometimes **bewildered|bewilder** by the noise and colour, but they soon relax. The atmosphere is warm and **benign**, and at the end of the day the bishop will **bestow** a small prize on the most popular stall."},"9":{"w":["blond","blouse","blunder","blunt","bluntly","blur","blush","boiler","bonus","bourgeois","boycott","brace","breakdown","bribe","bridegroom","bridle","brightness","broaden","browse","brutal"],"title":"A Wedding Full of Surprises","t":"When my cousin Wei got married last spring, everything that could go wrong did go wrong. The **bridegroom**, my cousin, arrived late because his car suffered a **breakdown** on the motorway. Then the hotel's **boiler** failed, and guests had to wash in cold water on the coldest morning of the year. \"We should have **braced|brace** ourselves for disaster,\" he joked later.\n\nThe bride, a quiet **blond** woman from Norway, handled it all with grace. She had chosen a simple white **blouse** and a long skirt instead of an expensive dress, which surprised some relatives. My grandmother, who dislikes anything **bourgeois**, declared it \"the most sensible wedding outfit\" she had ever seen.\n\nEarlier, my aunt had threatened to **boycott** the wedding because the couple had refused to hold it in her hometown. My father spoke to her **bluntly**: \"Be **blunt** with me if you like,\" he said, \"but don't punish the children.\" She came in the end, though she complained about the **brutal** cold all day.\n\nThe best man made a terrible **blunder** in his speech, calling the bride by the name of Wei's former girlfriend. The room went silent, and he began to **blush** deeply. Fortunately, the bride laughed, and the moment passed. The hotel manager, embarrassed by all the problems, gave the staff a **bonus** for working extra hours and refused a guest's offer of money to fix the heating faster. \"We don't take a **bribe**,\" he said with a smile.\n\nThe final surprise came in the evening, when a horse-drawn carriage arrived; the horse wore a **bridle** decorated with flowers. In the **brightness** of the lanterns, everything seemed to **blur** into a golden dream. Now, when I **browse** the wedding photos, I laugh at every disaster. The day helped to **broaden** my idea of what a perfect wedding is."},"10":{"w":["buddhism","buffalo","bug","bugle","bulletin","bump","bureaucracy","burglar","burial","burner","bushel","butt","buzz","by-product","bypass","calculus","calibration","cane","capability","capacitance"],"title":"The Biogas Engineer","t":"After graduating with a degree in engineering, my friend Lan returned to her hometown, a farming village in the south, to build a biogas plant. The village had plenty of animal waste from pigs and **buffalo**, and Lan wanted to turn it into clean fuel. The idea created a real **buzz** among the villagers.\n\nHer first challenge was **bureaucracy**. It took six months and dozens of forms to get permission to build. She found a way to **bypass** some delays by working with the village committee, which posted a **bulletin** explaining the project. A few people made her the **butt** of their jokes, calling her \"the girl who cooks with manure.\"\n\nThe technical work demanded every **capability** she had. She used **calculus** to model the flow of gas through the pipes, and careful **calibration** of the pressure sensors was essential. One sensor failed because of a **bug** in its software; another gave strange readings because of changes in the **capacitance** of its circuit in humid weather.\n\nThe plant opened after a year. Each kitchen received a new gas **burner**, and the slurry left behind became a valuable **by-product**: rich fertiliser that increased rice yields by several **bushels|bushel** per field. Farmers also used it on their sugar **cane**.\n\nVillage life continues around the plant. The road is full of every **bump** and hole, and trucks travel slowly. An old man who once played the **bugle** in the army still plays it at the village festival. Most families follow **Buddhism**, and the monks at the temple blessed the plant on its opening day; they also asked Lan to move one pipe away from an ancient **burial** ground. When a **burglar** tried to steal copper wire from the site, villagers caught him within an hour. \"Here,\" Lan says, \"everybody watches over everybody.\""},"11":{"w":["capacitor","cape","capsule","cartoon","cartridge","casualty","catalyst","catastrophe","category","cater","catholic","causal","caution","cavity","censor","centralize","ceramic","cereal","charcoal","charm"],"title":"The Night the Typhoon Came","t":"Our small fishing town sits on a rocky **cape** that points into the South China Sea. Typhoons are common here, but the storm that struck three years ago was a true **catastrophe**. It was classified in the highest **category**, with winds of over two hundred kilometres an hour.\n\nThe town had received warnings two days earlier, and the mayor urged extreme **caution**. Families were told to move to the old **Catholic** church on the hill, whose thick stone walls had survived storms for over a century. Volunteers carried bags of rice, boxes of **cereal** and sacks of **charcoal** for cooking, and a local restaurant agreed to **cater** for everyone in the shelter.\n\nThat night, the wind tore roofs from houses and flooded the harbour. Remarkably, there was only one **casualty**: a fisherman who broke his arm while securing his boat. Experts later explained that the low number of injuries had a clear **causal** link to the early evacuation.\n\nInside the church, people tried to stay calm. Children watched a **cartoon** on a laptop until its battery died, and an old radio stopped working when a **capacitor** burned out. A retired electrician repaired it with parts from a broken television. When the printer ran out of ink, someone found a spare **cartridge** so that volunteers could print lists of missing people.\n\nThe storm became a **catalyst** for change. The government decided to **centralize** emergency planning and built a new shelter with solar power. Some officials tried to **censor** reports criticising the slow repairs, but journalists published them anyway. The storm also exposed a large **cavity** beneath the old sea wall, which engineers have since filled.\n\nToday, a **ceramic** plaque on the church wall honours the volunteers, and pupils have buried a time **capsule** beside it, filled with letters about that night. The town has kept its old **charm**, but people no longer take the sea for granted."},"12":{"w":["charter","chasm","chatter","cherish","chestnut","chip","chord","chorus","chronic","chronicle","cigar","cigaret","circulation","circus","cite","clamp","clash","clatter","cleanliness","clearing"],"title":"Life with the Travelling Circus","t":"For six years, my uncle worked with a travelling **circus** that toured small towns across the north of the country. The company moved every week on a **charter** bus, followed by trucks carrying tents, animals and equipment. Whenever they arrived, they set up their big tent in a forest **clearing** or on an empty football field.\n\nHe loved the life, though it was never easy. The **chatter** of performers filled the camp from morning till night, and the **clatter** of hammers and metal poles began at dawn. The ringmaster smoked a fat **cigar** during rehearsals, while the acrobats rolled their own cigarettes; one old poster even advertised \"no **cigaret** smoking inside the tent.\" Despite the chaos, the manager insisted on strict **cleanliness**, and every animal cage was cleaned twice a day.\n\nNot everyone got along. A violent **clash** between two clowns once nearly ended the season, and the manager had to **clamp** down on fighting. Some performers suffered **chronic** back pain from years of hard training, and money was always a problem. Between the old performers and the young ones there seemed to be a **chasm** of different values.\n\nBut there were moments of pure magic. At the end of every show, the whole company sang a **chorus** together, and the band played a final **chord** that made the audience cheer. In autumn, children sold roasted **chestnuts|chestnut** outside the tent, and the smell of hot potato **chips|chip** filled the air.\n\nMy uncle kept a diary, a **chronicle** of every town they visited. A local newspaper with a wide **circulation** later published parts of it, and historians now **cite** it as a rare record of circus life. He still **cherishes|cherish** those years and says they taught him that joy is the best medicine."},"13":{"w":["client","climax","cling","clinic","clinical","clip","clockwise","closet","clown","cluster","clutch","coalition","coastal","coefficient","coffin","cognitive","coherent","coincide","coincidence","collaboration"],"title":"The Hospital Clown","t":"Every Tuesday, a man with a red nose and enormous shoes walks into the children's **clinic** at our city hospital. He is a professional **clown**, and his job is to make sick children laugh. Doctors say his visits have real **clinical** value: laughter reduces stress and can even help patients recover faster.\n\nHe knows this from experience. Ten years ago, he was seriously injured in a car crash on a **coastal** highway. He remembers how he would **clutch** the hand of a nurse during painful treatment and **cling** to the hope that he would walk again. \"I was nearly put in a **coffin**,\" he jokes. A hospital clown visited him every week, and he decided that if he recovered, he would do the same for others.\n\nHis work is carefully planned. He meets with doctors before each visit to learn about every young **client**, and he adapts his performance to each child's age and **cognitive** abilities. He carries a **closet** full of costumes and props, and he keeps funny photos in a **clip** on his belt. One favourite trick involves a toy clock whose hands run **clockwise**, then backwards, while he pretends to travel in time.\n\nThe programme is a **collaboration** between the hospital and a **coalition** of charities. Researchers have even measured its effects. They found a strong correlation **coefficient** between the number of visits and the children's mood scores, and they published a **coherent** report recommending clown visits in every children's hospital.\n\nThe **climax** of each visit comes when a **cluster** of children gathers around him for a magic show. By **coincidence**, his first day at the hospital happened to **coincide** with his own birthday, and the children made him a card. He has kept it ever since."},"14":{"w":["collide","colonial","colonist","combat","combustion","commence","commend","commentary","commodity","commonplace","commonsense","commonwealth","commute","compact","compatibility","compatible","compatriot","compensate","compensation","competence"],"title":"Commuting in a Former Colony","t":"Two years ago, my friend Xin moved to Singapore to work as an engineer. Like many young professionals there, she faces a daily **commute** of nearly an hour, first by bus and then by train. She uses the time to read the news or listen to the morning **commentary** on the radio.\n\nThe city is a fascinating mix of old and new. Its centre is full of **colonial** buildings from the nineteenth century, when British **colonist** families controlled the island's trade. Singapore is still a member of the **Commonwealth**, and English remains the main language of business. Xin has met many **compatriots|compatriot** from China, and they often share meals together at weekends.\n\nHer work involves improving public transport. She studies how buses and trains might **collide** in crowded junctions and designs systems to **combat** delays. Recently, her team tested a new electric bus that produces no **combustion** emissions. Its design is so **compact** that it can turn easily in narrow streets.\n\nShe has learned a lot about teamwork. **Compatibility** between team members matters as much as technical skill, she says. At first, she found it hard to work with a manager whose habits were not **compatible** with hers, but they gradually learned to respect each other. Her manager has since begun to **commend** her **competence** in public meetings.\n\nLife abroad also brings problems that are **commonplace** for migrants. Rent is high, and food is a costly **commodity**. When her flight home was cancelled last winter, the airline offered **compensation** of five hundred dollars, though it could not **compensate** for missing the Spring Festival. Her advice to newcomers is **commonsense**: be patient, ask questions and stay in touch with your family. A new project will **commence** next month, and she is excited to begin."},"15":{"w":["competitor","complement","completion","complexity","complication","compliment","comply","composer","composite","comprehend","compression","compulsory","concede","conceit","conceive","concentrated","conception","concession","concise","confer"],"title":"Writing a Symphony","t":"For three years, a young **composer** named Lu has been working on her first symphony. Last spring, she finally reached its **completion**, and the city orchestra agreed to perform it. \"I could hardly **comprehend** it,\" she says. \"It felt like a dream.\"\n\nThe idea for the symphony was **conceived|conceive** during a long train journey across western China. Her original **conception** was simple: four movements describing four seasons in the desert. As she wrote, however, the **complexity** of the work grew. She added parts for traditional instruments to **complement** the orchestra, creating a **composite** sound that mixes Eastern and Western traditions.\n\nThe process brought many a **complication**. Lu had to **comply** with strict rules set by the orchestra, which required that the piece last no more than forty minutes. She was forced to **concede** that some sections were too long, and she made a painful **concession** by cutting her favourite passage. \"Good music must be **concise**,\" her teacher told her. \"Every note must earn its place.\"\n\nShe also learned to avoid **conceit**. When an early recording received praise online, she was tempted to stop revising. But a friend warned her that a **competitor** in an international contest had written a similar piece, and she decided to work harder. She spent weeks in **concentrated** effort, sometimes practising for twelve hours a day. To send recordings to the orchestra, she used audio **compression**, though she always preferred live sound.\n\nOn the opening night, the audience stood and cheered. The music critic of a major newspaper paid her a rare **compliment**, calling the piece \"fresh and fearless.\" Next year, the music academy will **confer** an award on her. Lu now teaches a **compulsory** course on composition, and she tells her students that patience matters more than talent."},"16":{"w":["confidential","configuration","confirmation","conform","conformity","confront","confrontation","congest","conqueror","consecutive","consensus","consequent","consistently","console","consolidate","conspicuous","constituent","constitute","constrain","constraint"],"title":"The Battle over the Old Square","t":"In the centre of our city lies an old square dominated by a bronze statue of a medieval **conqueror** on horseback. For years, cars and buses have crowded the roads around it, and traffic has grown worse for five **consecutive** years. During rush hour, the streets become so **congested|congest** that a short trip can take forty minutes.\n\nLast year, the city council proposed a bold plan: close the square to cars and turn it into a pedestrian zone. A **confidential** report, later leaked to the press, showed that pollution in the area **consistently** exceeded national limits. The **consequent** public anger forced the council to act.\n\nNot everyone welcomed the idea. Shop owners feared they would lose customers, and several of them decided to **confront** the mayor at a public meeting. The **confrontation** was tense, but the mayor listened patiently. She tried to **console** worried business owners, promising financial support during the changes. \"Our **constituents|constituent** deserve clean air,\" she said, \"but we must also protect their jobs.\"\n\nReaching a **consensus** took months. Planners had to work within strict **constraints|constraint**, since a lack of money and space would **constrain** every design. Engineers tested several **configurations|configuration** of bus routes on a computer **console** before choosing the best one. The final plan had to **conform** to national safety rules, and some critics complained that the city was demanding too much **conformity** from local businesses.\n\nEventually, the council received **confirmation** that the national government would fund the project. The new square opened last month. Trees and benches now **constitute** most of the space, and a **conspicuous** fountain stands beside the statue. The mayor hopes to **consolidate** the plan's success by closing two more streets next year."},"17":{"w":["consul","consultancy","consultant","contaminate","contamination","contemplate","contemptuous","contend","context","continental","contradict","contradictory","contrive","contrived","controversial","controversy","convene","converge","converse","convict"],"title":"The Poisoned River","t":"Three years ago, farmers in a valley in the south noticed that their crops were dying and their fish were disappearing. Tests revealed that a chemical factory upstream had begun to **contaminate** the river. The **contamination** spread quickly, because three smaller streams **converge** just below the factory.\n\nThe case quickly became **controversial**. The factory's owners hired an international **consultancy** to defend them, and its **consultant** argued that the pollution came from natural sources. Local scientists **contended|contend** that this was nonsense; their data showed a clear connection with the factory's waste pipe. The two reports were completely **contradictory**, and each side accused the other of trying to **contrive** evidence.\n\nThe **controversy** attracted attention far beyond the valley. A foreign **consul**, whose country had invested in the factory, visited the area and met with farmers. Journalists noted the **contemptuous** tone of the factory managers, who refused to **converse** with villagers and dismissed their complaints as \"emotional.\" One manager's apology, written by a public relations firm, sounded so **contrived** that it made people even angrier.\n\nTo understand the case, it helps to consider the wider **context**. Across the region, rapid industrial growth had brought jobs but also serious environmental damage, a pattern seen in many **continental** economies. Officials had often ignored the problem.\n\nEventually, the provincial government decided to **convene** a special court. The judge reviewed the evidence carefully, and no witness was able to **contradict** the scientists' findings. The court **convicted|convict** two managers of breaking environmental laws and ordered the company to clean the river. Today the water is clear again. Many farmers now **contemplate** the future with cautious hope."},"18":{"w":["conviction","convincing","cooperative","corporate","corps","correctly","correlate","correlation","correspondence","corrode","corrosion","corrupt","cosmic","cosmos","costume","couch","counsel","counseling","counteract","counterpart"],"title":"From Office to Planetarium","t":"For fifteen years, Mr. He worked in a large **corporate** office, writing financial reports and attending endless meetings. He had a good salary, but he felt empty. In the evenings, he would lie on his **couch** and read books about the **cosmos**, dreaming of a different life.\n\nFive years ago, he made a decision. With a group of friends, he set up a **cooperative** that runs a small planetarium for schoolchildren. Every member owns a share of the business and has an equal vote. \"I had a deep **conviction** that science should be fun,\" he says. \"Children need to see the beauty of **cosmic** events with their own eyes.\"\n\nThe planetarium is in an old factory building, and renovating it was difficult. Years of rain had caused metal beams to **corrode**, and engineers had to treat the **corrosion** before installing the dome. Mr. He spent hours on **correspondence** with suppliers, and he sometimes discovered that his **counterpart** at a supplier had sent the wrong parts.\n\nThe shows are popular. Actors wear **costumes|costume** of famous astronomers, and a **corps** of volunteer students helps visitors find their seats. Teachers say that children who attend tend to perform better in science; one study found a strong **correlation** between visits and test scores, though it is hard to **correlate** the two **correctly** without more data.\n\nMr. He's life has not been free of trouble. A **corrupt** official once demanded money for a building permit, and he refused. He later sought legal **counsel** and received **counseling** to deal with the stress. \"The best way to **counteract** fear,\" he says, \"is to look up at the stars. They remind you how small our problems are.\" His argument is **convincing**: every year, more people leave the city lights to visit his dark, quiet dome."},"19":{"w":["courageous","courteous","courtesy","cozy","crab","cradle","crank","credential","crippling","crisp","criterion","crooked","cruelty","cruise","cuckoo","culminate","cumulative","curative","curb","curly"],"title":"The Café by the Harbour","t":"At the end of a **crooked** street near the old harbour, there is a small café run by a woman named Mrs. Lin. It is a **cozy** place with wooden tables, warm lights and a clock with a little **cuckoo** that sings every hour. Tourists from passing **cruise** ships often stop there, attracted by the smell of fresh bread.\n\nMrs. Lin is famous for two things: her **crisp** fried **crab** and her kindness. She is always **courteous** to customers, even rude ones, and she treats every guest with the same **courtesy**. Her husband, a cheerful man with **curly** grey hair, still uses an old coffee grinder with a hand **crank**.\n\nTheir story is more dramatic than most visitors realise. Ten years ago, Mrs. Lin suffered a **crippling** illness that kept her in bed for months. The **cumulative** cost of treatment almost forced the family to sell the café. She tried every medicine, including traditional herbs said to have **curative** power, and she slowly recovered. Her doctors called her **courageous**.\n\nDuring those months, she thought deeply about her life. She had once worked in a big hotel, where she saw a manager treat staff with shocking **cruelty**. She promised herself that if she ever ran a business, kindness would be its main **criterion** for success.\n\nToday, she hires young people who have no formal **credentials|credential** and trains them herself. She says the town where she grew up was the **cradle** of her values: honest, simple and generous. She works hard to **curb** waste, giving leftover food to the poor every evening. Her efforts **culminated|culminate** last year in a city award for community service, but she says the best reward is a full café on a rainy afternoon."},"20":{"w":["currency","currently","curriculum","curt","customary","cutter","cylinder","cynical","damn","datum","dazzle","deafen","dealer","dean","deceptive","decidedly","decimal","decisive","declaration","decompose"],"title":"The New Dean","t":"Last autumn, our engineering faculty welcomed a new **dean**, Professor Han. She had spent twenty years working in industry, and she arrived with **decidedly** different ideas from her predecessor. Many older teachers were **cynical**. \"New leaders always promise change,\" one said, \"and nothing ever happens.\"\n\nProfessor Han proved them wrong. Within a month, she made a **decisive** announcement: the faculty would redesign its entire **curriculum**. Students would spend less time memorising formulas and more time building real machines. \"A single **datum** is useless,\" she said, \"unless you know what to do with it.\" She issued a **declaration** of goals, which included opening a new workshop by the end of the year.\n\nThe workshop **currently** contains machines that **dazzle** first-year students: a laser **cutter**, 3D printers and an engine test room where a steel **cylinder** spins at enormous speed. The noise can **deafen** anyone who forgets to wear ear protection. A local car **dealer** donated two old engines, and students take them apart to see how every part works.\n\nNot every change was popular. Some teachers found her **curt** emails rude, and it was **customary** for staff to discuss decisions over long meetings, which she often cancelled. One professor muttered \"**damn** efficiency\" after a meeting lasted only ten minutes. Others warned that her plans were **deceptive**: they looked simple but required huge budgets in a foreign **currency** for imported equipment.\n\nBut results speak for themselves. In a chemistry project last term, students designed a system that uses bacteria to **decompose** plastic waste. In another, first-year students built a calculator that converts **decimal** numbers into binary code. Professor Han smiles when people praise her. \"I didn't do anything,\" she says. \"I just opened the doors.\""},"21":{"w":["decorative","decree","dedicate","deduct","deem","default","defendant","deferential","deficiency","deficient","deficit","deflect","deflection","deform","deformation","defy","degenerate","degradation","degrade","delicacy"],"title":"The Bridge on Trial","t":"Five years ago, a new bridge opened across the river in our city. It was praised for its elegant design and **decorative** lamps, and the mayor said it was **dedicated|dedicate** to the workers who built it. But within two years, engineers noticed something alarming: the main beam had begun to bend under heavy traffic.\n\nTests showed that the **deflection** of the beam was far greater than expected. Over time, the steel continued to **deform**, and cracks appeared in the concrete. Experts concluded that the **deformation** was caused by a serious **deficiency** in the materials. The steel used in the beam was **deficient** in strength, and rain had caused the concrete to **degrade** faster than normal.\n\nThe city took the construction company to court. The company, the **defendant** in the case, tried to **deflect** blame onto the designers. Its lawyers argued that the bridge had been used in ways that **defy** common sense, such as carrying trucks far heavier than allowed. But witnesses told a different story. One engineer, speaking in a quiet and **deferential** tone, admitted that managers had ordered workers to use cheaper steel to reduce costs.\n\nThe company was already in financial trouble. It had a large budget **deficit** and had gone into **default** on several bank loans. Some observers argued that its culture had begun to **degenerate** long before the bridge was built, as managers cared more about profits than safety.\n\nAfter months of hearings, the judge issued a **decree**: the company must pay for repairs, and the city would **deduct** the cost from any money it still owed. The judge **deemed|deem** the managers' behaviour a serious breach of public trust. The case showed how the slow **degradation** of standards can endanger lives. Handling public anger was a matter of great **delicacy** for the city, but the repaired bridge reopened last month, stronger than before."},"22":{"w":["delusion","demography","denial","denote","denounce","dentist","dependant","depict","deploy","deportation","deposition","deprive","derivation","descendant","descent","designate","desolate","despatch","desperately","dessert"],"title":"Tracing a Family's Roots","t":"Last year, my cousin decided to study our family history. She is a **descendant** of a farming family from Shandong, and she wanted to understand the **derivation** of our unusual surname. Her research took her through dusty archives, old letters and **desolate** villages that most people have forgotten.\n\nShe began with **demography**. Population records showed that our ancestors moved north in the 1890s, part of a mass migration caused by famine. Many families were **deprived|deprive** of their land and had to start again with nothing. Some records **denote** the number of each family's **dependants|dependant**, and she found that one ancestor had supported eleven relatives on a small farm.\n\nThe most moving discoveries were letters. One great-grandfather, a **dentist** in a small town, wrote to his brother in **desperately** sad words after his wife died. Another letter, sent by military **despatch** in 1938, described how soldiers were **deployed|deploy** near the village during the war. A third **depicted|depict** the family's **descent** into poverty after a flood destroyed their house. The flood also left a thick **deposition** of mud across the fields, which took years to clear.\n\nNot every relative welcomed the project. Some lived in **denial** about painful parts of our past, and one uncle insisted that our family had been wealthy landowners. \"That's a **delusion**,\" my cousin said gently. She also discovered that an ancestor had once publicly **denounced|denounce** a corrupt official, and that the official had arranged the **deportation** of his whole family to a distant province.\n\nWhen the research was complete, she **designated|designate** a room in her flat as a family archive. At our New Year dinner, she shared what she had learned. Over **dessert**, the room fell silent as she read the old letters aloud."},"23":{"w":["destine","destiny","detach","detain","detective","detector","deteriorate","deviate","deviation","devotion","devour","diagnose","diagnostic","dictator","diesel","diffuse","dignity","dilemma","diligence","dilute"],"title":"The Detective Writer","t":"Ms. Wei writes **detective** novels, and readers **devour** them as soon as they appear. Her hero, an elderly policewoman, solves crimes with patience, **diligence** and a sharp eye for detail. Ms. Wei says her character was **destined|destine** to be old: \"Young detectives run; old ones think.\"\n\nHer latest book is set in a fictional country ruled by a **dictator**. The heroine is **detained|detain** at the border while investigating a murder and must decide whether to cooperate with the secret police or protect a witness. \"Every good mystery contains a moral **dilemma**,\" the author explains. \"The question is not only who did it, but what is right.\"\n\nMs. Wei researches every detail. For her new book, she visited a hospital to learn how doctors **diagnose** poisoning and spent days studying **diagnostic** tests. She also learned how a metal **detector** works and how a small **diesel** engine can be modified to start a fire. Her **devotion** to accuracy is famous among her readers.\n\nShe admits that writing is a lonely job. During long periods of work, she must **detach** herself from family life, and she has noticed that her health can **deteriorate** if she works too late. She tries to follow a fixed routine and never **deviate** from it; even a small **deviation**, she says, can destroy her concentration.\n\nHer books also explore deeper themes. In the latest one, the heroine refuses to lose her **dignity**, even in prison. Ms. Wei believes that crime fiction can **diffuse** important ideas about justice to a wide audience without lecturing them. \"If you **dilute** the story with too many messages, readers stop reading,\" she warns. \"But if you trust them, they understand.\" She does not believe in **destiny**; she believes in hard work."},"24":{"w":["diminish","dine","dioxide","diploma","diplomatic","directory","disagreement","disappearance","disapproval","disastrous","discern","discontented","discourse","discrepancy","discrete","disgrace","disillusion","disillusioned","dismay","disobedience"],"title":"The Diplomat's Daughter","t":"My friend Ling grew up as the daughter of a **diplomatic** officer, moving to a new country every three years. Her childhood was exciting, but it was not easy. Every move meant new schools, new languages and new friends, and she often felt **discontented** and lonely.\n\nHer father dealt with serious international issues. At one conference on climate change, he spent days negotiating limits on carbon **dioxide** emissions. The talks nearly collapsed when a **disagreement** broke out over the figures; there was a large **discrepancy** between two countries' data. Her father had to **discern** which numbers were accurate before the talks could continue.\n\nAt home, Ling sometimes rebelled. As a teenager in Paris, she skipped school for a week, and her **disobedience** caused her parents great **dismay**. Their **disapproval** was clear, but they did not shout. Instead, they invited her to **dine** with them at a quiet restaurant and asked her to explain her feelings. \"I realised they were also tired of moving,\" she remembers.\n\nAs she grew older, Ling became **disillusioned** with the glamorous image of diplomatic life. She saw how public **discourse** about international relations often ignored ordinary families. One case left a deep impression: the sudden **disappearance** of a journalist her father knew, which ended in tragedy. The incident was **disastrous** for relations between two countries, and her father called it a **disgrace**.\n\nToday Ling works as a teacher. She earned her **diploma** in education and keeps a **directory** of former classmates scattered across five continents. Her experience has not made her bitter; it did **disillusion** her about power, but it did not **diminish** her belief in people. She tells her students that the world is not a set of **discrete** nations but one connected community."},"25":{"w":["disorient","dispatch","dispense","disperse","displace","displacement","disposition","disproportionate","disregard","disrupt","disruptive","dissatisfaction","disservice","dissipate","distil","distinctive","distinctly","distort","distortion","distract"],"title":"The Open-Plan Office","t":"When our company moved to a new building last year, managers introduced an open-plan office. The walls between departments disappeared, and two hundred employees now share one large room. The idea was to **disperse** information more quickly and to encourage teamwork, but the result was **distinctly** mixed.\n\nFrom the first week, there was clear **dissatisfaction**. Phones rang constantly, colleagues chatted loudly, and people found it hard not to **distract** one another. For staff who need to concentrate, the noise was **disruptive**. Even the sound from the coffee machine, which can **dispense** thirty cups an hour, seemed to **disrupt** meetings.\n\nThe change also affected different groups in a **disproportionate** way. Engineers and writers, who need quiet, suffered most. The staff in the **dispatch** department, who send hundreds of parcels every day, found that the noise **distorted|distort** their phone calls. Some employees felt the new layout had been designed with complete **disregard** for their needs. One manager admitted that the old office's **distinctive** character had been lost.\n\nNew employees sometimes felt **disoriented|disorient**. The room was so large that people got lost looking for their teams. Older staff complained of the **displacement** of familiar routines, as if they had been **displaced|displace** from their own homes. A survey showed that the excitement of the new building quickly began to **dissipate**.\n\nFinally, the company hired a consultant, who tried to **distil** the problem into one sentence: \"You have done your staff a **disservice** by ignoring how they actually work.\" He warned that complaints should not be treated as a **distortion** of the truth. The company has since built quiet rooms and allowed flexible working hours. Our manager, a person of calm **disposition**, says the lesson is simple: listen before you build."},"26":{"w":["disturbance","diverge","diversify","diversion","divert","divine","dizzy","doctrine","documentary","domain","dome","dominant","donate","doom","dormant","dove","downturn","drainage","drastic","drawback"],"title":"A Town Reinvents Itself","t":"For most of the twentieth century, our town depended on a single coal mine. When the mine closed in 2010, many people believed the town was **doomed|doom**. The economic **downturn** was severe: unemployment doubled, young people left, and shops closed one after another.\n\nThe local government knew that **drastic** action was needed. Its plan was to **diversify** the economy by developing tourism, farming and small industries. The town's most famous building, an old temple with a golden **dome**, became the centre of the new strategy. Its monks agreed to open the temple to visitors, and a television company made a **documentary** about its history and religious **doctrine**.\n\nThe temple stands on a hill above a **dormant** volcano, and legend says the site has **divine** protection. Every morning, hundreds of white **doves|dove** circle the dome. Visitors who climb the narrow tower often feel **dizzy**, but the view across the valley is worth it.\n\nThe plan had its **drawbacks|drawback**. Building new roads caused noise and **disturbance** for residents, and engineers had to **divert** a stream to make room for a car park. The **diversion** of the water damaged the old **drainage** system, and several streets flooded during the summer rains. Opinions began to **diverge** sharply: some residents supported tourism, while others feared it would destroy the town's quiet character.\n\nToday, the results are encouraging. Tourism has become the **dominant** industry, and a local company has registered an internet **domain** to sell handmade crafts online. Former miners now work as guides, and wealthy visitors often **donate** money to repair the temple. \"We lost the mine,\" the mayor says, \"but we found a new future.\""},"27":{"w":["dreadful","dubious","duke","dully","duplicate","dwarf","dwell","dynamical","dynamo","earnings","earthenware","eastward","eccentric","eclipse","ecology","economically","ecosystem","edible","edit","ego"],"title":"The Eccentric Duke","t":"Two hundred years ago, a wealthy English **duke** built a grand house on a hill and surrounded it with a huge private forest. He was famously **eccentric**: he slept during the day, worked at night and refused to allow anyone to cut down a single tree. Neighbours considered his ideas **dubious**, but today his forest is one of the richest **ecosystems|ecosystem** in the country.\n\nThe duke was passionate about nature long before **ecology** became a science. He kept detailed notebooks recording the plants and animals that **dwell** in the forest, and he identified hundreds of **edible** mushrooms and berries. He also built a small **dynamo** powered by a stream to light his study, one of the first in the region, and wrote papers on the **dynamical** behaviour of water.\n\nHis personal life was less happy. He had a large **ego** and argued constantly with his family. His son described him as a **dreadful** father who spoke **dully** at dinner and thought only of his work. When the duke died, his son tried to sell the forest, but the will forbade it.\n\nToday, the estate belongs to a charity. Its **earnings** come from visitors, who walk along paths that run **eastward** through ancient oaks so tall that they **dwarf** the house. A small museum displays the duke's notebooks and his collection of **earthenware** pots. Volunteers are working to **duplicate** and **edit** his writings so that they can be published online.\n\nThe estate is now run **economically** and sustainably. Its most popular event takes place during a solar **eclipse**, when visitors gather on the lawn to watch the sky darken. \"The duke was strange,\" the director says, \"but he saw what others missed: that nature is worth more than money.\""},"28":{"w":["eject","elapse","electrician","electrode","elegant","elemental","elevate","elevation","elicit","eligible","elite","elliptical","eloquence","eloquent","embark","embassy","embody","embroidery","emigrate","emission"],"title":"Stitches Across the Sea","t":"My great-aunt Mei was born in a mountain village at an **elevation** of almost two thousand metres. As a girl, she learned the traditional **embroidery** of her region, covering cloth with **elegant** patterns of flowers and birds. Her work seemed to **embody** the spirit of the mountains: patient, careful and quietly beautiful.\n\nIn 1985, she decided to **emigrate** to Canada. She had to visit the **embassy** in Beijing several times before she became **eligible** for a visa, and when she finally **embarked|embark** on the long flight, she carried only one suitcase and a box of silk threads. Years later, she remembered how slowly the hours seemed to **elapse** on that journey.\n\nLife in Canada was hard at first. Her husband worked as an **electrician**, and she sewed clothes in a factory. She was often lonely, but her embroidery kept her connected to home. A local museum eventually noticed her work and invited her to give classes. Her lessons were simple, but she spoke with natural **eloquence**, and her students were moved by her stories. She once said that the best embroidery is like an **eloquent** silence.\n\nHer designs became famous. One, showing an **elliptical** moon over the mountains, was bought by an **elite** art collection in Toronto. Critics praised its **elemental** power: earth, water and sky seemed to live in every stitch. The museum's director said her work helped to **elevate** folk art to the level of fine art and **elicit** respect for immigrant cultures.\n\nMei is now ninety. She jokes about modern technology, especially machines that **eject** paper for no reason, and she worries about factory **emissions|emission** polluting the air. Her husband's old toolbox, full of wires and an **electrode** from a welding kit, still sits beside her sewing table."},"29":{"w":["empirical","enchant","enclosure","endanger","endeavor","endow","endurance","energetic","energize","engagement","enlighten","enrich","ensue","entail","enterprise","entity","entreat","entrepreneur","envious","environmental"],"title":"The Bird Farm","t":"Six years ago, a young **entrepreneur** named Zhao left a high-paying job in finance to start an unusual **enterprise**: a farm that protects rare birds. Friends thought he was mad, and some were secretly **envious** of his courage. \"I wanted my work to have meaning,\" he says.\n\nThe project required great **endurance**. Building the farm would **entail** years of work and most of his savings. He began by creating a large **enclosure** in a wetland area, where he planted native trees and grasses. Rapid development nearby threatened to **endanger** several species, and he hoped the farm would give them a safe home.\n\nZhao approaches conservation with an **empirical** method. He keeps careful records of bird numbers, nesting habits and food sources. His data have helped scientists **enlighten** the public about the importance of wetlands, and a university now treats the farm as an independent research **entity**.\n\nThe farm also tries to **enrich** the lives of local people. It employs twenty villagers and runs programmes to encourage community **engagement**. Children visit every week, and the sight of hundreds of cranes taking off together seems to **enchant** them. An **energetic** volunteer leads tours, and her enthusiasm can **energize** even the most tired visitors.\n\nSuccess did not come easily. When the farm was flooded in its second year, Zhao knew that months of difficulty would **ensue**. Zhao **entreated|entreat** local officials to help, and they eventually agreed to build better drainage. A wealthy businessman later offered to **endow** a research fund in his name. Zhao says every **endeavor** to protect nature is worthwhile. \"**Environmental** work is slow,\" he says, \"but the birds remind me why it matters.\""},"30":{"w":["envisage","epidemic","epoch","equate","equator","equilibrium","eradicate","erosion","errand","erroneous","erupt","eruption","escort","essence","eternal","ethical","ethics","ethnic","evaluation","evaporation"],"title":"The Island Doctor","t":"Dr. Okafor works at a small hospital on an island just north of the **equator**. The climate is hot and humid all year, and rapid **evaporation** means that water is often scarce in the dry season. Above the island rises an active volcano, which last saw a major **eruption** in 1998.\n\nWhen Dr. Okafor arrived ten years ago, the island was suffering from a malaria **epidemic**. She set out to **eradicate** the disease, a goal that many experts considered impossible. Her team handed out bed nets, drained standing water and trained local volunteers. \"At first, people **equated|equate** malaria with fate,\" she explains. \"They thought it was an **eternal** part of life.\"\n\nHer work also has an **ethical** side. Many patients come from **ethnic** minorities with their own healing traditions, and she believes doctors must respect them. Medical **ethics**, she says, require honesty and humility. When she discovered that an earlier health report contained **erroneous** data that hid the real number of deaths, she insisted on publishing the correct figures.\n\nIsland life is never dull. Children run **errands|errand** for the hospital, carrying messages and medicine between villages. When the volcano began to rumble last year, the government feared it might **erupt** again, and soldiers had to **escort** patients to a safer town. Heavy rains also cause soil **erosion** on the hillsides, cutting off roads for days.\n\nA recent **evaluation** showed that malaria cases have fallen by ninety percent. Dr. Okafor **envisages|envisage** a future in which the disease disappears completely. \"Health is a delicate **equilibrium** between people and nature,\" she says. \"The **essence** of medicine is not machines but trust.\" Some islanders say her arrival marked the beginning of a new **epoch**."},"31":{"w":["evenly","everlasting","evoke","evolutionary","excel","exceptional","excessively","exclamation","excrete","execution","exemplify","exempt","exile","exotic","expedition","experimentally","experimentation","expertise","expire","explicit"],"title":"In Search of Rare Plants","t":"Last spring, a team of botanists set off on an **expedition** to the rainforests of southern Yunnan. Their goal was to find a rare orchid that had not been seen for forty years. The leader, Professor Bao, is a scientist of **exceptional** experience whose **expertise** in tropical plants is recognised worldwide.\n\nThe forest was full of **exotic** life. Giant butterflies floated between the trees, and strange flowers seemed to **evoke** a world from millions of years ago. Professor Bao explained that the region is a living museum of **evolutionary** history. One plant they found **exemplifies|exemplify** this: a meat-eating vine that traps insects and then **excretes|excrete** chemicals to digest them.\n\nThe trip required careful planning and precise **execution**. The team's research permits would **expire** after thirty days, and the rules were **explicit**: no plants could be removed without permission. As a university research group, they were **exempt** from the usual park fees, but they had to submit daily reports.\n\nThe work was exhausting. Heavy rain fell almost every afternoon, and the humidity was **excessively** high. The team divided tasks **evenly**, and each member had a specific role. One young researcher seemed to **excel** at climbing trees, while another was skilled at photography.\n\nOn the twenty-sixth day, they finally found the orchid growing on a cliff. Professor Bao gave an **exclamation** of joy that echoed through the valley. Back in the laboratory, the team began careful **experimentation** to understand how the plant survives, testing **experimentally** how it responds to light and water. The professor's own story is moving: as a young scientist, he spent years in **exile** during a time of political trouble. \"Nature gave me hope,\" he says. \"Its beauty feels **everlasting**.\""},"32":{"w":["exploitation","exploration","exposition","exquisite","extinction","extinguish","extract","extraction","extracurricular","extraordinarily","extravagant","fable","fabricate","fabrication","facet","facilitate","faction","fake","fantastic","fascinate"],"title":"The Hall of Lost Animals","t":"Last month, our city museum opened an **exposition** called \"The Hall of Lost Animals.\" It tells the story of species that have disappeared because of human activity, from the dodo to the Yangtze river dolphin. The exhibition is **extraordinarily** popular, attracting thousands of visitors every weekend.\n\nEach room explores a different **facet** of the problem. One shows how the **exploitation** of forests and oceans has led to **extinction**. Another describes the age of **exploration**, when European sailors hunted birds and animals on remote islands. A beautiful glass case contains **exquisite** models of extinct birds, made by artists using real feathers donated by zoos.\n\nThe museum also displays a **fable** written by a children's author about the last dodo. Children love it, and the museum's **extracurricular** science club uses it to start discussions about protecting nature. Teachers say the exhibition helps to **facilitate** learning in a way that textbooks cannot.\n\nNot all the stories are sad. One room explains how scientists **extract** DNA from ancient bones, hoping one day to bring some species back. However, the process of **extraction** is controversial. A few years ago, a researcher was found to **fabricate** results, and his **fabrication** damaged public trust in the field. Today scientists are divided into two **factions|faction**: those who support \"de-extinction\" and those who think it is an **extravagant** waste of money.\n\nThe museum is careful to show only genuine specimens. A curator explained that one object donated as a rare bird egg turned out to be a **fake**. Another exhibit recalls a fire in a national park that took weeks to **extinguish**. The exhibition continues to **fascinate** visitors of all ages. One boy summed it up perfectly: \"It's **fantastic** and sad at the same time.\""},"33":{"w":["fascinating","fastener","faultless","fearless","fell","fellowship","feminine","ferrous","ferry","fiber","fighter","filament","filth","finely","finite","fireplace","firework","firmness","first-rate","fission"],"title":"The Fearless Physicist","t":"My grandmother was one of the first women in China to study nuclear physics. In the 1960s, when few women entered science, she won a research **fellowship** at a top university. People said physics was not a **feminine** subject, but she ignored them. Her colleagues described her as **fearless** and her work as **first-rate**.\n\nHer research focused on nuclear **fission**, the process in which atoms split and release energy. She also studied **ferrous** metals used in reactors and tested how a thin **filament** of tungsten behaves under extreme heat. Her laboratory notes, written **finely** in tiny characters, are almost **faultless**; she rarely made mistakes.\n\nShe grew up on an island, and she still loves to tell stories about her childhood. Every morning, she took a small **ferry** to school, and in winter, her father would **fell** trees for firewood. She remembers how the family gathered around the **fireplace** at night, and how fishermen set off **fireworks|firework** at the Spring Festival.\n\nHer life was not always easy. During difficult political years, she was sent to work in a factory, where conditions were terrible. She spent months cleaning **filth** from machines and sorting metal **fasteners|fastener** by hand. But she never lost her **firmness** of purpose. \"Time is **finite**,\" she said, \"so I studied at night.\"\n\nLater, she returned to research and helped design safety systems for power stations. She also worked on new materials, including a strong carbon **fiber** that could replace steel. Her students called her a **fighter**, because she refused to give up. Today, at eighty-five, she still reads science journals every day. \"Physics is **fascinating**,\" she says. \"Every answer leads to a new question.\""},"34":{"w":["fitness","fitting","fixture","flake","flank","flannel","flap","flask","flatten","flatter","flaw","fling","fluctuate","fluctuation","fluorescent","flutter","flux","foam","foil","follower"],"title":"The Fitness Star","t":"Two years ago, my cousin Jie was an overweight office worker who rarely exercised. Today, he runs a small gym and has more than half a million **followers|follower** online. His story shows how quickly lives can change, though it is not as simple as his videos suggest.\n\nHis gym is in an old warehouse, and two tall buildings **flank** its entrance. Inside, **fluorescent** lights shine on rows of machines, and the floor is covered with thick **foam** mats. Every light **fixture** was installed by Jie himself. There is also a small **fitting** room where customers can try on sports clothes.\n\nJie's online videos focus on **fitness** for ordinary people. He wears a simple **flannel** shirt and keeps a **flask** of green tea beside him. He refuses to **flatter** his viewers with false promises. \"Your weight will **fluctuate**,\" he tells them, \"and every **fluctuation** is normal.\" He warns that the industry is in constant **flux**, with new trends appearing every month.\n\nNot everything about his business is perfect. He admits that his early training plans had a serious **flaw**: they were too hard for beginners. Some viewers injured themselves trying to **fling** heavy weights. He now teaches gentle exercises, such as standing with arms out and slowly letting them **flap** like wings.\n\nHis lifestyle is simple. He eats oat **flakes|flake** for breakfast and wraps his lunch in **foil** to take to work. He still feels his heart **flutter** before filming a new video. \"The hardest thing is not exercise,\" he says. \"It's learning to **flatten** your fears. Once you do, everything else is easy.\""},"35":{"w":["footpath","fore","foresee","forge","formerly","formidable","formulate","formulation","forsake","fort","forthcoming","fortress","forum","fossil","foster","foul","fowl","fracture","fragile","fragrant"],"title":"The Fort on the Hill","t":"On a hill above my hometown stands an old stone **fort**, built in the sixteenth century to defend the coast against pirates. It was **formerly** an important military base, but today it is a peaceful park. A narrow **footpath** winds up the hill through pine trees and **fragrant** wild flowers.\n\nThe **fortress** was once a **formidable** sight. Its walls are ten metres high, and its gates were made of iron that local blacksmiths would **forge** by hand. Historians say the officers who designed it were able to **foresee** almost every kind of attack. Their strategy, which they had to **formulate** without modern maps, was remarkably effective.\n\nRecently, workers repairing the walls made an exciting discovery: a **fossil** of an ancient fish embedded in one of the stones. Scientists believe the rock came from a nearby quarry that was once under the sea. A **forthcoming** exhibition will display the fossil, and the town has organised a public **forum** to discuss how best to protect the site.\n\nThe fort has a darker side. During one long siege, the soldiers inside ran out of food and had to eat their horses and every **fowl** they kept. Records describe the **foul** smell of disease and the many soldiers who suffered a **fracture** from falling rocks. Some chose to **forsake** their posts and flee.\n\nToday, the fort is **fragile**. Rain and wind are slowly destroying its walls, and engineers are testing a new **formulation** of mortar to repair them. Local schools use the site to **foster** students' interest in history. The town's leaders hope to bring the fort's story to the **fore** of the region's tourism, so that visitors can understand how much the past still shapes the present."},"36":{"w":["frail","fraud","freshen","fret","fright","frightful","fringe","fro","frock","fulfill","funding","fury","fuse","fuss","galaxy","gamble","gangster","gaol","garlic","garment"],"title":"The Last Star of Galaxy Studios","t":"In the 1930s, a film company called **Galaxy** Studios made dozens of popular movies in Shanghai. Most of them were crime dramas about a **gangster** who would **gamble** away his fortune, cheat his friends and end up in **gaol**. Today, the old studio is a museum, and its most famous actress, now ninety-four, still visits every year.\n\nShe is **frail** now and walks with a stick, but her eyes are bright. When she arrived last spring, the staff made a great **fuss**, bringing flowers and tea. She laughed and told them not to **fret**. \"I'm old, not broken,\" she said.\n\nThe museum's collection of costumes is remarkable. Visitors can see a silk **frock** she wore in her first film, decorated with a long **fringe** of beads, as well as hundreds of other **garments|garment** from the period. One display explains how studio electricians often blew a **fuse** when too many lights were used at once.\n\nShe told stories about her early career. Once, during filming, a fire broke out on the set, and she remembers the **fright** of seeing flames climb the curtains. The director's **fury** afterwards was **frightful**; he shouted for an hour. She also recalled a **fraud** case in which a producer stole the studio's money and fled abroad.\n\nHer advice to young actors is simple: work hard and **fulfill** your promises. She also has a strange habit: before every performance, she eats a clove of raw **garlic** for luck, then uses mint to **freshen** her breath. As she walks to and **fro** among the displays, visitors follow her with their phones. The museum hopes to receive government **funding** to record her memories before it is too late."},"37":{"w":["gathering","generalization","generalize","generosity","genetics","geographical","geometrical","gigantic","ginger","glamour","gleam","glider","glorify","glossary","gnaw","goddess","gorgeous","gorilla","gossip","grab"],"title":"Among the Mountain Gorillas","t":"Dr. Amani has spent fifteen years studying mountain **gorillas|gorilla** in the forests of central Africa. Her research combines field observation with **genetics**, comparing DNA samples from different groups. She wants to understand how **geographical** barriers, such as rivers and mountains, separate populations.\n\nHer days are long and hard. She hikes for hours through thick forest, following signs left by the animals: broken branches, footprints and plants the gorillas **gnaw** on. When she finally finds a group, she sits quietly for hours. \"They are **gigantic** but gentle,\" she says. \"A large male can weigh two hundred kilograms, yet he plays with babies like a grandfather.\"\n\nShe warns against easy **generalization**. \"People love to **generalize** about animals,\" she explains, \"but every gorilla has its own personality.\" One young female, with **ginger**-coloured hair, is famously curious and often tries to **grab** Dr. Amani's notebook.\n\nResearch has none of the **glamour** shown in films. Dr. Amani lives in a small hut and often eats only beans and rice. But she says the forest is **gorgeous** at dawn, when mist rises and light begins to **gleam** on the leaves. Local people call one mountain the home of a forest **goddess**, and they treat it with deep respect.\n\nShe worries that documentaries **glorify** scientists while ignoring local rangers, whose **generosity** and courage make research possible. At village **gatherings|gathering**, she thanks them publicly. She has also written a simple **glossary** of scientific terms in the local language. Recently, her team began using a small **glider** drone to map the forest, flying in **geometrical** patterns to cover every area. \"We still have much to learn,\" she says, smiling at the **gossip** that she prefers gorillas to people. \"Maybe it's true.\""},"38":{"w":["gradient","gramophone","granite","graphite","grasshopper","grassy","gravel","graze","grease","greed","grief","grim","grin","groove","grope","grove","growl","grumble","grunt","guilt"],"title":"The Quarry Village","t":"My grandfather grew up in a village built around a **granite** quarry in the hills of Fujian. For two hundred years, almost every family there worked with stone. Men cut huge blocks from the hillside, and women sorted **gravel** and loaded it onto carts that rolled down the steep **gradient** to the river.\n\nLife was hard and often **grim**. The work was dangerous, and accidents were common. My grandfather remembers the **grief** of the whole village when three workers died in a rockfall. The quarry owner, a man known for his **greed**, refused to pay compensation, and many workers felt anger mixed with **guilt** that they had not protested sooner.\n\nYet there were happy memories too. In the evenings, children played in a **grassy** field beside a **grove** of pine trees, catching **grasshoppers|grasshopper** and watching goats **graze**. The village had one **gramophone**, and on summer nights its owner played old records while people danced. My grandfather loved the scratch of the needle as it moved along the record's **groove**.\n\nHe also remembers his first job, at the age of twelve. He had to **grease** the wheels of the carts with oil and clean the stone dust from the machines. Sometimes the lights failed, and he had to **grope** his way through the dark tunnels. An old worker taught him to draw with a pencil of **graphite**, and he discovered a talent for art.\n\nWorkers in the village would often **grumble** about wages, and the foreman would **growl** at anyone who stopped to rest. But my grandfather says the men rarely complained in front of children. When they finished a long day, they would **grunt** with exhaustion and then **grin** at each other. \"We were poor,\" he says, \"but we were never alone.\""},"39":{"w":["guitar","gust","gutter","hail","halve","ham","hamper","handbook","handicap","handout","harbor","hardy","harmonious","harp","hassle","haughty","haul","haunt","hazardous","headlong"],"title":"The Musician on the Pier","t":"Every evening, a man named Old Lu plays music on the pier of our city's **harbor**. He carries a battered **guitar** and a small folding **harp**, and he sets up beside the fish market as the sun goes down. Tourists stop to listen, and fishermen pause as they **haul** their nets onto the shore.\n\nOld Lu has a physical **handicap**: he lost his left leg in an accident thirty years ago. But it has never seemed to **hamper** his music. \"Life gave me a hard road,\" he says, \"so I made it **harmonious**.\" He learned to play from a **handbook** he found in a second-hand shop, practising every day for years.\n\nHis life is not easy. He sleeps in a small room near the fish market and depends on the money people drop into his hat. On bad nights, he earns almost nothing, and he must **halve** his meals to save money. Some passers-by are **haughty** and treat him like a beggar, offering a **handout** of coins without listening. He doesn't mind. \"Music is for everyone,\" he says.\n\nThe weather by the sea can be **hazardous**. Strong **gusts|gust** of wind blow across the pier, and in winter **hail** sometimes falls without warning. Once, a storm sent him running **headlong** for shelter, and his guitar fell into a **gutter** full of water. A local carpenter repaired it for free.\n\nOld Lu is a **hardy** man who rarely complains, though dealing with officials who demand permits is a constant **hassle**. In the evenings, a café owner brings him a **ham** sandwich and a cup of tea. His songs **haunt** those who hear them; long after they leave the pier, many visitors say they can still hear the music in their heads."},"40":{"w":["hearth","hearty","heater","heave","heighten","hemisphere","henceforth","herald","herb","heritage","hierarchy","hike","hinder","hindrance","hinge","hip","hiss","historian","historic","hitherto"],"title":"The House by the Mountain Pass","t":"On an old mountain road in Shanxi stands a stone farmhouse that is more than four hundred years old. It was recently declared a **historic** building, part of the region's cultural **heritage**. A local **historian**, Mr. Duan, has spent years studying it, and he says it tells the story of ordinary life in a way that palaces cannot.\n\nThe centre of the house is the **hearth**, where families cooked, ate and kept warm. Today, a modern **heater** stands in the corner, but visitors still gather around the old fireplace. Mr. Duan says that, **hitherto**, historians had paid little attention to such buildings. \"We studied emperors, not farmers,\" he explains.\n\nThe house reveals the strict **hierarchy** of traditional families. The oldest man slept in the warmest room, while young wives lived in small rooms near the kitchen. Even the doors show social rank: the main gate turns on a heavy iron **hinge**, while servants used a simple wooden door at the back.\n\nRestoring the house has been difficult. Heavy rain and a lack of money continue to **hinder** progress, and the narrow road is a major **hindrance** to delivering materials. Workers must **heave** stones up the slope by hand, and one older volunteer injured his **hip**.\n\nVisitors who **hike** to the house are rewarded with a **hearty** meal of noodles and wild **herbs|herb**. On cold nights, the wind seems to **hiss** through the pass, which only seems to **heighten** the sense of history. Mr. Duan believes the house may **herald** a new interest in rural history across the northern **hemisphere**. \"**Henceforth**,\" he says, \"we must protect the homes of ordinary people too.\""},"41":{"w":["hoarse","hoe","hoist","homely","homogeneous","hop","hose","hospitality","hostage","hound","hover","howl","huddle","hug","hull","hum","humanitarian","humanity","humidity","humiliation"],"title":"The Aid Ship","t":"Last autumn, my cousin joined the crew of a **humanitarian** ship carrying food and medicine to islands hit by a flood. The ship was old, and its steel **hull** was covered in rust, but the captain said it had survived worse storms. \"What matters is not the ship,\" he told the volunteers, \"but our sense of **humanity**.\"\n\nThe voyage was exhausting. The heat and **humidity** were almost unbearable, and the crew worked long hours loading boxes. Each morning, sailors would **hoist** the flag and check the engines, while volunteers cleaned the deck with a **hose**. The engines would **hum** day and night, and my cousin soon learned to sleep through the noise.\n\nOn the third night, a storm struck. The wind began to **howl**, and waves crashed over the deck. The passengers had to **huddle** together in the dining room, and a seabird tried to **hover** above the ship before giving up and flying away. \"For two days, we were **hostage** to the weather,\" my cousin said. By the end, many of the crew were **hoarse** from shouting over the wind.\n\nWhen the ship finally arrived, the islanders greeted them with incredible **hospitality**. Although they had lost almost everything, they shared their food and invited the crew into their **homely** wooden houses. Children would **hop** onto the volunteers' backs, and old women would **hug** them in thanks. One farmer, holding a **hoe** he had saved from the flood, said he would start planting again the next day.\n\nThe trip changed my cousin. She had once suffered a deep **humiliation** at work, when her manager criticised her in front of everyone, and the memory had **hounded|hound** her for years. On the island, it no longer seemed important. She noticed that the islanders were a **homogeneous** community, sharing one language and one way of life, yet they welcomed strangers without hesitation."},"42":{"w":["hurl","hurrah","hurricane","hush","hydraulic","hydrocarbon","hymn","hypothesis","hysteria","hysterical","icy","idealism","ideally","ideology","idiot","idleness","ignite","ignorance","illiterate","illuminate"],"title":"The Night of the Hurricane","t":"Three years ago, a powerful **hurricane** struck the coastal town where my friend Rosa lives. Forecasters had warned that it would be the strongest in decades, but many people refused to leave their homes. \"There was a kind of **hysteria** on the news,\" Rosa remembers, \"so some people simply stopped believing it.\"\n\nThe storm arrived at night. Wind would **hurl** branches against the windows, and rain fell so heavily that the streets turned into rivers. When the power failed, Rosa's family lit candles to **illuminate** the room, and her grandmother sang an old **hymn** to calm the children. \"**Hush**,\" she whispered, \"it will pass.\"\n\nRosa's neighbour, an elderly man who was **illiterate**, had not understood the written warnings and stayed at home. When his roof collapsed, Rosa's brother rushed out to help him, ignoring the **icy** rain. His mother called him an **idiot** for taking such a risk, but she was secretly proud. When he returned safely with the old man, the whole family gave a loud \"**hurrah**.\"\n\nAfter the storm, scientists studied what had gone wrong. One **hypothesis** was that a leak of **hydrocarbon** fuel from a damaged factory had caused a fire to **ignite** near the harbour. Engineers also found that the town's **hydraulic** flood gates had failed because of poor maintenance.\n\nThe disaster sparked fierce debate. Some blamed **ignorance** and **idleness** among officials, who had ignored repeated warnings. Others argued that the problem was **ideology**: politicians cared more about cutting taxes than protecting people. Rosa, a student of environmental science, still believes in **idealism**. \"**Ideally**, we would prevent such disasters,\" she says. Her younger sister became almost **hysterical** with laughter when Rosa announced that she would run for mayor one day, but Rosa was serious."},"43":{"w":["imaginative","imitation","immerse","immigration","immortal","immune","immunity","impair","impart","impartial","imperative","imperial","impetus","implicit","implore","impoverish","improper","impulse","impurity","inaccessible"],"title":"The Language Teacher","t":"Ms. Kim came to China from Korea twenty years ago and now teaches Chinese to new immigrants in a large southern city. Her own experience of **immigration** was hard. She arrived with little money and spoke almost no Chinese. \"I felt as if I had fallen into a deep sea,\" she says. \"The only way to survive was to **immerse** myself completely.\"\n\nToday she runs a small language school famous for its **imaginative** teaching methods. Instead of memorising lists, students cook meals, visit markets and act out conversations. She believes that **imitation** is the first step in learning a language: \"Children copy sounds before they understand them. Adults should do the same.\" Mistakes, she adds, are like an **impurity** in water: with time and movement, they settle and disappear.\n\nShe also teaches culture. Her students visit the **imperial** palace in Beijing, and she explains the **implicit** rules of Chinese politeness, such as refusing a gift once before accepting it. She warns them that using the wrong words in formal situations can seem **improper**, even if the grammar is correct.\n\nMs. Kim believes it is **imperative** to give new immigrants practical help. Many arrive **impoverished|impoverish**, and some live in areas almost **inaccessible** by public transport. She often visits them herself. Once, a student's mother **implored|implore** her to help find a doctor for her sick son, whose **immune** system had been weakened by illness. Ms. Kim found a clinic that offered free care and helped the boy rebuild his **immunity**.\n\nHer teaching is **impartial**: rich or poor, every student receives the same attention. She says that fear and stress can **impair** learning, so her classroom is relaxed. \"A teacher should **impart** confidence as well as knowledge,\" she explains. Her **impulse** to help others gives her school its **impetus**. A writer once described her as \"an **immortal** spirit in a tired city.\" She laughs: \"I'm just a teacher.\""},"44":{"w":["inaccurate","inadequate","inasmuch","inaugurate","incandescent","incapable","incense","incentive","incidence","incidentally","inclination","inclusive","incompatible","incomplete","incorporate","incredible","incur","indefinitely","indicative","indignation"],"title":"Lighting the Old Street","t":"Last spring, our city decided to restore an old shopping street that had been neglected for years. The street's lamps were still **incandescent** bulbs from the 1980s, and many of them no longer worked. Shop owners complained that the lighting was **inadequate** and that customers felt unsafe at night.\n\nThe city council offered an **incentive**: shop owners who renovated their buildings would receive a tax reduction. The plan aimed to **incorporate** modern technology into the street's traditional design. Engineers proposed solar-powered lamps, but they soon found that the new system was **incompatible** with the old electrical wiring.\n\nThe project faced many problems. Early cost estimates proved **inaccurate**, and the city had to **incur** extra expenses. Some contractors were **incapable** of finishing their work on time, and one section of the street remained **incomplete** for months. Residents expressed **indignation** when the council suggested delaying the project **indefinitely**.\n\nLocal shop owners were divided. Some were **incensed|incense** by the noise and dust; others showed a strong **inclination** to cooperate. A tea shop owner, who burned **incense** every morning for good luck, said he trusted the project. \"**Inasmuch** as it brings more customers,\" he said, \"I support it.\"\n\nFinally, the mayor came to **inaugurate** the new street. The opening ceremony was **inclusive**, with performances by children, elderly dancers and migrant workers. The results were **incredible**: crime fell, and the **incidence** of road accidents dropped by half. The success is **indicative** of what careful planning can achieve. **Incidentally**, the tea shop owner now sells twice as much tea as before, and he says the new lights are the best luck he has ever had."},"45":{"w":["indiscriminately","induce","induction","indulge","industrious","inertia","inevitably","infancy","infectious","inference","inferiority","infinitely","infinity","inflate","inflation","inflict","infrastructure","infusion","ingenious","ingenuity"],"title":"The Village Inventor","t":"In a small village in Henan, a farmer named Mr. Gao has become famous for his **ingenious** inventions. He has no formal education beyond primary school, but his **ingenuity** has improved the lives of hundreds of families. His workshop is full of strange machines made from bicycle parts, old engines and pieces of wood.\n\nHis first invention came from necessity. When rising **inflation** made fertiliser too expensive, he designed a machine that turns kitchen waste into compost. Later, he built a simple pump that could **inflate** tyres using wind power. \"Problems are like **infinity**,\" he jokes. \"There's always one more.\"\n\nMr. Gao is extremely **industrious**. He wakes at five every morning and works until late at night. His **infectious** enthusiasm has inspired many young villagers to start their own projects. He believes that a feeling of **inferiority** often holds rural people back. \"City people think we can't invent anything,\" he says. \"That's a wrong **inference**.\"\n\nThe village's **infrastructure** was poor when he was young. Roads were muddy, and electricity was unreliable. Officials used to spray chemicals **indiscriminately** on the fields, which would **inflict** damage on the soil. Mr. Gao invented a small sprayer that uses much less chemical and has helped to **induce** better farming habits.\n\nHis success has attracted attention. A university invited him to give a lecture as part of its **induction** programme for engineering students. A recent **infusion** of government money will allow him to build a proper workshop. He admits that he sometimes **indulges|indulge** in buying unnecessary parts. \"Change is slow,\" he says, \"because people suffer from **inertia**. But **inevitably**, good ideas spread.\" Rural innovation, he believes, is still in its **infancy**, but its potential is **infinitely** greater than people imagine."},"46":{"w":["inherent","inhibit","initially","initiate","inject","inland","inlet","innate","innovation","innumerable","inorganic","insane","insanity","insidious","insistent","inspector","inspiration","instability","installment","instantaneous"],"title":"The Lake Inspector","t":"Mr. Ye works as an environmental **inspector** at a large **inland** lake in central China. Every morning, he takes a small boat out to collect water samples from each **inlet** where rivers enter the lake. His job is to protect a water source used by **innumerable** families, farms and factories.\n\n**Initially**, the lake's problems were easy to see: dead fish, green water and a terrible smell. Today, the danger is more **insidious**. Many pollutants are invisible, including **inorganic** chemicals from industry that build up slowly in the food chain. \"The risk is **inherent** in modern life,\" Mr. Ye explains. \"We use thousands of chemicals and know little about most of them.\"\n\nHe has an **innate** curiosity and loves solving problems. Years ago, he proposed a plan to **inject** beneficial bacteria into the lake's sediment to break down pollution. Many colleagues thought the idea was **insane**. \"They said it was pure **insanity**,\" he laughs. But he was **insistent**, and the government finally agreed to **initiate** a small trial.\n\nThe results were encouraging. The bacteria seemed to **inhibit** the growth of harmful algae, and water quality improved within a year. The project became an important **innovation** in lake management, and other provinces began to study it. The equipment was expensive, so the local government paid for it in monthly **installments|installment**.\n\nChallenges remain. Climate change has created **instability** in the lake's ecosystem, with sudden floods and droughts. New sensors now give **instantaneous** readings of water quality, sending alerts to Mr. Ye's phone whenever something goes wrong. He says his greatest **inspiration** comes from the fishermen who have lived beside the lake for generations. \"They taught me that the lake is alive,\" he says. \"Our job is to keep it that way.\""},"47":{"w":["instinctively","instructor","instrumental","insulate","insulator","intact","intake","integral","integrity","intellect","intellectually","intelligible","intensify","intent","interact","interconnect","intercourse","interface","interim","intermittent"],"title":"Learning to Fly","t":"Last year, my friend Hui decided to fulfil a childhood dream: she began training to become a pilot. Her **instructor**, a former air force officer named Captain Ma, has taught hundreds of students. \"Flying is not just about **intellect**,\" he told her on the first day. \"It's about judgement and **integrity**.\"\n\nThe training was demanding. Hui had to learn how the plane's systems **interact** and how every part is **interconnected|interconnect**. She studied the cockpit **interface** until she could find each switch with her eyes closed. She learned how electrical wires are wrapped in an **insulator** and why engineers **insulate** the cabin against extreme cold.\n\nAt first, the radio messages were barely **intelligible** to her. Air traffic controllers spoke quickly and used technical codes. But slowly she learned the language of aviation, which Captain Ma described as \"the international **intercourse** of the skies.\" Clear communication, he said, is **integral** to safety.\n\nHui found the course **intellectually** challenging but deeply rewarding. She was **intent** on passing every test the first time. Captain Ma pushed her hard, and his training became more difficult each week. \"We must **intensify** the pressure,\" he said, \"so that you stay calm when it really matters.\"\n\nOne day, during a practice flight, the engine made an **intermittent** noise. Hui **instinctively** checked the fuel **intake** and found a small blockage. She landed safely, and the plane was **intact**. Captain Ma praised her calm response, which he said was **instrumental** in avoiding an accident. While the plane was repaired, the school gave her an **interim** licence to continue training on another aircraft. She now hopes to fly passenger jets within three years."},"48":{"w":["intersection","intervene","intervention","intonation","intricacy","intricate","intrigue","intriguing","intrinsic","intuition","invalid","invaluable","invariably","inventory","inversely","invert","invoice","ion","ironically","irony"],"title":"The Clockmaker's Shop","t":"At a busy **intersection** in the old city, there is a tiny shop that repairs antique clocks. Its owner, Mr. Pei, has worked there for forty years. His windows are filled with **intricate** brass machines, and the sound of a hundred ticking clocks fills the room. Visitors often find the place **intriguing**; it feels like stepping back in time.\n\nMr. Pei says the **intricacy** of old clocks never stops amazing him. Each one contains hundreds of tiny parts that must work together perfectly. He keeps a detailed **inventory** of spare parts, many of which can no longer be found anywhere else. His knowledge is **invaluable** to collectors across the country.\n\nHe relies on **intuition** as much as skill. \"When a clock is sick, I can hear it,\" he says. \"Its rhythm changes, like the **intonation** of a person's voice.\" He explains that the speed of a clock is **inversely** related to the length of its pendulum: the longer the pendulum, the slower it swings. Some repairs require him to **invert** the clock and work on it upside down.\n\nCustomers **invariably** ask whether their clocks are valuable. Mr. Pei tells them that a clock's **intrinsic** value lies in its craftsmanship, not its price. **Ironically**, the most expensive clock he ever repaired was also the least accurate. He enjoys the **irony**.\n\nThe shop has survived many difficulties. When the city planned to demolish the building, residents had to **intervene**, and their **intervention** saved it. A modern watch company once tried to **intrigue** him with a job offer, but he refused. He still writes every **invoice** by hand, and he warns customers that a warranty becomes **invalid** if they open the clock themselves. His grandson, a chemistry student, jokes that he understands every **ion** in the air but nothing about clocks."},"49":{"w":["irregularity","irrespective","irrigation","irritate","ivory","jack","jealousy","jean","jelly","jeopardize","jerk","jingle","jog","jug","junction","junk","justification","kernel","kidnap","kidney"],"title":"The Harvest Market","t":"Every October, farmers from across the county gather at a crossroads **junction** outside our town for the annual harvest market. It is a noisy, colourful event, full of stalls selling corn, beans, honey and homemade **jelly**. Children **jog** between the tables, and a band plays a cheerful **jingle** that everyone knows by heart.\n\nThis year's harvest was good, thanks to a new **irrigation** system built last spring. Before that, the fields depended on rain, and **irregularity** in rainfall often damaged crops. The new system brings water to every farm, **irrespective** of its size or location.\n\nThe market sells more than food. One stall offers old farm tools, including a rusty car **jack** and a pile of **junk** that a collector hopes to sell as antiques. An old woman sells clay **jugs|jug** decorated with flowers, and a young man sells bags made from recycled **jean** fabric. A sign warns customers not to buy **ivory**, which is illegal, and the police check every stall.\n\nNot everyone gets along. Some farmers feel a touch of **jealousy** when their neighbours win prizes for the largest pumpkin. Loud music can **irritate** older visitors, and one farmer argued that the crowds would **jeopardize** the safety of his animals. A rude driver was called a **jerk** after he tried to park in the middle of the market.\n\nThe best moment comes at the end of the day, when farmers roast corn over a fire and share the sweet **kernels|kernel** with everyone. Local children love a game in which they pretend to **kidnap** the scarecrow and hide it, while adults look for it. One doctor gives free health checks, warning people that too much salt can damage the **kidney**. For the organisers, the market's greatest **justification** is simple: it brings the whole community together."},"50":{"w":["kilowatt","kin","kindle","kinetic","kit","knight","lamentation","landscape","latent","lathe","latitude","lattice","lawsuit","layman","layoff","leaflet","leakage","lease","legacy","legitimate"],"title":"The Toymaker's Workshop","t":"Three years ago, my uncle lost his job during a wave of factory **layoffs|layoff**. At fifty-two, he worried that no one would hire him. Then he remembered a skill he had learned as a boy: woodworking. He decided to **lease** an empty workshop and start making wooden toys.\n\nHe bought an old **lathe** and a basic tool **kit**, and he began by making simple toys: horses, cars and a wooden **knight** on horseback. Soon he became more ambitious. He designed **kinetic** toys that move when children turn a handle, and a **lattice** of wooden gears that spins like a clock. The workshop uses only a few **kilowatts|kilowatt** of electricity a day, since much of the work is done by hand.\n\nHis success revealed a **latent** talent. He had never thought of himself as an artist, but customers praised the beauty of his work. His small business began to **kindle** interest among young people in the town, and several asked to become his apprentices.\n\nNot everything went smoothly. A **leakage** of water from the roof damaged several toys, and the owner of the building refused to pay for repairs. My uncle considered a **lawsuit** but finally decided to settle the matter peacefully. As a **layman** in legal matters, he found the process confusing. He also faced questions about whether his business was **legitimate**, since he had no formal licence at first.\n\nToday, the workshop is thriving. My uncle prints a **leaflet** describing his toys and hands it out at local markets. He wants to leave a **legacy** for his **kin**, and his daughter now helps him run the business. When the old factory finally closed, there was much **lamentation** in the town, but my uncle's workshop shows that the economic **landscape** can change in unexpected ways. \"You have more **latitude** when you work for yourself,\" he says. \"And more happiness.\""},"51":{"w":["lengthen","leopard","liability","lighter","likeness","lily","limestone","limp","linear","linger","linguistic","lining","lipstick","literacy","literal","literally","litter","lobby","locality","locust"],"title":"The Cave Guide","t":"In the hills of Guangxi, where **limestone** peaks rise like green towers from the fields, an old man named Mr. Wei guides tourists through a famous cave. He walks with a slight **limp**, the result of a climbing accident in his youth, but he knows every passage by heart.\n\nThe cave stretches in an almost **linear** path for three kilometres underground. Mr. Wei carries a torch and an old **lighter**, which he uses to light candles at a small shrine near the entrance. Visitors often **linger** there, admiring rock formations that bear a strange **likeness** to animals and people. One looks like a sleeping **leopard**; another resembles a bunch of **lilies|lily**.\n\nMr. Wei speaks the local dialect, and he loves to explain the **linguistic** history of the area. Many place names, he says, should not be taken in a **literal** sense. \"Dragon Mountain does not **literally** contain a dragon,\" he jokes. Despite little schooling, he taught himself to read and now runs evening **literacy** classes for older villagers.\n\nTourism has brought both money and problems. Visitors sometimes **litter** the cave with plastic bottles, and Mr. Wei collects bags of rubbish every week. Last year, a hotel company tried to **lobby** the government to build a large resort nearby. Mr. Wei and his neighbours opposed the plan, arguing that it would damage the **locality**. The company also worried about legal **liability** if tourists were injured.\n\nThe region has faced natural threats too. Old people remember a summer when **locusts|locust** destroyed the crops. Today, Mr. Wei's daughter runs a small shop at the hotel **lobby**, selling scarves with silk **lining** and **lipstick** made from local flowers. The government plans to **lengthen** the tourist path next year, but Mr. Wei hopes it will not change the cave's quiet beauty."},"52":{"w":["lodging","lofty","logistics","longevity","longing","longitude","loosely","lounge","luminous","luncheon","luxurious","magician","magistrate","magnetism","magnify","magnitude","maiden","majesty","makeup","malice"],"title":"A Weekend at the Grand Hotel","t":"Last summer, my grandparents celebrated their sixtieth wedding anniversary with a weekend at a famous old hotel in Qingdao. Built in 1910, the hotel is known for its **luxurious** rooms and its **lofty** ceilings painted with stars. It has hosted presidents, film stars and even a visiting queen, whose **majesty** is still remembered in a portrait in the main hall.\n\nMy cousin organised the trip, and the **logistics** were complicated. She had to book **lodging** for twelve family members, arrange transport for my grandfather's wheelchair and plan a special **luncheon** on the terrace. The menu was **loosely** based on dishes my grandparents ate on their wedding day.\n\nThe hotel's **lounge** became our favourite place. In the evenings, a **magician** entertained guests with card tricks, and his personal **magnetism** kept even the children quiet. He performed one trick using a **magnifying|magnify** glass that made a coin seem to disappear. My grandmother, who never wears **makeup**, laughed like a young girl.\n\nOver dinner, my grandfather told stories from his career as a **magistrate**. He handled thousands of cases and said that he rarely saw true **malice**; most crimes, he believed, came from poverty or desperation. He also described the **magnitude** of the changes he has seen in his lifetime, from oil lamps to smartphones.\n\nOn the last evening, we walked along the beach. The sea was **luminous** with tiny glowing creatures, and my grandmother pointed at a map in the hotel showing the city's **longitude** and latitude. She told us how they had met on the **maiden** voyage of a ferry across the bay. Asked about the secret of their **longevity** and happiness, she smiled. \"We always had a **longing** to see each other,\" she said. \"Even after sixty years.\""},"53":{"w":["malicious","mall","malpractice","mammal","managerial","manifest","manipulate","mansion","manuscript","maple","marathon","marginal","maritime","marsh","marshal","martyr","marvel","masculine","masculinity","massacre"],"title":"The Writer's Mansion","t":"On the edge of a coastal town stands a red brick **mansion** surrounded by **maple** trees. It was the home of a famous novelist who wrote about **maritime** life in the early twentieth century. Today, it is a museum, and visitors can see the desk where he wrote, along with the original **manuscript** of his greatest novel.\n\nThe novel tells the story of a fishing family living beside a **marsh**, where birds and small **mammals|mammal** shared the land with people. Critics **marvel** at the way the writer described nature. His work also explored ideas of **masculinity**: his male characters are strong and **masculine** on the surface but deeply vulnerable inside.\n\nThe writer's life was not peaceful. Rivals spread **malicious** rumours about him, and some newspapers tried to **manipulate** public opinion against his books. His health suffered after a doctor's **malpractice** left him partly deaf. Yet his talent continued to **manifest** itself in every new book.\n\nHis most powerful chapter describes a historical **massacre** in the town during a war, when soldiers killed dozens of fishermen. The writer treated the victims as **martyrs|martyr** and honoured them with dignity. A memorial stone now stands in the town square.\n\nThe museum has faced its own struggles. A developer once planned to replace it with a shopping **mall**, and the museum's **managerial** team fought for years to save it. Its income is **marginal**, so it depends on donations. Every spring, the town holds a **marathon** that ends at the mansion's gate, and a race **marshal** in a bright jacket guides runners through the final kilometre."},"54":{"w":["mast","mastery","maternity","maturation","maturity","meaningful","mechanism","mediate","medieval","meditate","melancholy","melody","membership","menace","merchandise","merciful","merge","mesh","metabolic","metallic"],"title":"Music in the Maternity Ward","t":"At a large hospital in Chengdu, a music therapist named Ms. Hu works with patients in the **maternity** ward. She believes that a gentle **melody** can calm anxious mothers and help newborn babies sleep. Her work combines art and science, and doctors say it has a **meaningful** effect on patients' wellbeing.\n\nMs. Hu studied both music and medicine. She explains the **mechanism** behind her work: certain rhythms slow the heart rate and may even influence **metabolic** activity. She often plays **medieval** songs on a small harp, because their simple patterns seem to **merge** naturally with the sound of breathing.\n\nHer path to **mastery** was long. She practised for years before she felt ready to play in hospitals. She also learned to **meditate**, which helps her stay calm when patients are in pain. \"Music must **mesh** with the mood of the room,\" she says. \"Sometimes the right note is silence.\"\n\nMany new mothers feel tired or **melancholy** after giving birth. Ms. Hu's sessions give them a moment of peace. One mother described the music as \"a **merciful** gift.\" Doctors also note that the babies' **maturation** seems to benefit from regular exposure to soft sounds, though more research is needed.\n\nNot everyone supported the programme at first. Some staff thought noise was a **menace** to patients' rest, and Ms. Hu had to **mediate** between doctors and nurses with different views. Today, the hospital has a music club, and its **membership** includes doctors, patients and volunteers. The gift shop sells **merchandise** such as CDs and small **metallic** bells. Outside the ward, a flag flies from a tall **mast** in the garden. Ms. Hu says her work has taught her emotional **maturity**: \"Every life begins with sound. I want it to be beautiful.\""},"55":{"w":["metallurgy","meteorite","metropolitan","microprocessor","microscopic","microwave","mighty","migrant","migrate","militant","milky","millionaire","mingle","miniature","minimal","minimize","mint","mischief","miser","misery"],"title":"The Millionaire Who Lived Like a Miser","t":"For forty years, an old man named Mr. Qian lived in a tiny flat in a busy **metropolitan** district of Shanghai. Neighbours thought he was poor. He wore old clothes, cooked simple meals in a **microwave** and bought only the cheapest vegetables. Children joked that he was a **miser** who counted every coin. When he died last year, everyone was shocked to learn that he was a **millionaire**.\n\nMr. Qian had been an engineer specialising in **metallurgy**. In the 1980s, he helped design a new kind of **microprocessor** for factory machines, and he invested his savings wisely. Yet he spent **minimal** amounts on himself, preferring to **minimize** his needs. \"Comfort is not happiness,\" he once told a neighbour.\n\nHis will contained many surprises. He left most of his money to help **migrant** workers who **migrate** from the countryside to find jobs in the city. Many of them live in **misery**, he wrote, working long hours for little pay. He also donated a rare **meteorite** to a science museum, along with a collection of **microscopic** fossils.\n\nNeighbours remembered his kindness. He rarely joined in social events, preferring not to **mingle** with crowds, but he often helped children with their homework. He was tolerant of their **mischief**, laughing when they played tricks on him. He kept a **miniature** garden on his balcony, growing **mint** and small flowers.\n\nSome local activists, known for their **militant** campaigns against inequality, praised his generosity as a **mighty** example to the rich. His funeral was simple. Mourners drank **milky** tea in a small hall, and someone read his favourite line: \"A life is measured not by what we keep, but by what we give.\""},"56":{"w":["misfortune","missionary","mitten","mixer","mob","mobility","mobilize","mock","moderately","modesty","modification","modulate","module","molecular","momentary","momentum","monarch","monetary","monk","monopoly"],"title":"The Mountain Monastery","t":"High in the mountains of Sichuan stands a Buddhist monastery that was built eight hundred years ago by order of a **monarch**. For centuries it was home to about fifty **monks|monk**, but by the 1990s only three remained. Today, it has become a small guesthouse, and visitors come from all over the world to experience its quiet way of life.\n\nThe change began with **misfortune**. In 2008, an earthquake damaged the main hall, and the monks lacked the **monetary** resources to repair it. The head monk, a man known for his **modesty**, decided to **mobilize** help from local villagers and volunteers. Within months, the **momentum** of the project had grown, and donations arrived from around the country.\n\nGuests must follow simple rules. They wake at five, eat **moderately** and spend time in silence. In winter, the temperature falls far below zero, and visitors wear thick **mittens|mitten** while walking in the courtyard. The monks teach a short **module** on meditation, showing guests how to **modulate** their breathing.\n\nNot everyone respects the monastery's peace. Some tourists arrive in a noisy **mob**, taking photos everywhere, and a few even **mock** the monks' customs. The head monk responds with patience. \"Their anger is **momentary**,\" he says. \"Peace lasts longer.\"\n\nThe monastery has an interesting history. A Western **missionary** stayed there in the 1920s and wrote a book about the monks' lives. More recently, a **molecular** biologist spent a summer there studying the effects of meditation on the brain. Small **modifications|modification** have made the buildings safer, and a new road has improved **mobility** for elderly visitors. But the monks have refused offers from tour companies seeking a **monopoly** on bookings. A sound engineer once recorded their chanting using a portable **mixer**, and the recording has been played around the world."},"57":{"w":["monster","monstrous","moor","mop","morality","mortgage","mosque","moss","motorway","mountainous","multiplication","multitude","municipal","murmur","muscular","muse","mustard","myth","naive","napkin"],"title":"A House on the Moor","t":"Five years ago, my aunt and her husband left their jobs in London and bought an old stone cottage on a windswept **moor** in northern England. To pay for it, they took out a large **mortgage**, and friends thought they were **naive** to leave the city. \"We were tired of traffic, offices and the **motorway**,\" my aunt says. \"We wanted space.\"\n\nThe landscape is wild and **mountainous**, covered with heather, rocks and soft green **moss**. The nearest town is ten kilometres away, with a church, a **mosque**, a market and a small **municipal** library. My uncle, who is tall and **muscular**, does most of the heavy work, repairing walls and chopping wood. My aunt spends her mornings painting, finding her **muse** in the changing light over the hills.\n\nLocal people love to tell stories. One old **myth** claims that a **monster** lives in the lake below the cottage, a **monstrous** black dog with glowing eyes. My aunt laughs at the tale, but she admits that the wind sometimes sounds like a **murmur** of voices at night.\n\nLife on the moor has taught them a different **morality** of time. They no longer rush; they eat slowly, using cloth **napkins|napkin** and homemade **mustard** made from an old family recipe. On rainy days, my uncle has to **mop** the kitchen floor when water comes in under the door.\n\nTheir daughter attends the village school, where there are only twelve pupils. She is learning **multiplication** tables and the names of birds, and she can identify a **multitude** of wild flowers. \"I used to think the countryside was boring,\" she says. \"Now I know it's full of life.\""},"58":{"w":["narration","narrative","nave","necessitate","negligible","negotiate","negotiation","neighbouring","neuron","neutron","nickel","nickname","nightmare","nominal","nominate","nonetheless","norm","normalization","northward","northwards"],"title":"A Scientist's Story","t":"Last year, a famous neuroscientist, Professor Lin, published her memoirs. The book's **narrative** follows her from a poor childhood in a small village to a career at one of the world's top universities. Her clear, honest **narration** has made the book a bestseller, and she was recently **nominated|nominate** for a national literary prize.\n\nShe grew up in a family of scientists. Her father was a physicist who studied the behaviour of the **neutron**, and he encouraged her to ask questions about everything. As a child, she earned the **nickname** \"Little Why\" because of her endless curiosity. Her family moved **northward** several times for her father's work, and she attended schools in four provinces.\n\nHer own research focuses on the **neuron**, the basic cell of the brain. She studies how memories are formed and lost. One chapter describes the **nightmare** of losing years of data when her laboratory flooded, an experience that would **necessitate** starting several projects again. The financial loss was not **negligible**: it cost millions of yuan.\n\nShe also writes about life as a woman in science. When she began her career, it was the **norm** for women to leave research after marriage. She refused. Her salary was **nominal** at first, but she continued **nonetheless**. Later, she had to **negotiate** with university leaders for equal pay, and the **negotiation** lasted two years.\n\nThe book ends with her return to her home village. She describes walking **northwards** along a river to her old school, which was once a church; she remembers studying in its **nave**. She mentions the **normalization** of relations between China and **neighbouring** countries, which allowed scientists to collaborate across borders. The final page describes an old **nickel** coin her father gave her, which she still carries for luck."},"59":{"w":["notable","notation","noted","notification","notorious","notwithstanding","nought","nourish","nourishment","novelty","numerical","nun","nurture","nutrient","nutrition","oak","oar","oath","obedience","obedient"],"title":"The Convent Garden","t":"On a hillside in southern France, a group of elderly **nuns|nun** runs a large vegetable garden behind their convent. They have lived there for decades, following a life of prayer, work and **obedience**. When they entered the convent, each nun took an **oath** to live simply and serve others.\n\nThe garden is **notable** for its beauty and productivity. Rows of vegetables stand beneath an ancient **oak** tree, and herbs grow along the stone walls. The sisters believe that good soil must be **nourished|nourish** with compost, not chemicals. \"If we **nurture** the earth,\" says Sister Marie, the oldest nun and a **noted** expert on organic farming, \"it will feed us.\"\n\nNutrition is central to their work. They grow food rich in every important **nutrient**, and they give vegetables to poor families in the village. A local doctor says the convent's food has improved **nutrition** in the area and provided valuable **nourishment** for children. Sister Marie keeps detailed records of every harvest, using an old **numerical** **notation** she learned as a girl.\n\nThe convent has a long history. In the eighteenth century, a **notorious** thief hid there from the police, and the nuns gave him shelter. **Notwithstanding** his crimes, they believed everyone deserved kindness. Today, visitors find the convent a **novelty**, and some arrive by boat, rowing with a single **oar** across the lake.\n\nThe nuns are cheerful and practical. Sister Marie laughs when visitors ask whether she is always **obedient**. \"Not always,\" she admits. \"God knows I argue.\" When the local government sent an official **notification** that the convent's land might be sold for development, the sisters refused to give up. \"We have nothing to lose,\" Sister Marie said. \"We started with **nought**, and we will end with nought.\""},"60":{"w":["obese","obscene","obsession","obstinate","odor","offensive","offset","offspring","ohm","olive","onward","opaque","opium","optimal","optimism","optimum","orchard","ordinarily","orientation","ornamental"],"title":"The Olive Orchard","t":"On a sunny hillside in southern Spain, a woman named Elena runs an **olive** **orchard** that has belonged to her family for six generations. Some of the trees are over five hundred years old, with twisted trunks and silver-green leaves. Visitors often mistake them for **ornamental** trees, but they produce some of the finest oil in the region.\n\nElena's love of olives began in childhood and soon became an **obsession**. She studied agriculture at university and returned home determined to improve the farm. \"**Ordinarily**, young people leave the countryside,\" she says. \"I came back.\" Her parents were **obstinate** at first, refusing to change their traditional methods, but they gradually accepted her ideas.\n\nShe has worked hard to find the **optimal** conditions for her trees. The **orientation** of the slope, facing south, gives the trees **optimum** sunlight. She tests the soil regularly, and she uses solar panels to power the irrigation pumps; she jokes that she now understands more about **ohms|ohm** and volts than about cooking.\n\nThe work is not always pleasant. During the harvest, the press room fills with a strong **odor** of crushed fruit, and the oil must be stored in **opaque** containers to protect it from light. Some buyers make **offensive** comments about women farmers, and wealthy companies offer **obscene** sums of money for the land. Elena always refuses.\n\nShe is also concerned about health. Many people in Europe are now **obese**, and she believes olive oil can be part of a healthier diet. Her family history includes darker chapters: an ancestor traded **opium** in the nineteenth century, a fact she discusses openly. Profits from oil now help **offset** the cost of new trees. Elena's **optimism** is infectious. She hopes her **offspring** will continue the work. \"Olives teach patience,\" she says. \"From here, we can only move **onward**.\""},"61":{"w":["orthodox","outbreak","outcry","outermost","outlaw","outrage","overestimate","overflow","overhang","overhear","overlap","overload","overt","overthrow","overwhelm","overwhelming","oxide","oxidize","oyster","pal"],"title":"The Oyster Crisis","t":"For generations, families on a group of small islands off the east coast have made their living by farming **oysters|oyster**. The **outermost** island, where cliffs **overhang** the sea, is famous for producing the finest shellfish in the region. But three years ago, a sudden **outbreak** of disease killed almost half the oysters, and the islanders were desperate.\n\nScientists soon discovered the cause. During heavy rains, a sewage system on the mainland would **overflow**, sending waste into the sea. A nearby factory had also released metal **oxide** waste, and old iron cages in the water had begun to **oxidize**, adding rust to the polluted mix. The system had been under heavy **overload** for years, but nobody had acted.\n\nThe discovery caused a public **outcry**. Fishermen expressed their **outrage** at a meeting with officials, and newspapers accused the government of **overt** neglect. Different agencies had **overlapping|overlap** responsibilities, and each blamed the others. One fisherman claimed he had happened to **overhear** an official admitting that warnings had been ignored.\n\nThe scale of the damage was **overwhelming**. Many families lost their entire income, and the stress began to **overwhelm** the community. Some experts warned that people should not **overestimate** how quickly the sea could recover.\n\nEventually, the government acted. It **outlawed|outlaw** the dumping of industrial waste and spent millions upgrading the sewage system. Some young farmers rejected **orthodox** methods and began raising oysters in floating baskets, which proved healthier. One islander joked that the crisis had helped them **overthrow** a hundred years of bad habits. My **pal** Jun, whose family farms oysters, says business has finally returned to normal. \"We learned that the sea is not a rubbish bin,\" he says."},"62":{"w":["pamphlet","panic","panther","pantry","parachute","paradox","paradoxically","paralysis","paralyze","parameter","participant","partition","pasture","patent","pathetic","patriot","patriotic","patrol","patron","payoff"],"title":"The Night the Panther Escaped","t":"Last summer, a black **panther** escaped from a wildlife park near our town. For one night, there was **panic** in the neighbourhood. Police officers began to **patrol** the streets, and the park staff handed out a **pamphlet** telling residents to stay indoors and keep pets inside.\n\nThe escape seemed like a **paradox**: the park had recently installed new fences designed to the highest safety standards. **Paradoxically**, the problem was not the fence itself but a wooden **partition** inside the animal house, which had rotted during the rainy season. The panther simply pushed it aside and walked out.\n\nThe park's director, Mr. Liang, is a former soldier and a proud **patriot** who once served in a **patriotic** youth organisation. He spent the night leading the search. Every **participant** in the search team had to follow strict rules, and the search area was defined by careful **parameters|parameter**. A rescue team even prepared to drop supplies by **parachute** into the forest if the search continued for days.\n\nThe panther was finally found at dawn, hiding in a farmer's **pantry**, eating a bag of dried meat. It was calm and sleepy. A vet used a dart to **paralyze** it temporarily, and the **paralysis** wore off a few hours later, once the animal was safely back in its enclosure. The farmer was shocked but laughed about it later. \"My dog was **pathetic**,\" he said. \"He slept through the whole thing.\"\n\nThe incident had an unexpected **payoff**. Publicity attracted thousands of new visitors, and a wealthy **patron** donated money for new buildings. A local engineer even received a **patent** for a tracking collar inspired by the escape. The panther now lives in a larger enclosure beside an open **pasture**, where children can safely watch it from a viewing platform."},"63":{"w":["peacock","peck","peculiarity","pedal","pedestrian","pedlar","peer","penalty","pendulum","penetration","perception","perch","perfection","peril","perimeter","periodic","periodical","peripheral","perish","permeate"],"title":"Cycling Through Old Villages","t":"Last spring, I joined a group of friends on a cycling tour through the villages of southern Anhui. We spent a week riding along narrow country roads, and by the end, my legs ached from pushing the **pedals|pedal** up endless hills. It was one of the best trips of my life.\n\nEvery village had its own **peculiarity**. In one, a **peacock** walked freely through the streets, spreading its tail for tourists. In another, chickens would **peck** at grain in the square while an old **pedlar** sold sweets from a wooden box. Birds would **perch** on the roofs of white houses with black tiles, and the smell of tea seemed to **permeate** the air.\n\nOur guide warned us about the **peril** of riding on busy roads. Trucks often ignore cyclists, and a **pedestrian** was injured on the highway the day before we arrived. Local police issue a heavy **penalty** for dangerous driving, but accidents still happen. We usually stayed on **peripheral** roads around the **perimeter** of the villages.\n\nThe trip changed my **perception** of the countryside. I had imagined it as a place of poverty, but I found communities full of life and pride. In one village, an old man showed us a clock whose **pendulum** had swung for a hundred years. He said **perfection** was not possible, but care was.\n\nOur guide was a keen reader who brought a **periodical** about rural history. He explained that the region suffers **periodic** floods, and many old buildings **perish** when rivers rise. He also told us about the **penetration** of modern technology: almost every farmer now has a smartphone. As we left, I turned to **peer** back at the misty hills, hoping to return one day."},"64":{"w":["permissible","perpendicular","perpetual","perplex","persecute","persevere","persistence","persistent","perspiration","persuasion","pertinent","pest","petition","petty","philanthropic","philanthropy","photoelectric","photography","physically","physiological"],"title":"The Persistent Photographer","t":"For thirty years, Mr. Jiang has used **photography** to document poverty in rural China. His pictures have appeared in newspapers around the world, and he has used the money from his work to support **philanthropic** projects in the villages he visits. \"**Philanthropy** is not about money,\" he says. \"It's about paying attention.\"\n\nHis work is **physically** demanding. He often walks for days through mountains carrying heavy equipment, and in summer his shirts are soaked with **perspiration**. Doctors who examined him found remarkable **physiological** strength for a man of sixty-five. He says the secret is simple: **persistence**.\n\nHe began his career in difficult times. In the 1980s, photographers who showed poverty were sometimes **persecuted|persecute** by officials who wanted to hide problems. Mr. Jiang was **persistent**, and he refused to stop. He believes it is never **permissible** to look away from suffering.\n\nHis methods are unusual. He refuses to use modern cameras with **photoelectric** sensors and still develops his film by hand. Many younger photographers are **perplexed|perplex** by his choice. He often shoots from strange angles, holding the camera **perpendicular** to the ground to capture tall mountain houses.\n\nOver the years, he has used **persuasion** rather than anger to change things. He started a **petition** asking the government to build schools in remote areas, and it gathered a million signatures. Critics once called his concerns **petty**, but his questions were always **pertinent**. He admits that some officials treat him as a **pest**. His life has been a **perpetual** struggle, but he says he will **persevere** as long as he can hold a camera."},"65":{"w":["pickle","pier","pilgrim","pineapple","pious","pirate","piston","plague","plateau","plausible","plea","plead","pledge","plump","plunder","plutocrat","pneumatic","polar","polarity","polymer"],"title":"The Pirate Museum","t":"On an old **pier** in a southern port city, there is a small museum dedicated to the history of **pirates|pirate** in the South China Sea. In the eighteenth century, pirate fleets would **plunder** merchant ships carrying silk, tea and spices. One famous pirate leader is said to have controlled more ships than the emperor's navy.\n\nThe museum tells many stories. Sailors on long voyages survived on rice, salted fish and **pickle**, and many died from disease. A **plague** once killed half the population of the port. Ships also carried **pious** **pilgrims|pilgrim** travelling to temples on distant islands, and some pilgrims wrote diaries describing pirate attacks. Merchants traded **pineapple** and sugar from the islands, and a few became so rich that historians describe them as **plutocrats|plutocrat**.\n\nOne exhibit describes the trial of a captured pirate. He made a **plea** for mercy, and his wife came to **plead** on his behalf. The judge accepted a **pledge** that he would never return to the sea. A portrait shows him as a **plump**, smiling man, not at all like the frightening figures of films. Historians say this version of events is **plausible**, though records are incomplete.\n\nThe museum also covers modern shipping. Visitors can see a steam engine with a giant **piston**, **pneumatic** tools used to repair ships and ropes made from strong **polymer** fibres. A special room describes the voyage of a research ship through **polar** waters to Antarctica.\n\nVisitor numbers have reached a **plateau** in recent years, and the director worries about the future. Opinions about pirates show a strange **polarity**: some visitors see them as criminals, others as romantic heroes. \"The truth is somewhere in between,\" the director says."},"66":{"w":["ponder","pope","popularity","porcelain","pore","portray","positively","postal","poster","postulate","poultry","prairie","preach","precede","precedent","precipitate","preclude","predecessor","predestine","prediction"],"title":"The Porcelain Collector","t":"Mr. Tang spent most of his life on a farm on the northern **prairie**, raising **poultry** and growing wheat. Yet his real passion lay far from the fields: he collected antique Chinese **porcelain**. Over fifty years, he gathered more than three hundred pieces, and his collection is now one of the most admired in the country.\n\nHis interest began by chance. As a boy, he saw a **poster** advertising a museum exhibition and persuaded his father to take him. He would later **ponder** for hours over a single blue-and-white vase. In the evenings, after work, he would **pore** over books and auction catalogues, learning to identify the age and origin of each piece.\n\nHe bought most of his collection by **postal** order, long before the internet made such purchases easy. Experts warned him that he could not assume every piece was genuine, and he learned to **postulate** a theory about each object before testing it. His early **predictions|prediction** about which styles would rise in value proved remarkably accurate, and the **popularity** of his favourite pieces grew rapidly.\n\nHis most famous object is a bowl once given as a gift to a **pope** in the sixteenth century. Historians say it was made by a master potter whose **predecessor** had designed bowls for the imperial court. The bowl's journey from China to Rome set a **precedent** for cultural exchange between East and West.\n\nMr. Tang never liked to **preach** about art. When journalists asked him to **portray** himself as an expert, he refused. \"I'm a farmer who loves beautiful things,\" he said. His decision to donate the collection to a public museum was **precipitated|precipitate** by a serious illness. He did not want age to **preclude** others from enjoying it. Some say his love for porcelain was **predestined|predestine**, since his grandfather had been a potter. Visitors who see the collection respond **positively**, and the museum hopes a new wing will **precede** a national tour."},"67":{"w":["predominant","predominantly","predominate","pregnancy","pregnant","premature","premise","premium","prerequisite","prescription","presentation","preservation","preset","preside","presidency","prestige","presume","pretext","prevailing","prevalence"],"title":"A Young Doctor's First Year","t":"Dr. Chen began her career last year in a county hospital in western China. Her medical degree was a **prerequisite** for the job, but she quickly learned that textbooks could not prepare her for everything. Her first month was spent mainly in the obstetrics department, where she cared for women during **pregnancy** and childbirth.\n\nMost of her patients were young farmers, and many arrived at the hospital only when they were already heavily **pregnant**. One night, a baby was born two months **premature**, weighing just over a kilogram. Dr. Chen and the nurses worked through the night to keep the baby warm and breathing. \"That was the moment I truly understood my job,\" she says.\n\nShe noticed that a few diseases **predominate** in the region. The **prevalence** of high blood pressure is especially worrying, and the **prevailing** diet, rich in salt and fat, makes the problem worse. Women are **predominantly** responsible for cooking, so Dr. Chen gives regular talks to village women about healthy eating. Writing a **prescription** is easy, she says, but changing habits is harder. Still, she never **presumes|presume** that patients will ignore her advice.\n\nThe hospital has limited resources. Patients must pay a **premium** for certain imported drugs, and the operating rooms are used on a **preset** schedule. Dr. Chen once gave a **presentation** to county officials asking for more equipment. The director of health, who would later win the **presidency** of a national medical association, was impressed.\n\nDr. Chen says that her **predominant** feeling is gratitude. She knows that rural work carries less **prestige** than a job in a big city hospital, and some colleagues use any **pretext** to transfer away. But she believes the **premise** of medicine is to serve those in need. Next year, she will **preside** over a new programme for the **preservation** of traditional herbal knowledge."},"68":{"w":["prevalent","prevention","prey","prick","primate","principally","printer","prism","privacy","probe","proceeding","producer","productivity","proficiency","proficient","profile","profound","prohibition","projecting","projector"],"title":"Filming the Forest Primates","t":"Last year, a television **producer** named Ms. Kong spent six months in the rainforests of Borneo filming a documentary about orangutans and other **primates|primate**. The project was **principally** funded by an international wildlife charity, and the finished film has won several awards for its **profound** portrait of animal life.\n\nThe filming was difficult. The team had to carry heavy equipment through thick forest, where thorns would **prick** their hands and leeches attached themselves to their legs. Cameras were hidden in trees, with **projecting** branches providing natural cover. The team respected the animals' **privacy** and never approached too closely.\n\nMs. Kong learned a great deal about primate behaviour. She watched mothers teach their young to find food and avoid becoming **prey** to snakes and wild cats. Scientists on the team used a small electronic **probe** to measure temperature and humidity in the nests. Their data revealed that illegal logging is **prevalent** in the region, threatening many species.\n\nThe film also explores human efforts at **prevention**. Local rangers enforce a strict **prohibition** on hunting, and the charity runs programmes to increase farmers' **productivity** so that they do not need to clear more forest. One ranger, **proficient** in three languages, became the film's guide; his **proficiency** with local dialects helped the team build trust with villagers.\n\nAfter filming, Ms. Kong spent months editing. She built a rough cut using a **projector** in a small studio, showing the footage on a white wall. Light from a glass **prism** on the windowsill sometimes scattered colours across the images. When the charity's **printer** produced the first posters, her **profile** appeared beside a baby orangutan. The film was screened at the **proceedings|proceeding** of an international conservation conference."},"69":{"w":["prolong","prominence","promotion","promptly","prone","propaganda","propagate","propagation","propel","propeller","prophecy","prophet","proposition","propulsion","prose","prospective","protend","proton","prototype","proverb"],"title":"The Boat Builder's Prototype","t":"In a small workshop near the river, a retired engineer named Mr. Huang has spent five years building a new kind of electric boat. His **prototype** is quiet, light and powered by solar panels. Instead of a traditional **propeller**, it uses a fish-like tail to **propel** itself through the water, a form of **propulsion** inspired by nature.\n\nMr. Huang's interest in engineering began in childhood. His father loved to quote an old **proverb**: \"A river is crossed one stone at a time.\" As a student, he studied physics and wrote a thesis on the behaviour of the **proton**. Later, he worked for a shipbuilding company, where he rose to **prominence** as a designer before receiving a **promotion** to chief engineer.\n\nHis idea was not popular at first. Investors called his **proposition** unrealistic, and some colleagues made a **prophecy** that it would fail. \"Everyone is a **prophet** when it comes to other people's failures,\" he jokes. He admits that he is **prone** to stubbornness, but he believes that patience can **prolong** a dream until it becomes reality.\n\nThe boat is beautifully designed. Its long, thin front seems to **protend** over the water like the neck of a swan. **Prospective** buyers, including tourist companies and fishermen, have begun to show interest. When a company offered to buy the design, Mr. Huang **promptly** refused. He wants to share it freely.\n\nHe hopes the technology will **propagate** quickly, and he has written a short book in clear, simple **prose** explaining how it works. He dislikes commercial **propaganda** and prefers honest explanations. \"The **propagation** of good ideas is the best gift we can give the future,\" he says."},"70":{"w":["provocative","provoke","provost","proximity","prudent","psychology","publicity","pulley","pumpkin","puppy","pyjamas","qualification","qualitative","quantify","quantitative","quartz","quench","quest","questionable","questionnaire"],"title":"The Happiness Survey","t":"Last spring, the psychology department at our university launched a research project on student happiness. Each student received a **questionnaire** asking about sleep, study, friendship and stress. The project's goal was to **quantify** something that many people think cannot be measured.\n\nThe research combined **quantitative** and **qualitative** methods. The questionnaire produced numbers, while interviews gave students the chance to explain their feelings in their own words. The project leader, Professor Yuan, said that **psychology** must use both approaches. \"Numbers tell us what happens,\" she said. \"Stories tell us why.\"\n\nSome results were surprising. Students who lived in close **proximity** to parks reported higher levels of happiness. Those with pets were especially cheerful; one student said that adopting a **puppy** had changed her life. Many students admitted that they studied in their **pyjamas** at night and rarely slept before two in the morning.\n\nThe project attracted a lot of **publicity**, and some findings were **provocative**. One newspaper headline claimed that \"Exams Make Students Miserable,\" which seemed designed to **provoke** anger. The university **provost** warned that the media had used a **questionable** interpretation of the data. Professor Yuan urged everyone to be **prudent** in drawing conclusions.\n\nThe research also had a playful side. In autumn, students organised a festival where they carved **pumpkins|pumpkin** and competed to lift weights using a simple rope and **pulley**. A geology student gave away small pieces of **quartz** as lucky charms, and volunteers served lemonade to **quench** everyone's thirst. Professor Yuan says that the **quest** for happiness is not a luxury. Many students said that good friends mattered more to them than any formal **qualification**."},"71":{"w":["quiver","radial","radiant","radiator","rainforest","raisin","rally","ramble","ranch","random","rap","rapidity","rapture","rascal","rash","rating","rattle","reactor","realistic","realization"],"title":"A Summer on the Ranch","t":"When I was sixteen, I spent a summer working on my uncle's **ranch** in Inner Mongolia. I had grown up in a city apartment, where the only heat came from a **radiator** under the window, and I knew nothing about horses or sheep. My parents hoped the experience would teach me to be less **rash** and more responsible.\n\nThe first weeks were hard. I was clumsy and slow, and my uncle called me a lazy **rascal** whenever I slept late. One morning, I made the **rash** decision to ride a young horse without permission. It threw me into the grass, and I lay there with my whole body beginning to **quiver** with shock. My uncle didn't shout; he simply helped me up and said, \"Next time, ask.\"\n\nSlowly, I came to love the work. I learned to recognise each sheep and to read the weather from the clouds. I was amazed at the **rapidity** with which storms could appear. At night, the wind would **rattle** the windows, and I would listen to my uncle **ramble** on about his youth. He kept a box of **raisins|raisin** in his pocket and gave them to the horses as treats.\n\nThe highlight of the summer was a local horse **rally**. Riders came from across the region, and spectators shouted with **rapture** as the horses raced across the grassland. Riders started in a **random** order drawn from a hat, and judges gave each one a **rating** for speed and style. A young herder even performed a **rap** song about the grassland that made everyone laugh.\n\nBy the end of the summer, I had come to a **realization**: life on the ranch was not easy, but it was honest. My uncle was **realistic** about the challenges: drought, falling prices and a plan to build a nuclear **reactor** on nearby grassland all made the future uncertain. On my last evening, he pointed to the fields, which spread out in a **radial** pattern around the well. \"Some people dream of the **rainforest** or the sea,\" he said. \"This is my world.\" His face was **radiant** in the sunset."},"72":{"w":["reassure","rebuke","recession","recipe","recipient","reciprocal","reckless","reckon","reclaim","reconcile","recruitment","rectangle","rectangular","rectify","recur","recurrence","recycle","redundant","reef","referee"],"title":"Saving the Reef","t":"Two years ago, my friend Mei lost her job in a bank during the economic **recession**. Like many others, she was made **redundant** with only a month's notice. Instead of looking for another office job, she joined a volunteer programme to protect a coral **reef** in the South China Sea.\n\nThe programme's **recruitment** process was strict. Volunteers needed diving skills and a doctor's certificate, and Mei spent months training. Her parents thought her decision was **reckless**, and her father even issued a stern **rebuke**. But Mei managed to **reassure** them that the work was safe and meaningful.\n\nThe reef had been badly damaged by pollution and illegal fishing. Volunteers divided it into **rectangular** sections, each marked by a **rectangle** of floating rope, and they replanted young coral in each area. They also collected plastic waste, which was later **recycled|recycle** into building materials. Over time, they began to **reclaim** parts of the reef that had been lost.\n\nProblems would **recur** constantly. Storms damaged new coral, and the **recurrence** of illegal fishing frustrated everyone. The volunteers worked with local fishermen to **rectify** the situation. They agreed on a **reciprocal** arrangement: fishermen would avoid protected areas, and in return, the programme would help them sell fish at higher prices. A local official acted as a **referee** in disputes.\n\nMei has also learned to cook. She shares a simple **recipe** for seaweed soup with every new volunteer. Last year, she was the **recipient** of a national award for environmental work. \"I **reckon** losing my job was the best thing that ever happened to me,\" she says. \"It helped me **reconcile** my love of nature with my need to work.\""},"73":{"w":["refinery","refrain","refugee","regenerate","regenerative","regime","regiment","regularity","reimburse","rejection","rejuvenate","relay","relegate","remainder","remnant","renaissance","renovation","repay","repeal","repel"],"title":"The Theatre Reborn","t":"In the centre of an old industrial city stands a theatre built in the 1920s. For decades it was the pride of the city, but when the local oil **refinery** closed, the economy collapsed and the theatre was abandoned. Only a **remnant** of its former beauty remained: a painted ceiling and a few velvet seats.\n\nThe theatre's rebirth began with a **refugee** named Mr. Haddad. He had fled his country after the old **regime** collapsed into civil war. In his youth, he had served in an army **regiment**, but his real love was music and drama. When he arrived in the city, he saw the empty theatre and decided to bring it back to life.\n\nHis first proposals met with **rejection**. City officials doubted that an unknown immigrant could manage such a project, and some tried to **relegate** it to the bottom of the budget list. But Mr. Haddad persisted. He raised money from local businesses and promised to **repay** every loan. Volunteers worked with great **regularity**, meeting every weekend to clean and repair the building.\n\nThe **renovation** took four years. Workers installed **regenerative** heating systems that reuse energy, and a new sound system to **relay** performances to screens in the square. The council finally agreed to **reimburse** part of the costs, and it voted to **repeal** an old law that had prevented cultural events in the area.\n\nToday, the theatre is full almost every night. Critics speak of a cultural **renaissance** in the city. The theatre seems to **rejuvenate** the neighbourhood, and new cafés and shops have opened nearby. Mr. Haddad says that art can **regenerate** a community. Young people now spend their evenings there instead of on the streets. The chorus of the theatre's first musical contains a simple **refrain**: \"We are home.\" Mr. Haddad has used the **remainder** of the funds to offer free tickets to students. He says the theatre is a place that will always **repel** hatred and welcome everyone."},"74":{"w":["repertoire","replacement","representation","reproduction","reptile","requisite","resemblance","resent","resentment","reservoir","reside","residential","residual","resolute","resonance","respectable","resultant","retard","retention","retirement"],"title":"The Keeper's Last Day","t":"Last month, Mr. Wu celebrated his **retirement** after forty years as a keeper in the **reptile** house of our city zoo. He knows every snake, lizard and tortoise by name, and his **repertoire** of stories about them seems endless. Children love him, and his departure was felt with great **resonance** throughout the zoo.\n\nHe became a keeper by chance. As a young man, he worked at a **reservoir** outside the city, where he often found snakes near the water. A local scientist noticed his calm way with animals and offered him a job. \"I didn't have the **requisite** qualifications,\" he says, \"but I had patience.\"\n\nHis work focused on the **reproduction** of endangered species. He helped breed rare turtles, whose numbers in the wild had fallen sharply. The **resultant** success made the zoo famous among scientists. He also cared for older animals, some of which had **residual** injuries from being kept illegally as pets.\n\nNot everyone appreciated his work. Some residents of the nearby **residential** area complained about the zoo's noise and smells, and Mr. Wu sometimes felt **resentment** at their criticism. But he did not **resent** them for long. \"People fear what they don't understand,\" he says. He worked to **retard** the spread of fear by teaching visitors about reptiles.\n\nMr. Wu is **resolute** in his belief that every animal deserves respect. He remembers a turtle whose shell bore a strange **resemblance** to a map of China. His **retention** of such details amazes his colleagues. His **replacement**, a young biologist, says she will try to continue his work. The zoo has commissioned a **representation** of him in bronze, which will **reside** in the reptile house forever. \"He was a **respectable** man,\" the director said, \"and a good friend.\""},"75":{"w":["retort","retrospect","reunion","revelation","revision","revive","revolve","ridicule","rig","rigorous","rim","rinse","riot","rip","ripple","ritual","roam","robust","romance","rooster"],"title":"Twenty Years Later","t":"Last summer, my secondary school class held a **reunion** in our old hometown. Twenty years had passed since graduation, and many of us had not seen each other since. The organiser, my friend Hao, prepared a careful plan, though he joked that it needed constant **revision** as more people joined.\n\nThe evening began with a **ritual** from our school days: everyone stood and sang the school song. Some voices were shaky, but the moment seemed to **revive** old memories. In **retrospect**, we all agreed that those years had shaped our lives more than we realised.\n\nAs the conversation began to **revolve** around old stories, there were many surprises. The biggest **revelation** came from Lin, the quietest girl in our class, who is now a famous engineer working on an offshore oil **rig**. When someone asked if she still remembered how the boys used to **ridicule** her, she gave a sharp **retort**: \"I remember. That's why I worked so hard.\"\n\nThere were funny stories too. Hao reminded us of the day a **rooster** wandered into the classroom during an exam, causing a small **riot** of laughter. Another classmate described how he had **ripped|rip** his trousers climbing over the school wall. Our old teacher, who was known for his **rigorous** standards, laughed until tears filled his eyes.\n\nLater, we walked by the lake where we used to **roam** after school. The water was still, and a stone sent a **ripple** across the surface. One couple revealed that their **romance** had begun at that very spot. Our teacher is now eighty but remains **robust** and cheerful. Before leaving, we filled our glasses to the **rim** and drank a toast. Then someone went to **rinse** the cups in the lake, just as we did when we were young."},"76":{"w":["roundabout","royalty","ruby","rupture","ruthless","safeguard","salmon","salvation","sanction","sardine","satisfactorily","saturate","saturation","savage","scarcity","scarlet","scoff","scorch","scramble","scrap"],"title":"The Fish Cannery","t":"On the northern coast, in a small town reached by a single **roundabout** off the highway, stands an old fish cannery. For a century, it has packed **salmon** and **sardines|sardine** caught by local boats. In its best years, the factory employed half the town, and the smell of fish was everywhere.\n\nThe business has faced **ruthless** competition. Large foreign companies have flooded the market with cheap products, and **saturation** of supermarket shelves has pushed prices down. At the same time, overfishing has caused a **scarcity** of salmon in the region. Some economists **scoff** at the idea that a small cannery can survive.\n\nProblems arrived one after another. During a **savage** winter storm, a water pipe suffered a **rupture**, flooding the warehouse. The next summer, a heatwave seemed to **scorch** the town, and rising temperatures in the sea pushed fish further north. Then international **sanctions|sanction** against a major trading partner closed one of the factory's best markets. Workers began to **scramble** for jobs elsewhere.\n\nThe owner, Mrs. Holm, refused to give up. She sold **scrap** metal from old machines to raise money and found new ways to **safeguard** the business. She introduced a premium brand of tinned salmon with a **scarlet** label, sold in high-end shops. A celebrity chef used it in a television programme, and sales rose quickly. She even signed a contract to pay **royalties|royalty** to a local artist whose painting appears on the tins.\n\nThe plan has worked **satisfactorily** so far. The factory now sells in fourteen countries, and a visiting member of the Danish **royalty** once praised its quality. Mrs. Holm keeps a small **ruby** brooch that belonged to her grandmother, who founded the company, and wears it at every business meeting. \"The cannery was our family's **salvation** during hard times,\" she says. \"Now it's my turn to save it.\" Her advice to other small businesses is simple: don't try to **saturate** the market; make something special."},"77":{"w":["script","scripture","scrub","scrutiny","sculpture","seam","seaside","sector","sedentary","seemingly","segregate","seminate","senator","sensation","senseless","sensitivity","sensor","sentiment","sermon","serpent"],"title":"The Seaside Sculptor","t":"In a small **seaside** town in the south, an artist named Mr. Lu creates huge **sculptures|sculpture** from driftwood and stones found on the beach. His most famous work, a giant **serpent** curving along the sand, attracts thousands of visitors each year. **Seemingly** simple, it took him three years to finish.\n\nMr. Lu's journey to art was unusual. For twenty years, he worked in the financial **sector**, leading a **sedentary** life behind a desk. He often felt that his work was **senseless**, though it paid well. One day, while walking on the beach, he had a strange **sensation** that the shapes of the driftwood were speaking to him. He left his job a month later.\n\nHis work is now the subject of serious **scrutiny** by critics. Some praise its **sensitivity** to nature; others call it too sentimental. Mr. Lu ignores the debate. \"**Sentiment** is not a weakness,\" he says. \"Art should touch the heart.\"\n\nHis sculptures have also become part of local culture. Every spring, a priest gives a **sermon** on the beach, reading from **scripture** about the creation of the world. A local **senator** has praised Mr. Lu for bringing tourism to the town. Mr. Lu has even written a short **script** for a children's play about the serpent, and he hopes it will help **seminate** a love of art in young people.\n\nHis methods are careful. He uses a brush to **scrub** each piece of wood clean, and he joins pieces together so that each **seam** is almost invisible. He installs a small **sensor** in each large work to measure wind and moisture, protecting it from damage. He refuses to **segregate** art from daily life. \"My sculptures belong to everyone,\" he says. \"Children can climb on them.\""},"78":{"w":["setback","shabby","shadowy","shaft","shaky","sham","shameful","shark","shatter","sheer","sheriff","shipbuilding","shipment","shipwreck","shortcut","shorten","shortfall","shorthand","shove","shovel"],"title":"The Shipyard Town","t":"On the rocky coast of Maine, in the northeastern United States, there is a small town famous for **shipbuilding**. For two hundred years, its shipyard produced fishing boats, cargo ships and even navy vessels. The town lies below **sheer** cliffs, and the harbour is said to be the safest on the coast.\n\nThe town's history includes many tragedies. A famous **shipwreck** in 1898 killed forty sailors, and old stories describe **sharks|shark** circling the broken ship. A museum displays a **shaky** photograph of the survivors, along with a **shovel** used to dig graves on the beach. In the 1920s, the town **sheriff** arrested a gang of smugglers who used a **shadowy** cave below the cliffs to hide their goods.\n\nIn recent decades, the shipyard suffered a serious **setback**. Orders fell, and the company faced a large financial **shortfall**. Some managers tried to **shorten** working hours and cut wages, and a few even tried a **sham** sale of the yard to avoid paying debts. Workers called it **shameful**. Many families left, and the town's main street became **shabby** and quiet.\n\nThe turning point came when a young engineer proposed building electric ferries. Her idea seemed to **shatter** old assumptions about what the yard could do. There was no **shortcut** to success: workers had to learn new skills, and the first ferry took three years to complete. The company had to **shove** old machines aside and install new equipment in the main **shaft** of the workshop.\n\nToday, the yard is busy again. Its first **shipment** of electric ferries left last spring, headed for Norway. The old foreman still takes notes in **shorthand** during meetings, a skill he learned sixty years ago. \"We nearly lost everything,\" he says. \"Now we're building the future.\""},"79":{"w":["shrewd","shrill","shrimp","shrine","shrub","shutter","siege","sift","signify","silicon","similarity","simulate","simultaneous","simultaneously","sincerity","siren","sitting-room","situated","skeleton","skeptical"],"title":"The Hidden Shrine","t":"In a quiet valley in the mountains of Gansu, archaeologists recently discovered an ancient **shrine** hidden beneath a field of **shrubs|shrub**. The site is **situated** near an old trading road, and experts believe it dates back more than a thousand years. Local farmers had passed it every day without knowing it was there.\n\nThe discovery began with a **shrewd** observation. A young archaeologist noticed that the plants in one area grew in strange straight lines, which might **signify** walls beneath the surface. When the team began to dig, they found stone foundations, painted pottery and the **skeleton** of a horse buried with great care.\n\nThe team worked slowly. They had to **sift** every handful of soil to find small objects such as coins and beads. They also used modern technology: drones equipped with **silicon** sensors scanned the ground, and computer programs helped to **simulate** what the shrine might have looked like. The team used several methods **simultaneously**, combining traditional digging with digital mapping.\n\nHistorians were at first **skeptical** about the site's age. But **similarity** between the pottery and pieces found in other ancient cities helped confirm the date. Written records describe a **siege** in the area in the eighth century, and some experts believe the shrine was abandoned during that war. The **simultaneous** discovery of weapons nearby supports this theory.\n\nLocal villagers have welcomed the project. One elderly farmer invited the team into his **sitting-room** and served dried **shrimp** and tea. He spoke with great **sincerity** about his pride in the discovery. Every evening, a **shrill** wind blows through the valley, and the farmer's grandson takes photos with an old camera, its **shutter** clicking softly. Once, an ambulance **siren** echoed through the hills when a worker fell, but he was not seriously hurt."},"80":{"w":["skip","skyscraper","slack","slander","slang","slap","slaughter","slim","slot","slumber","smuggle","snack","snail","snatch","sneak","sneer","sneeze","sniff","snob","snobbish"],"title":"The Snob from the City","t":"When my cousin Liang moved from Shanghai to a small mountain village to teach English, he arrived as a terrible **snob**. He had grown up in a flat on the fortieth floor of a **skyscraper**, and he looked down on everything about rural life. He would **sneer** at the villagers' clothes and **sniff** at the simple food, and his **snobbish** attitude made him few friends.\n\nHis first weeks were difficult. The village had no restaurants, only a small shop that sold **snacks|snack** and cigarettes. He was allergic to dust and began to **sneeze** every time he entered the classroom. The children laughed at him and taught him local **slang** that he did not understand. Some tried to **sneak** out of his lessons, and one boy would **snatch** his textbook and run away.\n\nThen something changed. During a rainstorm, Liang slipped on the muddy path, and an old farmer helped him up. The farmer invited him home, gave him dry clothes and let him **slumber** by the fire. When Liang woke, the farmer's wife had cooked a feast. They had even prepared a pig that was sent for **slaughter** that morning, to celebrate the New Year early.\n\nLiang's attitude began to soften. He stopped wanting to **skip** village events and started joining in. He learned how farmers protect their vegetables from every **snail** and insect. When he heard a rumour that a neighbour had tried to **smuggle** wild animals to the city, he refused to repeat it, knowing that such **slander** could ruin a family's reputation.\n\nToday, Liang is a popular teacher. He has grown **slim** from walking up and down the mountain every day, and he fills every free **slot** in his timetable with extra lessons. When he visits Shanghai, his friends tease him. Once, a friend gave him a playful **slap** on the back and asked if he was getting **slack**. \"Not at all,\" Liang said. \"I've never worked harder.\""},"81":{"w":["snore","soar","sociology","sodium","sofa","soften","softness","solidarity","solidify","solitary","solo","solvable","sophisticate","sorrowful","southwards","souvenir","sovereign","soy","spacious","spatial"],"title":"A Homestay in the South","t":"Last winter, as part of a **sociology** project, I spent a month living with an elderly couple in a fishing village in Fujian. My research topic was how small communities maintain **solidarity** as young people move away. I travelled **southwards** by train for two days, carrying a notebook and a bag of **souvenirs|souvenir** from Beijing as gifts.\n\nThe couple's house was surprisingly **spacious**, with a courtyard full of plants. Mr. and Mrs. Lin had lived there for fifty years. Every evening, Mr. Lin fell asleep on the old **sofa** after dinner and began to **snore** loudly, while his wife laughed and covered him with a blanket. Their love seemed to **soften** every hard moment of village life.\n\nMrs. Lin was an excellent cook. She made her own **soy** sauce, and she taught me that the best fish needs only a little salt. \"Too much **sodium** is bad for the heart,\" she warned. I admired the **softness** of her voice and her patience with my clumsy questions.\n\nThrough interviews, I learned that the village's problems were serious but **solvable**. Many elderly people lived **solitary** lives after their children left for the cities. But neighbours had formed a strong network of mutual help, which seemed to **solidify** over time. One old fisherman, who called himself a \"village **sophisticate**\" because he had once lived in Hong Kong, organised weekly gatherings. Another man played **solo** on a bamboo flute every evening, and his music made the whole village feel less **sorrowful**.\n\nI also studied the **spatial** layout of the village. Houses were built close together around a central temple, encouraging daily contact. Villagers spoke proudly of their history; in the past, the area had resisted foreign rule and defended its **sovereign** rights. On my last day, I watched seabirds **soar** above the harbour and felt sad to leave."},"82":{"w":["specifically","specification","spectacular","spectator","spectrum","speculate","spherical","spice","spinal","spiral","spokesman","spouse","squash","squat","stabilize","stagger","stainless","stairway","stalk","stall"],"title":"The Spiral Tower","t":"Three years ago, our city completed a **spectacular** new tower on the riverbank. Its design is a giant **spiral**, twisting upwards like a seashell, and at the top sits a **spherical** viewing room made of glass. Every evening, lights in every colour of the **spectrum** move across its surface, and thousands of **spectators|spectator** gather to watch.\n\nBuilding the tower was a huge challenge. Engineers had to follow strict **specifications|specification** for wind resistance, since the river valley is famous for strong storms. The frame is made of **stainless** steel, and special weights at the top help **stabilize** the structure. A long **stairway** winds around the inside for emergencies, though most visitors take the lift.\n\nThe project was not without problems. Work began to **stall** during the second year, when money ran short. Some businessmen began to **speculate** on nearby land, driving prices higher. A worker suffered a **spinal** injury after a fall, and the company was criticised for poor safety. The company's **spokesman** apologised publicly and promised to improve conditions.\n\nNot everyone loves the tower. Some residents say its cost would **stagger** anyone who saw the final bill. Others complain that it blocks the view of the old town. Critics also argue that the city should have **specifically** invested in housing and schools instead.\n\nBut the tower has brought life to the riverbank. Around its base, there is a busy market where traders sell **spice**, fruit and vegetables. One farmer brings a cart of giant **squash** every autumn. Children **squat** on the ground watching street performers, and couples stroll along the water. One old man, whose **spouse** died last year, comes every evening to watch the lights. \"It's like a flower on its **stalk**,\" he says. \"It reminds me of her garden.\""},"83":{"w":["stammer","standardize","stationary","stationery","steak","stereo","stereotype","stew","stiffness","stillness","stimulus","stockholder","stout","straighten","straightforward","strait","strangler","stray","streamline","stride"],"title":"The Café by the Strait","t":"On a small island across a narrow **strait** from the mainland, a woman named Mrs. Ko runs a busy café near the ferry pier. She is a **stout**, cheerful woman who greets every customer by name. Her menu is **straightforward**: beef **stew**, grilled **steak**, noodles and strong tea.\n\nMrs. Ko did not always run a café. For twenty years she was a **stockholder** in a large shipping company, until it was sold and she decided to change her life. She bought the café, cleaned it from top to bottom and installed an old **stereo** that plays jazz all day. She also sells **stationery** to students who come to study at the corner tables.\n\nAs a child, Mrs. Ko had a **stammer**, and classmates laughed at her. She says the experience taught her not to judge others by **stereotype**. Today, she welcomes everyone: fishermen, tourists, students and even a **stray** cat that sleeps by the door.\n\nThe café has its own rhythm. In the early morning, the island is full of **stillness**, and the boats in the harbour remain **stationary** until the tide turns. Then the first ferry arrives, and customers rush in. Mrs. Ko moves through the room with long, confident **strides|stride**, carrying trays of food.\n\nBehind the café grows an enormous fig tree known locally as a **strangler**, because its roots wrap around other trees. Mrs. Ko says the tree inspires her: \"It keeps growing, no matter what.\" Last year, the local government tried to **standardize** café menus for tourists, but she refused to **streamline** hers. She believes that good food is a **stimulus** for good conversation. After long days, she complains of **stiffness** in her back, but she simply **straightens|straighten** up and keeps working."},"84":{"w":["strife","striking","strive","stroll","stubborn","studio","stuffy","stun","sturdy","subdivide","subdue","subjective","subordinate","subscribe","subscript","subscription","subsequently","subsidiary","substantially","subtle"],"title":"The Photographer's Studio","t":"In a quiet street behind the market, there is a small photography **studio** run by an elderly man named Mr. Ren. Its windows are filled with **striking** black-and-white portraits of local people: farmers, teachers, children and soldiers. Many visitors **stroll** past without noticing, but those who step inside are often **stunned|stun** by the quality of the work.\n\nThe studio is old and a little **stuffy**, but it is full of history. Mr. Ren's father opened it in 1950, and it has survived years of political **strife** and economic change. The building itself is **sturdy**, with thick walls that have survived two earthquakes. Mr. Ren has **subdivided|subdivide** the back room into a darkroom and a storage space.\n\nMr. Ren is famously **stubborn**. He refuses to use digital cameras and insists on developing every photograph by hand. \"Photography is **subjective**,\" he says. \"A machine cannot decide what is beautiful.\" He believes that a good photographer must **strive** for honesty, capturing the **subtle** emotions in a person's face.\n\nOver the years, his business has changed **substantially**. In the 1980s, he had three **subordinate** staff and a **subsidiary** shop in the next town. **Subsequently**, the rise of phone cameras reduced his income, and he had to close the second shop. Today, he works alone. He relies on loyal customers who **subscribe** to an annual portrait service; the **subscription** includes one family photo each year.\n\nMr. Ren also teaches photography to children. In one lesson, he explained how chemical formulas work, writing a small **subscript** number beside each letter on the board. When a lively boy could not keep still, Mr. Ren managed to **subdue** him with a single raised eyebrow. \"Patience,\" he says, \"is the photographer's real camera.\""},"85":{"w":["successor","sue","suffice","suicide","suitcase","sullen","summon","superb","superficially","superiority","supersonic","superstition","supervise","supervision","supplementary","suppress","surge","surpass","susceptible","suspension"],"title":"Breaking the Sound Barrier","t":"When Captain Zhao was a boy, he dreamed of flying faster than sound. Today, at forty-five, he is a test pilot for a company developing a new **supersonic** passenger jet. The project aims to **surpass** the speed of any commercial aircraft in history, cutting the journey from Beijing to New York to just six hours.\n\nHis work is dangerous and demanding. Before each flight, engineers **supervise** every detail, and Captain Zhao undergoes strict medical checks. At high speeds, the body is **susceptible** to extreme pressure, and pilots must be in **superb** physical condition. He never packs more than a small **suitcase**, because he spends weeks at the test centre in the desert.\n\nThe project has had difficulties. Last year, a fault in the landing gear's **suspension** system caused a near accident, and the company had to halt tests for three months. A competitor tried to **sue** the company, claiming that its designs had been copied. Some investors said that continuing the project would be commercial **suicide**, and the company's share price fell sharply before a **surge** of new funding restored confidence.\n\nCaptain Zhao has strong views about safety. He refuses to **suppress** bad news and insists on honest reports. \"Looking at a problem **superficially** is not enough,\" he says. \"We must understand every cause.\" He keeps **supplementary** notes on every flight and shares them with younger pilots under his **supervision**.\n\nHe admits that pilots have their own **superstition**: he always wears the same old watch when he flies. He is training a younger colleague to be his **successor**. On days when the young pilot seems **sullen** or nervous, Captain Zhao will **summon** him for a quiet talk. \"Technical **superiority** is not enough,\" he tells him. \"Courage and humility must **suffice** when machines fail.\""},"86":{"w":["sweeten","sweetness","symmetrical","symmetry","symphony","symposium","symptom","synthesis","synthesize","systematically","tabulate","tack","tackle","tact","tactics","tangle","tanker","taper","tar","tariff"],"title":"The Sugar Scientist","t":"Dr. Fang is a food chemist who has spent ten years trying to **synthesize** a natural sugar substitute. Her goal is to **sweeten** food without the health risks of sugar. \"People love **sweetness**,\" she says, \"but too much sugar is making the world sick.\" Doctors often see the **symptoms|symptom** of diabetes in patients as young as twenty.\n\nHer research is careful and slow. She tests hundreds of plant compounds **systematically**, recording their taste, safety and cost. Her assistant must **tabulate** the results each week. The **synthesis** of a single promising compound can take months, and many experiments fail.\n\nLast year, she presented her findings at an international **symposium** in Geneva. Her talk attracted great interest, because she had found a compound that tastes almost exactly like sugar. One food company wanted to buy the rights immediately, but Dr. Fang used **tact** and patience in the negotiations. She knew that companies sometimes use aggressive **tactics** to buy research cheaply.\n\nThere were other problems to **tackle**. High import **tariffs|tariff** on certain plants made production expensive, and the plants had to be shipped across the ocean in refrigerated containers on a giant **tanker**. Regulations became a **tangle** of rules from different countries. Her team had to change **tack** several times before finding a solution.\n\nDr. Fang also cares about design. She works with a chocolate maker who creates **symmetrical** sweets shaped like flowers, which **taper** to a fine point. \"**Symmetry** makes food look more delicious,\" she explains. On weekends, she relaxes by listening to a **symphony** at the city concert hall. She laughs that her life is not as sweet as people imagine: on the road to her laboratory, workers are always spreading fresh **tar** to repair the potholes."},"87":{"w":["tease","telex","temperament","tempo","tenable","tensile","tentative","terminate","terminology","terrace","terrain","terrify","terrorist","testify","testimony","texture","thanksgiving","theft","theorem","therapy"],"title":"The Engineer's Testimony","t":"Five years ago, my uncle, a retired engineer, was asked to **testify** in a court case about the **theft** of steel cables from a construction site. The cables were made of high-**tensile** steel, used in bridges, and they were worth millions of yuan. His **testimony** helped prove that the thieves had inside help.\n\nMy uncle has a calm **temperament**, but the case made him nervous. Lawyers used complex legal **terminology**, and he struggled to follow the fast **tempo** of the questions. He offered only a **tentative** answer at first, then explained his reasoning clearly. \"The defence's argument was not **tenable**,\" he told me later. \"The cables could not have disappeared without someone opening the gate.\"\n\nThe case reminded him of his early career. In the 1980s, he worked for a company that sent orders to suppliers by **telex**, and messages sometimes took hours to arrive. He helped build roads across mountain **terrain** in the southwest, where storms could **terrify** even experienced workers. He still loves mathematics and enjoys explaining a geometry **theorem** to his grandchildren.\n\nAfter retirement, he suffered from back pain and began physical **therapy**. He now lives in a **terrace** house with a small garden, where he grows vegetables of every colour and **texture**. His grandchildren like to **tease** him about his old-fashioned habits, such as writing letters by hand.\n\nHe also enjoys films. His favourite is a thriller about a detective who stops a **terrorist** attack on a bridge, though he complains that the engineering details are wrong. Last year, an American friend invited him to celebrate **Thanksgiving**, and he gave a short speech about gratitude. He says that the court case was the last major event of his working life, and he was relieved when the judge decided to **terminate** the hearings after only two days."},"88":{"w":["thereafter","therein","thereof","thermal","thesis","thicken","thigh","thoroughly","thoughtless","thrash","thresh","threshold","thrifty","tickle","tighten","tightly","tile","tilt","timely","tiresome"],"title":"Harvest Days","t":"Every autumn during my childhood, I returned to my grandfather's village to help with the rice harvest. The work was hard. We cut the rice by hand, tied it **tightly** in bundles and carried it to the yard, where we would **thresh** it by beating the stalks against wooden frames. My **thighs|thigh** and arms ached for days afterwards.\n\nMy grandfather was a **thrifty** man who wasted nothing. He used the straw to make mats, fed the husks to the pigs and saved every grain. His house had a **tile** roof that leaked in heavy rain, and he repaired it himself every year. He taught me to **tighten** the ropes on the cart before every trip, so that the load would not **tilt** on the rough road.\n\nThe harvest was a race against the weather. A **timely** warning from the radio could save a whole crop from a storm. Once, a sudden rain fell before we finished, and the rice in the field was **thoroughly** soaked. My grandfather was not angry. \"**Thereafter**, we'll start earlier,\" he said simply.\n\nEvenings were the best part. My grandmother would **thicken** the soup with sweet potatoes, and we sat outside wearing **thermal** jackets against the cold. My little cousin would **tickle** me until I fell off the bench. Grandfather told stories, and **therein** lay the true magic of those days.\n\nYears later, I wrote my university **thesis** on rural farming traditions. My grandfather, though uneducated, read every word **thereof**. He said that young people are often **thoughtless** about where their food comes from. The village basketball team once **thrashed|thrash** a team from the city, a victory still remembered with pride. Standing on the **threshold** of his house, my grandfather always said goodbye with the same words: \"Farming is **tiresome**, but it feeds the world.\""},"89":{"w":["toad","token","tolerant","torment","torpedo","torque","touchable","tow","toxic","tract","trademark","trader","tradesman","tragic","trait","trample","tranquil","transaction","transcend","transient"],"title":"Life on the River","t":"For twenty years, my neighbour Mr. Gu has worked as a **trader** on the Yangtze River, buying and selling goods between towns. He owns a small boat that he uses to **tow** cargo barges, and he knows every bend of the river. He has a reputation as an honest **tradesman**, and his business card carries his own simple **trademark**: a small blue fish.\n\nMr. Gu has a calm **trait** that makes him popular with customers. He is **tolerant** of late payments and treats every **transaction** with care. He says that wealth is **transient**, but trust lasts forever. His boat's old engine has impressive **torque**, which allows it to pull heavy loads even against strong currents.\n\nThe river has a history that **transcends|transcend** any single life. Mr. Gu's father worked on the same waters, and he remembers wartime stories of a **torpedo** that sank a ship near the town. A small museum displays the remains. A huge **tract** of land along the riverbank was once covered with factories, and their **toxic** waste polluted the water for decades.\n\nNot all his memories are happy. Floods have caused **tragic** losses, and he still feels **torment** when he remembers a friend who drowned during a storm. Once, a crowd of tourists began to **trample** the riverbank gardens, and he had to ask them politely to move.\n\nToday, the river is cleaner and more **tranquil**. Fishermen have returned, and in summer the banks are full of frogs and **toads|toad**. Mr. Gu keeps a small copper **token** that his father gave him, believing it brings good luck. He says the river feels almost **touchable** in the morning mist, like a living friend."},"90":{"w":["transit","transition","transverse","traverse","tread","treasurer","tremendously","trench","triangular","tribute","trigger","triple","triumphant","trivial","trolley","tropic","trot","tuberculosis","tug","tulip"],"title":"The Tulip Garden","t":"On a hill outside the city, there is a famous garden where thousands of **tulips|tulip** bloom every spring. It was founded in the 1950s by a doctor who had recovered from **tuberculosis**. While resting in the mountains, he began growing flowers, and he believed they played a part in his recovery. The garden is now a **tribute** to his memory.\n\nVisitors arrive by public **transit**, and a small electric **trolley** carries elderly guests up the hill. The paths **traverse** the slope in long curves, and a **transverse** path cuts across the middle, dividing the garden into **triangular** sections. Each section shows a different colour of tulip, from deep red to pale yellow.\n\nThe garden's success has been **tremendously** important to the local economy. Visitor numbers have **tripled|triple** in the past decade, and the garden's **treasurer** reports a healthy profit. The money supports a training programme for young gardeners. One gardener explained how the **transition** from winter to spring is the most delicate time: a sudden frost can **trigger** damage to the bulbs.\n\nThe work is hard. Gardeners dig a deep **trench** around each bed to drain water, and they must **tread** carefully to avoid damaging young plants. Children love to **trot** behind the gardeners and help them **tug** weeds from the soil. Some visitors complain about **trivial** problems, such as the price of tea, but most are delighted.\n\nThe garden also has a greenhouse with plants from the **tropic** regions, including orchids and banana trees. At the end of each season, the staff hold a **triumphant** celebration, sharing food and music beneath the flowering trees."},"91":{"w":["tuna","turmoil","turnover","turtle","twilight","tyranny","tyrant","ultrasonic","ultraviolet","unanimous","unavoidable","unconventional","underestimate","underlying","undermine","underwear","undesirable","unemployment","unfit","unfold"],"title":"The Turtle Rescue Centre","t":"On a quiet beach in Hainan, a small rescue centre cares for injured sea **turtles|turtle**. Many arrive tangled in fishing nets meant for **tuna**, and others have swallowed plastic. The centre's founder, a former teacher named Ms. Luo, started the project fifteen years ago with no money and no training. \"People **underestimate** what ordinary volunteers can do,\" she says. On her first rescue, she rushed to the beach so quickly that she had packed nothing but **underwear** and a toothbrush.\n\nHer methods were **unconventional** from the start. She used old swimming pools as tanks and borrowed equipment from a local hospital. Vets now use **ultrasonic** scanners to check the turtles' health, and **ultraviolet** lamps keep the water clean. Each rescue story seems to **unfold** like a drama: some turtles recover quickly, while others need months of care.\n\nThe centre has faced difficult times. During a period of economic **turmoil**, donations fell sharply, and the staff **turnover** was high because many volunteers had to find paid work. Rising **unemployment** in the region made it hard to keep people. Some officials even tried to **undermine** the project, calling it an **undesirable** use of public land.\n\nMs. Luo believes that the **underlying** problem is human behaviour. Overfishing and pollution make some damage **unavoidable**, but much could be prevented. She gives talks in schools and speaks about how the island's history shaped attitudes to the sea. In the old days, a cruel local **tyrant** controlled the fishing trade, and his **tyranny** forced poor fishermen to take more fish than the sea could support.\n\nThe centre's work now enjoys **unanimous** support from the village council. Each release is a moving event. At **twilight**, volunteers carry the turtles to the water's edge and watch them swim away. Ms. Luo admits she is now **unfit** for long swims herself, but she still wades in to say goodbye."},"92":{"w":["uniformly","unify","universally","unlimited","unlock","unorthodox","unpaid","unprecedented","unreasonable","unreliable","untie","update","upgrade","uphold","uproar","uranium","utensil","utilization","vaccinate","validity"],"title":"The Mountain Vaccination Campaign","t":"Last spring, a team of young doctors set out to **vaccinate** every child in a remote mountain county against measles. The task was **unprecedented**: the county had never had a complete vaccination programme, and many villages could be reached only on foot. The team aimed to provide protection **uniformly** across the region, so that no child would be left out.\n\nMost doctors on the team were **unpaid** volunteers. They carried vaccines in cool boxes on their backs and slept in village schools. Because the hills contain old **uranium** mines, they also tested the drinking water in every village. Each evening, they would **update** their records on tablets, though the internet connection was **unreliable**. The government promised to **upgrade** mobile networks in the area, but progress was slow.\n\nThe doctors faced unexpected challenges. Some parents doubted the **validity** of the vaccines, influenced by rumours online. In one village, a meeting ended in **uproar** when a man accused the doctors of hiding dangerous side effects. The team leader, Dr. Shi, stayed calm. She explained that vaccines are **universally** recommended by doctors around the world and that refusing them was **unreasonable**. Slowly, trust grew.\n\nDr. Shi's methods were sometimes **unorthodox**. She invited children to **untie** ribbons on small gift boxes after each injection, and she used cooking **utensils|utensil** to demonstrate how germs spread. She believed that good health could **unify** communities that had long been divided.\n\nThe campaign was a success. Careful **utilization** of resources meant that no vaccine was wasted. The county now has **unlimited** access to free vaccines through a new clinic. Dr. Shi says the work helped her **uphold** the values that made her choose medicine. \"Knowledge,\" she says, \"is the key that can **unlock** a healthier future.\""},"93":{"w":["valve","vault","vector","vegetation","vein","vengeance","vent","ventilate","verbal","verdict","verge","versatile","verse","versus","veto","vicinity","vicious","vigor","villa","violate"],"title":"The Battle for the Villa","t":"On a hill above the lake stands an old **villa** built in the 1920s by a wealthy poet. Its walls are covered with ivy, and the gardens are full of wild **vegetation**. For years, the building stood empty, until a property developer announced plans to demolish it and build luxury apartments.\n\nThe plan caused fierce debate. Local residents formed a group to protect the villa, and the case went to court: \"City Heritage Society **versus** Golden Hill Developers.\" The residents argued that demolition would **violate** laws protecting historic buildings. The developer claimed the building was unsafe and on the **verge** of collapse.\n\nThe trial lasted months. Engineers examined every **valve** and pipe in the building and discovered that the old heating system could be repaired. They found a hidden **vault** beneath the house, full of the poet's letters and unpublished **verse**. One expert said that poor drainage had made the area a breeding ground for mosquitoes, which act as a **vector** for disease, but the residents promised to fix the problem.\n\nThe developer's lawyers were **vicious** in their attacks, accusing residents of acting out of **vengeance** because they had lost earlier disputes. The residents' lawyer, a **versatile** woman who also wrote novels, defended them with great **vigor**. Her **verbal** skill impressed everyone in the courtroom.\n\nFinally, the judge delivered her **verdict**: the villa must be preserved. The mayor tried to **veto** the decision, but the city council overruled him. Today, the villa is being restored. Workers **ventilate** the damp rooms, clean every **vent** and repair the marble floors, which have a beautiful blue **vein** running through them. Families living in the **vicinity** say the house has become the heart of the neighbourhood."},"94":{"w":["violation","violently","virgin","visa","viscous","visualize","vitamine","vividly","vocal","vocation","vocational","void","vow","vowel","vulgar","vulnerable","wade","wag","waitress","walnut"],"title":"The Vocational School","t":"Mr. Kang is the director of a **vocational** school in a mountain town, where young people learn practical skills such as cooking, mechanics and nursing. For him, teaching is not just a job but a **vocation**. \"Every student deserves a chance,\" he says, \"whatever their background.\"\n\nMany of his students come from **vulnerable** families. Some have left school early; others have parents working far away in the cities. Mr. Kang has made a **vow** never to turn a student away for lack of money. He is **vocal** in his criticism of policies that ignore rural youth, and he often writes to newspapers.\n\nThe school's courses are practical and lively. In the cooking class, students learn to make **walnut** cakes and soups, and they study the role of each **vitamine**, as the old textbook still spells it, in a healthy diet. In the English class, teachers focus on pronunciation, helping students to distinguish each **vowel** sound. One student, a young **waitress** who works evenings in a hotel, hopes to apply for a **visa** to work abroad.\n\nMr. Kang describes his own childhood **vividly**. He grew up beside a **virgin** forest, where he had to **wade** across a river to reach school. A stray dog would **wag** its tail and follow him every morning. He says that hard times taught him to **visualize** a better future.\n\nNot every moment is easy. Once, two students argued **violently** in the canteen, and Mr. Kang had to separate them. Using **vulgar** language is a **violation** of school rules, but he prefers conversation to punishment. \"Punishment leaves a **void**,\" he says. \"Understanding fills it.\" In the mechanics workshop, students learn to handle **viscous** oils and repair engines. Many graduates now run their own businesses."},"95":{"w":["ward","wardrobe","ware","warehouse","warfare","warning","warrant","wasp","wasteful","watchful","watertight","watery","watt","waver","weaver","web","wedge","wharf","whereby","whisker"],"title":"The Old Wharf","t":"Along the river in our city, an old **wharf** that once handled ships from around the world has become a busy cultural district. Its giant brick **warehouse**, built in the nineteenth century, now contains shops, galleries and cafés. Visitors stroll between stalls where traders sell local **wares|ware**, from pottery to handmade baskets.\n\nThe building has a long history. During the war, it was used to store weapons, and a small museum explains how modern **warfare** changed the city. Bombs damaged the roof, but the walls survived. After the war, the warehouse fell into decline, and the city eventually issued a **warrant** for its demolition.\n\nA group of artists saved it. They proposed a plan **whereby** the city would lease the building cheaply in exchange for repairs. The city's leaders seemed to **waver** at first, but they finally agreed. One of the artists was a **weaver** who set up her loom in a corner of the warehouse, and her colourful fabrics soon attracted visitors.\n\nThe renovation was careful. Workers made the roof **watertight** and installed lamps of just a few **watts|watt** each to save energy. They forced a wooden **wedge** under each old door to keep it from slamming. Volunteers removed a **wasp** nest from the attic, though they were **watchful** for stings. The artists also discouraged **wasteful** practices and recycled most of the old timber.\n\nToday, the wharf is full of life. A café serves **watery** but cheap coffee, which customers drink while watching the boats. A cat with long white **whiskers|whisker** sleeps on the steps, and a **web** of fairy lights hangs over the courtyard. Each room has its own **wardrobe** of costumes for theatre performances. Signs give a **warning** to visitors not to lean over the river railings. Children from a nearby hospital **ward** visit every week for art classes."},"96":{"w":["wholesome","wield","wilful","willow","winding","windmill","windy","wink","wither","withhold","witty","woe","woodpecker","workpiece","wrath","wrench","wrestle","wretched","wring","wrinkle"],"title":"The Windmill Village","t":"In the north of the Netherlands, there is a small village famous for its old **windmill**. The area is flat and **windy**, and for three centuries the mill ground grain for local farmers. A **winding** road leads to it through fields lined with **willow** trees, and a **woodpecker** can often be heard knocking on the branches.\n\nThe miller, an old man named Mr. de Vries, has worked there for fifty years. He is a **witty** storyteller who loves to **wink** at visitors and make them laugh. His face is full of **wrinkles|wrinkle**, but his hands are strong. He can still **wield** a heavy hammer and turn a giant **wrench** to adjust the machinery. Each wooden **workpiece** he makes for the mill is cut by hand.\n\nLife has not always been easy. During the war, the village suffered great **woe**, and many families lived in **wretched** conditions. Mr. de Vries remembers his mother trying to **wring** water from wet clothes by the fire because there was no soap. Later, when a company tried to buy the mill and turn it into a hotel, the villagers' **wrath** was enormous. They refused to sell.\n\nThe mill nearly closed ten years ago, when the government decided to **withhold** funding for historic buildings. Without money, the wooden sails began to **wither** and crack. Mr. de Vries had to **wrestle** with the decision to close, but young volunteers came to help him.\n\nToday, the mill produces flour again. Local bakers make **wholesome** bread from it, and visitors buy bags to take home. Mr. de Vries admits that he was a **wilful** child who never listened to his father. \"Now I know he was right,\" he says. \"This mill is our family's heart.\""},"97":{"w":["xerox","yacht","yearn","yeast","yielding","yoke","yolk","zinc"],"title":"Breakfast on the Yacht","t":"Last summer, my uncle invited our family to spend a weekend on his small **yacht**. He had dreamed of owning a boat for years, and he would **yearn** for the sea every time he sat in his office. When he finally retired, he bought an old sailing yacht and repaired it himself, replacing the rusty fittings with new **zinc** parts that resist salt water.\n\nOn the first morning, he made breakfast in the tiny kitchen. He baked bread with **yeast** he had brought from home, and he fried eggs with bright yellow **yolks|yolk**. We ate on deck as the sun rose, watching seabirds circle the boat.\n\nMy uncle is not a stubborn man; he has a **yielding** nature and listens to everyone's opinions. But on the boat, he is the captain, and he insists that everyone follow the rules. Before we left, he made us read a safety sheet, which he had copied on an old **xerox** machine at the harbour office.\n\nHe says that sailing frees him from the **yoke** of his old working life. \"For thirty years, I sat at a desk,\" he said. \"Now the wind decides where I go.\""}},"ext":{"1":{"w":["abound","abusive","accessibility","acclaim","according","accountable","accumulation","accusation","acrobatics","activate","activation","activist","acupuncture","adaptability","adaptive","addiction","adherence","administrator","adorable","adulthood"],"title":"From Acrobat to Healer","t":"As a child, Mei trained in **acrobatics** at a famous circus school in Hebei. She spent her youth balancing on chairs and flying through the air, and audiences across the country **acclaimed|acclaim** her performances. But when she reached **adulthood**, a serious back injury ended her career.\n\nThe years that followed were difficult. She suffered constant pain and developed an **addiction** to painkillers. \"I felt useless,\" she remembers. Her recovery began when a doctor suggested **acupuncture**. After a few months of treatment, the pain eased, and she became fascinated by the method. **According** to her teacher, the needles help **activate** the body's own healing system; scientists describe this **activation** in terms of nerves and blood flow.\n\nMei decided to study traditional medicine. Her training required strict **adherence** to rules, and an **accumulation** of knowledge that took years. Her teachers praised her **adaptability**: she brought the discipline of a performer to her new career and developed an **adaptive** style suited to each patient.\n\nToday she runs a small clinic. Many of her patients are former athletes, and she treats them with special care. Her clinic is designed for **accessibility**, with ramps for wheelchairs and wide doors. Grateful patients **abound**: her waiting room is full from morning till night, and she works closely with a local hospital **administrator** to offer free sessions.\n\nNot everyone supports her work. Online, she sometimes receives **abusive** comments from people who believe acupuncture is nonsense. She once faced an **accusation** that she was promising false cures. Mei says she is **accountable** to her patients and always recommends modern medicine when it is needed. She has become an **activist** for honest health information. Her favourite patients are children, especially an **adorable** six-year-old girl who calls her \"Auntie Needle.\""},"2":{"w":["advancement","advent","advertiser","adviser","advisor","advocacy","aesthetics","affiliation","affirmative","affordability","affordable","afterwards","aging","agitate","agreeableness","ailment","airtime","aisle","akin","alertness"],"title":"The Radio Show for Older Listeners","t":"Every Sunday morning, a community radio station in our city broadcasts a programme called \"Golden Years,\" designed for older listeners. It explores the challenges of **aging** in a society that is changing rapidly. The host, a retired teacher named Mrs. Bai, is famous for her **agreeableness** and warm voice.\n\nThe programme covers practical topics. One week, a financial **adviser** explained how to plan for retirement; another week, a university **advisor** discussed courses for older students. Guests talk about the **affordability** of housing and medicine, and Mrs. Bai often asks officials why more **affordable** care homes are not available.\n\nMrs. Bai is passionate about **advocacy**. She believes that older people deserve respect and the chance for personal **advancement**, even late in life. When a government report suggested reducing pensions, she spoke strongly against it, and her words seemed to **agitate** some politicians. Her answer to criticism is always polite but firm. \"My reply is **affirmative**,\" she once said. \"Yes, we will keep asking questions.\"\n\nThe show has changed with the **advent** of new technology. Listeners can now hear it online, and **advertisers|advertiser** pay for **airtime** to promote health products. Mrs. Bai is careful about her station's **affiliation** with companies, accepting only adverts that are honest and useful.\n\nSome episodes are lighter. One discussed the **aesthetics** of traditional clothing, and another described a common **ailment**: back pain from carrying heavy shopping bags down the supermarket **aisle**. A doctor explained that staying active keeps the mind sharp and improves **alertness**. Mrs. Bai says the show feels **akin** to a family gathering. **Afterwards**, listeners often call to share their own stories."},"3":{"w":["algorithm","alienate","align","allergy","alleviation","alpine","amend","amiable","amnesia","ancestral","ancestry","anew","anguish","animate","animation","anonymity","antagonism","anthropologist","anti","antibiotic"],"title":"The Anthropologist in the Alpine Village","t":"Dr. Ruiz is an **anthropologist** who has spent five years living in an **alpine** village in the Swiss mountains. She studies how small communities preserve their traditions in a modern world. The villagers, who were at first suspicious of outsiders, soon came to see her as an **amiable** neighbour.\n\nHer research focuses on **ancestry** and memory. Many families have lived in the valley for centuries, and their **ancestral** farmhouses are full of old letters and photographs. Some younger villagers use DNA tests, which rely on a computer **algorithm** to estimate where their ancestors came from. Dr. Ruiz is interested in how these results **align** with family stories.\n\nShe has also seen painful moments. One old farmer suffers from **amnesia** after an accident and can no longer remember his childhood. His daughter described her **anguish** at watching him forget. Dr. Ruiz helped the family record his old songs, hoping to bring him some **alleviation** of his loneliness.\n\nThe village faces divisions. Plans for a new ski resort have created **antagonism** between those who want jobs and those who want peace. Some people are strongly **anti**, while others support the project. Dr. Ruiz worries that the conflict may **alienate** neighbours who have been friends for decades. The village council has had to **amend** its rules several times.\n\nVillage life has its lighter side. The local school produced a short **animation** about mountain legends, and children love to **animate** the old stories in drawings. When Dr. Ruiz developed an **allergy** to the hay in her rented barn, the village doctor gave her an **antibiotic** for the infection that followed. She values the **anonymity** of the mountains, where nobody cares about her academic titles. \"Here,\" she says, \"I learn to see the world **anew**.\""},"4":{"w":["anticipation","anymore","ape","app","appal","aptitude","arbitrate","arbor","archaeologist","archaeology","archive","archivist","arena","arguable","arguably","arousal","artery","artifact","artisan","aspirant"],"title":"The Old Arena","t":"In the centre of a small Italian town stands the ruin of a Roman **arena**, where crowds once watched gladiators fight. For **archaeology** students, it is one of the most exciting sites in Europe. Every summer, a team led by a British **archaeologist**, Dr. Hale, spends three months digging beneath its stones.\n\nEach season begins with great **anticipation**. Volunteers hope to find a rare **artifact**: a coin, a piece of jewellery or a tool used by an ancient **artisan**. Last year, the team discovered a carved stone showing an **ape**, which may have been brought from Africa for public shows. The find caused a strong **arousal** of interest in the international press.\n\nDr. Hale keeps careful records. Every object is photographed and stored in a digital **archive**, managed by the team's **archivist**. Students now use a mobile **app** to record their findings in the field, so they don't need paper notebooks **anymore**. \"Technology has changed everything,\" Dr. Hale says. \"**Arguably**, it has changed archaeology more than any discovery.\"\n\nNot all decisions are easy. When two universities disputed who should study a newly found statue, a professor was invited to **arbitrate** between them. Some theories about the site remain **arguable**: historians disagree about how many people the arena could hold. Dr. Hale was **appalled|appal** when tourists tried to take stones home as souvenirs.\n\nThe town has embraced the project. A wooden **arbor** covered with vines provides shade for workers, and local families bring food at lunchtime. The main road to the site, once a busy **artery** for trade, is now lined with cafés. Dr. Hale looks for one quality in every young **aspirant**: not just an **aptitude** for science, but patience. \"The past,\" she says, \"does not like to be rushed.\""},"5":{"w":["aspirational","aspire","assertion","assortment","astronomical","attainable","attendee","attic","auction","aura","auspicious","authorship","autonomy","aversion","avoidance","awaken","awareness","backlash","backyard","bacterial"],"title":"Treasures in the Attic","t":"When my grandmother died last year, we found an old wooden box in her **attic**. It contained an **assortment** of strange objects: letters, coins, silk scarves and a small painting of a mountain at dawn. The painting had no signature, and its **authorship** became a family mystery.\n\nMy cousin, who works at a museum, took the painting to an expert. After weeks of research, the expert made a surprising **assertion**: it might be the work of a famous nineteenth-century artist. The family decided to sell it at **auction**, hoping the money could pay for my younger sister's education.\n\nThe auction day felt **auspicious**. The room was full, and each **attendee** seemed excited. The painting had a special **aura**; it glowed under the lights. Bidding began slowly but quickly rose to **astronomical** levels. In the end, it sold for more than a million yuan.\n\nThe sale caused an unexpected **backlash**. Some relatives felt we should have kept the painting, and others accused us of greed. My mother, who has a strong **aversion** to conflict, chose **avoidance** and stopped answering phone calls for a week. Eventually, the family met in her **backyard** to talk things through.\n\nThe experience seemed to **awaken** something in all of us. We gained a new **awareness** of my grandmother's life; her letters showed that she had once longed to become an artist herself. We also discovered why the paper was damaged: **bacterial** growth from damp air in the attic. My sister now **aspires|aspire** to study art history. Her dream, once just **aspirational**, now feels **attainable**. My grandmother always valued **autonomy**, and we think she would be happy that her treasure gave a young woman the freedom to choose her own path."},"6":{"w":["bail","baseline","behavioral","behaviorally","beholder","benefaction","beverage","bike-sharing","bilingual","bilingualism","bio","biodiversity","biological","biologically","biologist","blender","blog","bloody","bolster","borrower"],"title":"The City Biologist","t":"Dr. Ma is a **biologist** who studies wildlife in the middle of a busy city. Her popular **blog**, \"Wild Streets,\" describes the birds, insects and small animals that live among skyscrapers and shopping centres. Her short **bio** on the website says simply: \"I look for nature where nobody expects it.\"\n\nHer research focuses on urban **biodiversity**. Five years ago, she recorded a **baseline** count of species in the city's parks, and she now measures changes every year. She is especially interested in **behavioral** adaptation: how animals change their habits to survive near humans. Some birds, she found, are **behaviorally** different from their countryside relatives; they sing louder to be heard over traffic.\n\nHer work has practical uses. Data from her surveys helped **bolster** a campaign to protect green spaces from development. A wealthy businessman, impressed by her findings, made a generous **benefaction** to fund a new nature reserve. Dr. Ma writes every post in both Chinese and English, and she believes **bilingualism** helps her reach a global audience. Her own children are **bilingual**, too.\n\nShe travels everywhere by **bike-sharing**, collecting samples as she rides. Last year, the bike company almost went bankrupt, and the city had to **bail** it out with public money. Dr. Ma once fell off a bike and got a **bloody** nose, but she laughed it off and kept working.\n\nDr. Ma also writes about food. She explains the **biological** effects of sugary **beverages|beverage** and shares recipes for healthy smoothies made in a **blender**. \"**Biologically**, we are designed to love sweetness,\" she writes, \"but too much is harmful.\" She once lent her favourite camera to a student, and the **borrower** returned it with photos of a rare owl. \"Beauty,\" she says, \"is in the eye of the **beholder**. In the city, you just have to look harder.\""},"7":{"w":["botanist","bother","bracket","breach","breastfeed","brew","buffer","builder","buildup","burger","calligraphy","calorie","canyon","capitalize","carbohydrate","carer","cashless","catastrophic","categorize","catwalk"],"title":"Into the Canyon","t":"Last autumn, a **botanist** named Dr. Wen led a small team into a deep **canyon** in the mountains of Yunnan. Their goal was to **categorize** the plants growing on its steep walls, many of which had never been studied. The only way across the river was a narrow metal **catwalk** built by local farmers decades ago.\n\nThe trip required careful planning. Each member carried enough food for ten days, and Dr. Wen calculated every **calorie**. Meals were rich in **carbohydrates|carbohydrate** such as rice and noodles, though one volunteer joked that he would give anything for a **burger**. Each evening, the team would **brew** strong tea over a campfire.\n\nThe canyon's ecology is fragile. Dense forests on the upper slopes act as a **buffer**, protecting the valley from heavy rain. But logging upstream has caused a **buildup** of mud in the river, and a single storm could be **catastrophic**. Dr. Wen worries that some developers **capitalize** on weak rules and are in **breach** of environmental laws.\n\nThe team met local villagers along the way. A young mother, who was about to **breastfeed** her baby, invited them into her home and offered them food. An elderly **carer** looked after her disabled brother. A village **builder** explained how houses were constructed without nails. Even in this remote area, people used phones to pay for everything, and markets were almost completely **cashless**.\n\nDr. Wen recorded her notes in beautiful **calligraphy**, a hobby she has practised since childhood. She grouped plants by height, writing each **bracket** of measurements carefully in her book. \"Some people ask why I **bother** with such slow work,\" she says. \"But every plant matters.\""},"8":{"w":["causation","celebrity","center","chaotic","charitable","cheeseburger","chemotherapy","chihuahua","childbearing","childbirth","chimpanzee","chore","chromosome","chunk","citizenship","civic","clarity","clause","clench","clog"],"title":"The Scientist on Television","t":"Professor Zhou is a geneticist who has become an unlikely **celebrity**. Her weekly television show explains science in simple language, and millions of viewers watch it. In one popular episode, she showed how humans and the **chimpanzee** share almost ninety-nine percent of their DNA, using a model of a **chromosome** made of coloured balls.\n\nHer research focuses on **causation** in disease. She studies how genes and lifestyle together affect health, especially during **childbearing** and **childbirth**. \"Correlation is not **causation**,\" she often reminds her audience, explaining that two things happening together does not prove one caused the other.\n\nProfessor Zhou is also deeply **charitable**. She runs a foundation that supports patients at a cancer **center** in her hometown, many of whom are undergoing **chemotherapy**. She visits them regularly, bringing books and her small **chihuahua**, which the patients love. She says her mother's illness inspired her to study genetics.\n\nHer life is often **chaotic**. Between research, filming and travel, she rarely has time for household **chores|chore**, and she admits that her kitchen is a disaster. Her favourite treat after a long day is a **cheeseburger**, which she eats in her car. A large **chunk** of her income goes to her foundation.\n\nShe believes that science is a **civic** duty. Good **citizenship**, she argues, requires understanding the basic facts behind public decisions. When a television company once tried to add a **clause** to her contract limiting what she could say about food companies, she refused to sign. \"**Clarity** matters more than money,\" she said, her hands **clenched|clench** in frustration. She warns that misinformation can **clog** public debate like rubbish in a drain."},"9":{"w":["cognition","collaborative","collaborator","collectivism","collectivistic","collector","colonize","colorful","comet","commemorate","communicator","commuter","compassion","compassionate","competency","competitiveness","complacent","complementary","condemnation","conditioner"],"title":"Two Ways of Thinking","t":"At a university in Shanghai, a psychology professor named Dr. Lam studies how culture shapes human **cognition**. Her research compares societies influenced by **collectivism**, where group harmony matters most, with those that value individual freedom. \"People in **collectivistic** cultures often notice relationships,\" she explains, \"while others focus on individual objects.\"\n\nHer work is highly **collaborative**. She leads a team of researchers from twelve countries, and each **collaborator** brings a different perspective. The project is a **colorful** mix of languages and ideas. Dr. Lam is an excellent **communicator**, able to explain complex theories in simple words.\n\nOne study examined how people behave on crowded trains. Researchers interviewed every kind of **commuter**, from students to office workers, about how they react when someone needs help. Those who showed the most **compassion** were not always the richest or best educated. Dr. Lam believes that **compassionate** behaviour is learned, not born.\n\nHer findings challenge some common beliefs. Many schools focus on **competitiveness**, but Dr. Lam argues that cooperation is an equally important **competency**. She warns that wealthy societies can become **complacent**, assuming their ways are best. She believes that different cultural values are **complementary** rather than opposed.\n\nNot everyone agrees. Some critics have expressed strong **condemnation** of her methods, claiming that cultural categories are too simple. Dr. Lam welcomes the debate. Outside work, she is a keen **collector** of old maps and once watched a **comet** from a mountain top. She plans a ceremony to **commemorate** her mentor, who taught her that ideas, like moss, slowly **colonize** new ground. Even the broken air **conditioner** in her office does not bother her. \"Science needs patience,\" she says, \"and a good fan.\""},"10":{"w":["conducive","confide","confucianism","congestion","conjure","connotation","conscientious","conservationist","conservatism","constructive","consultation","consumerism","contagious","contemplation","contentious","convergence","conveyor","cooperation","copyright","cornerstone"],"title":"The Old Teacher's Lessons","t":"In a quiet courtyard in Qufu, the hometown of Confucius, an elderly teacher named Mr. Kong runs evening classes on **Confucianism**. His students include office workers, retirees and university students, all looking for a moment of **contemplation** away from the noise and **congestion** of modern cities. The courtyard is peaceful, an atmosphere **conducive** to quiet thought.\n\nMr. Kong is a **conscientious** teacher who prepares every lesson carefully. He explains the **connotation** of ancient words, showing how their meanings have changed over time. He believes Confucian ideas are the **cornerstone** of Chinese culture, but he rejects blind **conservatism**. \"Tradition must be living,\" he says. \"Otherwise it becomes a museum.\"\n\nHis classes encourage **constructive** debate. Some topics are **contentious**: students argue about the role of women in traditional thought, or whether ancient values can survive modern **consumerism**. Mr. Kong listens patiently and offers advice in private **consultation** when students **confide** their personal problems to him.\n\nHe sees a **convergence** between Eastern and Western ideas. Both traditions, he argues, value honesty, learning and **cooperation**. One student, who works on a factory **conveyor** line, said the classes helped him see meaning in his repetitive job. Another, a **conservationist**, connected Confucian ideas about harmony with nature to her environmental work.\n\nMr. Kong's enthusiasm is **contagious**. His stories **conjure** vivid images of ancient scholars travelling between kingdoms. A publisher recently offered to buy the **copyright** to his lecture notes, but he refused. \"Knowledge should be shared freely,\" he says, smiling."},"11":{"w":["cosmetic","counterfeit","counterproductive","countless","couplet","courier","coverage","crackdown","craftsman","craftsmanship","cram","crave","creation","creativity","credibility","credible","crumble","culinary","cult","cultivation"],"title":"The Brush Maker","t":"In an old lane in Huzhou, a **craftsman** named Mr. Shen makes calligraphy brushes by hand, as his family has done for five generations. Each brush takes days to finish, and his **craftsmanship** is admired by artists across the country. Before every Spring Festival, he writes red **couplets|couplet** for his neighbours with his own brushes.\n\nHis workshop is small and **crammed|cram** with materials: bamboo handles, bundles of goat hair and jars of glue. The bamboo comes from his own fields, where careful **cultivation** produces straight, strong stems. \"Every brush is a **creation**,\" he says. \"It must have a soul.\"\n\nThe business faces many challenges. Cheap **counterfeit** brushes, sold online with fake labels, have damaged the reputation of real Huzhou brushes. Last year, the government launched a **crackdown** on fake products, and the media **coverage** helped consumers recognise genuine ones. Mr. Shen says that lowering his prices would be **counterproductive**; customers who **crave** quality are willing to pay for it.\n\nHe has adapted to modern life. Orders now arrive online, and a **courier** collects parcels from his door every afternoon. His daughter, a chef who runs a **culinary** school, designed the shop's website. She says that **creativity** in marketing is just as important as skill. The brushes have even developed a **cult** following among young artists on social media.\n\nMr. Shen is proud of his **credibility**. His customers trust him because he never exaggerates. When a journalist once asked whether his brushes really last longer, he gave a simple, **credible** answer: \"Use one for a year and see.\" He refuses to make **cosmetic** changes just to follow fashion. His old house is beginning to **crumble**, but he says it has witnessed **countless** brushes and happy customers, and he will never leave."},"12":{"w":["cultural","culturally","curtail","cyberloafing","cyborg","dataset","daytime","deadlock","decease","dedication","defensive","defer","deforest","defuse","delta","delusional","dementia","democrat","demographic","dependable"],"title":"The Data Scientist","t":"Dr. Liu is a data scientist who studies workplace behaviour. Her latest research examines **cyberloafing**: the habit of browsing the internet for personal reasons during working hours. Using a large **dataset** from two hundred companies, she found that almost every employee does it, especially during the **daytime** hours after lunch.\n\nHer findings surprised many managers. Some wanted to **curtail** internet access completely, but Dr. Liu argues that short breaks can actually improve productivity. Managers who become **defensive** about the issue, she says, often create more stress. She also found **cultural** differences: workers in some countries feel guiltier about breaks than others, and attitudes are **culturally** shaped.\n\nHer work has practical uses. In one company, talks between managers and workers reached a **deadlock** over a new monitoring system. Dr. Liu was invited to **defuse** the situation, and she suggested a compromise that both sides accepted. She later agreed to **defer** publishing the results until the company had time to review them.\n\nDr. Liu's interest in human behaviour began at home. Her grandmother, who suffers from **dementia**, often forgets names but remembers songs from childhood. Dr. Liu says caring for her taught her **dedication** and patience. Her family comes from the Pearl River **Delta**, and her grandmother tells stories of her **deceased|decease** husband, a farmer who helped **deforest** hills to grow rice.\n\nShe also studies the **demographic** changes in China's workforce. Older workers, she says, are more **dependable** than many employers assume. Some critics call her ideas **delusional**, but she ignores them. She jokes that one day we will all become a **cyborg**, half human and half machine. A colleague, a committed **democrat**, supports her proposal to let employees vote on workplace rules."},"13":{"w":["dependence","depiction","deplete","deplorable","deplore","designer","desolation","detachment","deter","detrimental","devastate","developer","developmental","diabetes","diagnosis","dietary","differentiate","diffusion","digestive","digit"],"title":"The Game Designer's Diagnosis","t":"At twenty-eight, Zhang Hao was a successful game **designer** working for a large technology company. He spent long hours in front of a screen, surviving on coffee, sweets and instant noodles. Then, during a routine health check, he received a shocking **diagnosis**: type 2 **diabetes**.\n\nThe news seemed to **devastate** him at first. \"I thought diabetes was a disease of old people,\" he says. His doctor explained that poor **dietary** habits and lack of exercise can be **detrimental** to anyone's health. She warned that his **dependence** on sugar had begun to **deplete** his body's ability to control blood sugar, and his **digestive** system was also affected.\n\nHao decided to change his life. He began cooking his own meals and walking to work. He learned to **differentiate** between healthy and unhealthy carbohydrates and checked every **digit** on his blood test reports. \"Numbers became my friends,\" he jokes.\n\nHis experience also changed his work. As a game **developer**, he designed a new mobile game that encourages players to exercise. Its **depiction** of a desert island, full of **desolation** until the player brings it back to life by walking, became surprisingly popular. Child psychologists praised its **developmental** benefits for young players.\n\nHao is now an advocate for healthier office culture. He criticises the **deplorable** working conditions at some technology companies and openly **deplores|deplore** the habit of rewarding long hours. He believes that companies should **deter** unhealthy behaviour rather than encourage it. He has learned to view his illness with calm **detachment**. \"The **diffusion** of good habits takes time,\" he says, \"but one game at a time, it can happen.\""},"14":{"w":["digitization","diner","diplomacy","diplomat","directive","directness","disbelieve","disciple","discomfort","disconnect","discredit","discreet","discriminate","dishearten","disparity","dissertation","distraction","diversity","doctoral","doctorate"],"title":"A Dissertation on Diplomacy","t":"Last year, my friend Yue completed her **doctoral** studies in history. Her **dissertation** examined how Chinese **diplomacy** developed during the nineteenth century, based on thousands of letters written by a forgotten **diplomat**. When she received her **doctorate**, her parents seemed to **disbelieve** the news at first; she was the first in her family to attend university.\n\nHer research depended on **digitization**. A national archive had recently scanned millions of old documents, making them available online. Yue spent months reading letters, reports and official **directives|directive** from the imperial court. She was struck by the diplomat's **directness**: unlike many officials of his time, he wrote honestly about China's weaknesses.\n\nThe diplomat's life was difficult. His enemies tried to **discredit** him by spreading rumours, and he was often **disheartened|dishearten** by the court's refusal to listen. He was a **disciple** of a reformist scholar, and he believed that China should learn from other nations. In private letters, he was **discreet**, using codes to protect his ideas.\n\nYue's research also revealed the **disparity** between official records and private letters. Officials often described meetings as friendly, while private notes showed great **discomfort** and tension. She found evidence that foreign governments would **discriminate** against Chinese merchants in trade agreements.\n\nWriting the dissertation was exhausting. Yue had to **disconnect** from social media, which she called her greatest **distraction**. She often worked in a small **diner** near the university, drinking tea late into the night. She now teaches history and encourages **diversity** of opinion in her classes. \"History is never simple,\" she tells her students."},"15":{"w":["dolphin","domesticate","dominance","donation","doodle","doze","drone","droplet","dual","dub","duckling","duplication","dweller","dysfunction","e-commerce","eagerness","earner","eater","echolocation","ecological"],"title":"Listening to Dolphins","t":"Dr. Ng is a marine biologist who studies **dolphins|dolphin** off the coast of Hong Kong. Her research focuses on **echolocation**, the system dolphins use to find food by sending out sounds and listening to the echoes. \"It's like seeing with your ears,\" she explains to visitors.\n\nHer team uses modern technology. A small **drone** flies above the water, filming dolphin groups from the air without disturbing them. The camera can even capture the tiny **droplets|droplet** of water that dolphins breathe out. The team then studies the videos to understand social **dominance** within each group.\n\nThe work has an **ecological** purpose. Busy shipping lanes, pollution and **e-commerce** deliveries by boat have increased noise in the sea, causing **dysfunction** in the dolphins' ability to hunt. Dr. Ng's team has found that some groups are moving away from areas where they have lived for generations, much like a city **dweller** escaping a noisy street.\n\nFunding comes partly from public **donations|donation**. Dr. Ng also serves a **dual** role as researcher and teacher, giving talks in schools. Children's **eagerness** to learn always inspires her. One boy drew a **doodle** of a dolphin wearing headphones, which she now uses as the project's logo. Journalists have **dubbed|dub** her \"the dolphin whisperer.\"\n\nShe often explains how humans **domesticate** animals, while dolphins remain wild and free. Though her salary is modest compared with other high **earners|earner** in the city, she says the job is priceless. After long days at sea, she sometimes falls into a **doze** on the boat. She warns against unnecessary **duplication** of research, and she shares all her data with other scientists. At home, she keeps a small pond for a **duckling** she rescued from a storm drain, and although she is a fussy **eater**, she never refuses fresh fish."},"16":{"w":["economist","educational","effectiveness","effortful","effortlessly","electrification","electrify","embodiment","emergence","empower","emulate","enact","encyclopedia","endorse","enhancement","enjoyment","enlightenment","enrage","enthusiast","entitlement"],"title":"Lights in the Mountains","t":"Twenty years ago, my grandfather's village in Guizhou had no electricity. Families cooked over wood fires and read by oil lamps. Then a government programme brought **electrification** to the region, and the arrival of power lines seemed to **electrify** the whole community. My grandfather still remembers the night the first bulb lit up: \"Everyone cheered as if it were the Spring Festival.\"\n\nAn **economist** who studied the programme later described it as one of the most important changes in rural China. Electricity allowed the **emergence** of small businesses, from rice mills to repair shops. It also had **educational** benefits: children could study at night, and the school received its first computer. The village's only **encyclopedia**, once read by candlelight, was soon replaced by the internet.\n\nMeasuring the programme's **effectiveness** was not simple. Some villagers adapted **effortlessly**, buying televisions and fridges within months. For others, especially the elderly, learning new technology was **effortful**. My grandmother, a keen gardening **enthusiast**, refused to use the electric cooker for years.\n\nThe changes helped to **empower** women in particular. With electric machines, they spent less time on household tasks and more on paid work. Officials began to **enact** new policies encouraging women to start businesses, and several famous entrepreneurs agreed to **endorse** the programme. One woman who opened a tea shop became the **embodiment** of the village's new spirit, and others tried to **emulate** her success.\n\nNot everything went smoothly. A rise in electricity prices once seemed to **enrage** villagers, who argued that cheap power was an **entitlement**, not a privilege. But over time, most people agreed that life had improved. The **enhancement** of daily comfort brought new **enjoyment**: films, music and long evenings with friends. For my grandfather, the biggest change was a kind of **enlightenment**. \"We could finally see the world,\" he says."},"17":{"w":["entrepreneurial","environmentalism","environmentalist","envoy","enzyme","epic","equinox","equity","erasure","erode","escalate","esteem","ethic","etiquette","euro","evacuation","evade","eventual","excerpt","exclusion"],"title":"The Climate Summit","t":"Last spring, a major climate conference was held in Brussels, attracting leaders, scientists and activists from around the world. Each government sent an **envoy**, and the meetings followed strict diplomatic **etiquette**. For two weeks, the city was full of journalists, protesters and police.\n\nThe talks began on the spring **equinox**, a date chosen to symbolise balance between rich and poor nations. **Equity** was a central issue: developing countries argued that wealthy nations should pay more, since they had produced most of the pollution. The cost of new programmes was estimated in billions of **euros|euro**.\n\nTensions began to **escalate** as negotiations continued. Some countries tried to **evade** responsibility for cutting emissions, while others threatened to walk out. The **exclusion** of small island nations from one meeting caused anger, and an **environmentalist** from the Pacific gave an emotional speech. An **excerpt** from her speech was shared millions of times online: \"Our islands are disappearing while you argue.\"\n\nThe conference also highlighted new ideas. An **entrepreneurial** young scientist presented an **enzyme** that can break down plastic in days rather than centuries. Others spoke about how rising seas **erode** coastlines and force the **evacuation** of entire communities. One historian warned against the **erasure** of indigenous knowledge about nature.\n\nThe **eventual** agreement was not perfect, but many saw it as an **epic** achievement. Observers held the chief negotiator in high **esteem** for her patience. She said that **environmentalism** must be guided by a strong **ethic** of fairness. \"This is not the end,\" she said. \"It is the beginning.\""},"18":{"w":["exclusivity","exemption","existential","expectancy","expectant","experimenter","exploratory","expressive","expressway","fabulous","factual","famed","fantasize","farmland","fascist","feasibility","feat","feebleness","femininity","fertility"],"title":"The Road Through the Farmland","t":"In a small valley in northern Italy, the government planned to build a new **expressway** through the middle of the region's best **farmland**. Officials argued that the road would cut travel times in half. But local farmers, many of whose families had worked the land for centuries, strongly opposed the plan.\n\nThe farmers hired independent experts to study the project's **feasibility**. Their **exploratory** report, led by a soil scientist who called herself \"an **experimenter** in the service of farmers,\" questioned the government's claims, presenting **factual** evidence that the road would damage the soil's **fertility** and pollute nearby rivers. The report also noted that the region is **famed** for its wine and cheese, which depend on clean land.\n\nThe debate had deep historical roots. The village had suffered under **fascist** rule in the 1930s, when land was taken from families without compensation. Older residents remembered stories of that time, and they feared history might repeat itself. A local teacher said that the conflict had an **existential** dimension: \"If we lose the land, we lose who we are.\"\n\nThe campaign was led by a remarkable woman, an eighty-year-old farmer known for her **expressive** speeches. Despite the **feebleness** of her body, her voice was strong. Journalists praised her courage, calling her campaign a **fabulous** example of community action. She rejected old ideas about **femininity**, saying that women had always been the strength of the countryside.\n\nEventually, the government agreed to change the route. The decision was celebrated as a great **feat**. The farmers did not seek **exclusivity** over the land or any tax **exemption**; they simply wanted to protect it. Young couples in the village, including an **expectant** mother who joined every protest, now **fantasize** about a peaceful future. Life **expectancy** in the valley is among the highest in Italy, and residents joke that clean air is the secret."},"19":{"w":["feudal","fewer","filial","finder","fingertip","firecracker","flavor","flexibility","flip","folklore","footprint","footwear","foreseeable","forestry","forgo","founder","frantic","frontline","frugality","frustration"],"title":"The Founder Returns Home","t":"Mr. Ou is the **founder** of a successful **footwear** company that sells shoes all over the world. Last winter, after twenty years in the city, he returned to his home village for the Spring Festival. He found a place that had changed, but whose **folklore** and traditions remained strong.\n\nOn New Year's Eve, the village was **frantic** with preparations. Children ran through the lanes, lighting a **firecracker** at every corner, while women prepared dumplings with every **flavor** imaginable. Old men told stories from **feudal** times, when landlords controlled the valley and farmers lived in poverty.\n\nMr. Ou was moved by the villagers' **frugality**. Many families had **fewer** possessions than his office staff, yet they were generous with food and time. He noticed how young people still showed **filial** respect to their parents, bowing to them on New Year's morning. \"In the city, we often **forgo** these traditions,\" he said. \"We are too busy.\"\n\nThe visit also revealed problems. The village's **forestry** jobs had disappeared, and many young people had left. Mr. Ou felt a deep **frustration** at the lack of opportunities. He decided to open a small factory in the village, bringing jobs back home. \"In the **foreseeable** future,\" he said, \"I want our village to be a place where young people can stay.\"\n\nHe is also concerned about the environment. His company has reduced its carbon **footprint** by using recycled materials. Mr. Ou gives his workers great **flexibility** in their hours, especially those with children. During the festival, he liked to **flip** through old photo albums with his mother, touching each picture with his **fingertip**. Before leaving, he donated money to health workers on the **frontline** of rural medicine. On his last day, he dropped his wallet in the market, and its **finder**, a small boy, ran after him to return it. Mr. Ou says that honesty is still the village's greatest treasure."},"20":{"w":["fulfillment","functional","futile","gadget","gassy","gender","generational","genre","geographic","geological","giver","glacier","glamorous","globalization","glutinous","gosh","governance","governmental","grader","graphic"],"title":"Drawing the Glaciers","t":"Lin Xue is a young artist who creates **graphic** novels about climate change. Her latest book tells the story of a **glacier** in Tibet that is slowly melting. It mixes science with fantasy, a **genre** that has become increasingly popular among young readers. \"I want to make science feel alive,\" she says.\n\nHer research took her to remote mountains, where she interviewed scientists studying the **geological** history of the region. They explained how **geographic** factors, such as altitude and wind patterns, affect ice formation. She found the work fascinating but sad. \"Trying to stop the melting feels **futile** sometimes,\" she admits, \"but we must try.\"\n\nThe book also explores **generational** differences. In one chapter, a grandmother and her granddaughter argue about **globalization**: the grandmother remembers a simple life, while the girl loves every new **gadget**. Lin Xue says she wanted to show both sides without judgement. The book also challenges traditional ideas about **gender**, featuring a female scientist as the hero.\n\nNot all readers are adults. A fifth-**grader** wrote to Lin Xue, saying the book had inspired her to become a scientist. \"**Gosh**, I cried when I read that letter,\" Lin Xue laughs. She believes that artists can be generous **givers|giver** of hope.\n\nHer life is less **glamorous** than people imagine. She works in a small, **functional** studio and lives on simple meals. Her favourite food is **glutinous** rice dumplings, which her mother sends from home, and she avoids **gassy** drinks because they give her headaches. She has opinions on politics too: she argues that better **governance** and stronger **governmental** action are needed to protect the environment. For her, creating art brings a deep sense of **fulfillment**. \"Each page is a small gift to the future,\" she says."},"21":{"w":["grassland","gravitate","grid","groundnut","guideline","gut","habitat","hack","halfway","hallway","handicraft","harass","harassment","haven","havoc","haze","hazy","headphone","healthcare","hearted"],"title":"Guardian of the Grassland","t":"Mr. Bayar is a ranger on the vast **grassland** of Inner Mongolia, where wild horses, gazelles and countless birds make their home. His job is to protect their **habitat** from overgrazing, illegal hunting and careless tourists. For many species, the protected area is the last safe **haven** in the region.\n\nHis work follows strict **guidelines|guideline**. Each spring, he divides the reserve into a **grid** of squares and counts the animals in each one. He travels by motorbike, often riding for hours to reach the most remote areas. Sometimes he must **hack** a path through thick bushes near the rivers. When he is **halfway** through a long patrol, he stops at a herder's tent for tea.\n\nThe grassland faces many threats. In recent years, dust storms have caused **havoc**, burying roads and damaging crops. On some days, a thick **haze** covers the sky, and the horizon becomes **hazy** and grey. Tourists sometimes **harass** animals to take photos, and Mr. Bayar has reported several cases of **harassment** to the police. Once, a group of young men even tried to **hack** into the reserve's website to find the location of rare birds.\n\nMr. Bayar trusts his **gut** when something seems wrong. \"Animals tell you a lot,\" he says. \"If birds suddenly fly away, someone is nearby.\" He wears **headphones|headphone** to listen to bird calls recorded by sensors placed across the reserve.\n\nHe is a **kind-hearted|hearted** man, admired by local herders. Their wives sell **handicraft** goods to tourists, and he helps them find buyers. He also supports better **healthcare** for herder families, many of whom live far from hospitals. In the evenings, children **gravitate** towards his tent to hear stories, and he shares a bag of roasted **groundnuts|groundnut** with them. His small house has a long **hallway** decorated with photos of every animal he has ever rescued."},"22":{"w":["hectare","herbal","herbicide","hierarchical","high-speed","hmm","holder","hologram","homecoming","homeless","homelessness","homeowner","honor","honorable","horrify","hostility","houseplant","humanities","hybrid","hydroelectric"],"title":"Homecoming","t":"Last spring, my cousin Wei took a **high-speed** train back to his hometown for the first time in ten years. The journey that once took two days now took only five hours. His **homecoming** was emotional: his elderly parents waited at the station, holding a sign with his name.\n\nThe town had changed beyond recognition. A new **hydroelectric** dam supplied power to the whole region, and rows of modern houses lined the river. Many farmers had become **homeowners|homeowner** for the first time, buying flats with money earned in the cities. Wei's father now farms just two **hectares|hectare** of land, growing a **hybrid** rice that produces much higher yields.\n\nBut not everything was better. Wei was **horrified|horrify** to learn that some farmers used too much **herbicide**, which was poisoning fish in the river. His mother, who still relies on **herbal** remedies, worried about the water. Wei also noticed **homeless** men sleeping under the new bridge, a sight he had never seen in his childhood. \"**Homelessness** has come with wealth,\" his father said quietly.\n\nWei studies the **humanities** at a university in Beijing, and he is interested in how social structures change. He observed that the town's old **hierarchical** system, in which elders made every decision, was weakening. Some young people showed open **hostility** towards traditional leaders, while others still treated them with **honor**.\n\nAt a family dinner, his uncle asked what he planned to do after graduation. \"**Hmm**,\" Wei said, thinking carefully. \"Maybe I'll come back and teach.\" His uncle smiled and called it an **honorable** choice. Before leaving, Wei bought his mother a **houseplant** and a phone **holder** for her kitchen. His younger nephew showed him a toy that projected a **hologram** of a dinosaur. \"Some things change fast,\" Wei laughed."},"23":{"w":["hydropower","hygiene","hype","hypocritical","hypothesize","iconic","idealise","idealistic","idol","immersive","immoral","imperfection","impersonal","implant","implementation","impractical","imprint","inability","inadequacy","inappropriate"],"title":"The Museum of Water","t":"Last year, a young engineer named Xiao Fang designed an **immersive** museum about **hydropower** in her hometown. Visitors walk through dark rooms where projections show rivers flowing, dams rising and turbines spinning. The experience is so realistic that children often reach out to touch the water.\n\nXiao Fang has always been **idealistic**. As a student, she dreamed of using clean energy to change the world. Her friends sometimes called her plans **impractical**, but she ignored them. \"If you don't **idealise** the future a little,\" she says, \"you'll never try to build it.\"\n\nThe museum became a sensation after a famous pop **idol** visited and posted photos online. The **hype** was enormous: thousands of fans arrived the next weekend. Some critics called the celebrity **hypocritical**, pointing out that he flew everywhere by private jet. Xiao Fang refused to comment, saying it would be **inappropriate** to judge him.\n\nThe museum also addresses difficult questions. One room shows how dams can displace communities, and visitors are asked whether forcing people to move is **immoral**. Another explains the importance of clean water and **hygiene** in developing countries. Xiao Fang wanted to show both the benefits and the **imperfection** of every technology.\n\nRunning the museum was harder than designing it. The **implementation** of her plans was slowed by an **impersonal** bureaucracy, and she often felt frustrated by her **inability** to raise enough money. She admits feeling a sense of **inadequacy** at times. A scientist friend helped her **hypothesize** how visitors learn best, and they tested ideas together. The museum's glass dome has become an **iconic** building in the city. Xiao Fang hopes the experience will leave a lasting **imprint** on young visitors and **implant** the idea that clean energy is possible."},"24":{"w":["inborn","inbox","incidental","inclusion","incomprehensible","inconsistency","inconsistent","inconvenience","indecision","indicator","indifference","indigenous","individualism","individualistic","individuality","indulgence","industrialization","ineffective","inequality","inequitable"],"title":"Two Ways of Life","t":"Dr. Tan is an anthropologist who studies an **indigenous** community living in the mountains of Taiwan. For decades, its people lived by farming and hunting, but rapid **industrialization** has transformed the region. Factories and roads have brought jobs, but also new problems.\n\nHer research compares two value systems. Traditional village life emphasises community, while modern city life encourages **individualism**. Young people who move to cities often become more **individualistic**, valuing personal freedom and **individuality** over family duty. Dr. Tan avoids judging either way of life. \"Each has strengths and weaknesses,\" she says.\n\nShe has observed growing **inequality** between those who benefit from development and those who do not. Land has been distributed in an **inequitable** way, and some families have lost their fields. Government programmes to help them have often been **ineffective**, partly because officials show **indifference** to local needs. One important **indicator** of the problem is the rising number of young people leaving school early.\n\nHer work faces challenges. Some officials find her reports **incomprehensible** because they are full of academic language, and she is trying to write more clearly. She also found **inconsistency** in government data: population figures were **inconsistent** across different reports. Gathering information can be slow, and she apologises to villagers for any **inconvenience** her visits cause.\n\nDr. Tan believes in the **inclusion** of local voices in every decision. She argues that a sense of fairness is **inborn** in all humans, and that development should never be an **indulgence** for the rich alone. Her **inbox** is full of messages from students who want to join her research. At present, the community is caught in **indecision** about whether to accept a new factory: the jobs are far from **incidental** to people's survival, but neither is their land. \"There is no simple answer,\" she says."},"25":{"w":["inequity","inevitability","infection","informative","infrequent","infringe","infringement","inheritance","inhibition","injustice","innovate","innovative","innovator","insecure","insecurity","inspirational","instinctive","institutional","instructive","insulation"],"title":"The Insulation Inventor","t":"At only twenty-four, Zhou Ning has become one of China's most celebrated young **innovators|innovator**. She invented a cheap form of **insulation** made from straw and recycled paper, which keeps rural homes warm in winter. Her story has become **inspirational** to students across the country.\n\nZhou grew up in a poor village in the north, where winters are bitterly cold. Many families could not afford heating, and children often caught a serious **infection** during the coldest months. She felt a deep sense of **injustice** at this **inequity**. \"Rich families lived comfortably while poor families froze,\" she remembers.\n\nHer decision to **innovate** came from an **instinctive** desire to help. As a university student, she spent months testing materials in her dormitory. Most experiments failed, but she found them **instructive**. When one design finally worked, she felt that success was not luck but an **inevitability** of hard work.\n\nHer **innovative** product soon attracted attention. However, a large company copied her design without permission, an **infringement** of her patent. Zhou took the company to court, arguing that it had tried to **infringe** her rights. She won the case, though the process was stressful. She admits that she sometimes feels **insecure** speaking in public, and she still struggles with **insecurity** about her abilities.\n\nZhou believes that **institutional** support is essential for young inventors. She criticises the **infrequent** funding for rural research and gives **informative** talks at universities about intellectual property. Her small **inheritance** from her grandmother paid for her first experiments. \"My grandmother taught me to overcome my **inhibition**,\" she says. \"She told me never to be afraid of trying.\""},"26":{"w":["intangible","integration","interactive","interdependence","internship","interpersonal","interviewee","interviewer","intolerance","introvert","intrusion","intuitive","invasive","investigator","investor","involuntary","involvement","irrational","irrationally","irrelevant"],"title":"The Summer Internship","t":"Last summer, my friend Rui, a shy **introvert** who studies biology, applied for an **internship** at a national park. The interview terrified her. The **interviewer** asked difficult questions about teamwork and **interpersonal** skills, and Rui felt like the weakest **interviewee** in the room. To her surprise, she got the job.\n\nHer project focused on an **invasive** plant species that was spreading rapidly through the park's wetlands. She worked with a senior **investigator** who had studied the problem for years. Rui discovered that she had an **intuitive** understanding of plant behaviour; she could often guess where the weed would appear next. Her supervisor praised her careful **involvement** in every stage of the research.\n\nThe work showed her the **interdependence** of living things. When the invasive plant took over, native insects disappeared, and birds lost their food. Rui helped design an **interactive** map that allowed visitors to see the changes over time. The park hoped the map would support better **integration** of science and public education.\n\nNot everyone welcomed the project. Some local farmers saw the researchers' visits as an **intrusion** on their land, and one wealthy **investor**, who planned to build a hotel nearby, argued that the weed was **irrelevant** to tourism. Rui found it hard to understand why people reacted so **irrationally** to scientific evidence. \"Fear makes people **irrational**,\" her supervisor explained.\n\nThe internship also taught Rui about culture. Local elders shared songs and stories, part of the region's **intangible** heritage. At one dinner, Rui politely refused milk tea because of her lactose **intolerance**, and an elder let out an **involuntary** laugh, saying that she was the first visitor ever to refuse it. By the end of the summer, Rui had grown more confident. \"I'm still quiet,\" she says, \"but now I know my voice matters.\""},"27":{"w":["irresponsible","irretrievably","irritation","islander","isle","isolation","jade","jargon","judgment","juvenile","knowledgeable","kungfu","labor","laborious","lacquer","lacrosse","landmark","landowner","laptop","lateness"],"title":"The Kungfu School on the Isle","t":"On a small **isle** off the coast of Fujian, there is a **kungfu** school that has trained students for more than a century. Its stone gate has become a famous **landmark**, and visitors often take photos there. The school was founded by a wealthy **landowner** who believed that martial arts built both body and character.\n\nLife on the island is simple. Its **isolation** means that students must focus completely on training. Most of them are **juvenile** boys and girls sent by parents who hope they will become more disciplined. Training is **laborious**: students rise at five, run along the beach and practise for hours. Physical **labor**, such as carrying water and repairing walls, is also part of the daily routine.\n\nThe headmaster, Master Lin, is calm and **knowledgeable**. He avoids technical **jargon** and explains ideas in simple words. He believes that good **judgment** matters more than strength. \"A fighter who acts without thinking is **irresponsible**,\" he says. He punishes **lateness** strictly: students who arrive late must clean the courtyard.\n\nIslanders are proud of the school. Local craftsmen carve **jade** ornaments and make red **lacquer** boxes that students buy as gifts for their families. Every **islander** knows the students by name and treats them like family. Last year, a group of American students visited and taught the children to play **lacrosse**, while the children taught them basic kungfu moves.\n\nModern life has reached the island too. Master Lin now uses a **laptop** to manage the school's records, though he admits it causes him great **irritation**. Some traditions have been **irretrievably** lost, such as old training songs that no one recorded. But Master Lin is hopeful. \"The heart of kungfu is patience,\" he says. \"That will never disappear.\""},"28":{"w":["lavish","lawmaker","learner","legendary","legislative","legislature","legitimacy","lenient","levy","lifeless","lifelong","lifespan","lifestyle","limelight","linkage","livelihood","livestream","livestreaming","loess","logo"],"title":"Livestreaming from the Loess Plateau","t":"On the **Loess** Plateau in northern Shaanxi, where yellow hills stretch to the horizon, a young farmer named Gao Lei has become an internet star. Every evening, he hosts a **livestream** selling apples, millet and dried dates from his village. Thousands of viewers watch him, and his sales have transformed the **livelihood** of dozens of families.\n\nGao never expected to enter the **limelight**. He grew up herding sheep and was always a slow **learner** at school. After working in a city factory for five years, he returned home because the village was dying. Farming on the soft **loess** soil was difficult, and many fields had become **lifeless** after years of erosion.\n\nHe started **livestreaming** with a cheap phone and a homemade **logo** painted on a piece of cardboard. His honesty attracted viewers. He showed his real **lifestyle**: early mornings, hard work and simple meals. Soon, he became something of a **legendary** figure in the region, and journalists began to visit.\n\nHis success caught the attention of a provincial **lawmaker**, who invited him to speak to the **legislature** about rural development. Gao argued for **legislative** changes to help small farmers, such as being **lenient** with online sellers and reducing the tax **levy** on farm products. Some critics questioned the **legitimacy** of internet sellers, but Gao insisted that his products were real.\n\nGao is careful not to live a **lavish** life. He reinvests most of his income in the village, and he has built a **linkage** between farmers and city buyers. He has also planted trees to protect the soil, which could extend the **lifespan** of the farmland. \"This is my **lifelong** mission,\" he says."},"29":{"w":["loneliness","longitudinal","loom","lure","lyric","magical","mainstream","maize","majestic","mandate","mandatory","maneuver","manipulative","manufacturing","markedly","marketable","marketer","marketing","marketplace","markup"],"title":"The Corn Festival","t":"Every autumn, a mountain village in Guizhou holds a festival to celebrate the **maize** harvest. The village **marketplace** fills with stalls selling corn cakes, roasted cobs and handwoven cloth made on a traditional wooden **loom**. Behind the village, **majestic** mountains rise into the clouds, and the evening light makes everything look **magical**.\n\nThe festival has changed **markedly** in recent years. It began as a small local event, but a clever **marketer** from the county tourism office saw its potential. Through social media **marketing**, the festival has entered the **mainstream**, attracting thousands of visitors from the cities. Local products have become far more **marketable**, and farmers can now sell their corn at a higher **markup**.\n\nNot everyone is happy. Some villagers worry that tourism will **lure** young people away from farming. Others dislike the large **manufacturing** companies that now produce cheap souvenirs. A **longitudinal** study by a university has followed the village for ten years and found that incomes have risen, but so has **loneliness** among older residents whose children have left.\n\nThe government has introduced new rules. A provincial **mandate** requires that all festival food meet safety standards, and it is now **mandatory** for stall owners to obtain licences. Some businessmen have tried every **maneuver** to avoid the rules, and one was accused of being **manipulative** in his dealings with farmers.\n\nThe highlight of the festival is a singing contest. Women in bright costumes sing folk songs whose **lyrics|lyric** describe love, harvest and mountains. As the competition approaches, excitement begins to **loom** over the whole village. Visitors say the festival is unforgettable."},"30":{"w":["martial","materialize","maximize","medication","memorable","mentality","mentor","metabolism","metaphor","meter","methodology","meticulous","metro","micro","mid","midlife","migration","milestone","mindfulness","mindlessly"],"title":"A Midlife Change","t":"At forty-five, Mr. Song faced a **midlife** crisis. He had worked for twenty years as an engineer, spending long hours in an office and taking the **metro** home late every night. His health was poor; his doctor warned that his **metabolism** had slowed and that he needed **medication** for high blood pressure. \"I felt like a machine,\" he says.\n\nThe change began when a friend introduced him to **martial** arts. Mr. Song joined a small class led by an elderly **mentor**, Master Ho, who taught him not only movement but also **mindfulness**. \"Most people live **mindlessly**,\" Master Ho told him. \"They eat, work and sleep without noticing anything.\"\n\nMr. Song approached training with the same **meticulous** care he used in engineering. He kept detailed notes, measuring his progress in every **micro** detail of his movements. He even developed a **methodology** for tracking his heart rate and breathing. Master Ho laughed at his notebooks but admired his dedication.\n\nOver time, his **mentality** changed. He began to see life through a new **metaphor**: a river that flows around obstacles instead of fighting them. He started walking to work, counting every **meter**, and his blood pressure fell. In **mid**-June, he reached an important **milestone**: he passed his first martial arts exam.\n\nMr. Song now teaches beginners on weekends. He says the most **memorable** moment came when his own son joined the class. He hopes to **maximize** the time he has with his family. Each spring, they watch the **migration** of birds over the lake near their home. \"Dreams can **materialize** at any age,\" he says. \"You just have to start.\""},"31":{"w":["mindset","mini","miscalculate","misconception","misguided","mishandle","misrepresent","mistreatment","modernise","modernity","modernization","monastery","monolingual","monotonous","moody","mooncake","moonlight","morale","mortality","motherhood"],"title":"Mid-Autumn at the Monastery","t":"Last Mid-Autumn Festival, I spent three days at a Buddhist **monastery** in the mountains of Zhejiang. I had expected silence and **monotonous** routines, but the experience was full of surprises. On the festival night, the monks shared **mooncakes|mooncake** with visitors in the courtyard, and we sat together under the **moonlight**.\n\nI arrived with a **misconception**. I believed that monks rejected **modernity** completely, living as people did a thousand years ago. In fact, the monastery had begun to **modernise** in many ways: solar panels powered the kitchen, and a young monk managed bookings online. The abbot explained that **modernization** was not the enemy of tradition. \"The **mindset** matters more than the tools,\" he said.\n\nThe monks told me about the region's history. Decades ago, poverty was severe, and infant **mortality** was high. The monastery ran a small clinic, and the abbot's mother, who had experienced the hardships of **motherhood** in those years, volunteered there. Today, the village is healthier, and the clinic has become a **mini** hospital.\n\nNot all visitors behave well. Some tourists are noisy or **moody**, complaining about simple food and early mornings. A few journalists have tried to **misrepresent** the monastery as a business, and one reporter published a **misguided** article claiming that monks lived in luxury. The abbot laughed it off. \"People often **miscalculate** what makes us happy,\" he said.\n\nThe monastery also cares for animals. Monks rescue dogs and cats that have suffered **mistreatment**, and they never **mishandle** even a small insect. The abbot is fluent in three languages and teaches **monolingual** visitors a few words of Sanskrit. When I left, I felt calm and grateful. The visit had lifted my **morale** more than any holiday."},"32":{"w":["motivational","motivator","multicultural","multifaceted","multinational","municipality","nationalist","navigate","needy","neighbor","neuroscience","neuroscientist","neutrality","newborn","nomad","nomadic","non","nonconformity","nonprofit","noteworthy"],"title":"Mapping the Nomad Brain","t":"Dr. Sarah Okoro is a **neuroscientist** who studies how people **navigate** the world. Her latest project took her to the grasslands of Mongolia, where she worked with **nomadic** herding families. \"**Nomads|nomad** have an extraordinary sense of direction,\" she explains. \"They can cross hundreds of kilometres without maps.\"\n\nHer research in **neuroscience** focuses on the parts of the brain that store spatial memory. Using portable equipment, her team measured brain activity while herders found their way across the steppe. The results were **noteworthy**: experienced herders showed stronger activity in memory regions than city dwellers.\n\nThe project was supported by a **nonprofit** organisation that works with **needy** rural families. In exchange for participation, the team provided medical checks, including care for every **newborn** in the camps. Dr. Okoro insisted on scientific **neutrality**, avoiding political debates about land rights. She also worked hard to explain her research to **non**-scientists in simple language.\n\nHer team was **multicultural**, with members from Nigeria, China, Germany and Mongolia. Dr. Okoro says that a **multifaceted** team brings better ideas. She praised one Mongolian assistant as the project's greatest **motivator**, and his **motivational** speeches kept everyone going during cold nights. Some local **nationalist** groups were suspicious of foreign scientists at first, but trust grew over time.\n\nDr. Okoro has always valued **nonconformity**. She turned down a well-paid job at a **multinational** technology company to continue her research. She now lives in a small apartment owned by the **municipality**, and her elderly **neighbor** waters her plants when she travels. \"The brain is the most amazing map in the world,\" she says. \"I'm still learning to read it.\""},"33":{"w":["nutritional","nutritionist","nutritious","obesity","observational","observatory","obsessive","odds","offender","offense","ongoing","online","onset","openness","opposition","opt","optimize","ordeal","organisational","organizer"],"title":"The School Nutritionist","t":"Ms. He is a **nutritionist** who works with primary schools in a large southern city. Her mission is to fight childhood **obesity**, which has risen sharply in recent years. \"The **odds** of a child becoming overweight have doubled in a decade,\" she says. \"This is an **ongoing** crisis.\"\n\nHer work began with an **observational** study. She visited dozens of school canteens and recorded what children actually ate. The results were worrying: many meals had little **nutritional** value, and sugary snacks were everywhere. Schools that allowed vending machines were the worst **offenders|offender**, and some parents took **offense** when she said so publicly.\n\nMs. He faced strong **opposition** from food companies, which feared losing sales. She also met resistance from school managers, who worried about the cost of change. But she was persistent. She worked as the chief **organizer** of a city-wide campaign, bringing together doctors, teachers and parents. Her **organisational** skills impressed everyone.\n\nThe campaign aimed to **optimize** school menus without increasing costs. Schools began serving **nutritious** meals with more vegetables and less sugar. Parents could **opt** for healthier lunch packages through an **online** system, and the response was enthusiastic. Ms. He encouraged **openness**, publishing all menus and test results.\n\nThe experience was a personal **ordeal** at times. She received angry messages, and her husband, an astronomer at the city **observatory**, worried about her health. She admits that she can be **obsessive** about details. But the results speak for themselves: fewer children now show signs of early **onset** diabetes. \"Changing habits is slow,\" she says, \"but children deserve the best start.\""},"34":{"w":["originality","outdate","outfit","outperform","outrageous","outreach","outright","outweigh","overabundance","overburden","overhaul","overindulge","overprice","override","oversee","overturn","overuse","overview","overwork","pagoda"],"title":"Restoring the Pagoda","t":"On a hill above the river stands a nine-storey brick **pagoda** that is more than eight hundred years old. For decades, it was neglected: plants grew from its cracks, and the top floors were closed to visitors. Two years ago, the city decided on a complete **overhaul** of the building.\n\nThe project was led by an architect named Mr. Su, who was chosen to **oversee** the work because of his reputation for **originality**. He began with an **overview** of the pagoda's history, studying old drawings and photographs. He discovered that earlier repairs, using **outdated|outdate** methods, had actually damaged the structure.\n\nMr. Su faced many challenges. The budget was tight, and some suppliers tried to **overprice** materials. One company's prices were so **outrageous** that he rejected its offer **outright**. He refused to **overuse** modern cement, which could crack the old bricks, and he insisted on traditional lime mortar. \"The benefits of authenticity **outweigh** the extra cost,\" he argued.\n\nNot everyone agreed. A city official tried to **override** his decisions to save money, and at one point it seemed the council might **overturn** the whole plan. Mr. Su's team worked long hours, and he worried that he might **overburden** them. \"I told them not to **overwork**,\" he says, \"but they loved the project too much.\"\n\nToday, the pagoda is open again. Its restoration has helped it **outperform** other tourist sites in the region. The city runs an **outreach** programme inviting schoolchildren to learn about ancient architecture. Visitors are warned not to **overindulge** in snacks sold at the bottom of the hill, where an **overabundance** of stalls has appeared. Mr. Su still wears the same work **outfit** he wore on the first day: a blue jacket and a hard hat."},"35":{"w":["pandemic","paper-cut","papermaking","paradigm","paradoxical","parasite","parental","parenting","parity","participation","partnership","passionate","password","pathway","patriotism","pavilion","paycheck","perfectionism","perfectionist","perishable"],"title":"The Paper-Cut Artist","t":"Grandma Zhao is a famous **paper-cut** artist from a village in Shaanxi. Using only scissors and red paper, she creates intricate designs of flowers, animals and scenes from village life. Her work is displayed in a **pavilion** at the provincial museum, and visitors are amazed by its detail.\n\nShe learned the craft as a child from her mother, who also taught her the history of **papermaking** in China. Grandma Zhao describes herself as a **perfectionist**; she sometimes spends weeks on a single piece. Her **perfectionism** can be frustrating for her students, but they admire her standards.\n\nHer life has not been easy. As a young woman, she raised five children while working in the fields. She describes **parenting** in those years as a constant struggle, with little **parental** support from the government. \"Our **paycheck** was a bag of rice,\" she jokes. She sees a **paradoxical** truth in her story: poverty made her stronger and more creative.\n\nToday, Grandma Zhao is **passionate** about passing on her skills. During the **pandemic**, when schools closed, she taught classes online, though she always forgot her **password**. Her granddaughter now runs the classes, and **participation** has grown enormously. The village has formed a **partnership** with a university, creating a new **pathway** for young people to study traditional art.\n\nShe believes that folk art deserves **parity** with fine art. \"Both come from the heart,\" she says. Her designs often express **patriotism** and love for her homeland. One of her favourite works shows farmers protecting their crops from a **parasite** that once destroyed the harvest. Some scholars describe her work as a new **paradigm** for rural art. Her paper is fragile and **perishable**, but she hopes her ideas will last forever."},"36":{"w":["perpetuate","perseverance","personalize","persuasive","pesticide","pharmaceutical","pharmacy","phd","philosophical","phoenix","phonic","piety","pizza","placement","plagiarism","planetary","planner","planter","plasticity","playful"],"title":"The Pharmacist's Daughter","t":"My friend Li Na grew up above her parents' small **pharmacy** in a quiet town in Hunan. As a child, she loved watching her father prepare medicines and listening to customers describe their problems. Today, she is finishing a **PhD** in chemistry, and her research focuses on safer alternatives to the **pesticide** chemicals used on farms.\n\nHer path was not easy. She failed her first university entrance exam, but her **perseverance** paid off the following year. During her studies, she completed a **placement** at a large **pharmaceutical** company, where she learned how new drugs are tested. She says the experience showed her both the power and the responsibility of science.\n\nLi Na is deeply **philosophical** about her work. She believes that science must protect the health of the planet as a whole, not just individuals. \"Our problems are **planetary**,\" she says. \"Pesticides travel through water and air, far beyond the fields.\" She hopes her research will help stop farming methods that **perpetuate** pollution.\n\nShe is also a natural teacher. At weekends, she tutors primary school children, using **phonic** games to help them read English. She tries to **personalize** each lesson and encourages a **playful** spirit in class. \"Children's brains have amazing **plasticity**,\" she explains. \"They learn best when they're having fun.\"\n\nLi Na takes academic honesty seriously. When a classmate was caught committing **plagiarism**, she was shocked and upset. Her own writing is clear and **persuasive**, and her supervisor calls her a born scientist. She shows great **piety** towards her parents, returning home every holiday. Her father keeps a **planter** of herbs on the windowsill and a **planner** full of customers' birthdays. When she finishes her degree, the family plans to celebrate with **pizza**, her favourite food. Her mother compares her to a **phoenix**: \"She rose from failure and flew higher.\""},"37":{"w":["plaza","pleasurable","plight","plumber","poetic","poise","polarization","policymaker","pollutant","popularize","populate","portrayal","positivity","postmaster","potent","pottery","practitioner","pragmatic","praiseworthy","predator"],"title":"The Pottery Market","t":"Every Sunday, the central **plaza** of our old town fills with stalls selling handmade **pottery**. Bowls, vases and teapots in every colour line the square, and craftsmen work at their wheels while visitors watch. For many tourists, browsing the market is the most **pleasurable** part of their trip.\n\nThe market was founded by a retired **postmaster**, Mr. Dong, who loved pottery and wanted to help local artists. Twenty years ago, their **plight** was serious: cheap factory goods had nearly destroyed traditional crafts. Mr. Dong persuaded the town's **policymakers|policymaker** to provide space for a weekly market. His **pragmatic** approach and quiet **positivity** won everyone's support.\n\nThe craftsmen are a colourful group. One potter, a former **plumber**, says that working with clay feels like working with pipes, but more **poetic**. Another, an experienced **practitioner** of a thousand-year-old glazing method, has helped to **popularize** the technique among young artists. Visitors admire the **poise** with which she lifts delicate pieces from the kiln.\n\nThe market has faced challenges. A nearby factory once released a **pollutant** into the river, damaging the clay beds where potters collect their materials. The issue caused **polarization** in the town: some residents defended the factory jobs, while others demanded its closure. Eventually, the factory installed filters, a decision many called **praiseworthy**.\n\nThe area around the plaza has become more densely **populated|populate**, and new cafés have opened. A local film offered a warm **portrayal** of the market, and its message about community proved **potent**. Mr. Dong compares big companies to a **predator** that hunts small businesses. \"But together,\" he says, \"we survive.\""},"38":{"w":["predictable","prehistoric","preoccupy","preschool","preschooler","prestigious","preteen","prioritize","pro","problematic","programmer","progression","proofread","prosecutor","prosper","protectionist","protector","provider","psychologist","purist"],"title":"A Programmer's Family","t":"Mr. Lu is a **programmer** at a technology company in Shenzhen. He works long hours, but his real passion is his family. He has two children: a **preschooler** who loves dinosaurs and a **preteen** daughter who wants to become a **prosecutor**. Balancing work and family is his constant challenge.\n\nHis younger son attends a **preschool** near the office, and Mr. Lu picks him up every afternoon. On weekends, they visit the city museum, where the boy stares at **prehistoric** skeletons for hours. \"He's completely **preoccupied|preoccupy** with dinosaurs,\" Mr. Lu laughs. His daughter, meanwhile, studies hard, hoping to attend a **prestigious** law school one day.\n\nA family **psychologist** once advised Mr. Lu to **prioritize** time with his children over overtime at work. He found the advice difficult but important. He now lists the **pros|pro** and cons of every extra project before agreeing to it. Long working hours, he admits, are **problematic** for many families in his industry.\n\nMr. Lu sees himself as both a **provider** and a **protector**. He worries about the **progression** of technology and its effect on children. He limits screen time, though he confesses he is not a **purist**: the children are allowed one cartoon every evening. He also helps his daughter **proofread** her essays, correcting her grammar and spelling.\n\nHis company has faced difficulties. Trade disputes and **protectionist** policies in some countries have made exports harder, and growth has become less **predictable**. But Mr. Lu remains optimistic. \"Good families and good companies **prosper** for the same reason,\" he says. \"They care about people.\""},"39":{"w":["qualifier","quinoa","quitter","radiance","rationality","readiness","rebound","receptive","recharge","recollection","reconstruct","recreate","recreational","recruiter","rectification","redefine","reexamine","reforestation","regain","regional"],"title":"The Forest Comes Back","t":"Twenty years ago, the hills around my hometown were bare and brown, the result of decades of logging. Today, they are covered with young pine and oak trees, thanks to a **regional** **reforestation** programme. The project has helped the land **regain** its health and brought birds and animals back to the valley.\n\nMy uncle, a forestry officer, has worked on the project from the start. His earliest **recollection** of the hills is of dust storms that blew across the fields every spring. \"We had to **redefine** our relationship with nature,\" he says. \"We had to **reexamine** everything we believed about progress.\"\n\nThe programme required careful planning. Scientists worked to **reconstruct** the original forest, studying old records to **recreate** its mixture of species. A government **rectification** campaign closed illegal logging companies, and a **recruiter** hired hundreds of local workers to plant trees. At first, villagers were not **receptive** to the idea, but their **readiness** to help grew when they saw the results.\n\nThe forest has also become a place for **recreational** activities. Families walk the trails at weekends, and city workers come to **recharge** their energy. The local economy has begun to **rebound**, with new guesthouses and cafés. A farmer who once grew corn now grows **quinoa** for health-food shops.\n\nMy uncle believes that **rationality** and patience saved the land. \"Some people said we'd fail,\" he says. \"But I'm not a **quitter**.\" His cousin, a long-distance runner, trains on the forest paths and recently became a national **qualifier** for the marathon. On autumn mornings, sunlight fills the valley with a soft **radiance**, and my uncle smiles at the hills he helped to save."},"40":{"w":["regulatory","rehabilitation","reinforcement","reintroduce","related","relaxation","relentless","reliant","relic","reminder","renewable","renewal","renovate","renowned","rental","repetitive","reportedly","reproductive","reputable","resentful"],"title":"The Stork Rescue Centre","t":"On the edge of a wetland in eastern China, a small centre carries out the **rehabilitation** of injured birds, especially the rare oriental stork. Its founder, Dr. Han, is a **renowned** biologist who has spent thirty years protecting the species. The centre has **reportedly** saved more than five hundred birds.\n\nThe work is **relentless**. Volunteers feed the birds several times a day, clean their cages and record their health. The tasks are **repetitive**, but everyone understands their importance. Dr. Han's team also studies the storks' **reproductive** habits, hoping to increase their numbers in the wild.\n\nThe centre's greatest success came last year, when it helped **reintroduce** twenty storks into a protected wetland. Government **regulatory** agencies supported the project, and a **reputable** international charity provided funding. A group of volunteers from a **related** organisation acted as **reinforcement** during the busy spring season.\n\nThe centre itself was once an abandoned farmhouse. Volunteers worked for months to **renovate** it, installing **renewable** energy systems such as solar panels and a small wind turbine. The building's **rental** contract is due for **renewal** next year, and Dr. Han hopes the owner will offer a lower price. In the garden, volunteers found an old stone **relic**, which now sits by the entrance as a **reminder** of the farm's long history.\n\nNot everyone supports the centre. Some local farmers are **resentful** because storks eat fish from their ponds. Dr. Han meets them regularly to find solutions. She believes that the wetland should not remain **reliant** on human help forever. For **relaxation**, she sits by the water at sunset, watching the storks return home."},"41":{"w":["reshape","resourceful","respondent","responsive","restoration","restructure","rethink","retrain","retrieval","reunite","revisit","revitalization","revitalize","revival","revolutionize","reword","rhetoric","riches","rigor","risky"],"title":"Bringing an Old Town Back to Life","t":"Ten years ago, the old mining town of Pingshan seemed to have no future. The mines had closed, young people had left, and the main street was full of empty shops. Today, it is one of the most popular tourist destinations in the province. Its **revitalization** shows how careful planning can **reshape** a community.\n\nThe change began when the town's new mayor decided to **rethink** its economy completely. She commissioned a survey of residents, and every **respondent** was asked what they valued most about the town. The answers were clear: history, nature and community. The mayor's plan was to **revitalize** the town around these strengths.\n\nThe work was **risky**. The council had to **restructure** its budget and borrow money to fund the **restoration** of old buildings. Former miners were invited to **retrain** as guides, builders and craftsmen. A **resourceful** group of retired teachers organised the **retrieval** of old photographs and documents from family homes, creating a museum of the town's history.\n\nThe mayor avoided empty **rhetoric**. When she presented her plans, she spoke simply and honestly, and she was **responsive** to criticism. Once, after residents complained that her proposal was too complicated, she agreed to **reword** it in plain language. Her team applied scientific **rigor** to every decision, measuring results carefully.\n\nThe results have been remarkable. Tourism has helped to **revolutionize** the local economy, and a **revival** of traditional crafts has created new jobs. Many families who left years ago have returned, and several have been able to **reunite** with relatives they had not seen for a decade. Visitors who **revisit** the town are amazed by the changes. \"We didn't find **riches**,\" the mayor says. \"We found ourselves.\""},"42":{"w":["roadside","robotic","roommate","ruinous","rum","runoff","sales","sameness","sarcasm","sarcastic","sarcastically","scanner","scary","scenario","scenic","scroll","scrutinize","seafood","seeker","selective"],"title":"The Road Trip","t":"Last summer, my **roommate** Kai and I took a road trip along the southern coast. We had planned the route carefully, choosing the most **scenic** roads and booking cheap hotels. Kai is famous for his **sarcasm**, and he joked that our old car would never survive the journey.\n\nThe first days were wonderful. We stopped at every **roadside** stall selling fresh fruit and grilled **seafood**, and we watched the sunset from cliffs above the sea. One evening, a bar owner offered us glasses of local **rum**, which tasted like burnt sugar. Kai is **selective** about food, but even he admitted it was delicious.\n\nThen things went wrong. On the third day, heavy rain caused **runoff** from the mountains to flood the road, and we had to wait for hours. The car began to make **scary** noises, and the repair bill was almost **ruinous** for two students. Kai said **sarcastically** that it was \"the best holiday ever.\" I couldn't help laughing at his **sarcastic** tone, even though I was worried.\n\nAt the garage, a mechanic used a **robotic** arm and a computer **scanner** to diagnose the problem. He explained the worst-case **scenario**: the engine might need replacing. While we waited, Kai would **scroll** through his phone, looking for cheaper solutions. I tried to **scrutinize** the bill, but I understood nothing.\n\nFortunately, the problem was small. As we drove on, Kai noticed the **sameness** of the new hotels along the coast; each one looked identical. The receptionist at one told us that **sales** of holiday packages had fallen because tourists wanted something different. \"We're adventure **seekers|seeker**,\" Kai told her proudly. \"We just want our car to work.\""},"43":{"w":["selectively","semaphore","sensational","sensory","sentimental","sequel","seriousness","severity","showcase","shyness","sidewalk","signaling","silkworm","simplistic","simulation","sinful","slash","sleeper","slump","smallness"],"title":"The Silk Museum","t":"In the city of Hangzhou, a small museum tells the story of silk, from the tiny **silkworm** to the finest fabric in the world. Its main hall is a **showcase** of ancient robes, embroidered shoes and painted fans. Visitors often describe the experience as a **sensory** journey: they can touch raw silk, smell mulberry leaves and listen to the sound of old looms.\n\nThe museum's director, Ms. Xu, is a quiet woman whose **shyness** disappears when she talks about silk. She grew up in a family of silk weavers and has a **sentimental** attachment to the craft. She dislikes **simplistic** stories about history and insists that the museum show the hard work of ordinary weavers, not just the luxury of emperors.\n\nOne room describes how silk was traded along ancient routes. A **simulation** allows visitors to \"travel\" by camel across deserts, and a display explains how merchants used flags and **semaphore** for **signaling** between caravans. Another room explores the **severity** of working conditions in early factories, treating the subject with **seriousness**.\n\nThe museum has faced difficulties. During an economic **slump**, the government had to **slash** its budget, and Ms. Xu worried it might close. A **sensational** news story claimed that the museum was selling fake antiques, but investigators proved it false. She admits that she reads such reports only **selectively** now.\n\nToday, the museum is thriving. Tourists arrive by overnight **sleeper** train from Beijing, and the **sidewalk** outside is often crowded. A popular documentary about the museum is getting a **sequel**. Old weavers once believed it was **sinful** to waste even a thread of silk. Ms. Xu says that the **smallness** of each silkworm makes its achievement all the more remarkable."},"44":{"w":["smartphone","snobbery","sociable","socialize","societal","socioeconomic","sociologist","solicit","solicitor","solitude","solstice","someday","sooner","southwestern","spa","sparingly","specialty","speculation","speedily","spending"],"title":"Life Through a Smartphone","t":"Dr. Pan is a **sociologist** who studies how the **smartphone** has changed daily life in China. Her research covers people from different **socioeconomic** backgrounds, from wealthy city professionals to farmers in remote **southwestern** villages. \"Phones have transformed every **societal** institution,\" she says, \"from families to markets.\"\n\nHer findings are surprising. Many people use phones to **socialize** more than ever, joining online groups and sharing photos. But Dr. Pan has also found that some **sociable** people feel lonelier than before. Others actively seek **solitude**, turning off their phones on weekends or visiting a mountain **spa** to escape notifications.\n\nDr. Pan studies **spending** habits too. Online shopping has made buying things easier, and many young people spend money **speedily** and without thinking. Advertising apps **solicit** personal data in exchange for discounts. She advises people to use apps **sparingly** and to read privacy rules carefully. A **solicitor** she interviewed warned that many users don't understand the contracts they accept.\n\nHer research has revealed a new kind of **snobbery**: some people judge others by the brand of phone they carry. Dr. Pan's **specialty** is the study of rural communities, where phones have helped farmers sell crops online. She dismisses **speculation** that technology will destroy village life. \"It's changing, not disappearing,\" she says.\n\nShe practises what she preaches. On the winter **solstice** each year, she spends a day without any devices, walking in the countryside. \"**Someday**, we will learn to balance technology and life,\" she says. \"The **sooner** we start, the better.\""},"45":{"w":["spicy","spire","splendour","spokesperson","sponsorship","sprawl","staffer","stagnation","staple","starter","startup","statutory","stifle","stigma","stimulation","stipulate","stressful","stuffiness","stump","sub"],"title":"The Hotpot Startup","t":"Three years ago, two friends from Chengdu launched a **startup** selling ready-to-cook **spicy** hotpot kits online. Their idea was simple: bring the taste of Sichuan to busy office workers across the country. Today, the company employs eighty people and sells millions of kits every year.\n\nThe founders, Xiao Ma and Xiao Liu, started with almost nothing. They rented a small kitchen with no windows, and its **stuffiness** in summer was almost unbearable. Early months were **stressful**: they worked through the night, and some days they barely sold anything. Hard questions from investors would often **stump** them, and Xiao Liu's mother was sure the business would never grow.\n\nTheir big break came through a **sponsorship** deal with a popular online cooking show. The company's **spokesperson**, a cheerful **staffer** named Lili, became famous for her funny videos. Sales grew rapidly, and the kits became a **staple** in many young people's kitchens. Each box includes a small **starter** dish of pickled vegetables.\n\nThe company has faced challenges. New government rules **stipulate** strict standards for food packaging, and meeting these **statutory** requirements was expensive. The founders worry that too many regulations could **stifle** innovation. They also fought the **stigma** that instant food is unhealthy, proving that their kits used fresh ingredients.\n\nTheir headquarters is now in a modern building in the city's eastern **sprawl**, with a view of an old temple **spire**. The founders believe that economic **stagnation** is not inevitable; creativity provides the **stimulation** that growth needs. On weekends, they play basketball, and Xiao Ma usually comes in as a **sub** for the second half. \"Success is not about **splendour**,\" he says. \"It's about good food and good friends.\""},"46":{"w":["submissive","subset","subsidize","subtlety","supercomputing","superintendent","supervisor","supplier","supporter","supportive","supposedly","surf","surgical","surveillance","susceptibility","sustainability","sustainable","sustainably","symbolic","symbolize"],"title":"The Green Supercomputer","t":"On the edge of a mountain city in Guizhou, there is a huge **supercomputing** centre hidden inside a cave. The cool air underground keeps the machines from overheating, and the centre is powered mainly by wind and water. Engineers say it is a model of **sustainability**: proof that advanced technology can be run **sustainably**.\n\nThe centre's director, Dr. Kong, is a **supportive** leader who encourages young engineers to share ideas. She began her career as a research **supervisor** at a university, and she still treats her staff like students. \"Every problem has a **subtlety** that only careful thinkers notice,\" she tells them.\n\nBuilding a **sustainable** centre was not easy. The government agreed to **subsidize** part of the cost, but the team had to find a **supplier** willing to provide recycled equipment. The building **superintendent** spent months checking cooling systems and safety rules. Some critics argued that the project was too expensive, but **supporters|supporter** insisted that it would pay for itself within ten years.\n\nThe supercomputer is used for many purposes. Doctors use it to design **surgical** robots, and scientists study the **susceptibility** of crops to climate change. Only a small **subset** of its power is used for commercial work. Dr. Kong firmly refuses to use it for mass **surveillance**, saying that technology must respect privacy.\n\nThe cave has become **symbolic** of the region's transformation. Guizhou was once one of China's poorest provinces, and the centre has come to **symbolize** its new confidence. Dr. Kong dislikes the old idea that rural regions must be **submissive** to big cities. She laughs at the claim that engineers spend their days **surfing|surf** the internet. \"**Supposedly** we have the easiest job in the world,\" she says. \"Come and try it.\""},"47":{"w":["syndrome","tactfully","tactic","talented","taoism","taxation","teahouse","teammate","tech","technological","teddy","teen","teenage","telecom","telecommunication","telegraphy","telemedicine","telomere","tenure","terraced"],"title":"The Teahouse on the Hill","t":"On a hillside covered with **terraced** tea fields in Fujian, there is a small **teahouse** run by an old man named Grandpa Xu. Visitors climb a narrow path to reach it, and they are rewarded with fresh tea and a view of green hills. Grandpa Xu is a follower of **Taoism**, and he believes that tea teaches patience and balance.\n\nThe teahouse has become popular with young people. A group of **teen** hikers recently stopped there, and one **teenage** girl, a **talented** photographer, took pictures that went viral online. She and her **teammate** from a school hiking club have since returned many times. One of them always carries a small **teddy** bear as a lucky charm.\n\nGrandpa Xu's grandson works in **tech**, and he has brought some **technological** changes to the teahouse. A new **telecom** tower on the next hill now provides internet access, and **telecommunication** has transformed village life. During a recent illness, Grandpa Xu used **telemedicine** to talk with a doctor in the city. He jokes that, as a boy, the only fast communication he knew was **telegraphy** at the county post office.\n\nHe handles difficult customers **tactfully**. When tourists complain about prices, he explains how **taxation** and transport costs affect small businesses. His favourite **tactic** is to offer a free second cup, which usually ends any argument.\n\nGrandpa Xu enjoys reading about science. He once read an article about **telomere** length and ageing, and he laughs that tea must be good for his. Doctors say he shows no sign of any age-related **syndrome**. He has held the **tenure** of the teahouse for fifty years, and he has no plans to stop."},"48":{"w":["terracotta","theatrical","theorist","therapist","thorny","tidiness","timer","toll","tonne","tranquility","transformative","transparency","traumatic","traveling","trendy","tribal","tributary","tricky","trillion","trustworthy"],"title":"Soldiers of Clay","t":"Last year, a **traveling** exhibition of **terracotta** warriors from Xi'an toured several European cities. The ancient clay soldiers, each more than two thousand years old, attracted huge crowds. Many visitors described the experience as **transformative**: standing face to face with the warriors made history feel real.\n\nTransporting the statues was a **tricky** task. Each figure weighs hundreds of kilograms, and some of the horses weigh almost a **tonne**. Experts packed them carefully in special boxes, and a **timer** recorded every moment the statues were outside controlled conditions. The insurance value of the collection was estimated in the hundreds of millions, though one journalist exaggerated it to a **trillion** yuan.\n\nThe exhibition raised some **thorny** questions. Critics asked about the **transparency** of agreements between museums, and others debated whether ancient treasures should travel at all. A historian and **theorist** of museum studies argued that sharing culture builds trust between nations. The museum also had to be **trustworthy**, keeping careful records of every object.\n\nThe display itself was designed with **theatrical** lighting, creating shadows that made the soldiers seem alive. Some visitors found it too **trendy**, preferring the calm **tranquility** of traditional museums. The curators were famous for their **tidiness**, and every label was perfectly aligned.\n\nThe exhibition also explored history's darker side. Thousands of workers built the emperor's tomb, and many died; the **toll** was enormous. A **therapist** who visited said the stories of forced labour were **traumatic** for some visitors. The exhibition compared the emperor's armies with **tribal** warriors in other cultures. One map showed the Yellow River and each **tributary** that fed the ancient capital."},"49":{"w":["truthful","tweet","ultra","unacceptable","unaffordable","unanticipated","unaware","uncanny","uncertainty","unclear","uncommon","uncontrollable","uncontrolled","uncritical","undeniably","underlie","underprivileged","underrepresented","undervalue","underway"],"title":"A Chance for Every Student","t":"Five years ago, a university in Beijing launched a programme to help **underprivileged** students from rural areas. These students are **underrepresented** at top universities, where most places go to children from wealthy city families. The programme, now well **underway**, has helped hundreds of young people achieve their dreams.\n\nIts founder, Professor Gu, says that the problem is **undeniably** serious. Many talented rural students are **unaware** of opportunities, and university fees can be **unaffordable** for poor families. \"Schools often **undervalue** students who speak with a rural accent,\" she says. \"That is **unacceptable**.\"\n\nThe programme has faced **uncertainty** from the start. Funding was **unclear** in the first year, and some officials were **uncritical** supporters of the old system. **Unanticipated** problems also appeared: some students felt lonely and struggled with **uncontrollable** homesickness. The programme added counselling services to help them adjust.\n\nProfessor Gu insists on being **truthful** about the challenges. She once posted a **tweet** criticising **ultra**-competitive exam culture, and it was shared thousands of times. Some critics accused her of encouraging **uncontrolled** admissions, but she replied calmly that standards remained high.\n\nThe success stories are inspiring. One student, the first in her village to attend university, now studies medicine. She has an **uncanny** ability to remember every detail of her textbooks. Such talent is not **uncommon**, Professor Gu says; it simply needs a chance. She believes that fairness should **underlie** every education policy."},"50":{"w":["undesired","undeveloped","undisguised","unequal","unerring","unexcused","unfamiliar","unfavorable","unhappiness","unheard","unhelpful","unintended","unintentionally","unleash","unpopular","unpredictable","unprocessed","unrealistic","unrelated","unrestricted"],"title":"The New Principal","t":"When Ms. Zhao became principal of a struggling secondary school, she found many problems. **Unexcused** absences were common, students' **unhappiness** was obvious, and teachers felt their opinions went **unheard**. The school had **unequal** resources: some classrooms had new computers, while others lacked basic textbooks.\n\nMs. Zhao was **unfamiliar** with the town, but she had an **unerring** sense of what students needed. Her first decisions were **unpopular**. She banned **unrestricted** phone use during lessons and replaced **unprocessed** snacks in the canteen with healthier meals. Some parents expressed **undisguised** anger, and a few teachers made **unhelpful** comments at meetings.\n\nShe knew that change can have **unintended** consequences. When she introduced longer school days, some students became more tired, an **undesired** effect she had not expected. She **unintentionally** upset the sports teachers by cutting their training time, and she quickly apologised and changed the schedule.\n\nHer boldest idea was to turn a piece of **undeveloped** land beside the school into a garden. Despite **unfavorable** weather in the first year, students grew vegetables and flowers. The project seemed to **unleash** their creativity and confidence. Even students who were often in trouble found something they loved.\n\nMs. Zhao admits that teaching is **unpredictable**. Some of her plans were **unrealistic**, and others failed for reasons **unrelated** to her decisions. But after three years, attendance has risen sharply and exam results have improved. \"Listening is the most important skill a principal can have,\" she says."},"51":{"w":["unscientific","unseen","unskilled","unsociable","unsold","unsolicited","unused","unwanted","unwelcome","unwillingness","upcoming","upload","upside","urbanise","urbanization","urgency","usable","vaccine","validate","validation"],"title":"The Second-Hand App","t":"As **urbanization** accelerates across China, millions of young people move into small city flats. Many arrive with little furniture and leave a few years later with piles of **unwanted** things. A young entrepreneur named Chen Bo saw an opportunity: an app where people can sell or give away **unused** items.\n\nThe idea came from personal experience. When Chen moved to Shanghai, he found that most of his neighbours were **unsociable**, rarely speaking to each other. He wondered whether sharing things could build community. The app lets users **upload** photos of old furniture, clothes and electronics, and buyers can collect them nearby. Every **usable** item gets a second life.\n\nThe project faced doubts. Investors showed **unwillingness** to fund it, and some called the idea **unscientific**, saying there was no proof that people would trust strangers. Chen had to **validate** his idea by testing it in three neighbourhoods. Their **validation** convinced investors, and the app soon spread across the city.\n\nThe app has an unexpected **upside**: it helps **unskilled** workers earn extra money by delivering and repairing goods. During a city **vaccine** campaign, clinics even used the app to find **unused** fridges for storing medicine. Chen says that **unsold** goods are the biggest **unseen** cost of modern consumption.\n\nNot every message is friendly. Users sometimes receive **unsolicited** advertisements, and some feel these are **unwelcome**. Chen's team works with great **urgency** to block spam. As cities continue to **urbanise**, he plans to expand to other regions. \"The **upcoming** generation,\" he says, \"cares more about sharing than owning.\""},"52":{"w":["variability","vegan","vegetarian","vend","ventilation","venue","verification","viability","viable","visionary","vitality","volatile","volcanic","voter","vulnerability","waiver","warranty","wary","wasteland","waterway"],"title":"The Vegan Food Festival","t":"Last summer, our city held its first **vegan** and **vegetarian** food festival. The **venue** was an old warehouse beside the river **waterway**, transformed with lights, plants and long wooden tables. More than twenty thousand people attended, surprising even the organisers.\n\nThe festival's founder, Ms. Lan, is a **visionary** who believes that plant-based food is the future. Ten years ago, people told her that a vegetarian business was not **viable**. Today, she runs five restaurants. \"People are **wary** of change at first,\" she says, \"but taste wins in the end.\"\n\nOrganising the festival involved many details. Each stall had to pass a **verification** process to confirm that its food contained no animal products. The old building needed better **ventilation**, so engineers installed large fans. Small businesses were offered a fee **waiver**, which allowed young cooks to **vend** their food for free. Equipment rental companies provided a **warranty** for every cooking machine.\n\nFood prices have been **volatile** in recent years, and there is great **variability** in the cost of vegetables. One farmer explained that his crops grow in rich **volcanic** soil, which produces sweet tomatoes. Another described how he had turned a **wasteland** into a farm through years of hard work. Experts discussed the economic **viability** of vegan farming and the **vulnerability** of small farmers to climate change.\n\nThe festival had a political side too. Local candidates came to speak with each **voter** about environmental policies. But most visitors simply came to eat. The atmosphere was full of energy and **vitality**, with music playing late into the night."},"53":{"w":["wearer","weaving","weird","wellbeing","wetland","wholesale","willpower","withdrawal","workload","workout","worldview","worrisome","worsen","worthiness","worthlessness","wow","yeah","yoga","yogurt","zodiac"],"title":"Finding Balance","t":"Last year, my friend Ling was exhausted. Her **workload** at a law firm had doubled, and she often worked until midnight. Her health began to **worsen**: she slept badly, ate junk food and stopped exercising. \"I felt a kind of **worthlessness**,\" she says. \"My job was all I had.\"\n\nThe turning point came during a holiday at a **wetland** park in the south. Watching birds float on the quiet water, she realised that her **worldview** had become too narrow. \"I thought success meant money,\" she says. \"I had forgotten about **wellbeing**.\"\n\nWhen she returned home, she made changes. She started a morning **workout** and joined a **yoga** class near her office. At first, the poses felt **weird**, and her muscles complained. \"**Wow**, this is harder than law,\" she laughed. Her teacher told her that **willpower** matters more than flexibility. Ling replaced her usual breakfast with **yogurt** and fruit.\n\nSome changes were difficult. She experienced a mild **withdrawal** from coffee, suffering headaches for a week. Her boss found her new schedule **worrisome**, but she argued that a healthier employee works better. \"**Yeah**, you were right,\" her boss later admitted.\n\nLing also discovered new hobbies. She took a class in **weaving** and made a scarf for her mother, and she now buys fabric **wholesale** to save money. Her mother, the scarf's proud **wearer**, believes in the Chinese **zodiac** and says that the year of the rabbit brought Ling good luck. Ling no longer measures her **worthiness** by her salary. \"Life is about balance,\" she says."}}});
